 from the counter's dependencies\\nwithout adding a fetch selection level or changing target capacity.\\nThis policy change has not been tried in the r\n25:source | Critical endpoint | Logic / routing | Total |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Winner / 1 | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[4], id_ex_w[54] | EX/MEM alu_result[25] D | 2.87 / 3.24 ns | 6.11 ns |\\n| Winner / 2 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[3], id_ex_w[169] | ID/EX valid RESET | 2.77 / 3.67 ns | 6.44 ns |\\n| Winn\n25:er / 3 | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[2], id_ex_w[52] | EX/MEM alu_result[25] D | 2.78 / 3.15 ns | 5.93 ns |\\n| Candidate / 1 | EX/MEM rd[0], ex_mem_w[194] | Row 0 pending_command_q[0] D | 4.25 / 4.57 ns | 8.82 ns |\\n| Candidate / 2 | ID/EX instr[24] / rs2_addr[4], id_ex_w[25] | Row 0 pending_\n25:command_q[0] D | 4.68 / 4.50 ns | 9.18 ns |\\n| Candidate / 3 | EX/MEM instr[10] / rd[3], ex_mem_w[11] | Row 0 pending_command_q[0] D | 4.15 / 5.22 ns | 9.37 ns |\\n\\nThe principal concerns are measured direction interference and t\n25:he larger routed capture path. Source-level removal of the intended wide tag dependency succeeded, but it did not improve the limiting clock/cycle product.\\n\\nEvidence directory:\\n\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.clau\n26:nextpnr reports reproduce\\n163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz. Seeds 1 and 3 have critical paths from\\nID/EX alu_op through result selection to EX/MEM alu_result[25], taking\\n6.10 and 5.93 ns. The final mapped LUT on those paths also receives\\nis_jump: ex_stage currently selects PC+4 after the complete ALU re\n26:nextpnr reports reproduce\\n163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz. Seeds 1 and 3 have critical paths from\\nID/EX alu_op through result selection to EX/MEM alu_result[25], taking\\n6.10 and 5.93 ns. The final mapped LUT on those paths also receives\\nis_jump: ex_stage currently selects PC+4 after the complete ALU re\n26:VFI operands, architectural next\\nPC, accepted predictions, predictor training, nret=1, and external\\nports exact. Preserve all control-bit bubble clearing, EX/MEM divide\\nbubbles, and MEM/WB forwarding retention during data stalls.\\n\\nAll existing integer and multiply expressions, product signedness,\\nAL\n26:\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Supply the instruction's existing PC+4 value to alu.link_value\\nand write alu_result directly to EX/MEM, removing the is_jump\\nresult override. Share the PC+4 expression with actual_next_pc's\\ndefault. Preserve target adders, forwarded source values, traps,\\nredi\n26:rects, predictor events, and all sequential hold/bubble rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Check ALU_LINK for legal JAL/JALR while retaining their complete\\ncontrol expectations. Keep all i\n26:eck ALU_LINK alignment with the held\\ninstruction through reset, flush, backend holds, and invalid input.\\nRetain all existing control-zero bubble and payload-owner checks;\\nupdate only jump operation expectations affected by the encoding.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise JA\n26:\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Supply the instruction's existing PC+4 value to alu.link_value\\nand write alu_result directly to EX/MEM, removing the is_jump\\nresult override. Share the PC+4 expression with actual_next_pc's\\ndefault. Preserve target adders, forwarded source values, traps,\\nredi\n26:rects, predictor events, and all sequential hold/bubble rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Check ALU_LINK for legal JAL/JALR while retaining their complete\\ncontrol expectations. Keep all i\n26:eck ALU_LINK alignment with the held\\ninstruction through reset, flush, backend holds, and invalid input.\\nRetain all existing control-zero bubble and payload-owner checks;\\nupdate only jump operation expectations affected by the encoding.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise JA\n26:nd ALTOPS builds. EX computes a single 32-bit pc_plus_4 for that input and sequential actual_next_pc, and captures alu_result directly into EX/MEM. There is no outer link override in the ALU.\\n\\nNo pipeline state, bundle fields, retirement width, external ports, forwarding rules, target adders, prediction\n26:/training rules, trap behavior, or sequential hold/bubble rules were changed. All integer, multiply, signed-product, ALTOPS and divider expressions and the eight-edge divider controller remain unchanged. Unsupported i\n26:cludes cycle positions, raw RVFI operands/results, exact accepted predictions, resolution and predictor-commit events, accepted memory data/masks and marker transactions. Deliberately cancelled pre-edge observations in reset tests are excluded from acceptance counts. Both complete pipeline suites passed\n26:ne ELF.\\n\\nMapped and routed cone inspection confirms the intended structural change. In the incoming netlist, ID/EX is_jump reaches all 32 EX/MEM alu_result D cones and directly drives I0 of bit 25's final LUT4. It reaches none of those 32 D cones in the candidate synthesis or any of the three final rout\n26:| Design / seed | Critical path | Total delay, ns | Logic / routing, ns |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Incoming / 1 | ID/EX alu_op[4] to EX/MEM alu_result[25] | 6.10 | 2.87 / 3.24 |\\n| Incoming / 2 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[3] through recovery to ID/EX valid reset | 6.44 | 2.77 / 3.67 |\\n| Incoming / 3\n26: | ID/EX alu_op[2] to EX/MEM alu_result[25] | 5.93 | 2.78 / 3.15 |\\n| Candidate / 1 | EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding and JALR target generation to EX/MEM pc_next[28] | 8.02 | 4.57 / 3.45 |\\n| Candidate / 2 | EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding and JALR ta\n26:rget generation to EX/MEM pc_next[29] | 8.96 | 4.54 / 4.43 |\\n| Candidate / 3 | EX/MEM rd[3] through forwarding and JALR target generation to EX/MEM pc_next[25] | 7.72 | 4.06 / 3.66 |\\n\\nTiming columns preserve nextpnr's printed rounding. Incomin\n26: \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r9s0 (regression, -23.90%): Folding `PC+4` into an `ALU_LINK` case arm removed `is_jump` from all EX/MEM result cones and reduced mapped selection depth, yet forwarding through the unchanged JALR target adder into `EX/MEM.pc_next` became critical in all three seed\n27:t bench RTL byte-for-byte;\\nits final routed reports reproduce 163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz.\\nSeeds 1 and 3 run from ID/EX ALU control to EX/MEM result data in\\n6.10 and 5.93 ns. The r7s0 netlist investigation identifies the\\nmultiply-high class decoder in this cone. Merely registering that\\nclass regre\n27:t bench RTL byte-for-byte;\\nits final routed reports reproduce 163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz.\\nSeeds 1 and 3 run from ID/EX ALU control to EX/MEM result data in\\n6.10 and 5.93 ns. The r7s0 netlist investigation identifies the\\nmultiply-high class decoder in this cone. Merely registering that\\nclass regre\n27: and all\\nordinary integer operations in EX. This is one execution-stage\\nrepartition: high products become available at MEM/WB instead of\\nEX/MEM, without adding an instruction stage or changing retirement\\nwidth. Remove high-product arithmetic and result arms from the fast\\nALU rather than retaining an \n27:high control bit, derived in ID only from a legal\\ndecoded MULH/MULHU/MULHSU. Capture it with the existing control bundle;\\nreset and every bubble clear it, and backend holds retain it. In EX,\\nkeep operand forwarding and capture the existing post-forward rs1_val\\nand rs2_val into EX/MEM. These existing f\n27:ation,\\nprediction, resolved pc_next, and trap metadata paired with operands.\\n\\nCompute the selected high product only from the registered EX/MEM\\noperands and operation. Move the current signed/signed,\\nunsigned/unsigned, and signed/unsigned expressions and their exact\\nhigh-half slices into the new lan\n27:Retain the current\\nnonzero-rd and raw source-field comparisons. An immediately dependent\\ninstruction holds at fetch and inserts one ID/EX bubble, exactly like\\na load consumer. On its later EX cycle, it uses MEM/WB forwarding.\\nQualify EX/MEM forwarding eligibility with NOT is_mul_high so the\\nplacehold\n27:er cannot override an older correct value. Retain the\\nestablished MEM/WB forwarding qualification, including forwarding\\nfrom retained payload after retirement valid clears during a memory\\nhold. Do not introduce operand-use filtering in this experiment.\\n\\nIndependent high multiplies can occupy EX an\n27: advance even if dmemReady is\\nlow; an older stalled load/store retains the existing backend hold.\\nPreserve the complete MEM/WB forwarding payload on a real memory\\nhold. Keep divider launch, accepted operand retention, eight-edge\\nreal/ALTOPS schedule, older-stage draining, and result consumption\\nunchan\n27:ed low/high\\nmultipliers may increase DSP/LUT demand, and existing recovery or\\npredictor paths may cap Fmax. Dependent high multiplies add bubbles;\\nmeasure their actual marker-bracketed frequency and cycle cost. Earlier\\nhigh operations can also change the random-ready phase at the start\\nmarker, so unc\n27:hanged static timed code does not imply identical cycles.\\nInspect mapped and all three routed cones to confirm high arithmetic\\nis fed by EX/MEM registers and cannot reach the fast EX result directly.\\n\\nRun unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled ISS cosim, and FPGA gates.\\nCompare exact iterations/bra\n27:ve is_mul_high from legal decoded MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and assign\\nit in ctrl_decoded. Preserve the winning split between control\\nflushes and payload holds, clearing this bit with every bubble.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove high-half multiply expressions and result cases from fast\\nexecu\n27:lu_result for in.ctrl.is_mul_high on normal\\nadvancement. Preserve all other data, trap, bus, and RVFI behavior,\\nincluding held forwarding payload and valid-only clearing. High\\noperations neither drive dmem nor wait for unrelated dmem readiness.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Send mem_read \n27:OR is_mul_high to the generalized late-result hazard\\ninput. Mask EX/MEM reg_write eligibility at the forwarding-unit\\nconnection when is_mul_high is set. Leave MEM/WB eligibility and\\nforwarding data intact; never add a combination\n27:\"description\": \"Generalize id_ex_mem_read to id_ex_late_result while retaining\\nexactly the existing dependency equation, nonzero-rd rule,\\nbubble/hold priorities, redirect handling, and memory/divide waits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt the fast-ALU runner to its new internal re\n27:e the exhaustive truth table for the renamed late-result\\ninput without removing any original combinations or assertions.\\nRetain exact one-bubble behavior and backend-hold precedence.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing control/payload matrix with all three high\\nop\n27:ndaries, and high results feeding ALU/MUL, another\\nhigh multiply, DIV/REM, branch/JALR, and load/store base or data.\\nCheck one dependency bubble with ready buses and no EX/MEM high\\nplaceholder forwarding. Exercise memory holds, retained MEM/WB\\nforwarding after valid clears, instruction stalls, redirec\n27:ve is_mul_high from legal decoded MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and assign\\nit in ctrl_decoded. Preserve the winning split between control\\nflushes and payload holds, clearing this bit with every bubble.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove high-half multiply expressions and result cases from fast\\nexecu\n27:lu_result for in.ctrl.is_mul_high on normal\\nadvancement. Preserve all other data, trap, bus, and RVFI behavior,\\nincluding held forwarding payload and valid-only clearing. High\\noperations neither drive dmem nor wait for unrelated dmem readiness.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Send mem_read \n27:OR is_mul_high to the generalized late-result hazard\\ninput. Mask EX/MEM reg_write eligibility at the forwarding-unit\\nconnection when is_mul_high is set. Leave MEM/WB eligibility and\\nforwarding data intact; never add a combination\n27:\"description\": \"Generalize id_ex_mem_read to id_ex_late_result while retaining\\nexactly the existing dependency equation, nonzero-rd rule,\\nbubble/hold priorities, redirect handling, and memory/divide waits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt the fast-ALU runner to its new internal re\n27:e the exhaustive truth table for the renamed late-result\\ninput without removing any original combinations or assertions.\\nRetain exact one-bubble behavior and backend-hold precedence.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing control/payload matrix with all three high\\nop\n27:ndaries, and high results feeding ALU/MUL, another\\nhigh multiply, DIV/REM, branch/JALR, and load/store base or data.\\nCheck one dependency bubble with ready buses and no EX/MEM high\\nplaceholder forwarding. Exercise memory holds, retained MEM/WB\\nforwarding after valid clears, instruction stalls, redirec\n27:partition in cores/bench only. MULH,\\nMULHU, and MULHSU now execute in mul_high_unit, instantiated by mem_stage\\non the existing registered EX/MEM post-forward operands and operation.\\nTheir original full-width signed/unsigned expressions and high-half\\nslices are preserved. Their ALTOPS formulas also mov\n27:the instruction. The load-use equation is generalized\\nto mem_read OR is_mul_high with the original nonzero-rd and raw source\\ncomparisons. EX/MEM forwarding excludes high operations. MEM/WB forwarding\\nqualification and every retained payload field remain unchanged when a\\nreal memory hold clears retirem\n27:ne and integrated retirement tests. Additional checks cover\\n  alternating signedness, carry-heavy operands, independent throughput,\\n  one-bubble immediate dependencies, one-gap dependencies, both sources,\\n  raw immediate-field hazards, repeated rd, x0, every M consumer, ALU,\\n  branch/JALR, load/store \n27:ectural results, operands, PCs, orders, and\\nretirement cycles match the incoming RTL. Non-memory mem_rdata is allowed\\nto differ with both masks zero. Measured cycle cost is zero for these\\ntwo workloads, including any ready-phase effects; unit tests separately\\nrequire exactly one bubble for an immedi\n27:ural evidence and concerns\\n\\nMapped netlist traversal confirms every sequential source of the MEM\\nDSP's operand/sign-selection cone is an EX/MEM register bit (70 bits,\\nincluding the operand and control bits). All 32 first sequential captures\\nreachable from its result are MEM/WB data inputs. There is n\n27:o direct\\ncombinational path from this high multiplier to EX/MEM result capture.\\nThe original single MULT36X36 becomes two MULT36X36 cells: one for EX MUL-low\\nand one for MEM high multiplication. Thus the predicted DSP cos\n27:e; the source\\narithmetic itself was not redesigned.\\n\\nAll three final routed netlists independently pass the same boundary\\ntraversal: 70 EX/MEM source register bits, 32 MEM/WB result capture bits,\\nand no direct path to EX result capture. The high DSP is at\\nX10Y18/MULT36X36 in every candidate route. T\n27:om the historical\\n388.66 2 KB result.\\n\\nFinal critical cones, inspected in each routed report:\\n\\n- Incoming seed 1: ID/EX ALU control to EX/MEM ALU result, 6.10 ns.\\n- Incoming seed 2: ID/EX operand through recovery to ID/EX control reset,\\n  6.44 ns.\\n- Incoming seed 3: ID/EX ALU control to EX/MEM ALU\n27: result, 5.93 ns.\\n- Candidate seed 1: ID/EX alu_op[1] (id_ex_w[51]) through fast ALU result\\n  selection to EX/MEM alu_result[29], 7.26 ns (2.46 logic / 4.80 routing).\\n- Candidate seed 2: EX/MEM rd[2] through forwarding and the JALR adder to\\n  predictor pending_target_q[2\n27:4], 7.45 ns (3.61 logic / 3.85 routing).\\n- Candidate seed 3: EX/MEM rd[4] through forwarding and the JALR adder to\\n  predictor pending_target_q[13], 7.46 ns (3.13 logic / 4.33 routing).\\n\\nThe MEM high multiplier is absent fro\n28:eld from resolve_target[1:0] != 0, independently of outcome.\\nContinue capturing the full tag and row validity at the existing edge.\\nEvery payload capture enable must remain resolve_valid && row_index_hit;\\nneither branch outcome nor target legality qualifies that enable.\\n\\nExpose the pre-trap decoder-le\n28:ed target path capture its raw event on every\\nresolve_valid, rather than only when live target_write is true. Retain\\nits index and target payload, and capture the same raw classification\\nfields alongside them. At the existing commit edge, generate its write\\nenable exclusively from that registered event\n28:redirects, so accepted events drain exactly once. Reset clears all\\ntable/event validity, suppresses writes, and cancels pending work. Raw\\npayloads need no reset because validity masks every commit and lookup.\\n\\nThis experiment targets the late EX classification and target-capture\\nenable cones; it does \n28:coverage for adjacent legal jumps,\\nuntaken branches, decoded traps, and target-alignment traps around\\nmemory holds, imem stalls, load-use bubbles, and divide completion.\\nCheck unchanged recovery cycles and training visibility, strict\\nRVFI order and PC chaining, link/operand data, and suppression of\\nw\n28:coverage for adjacent legal jumps,\\nuntaken branches, decoded traps, and target-alignment traps around\\nmemory holds, imem stalls, load-use bubbles, and divide completion.\\nCheck unchanged recovery cycles and training visibility, strict\\nRVFI order and PC chaining, link/operand data, and suppression of\\nw\n28:`resolve_target[1:0] != 0` without outcome qualification. Tag and all five fields use only `resolve_valid && resolve_index == row` as their payload capture enable.\\n- A combinational `classify_event` function is applied independently to each registered metadata event and to the registered target event. It \n28:freshes each edge regardless of holds or recovery. Reset clears table/event validity, cancels pending work, and suppresses publication. Raw payloads have no reset.\\n\\nThe table remains 944 source-level bits. Event state grows from 499 to 552 source-level bits: `16*(1+26+5) + (1+4+30+5)`. There is no extra \n28:order, PC chaining, bus and operand checks, and verifies the new decoder-legality output against the ID/EX control bundle even on holds and bubbles. New directed programs put adjacent jumps, untaken misaligned branches, decoded traps and target traps around memory holds, instruction stalls, load-use bubbl\n28:ve as distinct flip-flops: five per metadata row and five in the target event. There are no encoded pending-command registers.\\n- All **535 payload capture-enable endpoints** were checked: 16 private sets of 31 bits and 39 global target-event bits. Each private enable equals the existing event enable AND i\n28:ent has no branch-outcome/decoder-legality qualification.\\n- The eight inferred RAM16SDP4 target banks receive the thirty registered target payload bits and registered index. Their WRE truth tables depend only on registered raw classification, pending validity and reset, and implement legal JUMP/TAKEN publ\n28:fore raw outcome capture. The long autogenerated names containing `pending_target_q` on intermediate combinational nets do not imply target payload/classification mixing; the pin-level audit above verifies the actual sources.\\n\\n| Seed | Critical source | Critical endpoint | Logic / routing | Total |\\n| --\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Suppress load-use bubbles for non-source instruction fields\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current hazard_unit compares a load destination against both raw\\ninstruction \n29:format.\\nImmediate bits can therefore create a dependency that does not exist.\\nFor example, LW x5,0(x1) followed by ADDI x6,x2,5 inserts a bubble because\\nthe immediate's low five bits equal x5, although ADDI only reads x2.\\nLUI, AUIPC, and JAL can falsely match either field despite reading no\\ninteger s\n29:ource register. Removing these bubbles directly improves\\niterations per cycle without adding a datapath or pipeline stage.\\n\\nThe bench log contains no prior source-use qualification experiment.\\n\n29:nt. LESSONS.md repeatedly reports\\nunchanged CoreMark cycles and losses from remapping or another critical\\npath. The accepted r5s0 control/payload separation remains valuable and\\nis compatible with this small change to the interlock condition.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current hazard_unit com\n29:format.\\nImmediate bits can therefore create a dependency that does not exist.\\nFor example, LW x5,0(x1) followed by ADDI x6,x2,5 inserts a bubble because\\nthe immediate's low five bits equal x5, although ADDI only reads x2.\\nLUI, AUIPC, and JAL can falsely match either field despite reading no\\ninteger s\n29:ource register. Removing these bubbles directly improves\\niterations per cycle without adding a datapath or pipeline stage.\\n\\nThe bench log contains no prior source-use qualification experiment.\\n\n29:nt. LESSONS.md repeatedly reports\\nunchanged CoreMark cycles and losses from remapping or another critical\\npath. The accepted r5s0 control/payload separation remains valuable and\\nis compatible with this small change to the interlock condition.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Qualify the two existing load-use equality\n29:lds to suppress comparisons.\\n\\nPreserve every remaining hazard output equation and its priority.\\nA real load dependency still inserts one bubble when the backend is\\nfree; memory/divide waits retain their existing hold behavior. Stores\\nmust interlock on either address-base or write-data dependencies,\\n\n29:branches on either operand, and JALR on its base even when rd=x0.\\nA load to x0 never causes a dependency bubble. Retain r5s0's complete\\nctrl/valid clearing and payload capture on bubbles, instruction-to-NOP\\nflushing, redirect priority, EX/MEM divide bubbles, and MEM/WB\n29:lacement. During\\nevaluation, count false matches in the incoming design separately from\\ntrue dependencies. Count an immediately removable bubble only when\\ninstruction memory is ready and there is no redirect or backend hold;\\noverlapping stall reasons must not be claimed as independent savings.\\nCompar\n29:this count.\\n\\nRun unchanged lint, unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA\\ngates. Directed tests must prove both removal of false bubbles and\\nretention of every true dependency. Architectural results, used RVFI\\noperands, PC chaining, and accepted memory effects must remain correct;\\nretirement\n29: load destinations. Retain all existing combinations and\\nassertions for instruction/data readiness, redirects, divide waits,\\ncontrol-only bubbles, and backend-hold precedence. Include reserved\\nfunct encodings in integration coverage without relaxing legality.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"des\n29:cription\": \"Add zero-wait directed pairs proving no bubble for an independent\\nimmediate instruction whose immediate bits match the preceding load\\ndestination, and for LUI/AUIPC/JAL with matching non-source fields.\\nC\n29:rce R/M operations and\\nbranches, and stores dependent separately on base and write data;\\neach real load-use pair must retain its required bubble and correct\\nloaded operands. Repeat under instruction stalls, prolonged data\\nholds, recovery, divide completion, and reset. Preserve all existing\\ndecoder, a\n29: load destinations. Retain all existing combinations and\\nassertions for instruction/data readiness, redirects, divide waits,\\ncontrol-only bubbles, and backend-hold precedence. Include reserved\\nfunct encodings in integration coverage without relaxing legality.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"des\n29:cription\": \"Add zero-wait directed pairs proving no bubble for an independent\\nimmediate instruction whose immediate bits match the preceding load\\ndestination, and for LUI/AUIPC/JAL with matching non-source fields.\\nC\n29:rce R/M operations and\\nbranches, and stores dependent separately on base and write data;\\neach real load-use pair must retain its required bubble and correct\\nloaded operands. Repeat under instruction stalls, prolonged data\\nholds, recovery, divide completion, and reset. Preserve all existing\\ndecoder, a\n29:26-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r10s0 (broken, n/a): Qualifying only `hazard_unit` load-use stalls leaves raw unused source fields in RVFI, where EX/MEM forwarding supplies the preceding load's address instead of its data and fails `reg_ch0`; removing these bubbles requires consistent RVFI source reporting.\"}\n30:exact predicted_next_pc, and validity.\\nRead registers and decode at actual ID acceptance, never at enqueue time.\\nKeep the existing ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB stages and single retirement.\\n\\nSeparate the two acceptance events. Let q_valid denote slot occupancy,\\navailable = q_valid || io_imemReady, and i\n30:d detection from the selected oldest instruction, not the\\ninstruction at the now-ahead bus PC. Replace raw imem readiness in the\\nfrontend bubble/hold equations with instruction availability. A bus stall\\nmust not bubble a valid queued instruction. Retain the existing raw-source\\nload-use comparisons, ba\n30:ckend holds, complete control clearing on bubbles,\\nretained MEM/WB forwarding data, and eight-edge real/ALTOPS divider behavior.\\nPreserve the existing NOP substitution for recovery or no available\\ninstruct\n30:ion and the winning ID/EX control-versus-payload reset separation.\\n\\nEX recovery clears slot validity, suppresses both transfers on that edge,\\nkills the younger decode instruction, and redirects the fetch P\n30:ing the existing\\nmisprediction latency. Reset cancels buffered work. Flush only the slot's\\nvalid bit; do not distribute recovery into its wide payload reset/enable.\\nA stored prediction is immutable through all