ench/test/test_pipeline.py:800:async def predictor_reset_cancels_captured_resolution(dut):\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:810:    # _run resets on the very next edge, when the captured self-jump would\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:852:    \"\"\"Check exactly-once execution, full bubbles, saved metadata, and writes.\"\"\"\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:1108:async def divider_reset_during_execution(dut):\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py:1136:    # speed; a real divide on a flushed path must never enter the engine.\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Single pending-store slot with direct zero-wait completion\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"In cores/bench, hazard_unit freezes IF, ID, and EX whenever a memory\\ninstruction in EX/MEM encounters io_dmemReady=0. An aligned RAM store\\nalready has its address, data, and byte enables at this point, so retaining\\nthose fields separately can release the pipeline to execute younger\\narithmetic while the write waits for the bus. This directly targets the\\napproximately 22% random data-bus backpressure used for CoreMark fitness.\\n\\nBench has no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the baseline:\\n4,491,485 timed cycles and 127.03 MHz median Fmax, yielding 282.82 iter/s.\\nRead-only reference evidence from cores/v1, hyp-20260428-017-r8s1, supports\\na single pending store with a direct ready path: it passed formal/cosim\\nand improved fitness 5.89% on that different design. Its cycle saving was\\nunder 1%, and placement contributed to the result, so that improvement\\nmust not be assumed for bench. The main risk is losing more Fmax to bus\\narbitration than the saved stall cycles recover.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"In cores/bench, hazard_unit freezes IF, ID, and EX whenever a memory\\ninstruction in EX/MEM encounters io_dmemReady=0. An aligned RAM store\\nalready has its address, data, and byte enables at this point, so retaining\\nthose fields separately can release the pipeline to execute younger\\narithmetic while the write waits for the bus. This directly targets the\\napproximately 22% random data-bus backpressure used for CoreMark fitness.\\n\\nBench has no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the baseline:\\n4,491,485 timed cycles and 127.03 MHz median Fmax, yielding 282.82 iter/s.\\nRead-only reference evidence from cores/v1, hyp-20260428-017-r8s1, supports\\na single pending store with a direct ready path: it passed formal/cosim\\nand improved fitness 5.89% on that different design. Its cycle saving was\\nunder 1%, and placement contributed to the result, so that improvement\\nmust not be assumed for bench. The main risk is losing more Fmax to bus\\narbitration than the saved stall cycles recover.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decouple store retirement from bus completion with exactly one pending\\nstore slot. Allocate it only for a valid, legal, naturally aligned store\\nwhose complete access lies in normal RAM [0x00000000, 0x00100000), when\\nthe slot is empty and io_dmemReady=0. Capture the effective byte address,\\nexisting replicated write data, and byte mask atomically. Advance that\\ninstruction into MEM/WB once, carrying its original PC, instruction,\\noperands, and aligned RVFI memory fields. Its eventual bus drain produces\\nno additional retirement. The slot holds only the physical write request;\\nRVFI metadata continues along the existing pipeline.\\n\\nWith the slot empty and io_dmemReady=1, loads and stores use the existing\\ndirect MEM path without an enqueue cycle. While the slot is valid, give\\nits request sole ownership of dmem, hold address/data/byte enables stable\\nuntil a ready handshake, and suppress any younger external request.\\nDrain independently of frontend stalls and redirects. Permit younger\\nnon-memory instructions to advance and retire in order while it drains.\\nBlock every younger load or store until the slot is empty, including the\\ncycle that drains the old request; omit simultaneous drain/refill,\\ncoalescing, and store-to-load forwarding to keep arbitration small.\\n\\nAll MMIO, UART, start/stop marker accesses, and out-of-range addresses\\nremain on the existing unbuffered path. Never use buffering to hide an\\nout-of-bounds access from the existing checks. FENCE/FENCE.I, EBREAK,\\nand trapping instructions must also wait for an older pending store to\\ndrain. Marker writes complete only on their real bus handshake, after\\nprior writes, so the timed region cannot exclude undrained work. Invalid,\\nillegal, and misaligned stores never enqueue or drive a write. Redirects\\ncannot discard an already accepted older store; reset clears slot validity.\\n\\nReplace the hazard unit's raw dmem-stall predicate with a MEM-generated\\nmem_wait signal describing whether the current EX/MEM instruction can\\nadvance. A pending store alone must not assert mem_wait for arithmetic.\\nOn a real wait, preserve the existing EX/MEM and ID/EX holds and the\\nMEM/WB behavior that clears valid but retains forwarding data. Derive\\nmem_wait from current registered state, instruction classification, and\\nbus readiness, without a combinational dependency on hold_wb. Keep the\\ndirect zero-wait path short and retain the existing EX datapath and\\nload-use interlock. Preserve nret=1, all top-level ports, strict channel-0\\nretirement order, decoder legality, and every existing correctness gate.\\n\\nSuccess requires a measured fitness improvement over 282.82 using the\\nunchanged marker-bracketed CoreMark run and three-seed median Fmax. The\\nestimate is a 2% fitness gain from overlapping store waits with useful\\nexecution while keeping Fmax close to baseline; it is not a measured\\nresult. Verify the pending path with directed and random bus stalls,\\nsince a ready-tied-high formal configuration cannot exercise allocation.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry request register with valid, enqueue, address, data,\\nmask, and ready/drain controls. Retain its payload until one accepted\\nwrite, disallow replacement while occupied, and clear valid on reset.\\nDo not retain a second copy of the RVFI retirement bundle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept dmem readiness and pending-slot state. Classify valid aligned\\nRAM stores for enqueue, retain direct ready completion, and emit\\nexplicit mem_wait plus slot enqueue fields. Enqueue and advance the\\noriginal store into MEM/WB on the same edge. Serialize subsequent\\nmemory operations, fences, EBREAK, and traps behind the slot. Keep\\neach live instruction's RVFI masks/data separate from the draining\\nrequest and retain the forwarding payload when MEM/WB valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the slot, route enqueue and mem_wait controls, and give\\nthe pending request priority over the live MEM dmem outputs only\\nwhile slot-valid is set. Suppress live read/write enables during a\\ndrain. Preserve the core's existing single-channel RVFI and bus ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use mem_wait instead of !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op to drive the\\nexisting backend hold controls. Preserve load-use, instruction-bus,\\nredirect priorities, and MEM/WB forwarding retention, while allowing\\narithmetic to progress when only a buffered store is waiting.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Include store_slot.sv in the pipeline source list. Add a handshake-\\naccurate stalled-bus test mode without removing existing assertions.\\nCheck SW/SH/SB enqueue and drain, stable requests, one physical write\\nand one RVFI retirement per store, and arithmetic progress during a\\nprolonged store wait. Cover subsequent same/different-address loads,\\nconsecutive stores, load-use forwarding across a hold, imem stalls,\\nredirects, fences, EBREAK, reset, illegal/misaligned stores, and UART\\nand timing markers behind a pending store. Assert strict RVFI order,\\ncorrect byte data, and that serialization prevents premature markers.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry request register with valid, enqueue, address, data,\\nmask, and ready/drain controls. Retain its payload until one accepted\\nwrite, disallow replacement while occupied, and clear valid on reset.\\nDo not retain a second copy of the RVFI retirement bundle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept dmem readiness and pending-slot state. Classify valid aligned\\nRAM stores for enqueue, retain direct ready completion, and emit\\nexplicit mem_wait plus slot enqueue fields. Enqueue and advance the\\noriginal store into MEM/WB on the same edge. Serialize subsequent\\nmemory operations, fences, EBREAK, and traps behind the slot. Keep\\neach live instruction's RVFI masks/data separate from the draining\\nrequest and retain the forwarding payload when MEM/WB valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the slot, route enqueue and mem_wait controls, and give\\nthe pending request priority over the live MEM dmem outputs only\\nwhile slot-valid is set. Suppress live read/write enables during a\\ndrain. Preserve the core's existing single-channel RVFI and bus ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use mem_wait instead of !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op to drive the\\nexisting backend hold controls. Preserve load-use, instruction-bus,\\nredirect priorities, and MEM/WB forwarding retention, while allowing\\narithmetic to progress when only a buffered store is waiting.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Include store_slot.sv in the pipeline source list. Add a handshake-\\naccurate stalled-bus test mode without removing existing assertions.\\nCheck SW/SH/SB enqueue and drain, stable requests, one physical write\\nand one RVFI retirement per store, and arithmetic progress during a\\nprolonged store wait. Cover subsequent same/different-address loads,\\nconsecutive stores, load-use forwarding across a hold, imem stalls,\\nredirects, fences, EBREAK, reset, illegal/misaligned stores, and UART\\nand timing markers behind a pending store. Assert strict RVFI order,\\ncorrect byte data, and that serialization prevents premature markers.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 300.35, \"delta_pct\": 6.2, \"vs_baseline\": 6.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.89, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4457730, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10274, \"ff\": 1869, \"seeds\": [133.89, 139.94, 124.38], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T14:03:28.213389Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 (regression, +6.20%): With `mem_wait` blocking younger loads/stores through the pending store's drain cycle, CoreMark cycles fell only 0.75%, so the fitness gain came mainly from higher median Fmax (127.03 to 133.89 MHz).\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d['id'] in ['hyp-20260905-001-r2s0','hyp-20260905-001-r3s0']:\n        h=d['hypothesis']\n        print(d['id'])\n        print(h['hypothesis'] if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    d=json.loads(line)\n    if d['id'] in ['hyp-20260905-001-r2s0','hyp-20260905-001-r3s0']:\n        h=d['hypothesis']\n        print(d['id'])\n        print(h['hypothesis'] if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260905-001-r2s0\nMove the existing binary-to-operation decode across the ID/EX register.\nKeep the decoder's five-bit ALU operation encoding and legality rules,\nbut derive 19 individual selects in ID, one for each ALU_ADD through\nALU_REMU operation, and register them with the instruction in a dedicated\nid_ex_t field. Each select is a comparison against the existing encoded\noperation constant. For every accepted instruction the vector must agree\nwith dec_alu_op; it must hold during a data-memory stall and clear with\nthe existing reset/flush bubble. Do not derive instruction legality from\nthis vector or suppress the decoder's default ADD selection on traps.\n\nFeed those registered selects directly into the combinational ALU.\nReplace the binary-op case selection with parallel masked result terms\ncombined by a balanced OR reduction. Each term uses its corresponding\nselect bit directly; do not re-encode the vector, compare the complete\nvector with one-hot constants, or build a priority chain in EX. An\nall-zero vector returns zero. Preserve the existing arithmetic candidate\nexpressions, operand widths and signedness, shift masking, multiply-high\nvariants, divide-by-zero and signed-overflow handling. Keep all eight\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and their operation mappings identical.\nThis is a control-decode retiming and selection rewrite, with no new ALU\npipeline stage, arithmetic algorithm, or instruction scheduling policy.\n\nPreserve operand forwarding, load-use bubbles, MEM/WB data retention\nduring stalls, branch resolution, predictor training and recovery, and\nthe architectural RVFI values. The sole intended gain is removing\nencoded-op decode from the measured EX timing path. Expect approximately\nsix percent higher fitness from higher Fmax, with unchanged CoreMark\ncycles for the same binary and ready schedule. This is an unmeasured\nestimate. Extra control flops, one-hot fanout, added ID decode delay, or\nan operand-to-result path becoming critical could erase the benefit.\n\nValidate the existing real-arithmetic ALU tests, decoder and pipeline\nsuites, unchanged ALTOPS formal gate, and stalled architectural cosim.\nCheck operation-select alignment through holds and flushes. Run the\nunchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation and compare median Fmax times the\nfull-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles ratio against 319.45; report\nLUT/FF changes and the new critical path. Keep benchmark configurations\ncomparable, and verify the specified 6 KB canonical CRCs, timing markers,\nand validation UART literal without changing evaluation files. Preserve\nnret=1, every external core port, and all hard invariants.\n\nhyp-20260905-001-r3s0\nReplace only the predictor's shared, dynamically indexed training logic\nwith sixteen constant-index entry update blocks in rtl/if_stage.sv.\nFor entry i, decode selected_i = resolve_valid && (resolve_pc[5:2] == i)\nand independently compute hit_i = valid_q[i] &&\n(tag_q[i] == resolve_pc[31:6]). Read that entry's tag and two counter bits\nthrough constant indices, so tag comparison and counter next-state\ncalculation operate locally in parallel with index decoding. Do not\nreduce hits into a shared resolve_hit or select an old counter through\na resolve-index mux before calculating its update. Each state bit must\nhave one sequential owner, with reset taking precedence.\n\nPreserve the exact existing transition rules. Only the selected entry\nmay change. An illegal, trapping, or non-control resolution clears valid\nonly on a local full-tag hit. A legal jump writes valid, tag and target\nand sets its counter to 11. A legal taken branch writes valid, tag and\ntarget, initializes a miss to 10, and saturating-increments a hit. A\nlegal not-taken branch saturating-decrements only a matching entry,\nretains its target, and does nothing on a miss. Encode increment and\ndecrement as local two-bit Boolean equations or complete transition\ncases, including self-transitions at 11 and 00; avoid a shared arithmetic\nresult and separate saturation qualification of a shared write enable.\nReset clears all valid bits while leaving payloads unreset as today.\n\nKeep the sixteen-entry direct-mapped organization, PC[5:2] index, full\nPC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, two-bit hysteresis, and existing\ncombinational fetch lookup. Training remains visible only after its\nclock edge, with no update bypass or additional register stage. Preserve\nresolve_valid qualification from EX, exact predicted_next_pc capture,\nrecovery priority, stall behavior, and all nret=1 bus and RVFI semantics.\nThis is one implementation-level timing optimization of predictor\ntraining; instruction scheduling and prediction decisions remain equal\ncycle by cycle for the same inputs.\n\nRemoving the resolve-side read-mux, shared compare/update, and writeback\ndistribution chain should raise median Fmax with unchanged bracketed\ncycles. Estimate a five percent fitness gain, approximately 336.29 iter/s\nat 141.78 MHz under the current scored configuration. This is a hypothesis,\nnot a measurement. Replicated tag comparisons, increased resolve-signal\nfanout, or losing LUTRAM packing can increase area and routing delay;\nthe unchanged fetch/recovery path may also become limiting.\n\nValidate public prediction behavior against the existing policy across\nall indices and colliding full tags, then run the unchanged unit, formal,\nstalled cosim and FPGA gates. Compare against 320.28 using the same ELF,\nready schedule and benchmark configuration, full-precision iterations\ndivided by bracketed cycles, and three-seed median Fmax. Report LUT/FF\nusage and the new critical path. Require identical scored cycle counts\nand preserve all CRC, UART validation, memory-bound and timing-marker\nchecks. Prior notes distinguish the scored workload from a supplemental\n6 KB run; retain that distinction and verify the requested canonical\n6 KB CRCs separately if the unchanged gate uses another configuration.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,235p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\nsed -n '835,920p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\nsed -n '1,105p' cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '100,235p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\nsed -n '835,920p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\nsed -n '1,105p' cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\"","aggregated_output":"                self.rows[index] = (tag, old[\"target\"] & ~3,\n                                    min(3, entry[2] + 1) if hit else 2)\n            elif hit:\n                self.rows[index] = (tag, entry[1], max(0, entry[2] - 1))\n        self.pending = event\n\n\n# ── Harness ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nasync def _run(dut, program, dmem_init=None, max_cycles=200, *,\n               ready=None, on_retire=None, trace=None, retire_limit=None,\n               on_cycle=None):\n    \"\"\"Drive imem/dmem; capture RVFI retirements until EBREAK or max_cycles.\n\n    imem is read combinationally each cycle from imemAddr; dmem similarly.\n    `ready(cycle, fetch_pc, data_addr)` supplies deterministic (imem, dmem)\n    ready values. Stores commit only at the accepting edge. `on_retire`\n    can change the instruction memory to exercise stale predictor entries.\n    `trace` collects pre-edge fetch/resolution events and accepted stores.\n\n    Returns (retirements, dmem) where retirements is a list of dicts\n    sampled on every cycle that rvfi_valid=1.\n    \"\"\"\n    imem = dict(program) if isinstance(program, dict) else {\n        i * 4: instr & 0xFFFFFFFF for i, instr in enumerate(program)\n    }\n    dmem = dict(dmem_init or {})\n    retirements: list[dict] = []\n    stores = []\n    reads = []\n    predictor = _TrainingReference()\n    issued = []\n    resolutions = []\n    if trace is not None:\n        trace.update(cycles=[], stores=stores, reads=reads)\n\n    # Manual clocking keeps bus acceptance and RVFI sampling unambiguous,\n    # and allows a second reset/run on the same DUT without another clock.\n    dut.clock.value = 0\n    dut.reset.value = 1\n    dut.io_imemData.value = 0\n    dut.io_dmemRData.value = 0\n    dut.io_imemReady.value = 1\n    dut.io_dmemReady.value = 1\n    for _ in range(3):\n        await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n        dut.clock.value = 1\n        await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n        dut.clock.value = 0\n\n    dut.reset.value = 0\n\n    for cycle in range(max_cycles):\n        ia        = int(dut.io_imemAddr.value)\n        da        = int(dut.io_dmemAddr.value)\n        ir, dr = ready(cycle, ia, da) if ready else (1, 1)\n        dut.io_imemReady.value = ir\n        dut.io_dmemReady.value = dr\n        dut.io_imemData.value = imem.get(ia, EBREAK())\n        dut.io_dmemRData.value = dmem.get(da & ~3, 0)\n        await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n\n        prediction = predictor.predict(ia)\n        assert int(dut.u_if.predicted_next_pc.value) == prediction, (\n            f\"cycle {cycle}: prediction bypassed or missed a committed event at {ia:#x}\"\n        )\n        resolving = int(dut.resolve_valid.value)\n        accepted = not (int(dut.stall_if.value) or int(dut.flush_if.value))\n        redirect = int(dut.redirect.value)\n        actual = int(dut.redirect_target.value)\n        event = None\n        if resolving:\n            assert len(issued) == 1, \"duplicate/unaccepted EX training event\"\n            accepted_pc, accepted_prediction, accepted_insn = issued[0]\n            assert int(dut.resolve_pc.value) == accepted_pc\n            assert (int(dut.id_ex_w.value) >> 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF == accepted_insn\n            assert redirect == (actual != accepted_prediction), \"lost exact accepted prediction\"\n            event = dict(pc=accepted_pc, insn=accepted_insn, actual=actual,\n                         target=int(dut.resolve_target.value),\n                         legal=int(dut.resolve_legal.value),\n                         jump=int(dut.resolve_jump.value),\n                         branch=int(dut.resolve_branch.value),\n                         taken=int(dut.resolve_taken.value))\n        else:\n            assert not redirect\n            assert not issued or int(dut.stall_ex_mem.value) or int(dut.div_wait.value), (\n                \"advancing instruction failed to supply one training event\"\n            )\n\n        if trace is not None:\n            trace[\"cycles\"].append({\n                \"cycle\": cycle, \"pc\": ia, \"imem_ready\": ir, \"dmem_ready\": dr,\n                \"predicted\": int(dut.u_if.predicted_next_pc.value),\n                \"accepted\": not (int(dut.stall_if.value) or int(dut.flush_if.value)),\n                \"redirect\": int(dut.redirect.value),\n                \"actual\": int(dut.redirect_target.value),\n                \"resolve_valid\": int(dut.resolve_valid.value),\n                \"resolve_pc\": int(dut.resolve_pc.value),\n                \"resolve_taken\": int(dut.resolve_taken.value),\n                \"resolve_legal\": int(dut.resolve_legal.value),\n                \"commit\": predictor.pending,\n                \"ex_stall\": int(dut.stall_ex_mem.value),\n                \"div_wait\": int(dut.div_wait.value),\n                \"div_launch\": int(dut.u_ex.div_req_valid.value) and int(dut.u_ex.div_req_ready.value),\n                \"div_valid\": int(dut.u_ex.div_result_valid.value),\n                \"div_accept\": int(dut.u_ex.div_result_valid.value) and int(dut.u_ex.div_result_ready.value),\n                \"id_ex\": int(dut.id_ex_w.value),\n                \"ex_mem\": int(dut.ex_mem_w.value),\n                \"mem_wb\": int(dut.mem_wb_w.value),\n                \"fwd_rs1\": int(dut.fwd_rs1_sel.value),\n                \"fwd_rs2\": int(dut.fwd_rs2_sel.value),\n                \"wb_write\": int(dut.wb_w_en.value),\n                \"wb_rd\": int(dut.wb_w_addr.value),\n                \"wb_data\": int(dut.wb_w_data.value),\n            })\n\n        if on_cycle and on_cycle(cycle):\n            # A reset test can stop at a precise pre-edge controller state,\n            # then invoke _run again to reset and start a fresh program.\n            assert not stores, \"cannot abandon accepted stores before retirement\"\n            return retirements, dmem\n\n        if resolving:\n            issued.pop()\n            resolutions.append(event)\n        if accepted:\n            issued.append((ia, prediction, int(dut.io_imemData.value)))\n        assert len(issued) <= 1, \"unexpected additional younger issue slot\"\n        next_pc = actual if redirect else prediction if accepted else ia\n        predictor.edge(event)\n\n        if int(dut.io_dmemREn.value) and dr:\n            reads.append((da & ~3, int(dut.io_dmemRData.value)))\n\n        wen       = int(dut.io_dmemWEn.value)\n        if wen and dr:\n            wdata = int(dut.io_dmemWData.value)\n        ]\n        assert mem == {0x100: 6}\n        assert trace[\"stores\"] == [(0x100, 15, v) for v in range(1, 7)]\n        assert any(e[\"ex_stall\"] for e in trace[\"cycles\"])\n        assert any(not e[\"imem_ready\"] for e in trace[\"cycles\"])\n        for a, b in zip(trace[\"cycles\"], trace[\"cycles\"][1:]):\n            if not a[\"accepted\"] and not a[\"redirect\"]:\n                assert b[\"pc\"] == a[\"pc\"], \"stalled fetch consumed a prediction\"\n            if a[\"ex_stall\"]:\n                assert not a[\"resolve_valid\"] and not a[\"redirect\"]\n\n\ndef _is_div(insn):\n    return insn & 0xFE00007F == 0x02000033 and (insn >> 12) & 7 >= 4\n\n\ndef _assert_div_flow(rets, trace, *, bounded=False):\n    \"\"\"Check exactly-once execution, full bubbles, saved metadata, and writes.\"\"\"\n    events = trace[\"cycles\"]\n    launches = [e for e in events if e[\"div_launch\"]]\n    accepts = [e for e in events if e[\"div_accept\"]]\n    divides = [r for r in rets if _is_div(r[\"insn\"])]\n    assert len(launches) == len(accepts) == len(divides)\n    for launch, accept, retired in zip(launches, accepts, divides):\n        assert launch[\"resolve_pc\"] == accept[\"resolve_pc\"] == retired[\"pc\"]\n        # Result appears after launch plus seven more edges and transfers\n        # into EX/MEM on the following edge, then into the one WB channel.\n        assert accept[\"cycle\"] - launch[\"cycle\"] == 8\n        assert retired[\"cycle\"] == accept[\"cycle\"] + 1\n        waiting = events[launch[\"cycle\"]:accept[\"cycle\"]]\n        assert all(e[\"div_wait\"] and not e[\"div_valid\"] for e in waiting)\n        assert all(not e[\"resolve_valid\"] and not e[\"redirect\"] for e in waiting)\n        assert all(not e[\"accepted\"] for e in waiting)\n        assert all(e[\"id_ex\"] == launch[\"id_ex\"] for e in waiting + [accept])\n        assert all(e[\"pc\"] == launch[\"pc\"] for e in waiting + [accept])\n        assert all(e[\"ex_mem\"] == 0 for e in events[launch[\"cycle\"] + 1:accept[\"cycle\"] + 1])\n        assert all(not e[\"wb_write\"] for e in events[launch[\"cycle\"] + 2:accept[\"cycle\"] + 1])\n        assert accept[\"resolve_valid\"] and not accept[\"ex_stall\"]\n        assert retired[\"trap\"] == 0 and retired[\"pc_next\"] == retired[\"pc\"] + 4\n    for e in events:\n        if e[\"ex_stall\"]:\n            assert not e[\"div_launch\"] and not e[\"div_accept\"]\n            assert not e[\"resolve_valid\"] and not e[\"redirect\"]\n    assert [(e[\"wb_rd\"], e[\"wb_data\"]) for e in events if e[\"wb_write\"] and e[\"wb_rd\"]] == [\n        (r[\"rd\"], r[\"rd_wdata\"]) for r in rets if r[\"rd\"]\n    ]\n    assert trace[\"reads\"] == [(r[\"mem_addr\"], r[\"mem_rdata\"]) for r in rets if r[\"mem_rmask\"]]\n    if bounded:\n        assert all(b[\"cycle\"] - a[\"cycle\"] <= 10 for a, b in zip(rets, rets[1:]))\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def divider_forwarded_sources_and_consecutive(dut):\n    trace = {}\n    rets, _ = await _run(dut, [\n        ADDI(1, 0, 101), ADDI(2, 0, 7), DIV(3, 1, 2),\n        DIV(4, 3, 2), REM(5, 1, 4), DIVU(0, 1, 2),\n        ADD(6, 3, 5), REMU(7, 1, 2), EBREAK(),\n    ], trace=trace)\n    _assert_div_flow(rets, trace, bounded=True)\n    ds = [r for r in rets if _is_div(r[\"insn\"])]\n    assert [(r[\"rs1_rdata\"], r[\"rs2_rdata\"], r[\"rd_wdata\"]) for r in ds] == [\n        (101, 7, 14), (14, 7, 2), (101, 2, 1), (101, 7, 0), (101, 7, 3),\n    ]\n    assert rets[6][\"rd_wdata\"] == 15\n    launches = [e for e in trace[\"cycles\"] if e[\"div_launch\"]]\n    assert (launches[0][\"fwd_rs1\"], launches[0][\"fwd_rs2\"]) == (2, 1)\n    assert launches[1][\"fwd_rs1\"] == 1\n    assert launches[2][\"fwd_rs2\"] == 1\n\n\ndef _li(rd, value):\n    value &= 0xFFFFFFFF\n    return [LUI(rd, (value + 0x800) & 0xFFFFF000), ADDI(rd, rd, value & 0xFFF)]\n\n\ndef _div_reference(funct3, a, b):\n    a, b = a & 0xFFFFFFFF, b & 0xFFFFFFFF\n    if funct3 in (4, 6):\n        a = a - (1 << 32) if a & 0x80000000 else a\n        b = b - (1 << 32) if b & 0x80000000 else b\n        q = abs(a) // abs(b) if b else -1\n        if b and (a < 0) != (b < 0): q = -q\n    else:\n        q = a // b if b else 0xFFFFFFFF\n    return (q if funct3 in (4, 5) else a - q * b) & 0xFFFFFFFF\n// rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n//\n// Detects the only data hazard the textbook 5-stage doesn't cover via\n// forwarding: load-use. A LOAD in EX produces its data only after MEM,\n// so an instruction immediately behind that consumes the LOAD's rd\n// must be stalled by exactly one cycle.\n// Also interlocks IF/ID behind a divide without holding the older stages.\n//\n// Outputs:\n//   stall_if / stall_id : freeze the PC reg and the IF/ID combinational\n//                          payload (cleared by ID's flush input).\n//   flush_if / flush_id : on EX misprediction, kill the one younger\n//                          issue slot (IF/ID is combinational).\n//   flush_id            : also kills ID's own register on load-use to\n//                          inject a single-cycle bubble between LOAD\n//                          and the dependent instruction.\n//\n// Latency:        combinational.\n// RVFI fields:    n/a (governs validity of subsequent retirements).\nmodule hazard_unit (\n  input  logic       id_ex_mem_read,    // ID/EX.ctrl.mem_read (LOAD in EX)\n  input  logic [4:0] id_ex_rd,          // ID/EX.rd            (LOAD's dest)\n  input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs1,         // IF/ID instr[19:15]  (next rs1)\n  input  logic [4:0] if_id_rs2,         // IF/ID instr[24:20]  (next rs2)\n  input  logic       redirect,          // advancing EX prediction mismatch\n  // Bus backpressure (default-1 in zero-wait testbenches; VexRiscv-style\n  // random ~22% stall in vex_main.cpp). When low, the corresponding\n  // memory request is NOT serviced this cycle.\n  input  logic       imem_ready,\n  input  logic       dmem_ready,\n  // EX/MEM has a memory op in flight (the LOAD/STORE the dmem stall\n  // would actually be holding up). Computed at top level from the\n  // EX/MEM register's ctrl.mem_read | ctrl.mem_write.\n  input  logic       ex_mem_mem_op,\n  input  logic       div_wait,          // EX long op; older stages still drain\n  output logic       stall_if,          // PC reg holds\n  output logic       stall_id,          // ID/EX register holds (vs. bubble)\n  output logic       flush_if,          // IF/ID comb output -> NOP\n  output logic       flush_id,          // ID/EX register captures '0\n  output logic       stall_ex_mem,      // EX/MEM register holds\n  output logic       hold_mem_wb        // MEM/WB clears valid only;\n                                        // data fields stay (for fwd)\n);\n\n  logic load_use_hazard;\n  logic imem_stall;\n  logic dmem_stall;\n\n  // Separate equations keep the redirect -> flush path independent of\n  // the flushed instruction's source fields (no false combinational loop).\n  assign load_use_hazard = id_ex_mem_read\n                       && (id_ex_rd == if_id_rs1 || id_ex_rd == if_id_rs2)\n                       && (id_ex_rd != 5'b0);\n  assign imem_stall = !imem_ready;\n  // dmem stall only matters if there's actually a memory op in EX/MEM\n  // — otherwise bus-not-ready is irrelevant to the pipeline.\n  assign dmem_stall = !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op;\n\n  // PC reg holds on any stall reason.\n  assign stall_if = load_use_hazard || imem_stall || dmem_stall || div_wait;\n  // IF/ID combinational payload: NOP whenever we wouldn't have a valid\n  // instruction this cycle (EX recovery, or imem didn't deliver).\n  assign flush_if = redirect || imem_stall;\n  // ID/EX register:\n  //   - dmem_stall  -> hold        (preserve in-flight pipeline state)\n  //   - div_wait    -> hold        (EX supplies full downstream bubbles)\n  //   - load_use    -> bubble      (1-cycle stall between LOAD + use)\n  //   - redirect    -> bubble      (kill wrong-path)\n  //   - otherwise   -> capture\n  // dmem_stall takes precedence over load_use's bubble: re-evaluate\n  // load_use next cycle when the bus unblocks. flush_id is 1 only when\n  // we want bubble (not hold).\n  assign stall_id = dmem_stall || load_use_hazard || div_wait;\n  assign flush_id = (load_use_hazard || redirect) && !dmem_stall && !div_wait;\n  // EX/MEM register: holds on dmem_stall (LOAD waits in MEM until the\n  // bus delivers).\n  assign stall_ex_mem = dmem_stall;\n  // MEM/WB register: on dmem_stall the previously-retired instruction's\n  // data fields stay alive for forwarding (e.g. a held BNE needs the\n  // LOAD's load_data via fwd_mem_wb), but valid is cleared so we don't\n  // double-retire / double-write the regfile.\n  assign hold_mem_wb = dmem_stall;\n\nendmodule\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
