{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat CLAUDE.md; sed -n '1,150p' cores/bench/test/test_hazard_unit.py; sed -n '1,160p' cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\"","aggregated_output":"# CLAUDE.md — non-negotiable invariants and don't-touch list\n\nEvery item below was a real bug in the prior Chisel iteration. Each must be\nenforced by a check that fails loudly when violated. **Do not weaken these\nchecks** to make a hypothesis pass.\n\n## Hard invariants\n\n| # | Invariant                                                                                                  | Enforced by                                  |\n|---|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| 1 | Top module `core` exposes an RVFI port set selected by `cores/<target>/core.yaml`'s `nret` field (default 2). **nret=1**: only `io_rvfi_*_0` ports exist; the single retirement always lands on channel 0. **nret=2**: both `io_rvfi_*_0` and `io_rvfi_*_1` exist; channel 0 carries the older of two simultaneous retirements, channel 1 the younger; single-retire cycles MUST place the retirement on channel 0 with `io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0`; all channel-1 ports MUST be driven (no X/Z, tie unused fields to `'0`). | cocotb smoke test, `riscv-formal` wrapper (`wrapper.sv` for nret=2, `wrapper_si.sv` for nret=1) |\n| 2 | `rvfi_trap = 1` iff the retiring instruction is illegal per RV32IM (decoder default = illegal)             | `riscv-formal ill` check                     |\n| 3 | EBREAK is the *only* SYSTEM (opcode `0x73`) instruction the core treats as valid; ECALL / CSR / MRET trap  | dedicated decoder unit tests                 |\n| 4 | `rvfi_order` strictly monotonic +1 per retirement across both channels combined (no gaps, no duplicates). When both channels retire in the same cycle, channel-0 order = N and channel-1 order = N+1.                                       | `riscv-formal unique` check                  |\n| 5 | CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic instruction stream                                           | `riscv-formal liveness` check                |\n| 5b | M-extension arithmetic produces RV32M-correct bit results                                                | cocotb `test_alu.py` + `make formal-deep`    |\n| 6 | All memory accesses bounded to `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` plus UART/markers `[0x10000000, 0x10000200)`     | sim emits `oob:true`, eval treats as failure |\n| 7 | CoreMark CRCs match canonical 6 KB perf run: `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`       | `validate_coremark_uart` in fpga eval        |\n| 8 | CoreMark timing brackets `start_time` / `stop_time` markers — total elapsed cycles do NOT count            | MMIO writes at `0x10000100` / `0x10000104`   |\n| 9 | \"Correct operation validated.\" literal must appear in CoreMark UART output                                 | `validate_coremark_uart`                     |\n|10 | Decoder defaults `isIllegal = 1`; a bit is only cleared inside a validated opcode/funct match              | decoder unit tests with reserved encodings   |\n\n## Don't-touch list (the orchestrator never modifies these)\n\nThese directories are part of the eval contract and are *never* modified by\nhypothesis-implementation agents. The hypothesis is \"what RTL changes would\nimprove fitness\", not \"what eval relaxations would let this RTL pass\".\n\n- `tools/` — orchestrator, worktree manager, eval gates.\n- `schemas/` — hypothesis and eval-result JSON schemas.\n- `formal/wrapper.sv`, `formal/wrapper_si.sv`, `formal/checks.cfg`,\n  `formal/checks_si.cfg`, `formal/run_all.sh` — they define the\n  correctness contract (dual-channel + single-issue variants).\n- `formal/riscv-formal/` — vendored upstream submodule.\n- `bench/programs/` — selftest, crt0, link.ld, CoreMark sources, portme.\n- `fpga/core_bench.sv`, `fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, `fpga/scripts/*`,\n  `fpga/constraints/*` — they define the FPGA fitness contract\n  (dual-channel + single-issue variants).\n- `test/cosim/main.cpp`, `test/cosim/reference.py`, `test/cosim/run_cosim.py`\n  — Verilator harness + Python ISS = the cosim contract.\n- This file (`CLAUDE.md`), `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `README.md`, `setup.sh`,\n  `Makefile`.\n\n## What hypotheses MAY change\n\nEverything under `cores/<TARGET>/rtl/` and the cocotb unit tests under\n`cores/<TARGET>/test/test_*.py`. The agent's `implementation_notes.md`\nlives at `cores/<TARGET>/implementation_notes.md` (gitignored). The\norchestrator may also update `cores/<TARGET>/core.yaml` (current:\nsection only).\n\nThe only top-level invariant on `rtl/core.sv` is that it exposes a port\nnamed `core` whose IO matches the RVFI wrapper's expectations: the\nimem/dmem port set (`io_imemAddr`, `io_imemData`, `io_imemReady`,\n`io_dmemAddr`, `io_dmemRData`, `io_dmemWData`, `io_dmemWEn`,\n`io_dmemREn`, `io_dmemReady`), `clock`, `reset`, and the RVFI port\nset determined by `core.yaml`'s `nret` field — only `io_rvfi_*_0`\nfields for `nret: 1`; both `io_rvfi_*_0` and `io_rvfi_*_1` for\n`nret: 2` (default).\n\nA hypothesis may:\n- Merge stages into a single file or split a module across many.\n- Rename or delete files inside `rtl/`.\n- Add new modules (branch predictors, caches, reservation stations…).\n- Change pipeline depth (add or remove stages).\n- Restructure the pipeline topology end to end.\n- Rewrite any `rtl/` module from scratch.\n\nIt must not:\n- Change `core`'s top-level IO shape relative to the `nret` value declared in `core.yaml` — specifically: the imem/dmem port set, and the RVFI port set (only `_0` fields for `nret: 1`; both `_0` and `_1` for `nret: 2` with channel-0-older convention and the single-retire-on-channel-0 rule).\n- Change `core.yaml`'s `nret` value mid-hypothesis. nret picks the formal/FPGA wrappers used by eval; flipping it is a contract-level change, not a per-hypothesis decision.\n- Weaken any invariant in the table above.\n- Modify any path in the don't-touch list.\n- Edit any other core's directory (cores/<other>/...). Each loop is\n  scoped to a single TARGET.\n\n## Working notes\n\nThis file is the contract. `docs/bootstrap-prompt.md` is the long-form\nplan that produced it.\n\n### RVFI contract: nret=1 vs nret=2\n\nCores declare their retirement width in `cores/<TARGET>/core.yaml`\nunder the top-level `nret` field. Two values are supported; the\ndefault is 2 for backward compat with cores authored before this\nfield existed.\n\n- `nret: 1` — single-issue. Top module exposes only `io_rvfi_*_0`\n  ports (no `_1`). Orchestrator wires `formal/wrapper_si.sv` +\n  `formal/checks_si.cfg` for formal and `fpga/core_bench_si.sv` for\n  FPGA Fmax. No `_1` tie-offs to write; no vacuous-pass machinery\n  involved. This is the recommended shape for any in-order or\n  single-retire pipeline.\n- `nret: 2` — dual-issue (or single-issue cores that opt into the\n  wider contract). Top module exposes both `io_rvfi_*_0` and\n  `io_rvfi_*_1` with the channel-0-older convention.\n  `formal/wrapper.sv` + `formal/checks.cfg` and `fpga/core_bench.sv`\n  apply. Single-issue cores declared as `nret: 2` must still tie off\n  channel 1 (`io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0` plus all other `_1` fields driven\n  to `'0`) — about 21 `assign io_rvfi_*_1 = '0;` lines.\n\nTriple-issue or wider would be a future contract bump (NRET=K), not a\nper-hypothesis decision.\n\nContract-side files keyed off `nret`:\n- nret=1: `formal/wrapper_si.sv`, `formal/checks_si.cfg`,\n  `fpga/core_bench_si.sv`.\n- nret=2: `formal/wrapper.sv`, `formal/checks.cfg`,\n  `fpga/core_bench.sv`.\n- shared: `formal/run_all.sh`, `fpga/scripts/synth.tcl`,\n  `test/cosim/main.cpp` (per-channel drain that gracefully no-ops\n  on unused channels).\n\nVacuous-pass on channel 1 (`nret: 2` only): `riscv-formal`'s\nper-channel BMC checks assume `rvfi_valid_<ch>=1` to test their\nproperty. When a single-issue hypothesis declared as `nret: 2`\nhardwires `io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0`, that assumption becomes\nunsatisfiable and SBY exits `Status: PREUNSAT` (rc=16) — technically\na vacuous pass over the empty set of ch1-valid traces.\n`formal/run_all.sh` counts `*_ch1` PREUNSAT as pass, so such\nhypotheses pass formal naturally and true dual-issue hypotheses\n(where ch1 is sometimes valid) get the regular `DONE (PASS` outcome\non the same checks. Caveat: a hypothesis that *claims* dual-issue\nbut whose channel 1 silently never retires would also vacuous-pass\nthese checks; the FPGA-fitness IPC and the cover statement (which\nfires on either channel retiring) catch the fully-stuck case.\nCores declared `nret: 1` sidestep this machinery entirely —\nchecks_si.cfg generates no `_ch1` tasks at all.\n\n## Caveat: `make formal` runs ALTOPS-mode by default\n\n`formal/checks.cfg` defines `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`, which substitutes\nthe M-extension `MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU` operations\nwith simpler XOR-of-add/sub formulas (riscv-formal docs §7.6). The\nDUT's `alu.sv` mirrors the same substitution under the same `\\`ifdef`.\n\nThis means the per-iteration formal gate proves **M-ext bypassing,\noperand routing, stall/forwarding behavior, and trap discipline** — but\nit does NOT prove that the actual hardware multiplier and divider\nproduce RV32M-correct bit results. M-ext arithmetic correctness is\ncovered separately by:\n\n  1. `test/test_alu.py` (30+ vectors per M-ext op, exact bit results)\n  2. cosim against `test/cosim/reference.py` (each retired MUL/DIV/REM\n     diff'd field-by-field with the Python ISS)\n  3. `make formal-deep` (formal without ALTOPS — slow but the only path\n     that proves real arithmetic via SMT). Run periodically, not in\n     the orchestrator loop.\n\nA hypothesis that touches the multiplier/divider must keep both the\nALTOPS branch and the real branch in `alu.sv` consistent. The cocotb\nsuite is the fast guard against arithmetic regressions.\n\"\"\"Exhaustive control combinations for load-use bubbles and backend holds.\"\"\"\nimport itertools\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.triggers import Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def bubble_and_hold_truth_table(dut):\n    for load, rd, rs1, rs2, redirect, ir, dr, mem, divide in itertools.product(\n        (0, 1), (0, 7), (0, 7, 8), (0, 7, 8), *((0, 1),) * 5\n    ):\n        for name, value in dict(id_ex_mem_read=load, id_ex_rd=rd,\n                                if_id_rs1=rs1, if_id_rs2=rs2, redirect=redirect,\n                                imem_ready=ir, dmem_ready=dr,\n                                ex_mem_mem_op=mem, div_wait=divide).items():\n            getattr(dut, name).value = value\n        await Timer(1, \"ns\")\n        hazard = load and rd != 0 and rd in (rs1, rs2)\n        memory_hold = mem and not dr\n        expected = dict(stall_if=hazard or not ir or memory_hold or divide,\n                        stall_id=memory_hold or divide, flush_if=redirect or not ir,\n                        flush_id=(hazard or redirect) and not memory_hold and not divide,\n                        stall_ex_mem=memory_hold, hold_mem_wb=memory_hold)\n        for name, value in expected.items():\n            assert int(getattr(dut, name).value) == value, (name, expected)\n\n\ndef test_hazard_unit_runner():\n    run_cocotb(toplevel=\"hazard_unit\", sources=[\"hazard_unit.sv\"],\n               test_module=\"test_hazard_unit\")\n// rtl/ex_stage.sv\n//\n// Execute stage. Resolves the operand muxes (forwarding from EX/MEM and\n// MEM/WB), runs the ALU, resolves branches, computes the redirect\n// target. Owns the EX/MEM pipeline register.\n//\n// Forwarding select encoding (driven by forward_unit):\n//   00 = ID/EX register value (no forward)\n//   01 = EX/MEM aluResult (instruction immediately ahead in MEM)\n//   10 = MEM/WB result (instruction two ahead, post regfile-write mux)\n//\n// Latency:        1 cycle for ordinary work. Divide computes on eight\n//                 edges, then transfers its retained result into EX/MEM.\n// RVFI fields:    feeds pc_wdata (= pc_next), the rd_wdata path for\n//                 ALU and JAL/JALR (PC+4), and the branch resolve.\nmodule ex_stage (\n  input  logic               clock,\n  input  logic               reset,\n  input  logic               stall,         // freeze EX/MEM register (dmem stall)\n  input  id_ex_t   in,\n  input  logic [1:0]         fwd_rs1_sel,\n  input  logic [1:0]         fwd_rs2_sel,\n  input  logic [31:0]        fwd_ex_mem,    // EX/MEM.alu_result (registered)\n  input  logic [31:0]        fwd_mem_wb,    // WB-stage write-data mux output\n  output ex_mem_t  out,\n  output logic               div_wait,      // hold IF/ID, drain older stages\n  output logic               redirect,\n  output logic [31:0]        redirect_target,\n  output logic               resolve_valid,\n  output logic [31:0]        resolve_pc,\n  output logic [31:0]        resolve_target,\n  output logic               resolve_branch,\n  output logic               resolve_jump,\n  output logic               resolve_taken,\n  output logic               resolve_legal\n);\n\n  // ── Operand forwarding muxes ───────────────────────────────────────────\n  logic [31:0] rs1;\n  logic [31:0] rs2;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    case (fwd_rs1_sel)\n      2'd1:    rs1 = fwd_ex_mem;\n      2'd2:    rs1 = fwd_mem_wb;\n      default: rs1 = in.rs1_val;\n    endcase\n    case (fwd_rs2_sel)\n      2'd1:    rs2 = fwd_ex_mem;\n      2'd2:    rs2 = fwd_mem_wb;\n      default: rs2 = in.rs2_val;\n    endcase\n  end\n\n  // ── ALU operand selection ─────────────────────────────────────────────\n  logic [31:0] alu_a;\n  logic [31:0] alu_b;\n  always_comb begin\n    alu_a = in.ctrl.is_auipc ? in.pc  : rs1;\n    alu_b = in.ctrl.alu_src  ? in.imm : rs2;\n  end\n\n  logic [31:0] alu_result;\n  logic is_div;\n  logic div_req_valid, div_req_ready, div_result_valid, div_result_ready;\n  logic [31:0] div_rs1_q, div_rs2_q;\n\n  assign is_div = in.valid && !in.ctrl.is_illegal\n               && (in.ctrl.alu_op == ALU_DIV || in.ctrl.alu_op == ALU_DIVU\n                   || in.ctrl.alu_op == ALU_REM || in.ctrl.alu_op == ALU_REMU);\n  assign div_req_valid = is_div && !stall && !reset;\n  assign div_result_ready = is_div && !stall && !reset;\n  assign div_wait = is_div && !div_result_valid;\n\n  // The request stays offered while ID/EX is held. The engine deasserts\n  // req_ready while busy AND while retaining a result, so consumption\n  // cannot also relaunch the same instruction. ID/EX advances on that edge.\n\n  // ID/EX retains instruction metadata, but its register-file snapshots can\n  // be stale. Preserve both accepted post-forward values before WB drains.\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (reset) begin\n      div_rs1_q <= 32'b0;\n      div_rs2_q <= 32'b0;\n    end else if (div_req_valid && div_req_ready) begin\n      div_rs1_q <= rs1;\n      div_rs2_q <= rs2;\n    end\n  end\n\n  alu u_alu (\n    .clock (clock),\n    .reset (reset),\n    .div_req_valid (div_req_valid),\n    .div_req_ready (div_req_ready),\n    .div_result_valid (div_result_valid),\n    .div_result_ready (div_result_ready),\n    .op  (in.ctrl.alu_op),\n    .a   (alu_a),\n    .b   (alu_b),\n    .out (alu_result)\n  );\n\n  // ── Branch resolve ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n  logic        branch_cond;\n  logic        branch_taken;\n  logic [31:0] branch_target;\n  logic [31:0] jump_target;\n  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n  logic [31:0] jalr_sum;  // bit 0 deliberately dropped per RV JALR spec\n  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n\n  always_comb begin\n    case (in.ctrl.branch_op)\n      BR_BEQ:  branch_cond = (rs1 == rs2);\n      BR_BNE:  branch_cond = (rs1 != rs2);\n      BR_BLT:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) <  $signed(rs2));\n      BR_BGE:  branch_cond = ($signed(rs1) >= $signed(rs2));\n      BR_BLTU: branch_cond = (rs1 <  rs2);\n      BR_BGEU: branch_cond = (rs1 >= rs2);\n      default: branch_cond = 1'b0;\n    endcase\n    branch_taken  = in.ctrl.is_branch && branch_cond;\n    branch_target = in.pc + in.imm;\n    // JALR clears bit 0 (RV spec); JAL uses imm directly.\n    jalr_sum    = rs1 + in.imm;\n    jump_target = in.ctrl.is_jalr ? {jalr_sum[31:1], 1'b0}\n                                  : (in.pc + in.imm);\n  end\n\n  // ── Misaligned branch / jump target trap ──────────────────────────────\n  // riscv-formal's spec demands rvfi_trap=1 when next_pc is misaligned\n  // (without C extension that means [1:0] != 0). We trap the offending\n  // instruction, resolve to PC+4 (recovering any prediction), and clear\n  // reg_write so JAL/JALR don't write the return address on trap.\n  logic misalign_branch;\n  logic misalign_jump;\n  logic misalign_fault;\n  ctrl_t ctrl_with_trap;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    misalign_branch = in.ctrl.is_branch && branch_taken\n                      && (branch_target[1:0] != 2'b00);\n    misalign_jump   = in.ctrl.is_jump && (jump_target[1:0] != 2'b00);\n    misalign_fault  = misalign_branch || misalign_jump;\n\n    ctrl_with_trap = in.ctrl;\n    if (misalign_fault) begin\n      ctrl_with_trap.is_illegal = 1'b1;\n      ctrl_with_trap.reg_write  = 1'b0;\n    end\n  end\n\n  logic [31:0] actual_next_pc;\n\n  always_comb begin\n    actual_next_pc = in.pc + 32'd4;\n    if (!ctrl_with_trap.is_illegal) begin\n      if (in.ctrl.is_jump) actual_next_pc = jump_target;\n      else if (branch_taken) actual_next_pc = branch_target;\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n print(r['id'],r.get('category'),r['title'], 'delta='+str(r.get('delta_pct')),'Fmax='+str(r.get('fmax_mhz')))\n if r.get('category')=='micro_opt' and r.get('round_id') in (3,4,5,6):\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print((h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h))\n  print(r.get('implementation_notes','')[:4000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n r=json.loads(line)\n print(r['id'],r.get('category'),r['title'], 'delta='+str(r.get('delta_pct')),'Fmax='+str(r.get('fmax_mhz')))\n if r.get('category')=='micro_opt' and r.get('round_id') in (3,4,5,6):\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print((h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h))\n  print(r.get('implementation_notes','')[:4000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"baseline-bench-609cf36 micro_opt Baseline retest for cores/bench delta=0.0 Fmax=127.03\nhyp-20260905-001-r1s0 micro_opt Balance register-file reads with shared one-hot address decoding delta=0.54 Fmax=127.71\nhyp-20260905-002-r1s1 structural Single pending-store slot with direct zero-wait completion delta=6.2 Fmax=133.89\nhyp-20260905-003-r1s2 predictor Small tagged target predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis delta=12.95 Fmax=134.68\nhyp-20260905-001-r2s0 micro_opt Register one-hot ALU operation selects before execute delta=-3.92 Fmax=129.4\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1 structural Split execution into a fast ALU and an eight-cycle divider delta=0.26 Fmax=135.03\nhyp-20260905-003-r2s2 predictor Two-way target prediction at unchanged 16-entry capacity delta=-7.32 Fmax=124.94\nhyp-20260905-001-r3s0 micro_opt Localize predictor training to remove indexed feedback muxes delta=-36.48 Fmax=85.77\nReplace only the predictor's shared, dynamically indexed training logic\nwith sixteen constant-index entry update blocks in rtl/if_stage.sv.\nFor entry i, decode selected_i = resolve_valid && (resolve_pc[5:2] == i)\nand independently compute hit_i = valid_q[i] &&\n(tag_q[i] == resolve_pc[31:6]). Read that entry's tag and two counter bits\nthrough constant indices, so tag comparison and counter next-state\ncalculation operate locally in parallel with index decoding. Do not\nreduce hits into a shared resolve_hit or select an old counter through\na resolve-index mux before calculating its update. Each state bit must\nhave one sequential owner, with reset taking precedence.\n\nPreserve the exact existing transition rules. Only the selected entry\nmay change. An illegal, trapping, or non-control resolution clears valid\nonly on a local full-tag hit. A legal jump writes valid, tag and target\nand sets its counter to 11. A legal taken branch writes valid, tag and\ntarget, initializes a miss to 10, and saturating-increments a hit. A\nlegal not-taken branch saturating-decrements only a matching entry,\nretains its target, and does nothing on a miss. Encode increment and\ndecrement as local two-bit Boolean equations or complete transition\ncases, including self-transitions at 11 and 00; avoid a shared arithmetic\nresult and separate saturation qualification of a shared write enable.\nReset clears all valid bits while leaving payloads unreset as today.\n\nKeep the sixteen-entry direct-mapped organization, PC[5:2] index, full\nPC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, two-bit hysteresis, and existing\ncombinational fetch lookup. Training remains visible only after its\nclock edge, with no update bypass or additional register stage. Preserve\nresolve_valid qualification from EX, exact predicted_next_pc capture,\nrecovery priority, stall behavior, and all nret=1 bus and RVFI semantics.\nThis is one implementation-level timing optimization of predictor\ntraining; instruction scheduling and prediction decisions remain equal\ncycle by cycle for the same inputs.\n\nRemoving the resolve-side read-mux, shared compare/update, and writeback\ndistribution chain should raise median Fmax with unchanged bracketed\ncycles. Estimate a five percent fitness gain, approximately 336.29 iter/s\nat 141.78 MHz under the current scored configuration. This is a hypothesis,\nnot a measurement. Replicated tag comparisons, increased resolve-signal\nfanout, or losing LUTRAM packing can increase area and routing delay;\nthe unchanged fetch/recovery path may also become limiting.\n\nValidate public prediction behavior against the existing policy across\nall indices and colliding full tags, then run the unchanged unit, formal,\nstalled cosim and FPGA gates. Compare against 320.28 using the same ELF,\nready schedule and benchmark configuration, full-precision iterations\ndivided by bracketed cycles, and three-seed median Fmax. Report LUT/FF\nusage and the new critical path. Require identical scored cycle counts\nand preserve all CRC, UART validation, memory-bound and timing-marker\nchecks. Prior notes distinguish the scored workload from a supplemental\n6 KB run; retain that distinction and verify the requested canonical\n6 KB CRCs separately if the unchanged gate uses another configuration.\n\n\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 structural Pipeline resolution feedback with registered predictor-row reads delta=6.23 Fmax=143.41\nhyp-20260905-003-r3s2 predictor Row-local predictor metadata training to remove the indexed feedback path delta=9.42 Fmax=147.75\nhyp-20260905-001-r4s0 micro_opt Balance predictor training tag comparisons at LUT reduction boundaries delta=-14.62 Fmax=126.15\nRestructure only the resolve-side full-tag equality in each existing\nmetadata row of rtl/if_stage.sv. Replace the unconstrained 26-bit\ncomparison with an explicit balanced tree suited to four-input LUTs.\nFirst compute thirteen independent two-bit equalities: pair_eq[g]\ncompares tag_q[2*g +: 2] with resolve_pc[6 + 2*g +: 2], for g=0..12.\nEach equality has exactly four variable inputs. Next form four\nparallel reductions over pair_eq[3:0], [6:4], [9:7], and [12:10].\nAND those four results to obtain full_tag_equal, then qualify it with\nthe row's valid_q to obtain the existing row_hit. All 26 tag bits\nparticipate; no shortened tag, hash, or approximate match is allowed.\n\nPreserve these local pair and group boundaries with source-level\nYosys keep attributes on their combinational wires, so the mapper\ncannot simply absorb the whole expression back into the current\nserial update cone. Use portable combinational SystemVerilog in all\nsimulation, formal, and synthesis builds. The intended equality\nnetwork has three LUT4 levels before validity/update qualification.\nConfirm the boundaries survive the unchanged synthesis flow by\ninspecting the mapped netlist; an equivalent source expression alone\ndoes not demonstrate the intended timing optimization. Keep reset,\nresolve_valid, row selection, and event qualification outside the\nequality tree. No register, enable, or maintenance stage is added.\n\nRetain the sixteen scalar valid/tag/counter rows, their current\nfield-specific update rules, counter encoding and saturation, and\nthe separate indexed 16x30 target array. Fetch still uses the current\nindexed full-tag lookup. A legal jump allocates strongly taken; a\ntaken branch allocates weakly taken or increments a hit; a not-taken\nbranch decrements only a hit and retains the target; a non-control\nor illegal/trapping resolution invalidates only a matching row.\nInvalid payloads remain masked, reset clears valid bits with priority,\nand unselected rows retain state. Training still becomes visible\nafter the same edge, with no update bypass. Predictions, redirects,\nand accepted instructions must agree cycle by cycle with the current\nRTL for identical input schedules, including same-row collisions.\n\nThis is one logic-mapping micro-optimization. It leaves pipeline\nscheduling, predictor policy and capacity, instruction latency,\narithmetic and ALTOPS, decoder legality, memory handshakes, RVFI,\nnret=1, and every top-level port unchanged. The estimated fitness\ngain is five percent through Fmax alone, with unchanged bracketed\ncycles. Confidence is medium: preserving reduction boundaries can\nincrease LUT use or routing, resolve-PC fanout remains, and another\ncontrol path may become limiting. This estimate is not a measurement.\n\nRun the existing public-output predictor reference tests and pipeline\ntests, then the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and\nthree-seed FPGA gates. Audit equality depth, metadata flop count,\ntarget RAM inference, LUT/FF cost, and the new critical paths. Compare\noriginal and candidate using the same toolchain, ELF, and ready\nschedules, requiring identical marker-bracketed cycles. Calculate\nfitness using full-precision iterations/cycles and median Fmax.\nPrior notes distinguish the saved 350.46 scored workload from a\nsupplemental 6 KB run; keep comparisons within the same configuration\nand also verify the requested 6 KB canonical CRCs, validation UART\nliteral, timing markers, and memory bounds. Change no evaluation file\nor correctness assertion.\n\nImplemented the balanced resolve-tag comparison in `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` and extended `cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py`. Correctness checks pass, and the requested mapping boundaries survive synthesis. The measured hypothesis is a regression: median Fmax falls from 147.75 to 126.15 MHz, with identical bracketed cycles and a 14.6192893401% fitness loss. The implementation is retained for the orchestrator to evaluate.\n\nEach of the sixteen existing metadata rows now computes thirteen exact two-bit equalities, reduces pairs [3:0], [6:4], [9:7], and [12:10] independently, and ANDs the four groups into `full_tag_equal`. Only then does `valid_q` qualify `row_hit`. Source-level Yosys `keep` attributes preserve the pair and group wires. One small extension to the advisory change is that the final equality wire also has `keep`, ensuring that validity and update qualification stay beyond the complete three-level equality network.\n\nAll sequential RTL is unchanged. The sixteen scalar valid/tag/counter rows, counter encoding and saturation, indexed 16x30 target array, indexed full-tag fetch lookup, reset priority, row selection, resolve/event qualification, and training visibility are preserved. No register, maintenance stage, bypass, or latency was added. Legal jumps allocate strongly taken; taken branches allocate weakly taken or increment a hit; not-taken branches decrement only a hit and retain the target; invalidating resolutions clear only a matching valid row. Unselected rows retain state, and invalid payloads remain masked. All other RTL files, the nret=1 configuration, pipeline scheduling, arithmetic/ALTOPS, top-level ports, and evaluation files are unchanged.\n\nThe new directed test uses the existing independent public-output policy model and retains every existing assertion. It covers every adjacent pair boundary, the three group boundaries, simultaneous mismatches in all four groups, and all-tag-bit mismatches, across all sixteen rows. Consecutive alias allocation edges run while fetch holds the same row. Longer sequences exercise matching and mismatching taken, not-taken, and invalidating resolutions, counter threshold transitions, target retention, accepted-fetch collisions, invalid-row masking, and preservation of unrelated rows. The complete exported IF bundle and fetch address are checked before and after each edge. Existing coverage for every individual tag and target bit, high addresses, wraparound, saturation, reset, disabled resolution, and mixed schedules remains intact. The final four predictor tests pass against both original and candidate RTL.\n\nLocal validation:\n\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS without warnings.\n- Complete cocotb suite: PASS, seven pytest runners / 134 cocotb cases, including four predictor tests, all 33 pipeline tests, both real and ALTOPS ALU builds, decoder, immediate generator, and register-file tests. Predictor/pipeline tests ran first; the other runners followed. The final addition of consecutive alias updates was then rechecked on both original and candidate IF stages.\n- Unchanged `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6 bash formal/run_all.sh`: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, first gate attempt, zero RTL fix retries and no counterexamples. The script selected `wrapper_si.sv` and `checks_si.cfg`. This is the standard ALTOPS gate; arithmetic RTL was untouched.\n- Additional Yosys sequential equivalence of the original and candidate IF stages: PASS, all 1,673 equivalence cells proven, zero unproven, using `equiv_simple` followed by `equiv_induct -undef -seq 2` and `equiv_status -assert`. This checks the unchanged state transitions and outputs under identical input schedules, including collisions; a one-step equivalence attempt alone was insufficient to prove the retained sequential state.\n- Unchanged `python3 -m tools.eval.cosim . bench`: PASS, selftest RVFI comparison and stalled CoreMark CRC validation.\n-\nhyp-20260905-002-r4s1 structural Pipeline row-addressed resolution events before local predictor updates delta=6.02 Fmax=156.62\nhyp-20260905-003-r4s2 predictor Bank-local registered training commands for the target predictor delta=-1.18 Fmax=145.99\nhyp-20260905-001-r5s0 micro_opt Remove wide ID/EX payload registers from bubble-control fanout delta=4.61 Fmax=163.83\nSplit ID/EX sequential updates into a narrow control/valid group and\na payload group. On a flush, clear the entire ctrl field and valid\nexactly as the existing zero bundle does. Do not clear, mask, or hold\npayload registers merely because of flush. Keep their deterministic\nreset-to-zero behavior, but otherwise capture pc, predicted_next_pc,\nboth operand values, immediate, register addresses, rd, and instruction\nwhenever there is no actual backend hold. Each field must have exactly\none sequential driver; do not mix whole-struct and partial assignments\nacross separate always_ff blocks.\n\nMake stall_id mean only dmem_stall || div_wait. Remove load_use_hazard\nfrom this ID/EX hold signal while preserving stall_if, flush_if,\nflush_id, stall_ex_mem, and hold_mem_wb behavior. This is a redundant\nhold removal: whenever a load-use hazard occurs without a backend\nhold, the existing flush_id already forces an ID/EX bubble. On that\nedge the payload may capture the still-unaccepted younger instruction,\nbut ctrl=0 and valid=0 make it inert, and the held fetch is accepted\nnormally on the following eligible edge. When a memory or divide wait\nis active, both control and payload must remain held exactly as before.\nPreserve reset priority and flush priority for the control/valid group.\n\nClearing valid alone is insufficient: forwarding and memory control\nlogic consult ctrl fields even when valid is zero. Continue clearing\nall control bits on every flush, particularly reg_write, mem_read,\nmem_write, is_branch, and is_jump. Retained or newly captured bubble\npayload must never launch a divide, redirect, train the predictor,\nforward as a writer, access memory, or retire. Leave EX/MEM full-bundle\ndivide bubbles and MEM/WB forwarding-data retention unchanged. All\nlive instructions retain their exact operands, instruction metadata,\naccepted prediction, trap behavior, and RVFI fields through holds.\n\nThis single micro-optimization removes recovery and load-use logic\nfrom the wide payload registers' direct reset and enable controls.\nExisting upstream instruction-to-NOP selection may still affect data\ninputs; it need not be redesigned. Add no stage, state bit, execution\nshortcut, new prediction behavior, or architectural latency. Preserve\nnret=1, all external ports, all decoder legality rules, and every hard\ninvariant and evaluation check.\n\nExpect an unmeasured three-percent fitness improvement from higher\nmedian Fmax, with identical bracketed cycles for the same binary and\nbus-ready schedule. Confidence is medium: the other winning seeds are\nlimited by local predictor tag-to-counter-enable paths at 6.36 and\n6.42 ns, so they may cap the benefit; synthesis may also reconstruct\nunwanted control dependencies or alter placement adversely. Inspect\nthe mapped payload reset/enable cones and routed critical paths to\nestablish whether the intended control isolation survives synthesis.\n\nRun the existing unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA gates.\nCompare accepted bus transactions and valid RVFI retirements, including\ntheir cycle positions, against the current winner under identical\ninputs; inactive payload bit patterns need not match. Use full-precision\niterations/bracketed-cycles times median Fmax and report LUT/FF costs.\nThe saved 371.55 result is documented as a 2 KB scored run; the separate\ncanonical 6 KB winner run takes 30,822,790 bracketed cycles. Keep those\nconfigurations distinct and unchanged, and verify the required 6 KB\nCRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, memory bounds, markers, and validation UART literal.\n\n\nhyp-20260905-002-r5s1 structural Separate ID/EX validity from the wide recovery payload delta=-29.62 Fmax=110.22\nhyp-20260905-003-r5s2 predictor Eight-entry return stack with direct return recognition delta=-3.98 Fmax=150.13\nhyp-20260905-001-r6s0 micro_opt Replace local saturating-counter arithmetic with thermometer state delta=-8.81 Fmax=149.39\nRe-encode only each row's two-bit saturating direction counter as a\nthree-bit thermometer register q[2:0]. Logical counts 0, 1, 2, and 3\nmap respectively to 000, 001, 011, and 111. Fetch uses q[1] directly\nfor taken prediction, preserving the current threshold without a\nbinary conversion or extra lookup logic. A taken hit increments by\nshifting in one: {q[1:0], 1'b1}. A not-taken hit decrements by shifting\nin zero: {1'b0, q[2:1]}. These operations saturate intrinsically.\nA jump sets 111; a taken miss allocates 011. A not-taken miss,\ninvalidation, and absence of a pending event retain the counter.\n\nExpress counter next state as parallel Boolean data terms, followed\nby an unconditional clocked assignment, rather than arithmetic or\nnested conditional writes. Specifically, let E be pending_valid_q\nAND !reset and H be the existing valid-qualified full-tag row_hit.\nDefine J = E AND command_is_JUMP, A = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND !H,\nU = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND H, D = E AND command_is_NOT_TAKEN AND H,\nand K = !(J OR A OR U OR D). Use bitwise equations:\n  next_q[2] = J | (U & q[1]) | (K & q[2]);\n  next_q[1] = J | A | (U & q[0]) | (D & q[2]) | (K & q[1]);\n  next_q[0] = J | A | U | (D & q[1]) | (K & q[0]).\nDecode the pending command locally and retain a single sequential\nowner for each counter. Allocation terms must produce known constants\neven when the old, invalid counter payload is uninitialized. Counter\npayloads need no reset: reset suppresses updates and the existing\nvalid reset masks them until a complete legal allocation.\n\nThe intended mapped circuit has no saturation detection or binary\narithmetic and no tag-dependent counter CE, SET, or RESET input.\nHit qualification and self-retention are represented in the data\nequations. Inspect the mapped netlist to verify this property: an\nequivalent rewrite that synthesis folds back into hit-qualified\ncounter enables does not establish the intended optimization. Do not\nintroduce device primitives, synthesis-only behavior, or blanket keep\nattributes to force a result. The source-level cost is just sixteen\nadditional counter bits; actual mapped LUT/FF cost must be measured.\n\nPreserve the sixteen rows, full 26-bit tags, exact tag comparisons,\nvalid/tag update logic, separate indexed target store, all private\npending events, and the existing capture/commit edges. Commit against\ncurrent row state, including consecutive events and different tags\naliasing one row. Keep matching-only invalidation and not-taken updates,\ntarget retention, reset cancellation, and once-only pending-event drain\nthrough stalls and redirects. Fetch still observes committed state\nwith the same pre-edge semantics and no update bypass. This is a\ncounter-encoding and logic-mapping micro-optimization, with identical\nprediction policy, capacity, training latency, and instruction timing.\n\nEstimate an unmeasured four-percent fitness improvement through Fmax\nalone. For identical ELF and ready schedules, require identical public\nIF predictions, accepted memory transactions, valid RVFI retirements\nwith their cycle positions, and marker-bracketed cycles. Confidence is\nmedium: full-tag equality remains, valid-bit update or EX paths may\nbecome limiting, and added data gating may worsen routing or be\nremapped into the original enable structure.\n\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed\nFPGA gates. Compare against the current winner with the same tools,\nELF, and bus-ready schedules; report critical paths and LUT4/FF cost.\nCompute fitness from full-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles times\nmedian Fmax. Historical notes identify 388.66's scored workload as\n2 KB; keep that comparison separate from the requested canonical 6 KB\nrun, verifying d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, memory bounds, both timing markers,\nand the validation UART literal without altering evaluation files.\nPreserve nret=1, external IO, RVFI order and traps, decoder legality,\nmemory handshakes, all M-extension/ALTOPS logic, and every invariant.\n\n\nhyp-20260905-002-r6s1 structural Separate predictor tag validation from ordered metadata commit delta=-40.65 Fmax=97.2\nhyp-20260905-003-r6s2 predictor Eight-bit partial tags for the target predictor delta=-12.49 Fmax=143.37\nhyp-20260905-001-r7s0 micro_opt Predecode the multiply-high result class before execute delta=-9.83 Fmax=147.73\nhyp-20260905-002-r7s1 structural Move PC-relative address generation across the ID/EX boundary delta=-11.13 Fmax=145.6\nhyp-20260905-003-r7s2 predictor Eight-entry target predictor with shorter lookup and less training fanout delta=-8.6 Fmax=150.94\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
