{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/v1/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if x.get('id')=='hyp-20260428-017-r8s1':\n        print(json.dumps(x, indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20260428-017-r8s1\",\n  \"title\": \"One-Deep Store Retirement Slot With Direct Fast Path\",\n  \"category\": \"structural\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The current best core is dominated by the post-DIV/REM timing shape, with\\nmedian Fmax now high enough that broad datapath changes have mostly hurt\\nplacement. The remaining structural opportunity with measured evidence is\\nstore backpressure: the previous one-entry store buffer reduced CoreMark\\ncycles from 4,259,618 to 4,226,169, but its dmem arbitration and MEM-stage\\nmuxing cost slightly more Fmax than the cycle gain recovered.\\n\\nThe promising angle is therefore not a larger buffer, but a smaller and more\\ntiming-local retiring store slot. Keep the zero-wait store path identical to\\nthe current best design, and add state only for the uncommon case where a\\nnormal RAM store reaches MEM while io_dmemReady is low. That targets the same\\nrandom-dStall cycles as the prior structural attempt while avoiding a\\npermanent dmem-port mux on every load/store cycle.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-deep pending-store retirement slot that is used only on a legal,\\naligned normal-RAM store when io_dmemReady is low. When io_dmemReady is high\\nand the slot is empty, stores drive the existing dmem outputs directly and\\nretire exactly as they do today. When io_dmemReady is low, the MEM stage\\ncaptures the store address, replicated write data, byte mask, and RVFI memory\\nmetadata into the slot, retires that store once through MEM/WB, and lets\\nyounger non-memory instructions continue while the slot drains.\\n\\nThe slot has priority only while valid. Younger loads, younger stores, and all\\nMMIO/marker/UART stores stall until the pending store drains, preserving\\nin-order memory visibility and exact timing-marker behavior. Misaligned or\\ntrapping stores never enter the slot. This should keep most of the cycle-count\\nbenefit seen in the previous store-buffer experiment, but recover Fmax by\\nleaving the direct ready-store/load path structurally close to the current\\nbest RTL and confining the extra muxing to the stall-only drain path.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 2,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Add a one-entry pending-store slot with valid, enqueue, drain-valid, and\\ndrain-ready controls. The entry holds byte address, replicated write data,\\nand byte mask for legal aligned normal-RAM stores only. It is allocated\\nonly when the live store cannot handshake immediately.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Split store completion into a direct fast path and a low-ready enqueue\\npath. Keep the current dmem outputs and MEM/WB behavior unchanged when\\nio_dmemReady is high and no pending store exists. On a bufferable store\\nwith io_dmemReady low, enqueue the precomputed store fields, retire the\\nstore once with correct RVFI mem_addr, mem_wmask, and mem_wdata, and\\nreport explicit MEM backpressure for loads, nonbufferable stores, stores\\nbehind a valid slot, and full-slot cases.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Replace the generic dmem-stall predicate with MEM-stage wait inputs that\\ndistinguish live loads/nonbufferable stores from a draining pending store.\\nStall IF/ID/EX only when the instruction in MEM truly cannot retire or\\nwhen a younger memory operation must wait behind the slot; allow younger\\nnon-memory instructions to proceed while the slot drains.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Instantiate the pending-store slot and arbitrate the top-level dmem port\\nso the live MEM-stage request uses the direct path whenever the slot is\\nempty, while the slot drives the port only when valid. Preserve the top\\nmodule IO, RVFI NRET=2 fields, strict channel-0 retirement ordering, and\\ntied-off channel 1.\\n\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"expected_impact\": {\n    \"fitness_delta_pct\": 2,\n    \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Add a one-entry pending-store slot with valid, enqueue, drain-valid, and\\ndrain-ready controls. The entry holds byte address, replicated write data,\\nand byte mask for legal aligned normal-RAM stores only. It is allocated\\nonly when the live store cannot handshake immediately.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Split store completion into a direct fast path and a low-ready enqueue\\npath. Keep the current dmem outputs and MEM/WB behavior unchanged when\\nio_dmemReady is high and no pending store exists. On a bufferable store\\nwith io_dmemReady low, enqueue the precomputed store fields, retire the\\nstore once with correct RVFI mem_addr, mem_wmask, and mem_wdata, and\\nreport explicit MEM backpressure for loads, nonbufferable stores, stores\\nbehind a valid slot, and full-slot cases.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Replace the generic dmem-stall predicate with MEM-stage wait inputs that\\ndistinguish live loads/nonbufferable stores from a draining pending store.\\nStall IF/ID/EX only when the instruction in MEM truly cannot retire or\\nwhen a younger memory operation must wait behind the slot; allow younger\\nnon-memory instructions to proceed while the slot drains.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Instantiate the pending-store slot and arbitrate the top-level dmem port\\nso the live MEM-stage request uses the direct path whenever the slot is\\nempty, while the slot drives the port only when valid. Preserve the top\\nmodule IO, RVFI NRET=2 fields, strict channel-0 retirement ordering, and\\ntied-off channel 1.\\n\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"outcome\": \"improvement\",\n  \"fitness\": 397.55,\n  \"delta_pct\": 5.89,\n  \"vs_baseline\": 32.06,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 168.01,\n  \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06,\n  \"cycles\": 4226169,\n  \"iterations\": 10,\n  \"lut4\": 5854,\n  \"ff\": 2226,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    168.01,\n    173.97,\n    133.92\n  ],\n  \"formal_passed\": true,\n  \"cosim_passed\": true,\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Added `rtl/store_slot.sv`, a one-entry pending-store slot that holds byte address, replicated write data, and byte mask until `io_dmemReady` accepts the write.\\n- Split the MEM-stage dmem path into a live direct request and an enqueue path. Legal, aligned normal-RAM stores enqueue only when `io_dmemReady` is low and the slot is empty; ready stores keep the existing direct path.\\n- Added precise `mem_wait` classification in `rtl/mem_stage.sv` for live loads, nonbufferable stores, and younger memory operations blocked behind a valid slot.\\n- Updated `rtl/hazard_unit.sv` to consume `mem_wait` instead of rebuilding `!dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op`, allowing younger non-memory instructions to continue while the slot drains.\\n- Updated `rtl/core.sv` to instantiate the slot and arbitrate the top-level dmem port with slot priority while valid.\\n- Updated the cocotb pipeline source list to include `store_slot.sv`.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- The slot stores the physical drain fields only. RVFI memory metadata for an enqueued store is still generated and retired through the normal MEM/WB register on the enqueue cycle, so there is no second RVFI event when the slot drains.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not run in-place in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a read-only path outside the writable worktree, and the script failed while removing stale staged files. I copied the same formal tree, wrapper, checks config, and current `rtl/*.sv` into `/tmp/codex-formal-r8s1.FkHGdP` and ran the unchanged `formal/run_all.sh` there.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The riscv-formal wrapper ties `io_dmemReady` high, so formal proves the unchanged direct path but does not exercise pending-slot drain behavior. The slot path is covered by lint and structural review here; downstream random-dStall cosim remains the main gate for that behavior.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` from writable `/tmp` mirror: pass, `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n- `pytest -q test/test_pipeline.py`: pass, `1 passed`.\\n\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T05:00:14.609332\",\n  \"slot\": 1,\n  \"round_id\": 8\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
