delivered a\\nlarge CoreMark gain and a strong Fmax result: it hides random imem stalls by\\nreplaying hot instructions, while the current IF cycle still chooses only\\nbetween live imem data and an already registered candidate.\\n\\nThe remaining replay misses are likely conflict misses. The current two-bank\\ndesign stores two tags per raw `pc[8:2]` set, so any hot instructions spaced\\nby 512-byte multiples still contend for the same two physical slots. Earlier\\nalternatives show the tradeoff boundary: a single-bank XOR index changed the\\nconflict pattern but gave up the benefit of two-way capacity, and a victim\\nbuffer added associative compares that cost more Fmax than the extra hits\\nrecovered. A skewed second bank targets the same conflict class with no extra\\ntable entries, no victim search, and no current-cycle table read.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the current two-bank registered lookahead replay predictor, but use\\ndifferent indexes for the two banks. Bank 0 remains indexed by the raw\\n`pc[8:2]`. Bank 1 uses a small XOR-folded index such as\\n`pc[8:2] ^ pc[15:9]`. Both banks still store compressed high-PC tags\\n`pc[31:9]`, valid bits, and 32-bit instruction words behind resetless\\npayload arrays.\\n\\nOn live `io_imemReady=1` fetches, preserve live-imem-first behavior. If bank\\n0 at the raw index or bank 1 at the skewed index already matches the fetched\\nPC tag, refresh that matching bank. If neither bank matches, fill the\\npseudo-LRU/non-MRU choice for that PC's raw set, using the raw-index MRU bit\\nas a replacement hint while writing bank 1 at its skewed index. This retains\\nthe existing low-cost replacement state but lets the second candidate slot\\ncome from a different conflict class.\\n\\nDuring registered lookahead generation, read bank 0 at the raw index and\\nbank 1 at the skewed index for the selected next fetch PC, compare their\\ncompressed tags, and register a replay candidate on either hit. The current\\nIF cycle remains unchanged: `fetch_ready` is driven only by live imem\\nreadiness or the previously registered candidate guarded by the full\\ncandidate PC match. Same-cycle fill bypass must use the same bank-specific\\nindexes so a just-fetched word can seed the next candidate when appropriate.\\n\\nCorrectness is still protected by the compressed tag plus the existing full\\ncandidate-PC guard; a skew collision can only evict a useful replay word and\\ncause a miss. Static backward-branch and direct-JAL prediction, redirect\\nrecovery, pending-store behavior, divider interlock, hot/cold ALU encoding,\\ntop-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 ports, strict channel-0 order, and tied-off\\nchannel 1 remain unchanged. The expected benefit is a small iStall cycle\\nreduction with limited Fmax risk because the extra XOR is only in the\\nregistered lookahead/fill path, not the current-cycle replay-hit path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the second replay bank to use a skewed index such as\\n`pc[8:2] ^ pc[15:9]`, while keeping bank 0 on the raw `pc[8:2]` index.\\nApply the bank-specific indexes consistently to live fills, matching,\\nregistered lookahead reads, and same-cycle fill bypass. Preserve the\\ntwo-bank table size, compressed high-PC tags, resetless payload arrays,\\nMRU replacement hint, registered candidate PC guard, live-imem priority,\\nreplay-hit `fetch_ready` semantics, redirect priority, and the existing\\nstatic backward-branch plus direct-JAL prediction behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document that the registered\\nlookahead replay predictor uses one raw-index bank and one skew-index\\nbank, both protected by compressed high-PC tags and the registered\\ncurrent-PC guard. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state,\\ndecoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2\\ncontract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Change the second replay bank to use a skewed index such as\\n`pc[8:2] ^ pc[15:9]`, while keeping bank 0 on the raw `pc[8:2]` index.\\nApply the bank-specific indexes consistently to live fills, matching,\\nregistered lookahead reads, and same-cycle fill bypass. Preserve the\\ntwo-bank table size, compressed high-PC tags, resetless payload arrays,\\nMRU replacement hint, registered candidate PC guard, live-imem priority,\\nreplay-hit `fetch_ready` semantics, redirect priority, and the existing\\nstatic backward-branch plus direct-JAL prediction behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document that the registered\\nlookahead replay predictor uses one raw-index bank and one skew-index\\nbank, both protected by compressed high-PC tags and the registered\\ncurrent-PC guard. Do not change bundle widths, architectural state,\\ndecoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the NRET=2\\ncontract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 457.76, \"delta_pct\": -20.77, \"vs_baseline\": 52.06, \"fmax_mhz\": 157.6, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3442835, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5923, \"ff\": 2225, \"seeds\": [157.6, 164.15, 150.13], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Updated `rtl/if_stage.sv` so replay bank 0 uses the raw `pc[8:2]` index and replay bank 1 uses the skewed `pc[8:2] ^ pc[15:9]` index.\\n- Applied the bank-specific indexes consistently for live-fill matching, fill writes, registered lookahead hits, replay instruction selection, and same-cycle fill bypass.\\n- Kept the existing table sizes, compressed `pc[31:9]` tags, resetless payload arrays, raw-index MRU replacement hint, live-imem priority, registered candidate PC guard, redirect behavior, and static branch/JAL prediction.\\n- Updated the IF/ID replay predictor comments in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document the raw/skew bank structure and current-PC guard.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No RTL deviations from the hypothesis. The second bank uses the suggested XOR fold `pc[8:2] ^ pc[15:9]`.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No known correctness concerns. Bank 1 collisions can evict useful replay entries, but compressed tag plus the registered full-PC candidate guard still protect correctness.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- Direct `bash formal/run_all.sh` from the worktree was blocked by the session sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink outside the writable roots and the script stages files under that tree.\\n- Re-ran the same formal script from a temporary writable mirror under `/tmp` with the updated `rtl/`, `formal/run_all.sh`, `checks.cfg`, `wrapper.sv`, and `riscv-formal` contents: `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T08:43:28.177000\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 21}\n65:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-058-r22s0\", \"title\": \"Resetless Replay MRU State\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for correctness after reset: both replay-bank valid bits are cleared,\\nso the first live fill for a set may choose either bank. After that fill, the\\nMRU bit is immediately written to the bank that was filled and normal\\nreplacement behavior resumes.\\n\\nRemoving reset from this 128-bit replacement-state array is a narrower\\nversion of the reset-elision idea that has already worked for replay\\npayloads. It targets reset fanout and local IF replay placement pressure\\nwithout changing the current-cycle fetch-ready path, replay hit rate after\\nwarmup, branch/JAL prediction policy, ALU datapath, memory behavior, or RVFI\\nretirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design is dominated by the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay predictor. It is the only predictor-family structure that has both\\nreduced random-iStall cycles and improved Fmax, and the successful\\ncompressed-tag follow-up showed that narrow timing cleanups inside this IF\\nreplay block can still move median fitness without changing CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nThe replay table already leaves tag and instruction payload arrays resetless\\nbehind valid bits, but the per-index MRU replacement bits are still\\nsynchronously reset. Those MRU bits are not architectural state and are not\\nneeded for correctness after reset: both replay-bank valid bits are cleared,\\nso the first live fill for a set may choose either bank. After that fill, the\\nMRU bit is immediately written to the bank that was filled and normal\\nreplacement behavior resumes.\\n\\nRemoving reset from this 128-bit replacement-state array is a narrower\\nversion of the reset-elision idea that has already worked for replay\\npayloads. It targets reset fanout and local IF replay placement pressure\\nwithout changing the current-cycle fetch-ready path, replay hit rate after\\nwarmup, branch/JAL prediction policy, ALU datapath, memory behavior, or RVFI\\nretirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Make the two-bank instruction replay predictor's MRU replacement bits\\nresetless. On reset, continue clearing both replay-bank valid arrays and the\\nregistered replay-candidate valid bit, but do not assign replay_mru entries.\\nTag and instruction payload arrays remain resetless as today.\\n\\nThe fill-bank selection remains the same logical policy: refresh bank 0 or\\nbank 1 when the indexed high tag already matches, otherwise fill the\\nnon-MRU bank and mark the filled bank MRU. With both valid bits cleared by\\nreset, an arbitrary resetless MRU value can only choose which empty bank gets\\nthe first fill for a set. It cannot make a replay hit valid for the wrong PC,\\nbecause replay selection still requires a bank valid bit, compressed tag\\nmatch, registered candidate valid bit, and full candidate-PC guard.\\n\\nLive io_imemData still has priority whenever io_imemReady is high, replay\\nmisses still hold the PC and inject the existing IF bubble, and replay hits\\nstill feed the selected instruction through the existing static\\nbackward-branch/direct-JAL prediction path. The top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2\\nport set, channel-0-only retirement convention, strict rvfi_order sequence,\\npending-store slot, divider interlock, hot/cold ALU encoding, decoder\\nlegality rules, and channel-1 tie-off remain unchanged.\\n\\nCoreMark cycle count should be unchanged. The expected benefit is a small\\nmedian Fmax improvement from removing reset muxing and reset routing on the\\nreplay MRU bits in the timing-sensitive IF replay block.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove replay_mru from the reset loop so only replay_valid_b0,\\nreplay_valid_b1, and replay_cand_valid_q are reset. Keep replay_mru\\nupdated on every live imem fill exactly as today, and preserve the\\ntwo-bank table structure, compressed high-PC tags, resetless tag/data\\npayload arrays, registered candidate PC guard, live-imem priority,\\nsame-index fill bypass, redirect priority, fetch_ready semantics, and\\nstatic backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document that replay bank tags,\\ninstruction payloads, and MRU replacement bits are resetless behind\\nreset-cleared valid bits. Do not change bundle widths, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the\\nNRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove replay_mru from the reset loop so only replay_valid_b0,\\nreplay_valid_b1, and replay_cand_valid_q are reset. Keep replay_mru\\nupdated on every live imem fill exactly as today, and preserve the\\ntwo-bank table structure, compressed high-PC tags, resetless tag/data\\npayload arrays, registered candidate PC guard, live-imem priority,\\nsame-index fill bypass, redirect priority, fetch_ready semantics, and\\nstatic backward-branch/direct-JAL prediction behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document that replay bank tags,\\ninstruction payloads, and MRU replacement bits are resetless behind\\nreset-cleared valid bits. Do not change bundle widths, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the\\nNRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 515.21, \"delta_pct\": -10.83, \"vs_baseline\": 71.14, \"fmax_mhz\": 177.18, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3438981, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5964, \"ff\": 2223, \"seeds\": [189.54, 177.18, 176.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n- Removed the synchronous reset assignment for `replay_mru` in `rtl/if_stage.sv`.\\n- Kept reset clearing for `replay_valid_b0`, `replay_valid_b1`, and `replay_cand_valid_q`.\\n- Left replay tag and instruction payload arrays resetless.\\n- Preserved the existing fill policy: matching tags refresh their current bank, misses fill the non-MRU bank, and the filled bank becomes MRU.\\n- Updated replay comments in `rtl/if_stage.sv` and `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document that MRU replacement bits are resetless behind reset-cleared valid state.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No functional deviation from the hypothesis.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not complete directly in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a shared checkout outside the writable worktree, and the script stages files under that tree. I ran the same staging, genchecks, make, and pass/fail tally flow from a writable `/tmp` copy of `riscv-formal`.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No RTL concerns. After reset, both replay bank valid arrays are cleared, so the resetless MRU bit only chooses which empty bank receives the first post-reset fill for each set.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- Local formal equivalent to `bash formal/run_all.sh` from `/tmp` staging: pass, 105 passed / 0 failed.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T08:57:52.187641\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 22}\n66:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-059-r22s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Assisted ALU Pair Dual Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is now a fast single-issue design: cold DIV/REM is\\noff the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split improved Fmax, the one-entry\\nstore slot hides some dmem store stalls, and the two-bank registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall bubbles. The largest\\nremaining structural ceiling is therefore the single-retire backend. The\\ntop-level RVFI contract already exposes NRET=2, but channel 1 is unused.\\n\\nA normal dual-issue frontend would be unattractive because the external imem\\nport supplies only one 32-bit word per cycle. The banked replay predictor\\nchanges that tradeoff: after warmup, hot straight-line CoreMark code often\\nhas both the current instruction and the next sequential instruction in the\\nreplay table. That creates a narrow path to use RVFI channel 1 without\\nwidening the external memory interface or adding a full instruction cache.\\n\\nThe most promising structural angle is to issue a second instruction only\\nfor low-risk adjacent simple-integer pairs. This targets CoreMark's dense\\naddress, loop, CRC, and scalar arithmetic sequences while avoiding the\\nplacement failures seen from larger store buffers and the Fmax losses seen\\nfrom load replay tables, return predictors, and wider replay structures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is now a fast single-issue design: cold DIV/REM is\\noff the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split improved Fmax, the one-entry\\nstore slot hides some dmem store stalls, and the two-bank registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall bubbles. The largest\\nremaining structural ceiling is therefore the single-retire backend. The\\ntop-level RVFI contract already exposes NRET=2, but channel 1 is unused.\\n\\nA normal dual-issue frontend would be unattractive because the external imem\\nport supplies only one 32-bit word per cycle. The banked replay predictor\\nchanges that tradeoff: after warmup, hot straight-line CoreMark code often\\nhas both the current instruction and the next sequential instruction in the\\nreplay table. That creates a narrow path to use RVFI channel 1 without\\nwidening the external memory interface or adding a full instruction cache.\\n\\nThe most promising structural angle is to issue a second instruction only\\nfor low-risk adjacent simple-integer pairs. This targets CoreMark's dense\\naddress, loop, CRC, and scalar arithmetic sequences while avoiding the\\nplacement failures seen from larger store buffers and the Fmax losses seen\\nfrom load replay tables, return predictors, and wider replay structures.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a replay-assisted two-wide issue mode for adjacent straight-line RV32I\\nsimple-integer instruction pairs. Lane 0 remains the existing older\\ninstruction path. Lane 1 is enabled only when the instruction at pc+4 is\\nalready available from the registered replay frontend and both instructions\\nare eligible simple operations: OP, OP-IMM, LUI, or AUIPC with no memory\\naccess, no branch or jump, no SYSTEM/FENCE side effects, no illegal decode,\\nand no M-extension or divider-long operation. If any eligibility check fails,\\nthe core falls back to the current single-issue behavior.\\n\\nThe pair issue predicate must be conservative. Do not issue lane 1 when\\nlane 1 reads lane 0's destination, when both lanes write the same nonzero\\ndestination, when either lane depends on a load-use interlock, when a MEM\\nwait or divider hold is active, or when a redirect/flush is pending. For a\\nvalid pair, fetch advances by pc+8; for every other cycle, the existing\\nstatic branch/direct-JAL prediction and replay-hit behavior continues to\\ndrive the next PC exactly as today. Since lane 1 is never control-flow or\\nmemory, no second dmem access or second branch redirect is introduced.\\n\\nAdd a second simple ALU lane and carry lane-1 metadata through EX/MEM and\\nMEM/WB beside lane 0. Both lanes retire together only when the shared MEM/WB\\nboundary is not held. RVFI channel 0 reports the older lane-0 instruction\\nwith order N, and RVFI channel 1 reports the younger lane-1 instruction\\nwith order N+1. Single-retire cycles still place the sole retirement on\\nchannel 0 with channel 1 fully driven to zero. The RVFI order counter must\\nincrement by two on dual-retire cycles and by one on single-retire cycles.\\n\\nThe register file should gain the minimal extra ports needed for lane-1\\noperands and dual writeback. If both lanes write in the same cycle, writes\\noccur in program order so lane 1 is architecturally younger; the pair\\npredicate may also simply disallow same-rd pairs to keep this path small.\\nExisting EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding remains authoritative for lane 0, and\\nlane 1 can use the same forwarding sources plus the conservative pair\\ndependency checks to avoid same-cycle lane0-to-lane1 bypassing.\\n\\nThis is intentionally not a general superscalar core. It is a replay-fed\\nALU-pair fast path that preserves the current single-issue behavior for all\\nmemory, control-flow, illegal, M-extension, stalled, and uncertain cases.\\nThe top-level IO shape, memory ordering, pending-store slot semantics,\\ndivider interlock, branch/JAL resolved-next-PC recovery, decoder\\nillegal-default discipline, and NRET=2 RVFI field widths remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add lane-1 pipeline metadata or paired bundle fields beside the existing\\nIF/ID, ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB payloads. Include per-lane valid,\\ndecoded control, source/destination registers, operands, PC, instruction,\\nprediction metadata, and RVFI-visible source/result fields. Keep the\\nexisting lane-0 fields semantically unchanged and document that lane 1\\nis a restricted simple-integer younger instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the banked registered replay frontend to provide an optional\\npc+4 instruction candidate for lane 1. The second candidate may come\\nonly from replay, not from a second external imem read. Advance the PC\\nby pc+8 only when ID accepts a valid dual-issue pair; otherwise preserve\\nthe current live-imem/replay selection, pc+4, branch, direct-JAL, flush,\\nredirect, and replay-miss behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode lane 0 as today and decode the optional lane-1 candidate with a\\nconservative simple-integer eligibility check. Generate a dual-issue\\naccept bit only for legal adjacent OP/OP-IMM/LUI/AUIPC pairs with no\\nlane-to-lane RAW or WAW hazard and no control, memory, M-extension,\\nSYSTEM, FENCE, or illegal instruction in either lane.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the extra read and write capability needed for the restricted\\nsecond lane. Preserve x0 hardwiring and write-first behavior. If two\\nnonzero writes occur in one cycle, commit them in program order with\\nlane 0 older and lane 1 younger, or rely on the pair predicate to\\nsuppress same-destination pairs.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a second simple ALU execution path for lane 1 using the same RV32I\\nALU semantics as lane 0 but excluding memory, control-flow, and long\\nM-extension operations. Carry lane-1 metadata into EX/MEM only when the\\ndual-issue predicate is valid and the shared pipeline is not stalled.\\nKeep lane-0 divider, branch/JAL/JALR redirect, forwarding, and trap\\nbehavior unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend forwarding decisions for the second lane using existing EX/MEM\\nand MEM/WB producer metadata, while preserving lane-0 priority and the\\ncurrent load-use discipline. The pair predicate should avoid requiring\\nsame-cycle lane0-to-lane1 forwarding.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add conservative dual-issue gating to the existing stall network. Any\\nload-use hazard, MEM wait, divider long operation, imem/replay miss,\\nredirect, flush, or uncertain lane-1 source dependency should suppress\\nlane 1 and fall back to single issue without changing the existing\\nlane-0 stall, flush, EX/MEM hold, or MEM/WB hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry lane-1 simple-ALU results through the shared MEM/WB boundary\\nwithout adding a second dmem request. Lane 1 must be invalid whenever\\nlane 0 is a memory operation that is waiting, trapping, or otherwise\\nunable to retire. Preserve all existing load/store, misalignment,\\npending-store-slot, RVFI memory-field, and hold_wb behavior for lane 0.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add writeback selection for an optional second simple-ALU lane. Generate\\nindependent lane-0 and lane-1 write enables and data, preserving x0\\nsuppression and program-order write semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the two-lane pipeline, dual writeback, and dual RVFI retirement.\\nChannel 0 must always carry the older retirement; channel 1 is valid\\nonly for accepted paired lane-1 instructions and is otherwise tied to\\nzero. Update the segmented RVFI order counter by two on dual-retire\\ncycles and by one on single-retire cycles while preserving the top-level\\ncore IO exactly.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add lane-1 pipeline metadata or paired bundle fields beside the existing\\nIF/ID, ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB payloads. Include per-lane valid,\\ndecoded control, source/destination registers, operands, PC, instruction,\\nprediction metadata, and RVFI-visible source/result fields. Keep the\\nexisting lane-0 fields semantically unchanged and document that lane 1\\nis a restricted simple-integer younger instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the banked registered replay frontend to provide an optional\\npc+4 instruction candidate for lane 1. The second candidate may come\\nonly from replay, not from a second external imem read. Advance the PC\\nby pc+8 only when ID accepts a valid dual-issue pair; otherwise preserve\\nthe current live-imem/replay selection, pc+4, branch, direct-JAL, flush,\\nredirect, and replay-miss behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode lane 0 as today and decode the optional lane-1 candidate with a\\nconservative simple-integer eligibility check. Generate a dual-issue\\naccept bit only for legal adjacent OP/OP-IMM/LUI/AUIPC pairs with no\\nlane-to-lane RAW or WAW hazard and no control, memory, M-extension,\\nSYSTEM, FENCE, or illegal instruction in either lane.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the extra read and write capability needed for the restricted\\nsecond lane. Preserve x0 hardwiring and write-first behavior. If two\\nnonzero writes occur in one cycle, commit them in program order with\\nlane 0 older and lane 1 younger, or rely on the pair predicate to\\nsuppress same-destination pairs.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a second simple ALU execution path for lane 1 using the same RV32I\\nALU semantics as lane 0 but excluding memory, control-flow, and long\\nM-extension operations. Carry lane-1 metadata into EX/MEM only when the\\ndual-issue predicate is valid and the shared pipeline is not stalled.\\nKeep lane-0 divider, branch/JAL/JALR redirect, forwarding, and trap\\nbehavior unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend forwarding decisions for the second lane using existing EX/MEM\\nand MEM/WB producer metadata, while preserving lane-0 priority and the\\ncurrent load-use discipline. The pair predicate should avoid requiring\\nsame-cycle lane0-to-lane1 forwarding.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add conservative dual-issue gating to the existing stall network. Any\\nload-use hazard, MEM wait, divider long operation, imem/replay miss,\\nredirect, flush, or uncertain lane-1 source dependency should suppress\\nlane 1 and fall back to single issue without changing the existing\\nlane-0 stall, flush, EX/MEM hold, or MEM/WB hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry lane-1 simple-ALU results through the shared MEM/WB boundary\\nwithout adding a second dmem request. Lane 1 must be invalid whenever\\nlane 0 is a memory operation that is waiting, trapping, or otherwise\\nunable to retire. Preserve all existing load/store, misalignment,\\npending-store-slot, RVFI memory-field, and hold_wb behavior for lane 0.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add writeback selection for an optional second simple-ALU lane. Generate\\nindependent lane-0 and lane-1 write enables and data, preserving x0\\nsuppression and program-order write semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the two-lane pipeline, dual writeback, and dual RVFI retirement.\\nChannel 0 must always carry the older retirement; channel 1 is valid\\nonly for accepted paired lane-1 instructions and is otherwise tied to\\nzero. Update the segmented RVFI order counter by two on dual-retire\\ncycles and by one on single-retire cycles while preserving the top-level\\ncore IO exactly.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 22}\n67:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-060-r22s2\", \"title\": \"Stream-Resistant Replay Insertion\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table, which reached 577.76 fitness by hiding random imem stalls\\nwithout putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the remaining boundary clearly: adding more branch\\ndirection state, return prediction, victim search, skewed indexing, cached\\nnext-PC metadata, or bank-aware fill logic either saved too few cycles or\\ncost more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe least intrusive remaining replay opportunity is replacement policy. The\\ncurrent two-bank table uses a per-index MRU bit and marks every miss-filled\\nentry as MRU. That is a normal two-way cache policy, but it is vulnerable to\\none-shot or low-reuse instruction streams, wrong-path fetches, and cold helper\\ncode displacing hot-loop replay entries. CoreMark repeatedly executes compact\\nloops, so protecting the most recently hit replay word from streaming\\npollution may recover some random-iStall hits without adding a table, a tag\\ncompare, a branch update path, or any current-cycle fetch-ready logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor is the two-bank registered lookahead instruction\\nreplay table, which reached 577.76 fitness by hiding random imem stalls\\nwithout putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF decision path. Follow-up\\npredictor attempts show the remaining boundary clearly: adding more branch\\ndirection state, return prediction, victim search, skewed indexing, cached\\nnext-PC metadata, or bank-aware fill logic either saved too few cycles or\\ncost more Fmax than it recovered.\\n\\nThe least intrusive remaining replay opportunity is replacement policy. The\\ncurrent two-bank table uses a per-index MRU bit and marks every miss-filled\\nentry as MRU. That is a normal two-way cache policy, but it is vulnerable to\\none-shot or low-reuse instruction streams, wrong-path fetches, and cold helper\\ncode displacing hot-loop replay entries. CoreMark repeatedly executes compact\\nloops, so protecting the most recently hit replay word from streaming\\npollution may recover some random-iStall hits without adding a table, a tag\\ncompare, a branch update path, or any current-cycle fetch-ready logic.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the current two-bank registered lookahead replay predictor exactly the\\nsame in size, indexing, compressed tags, resetless payload arrays, full\\ncandidate-PC guard, fill bypass, and live-imem-first behavior. Change only\\nthe per-set insertion policy.\\n\\nOn a live `io_imemReady=1` fetch that hits an existing bank entry, refresh\\nthat bank and mark it MRU as today. On a miss where one bank is invalid, fill\\nthe invalid bank and mark it MRU so cold startup still quickly populates both\\nways. On a miss where both banks are valid, fill the non-MRU bank but leave\\nthe MRU bit pointing at the older protected bank. The newly inserted line is\\ntherefore the replacement candidate until it proves reuse by a later hit.\\n\\nThis is a stream-resistant replay insertion policy, similar in spirit to a\\ntiny bimodal insertion policy but with no new counters or throttle state. It\\ncan only affect replay hit rate: tags and the existing registered candidate\\nPC guard still prevent wrong instructions from entering the pipeline, replay\\nmisses still hold the PC and inject the existing IF bubble, and live imem\\ndata remains authoritative whenever ready. Static backward-branch and direct\\nJAL prediction, EX resolved-next-PC repair, pending-store behavior, divider\\ninterlock, hot/cold ALU encoding, top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 ports, strict\\nchannel-0 order, and tied-off channel 1 remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Modify only the two-bank replay predictor's MRU update policy. Keep the\\nexisting bank match detection and replacement bank selection, but on a\\nlive-fill miss with both banks valid, write the non-MRU bank without\\nchanging the MRU bit. Continue marking hit fills and invalid-bank fills\\nas MRU. Preserve both 128-entry banks, raw `pc[8:2]` indexing,\\ncompressed `pc[31:9]` tags, resetless payload arrays, registered\\ncandidate PC guard, same-index fill bypass, live-imem priority,\\nreplay-hit `fetch_ready` semantics, redirect priority, and static\\nbackward-branch plus direct-JAL prediction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document the stream-resistant\\ninsertion policy: replay hits and cold invalid fills update the MRU bit,\\nwhile both-valid miss fills replace the non-MRU bank without immediately\\nmaking the new line MRU. Do not change bundle widths, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the\\nNRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Modify only the two-bank replay predictor's MRU update policy. Keep the\\nexisting bank match detection and replacement bank selection, but on a\\nlive-fill miss with both banks valid, write the non-MRU bank without\\nchanging the MRU bit. Continue marking hit fills and invalid-bank fills\\nas MRU. Preserve both 128-entry banks, raw `pc[8:2]` indexing,\\ncompressed `pc[31:9]` tags, resetless payload arrays, registered\\ncandidate PC guard, same-index fill bypass, live-imem priority,\\nreplay-hit `fetch_ready` semantics, redirect priority, and static\\nbackward-branch plus direct-JAL prediction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID replay comments to document the stream-resistant\\ninsertion policy: replay hits and cold invalid fills update the MRU bit,\\nwhile both-valid miss fills replace the non-MRU bank without immediately\\nmaking the new line MRU. Do not change bundle widths, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the\\nNRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 390.36, \"delta_pct\": -32.44, \"vs_baseline\": 29.67, \"fmax_mhz\": 136.18, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3488613, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5938, \"ff\": 2223, \"seeds\": [141.04, 136.18, 129.84], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Updated `rtl/if_stage.sv` so live imem fills still write the matched bank or the selected replacement bank, but the per-set MRU bit is only refreshed on replay-table hits or on misses where at least one bank is invalid.\\n- Both-valid live-fill misses continue to replace the non-MRU bank, but now preserve the existing MRU bit so the newly inserted line remains the next replacement candidate until it is hit later.\\n- Updated the IF/ID replay predictor comments in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` to document the stream-resistant insertion policy.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No functional deviations from the hypothesis. The table size, indexing, compressed tags, resetless payload arrays, registered candidate PC guard, same-index fill bypass, live-imem priority, replay-hit fetch-ready behavior, redirect priority, and static branch/JAL prediction were left unchanged.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No known RTL correctness concerns. The direct `bash formal/run_all.sh` command could not stage files in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a shared directory outside the writable worktree and `rm` returned `Operation not permitted`.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked before checks by sandbox permissions on the shared `formal/riscv-formal/cores/auto-arch-researcher` staging directory.\\n- Equivalent formal run from a writable `/tmp` mirror of `formal/run_all.sh` and riscv-formal support files, with this worktree's `rtl/` symlinked in: pass, `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T08:59:10.877288\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 22}\n68:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-061-r23s0\", \"title\": \"Power-of-Two RAM Range Decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large timing wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split simplified the common\\nresult mux, the RVFI order counter is segmented, and the banked registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall loss. Recent broader\\nmicro-opt attempts that changed replay replacement, reset policy, ALU\\nfunct3 encoding, or source-qualified hazards tended to lose Fmax through\\nplacement noise or extra muxing. The next micro_opt should therefore be a\\nvery narrow equivalence rewrite on logic that is still always present.\\n\\nThe MEM stage still classifies bufferable stores with an unsigned 32-bit\\ncomparison against `32'h0010_0000`. That limit is exactly 1 MiB, so for a\\n32-bit byte address the predicate is equivalent to `addr[31:20] == 0`.\\nThe result feeds store-slot enqueue and MEM backpressure, which in turn\\ndrives the global stall network. Replacing the generic less-than form with\\nan explicit high-bit decode should remove carry/comparator structure from\\nthe store-bufferable path without changing the direct ready-store behavior,\\npending-store semantics, memory ordering, or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best design has already captured the large timing wins: cold\\nDIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU split simplified the common\\nresult mux, the RVFI order counter is segmented, and the banked registered\\ninstruction replay predictor hides most random iStall loss. Recent broader\\nmicro-opt attempts that changed replay replacement, reset policy, ALU\\nfunct3 encoding, or source-qualified hazards tended to lose Fmax through\\nplacement noise or extra muxing. The next micro_opt should therefore be a\\nvery narrow equivalence rewrite on logic that is still always present.\\n\\nThe MEM stage still classifies bufferable stores with an unsigned 32-bit\\ncomparison against `32'h0010_0000`. That limit is exactly 1 MiB, so for a\\n32-bit byte address the predicate is equivalent to `addr[31:20] == 0`.\\nThe result feeds store-slot enqueue and MEM backpressure, which in turn\\ndrives the global stall network. Replacing the generic less-than form with\\nan explicit high-bit decode should remove carry/comparator structure from\\nthe store-bufferable path without changing the direct ready-store behavior,\\npending-store semantics, memory ordering, or CoreMark cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the normal-RAM range check used for store buffering in\\n`rtl/mem_stage.sv` with an explicit power-of-two high-bit decode. A legal\\naligned normal-RAM store should be considered bufferable when the effective\\nbyte address has `in.alu_result[31:20] == 12'b0`; this is bit-identical to\\nthe existing unsigned check `in.alu_result < 32'h0010_0000` for the current\\nRAM window `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)`.\\n\\nKeep all other memory behavior unchanged. Misaligned or illegal memory\\noperations still trap and do not issue dmem requests. MMIO, UART, timing\\nmarker, and any other non-RAM stores remain nonbufferable and still wait for\\nthe external dmem handshake. The pending store slot still enqueues only on a\\nbufferable store when the slot is empty and `io_dmemReady` is low, and live\\nloads/stores still observe the same `mem_wait` behavior as today.\\n\\nThis is intended as a pure synthesis-shape micro-optimization. It does not\\nalter the top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 field set, channel-0-only retirement\\nconvention, strict `rvfi_order` sequence, banked IF replay predictor,\\nhot/cold ALU encoding, divider interlock, branch recovery, load extraction,\\nstore byte masks, pending-store drain behavior, or decoder legality\\ndiscipline. The expected effect is a small median Fmax improvement from\\nreplacing a 32-bit range comparator in the MEM/stall path with a 12-bit\\nzero-detect, with no intended change to iterations per cycle.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the `in.alu_result < NORMAL_RAM_LIMIT` store-bufferable test\\nwith an explicit upper-address zero check for the 1 MiB RAM window,\\ne.g. `in.alu_result[31:20] == 12'b0`. Keep `NORMAL_RAM_LIMIT` only if\\nit remains useful for comments, or replace it with a local upper-bit\\nconstant. Preserve all existing `mem_access`, misalignment trap,\\ndmem request gating, pending-store enqueue, `mem_wait`, MEM/WB, and\\nRVFI memory-field behavior.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 1, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the `in.alu_result < NORMAL_RAM_LIMIT` store-bufferable test\\nwith an explicit upper-address zero check for the 1 MiB RAM window,\\ne.g. `in.alu_result[31:20] == 12'b0`. Keep `NORMAL_RAM_LIMIT` only if\\nit remains useful for comments, or replace it with a local upper-bit\\nconstant. Preserve all existing `mem_access`, misalignment trap,\\ndmem request gating, pending-store enqueue, `mem_wait`, MEM/WB, and\\nRVFI memory-field behavior.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 577.76, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 91.92, \"fmax_mhz\": 198.69, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3438981, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5944, \"ff\": 2235, \"seeds\": [202.51, 198.69, 182.82], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Updated `rtl/mem_stage.sv` so normal-RAM store buffering uses an explicit\\n  high-address decode:\\n  `in.alu_result[31:20] == 12'b0`.\\n- Removed the 32-bit unsigned `< 32'h0010_0000` comparator from the\\n  `store_bufferable` predicate.\\n- Kept the existing `mem_access`, misalignment trap, dmem request gating,\\n  pending-store enqueue, `mem_wait`, MEM/WB, and RVFI memory-field behavior\\n  unchanged.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No RTL behavior deviations from the hypothesis.\\n- The direct in-worktree `bash formal/run_all.sh` invocation could not start\\n  proof work in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a\\n  shared read-only tree and the script must remove/copy staged core files\\n  under `formal/riscv-formal/cores/auto-arch-researcher`.\\n- To self-check the same gate, I copied the worktree to `/tmp`, replaced the\\n  riscv-formal symlink there with a writable copy of the framework excluding\\n  stale `cores/`, and ran the original `bash formal/run_all.sh` from that\\n  writable temp copy.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No known implementation concerns. The decode is bit-equivalent to\\n  `addr < 32'h0010_0000` for 32-bit byte addresses.\\n\\n## Local verification status\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass in writable temp-copy staging,\\n  `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n- RTL fix attempts after formal: 0.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T11:48:35.228512\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 23}\n69:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-062-r23s1\", \"title\": \"MEM-Wait Simple-ALU Skid Slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simple ALU operation whose operands are already\\navailable and whose execution has no memory or control-flow side effects.\\n\\nPrior attempts to attack this bottleneck by replaying load data or adding\\nbroader store buffering reduced cycles but cost too much Fmax or placement\\nquality. A smaller structural decoupling point is more promising: let EX do\\none cycle of useful independent simple-ALU work while the older MEM load or\\nnonbufferable store is waiting, but keep retirement strictly in order. This\\ntargets the exact full-pipeline freeze caused by random dmem backpressure\\nwithout adding load data to EX, another replay table, another store-buffer\\nentry, a branch predictor, or a general superscalar backend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a fast single-issue design with the largest proven\\nwins already in place: cold DIV/REM is off the EX datapath, the hot/cold ALU\\nsplit improved Fmax, the one-entry pending-store slot hides some store-only\\ndmem stalls, and the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides\\nmost random imem stalls. The remaining structural loss is therefore mainly\\nbackend pressure from dmem waits, especially loads. Today a load in MEM with\\n`io_dmemReady=0` freezes IF, ID, and EX even when the younger instruction in\\nID/EX is an independent simple ALU operation whose operands are already\\navailable and whose execution has no memory or control-flow side effects.\\n\\nPrior attempts to attack this bottleneck by replaying load data or adding\\nbroader store buffering reduced cycles but cost too much Fmax or placement\\nquality. A smaller structural decoupling point is more promising: let EX do\\none cycle of useful independent simple-ALU work while the older MEM load or\\nnonbufferable store is waiting, but keep retirement strictly in order. This\\ntargets the exact full-pipeline freeze caused by random dmem backpressure\\nwithout adding load data to EX, another replay table, another store-buffer\\nentry, a branch predictor, or a general superscalar backend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a one-entry stall-only skid slot for an instruction that has completed EX\\nwhile the older EX/MEM instruction is held in MEM by `mem_wait`. On the first\\ncycle of a MEM wait, if ID/EX contains a valid independent simple RV32I ALU\\ninstruction, capture its fully resolved EX/MEM payload into the skid slot\\ninstead of discarding that cycle. Eligible skid instructions are limited to\\nside-effect-free, non-control, non-memory, non-M-extension operations such as\\nOP, OP-IMM, LUI, and AUIPC. The slot must not accept branches, jumps, loads,\\nstores, FENCE/SYSTEM, illegal instructions, DIV/REM, high-latency operations,\\nor anything whose source depends on the older waiting load.\\n\\nWhile the skid slot is valid, IF and ID remain stalled so no second younger\\ninstruction can overtake it. When the MEM wait clears, the older instruction\\nadvances and retires normally first; the skid entry is then injected into the\\nEX/MEM-to-MEM path as the next in-order instruction without re-executing it.\\nIf no skid entry is present, the pipeline behaves exactly as it does today.\\nThe no-stall fast path should remain structurally close to the current best:\\nEX writes directly into EX/MEM on normal cycles, and the skid slot is only\\nused when EX/MEM would otherwise be held by a precise MEM wait.\\n\\nThis is not out-of-order retirement and not dual issue. It is a single-entry\\nexecution skid buffer behind an older blocked MEM operation. RVFI channel 0\\nremains the only retirement channel, channel 1 stays tied off, and\\n`rvfi_order` still increments by one per visible retirement. Branch/JAL/JALR\\nrecovery, the banked IF replay predictor, pending-store slot ordering,\\ndivider interlock, decoder illegal-default discipline, top-level IO, memory\\naccess bounds, and timing-marker behavior remain unchanged. The expected\\nbenefit is a modest CoreMark cycle reduction under the random dStall model\\nwith less Fmax exposure than MEM-stage load replay or larger store buffers,\\nbecause the added state is one EX/MEM-shaped entry used only on stall cycles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small skid payload typedef if useful, or document reuse of the\\nexisting `ex_mem_t` as the one-entry EX skid slot. Clarify that skid\\nentries are valid only for conservative simple-ALU instructions that\\nhave already completed EX and are waiting behind an older MEM-stage wait.\\nDo not change RVFI fields, top-level ports, decoder legality defaults,\\nALU op constants, architectural state semantics, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add stall-only capture of an eligible younger simple-ALU instruction into\\na one-entry skid slot when the normal EX/MEM register is held by\\n`mem_wait`. Capture the same post-forwarded operands, ALU result, rd,\\nsource metadata, instruction, PC, and control payload that would normally\\nenter EX/MEM. Provide controls to mark the ID/EX instruction consumed,\\nhold further younger work while the slot is full, and later inject the\\nskid entry into EX/MEM after the older MEM wait clears. Preserve normal\\nno-stall EX/MEM behavior, divider launch/completion, redirect generation,\\nforwarding inputs, and RVFI metadata for lane-0 single issue.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add precise control for the skid capture and drain states. During a MEM\\nwait, allow exactly one eligible ID/EX simple-ALU instruction to be\\nconsumed into the skid slot when the slot is empty; otherwise keep the\\ncurrent IF/ID stalls. Once the slot is valid, stall IF and ID until the\\nolder MEM wait clears and the skid entry can drain in order. Keep the\\nexisting load-use interlock, imem replay readiness, EX long-operation\\nstall, redirect flushes, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB hold semantics intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the skid-slot status and capture/drain controls between EX,\\nhazard, and the existing MEM wait path. Gate skid eligibility with valid\\nstage metadata and conservative control checks so resetless or invalid\\npayloads cannot be captured. Preserve the core module IO, RVFI NRET=2\\nchannel layout, strict channel-0 order counter, tied-off channel 1,\\npending-store-slot dmem arbitration, banked IF replay wiring, and\\nwriteback behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"If needed, make the existing forwarding producer metadata aware of the\\ndrained skid entry exactly as it is aware of a normal EX/MEM producer.\\nDo not introduce load-data forwarding or lane-to-lane bypassing; the\\nskid entry should carry only a previously completed simple-ALU result and\\nshould preserve the current priority of EX/MEM over MEM/WB forwarding.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small skid payload typedef if useful, or document reuse of the\\nexisting `ex_mem_t` as the one-entry EX skid slot. Clarify that skid\\nentries are valid only for conservative simple-ALU instructions that\\nhave already completed EX and are waiting behind an older MEM-stage wait.\\nDo not change RVFI fields, top-level ports, decoder legality defaults,\\nALU op constants, architectural state semantics, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add stall-only capture of an eligible younger simple-ALU instruction into\\na one-entry skid slot when the normal EX/MEM register is held by\\n`mem_wait`. Capture the same post-forwarded operands, ALU result, rd,\\nsource metadata, instruction, PC, and control payload that would normally\\nenter EX/MEM. Provide controls to mark the ID/EX instruction consumed,\\nhold further younger work while the slot is full, and later inject the\\nskid entry into EX/MEM after the older MEM wait clears. Preserve normal\\nno-stall EX/MEM behavior, divider launch/completion, redirect generation,\\nforwarding inputs, and RVFI metadata for lane-0 single issue.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add precise control for the skid capture and drain states. During a MEM\\nwait, allow exactly one eligible ID/EX simple-ALU instruction to be\\nconsumed into the skid slot when the slot is empty; otherwise keep the\\ncurrent IF/ID stalls. Once the slot is valid, stall IF and ID until the\\nolder MEM wait clears and the skid entry can drain in order. Keep the\\nexisting load-use interlock, imem replay readiness, EX long-operation\\nstall, redirect flushes, EX/MEM hold, and MEM/WB hold semantics intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the skid-slot status and capture/drain controls between EX,\\nhazard, and the existing MEM wait path. Gate skid eligibility with valid\\nstage metadata and conservative control checks so resetless or invalid\\npayloads cannot be captured. Preserve the core module IO, RVFI NRET=2\\nchannel layout, strict channel-0 order counter, tied-off channel 1,\\npending-store-slot dmem arbitration, banked IF replay wiring, and\\nwriteback behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"If needed, make the existing forwarding producer metadata aware of the\\ndrained skid entry exactly as it is aware of a normal EX/MEM producer.\\nDo not introduce load-data forwarding or lane-to-lane bypassing; the\\nskid entry should carry only a previously completed simple-ALU result and\\nshould preserve the current priority of EX/MEM over MEM/WB forwarding.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 469.85, \"delta_pct\": -18.68, \"vs_baseline\": 56.08, \"fmax_mhz\": 161.58, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3438981, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5908, \"ff\": 2225, \"seeds\": [158.88, 169.98, 161.58], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Added a one-entry MEM-wait skid slot inside `rtl/ex_stage.sv`, reusing the existing `ex_mem_t` payload shape.\\n- Added `skid_capture`, `skid_drain`, and `skid_valid` controls between `core`, `hazard_unit`, and `ex_stage`.\\n- During a precise `mem_wait`, hazard can consume one valid simple RV32I ALU instruction from ID/EX, clear ID/EX, and keep IF held behind it.\\n- When the MEM wait clears, EX injects the captured payload into the normal EX/MEM register so MEM/WB and RVFI retirement remain single-channel and in order.\\n- Added conservative skid eligibility in `rtl/core.sv`: OP, OP-IMM, LUI, and AUIPC only; no illegal, memory, control-flow, M-extension, or load-dependent source operands.\\n- Documented in `rtl/core_pkg.sv` that the skid slot reuses `ex_mem_t`.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No new typedef was added. Reusing `ex_mem_t` keeps the skid payload identical to the normal EX/MEM register.\\n- IF/ID is released on the same clock edge that a valid skid entry drains into EX/MEM. The younger instruction cannot execute until the following cycle, so it still remains behind the drained skid instruction without adding an extra bubble.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The eligibility gate is intentionally conservative and may miss some harmless captures, especially false rs2 dependencies on OP-IMM immediate bits are avoided but other uncommon safe cases are not pursued.\\n- This adds a small combinational eligibility/control path in `core.sv` and a second `ex_mem_t` register in EX; Fmax impact remains for the FPGA gate to measure.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- Lint: `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv` passed.\\n- Formal: direct `bash formal/run_all.sh` in this sandbox failed before proof because `formal/riscv-formal` points outside the writable root and the script could not clean its generated core directory.\\n- Formal workaround: staged the current `rtl/` plus formal wrapper/configs into `/tmp/autoarch-formal-r23s1` with a writable copy of the riscv-formal framework, then ran the same `bash formal/run_all.sh`. Result: `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T11:50:58.385213\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 23}\n70:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-063-r23s2\", \"title\": \"Low-Bit Static Target Alignment Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best result is the two-bank registered instruction replay\\npredictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is the only\\npredictor-family structure that has produced a large CoreMark cycle win and\\na strong Fmax result. Follow-up predictor attempts that added more state,\\nchanged replacement, added victim/skewed indexing, cached next-PC metadata,\\nor added branch-direction tables generally lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor hypothesis should therefore avoid new predictor memories\\nand preserve the successful two-bank replay structure. The remaining\\nlow-risk predictor timing issue is in the zero-state static control\\npredictor itself: IF currently waits for the full branch and JAL target\\nadders to produce `[1:0]` before deciding whether the predicted target is\\nword-aligned. For B-type and J-type immediates, bit 0 is always zero and bit\\n1 of the target can be derived from `pc[1]` and one encoded immediate bit.\\n\\nA previous single-adder static-control rewrite removed a target adder but\\nintroduced a selected-immediate mux before the target add and regressed. This\\nhypothesis is narrower: keep the existing branch and JAL target adders, but\\nremove their low-bit outputs from the prediction-enable path. That should\\nkeep CoreMark cycles identical while shortening the IF predictor select path\\nin the current best frontend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best result is the two-bank registered instruction replay\\npredictor plus the hot/cold ALU split. The replay predictor is the only\\npredictor-family structure that has produced a large CoreMark cycle win and\\na strong Fmax result. Follow-up predictor attempts that added more state,\\nchanged replacement, added victim/skewed indexing, cached next-PC metadata,\\nor added branch-direction tables generally lost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe next predictor hypothesis should therefore avoid new predictor memories\\nand preserve the successful two-bank replay structure. The remaining\\nlow-risk predictor timing issue is in the zero-state static control\\npredictor itself: IF currently waits for the full branch and JAL target\\nadders to produce `[1:0]` before deciding whether the predicted target is\\nword-aligned. For B-type and J-type immediates, bit 0 is always zero and bit\\n1 of the target can be derived from `pc[1]` and one encoded immediate bit.\\n\\nA previous single-adder static-control rewrite removed a target adder but\\nintroduced a selected-immediate mux before the target add and regressed. This\\nhypothesis is narrower: keep the existing branch and JAL target adders, but\\nremove their low-bit outputs from the prediction-enable path. That should\\nkeep CoreMark cycles identical while shortening the IF predictor select path\\nin the current best frontend.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Refine the IF-stage static branch/JAL predictor so target word alignment is\\nchecked from encoded low bits instead of from the full target-adder result.\\nFor B-type branches, the predicted target is word-aligned when `pc[0] == 0`\\nand `(pc[1] ^ fetch_instr[8]) == 0`, because the branch immediate's bit 1 is\\nencoded in `instr[8]`. For JAL, the corresponding check is `pc[0] == 0` and\\n`(pc[1] ^ fetch_instr[21]) == 0`, because the J immediate's bit 1 is encoded\\nin `instr[21]`. The existing misaligned-target trap behavior in EX remains\\nauthoritative for all real control-flow instructions.\\n\\nKeep the prediction policy exactly the same as the current best design:\\nlegal backward conditional branches predict taken, direct aligned JALs\\npredict their encoded target, and JALR remains unpredicted. Keep the\\nexisting parallel branch and JAL target adders for the actual predicted\\ntarget value, so this does not repeat the earlier single-adder mux\\nexperiment. Only the alignment guard feeding `branch_predict_taken` and\\n`jal_predict_taken` changes.\\n\\nThis is correctness-neutral because the low-bit equations are exactly the\\nlow two bits of `pc + imm` for B/J immediates, whose bit 0 is fixed zero.\\nIf any future alias or unusual PC state violated the assumption, EX still\\nrepairs wrong control-flow with the existing resolved-next-PC mismatch path\\nand traps misaligned targets as today. The two-bank replay predictor,\\nreplay hit/miss behavior, live-imem priority, fill bypass, pending-store\\nslot, divider interlock, hot/cold ALU encoding, top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2\\nfields, strict channel-0 order sequence, and channel-1 tie-off remain\\nunchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the `branch_target[1:0] == 2'b00` and\\n`jal_target[1:0] == 2'b00` prediction guards with low-bit predecode\\nbased on `pc[0]`, `pc[1]`, `fetch_instr[8]`, and `fetch_instr[21]`.\\nKeep the existing branch and JAL target adders for the selected\\npredicted target value, preserve the current legal-branch guard,\\nbackward-branch-taken policy, direct-JAL prediction, JALR-unpredicted\\nbehavior, two-bank replay predictor, candidate PC guard, fill bypass,\\nredirect priority, and `fetch_ready` semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID prediction comments to document that the static\\nbranch/JAL predictor derives target alignment from encoded low immediate\\nbits while still carrying the same `predicted_taken` and\\n`predicted_target` metadata. Do not change bundle widths, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the\\nNRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the `branch_target[1:0] == 2'b00` and\\n`jal_target[1:0] == 2'b00` prediction guards with low-bit predecode\\nbased on `pc[0]`, `pc[1]`, `fetch_instr[8]`, and `fetch_instr[21]`.\\nKeep the existing branch and JAL target adders for the selected\\npredicted target value, preserve the current legal-branch guard,\\nbackward-branch-taken policy, direct-JAL prediction, JALR-unpredicted\\nbehavior, two-bank replay predictor, candidate PC guard, fill bypass,\\nredirect priority, and `fetch_ready` semantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Update only IF/ID prediction comments to document that the static\\nbranch/JAL predictor derives target alignment from encoded low immediate\\nbits while still carrying the same `predicted_taken` and\\n`predicted_target` metadata. Do not change bundle widths, architectural\\nstate, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields, top-level IO, or the\\nNRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 490.61, \"delta_pct\": -15.08, \"vs_baseline\": 62.97, \"fmax_mhz\": 168.72, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3438981, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5911, \"ff\": 2222, \"seeds\": [175.41, 168.38, 168.72], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What Changed\\n\\n- Updated `rtl/if_stage.sv` so IF static branch/JAL prediction no longer uses\\n  `branch_target[1:0]` or `jal_target[1:0]` in the prediction-enable path.\\n- Added low-bit alignment predecode:\\n  - B-type branch target word alignment: `pc[0] == 0` and\\n    `(pc[1] ^ fetch_instr[8]) == 0`.\\n  - JAL target word alignment: `pc[0] == 0` and\\n    `(pc[1] ^ fetch_instr[21]) == 0`.\\n- Kept the existing parallel branch and JAL target adders for\\n  `predicted_target`.\\n- Updated `rtl/core_pkg.sv` IF/ID and ID/EX prediction comments to document\\n  encoded-low-bit static alignment predecode while preserving the existing\\n  `predicted_taken` / `predicted_target` metadata contract.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- No RTL behavioral deviations from the hypothesis plan.\\n- The direct `bash formal/run_all.sh` command could not stage files because\\n  `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a repository outside the writable\\n  worktree, and this sandbox denied its cleanup writes with\\n  `Operation not permitted`. To keep the self-check meaningful without\\n  modifying `formal/`, I copied the formal runner/framework to `/tmp`, symlinked\\n  the current `rtl/` directory into that temporary project root, and ran the\\n  same `run_all.sh` flow there.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- No implementation concerns. EX remains authoritative for resolved next-PC\\n  mismatch repair and misaligned-target traps.\\n\\n## Local Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked before checks by sandbox permission on the\\n  repository's symlinked formal staging directory.\\n- Equivalent temporary-workspace formal run using the same `run_all.sh`: pass,\\n  `Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T11:46:33.299974\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 23}\n71:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-064-r24s0\", \"title\": \"Unsigned Multiplier With Signed High Correction\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core has already harvested the broad timing wins: DIV/REM is\\niterative, the ALU has a hot/cold RV32I/RV32M split, RVFI order is segmented,\\nand the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall cycles. The next micro_opt should therefore avoid new predictor state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or latency changes and instead trim logic still\\npresent in the hot EX arithmetic block.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still builds three independent 64-bit products for RV32M\\nmultiply: unsigned*unsigned, signed*signed, and signed*unsigned. CoreMark's\\ntimed region needs low-half MUL, but high-half MULH/MULHU/MULHSU are cold.\\nPrior multiplier experiments showed what not to do: a muxed 33x33 shared\\nmultiplier badly hurt Fmax, and moving high-half multiply to a long-latency\\nunit also regressed. A better micro-optimization is to keep the existing\\nunsigned 32x32 product for the hot MUL path and derive the signed high halves\\nwith narrow correction subtracts after the product.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core has already harvested the broad timing wins: DIV/REM is\\niterative, the ALU has a hot/cold RV32I/RV32M split, RVFI order is segmented,\\nand the banked registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall cycles. The next micro_opt should therefore avoid new predictor state,\\nqueues, reset rewrites, or latency changes and instead trim logic still\\npresent in the hot EX arithmetic block.\\n\\n`rtl/alu.sv` still builds three independent 64-bit products for RV32M\\nmultiply: unsigned*unsigned, signed*signed, and signed*unsigned. CoreMark's\\ntimed region needs low-half MUL, but high-half MULH/MULHU/MULHSU are cold.\\nPrior multiplier experiments showed what not to do: a muxed 33x33 shared\\nmultiplier badly hurt Fmax, and moving high-half multiply to a long-latency\\nunit also regressed. A better micro-optimization is to keep the existing\\nunsigned 32x32 product for the hot MUL path and derive the signed high halves\\nwith narrow correction subtracts after the product.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the signed*signed and signed*unsigned multiplier expressions in\\n`rtl/alu.sv` with correction logic derived from the existing unsigned\\nproduct. Keep one unsigned product `mul_uu = {32'b0, a} * {32'b0, b}`.\\n`MUL` continues to return `mul_uu[31:0]`, and `MULHU` returns\\n`mul_uu[63:32]`.\\n\\nFor signed high-half operations, use the standard two's-complement correction\\nidentities on the high 32 bits of the unsigned product:\\n\\n- `MULH`: `mul_uu[63:32] - (a[31] ? b : 0) - (b[31] ? a : 0)`\\n- `MULHSU`: `mul_uu[63:32] - (a[31] ? b : 0)`\\n\\nThese formulas are bit-exact modulo 32 bits for RV32M because the omitted\\nsign*sign term is a multiple of 2^64 and does not affect the low 64 product\\nbits. The hot low-half MUL path remains a direct unsigned product with no\\noperand signedness mux and no extra latency. High-half variants remain\\nsingle-cycle and architecturally exact, avoiding the IPC risk of the prior\\nlong-latency high-half experiment.\\n\\nKeep the existing hot/cold `op[4] ? m_out : simple_out` ALU split, keep the\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS multiply substitutions unchanged, and leave DIV/REM in\\nthe iterative divider. No forwarding, stall, branch prediction, memory,\\nreplay-table, RVFI, top-level IO, retirement-order, or channel-1 behavior\\nchanges. CoreMark cycles should be unchanged; the expected benefit is a\\nmodest Fmax improvement from removing two always-present wide multiplier\\nprimitives and their routing pressure while avoiding the failed 33x33\\npre-multiplier mux shape.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the `mul_ss` and `mul_su` product expressions. Keep the existing\\nunsigned 32x32 product for `MUL` and `MULHU`, and compute `MULH` and\\n`MULHSU` high halves from `mul_uu[63:32]` with the signed correction\\nsubtracts described above. Preserve the current ALTOPS formulas, the\\ndummy zero result for DIV/REM opcodes handled by `div_unit.sv`, and the\\nfinal hot/cold `op[4]` result select.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the `mul_ss` and `mul_su` product expressions. Keep the existing\\nunsigned 32x32 product for `MUL` and `MULHU`, and compute `MULH` and\\n`MULHSU` high halves from `mul_uu[63:32]` with the signed correction\\nsubtracts described above. Preserve the current ALTOPS formulas, the\\ndummy zero result for DIV/REM opcodes handled by `div_unit.sv`, and the\\nfinal hot/cold `op[4]` result select.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"sandbox_violation: agent touched off-limits paths: ['test/_helpers.py']\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 24}\n72:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-065-r24s1\", \"title\": \"Replay-Fed Memory Plus ADDI Pair Issue\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patterns such as\\na load or store followed by an independent ADDI pointer/index/stack update.\\nA full two-wide machine is risky, but this very narrow pair class uses only\\nthe existing single dmem access plus one younger simple immediate add. It\\ntargets a common structural bubble source without adding another memory port,\\nanother replay table, a load-data-to-EX bypass, a branch predictor, or a\\nsecond general ALU issue path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core is a very strong single-issue design. The large wins\\nalready come from removing cold DIV/REM from the hot EX path, splitting the\\nALU hot/cold mux, adding a one-deep pending-store slot, segmenting the RVFI\\norder counter, and using a two-bank registered instruction replay predictor\\nto hide most random imem stalls. Recent structural attempts show that broad\\nbuffers, data replay tables, and general dual issue are too costly or too\\ndifficult, but they also point at the remaining ceiling: only one instruction\\ncan retire per cycle even when CoreMark presents simple pointer updates next\\nto memory operations.\\n\\nCoreMark's list, matrix, state, and stack code is dense with patterns such as\\na load or store followed by an independent ADDI pointer/index/stack update.\\nA full two-wide machine is risky, but this very narrow pair class uses only\\nthe existing single dmem access plus one younger simple immediate add. It\\ntargets a common structural bubble source without adding another memory port,\\nanother replay table, a load-data-to-EX bypass, a branch predictor, or a\\nsecond general ALU issue path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a restricted replay-fed pair issue mode for an older memory instruction\\nin lane 0 and a younger adjacent ADDI in lane 1. Lane 0 remains the existing\\nsingle-issue path and may be a legal aligned LOAD or STORE. Lane 1 is enabled\\nonly when the instruction at pc+4 is already available from the registered\\ninstruction replay frontend and decodes exactly as a legal ADDI that has no\\ncontrol-flow, memory, SYSTEM/FENCE, illegal, or M-extension behavior. If the\\nlane-1 replay candidate is absent or any check is uncertain, the core falls\\nback to the current single-issue behavior.\\n\\nThe pair predicate must be conservative. Do not issue lane 1 when it reads\\nthe lane-0 load destination, when both lanes write the same nonzero rd, when\\na load-use hazard, MEM wait, divider long operation, redirect, flush, or\\nreplay miss is active, or when lane 0 is a trapping/misaligned memory access.\\nIt is explicitly allowed for lane 1 to update the base register used by the\\nolder memory access, because lane 0's address and store data are computed\\nfrom the old operands before the younger ADDI writes back. Stores still use\\nthe single dmem port; loads still produce their data at MEM; both lanes wait\\ntogether under dmem backpressure and retire in order.\\n\\nFor accepted pairs, fetch advances to pc+8. Lane 0 carries normal memory\\nmetadata through EX/MEM and MEM/WB. Lane 1 carries only a simple ADDI result,\\nsource metadata, rd, instruction, pc, and pc+4/pc+8 next-PC metadata beside\\nlane 0; it never drives dmem and never redirects. At the MEM/WB boundary,\\nretire lane 0 on RVFI channel 0 with order N and lane 1 on channel 1 with\\norder N+1. Single-retire cycles remain exactly as today: channel 0 carries\\nthe sole retirement and channel 1 is fully tied to zero. The segmented RVFI\\norder counter increments by two only on a dual-retire cycle.\\n\\nThis is not a general superscalar core. It is a narrow memory-plus-ADDI\\nstructural fast path aimed at CoreMark pointer updates. The top-level IO,\\nmemory ordering, pending-store slot semantics, divider interlock,\\nbranch/JAL/JALR resolved-next-PC recovery, decoder illegal-default behavior,\\nRVFI NRET=2 field widths, and timing-marker behavior remain unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the registered two-bank replay frontend to provide an optional\\npc+4 instruction candidate for lane 1. The second word may come only from\\nreplay, not from a second external imem read. Advance the architectural\\nPC by pc+8 only when the downstream pair-issue accept signal is true;\\notherwise preserve the existing live-imem/replay selection, pc+4,\\nbranch, direct-JAL, flush, redirect, and replay-miss behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact lane-1 metadata beside the existing pipeline bundles for the\\nrestricted younger ADDI: valid, pc, instruction, rs1/rd, rs1 value,\\nimmediate, result, and RVFI-visible fields. Keep lane-0 bundle semantics\\nunchanged and document that lane 1 is only valid for replay-fed ADDI\\npairs behind an older memory instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode the optional pc+4 candidate with the existing decoder and produce\\na conservative pair-eligible signal only for legal ADDI. Read the extra\\nlane-1 rs1 operand, reject all lane-to-lane hazards that would require a\\nsame-cycle bypass from a lane-0 load, and keep the current single-issue\\ndecode path unchanged when the pair is not accepted.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the minimal extra read and write capability for the lane-1 ADDI path.\\nPreserve x0 hardwiring and write-first behavior. On dual retirement,\\ncommit lane-0 and lane-1 writes in program order, or rely on the pair\\npredicate to suppress same-destination nonzero pairs so the final\\narchitectural register state is unambiguous.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small lane-1 ADDI adder and metadata path that advances only for an\\naccepted memory-plus-ADDI pair. Lane 0 keeps the existing ALU, memory\\naddress generation, forwarding, divider, and redirect behavior. Lane 1\\nnever issues memory or control-flow operations and is invalidated on any\\nlane-0 trap, redirect, long operation, or pipeline hold.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry lane-1 ADDI results through the MEM/WB boundary beside the lane-0\\nmemory operation without adding a second dmem request. If lane 0 waits\\nfor dmem or the pending-store slot, lane 1 waits with it and does not\\nretire early. Preserve all existing load/store, misalignment, store-slot,\\nMEM/WB hold, and RVFI memory-field behavior for lane 0.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate independent writeback controls for lane 0 and the optional\\nlane-1 ADDI. Suppress x0 writes and preserve program-order write\\nsemantics on dual-retire cycles. Keep the existing single-lane writeback\\nbehavior bit-identical when lane 1 is invalid.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add pair-issue gating to the existing stall network. Suppress lane 1 on\\nany load-use hazard, MEM wait, EX long operation, redirect, flush, replay\\nmiss, or uncertain source dependency. The lane-0 stall, flush, EX/MEM\\nhold, and MEM/WB hold behavior remains the authority for precise\\nsingle-issue operation.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend forwarding only as needed for lane-1 ADDI operands from existing\\nolder EX/MEM and MEM/WB producers. Do not add load-data forwarding from\\nMEM into EX and do not require a same-cycle lane0-to-lane1 bypass; the\\nconservative pair predicate should reject those cases.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the restricted pair pipeline, dual writeback, pair PC advance, and\\ndual RVFI retirement. Channel 0 must always report the older lane-0\\ninstruction; channel 1 is valid only for the younger lane-1 ADDI and is\\notherwise tied to zero. Update the segmented RVFI order counter by two on\\ndual-retire cycles and by one on single-retire cycles while preserving\\nthe top-level core IO exactly.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the registered two-bank replay frontend to provide an optional\\npc+4 instruction candidate for lane 1. The second word may come only from\\nreplay, not from a second external imem read. Advance the architectural\\nPC by pc+8 only when the downstream pair-issue accept signal is true;\\notherwise preserve the existing live-imem/replay selection, pc+4,\\nbranch, direct-JAL, flush, redirect, and replay-miss behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact lane-1 metadata beside the existing pipeline bundles for the\\nrestricted younger ADDI: valid, pc, instruction, rs1/rd, rs1 value,\\nimmediate, result, and RVFI-visible fields. Keep lane-0 bundle semantics\\nunchanged and document that lane 1 is only valid for replay-fed ADDI\\npairs behind an older memory instruction.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode the optional pc+4 candidate with the existing decoder and produce\\na conservative pair-eligible signal only for legal ADDI. Read the extra\\nlane-1 rs1 operand, reject all lane-to-lane hazards that would require a\\nsame-cycle bypass from a lane-0 load, and keep the current single-issue\\ndecode path unchanged when the pair is not accepted.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the minimal extra read and write capability for the lane-1 ADDI path.\\nPreserve x0 hardwiring and write-first behavior. On dual retirement,\\ncommit lane-0 and lane-1 writes in program order, or rely on the pair\\npredicate to suppress same-destination nonzero pairs so the final\\narchitectural register state is unambiguous.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small lane-1 ADDI adder and metadata path that advances only for an\\naccepted memory-plus-ADDI pair. Lane 0 keeps the existing ALU, memory\\naddress generation, forwarding, divider, and redirect behavior. Lane 1\\nnever issues memory or control-flow operations and is invalidated on any\\nlane-0 trap, redirect, long operation, or pipeline hold.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry lane-1 ADDI results through the MEM/WB boundary beside the lane-0\\nmemory operation without adding a second dmem request. If lane 0 waits\\nfor dmem or the pending-store slot, lane 1 waits with it and does not\\nretire early. Preserve all existing load/store, misalignment, store-slot,\\nMEM/WB hold, and RVFI memory-field behavior for lane 0.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate independent writeback controls for lane 0 and the optional\\nlane-1 ADDI. Suppress x0 writes and preserve program-order write\\nsemantics on dual-retire cycles. Keep the existing single-lane writeback\\nbehavior bit-identical when lane 1 is invalid.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add pair-issue gating to the existing stall network. Suppress lane 1 on\\nany load-use hazard, MEM wait, EX long operation, redirect, flush, replay\\nmiss, or uncertain source dependency. The lane-0 stall, flush, EX/MEM\\nhold, and MEM/WB hold behavior remains the authority for precise\\nsingle-issue operation.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend forwarding only as needed for lane-1 ADDI operands from existing\\nolder EX/MEM and MEM/WB producers. Do not add load-data forwarding from\\nMEM into EX and do not require a same-cycle lane0-to-lane1 bypass; the\\nconservative pair predicate should reject those cases.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the restricted pair pipeline, dual writeback, pair PC advance, and\\ndual RVFI retirement. Channel 0 must always report the older lane-0\\ninstruction; channel 1 is valid only for the younger lane-1 ADDI and is\\notherwise tied to zero. Update the segmented RVFI order counter by two on\\ndual-retire cycles and by one on single-retire cycles while preserving\\nthe top-level core IO exactly.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"placement_failed\", \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": \"placement_failed\", \"seeds\": [49.43, null, null], \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 24}\n73:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-066-r24s2\", \"title\": \"Registered Lookahead JALR Target Predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family mechanism that has produced a\\nlarge CoreMark cycle win without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. Follow-up replay changes, direction tables, return stacks,\\nvictim buffers, skewed indexes, and cached static next-PC metadata mostly\\nlost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining control-flow hole is narrower: JALR still resolves only in EX.\\nEvery return and indirect call therefore pays the wrong-path recovery cost\\neven when the target is stable from one execution of that JALR PC to the\\nnext. Earlier return predictors showed that JALR bubbles exist, but they put\\ncurrent-cycle compares or stack bookkeeping directly in IF. The successful\\nreplay predictor suggests a better timing shape: look up any target state one\\ncycle ahead, register a candidate for the current fetch PC, and keep the\\ncurrent IF cycle dependent only on that candidate plus the fetched\\ninstruction's opcode bits.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best predictor structure is the two-bank registered instruction\\nreplay table. It is the only predictor-family mechanism that has produced a\\nlarge CoreMark cycle win without putting a same-cycle table lookup on the IF\\ndecision path. Follow-up replay changes, direction tables, return stacks,\\nvictim buffers, skewed indexes, and cached static next-PC metadata mostly\\nlost more Fmax than they recovered.\\n\\nThe remaining control-flow hole is narrower: JALR still resolves only in EX.\\nEvery return and indirect call therefore pays the wrong-path recovery cost\\neven when the target is stable from one execution of that JALR PC to the\\nnext. Earlier return predictors showed that JALR bubbles exist, but they put\\ncurrent-cycle compares or stack bookkeeping directly in IF. The successful\\nreplay predictor suggests a better timing shape: look up any target state one\\ncycle ahead, register a candidate for the current fetch PC, and keep the\\ncurrent IF cycle dependent only on that candidate plus the fetched\\ninstruction's opcode bits.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small registered lookahead target predictor for legal JALR\\ninstructions. Use a direct-mapped table, for example eight entries indexed by\\nJALR PC bits, with valid bits, full or non-index PC tags, and a 32-bit\\nresolved target. The table is read using the same selected next-fetch PC that\\nalready drives the registered instruction replay lookahead candidate:\\nredirect target first, then predicted/fallthrough next PC when fetch\\nadvances, otherwise the held PC. The current IF cycle may use only the\\nregistered JALR target candidate for the current PC; it must not perform a\\nfresh target-table read on the fetch-ready path.\\n\\nIF predicts a JALR only when the fetched instruction is a legal JALR encoding\\n(`opcode=1100111`, `funct3=000`), the registered target candidate matches the\\ncurrent PC, and the stored target is word-aligned. The prediction uses the\\nexisting `predicted_taken` and `predicted_target` metadata path. Conditional\\nbranch prediction, direct-JAL prediction, instruction replay hits, live-imem\\npriority, and replay-miss PC holds remain exactly as in the current best\\ndesign.\\n\\nEX updates the target table when a valid non-trapping JALR advances into\\nEX/MEM, storing that JALR PC and its resolved aligned target. JALR redirect\\ngeneration changes from unconditional redirect to the same resolved-next-PC\\nmismatch repair used by predicted branches and direct JALs. Uncached or\\nincorrectly cached JALRs still redirect in EX because their carried predicted\\nnext PC is `pc+4` or the wrong target. Correctly cached JALRs avoid the\\nrecovery flush. If a JALR resolves as a misaligned-target trap, EX treats the\\nresolved next PC as `pc+4` and redirects there if IF had used a stale cached\\ntarget, preserving RVFI `pc_wdata` and the next-retired-PC relation.\\n\\nThis is deliberately not a branch-direction table, BTB for all control flow,\\nor return-address stack. It targets only repeated JALR PCs with a registered\\nlookahead candidate. A tag alias or dynamically changing JALR target can only\\ncost a normal EX redirect; it cannot weaken decoder legality, RVFI traps,\\nretirement order, memory ordering, or the NRET=2 IO contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped JALR target table and a registered lookahead\\ntarget candidate keyed by the same selected next fetch PC used for the\\ninstruction replay candidate. In the current IF cycle, predict only legal\\nJALR encodings whose registered candidate matches the current PC and has\\nan aligned target. Drive the existing `predicted_taken` and\\n`predicted_target` metadata for those JALRs, while preserving the current\\ntwo-bank replay predictor, live-imem priority, static backward-branch\\nprediction, direct-JAL prediction, redirect priority, and `fetch_ready`\\nsemantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit update metadata for valid, non-misaligned JALR instructions when\\nEX/MEM is allowed to advance: update valid, update PC, and resolved\\ntarget. Change JALR redirect handling to compare the resolved next PC\\nagainst the carried prediction, using `pc+4` as the resolved next PC for\\nmisaligned-target traps so stale predictions are repaired precisely.\\nPreserve branch resolution, direct-JAL handling, divider interlock,\\nforwarding, trap marking, and RVFI metadata propagation.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX-stage JALR target predictor update signals back to IF without\\nchanging the core module IO, RVFI NRET=2 field set, channel-0-only\\nretirement convention, strict `rvfi_order` sequence, pending-store-slot\\narbitration, banked instruction replay wiring, or channel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the IF/ID and ID/EX prediction metadata comments to document that\\n`predicted_taken` and `predicted_target` may also carry a registered\\nlookahead JALR target prediction. Do not change bundle widths,\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields,\\ntop-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped JALR target table and a registered lookahead\\ntarget candidate keyed by the same selected next fetch PC used for the\\ninstruction replay candidate. In the current IF cycle, predict only legal\\nJALR encodings whose registered candidate matches the current PC and has\\nan aligned target. Drive the existing `predicted_taken` and\\n`predicted_target` metadata for those JALRs, while preserving the current\\ntwo-bank replay predictor, live-imem priority, static backward-branch\\nprediction, direct-JAL prediction, redirect priority, and `fetch_ready`\\nsemantics.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit update metadata for valid, non-misaligned JALR instructions when\\nEX/MEM is allowed to advance: update valid, update PC, and resolved\\ntarget. Change JALR redirect handling to compare the resolved next PC\\nagainst the carried prediction, using `pc+4` as the resolved next PC for\\nmisaligned-target traps so stale predictions are repaired precisely.\\nPreserve branch resolution, direct-JAL handling, divider interlock,\\nforwarding, trap marking, and RVFI metadata propagation.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX-stage JALR target predictor update signals back to IF without\\nchanging the core module IO, RVFI NRET=2 field set, channel-0-only\\nretirement convention, strict `rvfi_order` sequence, pending-store-slot\\narbitration, banked instruction replay wiring, or channel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Clarify the IF/ID and ID/EX prediction metadata comments to document that\\n`predicted_taken` and `predicted_target` may also carry a registered\\nlookahead JALR target prediction. Do not change bundle widths,\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, RVFI fields,\\ntop-level IO, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 154.53, \"delta_pct\": -73.25, \"vs_baseline\": -48.67, \"fmax_mhz\": 52.99, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3429014, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6378, \"ff\": 2396, \"seeds\": [52.99, 52.58, 54.72], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n- Added an 8-entry direct-mapped JALR target predictor in `rtl/if_stage.sv`.\\n- The JALR table is indexed by `pc[4:2]`, stores a full 32-bit PC tag and a 32-bit target, and produces a registered lookahead candidate from the same selected next-fetch PC used by the replay predictor.\\n- IF now predicts only legal JALR encodings (`opcode=1100111`, `funct3=000`) when the registered candidate matches the current PC and the stored target is word-aligned.\\n- Added EX-to-IF JALR update wiring through `rtl/core.sv`.\\n- Changed `rtl/ex_stage.sv` jump redirect handling so JALR uses the same resolved-next-PC mismatch repair as predicted branches and direct JALs. Misaligned jump targets resolve to `pc+4`, which repairs stale JALR predictions while preserving RVFI `pc_wdata`.\\n- Clarified the prediction metadata comments in `rtl/core_pkg.sv`.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Used full 32-bit PC tags instead of non-index tags. This keeps the table simple, uses all update-PC bits cleanly under lint, and still leaves the table read on the registered lookahead side rather than the current IF decision path.\\n- Local `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not stage files in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` is a symlink to a protected shared checkout outside the writable roots. I ran the same script from a temporary writable mirror under `/tmp` instead.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The predictor is intentionally small and direct-mapped, so conflicting JALR PCs or changing indirect targets can still miss or mispredict. Those cases fall back to the normal EX redirect repair path.\\n- The full-PC table compare is in the lookahead-register input path, not the current fetch-ready decision path.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh`: blocked before checks by sandbox permission errors while removing staged files under the protected symlink target.\\n- Same `formal/run_all.sh` executed from a writable `/tmp` mirror: pass (`Formal: 105 passed, 0 failed`).\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T12:08:33.826174\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 24}\n74:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260428-068-r25s1\", \"title\": \"Single-Entry Last-Load Replay Latch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix kernels often reload nearby or identical words\\nacross short loops, so a single MRU word can catch some random dStall cases\\nwithout a table, replacement policy, associative search, or load-data path\\ninto EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current best core already has the structural wins that have survived the\\nFPGA gate: the one-deep pending-store slot hides some store backpressure, and\\nthe two-bank registered instruction replay predictor hides most random\\niStall bubbles. The remaining structural stalls are mainly data-side load\\nwaits. When a normal-RAM load reaches MEM and `io_dmemReady` is low, the\\nwhole in-order backend freezes even if the same aligned word was just read by\\nan earlier load.\\n\\nLarger load replay attempts proved that this opportunity is real because they\\nreduced CoreMark cycles, but their table lookups, tag compares, and store\\nupdate paths cost too much Fmax. The next structural bet should keep only the\\ncheapest useful form of that idea: one last-load replay entry. CoreMark's\\nstack, list, state, and matrix kernels often reload nearby or identical words\\nacross short loops, so a single MRU word can catch some random dStall cases\\nwithout a table, replacement policy, associative search, or load-data path\\ninto EX.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one normal-RAM last-load replay latch in the MEM stage. The latch holds a\\nvalid bit, one word-aligned byte address tag, and the raw 32-bit word returned\\nby the most recent accepted legal normal-RAM load. On `io_dmemReady=1`, loads\\nkeep using live `io_dmemRData` exactly as today and refresh the latch with\\ntheir aligned word address and raw data.\\n\\nOn `io_dmemReady=0`, a valid legal normal-RAM load may complete from the\\nlatch only when the pending store slot is not valid and the aligned word\\naddress matches the latch tag. A replayed load suppresses the external dmem\\nread, clears `mem_wait`, uses the cached raw word for sign/zero extension and\\nRVFI `mem_rdata`, and retires once through the normal channel-0 MEM/WB\\nboundary. Replay misses preserve the current dmem wait behavior exactly.\\n\\nKeep replay conservative. Disable it for MMIO, UART, timing markers,\\nnon-normal-RAM addresses, misaligned or trapping accesses, stores, and any\\ncycle where a pending store slot is valid. A legal normal-RAM store that\\nretires directly or enqueues into the pending slot should invalidate the\\nlatch on a matching word address; byte-merge updating is optional, but\\ninvalidation is the lower-timing-risk default. This prevents a later replayed\\nload from observing stale bytes.\\n\\nThis is not a cache and not a MEM-to-EX bypass. Cached data is consumed only\\nat the MEM retirement boundary, dependent younger instructions still use the\\nexisting load-use interlock, and all memory ordering behind the pending store\\nslot remains unchanged. The top-level IO, RVFI NRET=2 field set, channel-0\\nsingle-retire convention, strict `rvfi_order` sequence, channel-1 tie-off,\\nbanked IF replay predictor, hot/cold ALU encoding, divider interlock, branch\\nrecovery, timing-marker behavior, and decoder legality defaults remain\\nunchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_last_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry last-load replay latch with valid, word-address tag, and\\nraw 32-bit data state. Refresh it from accepted live normal-RAM loads,\\nproduce a hit/data result for the current MEM load address, and\\ninvalidate it on matching legal normal-RAM stores. Reset clears only the\\nvalid bit; tag and data payloads remain ignored until valid.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the last-load replay hit into load handling. Select cached raw\\ndata only when `dmem_ready` is low, the current access is a valid legal\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot is valid, and the latch tag\\nmatches the aligned word address. On a hit, suppress `dmem_ren`, clear\\n`mem_wait`, and drive load extraction plus RVFI `mem_rdata` from the\\ncached word. Keep misses and all nonbufferable, trapping, misaligned,\\nstore, MMIO, UART, and marker accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the optional `load_last_replay` module if it is instantiated outside\\n`mem_stage`; otherwise no top-level wiring should be needed. Preserve the\\nexisting pending-store-slot dmem arbitration, banked IF replay wiring,\\ncore module IO, RVFI channel layout, strict channel-0 order behavior, and\\nchannel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if useful to document the\\nlast-load replay eligibility and raw-word metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, ALU op\\nencodings, RVFI field widths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/load_last_replay.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry last-load replay latch with valid, word-address tag, and\\nraw 32-bit data state. Refresh it from accepted live normal-RAM loads,\\nproduce a hit/data result for the current MEM load address, and\\ninvalidate it on matching legal normal-RAM stores. Reset clears only the\\nvalid bit; tag and data payloads remain ignored until valid.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Integrate the last-load replay hit into load handling. Select cached raw\\ndata only when `dmem_ready` is low, the current access is a valid legal\\nnormal-RAM load, no pending store slot is valid, and the latch tag\\nmatches the aligned word address. On a hit, suppress `dmem_ren`, clear\\n`mem_wait`, and drive load extraction plus RVFI `mem_rdata` from the\\ncached word. Keep misses and all nonbufferable, trapping, misaligned,\\nstore, MMIO, UART, and marker accesses on the existing wait path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the optional `load_last_replay` module if it is instantiated outside\\n`mem_stage`; otherwise no top-level wiring should be needed. Preserve the\\nexisting pending-store-slot dmem arbitration, banked IF replay wiring,\\ncore module IO, RVFI channel layout, strict channel-0 order behavior, and\\nchannel-1 tie-off.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only comments or compact helper typedefs if useful to document the\\nlast-load replay eligibility and raw-word metadata. Do not change\\narchitectural state semantics, decoder legality defaults, ALU op\\nencodings, RVFI field widths, or the NRET=2 contract.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 529.39, \"delta_pct\": -8.37, \"vs_baseline\": 75.85, \"fmax_mhz\": 181.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 3e-06, \"cycles\": 3433982, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5916, \"ff\": 2227, \"seeds\": [180.93, 193.35, 181.79], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Implementation Notes\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n- Added `rtl/load_last_replay.sv`, a one-entry last-load replay latch with a valid bit, word-address tag, and raw 32-bit data payload.\\n- Integrated the latch inside `rtl/mem_stage.sv`; no top-level `core` IO or RVFI channel wiring changed.\\n- Normal-RAM loads refresh the latch only when the live dmem port is actually usable: legal, aligned, non-trapping, address `< 0x0010_0000`, no pending store slot, and `dmem_ready=1`.\\n- When `dmem_ready=0`, a legal normal-RAM load may retire from the latch only if the pending-store slot is invalid and the word tag matches. On hit, `dmem_ren` is suppressed, `mem_wait` is cleared, and both load extraction and RVFI `mem_rdata` use the cached raw word.\\n- Legal normal-RAM stores invalidate a matching replay entry when they retire directly or enqueue into the pending-store slot.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n\\n- Store byte-merge updating was not implemented; matching stores invalidate the latch conservatively as proposed.\\n- The helper uses a 30-bit word-address input rather than a 32-bit byte address to avoid unused low-bit logic and lint warnings.\\n\\n## Concerns\\n\\n- The direct command `bash formal/run_all.sh` could not stage files in this sandbox because `formal/riscv-formal` points outside the writable worktree area and its core staging directory rejects writes. The same script was run from a `/tmp` mirror of the current RTL and formal harness instead.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv rtl/*.sv`: pass.\\n- Formal self-check from `/tmp/llr_formal.g2j1qf` using the same `formal/run_all.sh`: pass, `105 passed, 0 failed`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-04-28T13:30:43.017656\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 25}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
