{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    e=json.loads(line)\n    print({k:e.get(k) for k in ('id','title','category','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles')})\n    if e.get('id')=='hyp-20260905-002-r3s1':\n        print('HYPOTHESIS:',e.get('hypothesis'))\n        print('IMPLEMENTATION NOTES:',e.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{'id': 'baseline-bench-609cf36', 'title': 'Baseline retest for cores/bench', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 282.82, 'fmax_mhz': 127.03, 'cycles': 4491485}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-001-r1s0', 'title': 'Balance register-file reads with shared one-hot address decoding', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 284.34, 'fmax_mhz': 127.71, 'cycles': 4491485}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-002-r1s1', 'title': 'Single pending-store slot with direct zero-wait completion', 'category': 'structural', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 300.35, 'fmax_mhz': 133.89, 'cycles': 4457730}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-003-r1s2', 'title': 'Small tagged target predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis', 'category': 'predictor', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 319.45, 'fmax_mhz': 134.68, 'cycles': 4215937}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-001-r2s0', 'title': 'Register one-hot ALU operation selects before execute', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 306.93, 'fmax_mhz': 129.4, 'cycles': 4215937}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-002-r2s1', 'title': 'Split execution into a fast ALU and an eight-cycle divider', 'category': 'structural', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 320.28, 'fmax_mhz': 135.03, 'cycles': 4215937}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-003-r2s2', 'title': 'Two-way target prediction at unchanged 16-entry capacity', 'category': 'predictor', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 296.07, 'fmax_mhz': 124.94, 'cycles': 4220017}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-001-r3s0', 'title': 'Localize predictor training to remove indexed feedback muxes', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 203.44, 'fmax_mhz': 85.77, 'cycles': 4215937}\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-002-r3s1', 'title': 'Pipeline resolution feedback with registered predictor-row reads', 'category': 'structural', 'outcome': 'regression', 'fitness': 340.22, 'fmax_mhz': 143.41, 'cycles': 4215262}\nHYPOTHESIS: {'motivation': \"The current bench winner reaches 320.28 iter/s at 135.03 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1 implementation notes identify the predictor's\\nindexed tag lookup through counter-write control as the critical path\\nin all three FPGA seeds; the median path is 7.41 ns. Removing nearly\\nhalf the LUTs with a separate divider improved fitness only 0.26%,\\nwith unchanged CoreMark cycles. The other prior structural attempt,\\nhyp-20260905-002-r1s1, saved only 0.75% of cycles by buffering stores.\\nThese results favor splitting the measured feedback path over another\\nbackend resource change. LESSONS.md also records the timing regression\\nfrom two-way prediction, despite essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nIn the present if_stage.sv, one cycle contains an indexed table read,\\na full-tag comparison, saturating-counter decisions, and table write\\ncontrol. Registering the selected row before comparison creates a\\nstructural pipeline boundary in this read/modify/write transaction.\\nThe prediction algorithm, capacity, instruction pipeline, and immediate\\nEX recovery can retain their existing organization.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Split resolved-instruction feedback into a row-read stage and an update\\nstage inside if_stage. Retain exactly sixteen direct-mapped entries,\\nPC[5:2] indexing, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, and the\\nexisting two-bit counter policy. The single architectural change is\\nadding one register boundary to the training transaction; do not add\\nan instruction pipeline stage or change execution-resource scheduling.\\n\\nOn edge N with resolve_valid asserted, capture the resolution's index,\\nPC tag, target, legal/branch/jump/taken flags, and the selected row's\\nvalid, tag, and two-bit counter into a maintenance register. Capture\\nraw row state here, not an already-computed tag hit or counter update:\\nthe indexed read must end at this register. The existing target need\\nnot be read for training because neither hit detection nor counter\\narithmetic consumes it. Carry a separate maintenance-valid bit.\\n\\nDuring the following cycle, compare the captured tags and compute the\\nrow update solely from the captured event and row state. Commit it on\\nedge N+1. A legal jump writes its full target and counter 11. A legal\\ntaken branch writes its target, allocates counter 10 on a miss, and\\nincrements with saturation on a hit. A legal not-taken branch only\\ndecrements a matching counter with saturation, retaining the target\\nand allocating nothing on a miss. An illegal, trapping, or non-control\\nresolution invalidates only a matching tag. Preserve the existing\\nfield-specific write enables and full architectural address widths.\\nIn particular, no live indexed tag lookup may feed the update-stage\\nwrite enable, or the proposed timing cut would disappear.\\n\\nSustain one resolution per cycle by committing the older transaction\\nwhile accepting the next. Handle their read-after-write hazard with\\nexplicit maintenance forwarding: when the committing transaction and\\nincoming resolution address the same index, capture the older\\ntransaction's post-update valid/tag/counter as the incoming row\\nsnapshot instead of the pre-edge table values. Forward the complete\\nlogical state of those fields, including invalidation, allocation,\\nsaturation, and unchanged fields. Compare indices for this bypass,\\nnot tags: different PCs aliasing one row require forwarding too.\\nThis bypass is confined to the maintenance pipeline. After draining,\\ntable state must equal applying the accepted resolution stream in\\norder under the original update policy; no event may be lost or\\ncounted twice, including consecutive updates to the same branch.\\n\\nSet maintenance-valid from resolve_valid every non-reset edge, rather\\nthan holding it on a frontend or backend stall. A previously accepted\\nevent commits once even if IF is stalled, a later divide is waiting,\\nor a younger instruction redirects. Acceptance still uses the existing\\nadvancing-EX resolve_valid qualification; held EX instructions cannot\\nenqueue repeatedly. Reset has priority over both capture and commit,\\nclears all table valid bits and maintenance-valid, and cannot permit\\na pre-reset transaction to repopulate the predictor afterward.\\n\\nFetch continues to consult committed table state only. There is no\\npending-update bypass to predicted_next_pc, no training backpressure,\\nand no extra delay on the EX actual-next-PC comparison or redirect.\\nPreserve the exact prediction accepted with each instruction through\\nID/EX and retain resolved architectural pc_next at RVFI retirement.\\nRecovery still overrides imem stalls, and dmem/divide holds keep their\\ncurrent priorities. Training becomes visible one cycle later than\\ntoday; a very short cold loop can therefore mispredict an additional\\ntime. Warm self-jumps must still sustain one retirement per cycle,\\nand each recovery retains its existing single younger-slot bubble.\\n\\nExpect a 6% fitness improvement, with medium confidence, from separating\\nthe table-read mux from tag comparison and update write control. This\\nis an estimate, not a measured result. The maintenance bypass could\\nbecome critical, delayed training can increase cycles, and fetch or\\nEX recovery may set the new timing limit. Evaluate the unchanged\\nmarker-bracketed stalled CoreMark workload and three-seed median Fmax;\\nreport exact iterations/cycles, LUT/FF usage, and post-route critical\\npaths. Compare the fitness product with 320.28 using the same workload\\nconfiguration, identifying the configuration in the results. Preserve\\nnret=1 and every external port, all RVFI order/operand/trap rules,\\ndecoder legality, divider and ALTOPS behavior, memory handshakes and\\nbounds, and the required CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. All formal\\nconfigurations, test harnesses, and evaluation gates remain intact.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 6, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'description': 'Insert the registered row-read and resolution-event stage ahead\\nof tag comparison and counter-update generation. Implement one\\ncommit and one capture per cycle with same-index post-update\\nforwarding of valid/tag/counter, field-specific table writes,\\nexact event validity, and reset cancellation. Keep fetch lookup,\\nprediction policy, module ports, PC advancement, and immediate\\nrecovery behavior as specified. Document the two-edge training\\nschedule and keep the maintenance bypass off the fetch path.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_if_stage.py', 'description': 'Extend the existing behavior checks with a sequential reference\\nmodel and cycle-exact capture/commit assertions. Update observation\\ntiming for the intentional extra training stage while retaining\\nevery existing policy, full-address, reset, and no-fetch-bypass\\nassertion. Test one accepted event per clock, same-PC counter\\nsaturation and threshold crossings, different-tag same-index\\nallocation, invalidation followed by allocation, not-taken misses,\\nno-op updates, different indices, idle drain, and reset with a\\npending update. Check predictions at each edge and final table\\nbehavior after draining a randomized resolution stream.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Add directed cold and warm self-jumps, short loops, alternating\\nsame-index control-flow PCs, changing JALR targets, and stale-code\\ninvalidations to exercise the added feedback latency and forwarding.\\nAssert sustained warm one-per-cycle retirement and the unchanged\\nredirect bubble length, with explicit expectations for extra cold\\nmisses. Cover a pending update draining during imem/dmem stalls,\\ndivide waits, redirects, and reset; prove exactly-once training\\nfor held EX instructions. Retain all RVFI order, PC chain, operand,\\ntrap, wrong-path-store, memory, and arithmetic assertions, and run\\nthe unchanged full cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA evaluation gates.\\n'}]}\nIMPLEMENTATION NOTES: # Registered predictor maintenance: hyp-20260905-002-r3s1\n\nImplemented the proposed training pipeline. All local evaluation gates pass.\nThe unchanged gate reports **340.22 iter/s**, with **143.41 MHz** three-seed\nmedian Fmax, **5,920 LUT4s**, and **2,382 FFs**. Using the unrounded fitness\nproduct, the improvement over the supplied 320.28 is **6.2245848777%**.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Only `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` changes hardware. At edge N it captures\n  the resolution index, full 26-bit tag, aligned 30-bit target, four event\n  flags, and raw selected-row valid/tag/counter. It does not capture a hit\n  result, a computed counter, or the old target.\n- Captured tags and counter drive the following cycle's update. Edge N+1\n  commits it while capturing the next event. Update write enables have no\n  live indexed tag/counter input. Valid, tag/target, and counter writes\n  retain the original field-specific policy, including saturated no-ops.\n- Same-index maintenance forwarding captures the committing transaction's\n  complete post-update valid/tag/counter, including unchanged fields,\n  invalidation, and replacement by another full tag. This bypass never\n  reaches fetch prediction.\n- Maintenance-valid is assigned from resolve-valid on every non-reset edge.\n  IF/backend holds and younger redirects do not hold or cancel accepted\n  training. Reset cancels capture and commit, clears maintenance-valid and\n  all table valids, and leaves unused payloads unreset.\n- Capacity, indexing, tag/target widths, prediction algorithm, EX resolution\n  qualification and immediate recovery, instruction-stage count, execution\n  scheduling, nret=1, external ports, RVFI, decoder, divider, and ALTOPS are\n  unchanged. No architectural deviation from the hypothesis.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `test_if_stage.py`: independent sequential policy model checked before\n  and after edges, exact committed table fields and captured raw snapshots,\n  one event per edge, counter saturation/thresholds, target retention,\n  different-tag same-index replacement, invalidation/allocation, misses,\n  no-ops, different indices, idle drain, full addresses, no fetch bypass,\n  and reset cancellation. A deterministic 1,200-cycle random stream includes\n  aliases, bubbles, stalls, redirects, and resets, followed by a full probe.\n- `test_pipeline.py`: applies the model to every existing pipeline test and\n  checks redirects against the prediction actually accepted with the\n  instruction. Adds short branch loops and pending-update drain during\n  imem stalls, dmem holds, divide waits, overlapping redirects, and reset.\n  Existing JALR target changes, stale code/traps, held-EX exactly-once\n  checks, operand/order/PC-chain checks, wrong-path stores, memory, and\n  arithmetic assertions remain active.\n- Intentional latency expectation changes: self-jumps have two cold misses,\n  each followed by one younger-slot bubble; warm retirement remains one\n  per cycle. Alternating full-tag aliases can hit an older committed row\n  before its pending replacement commits, so that test now asserts the\n  exact delayed-update prediction/redirect sequence instead of all misses.\n\n## Local validation\n\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings/errors.\n- Full cocotb suite: **7 pytest runners passed**, including **38 pipeline\n  tests**, **4 IF tests**, and both real-arithmetic and ALTOPS ALU builds.\n- Local formal status: **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, using the unchanged\n  `bash formal/run_all.sh`, `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl`, `CORE_NAME=bench`,\n  `JOBS=6`; automatic nret=1 selection chose `wrapper_si.sv` and\n  `checks_si.cfg`. The first shell invocation exited before generation\n  because its Python lacked PyYAML; selecting the installed conda Python\n  resolved this. First actual proof run passed; no counterexample and no\n  RTL fix/retry for formal. Formal-deep was not run; arithmetic is unchanged.\n- Unchanged cosim evaluator: **PASS, 2 ELFs** (selftest full RVFI comparison\n  and stalled CoreMark CRC/UART validation).\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench`, with `NRET=1` and\n  `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: PASS, all three seeds routed and benchmark\n  validation completed. `git diff --check`: PASS.\n- Formal, cosim, and FPGA run in an isolated `$TMPDIR` copy; 48 evaluated\n  RTL/config/harness/source files were byte-compared with the worktree.\n  No protected harness, source, configuration, or other core was edited.\n\n## Workload configuration and exact performance\n\nThe checked-in `bench/programs/Makefile` defaults to `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000`,\n`-O3`, and the evaluator's CRC goldens are for that 2 KB configuration.\nThis conflicts with the prompt's 6 KB description. The gates were preserved.\nBoth sizes were measured separately using the unchanged harness,\n`ITERATIONS=10`, `--bench --istall --dstall`, default stall seed\n`0xDEADBEEF`, and only the `0x10000100` start / `0x10000104` stop bracket.\nThe original HEAD RTL was rebuilt in scratch to measure baseline cycles.\nBaseline Fmax and resource counts below are the supplied winner's values;\nbaseline FPGA routing was not repeated.\n\n| Configuration | Original RTL cycles | New RTL cycles | Original iter/s at 135.03 MHz | New iter/s at 143.41 MHz |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Unchanged gate: 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 4,215,937 | 4,215,262 | 320.2846721856 | 340.2161004464 |\n| Supplemental: 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 30,823,120 | 30,822,790 | 43.8080246257 | 46.5272611597 |\n\nThe 2 KB baseline reproduces **320.28** under the same configuration.\nThe new exact throughput is `10 / 4215262 = 2.372331779139707e-6`\niterations/cycle; fitness is `143410000 * 10 / 4215262`.\nIt saves 675 bracketed cycles. The 6 KB result saves 330 cycles and improves\nthe same-configuration fitness by **6.2071653704%**; comparing its absolute\nscore directly with 320.28 would mix workloads.\n\nThe primary gate validates its unchanged 2 KB goldens: seedcrc=e9f5,\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\nThe supplemental 6 KB run explicitly checked the requested crclist=d4b0,\ncrcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, plus seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949.\nBoth baseline and candidate supplemental runs reached EBREAK, had\n`oob=false`, reported 10 iterations and the literal\n`Correct operation validated.`, and contained valid timing markers.\nFor new 6 KB RTL, start=53,747 and stop=30,876,537. The old stop was\n30,876,867 with the same start. The UART's compiler-flags string remains\nthe inherited hardcoded `-O2 -march=rv32im`; the actual build command is\n`-O3`. The UART size field is per algorithm (666 for 2 KB, 2000 for 6 KB).\n\n## Three-seed post-route timing\n\nDevice: `GW2A-LV18QN88C8/I7`, family `GW2A-18C`, unchanged Tang Nano 20K\nconstraints. These are final post-route reports, not placement estimates.\n\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Critical path ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 143.41 | 6.97 | 3.95 | 3.02 |\n| 2 | 137.51 | 7.27 | 2.32 | 4.95 |\n| 3 | 155.74 | 6.42 | 2.32 | 4.10 |\n\n- Seed 1: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_175.Q` (`cpu.id_ex_w[54]`) through\n  hazard/control logic to `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_63.D`.\n- Seed 2: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_161.Q` (`cpu.id_ex_w[11]`) through\n  hazard/divide-wait control to `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_71.RESET`.\n- Seed 3: same source and hazard/divide-wait control class as seed 2,\n  ending at `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_98.RESET`.\n\nMedian Fmax increases from 135.03 to 143.41 MHz. The median critical path\nfalls from the supplied 7.41 ns to 6.97 ns. None of the three final\ncritical paths crosses the predictor indexed maintenance lookup.\nAll seeds use 5,920 LUT4s and 2,382 DFFs, versus 5,871 / 2,292 supplied\nfor the winner: **+49 LUTs, +90 FFs**. The maintenance payload contains\n94 source-level bits before synthesis optimization.\n\n## Concerns and evidence\n\nThe intended extra cold self-loop miss remains a performance cost, and\nalias timing changes can help or hurt particular streams. Observed cycle\nchanges also include the deterministic stall generator's changed phase.\nMaintenance forwarding is combinational but was not the reported timing\nlimit in any seed. Hazard/control routing is the new measured limit;\nthis change intentionally does not alter it.\n\nScratch evidence directory:\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-r3-validation-3dg8_bd1/`\n\nIt contains `formal-gate.log`, `cocotb-gate.log`,\n`cosim-gate-result.log`, `fpga-gate.log`, `comparison.json`, the raw\n`baseline-2k.jsonl`, `baseline-6k.jsonl`, `candidate-6k.jsonl`, and\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` with the complete\npost-route paths. Implementation notes are at the worktree root as\nrequested by instruction 5 and consumed by the orchestrator's notes reader.\n\n{'id': 'hyp-20260905-003-r3s2', 'title': 'Row-local predictor metadata training to remove the indexed feedback path', 'category': 'predictor', 'outcome': 'improvement', 'fitness': 350.46, 'fmax_mhz': 147.75, 'cycles': 4215937}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
