{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\":\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|forward|bypass|register.file|operand|execute|pipeline' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Balance register-file reads with shared one-hot address decoding\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The bench register file implements two asynchronous reads using variable\\narray indexing, followed by write-first bypass and x0 selection. These\\n32-bit read paths feed ID/EX every cycle and expose wide address-controlled\\nmultiplexers to FPGA synthesis. Shared address decoding and parallel data\\nselection offer a localized opportunity to improve timing without adding\\npipeline cycles. The only recorded bench experiment is the baseline retest:\\n127.03 MHz median Fmax, 9563 LUT4s, 4491485 bracketed CoreMark cycles, and\\n282.82 iterations/sec. LESSONS.md is absent and there are no prior read-path\\nattempts in the bench log. No detailed timing report is available, so the\\nregister file is a candidate bottleneck, not a confirmed critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench register file implements two asynchronous reads using variable\\narray indexing, followed by write-first bypass and x0 selection. These\\n32-bit read paths feed ID/EX every cycle and expose wide address-controlled\\nmultiplexers to FPGA synthesis. Shared address decoding and parallel data\\nselection offer a localized opportunity to improve timing without adding\\npipeline cycles. The only recorded bench experiment is the baseline retest:\\n127.03 MHz median Fmax, 9563 LUT4s, 4491485 bracketed CoreMark cycles, and\\n282.82 iterations/sec. LESSONS.md is absent and there are no prior read-path\\nattempts in the bench log. No detailed timing report is available, so the\\nregister file is a candidate bottleneck, not a confirmed critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite only the combinational read implementation in rtl/reg_file.sv.\\nFor each read port, decode its five-bit address once into mutually exclusive\\nselects for x1 through x31, shared across all 32 data bits. AND-mask each\\nstored word with its select and combine the terms with an explicitly\\nbalanced OR reduction tree rather than a serial accumulation or a priority\\nmux. Supply a zero leaf for x0 so reading x0 always returns zero.\\n\\nInclude the existing write-first bypass as an exclusive parallel data term:\\nbypass = w_en && (read_addr != 0) && (w_addr == read_addr). When bypass is\\ntrue, suppress all stored-word selects and select w_data; otherwise select\\nexactly the addressed stored word. Preserve the current synchronous write\\nand reset behavior, two independent combinational read ports, module ports,\\nand all architectural register values. Add no pipeline state or latency.\\n\\nThe intended benefit is less serial mux logic on the register-file read\\npaths and better sharing of address decode logic across data bits. With\\nunchanged cycle-level behavior, any median Fmax gain translates directly\\ninto the same percentage fitness gain. The estimate is 5 percent with\\nmedium confidence; decode fanout, routing congestion, or a critical path\\nelsewhere could erase the gain. Validate existing register-file and\\npipeline tests, formal and stalled cosim, then compare the unchanged\\nthree-seed FPGA fitness evaluation against 282.82. Preserve nret=1, all\\nRVFI and bus-handshake semantics, decoder legality, and every eval check.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the two indexed-array read muxes and serial read selection with\\nper-port shared one-hot address decoders, masked data terms, and an\\nexplicitly balanced OR tree. Make write-first bypass an exclusive\\nparallel term, omitting x0 from both stored-data and bypass selection.\\nPreserve storage, synchronous reset and write behavior, and zero-cycle\\nreads; do not change any other datapath or pipeline mechanism.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Extend functional coverage with distinct patterns in all writable\\nregisters and all 32-by-32 read-address pairs on the two ports. Check\\nsimultaneous bypass on both ports, bypass on either port while the\\nother reads a different stored value, w_en=0 matches, attempted x0\\nwrites with both ports reading x0, and reset after nonzero contents.\\nCheck bypass before the write clock edge and stored results afterward\\nusing simulator-safe sampling. Preserve all existing assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the two indexed-array read muxes and serial read selection with\\nper-port shared one-hot address decoders, masked data terms, and an\\nexplicitly balanced OR tree. Make write-first bypass an exclusive\\nparallel term, omitting x0 from both stored-data and bypass selection.\\nPreserve storage, synchronous reset and write behavior, and zero-cycle\\nreads; do not change any other datapath or pipeline mechanism.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Extend functional coverage with distinct patterns in all writable\\nregisters and all 32-by-32 read-address pairs on the two ports. Check\\nsimultaneous bypass on both ports, bypass on either port while the\\nother reads a different stored value, w_en=0 matches, attempted x0\\nwrites with both ports reading x0, and reset after nonzero contents.\\nCheck bypass before the write clock edge and stored results afterward\\nusing simulator-safe sampling. Preserve all existing assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 284.34, \"delta_pct\": 0.54, \"vs_baseline\": 0.54, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.71, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9488, \"ff\": 1855, \"seeds\": [118.76, 127.71, 130.24], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T13:58:48.671185Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1}\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Single pending-store slot with direct zero-wait completion\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"In cores/bench, hazard_unit freezes IF, ID, and EX whenever a memory\\ninstruction in EX/MEM encounters io_dmemReady=0. An aligned RAM store\\nalready has its address, data, and byte enables at this point, so retaining\\nthose fields separately can release the pipeline to execute younger\\narithmetic while the write waits for the bus. This directly targets the\\napproximately 22% random data-bus backpressure used for CoreMark fitness.\\n\\nBench has no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the baseline:\\n4,491,485 timed cycles and 127.03 MHz median Fmax, yielding 282.82 iter/s.\\nRead-only reference evidence from cores/v1, hyp-20260428-017-r8s1, supports\\na single pending store with a direct ready path: it passed formal/cosim\\nand improved fitness 5.89% on that different design. Its cycle saving was\\nunder 1%, and placement contributed to the result, so that improvement\\nmust not be assumed for bench. The main risk is losing more Fmax to bus\\narbitration than the saved stall cycles recover.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"In cores/bench, hazard_unit freezes IF, ID, and EX whenever a memory\\ninstruction in EX/MEM encounters io_dmemReady=0. An aligned RAM store\\nalready has its address, data, and byte enables at this point, so retaining\\nthose fields separately can release the pipeline to execute younger\\narithmetic while the write waits for the bus. This directly targets the\\napproximately 22% random data-bus backpressure used for CoreMark fitness.\\n\\nBench has no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the baseline:\\n4,491,485 timed cycles and 127.03 MHz median Fmax, yielding 282.82 iter/s.\\nRead-only reference evidence from cores/v1, hyp-20260428-017-r8s1, supports\\na single pending store with a direct ready path: it passed formal/cosim\\nand improved fitness 5.89% on that different design. Its cycle saving was\\nunder 1%, and placement contributed to the result, so that improvement\\nmust not be assumed for bench. The main risk is losing more Fmax to bus\\narbitration than the saved stall cycles recover.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decouple store retirement from bus completion with exactly one pending\\nstore slot. Allocate it only for a valid, legal, naturally aligned store\\nwhose complete access lies in normal RAM [0x00000000, 0x00100000), when\\nthe slot is empty and io_dmemReady=0. Capture the effective byte address,\\nexisting replicated write data, and byte mask atomically. Advance that\\ninstruction into MEM/WB once, carrying its original PC, instruction,\\noperands, and aligned RVFI memory fields. Its eventual bus drain produces\\nno additional retirement. The slot holds only the physical write request;\\nRVFI metadata continues along the existing pipeline.\\n\\nWith the slot empty and io_dmemReady=1, loads and stores use the existing\\ndirect MEM path without an enqueue cycle. While the slot is valid, give\\nits request sole ownership of dmem, hold address/data/byte enables stable\\nuntil a ready handshake, and suppress any younger external request.\\nDrain independently of frontend stalls and redirects. Permit younger\\nnon-memory instructions to advance and retire in order while it drains.\\nBlock every younger load or store until the slot is empty, including the\\ncycle that drains the old request; omit simultaneous drain/refill,\\ncoalescing, and store-to-load forwarding to keep arbitration small.\\n\\nAll MMIO, UART, start/stop marker accesses, and out-of-range addresses\\nremain on the existing unbuffered path. Never use buffering to hide an\\nout-of-bounds access from the existing checks. FENCE/FENCE.I, EBREAK,\\nand trapping instructions must also wait for an older pending store to\\ndrain. Marker writes complete only on their real bus handshake, after\\nprior writes, so the timed region cannot exclude undrained work. Invalid,\\nillegal, and misaligned stores never enqueue or drive a write. Redirects\\ncannot discard an already accepted older store; reset clears slot validity.\\n\\nReplace the hazard unit's raw dmem-stall predicate with a MEM-generated\\nmem_wait signal describing whether the current EX/MEM instruction can\\nadvance. A pending store alone must not assert mem_wait for arithmetic.\\nOn a real wait, preserve the existing EX/MEM and ID/EX holds and the\\nMEM/WB behavior that clears valid but retains forwarding data. Derive\\nmem_wait from current registered state, instruction classification, and\\nbus readiness, without a combinational dependency on hold_wb. Keep the\\ndirect zero-wait path short and retain the existing EX datapath and\\nload-use interlock. Preserve nret=1, all top-level ports, strict channel-0\\nretirement order, decoder legality, and every existing correctness gate.\\n\\nSuccess requires a measured fitness improvement over 282.82 using the\\nunchanged marker-bracketed CoreMark run and three-seed median Fmax. The\\nestimate is a 2% fitness gain from overlapping store waits with useful\\nexecution while keeping Fmax close to baseline; it is not a measured\\nresult. Verify the pending path with directed and random bus stalls,\\nsince a ready-tied-high formal configuration cannot exercise allocation.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry request register with valid, enqueue, address, data,\\nmask, and ready/drain controls. Retain its payload until one accepted\\nwrite, disallow replacement while occupied, and clear valid on reset.\\nDo not retain a second copy of the RVFI retirement bundle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept dmem readiness and pending-slot state. Classify valid aligned\\nRAM stores for enqueue, retain direct ready completion, and emit\\nexplicit mem_wait plus slot enqueue fields. Enqueue and advance the\\noriginal store into MEM/WB on the same edge. Serialize subsequent\\nmemory operations, fences, EBREAK, and traps behind the slot. Keep\\neach live instruction's RVFI masks/data separate from the draining\\nrequest and retain the forwarding payload when MEM/WB valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the slot, route enqueue and mem_wait controls, and give\\nthe pending request priority over the live MEM dmem outputs only\\nwhile slot-valid is set. Suppress live read/write enables during a\\ndrain. Preserve the core's existing single-channel RVFI and bus ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use mem_wait instead of !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op to drive the\\nexisting backend hold controls. Preserve load-use, instruction-bus,\\nredirect priorities, and MEM/WB forwarding retention, while allowing\\narithmetic to progress when only a buffered store is waiting.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Include store_slot.sv in the pipeline source list. Add a handshake-\\naccurate stalled-bus test mode without removing existing assertions.\\nCheck SW/SH/SB enqueue and drain, stable requests, one physical write\\nand one RVFI retirement per store, and arithmetic progress during a\\nprolonged store wait. Cover subsequent same/different-address loads,\\nconsecutive stores, load-use forwarding across a hold, imem stalls,\\nredirects, fences, EBREAK, reset, illegal/misaligned stores, and UART\\nand timing markers behind a pending store. Assert strict RVFI order,\\ncorrect byte data, and that serialization prevents premature markers.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry request register with valid, enqueue, address, data,\\nmask, and ready/drain controls. Retain its payload until one accepted\\nwrite, disallow replacement while occupied, and clear valid on reset.\\nDo not retain a second copy of the RVFI retirement bundle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept dmem readiness and pending-slot state. Classify valid aligned\\nRAM stores for enqueue, retain direct ready completion, and emit\\nexplicit mem_wait plus slot enqueue fields. Enqueue and advance the\\noriginal store into MEM/WB on the same edge. Serialize subsequent\\nmemory operations, fences, EBREAK, and traps behind the slot. Keep\\neach live instruction's RVFI masks/data separate from the draining\\nrequest and retain the forwarding payload when MEM/WB valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the slot, route enqueue and mem_wait controls, and give\\nthe pending request priority over the live MEM dmem outputs only\\nwhile slot-valid is set. Suppress live read/write enables during a\\ndrain. Preserve the core's existing single-channel RVFI and bus ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use mem_wait instead of !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op to drive the\\nexisting backend hold controls. Preserve load-use, instruction-bus,\\nredirect priorities, and MEM/WB forwarding retention, while allowing\\narithmetic to progress when only a buffered store is waiting.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Include store_slot.sv in the pipeline source list. Add a handshake-\\naccurate stalled-bus test mode without removing existing assertions.\\nCheck SW/SH/SB enqueue and drain, stable requests, one physical write\\nand one RVFI retirement per store, and arithmetic progress during a\\nprolonged store wait. Cover subsequent same/different-address loads,\\nconsecutive stores, load-use forwarding across a hold, imem stalls,\\nredirects, fences, EBREAK, reset, illegal/misaligned stores, and UART\\nand timing markers behind a pending store. Assert strict RVFI order,\\ncorrect byte data, and that serialization prevents premature markers.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 300.35, \"delta_pct\": 6.2, \"vs_baseline\": 6.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.89, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4457730, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10274, \"ff\": 1869, \"seeds\": [133.89, 139.94, 124.38], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T14:03:28.213389Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 (regression, +6.20%): With `mem_wait` blocking younger loads/stores through the pending store's drain cycle, CoreMark cycles fell only 0.75%, so the fitness gain came mainly from higher median Fmax (127.03 to 133.89 MHz).\"}\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Small tagged target predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core always fetches PC+4 and redirects in EX for every taken\\nconditional branch, JAL, and JALR. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so\\neach such redirect discards one younger issue slot, not two registered\\nfrontend stages. Predicting recurring taken transfers can remove that\\nbubble. A small PC-indexed target table avoids adding instruction-memory\\ndecode and a branch-immediate adder to the fetch-to-PC path. The target\\nhas no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the 282.82 baseline;\\nthere is no recorded predictor attempt to repeat or rule out.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench core always fetches PC+4 and redirects in EX for every taken\\nconditional branch, JAL, and JALR. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so\\neach such redirect discards one younger issue slot, not two registered\\nfrontend stages. Predicting recurring taken transfers can remove that\\nbubble. A small PC-indexed target table avoids adding instruction-memory\\ndecode and a branch-immediate adder to the fetch-to-PC path. The target\\nhas no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the 282.82 baseline;\\nthere is no recorded predictor attempt to repeat or rule out.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one 16-entry direct-mapped branch target predictor inside if_stage.\\nIndex by PC[5:2]; each entry stores valid, the full remaining PC tag\\nPC[31:6], an aligned target[31:2], and a two-bit saturating counter.\\nA valid tag hit with counter[1] set predicts the stored target; otherwise\\npredict PC+4. This is 944 bits of table state before synthesis. Use a\\ncombinational lookup and synchronous updates, with no same-cycle update\\nbypass. Reset all valid bits and mask invalid payloads from predictions.\\nDo not specialize addresses or instruction sequences to CoreMark.\\n\\nTrain exactly once when a valid EX instruction advances: in.valid &&\\n!stall && !reset. A legal, nontrapping taken conditional branch allocates\\na missing entry at 2'b10 or increments a matching entry, and writes its\\nresolved aligned target. A legal JAL/JALR writes its resolved target and\\ncounter 2'b11; JALR uses the last resolved target without a return stack.\\nA legal not-taken conditional branch decrements a matching counter without\\nreplacing its target and does not allocate on a miss. Invalidate a matching\\nentry when that PC resolves to a non-control instruction or a trap. Keep\\nprediction entirely advisory and preserve full architectural addresses.\\n\\nCarry the exact predicted next PC selected for each accepted instruction\\nthrough IF/ID and ID/EX. In EX, compute actual_next_pc using the existing\\nforwarded operands and misalignment rules, including PC+4 on the existing\\ntrap path. Redirect only when a valid advancing instruction's actual next\\nPC differs from its recorded prediction. Compare for every instruction,\\nso stale entries on non-branches and taken-to-taken JALR target changes\\nrecover correctly. Feed actual_next_pc, never the prediction, into RVFI.\\n\\nA prediction advances the PC only when ID accepts that fetch; load-use,\\ninstruction-bus, and data-bus stalls must not consume a prediction.\\nEX recovery overrides an instruction-bus stall. Defer recovery and table\\ntraining while EX is held by a data-bus stall, then resolve once on release.\\nReuse the existing hazard flush path for mispredictions; a correct taken\\nprediction must not flush the fetched target instruction. Preserve the\\nexisting MEM/WB forwarding retention, trap behavior, and nret=1 interface.\\n\\nExpect an approximately eight percent net fitness improvement from fewer\\ncontrol-transfer bubbles, with medium confidence because table conflicts\\nand added lookup/recovery logic may offset the cycle savings through Fmax.\\nValidate with the unchanged correctness gates and bracketed, stalled\\nCoreMark run; compare median three-seed Fmax times iterations/cycle against\\n282.82 and report LUT/FF growth. This estimate is not a measured result.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the fixed 16-entry tagged target table, valid-gated lookup,\\nand EX-resolution training described above. Select predicted_next_pc\\nfor ordinary accepted fetches and export that exact selection with the\\ninstruction. Preserve reset and recovery priority over normal PC\\nadvancement. Do not advance on a stall, flush the instruction merely\\nbecause it predicts taken, or depend on imem_data for the table lookup.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t. Leave\\ndownstream architectural pc_next and all RVFI bundle meanings intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc atomically with the accepted PC, instruction,\\nand validity. Hold it with the rest of ID/EX on a data-bus stall and\\nclear it with the existing reset/flush bubble; never recompute a held\\ninstruction's prediction from the current table contents.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Factor the existing resolved pc_next into actual_next_pc and use it\\nboth for EX/MEM and mismatch recovery. Qualify recovery and predictor\\nfeedback by valid EX advancement, not merely branch control bits.\\nExport resolution PC, control-flow kind, taken outcome, target, and\\nlegal/nontrapping status for training. Preserve forwarded comparisons,\\nJAL/JALR PC+4 link writes, JALR bit-zero clearing, and misalignment traps.\\nCorrect a wrong predicted destination even when the instruction is\\nnot a branch or when both prediction and actual transfer are taken.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect EX resolution feedback to the IF predictor using internal\\nsignals only. Route the new misprediction-qualified redirect through\\nthe existing hazard unit and IF recovery ports. Keep every external\\nimem/dmem and channel-0 RVFI port unchanged, including nret=1.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add architectural trace and cycle checks for cold/warm backward loops,\\nloop exits, forward taken branches, direct jumps, changing JALR targets,\\nand distinct PCs sharing a table index. Verify reset invalidation and\\nstale-entry recovery when an instruction at a trained PC changes.\\nExtend the local test harness with deterministic ready schedules to\\ncover prediction stalls, load-use dependencies, EX recovery during\\nimem stalls, and an EX branch held behind a stalled memory operation.\\nAssert no wrong-path retirement or store, exact PC-to-next-PC chaining,\\nstrict RVFI order increments, correct link data, and preserved illegal\\nand misaligned-target traps. A warmed, correctly predicted transfer\\nmust eliminate the baseline redirect bubble in a zero-wait tight loop.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 8, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the fixed 16-entry tagged target table, valid-gated lookup,\\nand EX-resolution training described above. Select predicted_next_pc\\nfor ordinary accepted fetches and export that exact selection with the\\ninstruction. Preserve reset and recovery priority over normal PC\\nadvancement. Do not advance on a stall, flush the instruction merely\\nbecause it predicts taken, or depend on imem_data for the table lookup.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit predicted_next_pc field to if_id_t and id_ex_t. Leave\\ndownstream architectural pc_next and all RVFI bundle meanings intact.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture predicted_next_pc atomically with the accepted PC, instruction,\\nand validity. Hold it with the rest of ID/EX on a data-bus stall and\\nclear it with the existing reset/flush bubble; never recompute a held\\ninstruction's prediction from the current table contents.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Factor the existing resolved pc_next into actual_next_pc and use it\\nboth for EX/MEM and mismatch recovery. Qualify recovery and predictor\\nfeedback by valid EX advancement, not merely branch control bits.\\nExport resolution PC, control-flow kind, taken outcome, target, and\\nlegal/nontrapping status for training. Preserve forwarded comparisons,\\nJAL/JALR PC+4 link writes, JALR bit-zero clearing, and misalignment traps.\\nCorrect a wrong predicted destination even when the instruction is\\nnot a branch or when both prediction and actual transfer are taken.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect EX resolution feedback to the IF predictor using internal\\nsignals only. Route the new misprediction-qualified redirect through\\nthe existing hazard unit and IF recovery ports. Keep every external\\nimem/dmem and channel-0 RVFI port unchanged, including nret=1.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add architectural trace and cycle checks for cold/warm backward loops,\\nloop exits, forward taken branches, direct jumps, changing JALR targets,\\nand distinct PCs sharing a table index. Verify reset invalidation and\\nstale-entry recovery when an instruction at a trained PC changes.\\nExtend the local test harness with deterministic ready schedules to\\ncover prediction stalls, load-use dependencies, EX recovery during\\nimem stalls, and an EX branch held behind a stalled memory operation.\\nAssert no wrong-path retirement or store, exact PC-to-next-PC chaining,\\nstrict RVFI order increments, correct link data, and preserved illegal\\nand misaligned-target traps. A warmed, correctly predicted transfer\\nmust eliminate the baseline redirect bubble in a zero-wait tight loop.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 319.45, \"delta_pct\": 12.95, \"vs_baseline\": 12.95, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.68, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10645, \"ff\": 2075, \"seeds\": [134.68, 141.88, 127.71], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the 16-entry direct-mapped target predictor in `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv`: PC[5:2] index, full PC[31:6] tag, aligned target[31:2], two-bit saturating counter, and one valid bit per entry (944 table bits before synthesis). Only valid tag hits with counter[1] set predict a target. Payloads are not reset; reset clears every valid bit. Lookup is combinational, updates are synchronous, and there is no update bypass or instruction-memory decode in the lookup.\\n\\nTraining is qualified by `in.valid && !stall && !reset` in EX. Taken conditional branches allocate weakly taken or increment a matching counter and update the target; not-taken branches decrement only matching entries and retain their target. Legal JAL/JALR train strongly taken with the last resolved target. Non-control instructions and traps invalidate matching entries. No addresses or instruction sequences are specialized to CoreMark.\\n\\nIF/ID and ID/EX carry the exact selected 32-bit predicted next PC. EX compares it with the resolved architectural next PC for every advancing instruction, including non-control instructions, illegal instructions, misaligned transfers, and changing JALR destinations. RVFI receives only the architectural next PC. Recovery uses the existing hazard flush path, overrides instruction-bus stalls, and is deferred during data-bus stalls. Correct predictions do not flush the target. MEM/WB forwarding retention, link writes, JALR bit-zero clearing, trap behavior, and the complete external nret=1 port declaration are preserved.\\n\\nSmall implementation deviations: the hazard unit's unchanged equations are expressed as separate continuous assignments to eliminate false combinational-loop warnings in the cocotb build with public signal visibility. A guarded core_pkg.sv include makes the requested alphabetical-glob lint command work as well as the existing package-first builds. Added `test_if_stage.py` for direct lookup/update timing and reset checks, in addition to the requested pipeline tests.\\n\\nThe pipeline harness now uses deterministic bus-ready schedules and applies stores only on accepting clock edges. Every run checks strict RVFI order increments, exact PC-to-next-PC chaining, and one-to-one agreement between bus stores and retired stores. Added coverage for cold/warm backward loops and exits, forward taken branches, direct jumps, changing load-dependent JALR targets, full high address tags and targets, conflicting indices, counter hysteresis, reset, stale non-control/illegal/misaligned instructions, prediction stalls, recovery during instruction stalls, and an EX branch held behind a stalled load using retained MEM/WB forwarding. The held-branch test verifies that a single taken resolution trains once, rather than saturating during the hold.\\n\\nLocal correctness status:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings. Also passed with `--public-flat-rw`.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench`: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including on the final source. The script automatically selected the nret=1 wrapper/config. No formal counterexamples or formal fix attempts were needed. This is the unchanged ALTOPS gate.\\n- Full cocotb suite: PASS, 6 pytest runners / 93 cocotb tests, including 24 pipeline and 2 predictor unit tests.\\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim` gate: PASS, selftest RVFI comparison and bracketed stalled CoreMark CRC validation (2 ELFs).\\n- Warm backward-loop branch-to-target retirement gaps: 2 cycles for the cold taken branch, then 1 cycle for each warm taken branch; the loop exit costs 2 cycles. A warm self-JAL retires every cycle.\\n- External core port declaration matches the baseline exactly; all RTL/test files in the validation copy match the delivered source.\\n\\nAll build, formal, synthesis, and simulation artifacts were staged under `$TMPDIR/hyp-20260905-003-r1s2-validation`. The gate scripts, wrappers, reference model, and benchmark sources were copied without modification. The source worktree changes are limited to bench RTL/tests and this requested notes file.\\n\\nThe prompt's 6 KB description differs from the checked-in fitness contract: `bench/programs/Makefile` defaults to TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, and `tools/eval/fpga.py` requires the 2 KB CRCs. Both configurations were exercised without changing either contract file. The comparable score against the recorded 282.82 baseline uses the unchanged 2 KB gate. The separate requested 6 KB build uses TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, -O3, and ITERATIONS=10.\\n\\nThe 6 KB final-source simulation completed under `--bench --istall --dstall`, with no out-of-bounds access. Markers were start=53,747 and stop=30,876,867, giving 30,823,120 measured cycles. It reported crclist=0xd4b0, crcmatrix=0xbe52, crcstate=0x5e47, crcfinal=0x5949, and the literal \\\"Correct operation validated.\\\" The UART's size field is per algorithm; its compiler-flags string is hardcoded in the unchanged port, while the actual build command used -O3.\\n\\nFinal-source synthesis and all three unchanged nextpnr seed runs passed. Fmax for seeds 1/2/3 was 134.68 / 141.88 / 127.71 MHz; the median is 134.68 MHz. The unchanged stalled 2 KB gate measured 4,215,937 bracketed cycles for 10 iterations, or approximately 2.371952e-6 iterations/cycle. Fitness is computed from that full-precision ratio, rather than the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark` display.\\n\\n| Metric | Recorded baseline | Final predictor | Change |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Fitness (iterations/s) | 282.82 | 319.45 | +12.95% |\\n| Median Fmax (MHz) | 127.03 | 134.68 | +6.02% |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,215,937 | -6.14% |\\n| LUT4 | 9,563 | 10,645 | +1,082 (+11.31%) |\\n| FF | 1,866 | 2,075 | +209 (+11.20%) |\\n\\nAt the same measured median Fmax, the separate 6 KB run corresponds to 43.69 iterations/s. The recorded 282.82 baseline belongs to the checked-in 2 KB gate, so it is not a like-for-like baseline for that 6 KB result.\\n\\nThe measured final-source improvement exceeds the hypothesis's estimated eight percent. Area growth and the spread between placement seeds remain costs/uncertainties; this local result does not replace the orchestrator's independent acceptance run. The final measurement above supersedes the preliminary measurement taken before the hazard-equation cleanup. Evidence is in the scratch copy's `cores/bench/generated/fitness-final.json`, `formal-final.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `cosim-result.json`, `coremark-6k-final.log`, and `pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T14:13:39.384001Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r1s2 (improvement, +12.95%): A 16-entry tagged target predictor removes the combinational IF/ID frontend's one-slot EX redirect bubble on correctly predicted taken branches/jumps, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles 6.14% while median Fmax rose 6.02%, at about 11% LUT/FF growth.\"}\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Register one-hot ALU operation selects before execute\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 319.45 iterations/sec at 134.68 MHz\\nmedian Fmax. Its saved final nextpnr reports, whose validation-copy RTL\\nmatches the current bench RTL byte-for-byte, identify a concrete control\\ntiming bottleneck: seeds 1 and 3 launch from ID/EX ALU op[3], and seed 2\\nlaunches from op[4]. These paths end at EX/MEM alu_result bits 27, 6,\\nand 3 respectively, through execute result-selection logic. The reports\\nare under .tmp/hyp-20260905-003-r1s2-validation/cores/bench/generated/\\npnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log; synth.json maps the named flops to these bits.\\nPredecoding the ALU operation before its pipeline register targets the\\nmeasured launch path without changing instruction latency.\\n\\nThe bench log's previous micro_opt, hyp-20260905-001-r1s0, changed\\nregister-file read selection and gained only 0.54% with identical cycle\\ncounts. There is no prior registered ALU-predecode attempt in this log.\\nLESSONS.md records the successful target predictor and the limited cycle\\nbenefit of the pending-store attempt. This proposal preserves the current\\npredictor and concentrates on execute control timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 319.45 iterations/sec at 134.68 MHz\\nmedian Fmax. Its saved final nextpnr reports, whose validation-copy RTL\\nmatches the current bench RTL byte-for-byte, identify a concrete control\\ntiming bottleneck: seeds 1 and 3 launch from ID/EX ALU op[3], and seed 2\\nlaunches from op[4]. These paths end at EX/MEM alu_result bits 27, 6,\\nand 3 respectively, through execute result-selection logic. The reports\\nare under .tmp/hyp-20260905-003-r1s2-validation/cores/bench/generated/\\npnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log; synth.json maps the named flops to these bits.\\nPredecoding the ALU operation before its pipeline register targets the\\nmeasured launch path without changing instruction latency.\\n\\nThe bench log's previous micro_opt, hyp-20260905-001-r1s0, changed\\nregister-file read selection and gained only 0.54% with identical cycle\\ncounts. There is no prior registered ALU-predecode attempt in this log.\\nLESSONS.md records the successful target predictor and the limited cycle\\nbenefit of the pending-store attempt. This proposal preserves the current\\npredictor and concentrates on execute control timing.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move the existing binary-to-operation decode across the ID/EX register.\\nKeep the decoder's five-bit ALU operation encoding and legality rules,\\nbut derive 19 individual selects in ID, one for each ALU_ADD through\\nALU_REMU operation, and register them with the instruction in a dedicated\\nid_ex_t field. Each select is a comparison against the existing encoded\\noperation constant. For every accepted instruction the vector must agree\\nwith dec_alu_op; it must hold during a data-memory stall and clear with\\nthe existing reset/flush bubble. Do not derive instruction legality from\\nthis vector or suppress the decoder's default ADD selection on traps.\\n\\nFeed those registered selects directly into the combinational ALU.\\nReplace the binary-op case selection with parallel masked result terms\\ncombined by a balanced OR reduction. Each term uses its corresponding\\nselect bit directly; do not re-encode the vector, compare the complete\\nvector with one-hot constants, or build a priority chain in EX. An\\nall-zero vector returns zero. Preserve the existing arithmetic candidate\\nexpressions, operand widths and signedness, shift masking, multiply-high\\nvariants, divide-by-zero and signed-overflow handling. Keep all eight\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS formulas and their operation mappings identical.\\nThis is a control-decode retiming and selection rewrite, with no new ALU\\npipeline stage, arithmetic algorithm, or instruction scheduling policy.\\n\\nPreserve operand forwarding, load-use bubbles, MEM/WB data retention\\nduring stalls, branch resolution, predictor training and recovery, and\\nthe architectural RVFI values. The sole intended gain is removing\\nencoded-op decode from the measured EX timing path. Expect approximately\\nsix percent higher fitness from higher Fmax, with unchanged CoreMark\\ncycles for the same binary and ready schedule. This is an unmeasured\\nestimate. Extra control flops, one-hot fanout, added ID decode delay, or\\nan operand-to-result path becoming critical could erase the benefit.\\n\\nValidate the existing real-arithmetic ALU tests, decoder and pipeline\\nsuites, unchanged ALTOPS formal gate, and stalled architectural cosim.\\nCheck operation-select alignment through holds and flushes. Run the\\nunchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation and compare median Fmax times the\\nfull-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles ratio against 319.45; report\\nLUT/FF changes and the new critical path. Keep benchmark configurations\\ncomparable, and verify the specified 6 KB canonical CRCs, timing markers,\\nand validation UART literal without changing evaluation files. Preserve\\nnret=1, every external core port, and all hard invariants.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 19-bit ALU operation-select field to id_ex_t only, with bits\\nindexed by the existing ALU operation constants. Retain the decoder\\nencoding and existing ctrl_t meanings; no select-vector state is\\nneeded in EX/MEM or MEM/WB.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode dec_alu_op into individual operation selects combinationally\\nin ID and capture them atomically with ID/EX. Reuse its existing\\nreset, flush, and hold priorities. Preserve the decoder and trap\\ncontrols, including default ADD on otherwise illegal instructions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the registered operation-select vector directly to the ALU\\ninstead of supplying the encoded operation. Preserve both forwarded\\nsource values, ALU operand selection, result capture, PC+4 link\\nwrites, and all branch, stall, and predictor behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the internal ALU module's five-bit op input with the 19-bit\\noperation-select input. Select unchanged arithmetic candidates using\\nmutually exclusive masked terms and a balanced OR tree, returning\\nzero when all selects are clear. Preserve every real RV32IM result\\nand the exact corresponding ALTOPS formula. Add no state or latency.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt the common drive helper to supply the one-hot bit for each\\nexisting operation index, mapping unsupported indices to all-zero\\nselection for default-result coverage. Retain every independent\\nexpected arithmetic result and corner-case assertion, including all\\nM-extension tests. Check zero selection and consecutive changes\\nbetween operation selects so stale or crossed selects are detected.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed instruction sequences with alternating ALU operations,\\nincluding MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, and verify exact\\nretirement results against independent expectations. Exercise these\\ntransitions through data-bus holds, instruction-bus stalls, load-use\\nbubbles, and misprediction flushes, checking that each advancing\\ninstruction retains its own operation select and that wrong-path\\noperations cannot retire or write memory. Preserve all existing\\nRVFI ordering, PC chaining, trap, and predictor assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 19-bit ALU operation-select field to id_ex_t only, with bits\\nindexed by the existing ALU operation constants. Retain the decoder\\nencoding and existing ctrl_t meanings; no select-vector state is\\nneeded in EX/MEM or MEM/WB.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode dec_alu_op into individual operation selects combinationally\\nin ID and capture them atomically with ID/EX. Reuse its existing\\nreset, flush, and hold priorities. Preserve the decoder and trap\\ncontrols, including default ADD on otherwise illegal instructions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the registered operation-select vector directly to the ALU\\ninstead of supplying the encoded operation. Preserve both forwarded\\nsource values, ALU operand selection, result capture, PC+4 link\\nwrites, and all branch, stall, and predictor behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the internal ALU module's five-bit op input with the 19-bit\\noperation-select input. Select unchanged arithmetic candidates using\\nmutually exclusive masked terms and a balanced OR tree, returning\\nzero when all selects are clear. Preserve every real RV32IM result\\nand the exact corresponding ALTOPS formula. Add no state or latency.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt the common drive helper to supply the one-hot bit for each\\nexisting operation index, mapping unsupported indices to all-zero\\nselection for default-result coverage. Retain every independent\\nexpected arithmetic result and corner-case assertion, including all\\nM-extension tests. Check zero selection and consecutive changes\\nbetween operation selects so stale or crossed selects are detected.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed instruction sequences with alternating ALU operations,\\nincluding MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU, and verify exact\\nretirement results against independent expectations. Exercise these\\ntransitions through data-bus holds, instruction-bus stalls, load-use\\nbubbles, and misprediction flushes, checking that each advancing\\ninstruction retains its own operation select and that wrong-path\\noperations cannot retire or write memory. Preserve all existing\\nRVFI ordering, PC chaining, trap, and predictor assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 306.93, \"delta_pct\": -3.92, \"vs_baseline\": 8.52, \"fmax_mhz\": 129.4, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10268, \"ff\": 2076, \"seeds\": [129.4, 129.2, 130.92], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T15:08:24.814852Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r2s0 (regression, -3.92%): Registered one-hot ALU selects with balanced OR selection shifted the timing bottleneck to the `predicted_next_pc` mismatch recovery path feeding ID/EX, lowering median Fmax from 134.68 to 129.40 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles despite 377 fewer LUT4s.\"}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Split execution into a fast ALU and an eight-cycle divider\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench ALU places combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware behind the\\nsame forwarded operands and result selection as ordinary integer work.\\nSharing a bounded iterative execution resource for these four operations\\ncan reduce arithmetic depth, LUT use, and routing pressure without adding\\nstages to RV32I instructions or multiplication. The current winner scores\\n319.45 iter/s at 134.68 MHz and uses 10645 LUT4s. Divider removal is a\\nplausible timing opportunity, not a confirmed current critical path.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the bench structural attempt hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 show\\nonly 0.75% fewer CoreMark cycles from store buffering; most of that\\ncandidate's fitness gain came from Fmax. The adopted target predictor\\nalready reduces control-transfer bubbles. A dedicated multicycle divide\\nexecution path therefore offers a more substantial new structural bet.\\n\\nRead-only reference hyp-20260428-010-r6s0 in cores/v1 successfully removed\\ncombinational division, reporting 159.92 MHz and 5888 LUT4s on that\\ndifferent core. Its 32-step hardware divider used shorter ALTOPS timing,\\nso its formal result does not establish this proposal's control timing.\\nAn earlier reference attempt failed selftest cosim, making operand capture\\nand exactly-once completion essential. Bench has no recorded divider-split\\nattempt. Low timed-region DIV/REM traffic is an expectation to verify,\\nrather than a reason to omit those instructions or arithmetic checks.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench ALU places combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware behind the\\nsame forwarded operands and result selection as ordinary integer work.\\nSharing a bounded iterative execution resource for these four operations\\ncan reduce arithmetic depth, LUT use, and routing pressure without adding\\nstages to RV32I instructions or multiplication. The current winner scores\\n319.45 iter/s at 134.68 MHz and uses 10645 LUT4s. Divider removal is a\\nplausible timing opportunity, not a confirmed current critical path.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the bench structural attempt hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 show\\nonly 0.75% fewer CoreMark cycles from store buffering; most of that\\ncandidate's fitness gain came from Fmax. The adopted target predictor\\nalready reduces control-transfer bubbles. A dedicated multicycle divide\\nexecution path therefore offers a more substantial new structural bet.\\n\\nRead-only reference hyp-20260428-010-r6s0 in cores/v1 successfully removed\\ncombinational division, reporting 159.92 MHz and 5888 LUT4s on that\\ndifferent core. Its 32-step hardware divider used shorter ALTOPS timing,\\nso its formal result does not establish this proposal's control timing.\\nAn earlier reference attempt failed selftest cosim, making operand capture\\nand exactly-once completion essential. Bench has no recorded divider-split\\nattempt. Low timed-region DIV/REM traffic is an expectation to verify,\\nrather than a reason to omit those instructions or arithmetic checks.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Partition execution into the existing one-cycle integer/multiply datapath\\nand one blocking, eight-cycle divide/remainder engine. Only legal valid\\nDIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU instructions enter the engine. Retain the current\\npipeline depth and forwarding for every other instruction, including all\\nfour multiply variants. This changes execution-resource scheduling and\\npipeline advancement; it does not add another retirement channel.\\n\\nImplement unsigned restoring division with four quotient bits computed\\nper clock, using four cascaded shift/subtract/select steps and one shared\\nremainder/quotient state. Perform the first four steps on the accepting\\nlaunch edge and the remaining seven groups on the next seven edges.\\nNormalize signed operands on launch and include quotient/remainder sign\\ncorrection in the final result capture. Do not add separate normalization\\nor correction cycles. Capture the original operands and operation at\\nlaunch; changing live forwarding values afterward cannot affect the\\noperation. Preserve truncation toward zero, remainder sign matching the\\ndividend, quotient all-ones on division by zero, original dividend on\\nremainder by zero, and INT_MIN/-1 quotient INT_MIN with remainder zero.\\nSpecial cases use the same eight-edge schedule. No real / or % operator\\nremains in the synthesized execution datapath.\\n\\nUse request acceptance and a retained result-valid/result-ready handshake.\\nLaunch once when a valid legal divide is in ID/EX, the engine is idle,\\nand an older EX/MEM memory instruction is not blocking advancement.\\nFreeze the fetch PC and ID/EX while the divide is incomplete. Retain PC,\\ninstruction, destination, control, and exact predicted_next_pc in ID/EX;\\nlatch both post-forwarded source values separately for arithmetic and\\nRVFI, since older WB values can disappear while the divider runs.\\n\\nSeparate the divider wait from the existing data-memory hold. While\\ndividing, let older EX/MEM and MEM/WB instructions drain once, then put\\ncomplete bubbles into EX/MEM, clearing valid and all write/memory control\\nbits. Merely holding the previous EX/MEM instruction or clearing valid\\nalone is insufficient because existing bus/forwarding logic also reads\\nits control fields. A real data-bus stall still holds EX/MEM and retains\\nMEM/WB forwarding payload with valid cleared, exactly as today. Starting\\nor accepting a divide result must never override that older memory hold.\\n\\nOnce the result is valid and EX/MEM can accept it, advance the divide\\nexactly once with its saved operands and original instruction metadata,\\nand release ID/EX to the next instruction. Hold result-valid and data\\nuntil that acceptance; never relaunch the held instruction or publish a\\npartial quotient. Forward the completed result through the existing\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB routes, including immediate dependent arithmetic,\\nloads, stores, branches, JALR, and another divide. No younger instruction\\novertakes the divide or issues a memory request before it completes.\\n\\nQualify EX resolution, predictor training, and mismatch recovery by actual\\ninstruction advancement, including divide completion. A stale prediction\\non a divide still recovers to its architectural PC+4 exactly once. Keep\\nthe current predictor organization and all ordinary branch behavior.\\nReset cancels the pending operation and prevents a stale completion.\\n\\nPreserve all eight existing ALTOPS formulas. For DIV/REM, capture the\\ncorresponding existing formula result from the accepted operands and\\nrelease it using the SAME eight-cycle controller, holds, and completion\\nhandshake as real arithmetic. Do not use a shorter formal-only schedule,\\nbypass the engine in formal, or change formal depths. With the first\\ndigit group on launch and correction on the last group, ready-bus\\nexecution must satisfy the existing ten-cycle successor-retirement\\nwindow, including a divide following redirect recovery; verify this in\\nthe unchanged liveness gate. This timing bound is a design requirement.\\n\\nExpect approximately 12% higher fitness from reduced divider hardware\\nand timing pressure, with medium confidence. Four subtract steps plus\\nlaunch normalization or final correction may still limit Fmax, and any\\ntimed DIV/REM instructions add cycles. Measure both effects using the\\nunchanged bracketed, stalled CoreMark evaluation and median of three\\nFPGA seeds against 319.45; report LUT/FF use and inspect the resulting\\ncritical path. This estimate is not a measured result. Preserve nret=1,\\nevery external port and RVFI field, strict retirement order, decoder and\\ntrap rules, memory ordering and bounds, marker handshakes, canonical\\nCoreMark CRC/UART validation, and every existing correctness check.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fixed eight-cycle divider described above, computing four\\nrestoring quotient bits per edge, including the accepting edge.\\nCapture operands, signedness, result selection, and special cases;\\nregister the corrected result on the eighth edge and retain it until\\nconsumed. Use identical control latency in real and ALTOPS modes.\\nReset clears busy/result validity. Reject new requests while occupied.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational real division/remainder and integrate div_unit\\nalongside the unchanged fast ALU. Add internal-module clock, reset,\\ndivide request/acceptance, and result-valid/result-ready signals so\\nthe existing ALU test suite can exercise the actual integrated engine.\\nKeep op/a/b/out and zero-cycle behavior for ordinary ALU and all MUL\\noperations. Route valid divide results from div_unit; preserve the\\nexact ALTOPS formulas across the two arithmetic paths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Launch the engine once from post-forwarded operands and expose a\\nseparate divide-wait signal. Save both source values while ID/EX\\nretains instruction metadata. Drain older work using full EX/MEM\\nbubbles during divide waits, while respecting real memory holds.\\nPublish the completed divide only on result acceptance, with saved\\nRVFI operands, and qualify prediction resolution/training/recovery\\nby that advancement. Keep ordinary instruction timing unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the divide-wait input to fetch and ID/EX hold control without\\ntreating it as a data-memory stall or a flush. Keep EX/MEM hold and\\nMEM/WB forwarding retention governed by actual older memory waits;\\nex_stage supplies bubbles for divide waits. Preserve existing\\nload-use, instruction-ready, reset, and redirect priorities.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the separate long-operation interlock between EX and the hazard\\nunit. Preserve the complete nret=1 core port declaration and existing\\nregister-file, forwarding, memory, predictor, and RVFI connections.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Include div_unit.sv in this runner's local source list and adapt the\\nexisting divide helper to clock and handshake the integrated ALU.\\nRetain every existing exact arithmetic expectation and all multiply\\ntests; do not replace DIV/REM checks with dummy fast-path values.\\nAdd at least 30 deterministic and boundary vectors per M operation,\\nchecking signed division with an integer truncation-toward-zero\\nreference. Verify eight-edge completion, input changes after launch,\\nheld results, reset cancellation, and consecutive requests. Exercise\\nreal arithmetic and ALTOPS builds with identical control timing;\\nkeep any extra runner configuration within this permitted test file.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add div_unit.sv to the local source list. Cover divide operands from\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB, a stalled load feeding a divide, consecutive\\ndivides, rd=x0, all signed/unsigned corner cases, and immediate result\\nconsumers of each instruction class. Cover a divider after branch\\nrecovery, stale prediction at a divide PC, simultaneous imem/dmem\\nstalls, reset during execution, and stores/markers surrounding it.\\nAssert no duplicate retirement, write, request, or predictor update;\\nexact saved RVFI source values and PC chaining; strict order; and\\nbounded completion with ready buses. Preserve all existing assertions\\nand run the unchanged formal, selftest cosim, and CoreMark gates.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fixed eight-cycle divider described above, computing four\\nrestoring quotient bits per edge, including the accepting edge.\\nCapture operands, signedness, result selection, and special cases;\\nregister the corrected result on the eighth edge and retain it until\\nconsumed. Use identical control latency in real and ALTOPS modes.\\nReset clears busy/result validity. Reject new requests while occupied.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational real division/remainder and integrate div_unit\\nalongside the unchanged fast ALU. Add internal-module clock, reset,\\ndivide request/acceptance, and result-valid/result-ready signals so\\nthe existing ALU test suite can exercise the actual integrated engine.\\nKeep op/a/b/out and zero-cycle behavior for ordinary ALU and all MUL\\noperations. Route valid divide results from div_unit; preserve the\\nexact ALTOPS formulas across the two arithmetic paths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Launch the engine once from post-forwarded operands and expose a\\nseparate divide-wait signal. Save both source values while ID/EX\\nretains instruction metadata. Drain older work using full EX/MEM\\nbubbles during divide waits, while respecting real memory holds.\\nPublish the completed divide only on result acceptance, with saved\\nRVFI operands, and qualify prediction resolution/training/recovery\\nby that advancement. Keep ordinary instruction timing unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the divide-wait input to fetch and ID/EX hold control without\\ntreating it as a data-memory stall or a flush. Keep EX/MEM hold and\\nMEM/WB forwarding retention governed by actual older memory waits;\\nex_stage supplies bubbles for divide waits. Preserve existing\\nload-use, instruction-ready, reset, and redirect priorities.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the separate long-operation interlock between EX and the hazard\\nunit. Preserve the complete nret=1 core port declaration and existing\\nregister-file, forwarding, memory, predictor, and RVFI connections.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Include div_unit.sv in this runner's local source list and adapt the\\nexisting divide helper to clock and handshake the integrated ALU.\\nRetain every existing exact arithmetic expectation and all multiply\\ntests; do not replace DIV/REM checks with dummy fast-path values.\\nAdd at least 30 deterministic and boundary vectors per M operation,\\nchecking signed division with an integer truncation-toward-zero\\nreference. Verify eight-edge completion, input changes after launch,\\nheld results, reset cancellation, and consecutive requests. Exercise\\nreal arithmetic and ALTOPS builds with identical control timing;\\nkeep any extra runner configuration within this permitted test file.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add div_unit.sv to the local source list. Cover divide operands from\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB, a stalled load feeding a divide, consecutive\\ndivides, rd=x0, all signed/unsigned corner cases, and immediate result\\nconsumers of each instruction class. Cover a divider after branch\\nrecovery, stale prediction at a divide PC, simultaneous imem/dmem\\nstalls, reset during execution, and stores/markers surrounding it.\\nAssert no duplicate retirement, write, request, or predictor update;\\nexact saved RVFI source values and PC chaining; strict order; and\\nbounded completion with ready buses. Preserve all existing assertions\\nand run the unchanged formal, selftest cosim, and CoreMark gates.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 320.28, \"delta_pct\": 0.26, \"vs_baseline\": 13.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5871, \"ff\": 2292, \"seeds\": [135.03, 144.68, 126.39], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Eight-edge divider implementation\\n\\nImplemented the proposed divider split in `cores/bench` only. The requested implementation notes are at the worktree root, as specified in instruction 5; all RTL and test changes are confined to the permitted bench files.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- Added `rtl/div_unit.sv`: one restoring remainder/quotient state, four cascaded shift/subtract/select steps per edge, first group on request acceptance, and corrected registered result on edge eight. Signed normalization happens on launch. Divide by zero, signed overflow, and remainder signs use the same schedule as ordinary operands.\\n- Integrated the engine into `alu.sv` with request-ready and retained result-valid/result-ready signals. Ordinary integer operations and all four multiplies remain combinational. All eight original ALTOPS formulas are preserved; DIV/REM capture their formula from accepted operands and use the identical eight-edge controller.\\n- EX saves both post-forwarded source operands for RVFI while ID/EX retains the instruction, PC, exact prediction, destination, and controls. Request readiness prevents relaunch while computing or retaining a result. The result advances once through the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB path.\\n- Added a separate divide interlock to EX, hazard control, and core wiring. Divide waits freeze fetch and ID/EX but drain older instructions once and insert complete EX/MEM bubbles. Actual memory holds retain their original EX/MEM priority and MEM/WB forwarding behavior. Resolution, predictor training, and redirects occur only when EX advances, including divide completion.\\n- Reset cancels computation and retained results. The nret=1 top-level ports, retirement logic, decoder/traps, predictor organization, register file, forwarding unit, and memory stage remain unchanged.\\n- Expanded integrated ALU tests and pipeline tests. No gate, formal depth, arithmetic expectation, or existing assertion was weakened.\\n\\nThe controller produces result-valid after edge eight; EX/MEM accepts on edge nine and MEM/WB retires on edge ten. A divide after a cold redirect retires exactly ten cycles after the redirecting instruction. Back-to-back divides retire nine cycles apart with ready buses.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings. Also PASS with `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` defined.\\n- Yosys elaboration/process/optimization followed by `select -assert-none` for `$div`, `$mod`, `$divfloor`, and `$modfloor`: PASS. No real division/remainder operator remains in the synthesized execution datapath.\\n- Complete cocotb suite: PASS, all seven pytest runner cases. ALU runs in real and ALTOPS builds, each checking 185 boundary/deterministic vectors per M operation plus existing exact tests. Tests cover eight-edge completion, changing live operands/opcodes, rejected requests while occupied, retained results, ready asserted before completion, fast ALU operation during division, reset at every phase, and consecutive requests.\\n- Pipeline tests: PASS. Coverage includes EX/MEM and MEM/WB operands, stalled load dependencies, retained older WB payload, consecutive divides, rd=x0, 34 vector pairs for every DIV/REM variant, immediate and one-gap consumers, all four multiply consumers, loads, store data/address, branches, JALR, redirect recovery, stale predictions, simultaneous bus stalls, memory markers, illegal/flushed instructions, and reset during execution. Assertions check saved RVFI operands, full bubbles, strict order/PC chaining, exactly-once launch/completion/resolution, writes and memory requests, and ready-bus retirement bounds.\\n- Unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh`, selecting bench nret=1: **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, including `liveness_ch0`, `unique_ch0`, and all four DIV/REM instruction checks. First completed formal suite passed; zero RTL fix retries. Formal ran in a scratch copy under `$TMPDIR`, with unchanged wrapper, checks, and depth settings.\\n- Unchanged selftest cosim plus CoreMark CRC gate: PASS, two ELFs tested. Additional selftest cosim with both random bus stalls and seeds 3735928559, 1, and 42: PASS, 115 retirements each, byte-exact against the unchanged ISS.\\n- Full real-arithmetic `formal-deep` was not run. ALTOPS formal validates the real controller latency and routing, while real arithmetic is checked by the simulation vectors and cosim.\\n\\n## Measured FPGA and benchmark results\\n\\nThe unchanged local evaluator reports:\\n\\n| Metric | Prior winner | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Median Fmax | 134.68 MHz | 135.03 MHz |\\n| Fitness | 319.45 iter/s | 320.28 iter/s |\\n| LUT4 | 10,645 | 5,871 |\\n| FF | 2,075 | 2,292 |\\n| Bracketed stalled CoreMark cycles | 4,215,937 | 4,215,937 |\\n\\nAll three FPGA seeds routed successfully: seed 1 = 135.03 MHz, seed 2 = 144.68 MHz, seed 3 = 126.39 MHz. Fitness improved **0.26%**, LUT4 use fell **44.85%**, and FF use rose **10.46%**. The approximately 12% anticipated fitness gain was not realized.\\n\\nThe baseline cycle count was independently reproduced from the original HEAD bench RTL using the same ELF and unchanged stalled harness. A streamed full candidate retirement trace contained 2,937,037 instructions: the measured region executed 93,960 MULs and **zero DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU instructions**. The complete program executed two DIVUs outside the markers. Thus divider latency did not affect the scored cycle count.\\n\\nAll three post-route critical paths run through predictor tag lookup and counter write control. For the median seed, the path starts at `cpu.u_if.tag_q.0.1_RAD_DFFR_Q_3.Q` and ends at `cpu.u_if.counter_q.1.0.WRE`: 7.41 ns total, comprising 3.77 ns logic and 3.63 ns routing. The divider is not on the reported critical path. The small fitness change and substantial seed spread do not establish the predicted timing benefit.\\n\\n## Benchmark contract discrepancy and supplemental 6 KB run\\n\\nThe request describes a 6 KB performance workload with CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47. The checked-in `bench/programs/Makefile` actually defaults to `COREMARK_SIZE=2000`, and the unchanged evaluator requires the 2 KB CRC set e714/1fd7/8e3a (seed e9f5, final fcaf). The 319.45 reference is consistent with this checked-in configuration. No benchmark source, Makefile, evaluator, or CRC check was changed; the primary fitness above uses the unchanged evaluation.\\n\\nA supplemental scratch build used the existing `COREMARK_SIZE=6000` option, `-O3`, and ITERATIONS=10 with both random stalls. It passed EBREAK, memory bounds, marker bracketing, the literal `Correct operation validated.`, and exact CRCs: seed 8a02, list d4b0, matrix be52, state 5e47, final 5949. It measured 30,823,120 bracketed cycles, equivalent to 43.81 iter/s at the measured 135.03 MHz median. This supplemental score is not compared to the differently configured 319.45 baseline.\\n\\n## Evidence and remaining concerns\\n\\nScratch artifacts are under `$TMPDIR/hyp-20260905-002-r2s1-checks`; post-route reports are in its `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`. Logs alongside that scratch directory use the prefix `hyp-20260905-002-r2s1-`, including `formal.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `cosim.log`, `selftest-stalls.log`, `fpga.log`, `baseline-cycles.log`, `div-traffic.log`, and `6k.log`.\\n\\nNo functional deviation from the proposed divider schedule was needed. The material limitations are the unchanged predictor critical path, the small measured fitness gain relative to seed variation, and the workload-dependent eight extra EX hold cycles for each divide/remainder compared with the old combinational ALU. Synthesis emitted only the expected array-to-wire lowering messages for the four combinational digit steps; required lint is clean.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T15:02:37.734111Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.26%): An eight-cycle DIV/REM engine cut LUT4 use 44.85% without changing CoreMark cycles because the timed region executes no DIV/REM, but predictor tag lookup and counter-write control remained the critical path, leaving the fitness gain small relative to FPGA seed variation.\"}\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Two-way target prediction at unchanged 16-entry capacity\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor in hyp-20260905-003-r1s2 cut stalled CoreMark cycles by\\n6.14% and reached 319.45 fitness, establishing that removing the\\ncombinational frontend's one-slot recovery bubbles is valuable. Its\\n16-entry direct-mapped table indexes PC[5:2], so recurring branches and\\njumps separated by multiples of 64 bytes evict each other's targets\\nand two-bit direction history. Such interference can persist even when\\nonly two control-flow PCs are hot. The experiment log contains one\\npredictor attempt and no associativity experiment; conflict frequency\\nhas not yet been measured. Since the winning predictor already added\\nabout 11% LUT/FF usage, retaining its entry count makes associativity a\\nfocused way to seek further cycle savings with limited storage growth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The predictor in hyp-20260905-003-r1s2 cut stalled CoreMark cycles by\\n6.14% and reached 319.45 fitness, establishing that removing the\\ncombinational frontend's one-slot recovery bubbles is valuable. Its\\n16-entry direct-mapped table indexes PC[5:2], so recurring branches and\\njumps separated by multiples of 64 bytes evict each other's targets\\nand two-bit direction history. Such interference can persist even when\\nonly two control-flow PCs are hot. The experiment log contains one\\npredictor attempt and no associativity experiment; conflict frequency\\nhas not yet been measured. Since the winning predictor already added\\nabout 11% LUT/FF usage, retaining its entry count makes associativity a\\nfocused way to seek further cycle savings with limited storage growth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Reorganize the existing target predictor into eight sets with two ways\\nper set, retaining sixteen total entries. Index both fetch and EX\\nresolution with PC[4:2]. Each way stores valid, the full PC[31:5] tag,\\naligned target[31:2], and the existing two-bit saturating counter. Add\\none replacement bit per set, naming the next victim when both ways are\\nvalid. Total logical table state becomes 968 bits: sixteen 60-bit\\nentries plus eight replacement bits, versus the current 944 bits.\\nUse separate way arrays so two eight-entry reads and tag comparisons\\noperate in parallel. Combine the matching taken way's target into the\\nexisting predicted_next_pc selection; otherwise select PC+4. Keep the\\nlookup combinational and dependent only on registered predictor state\\nand fetch PC. There is no additional pipeline stage or EX-update bypass.\\n\\nOn each existing resolve_valid pulse, compute both full-tag matches\\nfrom the resolving PC. For a legal taken branch or legal jump, update\\nthe matching way. On a miss, select invalid way 0 first, then invalid\\nway 1, then the set's recorded victim. Write the tag and aligned target.\\nA new conditional branch starts at 2'b10; a matching taken branch\\nincrements its own counter with saturation; a jump sets 2'b11. A legal\\nnot-taken branch decrements only its matching counter, retaining the\\ntarget, and does not allocate on a miss. After any legal control-flow\\nhit, including not-taken and saturated-counter hits, or any allocation,\\nname the other way as the next victim. Replacement state changes only\\non these EX events, so wrong-path fetches cannot affect replacement.\\nAn illegal, trapping, or non-control resolution invalidates only a\\nmatching way and leaves the other tag's entry intact. Always update a\\nhit before considering allocation, ensuring that a PC cannot occupy\\nboth ways. Reset all valid and replacement bits; payloads remain\\nunreset and are masked by validity.\\n\\nPreserve the current exact predicted_next_pc capture through ID/EX,\\narchitectural next-PC comparison for every advancing EX instruction,\\nand RVFI reporting of the resolved destination. Training remains\\nqualified by in.valid && !stall && !reset. Prediction consumption,\\nrecovery priority over instruction-bus backpressure, data-stall holds,\\ntrap handling, and the external nret=1 interface retain their existing\\nbehavior. All instruction and target addresses retain their full\\narchitectural width; the indexing policy is independent of workload.\\n\\nThe expected benefit is fewer repeated conflict misses and less loss\\nof branch hysteresis. Alternating control transfers at two PCs sharing\\nthe old index should both remain warm and avoid recovery bubbles.\\nThe second tag comparison and target selection may reduce Fmax, and\\nthree hot PCs in one new set can still thrash. The eight-entry reads\\nand nearly unchanged storage make a four percent net fitness gain a\\nplausible estimate with medium confidence, not a measured result.\\nEvaluate using the unchanged formal, cocotb, cosim, CRC, and timing\\nmarker contracts, reporting bracketed cycles, three-seed median Fmax,\\ntheir full-precision fitness product, and LUT/FF usage. Compare with\\n319.45 only under the same benchmark configuration. The prior log\\ndistinguishes its scored gate from a separate 6 KB run; record the\\nactual configuration and report the requested 6 KB validation\\nseparately when needed, without altering the evaluation contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the sixteen-row direct-mapped predictor with two explicit\\neight-row way arrays, PC[4:2] set indexing, full PC[31:5] tags,\\nparallel valid/tag/counter lookup, and eight EX-updated replacement\\nbits. Implement invalid-first allocation and per-way training and\\ninvalidation exactly as described. Preserve the current ports,\\nprediction metadata, synchronous update visibility, PC advancement,\\nreset priority, and redirect handling.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing predictor tests to verify that two PCs sharing\\na set retain independent targets and counters, including PCs that\\npreviously collided at PC[5:2]. Exercise a third conflicting PC,\\ninvalid-way preference, victim selection after a not-taken hit,\\nsaturated-counter hits, and miss-without-allocation behavior.\\nCheck that stale or trapping resolution invalidates only its own\\nway and never an unrelated tag. Retain full high-address checks,\\npre-edge lookup semantics, reset invalidation, and resolve_valid=0\\nsuppression; cover both ways and every set with public predictions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add an alternating two-JAL loop at PCs separated by 64 bytes and\\nassert consecutive branch-to-target retirement after both entries\\nwarm in zero-wait operation. Add a finite conditional loop with\\nsame-set interference to exercise independent hysteresis and exits.\\nCover three-PC replacement, changing JALR targets, and stale code\\nin either way. Run relevant sequences with deterministic imem/dmem\\nstalls and load-use dependencies, including EX held behind memory,\\nand retain the existing exact PC chain, RVFI order, link-value,\\nillegal/misaligned trap, and no-wrong-path-store assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the sixteen-row direct-mapped predictor with two explicit\\neight-row way arrays, PC[4:2] set indexing, full PC[31:5] tags,\\nparallel valid/tag/counter lookup, and eight EX-updated replacement\\nbits. Implement invalid-first allocation and per-way training and\\ninvalidation exactly as described. Preserve the current ports,\\nprediction metadata, synchronous update visibility, PC advancement,\\nreset priority, and redirect handling.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing predictor tests to verify that two PCs sharing\\na set retain independent targets and counters, including PCs that\\npreviously collided at PC[5:2]. Exercise a third conflicting PC,\\ninvalid-way preference, victim selection after a not-taken hit,\\nsaturated-counter hits, and miss-without-allocation behavior.\\nCheck that stale or trapping resolution invalidates only its own\\nway and never an unrelated tag. Retain full high-address checks,\\npre-edge lookup semantics, reset invalidation, and resolve_valid=0\\nsuppression; cover both ways and every set with public predictions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add an alternating two-JAL loop at PCs separated by 64 bytes and\\nassert consecutive branch-to-target retirement after both entries\\nwarm in zero-wait operation. Add a finite conditional loop with\\nsame-set interference to exercise independent hysteresis and exits.\\nCover three-PC replacement, changing JALR targets, and stale code\\nin either way. Run relevant sequences with deterministic imem/dmem\\nstalls and load-use dependencies, including EX held behind memory,\\nand retain the existing exact PC chain, RVFI order, link-value,\\nillegal/misaligned trap, and no-wrong-path-store assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 296.07, \"delta_pct\": -7.32, \"vs_baseline\": 4.68, \"fmax_mhz\": 124.94, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4220017, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10554, \"ff\": 2227, \"seeds\": [126.36, 124.94, 120.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T14:46:28.160680Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"scribe_skipped\": \"TimeoutError: scribe timed out after 240s\"}\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Localize predictor training to remove indexed feedback muxes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 320.28 iter/s at 135.03 MHz. Its implementation\\nnotes report predictor tag lookup to counter write control as the critical\\npath in all three routed seeds; the median path is 7.41 ns, with 3.77 ns\\nlogic and 3.63 ns routing. In if_stage.sv, training reads a dynamically\\nindexed tag and counter, computes one shared resolve_hit and counter\\nupdate, then distributes the result back to the selected entry.\\n\\nPrevious micro_opt attempts changed register-file reads (+0.54% fitness)\\nand registered ALU selects (-3.92%), without reducing CoreMark cycles.\\nThe divider split left the predictor bottleneck intact, while two-way\\nprediction lost timing with essentially unchanged cycles. A local rewrite\\nof the existing training feedback path directly targets the reported\\nbottleneck without changing prediction policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 320.28 iter/s at 135.03 MHz. Its implementation\\nnotes report predictor tag lookup to counter write control as the critical\\npath in all three routed seeds; the median path is 7.41 ns, with 3.77 ns\\nlogic and 3.63 ns routing. In if_stage.sv, training reads a dynamically\\nindexed tag and counter, computes one shared resolve_hit and counter\\nupdate, then distributes the result back to the selected entry.\\n\\nPrevious micro_opt attempts changed register-file reads (+0.54% fitness)\\nand registered ALU selects (-3.92%), without reducing CoreMark cycles.\\nThe divider split left the predictor bottleneck intact, while two-way\\nprediction lost timing with essentially unchanged cycles. A local rewrite\\nof the existing training feedback path directly targets the reported\\nbottleneck without changing prediction policy.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace only the predictor's shared, dynamically indexed training logic\\nwith sixteen constant-index entry update blocks in rtl/if_stage.sv.\\nFor entry i, decode selected_i = resolve_valid && (resolve_pc[5:2] == i)\\nand independently compute hit_i = valid_q[i] &&\\n(tag_q[i] == resolve_pc[31:6]). Read that entry's tag and two counter bits\\nthrough constant indices, so tag comparison and counter next-state\\ncalculation operate locally in parallel with index decoding. Do not\\nreduce hits into a shared resolve_hit or select an old counter through\\na resolve-index mux before calculating its update. Each state bit must\\nhave one sequential owner, with reset taking precedence.\\n\\nPreserve the exact existing transition rules. Only the selected entry\\nmay change. An illegal, trapping, or non-control resolution clears valid\\nonly on a local full-tag hit. A legal jump writes valid, tag and target\\nand sets its counter to 11. A legal taken branch writes valid, tag and\\ntarget, initializes a miss to 10, and saturating-increments a hit. A\\nlegal not-taken branch saturating-decrements only a matching entry,\\nretains its target, and does nothing on a miss. Encode increment and\\ndecrement as local two-bit Boolean equations or complete transition\\ncases, including self-transitions at 11 and 00; avoid a shared arithmetic\\nresult and separate saturation qualification of a shared write enable.\\nReset clears all valid bits while leaving payloads unreset as today.\\n\\nKeep the sixteen-entry direct-mapped organization, PC[5:2] index, full\\nPC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, two-bit hysteresis, and existing\\ncombinational fetch lookup. Training remains visible only after its\\nclock edge, with no update bypass or additional register stage. Preserve\\nresolve_valid qualification from EX, exact predicted_next_pc capture,\\nrecovery priority, stall behavior, and all nret=1 bus and RVFI semantics.\\nThis is one implementation-level timing optimization of predictor\\ntraining; instruction scheduling and prediction decisions remain equal\\ncycle by cycle for the same inputs.\\n\\nRemoving the resolve-side read-mux, shared compare/update, and writeback\\ndistribution chain should raise median Fmax with unchanged bracketed\\ncycles. Estimate a five percent fitness gain, approximately 336.29 iter/s\\nat 141.78 MHz under the current scored configuration. This is a hypothesis,\\nnot a measurement. Replicated tag comparisons, increased resolve-signal\\nfanout, or losing LUTRAM packing can increase area and routing delay;\\nthe unchanged fetch/recovery path may also become limiting.\\n\\nValidate public prediction behavior against the existing policy across\\nall indices and colliding full tags, then run the unchanged unit, formal,\\nstalled cosim and FPGA gates. Compare against 320.28 using the same ELF,\\nready schedule and benchmark configuration, full-precision iterations\\ndivided by bracketed cycles, and three-seed median Fmax. Report LUT/FF\\nusage and the new critical path. Require identical scored cycle counts\\nand preserve all CRC, UART validation, memory-bound and timing-marker\\nchecks. Prior notes distinguish the scored workload from a supplemental\\n6 KB run; retain that distinction and verify the requested canonical\\n6 KB CRCs separately if the unchanged gate uses another configuration.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the shared resolve_hit and variable-index training reads and\\nwrites with constant-index generated entry blocks, local full-tag\\nmatches, and local saturating counter transitions. Preserve storage\\ncontents and transition timing, all fetch equations, reset validity,\\nmodule ports, prediction policy and EX recovery behavior. Keep the\\nrewrite confined to this training feedback path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend public-interface prediction checks across all sixteen indices\\nusing distinct full tags and targets. Exercise independent counter\\nhistories through both saturation limits, taken replacement, jump\\nreplacement, not-taken misses, and hit-only invalidation by illegal\\nor non-control resolution. Interleave same-index and different-index\\nfetch/training events, resolve_valid=0, fetch stalls and reset with\\npending training. Check pre-edge prediction and post-edge visibility,\\nfull-address matching and isolation of unselected entries while\\npreserving every existing assertion and test-runner contract.\\n\"}]}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the shared resolve_hit and variable-index training reads and\\nwrites with constant-index generated entry blocks, local full-tag\\nmatches, and local saturating counter transitions. Preserve storage\\ncontents and transition timing, all fetch equations, reset validity,\\nmodule ports, prediction policy and EX recovery behavior. Keep the\\nrewrite confined to this training feedback path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend public-interface prediction checks across all sixteen indices\\nusing distinct full tags and targets. Exercise independent counter\\nhistories through both saturation limits, taken replacement, jump\\nreplacement, not-taken misses, and hit-only invalidation by illegal\\nor non-control resolution. Interleave same-index and different-index\\nfetch/training events, resolve_valid=0, fetch stalls and reset with\\npending training. Check pre-edge prediction and post-edge visibility,\\nfull-address matching and isolation of unselected entries while\\npreserving every existing assertion and test-runner contract.\\n\"}], \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 203.44, \"delta_pct\": -36.48, \"vs_baseline\": -28.07, \"fmax_mhz\": 85.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6792, \"ff\": 3099, \"seeds\": [86.27, 85.77, 83.04], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T16:01:58.585145Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3}\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline resolution feedback with registered predictor-row reads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 320.28 iter/s at 135.03 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1 implementation notes identify the predictor's\\nindexed tag lookup through counter-write control as the critical path\\nin all three FPGA seeds; the median path is 7.41 ns. Removing nearly\\nhalf the LUTs with a separate divider improved fitness only 0.26%,\\nwith unchanged CoreMark cycles. The other prior structural attempt,\\nhyp-20260905-002-r1s1, saved only 0.75% of cycles by buffering stores.\\nThese results favor splitting the measured feedback path over another\\nbackend resource change. LESSONS.md also records the timing regression\\nfrom two-way prediction, despite essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nIn the present if_stage.sv, one cycle contains an indexed table read,\\na full-tag comparison, saturating-counter decisions, and table write\\ncontrol. Registering the selected row before comparison creates a\\nstructural pipeline boundary in this read/modify/write transaction.\\nThe prediction algorithm, capacity, instruction pipeline, and immediate\\nEX recovery can retain their existing organization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 320.28 iter/s at 135.03 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1 implementation notes identify the predictor's\\nindexed tag lookup through counter-write control as the critical path\\nin all three FPGA seeds; the median path is 7.41 ns. Removing nearly\\nhalf the LUTs with a separate divider improved fitness only 0.26%,\\nwith unchanged CoreMark cycles. The other prior structural attempt,\\nhyp-20260905-002-r1s1, saved only 0.75% of cycles by buffering stores.\\nThese results favor splitting the measured feedback path over another\\nbackend resource change. LESSONS.md also records the timing regression\\nfrom two-way prediction, despite essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nIn the present if_stage.sv, one cycle contains an indexed table read,\\na full-tag comparison, saturating-counter decisions, and table write\\ncontrol. Registering the selected row before comparison creates a\\nstructural pipeline boundary in this read/modify/write transaction.\\nThe prediction algorithm, capacity, instruction pipeline, and immediate\\nEX recovery can retain their existing organization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split resolved-instruction feedback into a row-read stage and an update\\nstage inside if_stage. Retain exactly sixteen direct-mapped entries,\\nPC[5:2] indexing, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, and the\\nexisting two-bit counter policy. The single architectural change is\\nadding one register boundary to the training transaction; do not add\\nan instruction pipeline stage or change execution-resource scheduling.\\n\\nOn edge N with resolve_valid asserted, capture the resolution's index,\\nPC tag, target, legal/branch/jump/taken flags, and the selected row's\\nvalid, tag, and two-bit counter into a maintenance register. Capture\\nraw row state here, not an already-computed tag hit or counter update:\\nthe indexed read must end at this register. The existing target need\\nnot be read for training because neither hit detection nor counter\\narithmetic consumes it. Carry a separate maintenance-valid bit.\\n\\nDuring the following cycle, compare the captured tags and compute the\\nrow update solely from the captured event and row state. Commit it on\\nedge N+1. A legal jump writes its full target and counter 11. A legal\\ntaken branch writes its target, allocates counter 10 on a miss, and\\nincrements with saturation on a hit. A legal not-taken branch only\\ndecrements a matching counter with saturation, retaining the target\\nand allocating nothing on a miss. An illegal, trapping, or non-control\\nresolution invalidates only a matching tag. Preserve the existing\\nfield-specific write enables and full architectural address widths.\\nIn particular, no live indexed tag lookup may feed the update-stage\\nwrite enable, or the proposed timing cut would disappear.\\n\\nSustain one resolution per cycle by committing the older transaction\\nwhile accepting the next. Handle their read-after-write hazard with\\nexplicit maintenance forwarding: when the committing transaction and\\nincoming resolution address the same index, capture the older\\ntransaction's post-update valid/tag/counter as the incoming row\\nsnapshot instead of the pre-edge table values. Forward the complete\\nlogical state of those fields, including invalidation, allocation,\\nsaturation, and unchanged fields. Compare indices for this bypass,\\nnot tags: different PCs aliasing one row require forwarding too.\\nThis bypass is confined to the maintenance pipeline. After draining,\\ntable state must equal applying the accepted resolution stream in\\norder under the original update policy; no event may be lost or\\ncounted twice, including consecutive updates to the same branch.\\n\\nSet maintenance-valid from resolve_valid every non-reset edge, rather\\nthan holding it on a frontend or backend stall. A previously accepted\\nevent commits once even if IF is stalled, a later divide is waiting,\\nor a younger instruction redirects. Acceptance still uses the existing\\nadvancing-EX resolve_valid qualification; held EX instructions cannot\\nenqueue repeatedly. Reset has priority over both capture and commit,\\nclears all table valid bits and maintenance-valid, and cannot permit\\na pre-reset transaction to repopulate the predictor afterward.\\n\\nFetch continues to consult committed table state only. There is no\\npending-update bypass to predicted_next_pc, no training backpressure,\\nand no extra delay on the EX actual-next-PC comparison or redirect.\\nPreserve the exact prediction accepted with each instruction through\\nID/EX and retain resolved architectural pc_next at RVFI retirement.\\nRecovery still overrides imem stalls, and dmem/divide holds keep their\\ncurrent priorities. Training becomes visible one cycle later than\\ntoday; a very short cold loop can therefore mispredict an additional\\ntime. Warm self-jumps must still sustain one retirement per cycle,\\nand each recovery retains its existing single younger-slot bubble.\\n\\nExpect a 6% fitness improvement, with medium confidence, from separating\\nthe table-read mux from tag comparison and update write control. This\\nis an estimate, not a measured result. The maintenance bypass could\\nbecome critical, delayed training can increase cycles, and fetch or\\nEX recovery may set the new timing limit. Evaluate the unchanged\\nmarker-bracketed stalled CoreMark workload and three-seed median Fmax;\\nreport exact iterations/cycles, LUT/FF usage, and post-route critical\\npaths. Compare the fitness product with 320.28 using the same workload\\nconfiguration, identifying the configuration in the results. Preserve\\nnret=1 and every external port, all RVFI order/operand/trap rules,\\ndecoder legality, divider and ALTOPS behavior, memory handshakes and\\nbounds, and the required CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. All formal\\nconfigurations, test harnesses, and evaluation gates remain intact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert the registered row-read and resolution-event stage ahead\\nof tag comparison and counter-update generation. Implement one\\ncommit and one capture per cycle with same-index post-update\\nforwarding of valid/tag/counter, field-specific table writes,\\nexact event validity, and reset cancellation. Keep fetch lookup,\\nprediction policy, module ports, PC advancement, and immediate\\nrecovery behavior as specified. Document the two-edge training\\nschedule and keep the maintenance bypass off the fetch path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing behavior checks with a sequential reference\\nmodel and cycle-exact capture/commit assertions. Update observation\\ntiming for the intentional extra training stage while retaining\\nevery existing policy, full-address, reset, and no-fetch-bypass\\nassertion. Test one accepted event per clock, same-PC counter\\nsaturation and threshold crossings, different-tag same-index\\nallocation, invalidation followed by allocation, not-taken misses,\\nno-op updates, different indices, idle drain, and reset with a\\npending update. Check predictions at each edge and final table\\nbehavior after draining a randomized resolution stream.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cold and warm self-jumps, short loops, alternating\\nsame-index control-flow PCs, changing JALR targets, and stale-code\\ninvalidations to exercise the added feedback latency and forwarding.\\nAssert sustained warm one-per-cycle retirement and the unchanged\\nredirect bubble length, with explicit expectations for extra cold\\nmisses. Cover a pending update draining during imem/dmem stalls,\\ndivide waits, redirects, and reset; prove exactly-once training\\nfor held EX instructions. Retain all RVFI order, PC chain, operand,\\ntrap, wrong-path-store, memory, and arithmetic assertions, and run\\nthe unchanged full cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA evaluation gates.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert the registered row-read and resolution-event stage ahead\\nof tag comparison and counter-update generation. Implement one\\ncommit and one capture per cycle with same-index post-update\\nforwarding of valid/tag/counter, field-specific table writes,\\nexact event validity, and reset cancellation. Keep fetch lookup,\\nprediction policy, module ports, PC advancement, and immediate\\nrecovery behavior as specified. Document the two-edge training\\nschedule and keep the maintenance bypass off the fetch path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing behavior checks with a sequential reference\\nmodel and cycle-exact capture/commit assertions. Update observation\\ntiming for the intentional extra training stage while retaining\\nevery existing policy, full-address, reset, and no-fetch-bypass\\nassertion. Test one accepted event per clock, same-PC counter\\nsaturation and threshold crossings, different-tag same-index\\nallocation, invalidation followed by allocation, not-taken misses,\\nno-op updates, different indices, idle drain, and reset with a\\npending update. Check predictions at each edge and final table\\nbehavior after draining a randomized resolution stream.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cold and warm self-jumps, short loops, alternating\\nsame-index control-flow PCs, changing JALR targets, and stale-code\\ninvalidations to exercise the added feedback latency and forwarding.\\nAssert sustained warm one-per-cycle retirement and the unchanged\\nredirect bubble length, with explicit expectations for extra cold\\nmisses. Cover a pending update draining during imem/dmem stalls,\\ndivide waits, redirects, and reset; prove exactly-once training\\nfor held EX instructions. Retain all RVFI order, PC chain, operand,\\ntrap, wrong-path-store, memory, and arithmetic assertions, and run\\nthe unchanged full cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA evaluation gates.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 340.22, \"delta_pct\": 6.23, \"vs_baseline\": 20.3, \"fmax_mhz\": 143.41, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5920, \"ff\": 2382, \"seeds\": [143.41, 137.51, 155.74], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Registered predictor maintenance: hyp-20260905-002-r3s1\\n\\nImplemented the proposed training pipeline. All local evaluation gates pass.\\nThe unchanged gate reports **340.22 iter/s**, with **143.41 MHz** three-seed\\nmedian Fmax, **5,920 LUT4s**, and **2,382 FFs**. Using the unrounded fitness\\nproduct, the improvement over the supplied 320.28 is **6.2245848777%**.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- Only `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` changes hardware. At edge N it captures\\n  the resolution index, full 26-bit tag, aligned 30-bit target, four event\\n  flags, and raw selected-row valid/tag/counter. It does not capture a hit\\n  result, a computed counter, or the old target.\\n- Captured tags and counter drive the following cycle's update. Edge N+1\\n  commits it while capturing the next event. Update write enables have no\\n  live indexed tag/counter input. Valid, tag/target, and counter writes\\n  retain the original field-specific policy, including saturated no-ops.\\n- Same-index maintenance forwarding captures the committing transaction's\\n  complete post-update valid/tag/counter, including unchanged fields,\\n  invalidation, and replacement by another full tag. This bypass never\\n  reaches fetch prediction.\\n- Maintenance-valid is assigned from resolve-valid on every non-reset edge.\\n  IF/backend holds and younger redirects do not hold or cancel accepted\\n  training. Reset cancels capture and commit, clears maintenance-valid and\\n  all table valids, and leaves unused payloads unreset.\\n- Capacity, indexing, tag/target widths, prediction algorithm, EX resolution\\n  qualification and immediate recovery, instruction-stage count, execution\\n  scheduling, nret=1, external ports, RVFI, decoder, divider, and ALTOPS are\\n  unchanged. No architectural deviation from the hypothesis.\\n\\n## Tests\\n\\n- `test_if_stage.py`: independent sequential policy model checked before\\n  and after edges, exact committed table fields and captured raw snapshots,\\n  one event per edge, counter saturation/thresholds, target retention,\\n  different-tag same-index replacement, invalidation/allocation, misses,\\n  no-ops, different indices, idle drain, full addresses, no fetch bypass,\\n  and reset cancellation. A deterministic 1,200-cycle random stream includes\\n  aliases, bubbles, stalls, redirects, and resets, followed by a full probe.\\n- `test_pipeline.py`: applies the model to every existing pipeline test and\\n  checks redirects against the prediction actually accepted with the\\n  instruction. Adds short branch loops and pending-update drain during\\n  imem stalls, dmem holds, divide waits, overlapping redirects, and reset.\\n  Existing JALR target changes, stale code/traps, held-EX exactly-once\\n  checks, operand/order/PC-chain checks, wrong-path stores, memory, and\\n  arithmetic assertions remain active.\\n- Intentional latency expectation changes: self-jumps have two cold misses,\\n  each followed by one younger-slot bubble; warm retirement remains one\\n  per cycle. Alternating full-tag aliases can hit an older committed row\\n  before its pending replacement commits, so that test now asserts the\\n  exact delayed-update prediction/redirect sequence instead of all misses.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings/errors.\\n- Full cocotb suite: **7 pytest runners passed**, including **38 pipeline\\n  tests**, **4 IF tests**, and both real-arithmetic and ALTOPS ALU builds.\\n- Local formal status: **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, using the unchanged\\n  `bash formal/run_all.sh`, `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl`, `CORE_NAME=bench`,\\n  `JOBS=6`; automatic nret=1 selection chose `wrapper_si.sv` and\\n  `checks_si.cfg`. The first shell invocation exited before generation\\n  because its Python lacked PyYAML; selecting the installed conda Python\\n  resolved this. First actual proof run passed; no counterexample and no\\n  RTL fix/retry for formal. Formal-deep was not run; arithmetic is unchanged.\\n- Unchanged cosim evaluator: **PASS, 2 ELFs** (selftest full RVFI comparison\\n  and stalled CoreMark CRC/UART validation).\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench`, with `NRET=1` and\\n  `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: PASS, all three seeds routed and benchmark\\n  validation completed. `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Formal, cosim, and FPGA run in an isolated `$TMPDIR` copy; 48 evaluated\\n  RTL/config/harness/source files were byte-compared with the worktree.\\n  No protected harness, source, configuration, or other core was edited.\\n\\n## Workload configuration and exact performance\\n\\nThe checked-in `bench/programs/Makefile` defaults to `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000`,\\n`-O3`, and the evaluator's CRC goldens are for that 2 KB configuration.\\nThis conflicts with the prompt's 6 KB description. The gates were preserved.\\nBoth sizes were measured separately using the unchanged harness,\\n`ITERATIONS=10`, `--bench --istall --dstall`, default stall seed\\n`0xDEADBEEF`, and only the `0x10000100` start / `0x10000104` stop bracket.\\nThe original HEAD RTL was rebuilt in scratch to measure baseline cycles.\\nBaseline Fmax and resource counts below are the supplied winner's values;\\nbaseline FPGA routing was not repeated.\\n\\n| Configuration | Original RTL cycles | New RTL cycles | Original iter/s at 135.03 MHz | New iter/s at 143.41 MHz |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| Unchanged gate: 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 4,215,937 | 4,215,262 | 320.2846721856 | 340.2161004464 |\\n| Supplemental: 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 30,823,120 | 30,822,790 | 43.8080246257 | 46.5272611597 |\\n\\nThe 2 KB baseline reproduces **320.28** under the same configuration.\\nThe new exact throughput is `10 / 4215262 = 2.372331779139707e-6`\\niterations/cycle; fitness is `143410000 * 10 / 4215262`.\\nIt saves 675 bracketed cycles. The 6 KB result saves 330 cycles and improves\\nthe same-configuration fitness by **6.2071653704%**; comparing its absolute\\nscore directly with 320.28 would mix workloads.\\n\\nThe primary gate validates its unchanged 2 KB goldens: seedcrc=e9f5,\\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\\nThe supplemental 6 KB run explicitly checked the requested crclist=d4b0,\\ncrcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, plus seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949.\\nBoth baseline and candidate supplemental runs reached EBREAK, had\\n`oob=false`, reported 10 iterations and the literal\\n`Correct operation validated.`, and contained valid timing markers.\\nFor new 6 KB RTL, start=53,747 and stop=30,876,537. The old stop was\\n30,876,867 with the same start. The UART's compiler-flags string remains\\nthe inherited hardcoded `-O2 -march=rv32im`; the actual build command is\\n`-O3`. The UART size field is per algorithm (666 for 2 KB, 2000 for 6 KB).\\n\\n## Three-seed post-route timing\\n\\nDevice: `GW2A-LV18QN88C8/I7`, family `GW2A-18C`, unchanged Tang Nano 20K\\nconstraints. These are final post-route reports, not placement estimates.\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Critical path ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 1 | 143.41 | 6.97 | 3.95 | 3.02 |\\n| 2 | 137.51 | 7.27 | 2.32 | 4.95 |\\n| 3 | 155.74 | 6.42 | 2.32 | 4.10 |\\n\\n- Seed 1: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_175.Q` (`cpu.id_ex_w[54]`) through\\n  hazard/control logic to `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_63.D`.\\n- Seed 2: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_161.Q` (`cpu.id_ex_w[11]`) through\\n  hazard/divide-wait control to `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_71.RESET`.\\n- Seed 3: same source and hazard/divide-wait control class as seed 2,\\n  ending at `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_98.RESET`.\\n\\nMedian Fmax increases from 135.03 to 143.41 MHz. The median critical path\\nfalls from the supplied 7.41 ns to 6.97 ns. None of the three final\\ncritical paths crosses the predictor indexed maintenance lookup.\\nAll seeds use 5,920 LUT4s and 2,382 DFFs, versus 5,871 / 2,292 supplied\\nfor the winner: **+49 LUTs, +90 FFs**. The maintenance payload contains\\n94 source-level bits before synthesis optimization.\\n\\n## Concerns and evidence\\n\\nThe intended extra cold self-loop miss remains a performance cost, and\\nalias timing changes can help or hurt particular streams. Observed cycle\\nchanges also include the deterministic stall generator's changed phase.\\nMaintenance forwarding is combinational but was not the reported timing\\nlimit in any seed. Hazard/control routing is the new measured limit;\\nthis change intentionally does not alter it.\\n\\nScratch evidence directory:\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-r3-validation-3dg8_bd1/`\\n\\nIt contains `formal-gate.log`, `cocotb-gate.log`,\\n`cosim-gate-result.log`, `fpga-gate.log`, `comparison.json`, the raw\\n`baseline-2k.jsonl`, `baseline-6k.jsonl`, `candidate-6k.jsonl`, and\\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` with the complete\\npost-route paths. Implementation notes are at the worktree root as\\nrequested by instruction 5 and consumed by the orchestrator's notes reader.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T15:53:57.352318Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 (regression, +6.23%): Registering raw predictor-row reads before tag comparison/counter writes, with same-index post-update forwarding for back-to-back resolutions, removed the maintenance lookup from all three critical paths and raised median Fmax from 135.03 to 143.41 MHz with essentially unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles, exposing hazard/divide-wait control as the next timing limit.\"}\n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Row-local predictor metadata training to remove the indexed feedback path\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing 16-entry direct-mapped predictor already reduced stalled\\nCoreMark cycles by 6.14%. The two-way experiment hyp-20260905-003-r2s2\\nmeasured 4,220,017 cycles versus 4,215,937 for the direct-mapped winner,\\nwhile lowering median Fmax to 124.94 MHz. More lookup complexity has\\ntherefore not paid for itself in this core. The current winner,\\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1, reports all three post-route critical paths through\\npredictor tag lookup and counter-write control. Its median path runs\\nfrom tag storage to counter WRE in 7.41 ns, with 3.77 ns logic and\\n3.63 ns routing. The current RTL reads tag_q[resolve_index] and\\ncounter_q[resolve_index], computes a shared hit/update decision, and\\nwrites back through the same variable index. Removing this training\\nfeedback path is a focused opportunity to raise the current 135.03 MHz\\nmedian Fmax without changing prediction accuracy or recovery latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The existing 16-entry direct-mapped predictor already reduced stalled\\nCoreMark cycles by 6.14%. The two-way experiment hyp-20260905-003-r2s2\\nmeasured 4,220,017 cycles versus 4,215,937 for the direct-mapped winner,\\nwhile lowering median Fmax to 124.94 MHz. More lookup complexity has\\ntherefore not paid for itself in this core. The current winner,\\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1, reports all three post-route critical paths through\\npredictor tag lookup and counter-write control. Its median path runs\\nfrom tag storage to counter WRE in 7.41 ns, with 3.77 ns logic and\\n3.63 ns routing. The current RTL reads tag_q[resolve_index] and\\ncounter_q[resolve_index], computes a shared hit/update decision, and\\nwrites back through the same variable index. Removing this training\\nfeedback path is a focused opportunity to raise the current 135.03 MHz\\nmedian Fmax without changing prediction accuracy or recovery latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Partition the existing predictor into sixteen independently updated\\nmetadata rows and the existing indexed target store. Each metadata row\\ncontains its own valid bit, full 26-bit PC[31:6] tag, and two-bit\\nsaturating counter. Keep sixteen entries, PC[5:2] indexing, full\\naligned target[31:2] storage, and the same 944 logical state bits.\\nImplement tag, counter, and valid as local scalar registers inside a\\nconstant-index generate loop. Each row compares its own registered tag\\ndirectly with resolve_pc[31:6] and computes its own counter next state.\\nDecode resolve_pc[5:2] into row enables in parallel with these local\\ncomparisons. No training decision may read metadata through a variable\\nresolve index or broadcast a selected row's hit/counter back to every\\nrow. Read-only views of the local registers may serve fetch selection.\\n\\nRetain the current single indexed fetch selection and full-tag match:\\nvalid && tag_match && counter[1] selects the stored target; otherwise\\nselect PC+4. Keep the 16x30 target array separate, with its existing\\nindexed synchronous write on a legal taken branch or jump. That target\\nwrite is independent of the prior tag or counter. This permits target\\nstorage to retain its current inferred implementation while metadata\\ntraining uses local register-to-register paths. The intended tradeoff\\nis more metadata flops and parallel tag comparisons in exchange for\\nremoving the shared metadata read mux and counter write-port feedback.\\n\\nPreserve the current state transitions exactly. Only the selected row\\nacts on an existing resolve_valid pulse. A legal jump writes its tag\\nand target, sets valid, and sets its counter to 11. A legal taken branch\\nwrites tag/target and valid, initializes a miss to 10, or increments a\\nmatching counter with saturation at 11. A legal not-taken branch only\\ndecrements a matching counter with saturation at 00, retaining its\\ntarget and never allocating on a miss. Express saturation in each\\nrow's next-data function, avoiding a shared indexed saturation test in\\nwrite control. An illegal, trapping, or non-control resolution clears\\nonly a matching row's valid bit. Reset clears valid bits with priority\\nover training; invalid tag, target, and counter payloads remain masked\\nand need no reset. Unselected rows retain all state.\\n\\nThis is a change to predictor storage and training organization with\\nno extra update stage or forwarding bypass. A fetch accepted on an\\nupdate edge must still consume the pre-edge prediction, including\\nsame-PC and same-index collisions; the update becomes visible after\\nthat edge. Preserve the exact prediction captured with each instruction,\\nEX comparison against the architectural next PC for every advancing\\ninstruction, and the current resolve_valid qualification across data\\nstalls and divide waits. Keep recovery priority over imem stalls,\\nmisalignment and illegal-instruction behavior, resolved RVFI next-PC\\nreporting, and all external nret=1 ports unchanged.\\n\\nExpect approximately five percent higher fitness from Fmax alone,\\nwith identical bracketed cycles for identical program and ready\\nschedules. This is an unmeasured estimate with medium confidence:\\nadditional metadata flops, resolve-PC fanout, and fetch mux routing\\ncould offset the shorter training path, or expose the existing EX\\nprediction-mismatch recovery path as the next bottleneck. Inspect\\nsynthesis and three-seed post-route reports to confirm the shared\\ntag-read-to-counter-WRE path is removed and report LUT4/FF costs.\\nRun the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, cosim, CRC, and marker gates.\\nCompute fitness from full-precision iterations/cycle times median\\nFmax and compare with 320.28 under the identical benchmark setup.\\nPrior notes distinguish the scored workload from the requested 6 KB\\nworkload; report the 6 KB run and its canonical CRCs separately if\\nneeded, without modifying either evaluation contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the variable-index metadata training read-modify-write with\\nsixteen constant-index local valid/tag/counter register banks and\\nper-row tag comparison, counter next state, and update enable.\\nPreserve the indexed target array and single indexed fetch lookup,\\nall predictor policies, full address widths, pre-edge read behavior,\\nreset semantics, PC update priority, and existing module ports.\\nRemove the shared resolve_hit and selected-counter feedback path;\\nintroduce no extra prediction or training latency.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add a deterministic reference-model comparison of public predicted\\nnext-PC and fetch-PC behavior before and after each edge. Cover all\\nsixteen indices, high tag/target bits, repeated same-row updates,\\nalternating alias tags, saturation in both directions, allocation,\\njump target replacement, not-taken misses, matching invalidation,\\nand preservation of unrelated tags. Exercise simultaneous fetch\\nand training on the same PC and on conflicting PCs, fetch stalls\\nwhile EX trains, resolve_valid=0, redirect/flush priority, and reset\\nduring an offered update. Preserve every existing assertion and\\nrun the existing pipeline tests for exactly-once training during\\nmemory/divide holds, warm-loop throughput, stale predictions,\\ntraps, RVFI order, and absence of wrong-path stores.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the variable-index metadata training read-modify-write with\\nsixteen constant-index local valid/tag/counter register banks and\\nper-row tag comparison, counter next state, and update enable.\\nPreserve the indexed target array and single indexed fetch lookup,\\nall predictor policies, full address widths, pre-edge read behavior,\\nreset semantics, PC update priority, and existing module ports.\\nRemove the shared resolve_hit and selected-counter feedback path;\\nintroduce no extra prediction or training latency.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add a deterministic reference-model comparison of public predicted\\nnext-PC and fetch-PC behavior before and after each edge. Cover all\\nsixteen indices, high tag/target bits, repeated same-row updates,\\nalternating alias tags, saturation in both directions, allocation,\\njump target replacement, not-taken misses, matching invalidation,\\nand preservation of unrelated tags. Exercise simultaneous fetch\\nand training on the same PC and on conflicting PCs, fetch stalls\\nwhile EX trains, resolve_valid=0, redirect/flush priority, and reset\\nduring an offered update. Preserve every existing assertion and\\nrun the existing pipeline tests for exactly-once training during\\nmemory/divide holds, warm-loop throughput, stale predictions,\\ntraps, RVFI order, and absence of wrong-path stores.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 350.46, \"delta_pct\": 9.42, \"vs_baseline\": 23.92, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.75, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6567, \"ff\": 2635, \"seeds\": [147.75, 137.44, 154.08], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented row-local predictor metadata training in `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` and added a deterministic public-output reference test in `cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py`. The requested notes are at the worktree root, which is explicitly allowed by `tools/orchestrator.py` and is the location its `_read_notes` function reads. No other source or evaluation-contract file was changed.\\n\\nEach of sixteen constant-index generate blocks owns a scalar valid bit, full 26-bit PC[31:6] tag, and two-bit counter. Its resolve-index decode operates in parallel with its local full-tag comparison. Counter saturation is expressed in local next data. Training never reads metadata through a variable index or broadcasts a selected row's hit/counter. Read-only metadata views supply the unchanged indexed fetch lookup.\\n\\nThe separate 16x30 target array retains one indexed synchronous write on legal jumps and legal taken branches. Its write enable does not depend on prior metadata. The predictor still has 944 logical state bits. Legal jumps train strongly taken; taken branches allocate weakly taken or saturating-increment a hit; not-taken branches decrement only a hit and preserve targets; non-control and illegal/trapping resolutions invalidate only a matching tag. Reset has priority over all training and clears only valid bits. All unselected state and invalid payloads retain their previous values.\\n\\nThere is no added stage or bypass. A fetch accepted on an update edge uses the pre-edge prediction, including same-PC and same-index collisions. The PC update priority, exact prediction captured with the instruction, EX architectural-next-PC comparison, resolve qualification during memory/divide holds, recovery, traps, RVFI reporting, and complete nret=1 external interface are unchanged. No architectural deviation from the hypothesis was needed.\\n\\nThe new reference test checks the complete public IF output bundle and fetch address before and after every checked edge. Directed coverage includes all sixteen rows, every tag bit, every stored target bit, high addresses and PC wraparound, both saturation directions, allocation, not-taken misses, repeated target replacement, alternating alias tags, matching invalidation, unrelated-row preservation, simultaneous fetch/training collisions, held fetches while training, resolve_valid=0, flush/redirect/stall priority, and reset during an offered update. A fixed-seed 512-cycle mixed schedule adds combinations of these events. Every existing assertion was retained.\\n\\nLocal validation passed:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS without warnings.\\n- Complete cocotb suite: PASS, seven pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU builds, all 33 existing pipeline tests, and three IF tests. Existing tests retain their checks for exactly-once training through memory/divide holds, warm-loop throughput, stale predictions, traps, RVFI order/PC chaining, and absence of wrong-path stores.\\n- Unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench`: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed. The script selected the nret=1 wrapper and configuration. Zero formal counterexamples and zero RTL fix retries. This is the standard ALTOPS suite; formal-deep was not needed for this predictor-only change.\\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim` gate: PASS, both selftest RVFI comparison and CoreMark CRC validation.\\n- Unchanged FPGA evaluator: all three seeds routed successfully; EBREAK completion, memory bounds, UART validation/CRCs, and start/stop marker gates passed.\\n- Synthesized-netlist inspection: exactly 464 metadata flops (16 x (1+26+2)) and eight RAM16SDP4 blocks, all belonging to the indexed target store. A combinational-cone audit of all 96 counter data/control input pins found no dependencies on another metadata row. All target RAM WRE cones were independent of metadata. The shared selected-tag-to-counter-RAM-WRE feedback structure is absent.\\n\\nFinal timing measurements use the same OSS CAD Suite Yosys 0.64+149, git `2dc69a757-dirty`, as the prior winner, with the unchanged synthesis script, single-retirement FPGA wrapper, device/constraints, and nextpnr seed set. Simulation builds use OSS Verilator 5.047. An initial Homebrew-toolchain diagnostic run was superseded by the full reference-toolchain run; the final results below come from the latter.\\n\\n| Metric | Prior winner | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Median Fmax | 135.03 MHz | 147.75 MHz |\\n| Fitness | 320.28 iter/s | 350.46 iter/s |\\n| LUT4 | 5,871 | 6,567 |\\n| FF | 2,292 | 2,635 |\\n| Bracketed stalled cycles | 4,215,937 | 4,215,937 |\\n\\nThe scored workload remains the checked-in 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 build with both random bus stalls and default seed 0xDEADBEEF. Full-precision iterations/cycle is `10 / 4215937 = 2.3719519527924634e-6`. Fitness is `147.75 * 1e6 * 10 / 4215937 = 350.45590102508646` iter/s, or **+9.421725% versus 320.28**. The evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark=0.000002` was not used in this calculation. LUT4 cost rises by 696 (+11.85%); FF cost rises by 343 (+14.97%).\\n\\nAll final post-route critical paths now start at an ID/EX register holding resolve-PC bits and end at a local metadata enable:\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax | Source under cpu.u_id | Destination under cpu.u_if | Total | Logic | Routing |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 147.75 MHz | reg_q_DFFRE_Q_14.Q | metadata[4].valid_q_DFFRE_Q.CE | 6.77 ns | 3.05 ns | 3.72 ns |\\n| 2 | 137.44 MHz | reg_q_DFFRE_Q_19.Q | metadata[4].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE | 7.28 ns | 2.76 ns | 4.51 ns |\\n| 3 | 154.08 MHz | reg_q_DFFRE_Q_3.Q | metadata[10].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE | 6.49 ns | 2.58 ns | 3.91 ns |\\n\\nThe prior median path was tag storage to indexed counter WRE, 7.41 ns (3.77 logic + 3.63 routing). That path is removed. Resolve-PC fanout and local full-tag matching now limit timing; the EX prediction-mismatch recovery path did not become the reported critical path. Seed spread and the increased LUT/FF cost remain relevant concerns. The measured fitness gain exceeds the hypothesis's unmeasured five-percent estimate, but one three-seed experiment does not establish repeatability across toolchains or placements.\\n\\nAn independent cosim build of the original HEAD bench RTL used exactly the same ELF and ready schedules as the candidate. For stalled 2 KB runs, baseline and candidate bracketed cycles were identical: 4,215,937 (seed 0xDEADBEEF), 4,214,768 (seed 1), and 4,216,052 (seed 42). Both complete final-retirement records and all completion/marker/UART fields matched, including absolute start/stop cycles. For the scored seed, start=15,229 and stop=4,231,166. ELF SHA-256: `c7b024c15b74fad435730b0c6fbb7d853635542de982dba0ac7efbe2378deddc`.\\n\\nThe requested supplemental 6 KB workload was compiled separately using the existing `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options and ITERATIONS=10. Neither evaluation contract was changed. With both random stalls and seed 0xDEADBEEF, baseline and candidate both measured **30,823,120** bracketed cycles (start=53,747; stop=30,876,867), with identical final records and UART. EBREAK, bounds, markers, and the literal `Correct operation validated.` passed. Exact CRCs were seed=0x8a02, list=0xd4b0, matrix=0xbe52, state=0x5e47, final=0x5949. This corresponds to 47.93479699654026 iter/s at the candidate median Fmax; it is reported separately from the 2 KB fitness comparison. The 6 KB ELF SHA-256 is `df1b781ed576e32298f35b4151eca394fc3c37cdb767291a04c5e7c7a0e0eadc`.\\n\\nAll generated validation artifacts and unchanged gate copies are under `$TMPDIR/hyp-20260905-003-r3s2-validation`; the baseline and supplemental builds use sibling directories ending in `-baseline` and `-6k`. Final post-route reports are `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` within the validation directory. Logs alongside it use prefix `hyp-20260905-003-r3s2-`: `formal.log`, `unit-oss.log`, `synth-oss.log`, `build-oss.log`, `cosim-oss.log`, and `fpga-oss.log`. Structured evidence is in `structure.json`, `metrics.json`, and `cycle-equivalence.json` with the same prefix. No generated artifact or helper script was added to the worktree.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T15:55:45.939086Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r3s2 (improvement, +9.42%): Constant-index predictor metadata registers with row-local tag comparison and counter saturation remove the shared tag-read-to-counter-WRE feedback path without an extra update stage, raising median Fmax from 135.03 to 147.75 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles at +11.85% LUT4 and +14.97% FF cost, with resolve-PC fanout and local tag matching becoming critical.\"}\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Balance predictor training tag comparisons at LUT reduction boundaries\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz median Fmax.\\nIts saved final three-seed timing reports all start at ID/EX resolve-PC\\ntag bits and end at a local predictor metadata clock enable. Mapping\\nthose launch flops through synth.json identifies PC bits 17, 12, and\\n28. The median path takes 6.77 ns, including 3.05 ns logic and 3.72 ns\\nrouting, and traverses seven LUTs. The saved validation RTL matches\\nthe current cores/bench/rtl files byte-for-byte. Evidence is under\\n.tmp/hyp-20260905-003-r3s2-validation/cores/bench/generated/ in\\nsynth.json and pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the bench experiment log show that register-file read\\nbalancing gained only 0.54%, ALU predecode lost 3.92%, and the earlier\\nbroad local-training micro_opt lost 36.48%. The subsequent winning\\nimplementation retained indexed target storage and localized only\\nmetadata. Its remaining full-tag equality expressions are absorbed\\ninto long, irregular mapped update cones. A bounded combinational\\nreduction rewrite now targets that measured path while retaining the\\nwinning storage organization and every prediction decision.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz median Fmax.\\nIts saved final three-seed timing reports all start at ID/EX resolve-PC\\ntag bits and end at a local predictor metadata clock enable. Mapping\\nthose launch flops through synth.json identifies PC bits 17, 12, and\\n28. The median path takes 6.77 ns, including 3.05 ns logic and 3.72 ns\\nrouting, and traverses seven LUTs. The saved validation RTL matches\\nthe current cores/bench/rtl files byte-for-byte. Evidence is under\\n.tmp/hyp-20260905-003-r3s2-validation/cores/bench/generated/ in\\nsynth.json and pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the bench experiment log show that register-file read\\nbalancing gained only 0.54%, ALU predecode lost 3.92%, and the earlier\\nbroad local-training micro_opt lost 36.48%. The subsequent winning\\nimplementation retained indexed target storage and localized only\\nmetadata. Its remaining full-tag equality expressions are absorbed\\ninto long, irregular mapped update cones. A bounded combinational\\nreduction rewrite now targets that measured path while retaining the\\nwinning storage organization and every prediction decision.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure only the resolve-side full-tag equality in each existing\\nmetadata row of rtl/if_stage.sv. Replace the unconstrained 26-bit\\ncomparison with an explicit balanced tree suited to four-input LUTs.\\nFirst compute thirteen independent two-bit equalities: pair_eq[g]\\ncompares tag_q[2*g +: 2] with resolve_pc[6 + 2*g +: 2], for g=0..12.\\nEach equality has exactly four variable inputs. Next form four\\nparallel reductions over pair_eq[3:0], [6:4], [9:7], and [12:10].\\nAND those four results to obtain full_tag_equal, then qualify it with\\nthe row's valid_q to obtain the existing row_hit. All 26 tag bits\\nparticipate; no shortened tag, hash, or approximate match is allowed.\\n\\nPreserve these local pair and group boundaries with source-level\\nYosys keep attributes on their combinational wires, so the mapper\\ncannot simply absorb the whole expression back into the current\\nserial update cone. Use portable combinational SystemVerilog in all\\nsimulation, formal, and synthesis builds. The intended equality\\nnetwork has three LUT4 levels before validity/update qualification.\\nConfirm the boundaries survive the unchanged synthesis flow by\\ninspecting the mapped netlist; an equivalent source expression alone\\ndoes not demonstrate the intended timing optimization. Keep reset,\\nresolve_valid, row selection, and event qualification outside the\\nequality tree. No register, enable, or maintenance stage is added.\\n\\nRetain the sixteen scalar valid/tag/counter rows, their current\\nfield-specific update rules, counter encoding and saturation, and\\nthe separate indexed 16x30 target array. Fetch still uses the current\\nindexed full-tag lookup. A legal jump allocates strongly taken; a\\ntaken branch allocates weakly taken or increments a hit; a not-taken\\nbranch decrements only a hit and retains the target; a non-control\\nor illegal/trapping resolution invalidates only a matching row.\\nInvalid payloads remain masked, reset clears valid bits with priority,\\nand unselected rows retain state. Training still becomes visible\\nafter the same edge, with no update bypass. Predictions, redirects,\\nand accepted instructions must agree cycle by cycle with the current\\nRTL for identical input schedules, including same-row collisions.\\n\\nThis is one logic-mapping micro-optimization. It leaves pipeline\\nscheduling, predictor policy and capacity, instruction latency,\\narithmetic and ALTOPS, decoder legality, memory handshakes, RVFI,\\nnret=1, and every top-level port unchanged. The estimated fitness\\ngain is five percent through Fmax alone, with unchanged bracketed\\ncycles. Confidence is medium: preserving reduction boundaries can\\nincrease LUT use or routing, resolve-PC fanout remains, and another\\ncontrol path may become limiting. This estimate is not a measurement.\\n\\nRun the existing public-output predictor reference tests and pipeline\\ntests, then the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and\\nthree-seed FPGA gates. Audit equality depth, metadata flop count,\\ntarget RAM inference, LUT/FF cost, and the new critical paths. Compare\\noriginal and candidate using the same toolchain, ELF, and ready\\nschedules, requiring identical marker-bracketed cycles. Calculate\\nfitness using full-precision iterations/cycles and median Fmax.\\nPrior notes distinguish the saved 350.46 scored workload from a\\nsupplemental 6 KB run; keep comparisons within the same configuration\\nand also verify the requested 6 KB canonical CRCs, validation UART\\nliteral, timing markers, and memory bounds. Change no evaluation file\\nor correctness assertion.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement each row's resolve tag match as thirteen two-bit\\ncomparisons, four parallel group reductions, and one final\\nreduction, preserving the pair/group wires with local keep\\nattributes. Feed the resulting exact equality into the existing\\nvalid-qualified row_hit. Preserve all sequential logic, fetch\\nselection, indexed target storage, ports, and update timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Retain the independent public-output policy model and all current\\nassertions. Extend directed alias coverage with mismatches in\\nseveral comparison groups at once and with successive aliases\\ncrossing pair/group boundaries. Exercise matching and mismatching\\ntaken, not-taken, and invalidating resolutions while fetch uses\\nthe same row, checking predictions before and after each edge.\\nContinue to test every tag bit, invalid-row masking, reset,\\nhigh addresses, saturation, and preservation of unrelated rows.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement each row's resolve tag match as thirteen two-bit\\ncomparisons, four parallel group reductions, and one final\\nreduction, preserving the pair/group wires with local keep\\nattributes. Feed the resulting exact equality into the existing\\nvalid-qualified row_hit. Preserve all sequential logic, fetch\\nselection, indexed target storage, ports, and update timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Retain the independent public-output policy model and all current\\nassertions. Extend directed alias coverage with mismatches in\\nseveral comparison groups at once and with successive aliases\\ncrossing pair/group boundaries. Exercise matching and mismatching\\ntaken, not-taken, and invalidating resolutions while fetch uses\\nthe same row, checking predictions before and after each edge.\\nContinue to test every tag bit, invalid-row masking, reset,\\nhigh addresses, saturation, and preservation of unrelated rows.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 299.22, \"delta_pct\": -14.62, \"vs_baseline\": 5.8, \"fmax_mhz\": 126.15, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6388, \"ff\": 2671, \"seeds\": [113.86, 126.15, 140.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the balanced resolve-tag comparison in `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` and extended `cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py`. Correctness checks pass, and the requested mapping boundaries survive synthesis. The measured hypothesis is a regression: median Fmax falls from 147.75 to 126.15 MHz, with identical bracketed cycles and a 14.6192893401% fitness loss. The implementation is retained for the orchestrator to evaluate.\\n\\nEach of the sixteen existing metadata rows now computes thirteen exact two-bit equalities, reduces pairs [3:0], [6:4], [9:7], and [12:10] independently, and ANDs the four groups into `full_tag_equal`. Only then does `valid_q` qualify `row_hit`. Source-level Yosys `keep` attributes preserve the pair and group wires. One small extension to the advisory change is that the final equality wire also has `keep`, ensuring that validity and update qualification stay beyond the complete three-level equality network.\\n\\nAll sequential RTL is unchanged. The sixteen scalar valid/tag/counter rows, counter encoding and saturation, indexed 16x30 target array, indexed full-tag fetch lookup, reset priority, row selection, resolve/event qualification, and training visibility are preserved. No register, maintenance stage, bypass, or latency was added. Legal jumps allocate strongly taken; taken branches allocate weakly taken or increment a hit; not-taken branches decrement only a hit and retain the target; invalidating resolutions clear only a matching valid row. Unselected rows retain state, and invalid payloads remain masked. All other RTL files, the nret=1 configuration, pipeline scheduling, arithmetic/ALTOPS, top-level ports, and evaluation files are unchanged.\\n\\nThe new directed test uses the existing independent public-output policy model and retains every existing assertion. It covers every adjacent pair boundary, the three group boundaries, simultaneous mismatches in all four groups, and all-tag-bit mismatches, across all sixteen rows. Consecutive alias allocation edges run while fetch holds the same row. Longer sequences exercise matching and mismatching taken, not-taken, and invalidating resolutions, counter threshold transitions, target retention, accepted-fetch collisions, invalid-row masking, and preservation of unrelated rows. The complete exported IF bundle and fetch address are checked before and after each edge. Existing coverage for every individual tag and target bit, high addresses, wraparound, saturation, reset, disabled resolution, and mixed schedules remains intact. The final four predictor tests pass against both original and candidate RTL.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS without warnings.\\n- Complete cocotb suite: PASS, seven pytest runners / 134 cocotb cases, including four predictor tests, all 33 pipeline tests, both real and ALTOPS ALU builds, decoder, immediate generator, and register-file tests. Predictor/pipeline tests ran first; the other runners followed. The final addition of consecutive alias updates was then rechecked on both original and candidate IF stages.\\n- Unchanged `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6 bash formal/run_all.sh`: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, first gate attempt, zero RTL fix retries and no counterexamples. The script selected `wrapper_si.sv` and `checks_si.cfg`. This is the standard ALTOPS gate; arithmetic RTL was untouched.\\n- Additional Yosys sequential equivalence of the original and candidate IF stages: PASS, all 1,673 equivalence cells proven, zero unproven, using `equiv_simple` followed by `equiv_induct -undef -seq 2` and `equiv_status -assert`. This checks the unchanged state transitions and outputs under identical input schedules, including collisions; a one-step equivalence attempt alone was insufficient to prove the retained sequential state.\\n- Unchanged `python3 -m tools.eval.cosim . bench`: PASS, selftest RVFI comparison and stalled CoreMark CRC validation.\\n- Additional stalled selftest comparison: PASS for ready-schedule seeds 0xDEADBEEF, 1, and 42. All 115 retirement records match the unchanged Python ISS and are byte-for-byte identical between original and candidate for each schedule.\\n- Unchanged `NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv make fpga TARGET=bench`: PASS for all three placements and benchmark completion/CRC/bounds/marker checks, for both original and candidate. This is a successful gate execution with regressed fitness, not a performance improvement.\\n\\nThe same toolchain was used for both implementations: OSS CAD Suite Yosys 0.64+149, git `2dc69a757-dirty`; Verilator 5.047, `v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6`; and nextpnr-himbaechel `nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f`. Original RTL was extracted from HEAD and verified byte-for-byte against the saved winning RTL. Original synthesis and all three placements were rerun and reproduced the saved 147.75 / 137.44 / 154.08 MHz results exactly. Gate/configuration copies and the tested candidate RTL were checked against the worktree sources.\\n\\nMapped-netlist audit, using the unchanged `fpga/scripts/synth.tcl` flow:\\n\\n- Every row has thirteen LUT4 pair comparisons, one LUT4 plus three LUT3 group reductions, and one final LUT4: exactly three LUT levels from any tag/resolve-PC bit to full equality. All 288 equality-network cells were checked by input connectivity and exhaustive individual LUT truth tables. All 26 tag bits participate in every row; no control or validity signal enters the tree. All pair/group/final keep attributes remain in `synth.json`.\\n- Both implementations have exactly 464 metadata flops, 16 x (1 valid + 26 tag + 2 counter). All metadata update cones remain local to their own row.\\n- Both retain eight `RAM16SDP4` blocks, all belonging to the separate indexed target store. Every target WRE cone is independent of metadata. Logical predictor storage remains 944 bits.\\n- Synthesis LUT1-4 count changes from 5,957 to 5,778; MUX2_LUT5 count changes from 551 to 506. Synthesis FF count changes from 2,587 to 2,623. Reported post-pack LUT4 and FF costs are below.\\n- The 36 additional mapped FFs are outside the metadata: 32 under `u_rf.regs[0]`, plus two each under `u_ex.reg_q` and `u_mem.reg_q`. Those source modules are unchanged. The comparison rewrite perturbed whole-design optimization; no unrelated RTL was changed to compensate.\\n\\nScored configuration: the unchanged evaluator and Makefile use TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10, with both random bus stalls and default ready seed 0xDEADBEEF. This is the configuration behind the saved 350.46 score; it is kept separate from the requested 6 KB run.\\n\\n| Metric | Original | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 147.75 | 113.86 |\\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 137.44 | 126.15 |\\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 154.08 | 140.21 |\\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 147.75 | 126.15 |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 4,215,937 | 4,215,937 |\\n| Full-precision fitness, iter/s | 350.455901025086475 | 299.221738844769265 |\\n| Evaluator-rounded fitness, iter/s | 350.46 | 299.22 |\\n| Reported LUT4 | 6,567 | 6,388 |\\n| Reported FF | 2,635 | 2,671 |\\n\\nFitness uses `median_Fmax_MHz * 1e6 * 10 / 4215937`, with iterations/cycle `0.000002371951952792463454743275338317436906671`. The evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark=0.000002` was not used. Fitness/Fmax changes by -14.6192893401015%; LUT4 changes by -179 (-2.72575%); FF changes by +36 (+1.36622%). The estimated five-percent gain was not observed.\\n\\nAll candidate critical paths now pass through operand-forwarding selection and the EX unsigned branch comparator before reaching local predictor counter data. The previously reported resolve-PC-to-metadata-enable paths are absent from the three critical-path reports.\\n\\n| Seed | Launch signal and mapped source | Endpoint under cpu.u_if | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | EX/MEM rd[1], `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_67.Q` | `metadata[12].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.D` | 8.78 | 4.05 | 4.73 |\\n| 2 | ID/EX rs1[1], `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_168.Q` | `metadata[9].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.D` | 7.93 | 3.93 | 4.00 |\\n| 3 | EX/MEM rd[1], `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_67.Q` | `metadata[8].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.D` | 7.13 | 3.88 | 3.26 |\\n\\nDisplayed component times are independently rounded by nextpnr. The original median path was 6.77 ns, comprising 3.05 ns logic and 3.72 ns routing, from resolve-PC[17] to metadata row 4 valid CE. The intended equality structure was achieved, but its local shape did not translate into better whole-core timing. The new comparator/control path and the large candidate seed spread are the principal performance concerns.\\n\\nIdentical-ELF, identical-ready-schedule comparisons of original and candidate:\\n\\n| Working set | Ready seed | Original and candidate bracketed cycles | Start | Stop |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2 KB | 0xDEADBEEF | 4,215,937 | 15,229 | 4,231,166 |\\n| 2 KB | 1 | 4,214,768 | 15,265 | 4,230,033 |\\n| 2 KB | 42 | 4,216,052 | 15,327 | 4,231,379 |\\n| 6 KB | 0xDEADBEEF | 30,823,120 | 53,747 | 30,876,867 |\\n\\nThe entire final retirement and completion/marker/UART records agree for every pair, including absolute start/stop cycles. Both binaries used the exact same ELF path in each comparison. For 2 KB this was the saved scored ELF, SHA-256 `c7b024c15b74fad435730b0c6fbb7d853635542de982dba0ac7efbe2378deddc`. Freshly compiled original/candidate ELFs have byte-identical PT_LOAD segments to that ELF; non-loadable temporary object-name strings account for their differing whole-file hashes.\\n\\nThe separate 6 KB build used the unchanged Makefile with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, ITERATIONS=10, and both stalls. Its SHA-256 is `ce9d9ed1dbc1589553031c2133c01e04e64ac4aec24a26b427163f9b740b7ae8`. Both cores pass EBREAK completion, no out-of-bounds accesses, start/stop markers, and the exact UART literal `Correct operation validated.`. CRCs are seed=0x8a02, list=0xd4b0, matrix=0xbe52, state=0x5e47, final=0x5949. At the respective median Fmax values this run gives 47.93479699654026 original versus 40.92707032902574 candidate iter/s; it is not compared with the 2 KB 350.46 score. The UART's `CoreMark Size: 2000` denotes each algorithm's share of the 6000-byte total. Its compiler-flags string is hardcoded to -O2 in the unchanged port header; the actual compile commands use -O3, as recorded in the build logs.\\n\\nAll generated artifacts and unchanged gate copies are under `$TMPDIR/hyp-20260905-001-r4s0-validation`; the original and 6 KB builds use sibling directories ending in `-original` and `-6k`. Final netlists and timing reports are `cores/bench/generated/synth.json` and `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` in the respective original/candidate directories. Logs alongside those directories have prefix `hyp-20260905-001-r4s0-`, including `formal.log`, `predictor-pipeline.log`, `predictor-final.log`, `original-predictor.log`, `unit.log`, `synth.log`, `original-synth.log`, `cosim.log`, `fpga.log`, and `original-fpga.log`. Structured evidence uses the same prefix: `structure.json`, `critical-paths.json`, `metrics.json`, `cycle-equivalence.json`, `stalled-selftest.json`, and `provenance.json`. No helper script or generated build artifact was added to the worktree. The requested notes are at its root, the location read by `tools/orchestrator.py`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T16:43:32.117937Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r4s0 (regression, -14.62%): Preserving three LUT levels for resolve-tag equality with `keep` shifted the critical path to operand forwarding through the EX unsigned branch comparator into predictor counter data, cutting median Fmax from 147.75 to 126.15 MHz despite fewer LUTs and unchanged CoreMark cycles; equality depth alone does not bound the training path.\"}\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline row-addressed resolution events before local predictor updates\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner scores 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-003-r3s2 implementation notes identify all three critical\\npaths as ID/EX resolve-PC registers through local predictor tag matching\\nto metadata enables; the median path is 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of\\nrouting. Row-local metadata removed the indexed feedback mux, but live\\nEX PC bits still feed sixteen full-tag comparisons and tag-write paths.\\n\\nThe structural history favors a targeted timing cut. Store buffering\\nin hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 saved only 0.75% of CoreMark cycles; separating\\ndivision in hyp-20260905-002-r2s1 substantially reduced area but improved\\nfitness only 0.26%. The registered row-read experiment\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached 143.41 MHz with nearly unchanged cycles,\\nlosing to the current winner. It captured selected metadata and needed\\nforwarding between maintenance transactions. The current local rows\\npermit a different cut: register each resolution at its destination row\\nbefore comparison, then update that row directly from its current state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner scores 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-003-r3s2 implementation notes identify all three critical\\npaths as ID/EX resolve-PC registers through local predictor tag matching\\nto metadata enables; the median path is 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of\\nrouting. Row-local metadata removed the indexed feedback mux, but live\\nEX PC bits still feed sixteen full-tag comparisons and tag-write paths.\\n\\nThe structural history favors a targeted timing cut. Store buffering\\nin hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 saved only 0.75% of CoreMark cycles; separating\\ndivision in hyp-20260905-002-r2s1 substantially reduced area but improved\\nfitness only 0.26%. The registered row-read experiment\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached 143.41 MHz with nearly unchanged cycles,\\nlosing to the current winner. It captured selected metadata and needed\\nforwarding between maintenance transactions. The current local rows\\npermit a different cut: register each resolution at its destination row\\nbefore comparison, then update that row directly from its current state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one pipeline boundary to resolution maintenance, using a private\\npending-event register at each of the sixteen existing metadata rows.\\nThis is one structural retiming of the feedback transaction. Keep the\\ninstruction pipeline, immediate EX redirect, predictor algorithm,\\ncapacity, fetch lookup, and execution-resource scheduling unchanged.\\n\\nEach row gets a pending-valid bit, full 26-bit resolving PC tag, and\\ntwo-bit command. Encode the four commands as matching-tag invalidation\\nfor illegal/trapping/non-control instructions, legal jump, legal taken\\nbranch, and legal not-taken branch. Classification uses the existing\\nresolve_legal/branch/jump/taken outputs, with illegality taking priority.\\nOn edge N, pending_valid[row] becomes resolve_valid &&\\n(resolve_pc[5:2] == row). Update that row's pending tag and command only\\non this acceptance; otherwise retain its payload. These independently\\nenabled registers must remain local to their rows, rather than becoming\\none shared registered PC broadcast into sixteen comparisons. Capture\\nthe event only; do not read, snapshot, or compare old table metadata\\non this edge. At most one row accepts per cycle, without backpressure.\\n\\nOn edge N+1, each row with its old pending-valid asserted commits using\\nits private pending tag/command and its current local valid/tag/counter.\\nA jump writes the pending tag, sets valid, and sets the counter to 11.\\nA taken branch writes the tag and valid, allocates counter 10 on a miss,\\nor increments a hit with saturation. A not-taken branch decrements only\\na matching counter with saturation and retains its target. Invalidation\\nclears only a matching row's valid bit. Preserve all other fields.\\nCommit-time tag comparisons, saturation, tag data, and metadata enables\\nmust have no combinational dependency on live EX resolution inputs.\\n\\nCommit the old event while accepting the next, including consecutive\\nevents to the same row with different tags. Do not capture a counter or\\nhit snapshot: the next event sees the preceding event's committed local\\nstate during its own update cycle. Consequently there is no indexed\\nmetadata read and no maintenance forwarding mux. After draining, table\\nstate must equal applying every accepted event in order under the\\nexisting policy, including aliases, invalidations, and saturated updates.\\n\\nDelay the separate indexed target write by the same edge. Capture one\\nshared write-valid bit, four-bit index, and full 30-bit aligned target\\nfor a legal jump or taken branch, then write the existing target array\\non edge N+1. Its delayed write-valid is refreshed every cycle and is\\nindependent of old tags/counters. Tag, valid, counter, and target changes\\nfor an event become visible together; never publish a new tag with an\\nold target. Retain the existing indexed target storage implementation.\\nThis adds approximately 499 source-level event-state bits: 16 times\\n(1+26+2), plus one shared (1+4+30) target-write record.\\n\\nRefresh pending-valid bits every non-reset edge, even during fetch or\\nbackend stalls. An accepted event commits once while a younger divide\\nwaits, a memory request stalls, or a younger instruction redirects.\\nAcceptance remains qualified by the existing advancing-EX resolve_valid;\\nheld instructions cannot retrain repeatedly. Reset overrides capture\\nand commit, clears all pending/table validity, and cancels pending target\\nwrites. Masked payloads need no reset. No added architectural stall is\\npermitted when a maintenance event is pending.\\n\\nFetch consults committed state only, with no pending-event bypass. A\\nprediction accepted on a commit edge uses pre-edge state. Preserve the\\nexact predicted next PC carried with each instruction, the EX comparison\\nagainst actual next PC for every advancing instruction, and resolved\\nRVFI next-PC reporting. Training visibility moves one edge later, which\\ncan add cold-loop misses; warm self-jumps must still retire once per\\ncycle and recovery must still kill only the existing younger slot.\\nPreserve nret=1 and every external port, ordered retirement, operand\\nforwarding, decoder and trap rules, real/ALTOPS arithmetic, memory\\nhandshakes and bounds, and every protected evaluation check.\\n\\nEstimate four percent higher fitness from shorter maintenance paths,\\nwith little change in bracketed cycles. This is unmeasured. Additional\\nevent flops, capture-enable fanout, later training, or the hazard/divide\\ncontrol path exposed in r3s1 could erase the gain. Inspect the synthesized\\ncones and three-seed post-route reports: live resolve-PC paths should\\nterminate at event capture, while tag comparison starts at private row\\nevent registers. Report actual LUT4/FF costs and exact iterations/cycle\\ntimes median Fmax, using the same toolchain and workload for comparison.\\n\\nRun unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA gates. The historical\\nnotes identify the 350.46 score as the existing 2 KB gate and separately\\nreport 30,823,120 cycles for the canonical 6 KB workload. Preserve both\\ncontracts: compare the unchanged scored workload with 350.46, and also\\nrun the required 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration workload with both bus stalls\\nagainst the original RTL. Require canonical d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, the\\nvalidation literal, bounds checks, and start/stop marker bracketing.\\nReport the two configurations separately; do not mix their fitnesses\\nor modify harnesses, benchmark sources, wrappers, or other cores.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert sixteen independently enabled pending-event registers before\\nlocal metadata comparison and update. Implement old-event commit\\nalongside new-event capture, refreshing validity without stall holds.\\nDrive metadata exclusively from private registered events and live\\nlocal row state. Delay the single indexed target write to commit\\natomically with metadata. Preserve all prediction policies, full\\naddress widths, fetch timing, module ports, immediate recovery, and\\nreset cancellation. Introduce no table snapshots or training bypass.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Model accepted resolution events with an explicit one-edge delay and\\ncheck public predictions before and after every edge against an\\nindependent sequential policy model. Retain all existing functional\\nassertions, adjusting only expected training visibility. Cover all\\nrows and tag/target bits, continuous same-row commits and captures,\\nalternating aliases, threshold crossings, saturation, target changes,\\nnot-taken misses, invalidation/allocation, and idle draining. Detect\\nmismatched tag/target publication and fetch bypass. Test reset with\\na pending event, stalled fetch, concurrent recovery, and randomized\\nresolution streams followed by a complete table-behavior probe.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update the training reference model for the intentional delay while\\npreserving every architectural assertion. Check cold and warm short\\nloops, changing JALR targets, stale-code invalidations, and exactly\\none accepted training event per advancing instruction. Exercise\\npending-event commits during imem stalls, dmem holds, divide waits,\\nyounger redirects, and reset. Assert warm one-retirement-per-cycle\\nthroughput, unchanged recovery bubble length, strict RVFI order and\\nPC chaining, correct operands/traps, and no wrong-path memory writes.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert sixteen independently enabled pending-event registers before\\nlocal metadata comparison and update. Implement old-event commit\\nalongside new-event capture, refreshing validity without stall holds.\\nDrive metadata exclusively from private registered events and live\\nlocal row state. Delay the single indexed target write to commit\\natomically with metadata. Preserve all prediction policies, full\\naddress widths, fetch timing, module ports, immediate recovery, and\\nreset cancellation. Introduce no table snapshots or training bypass.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Model accepted resolution events with an explicit one-edge delay and\\ncheck public predictions before and after every edge against an\\nindependent sequential policy model. Retain all existing functional\\nassertions, adjusting only expected training visibility. Cover all\\nrows and tag/target bits, continuous same-row commits and captures,\\nalternating aliases, threshold crossings, saturation, target changes,\\nnot-taken misses, invalidation/allocation, and idle draining. Detect\\nmismatched tag/target publication and fetch bypass. Test reset with\\na pending event, stalled fetch, concurrent recovery, and randomized\\nresolution streams followed by a complete table-behavior probe.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update the training reference model for the intentional delay while\\npreserving every architectural assertion. Check cold and warm short\\nloops, changing JALR targets, stale-code invalidations, and exactly\\none accepted training event per advancing instruction. Exercise\\npending-event commits during imem stalls, dmem holds, divide waits,\\nyounger redirects, and reset. Assert warm one-retirement-per-cycle\\nthroughput, unchanged recovery bubble length, strict RVFI order and\\nPC chaining, correct operands/traps, and no wrong-path memory writes.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 371.55, \"delta_pct\": 6.02, \"vs_baseline\": 31.37, \"fmax_mhz\": 156.62, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6584, \"ff\": 3194, \"seeds\": [157.11, 156.62, 155.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the row-addressed resolution-event pipeline in `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv`, with corresponding coverage in `cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py` and `cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`. No other RTL, core configuration, benchmark source, wrapper, harness, or evaluation gate changed. This requested notes file is at the worktree root, as read by the orchestrator and explicitly permitted by its path checks.\\n\\nEach of the sixteen metadata rows now owns a pending-valid bit, independently enabled 26-bit resolving tag, and two-bit command: invalidate=00, jump=01, taken branch=10, not-taken branch=11. Illegality takes priority. Acceptance captures only the event; it neither reads nor snapshots metadata. Each row commits its old event against its current local valid/tag/counter while accepting the next event, including consecutive aliases. The policy remains jump-to-11, taken-miss-to-10, saturating taken-hit increment, matching-only saturating not-taken decrement with retained target, and matching-only invalidation.\\n\\nA shared valid/index/30-bit target record delays the existing indexed target-array write to the same edge as metadata commit. Both pending-valid paths refresh on every non-reset edge, independently of stalls and redirects. Reset cancels capture and commit and clears pending/table validity; masked payloads retain their values. Fetch sees committed state only and uses the pre-edge prediction on a commit edge. There is no training bypass, metadata snapshot, maintenance forwarding, or added architectural interlock. Immediate EX recovery, the exact prediction carried with each instruction, resolved RVFI next PC, nret=1, all external ports, and execution scheduling remain unchanged.\\n\\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis was needed. Source event state is exactly `16*(1+26+2) + (1+4+30) = 499` bits. Yosys automatically recodes each two-bit command into four state bits, so mapped event storage is 496 row flops plus 35 shared target flops, or 531 flops. The complete routed core gains 559 FFs after other synthesis/mapping differences.\\n\\nThe IF reference applies accepted events with an explicit one-edge delay and checks the public output bundle and fetch address before and after every checked edge. Coverage includes all rows, every tag and target bit, allocation, threshold crossings, saturation, target replacement/retention, illegal-control priority, not-taken misses, matching/nonmatching invalidations, simultaneous capture/commit, continuous aliases, idle draining, fetch stalls, recovery, flush, and reset with pending work. The existing deterministic 512-cycle random stream is retained, followed by probes of every exercised tag and each row's counter behavior.\\n\\nThe pipeline harness now checks predictions before and after every edge against an independent ordered policy model. It pairs accepted fetches with exactly one advancing-EX resolution, verifies recovery against the exact accepted prediction, and pairs retirements with those resolutions. Existing order, PC-chain, operand, arithmetic, trap, and wrong-path-store assertions remain. New directed tests cover pending commits during instruction stalls, memory holds, divide waits, younger redirects, and reset. Only two existing timing expectations changed: a cold self-jump now misses twice before sustaining one retirement per cycle, and immediate alternating aliases can sometimes hit the still-committed previous entry. Each recovery still kills exactly the existing younger slot and costs one bubble.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS without warnings.\\n- Complete `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, seven pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU builds, four IF tests, and all 35 pipeline tests.\\n- Unchanged `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, using the automatically selected nret=1 wrapper/configuration. No counterexamples and zero RTL fix attempts. The gate was also repeated successfully with the reference toolchain explicitly selected. This is the standard ALTOPS formal suite; real arithmetic RTL was untouched and its cocotb checks passed.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1`: PASS for both original and candidate, covering selftest RVFI comparison and the existing CoreMark CRC gate.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: PASS for both original and candidate; all three seeds routed and the benchmark completed with valid bounds, CRCs, and timing markers.\\n- Synthesized-cone audit: PASS on all 976 metadata data/control input pins. Their combinational sources are exclusively the same row's current metadata, private pending-event registers, and reset. No live EX signal or another row's state reaches commit. All 416 pending-tag bits remain distinct across the sixteen rows; event capture has no table-state dependency. All eight RAM16SDP4 primitives still implement the indexed target store, and all 24 target write-port groups depend only on the delayed target record and reset.\\n\\nFinal results use OSS CAD Suite Yosys 0.64+149 (`2dc69a757-dirty`), Verilator 5.047 (`v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6`), nextpnr 0.10-45 (`g98c18d7f`), and xPack RISC-V GCC 15.2.0. The existing GW2A device, constraints, FPGA wrapper, scripts, and seeds 1/2/3 are unchanged. An initial run selected Homebrew Yosys through shell PATH ordering; all gates were rerun with the reference OSS directory explicitly first. The final original-RTL run reproduces the historical Fmax, area, and scored cycles exactly.\\n\\nThe scored configuration is the unchanged 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 workload with `--bench --istall --dstall`, default random seed 0xDEADBEEF:\\n\\n| Metric | Original RTL | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 Fmax | 147.75 MHz | 157.11 MHz |\\n| Seed 2 Fmax | 137.44 MHz | 156.62 MHz |\\n| Seed 3 Fmax | 154.08 MHz | 155.67 MHz |\\n| Median Fmax | 147.75 MHz | 156.62 MHz |\\n| LUT4 | 6,567 | 6,584 |\\n| FF | 2,635 | 3,194 |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 4,215,937 | 4,215,262 |\\n| Start marker cycle | 15,229 | 15,229 |\\n| Stop marker cycle | 4,231,166 | 4,230,491 |\\n| Fitness | 350.45590102508646 iter/s | 371.55460324886093 iter/s |\\n\\nThe exact candidate calculation is `156.62 * 1e6 * 10 / 4215262`, using `10 / 4215262 = 2.372331779139707e-6` iterations/cycle. This is **+6.019119% versus the specified 350.46**, with 675 fewer cycles, 17 additional LUT4s, and 559 additional FFs. The evaluator's rounded IPC field was not used to compute fitness. Both runs passed the unchanged 2 KB CRC gate: seed=e9f5, list=e714, matrix=1fd7, state=8e3a, final=fcaf, ten iterations, and the exact `Correct operation validated.` literal.\\n\\nFinal candidate post-route paths:\\n\\n| Seed | Launch | Endpoint | Total | Logic | Routing |\\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | `cpu.u_if.metadata[0].tag_q_DFFE_Q_19.Q` | same row `counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE` | 6.36 ns | 2.61 ns | 3.76 ns |\\n| 2 | `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_63.Q` | `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_82.RESET` | 6.39 ns | 2.94 ns | 3.44 ns |\\n| 3 | `cpu.u_if.metadata[12].pending_tag_q_DFFE_Q_14.Q` | same row `counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE` | 6.42 ns | 2.13 ns | 4.30 ns |\\n\\nThe old live resolve-PC-to-metadata-enable paths are cut. Seeds 1 and 3 now traverse local committed-tag/private-event comparisons. The median seed exposes the existing prediction-mismatch/hazard/divide-qualified ID/EX flush path: the launching register is `predicted_next_pc[0]`, and the destination is an ID/EX operand register's reset input. Reported component delays are independently rounded by nextpnr. Local comparator routing and the existing recovery-control path remain timing concerns; the extra FF cost is material. Later training can still add cold-loop misses, and one three-seed result does not establish gains for every workload or placement.\\n\\nThe supplemental 6 KB contract was run separately using the unchanged benchmark Makefile with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, ITERATIONS=10, and both bus stalls. Both original and candidate used the exact same ELF and simulator flags:\\n\\n| 6 KB metric | Original RTL | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 30,823,120 | 30,822,790 |\\n| Start marker cycle | 53,747 | 53,747 |\\n| Stop marker cycle | 30,876,867 | 30,876,537 |\\n| Exact iterations/cycle | 10 / 30,823,120 | 10 / 30,822,790 |\\n| Rate at each median Fmax | 47.93479699654026 iter/s | 50.813050992463694 iter/s |\\n\\nBoth 6 KB runs passed EBREAK completion, `oob=false`, positive start/stop bracketing, ten iterations, the validation literal, and exact CRCs seed=8a02, **list=d4b0, matrix=be52, state=5e47**, final=5949. Their UART and final architectural retirement fields match, excluding elapsed cycle. The 330-cycle reduction is reported separately from the 2 KB fitness. CoreMark's existing UART banner statically says -O2; the saved compiler commands actually use -O3. Its displayed sizes 666 and 2000 are per-algorithm sizes; the total build sizes are 2000 and 6000. No benchmark or reporting source was edited.\\n\\nAll scratch sources, unchanged gate copies, builds, and logs are under `$TMPDIR/row-events-j26_tyt7/`. `candidate/` contains the tested RTL/tests and final gate logs at `cores/bench/generated/{formal-gate.log,cocotb-final.log,cosim-gate.log,fpga-gate.log}`. Final routed reports are `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` in each of `candidate/` and `original/`. The initial diagnostic artifacts are separately retained with `-homebrew` directory suffixes. The original snapshot is Git HEAD `00bf3f9679e60ab7058f8e7f9572388b3ae8a635`.\\n\\nStructured evidence at that scratch root: `metrics.json`, `structure.json`, `workload-comparison.json`, `workload-equivalence.json`, and `input-audit.json`. Raw benchmark output is in each final generated directory as `coremark-2k.jsonl` and `coremark-6k.jsonl`; the supplemental build command is in `supplemental-6k/build.log`. Tested source files and all protected gate copies were byte-compared with this worktree. Both separately linked 2 KB binaries have identical loadable segments; the direct comparison runs use the same candidate ELF for both RTL versions. Its SHA-256 is `0044a04b680dbafccb5ba08117cd7d44e7b1c371a0a70f021da53ff6b528a7d5`; the shared 6 KB ELF SHA-256 is `2f677bbdd235a9bf20cdc7eb4cc58274f1c58f324e7edd763bbfddc1bc8ff314`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T16:54:45.830594Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r4s1 (improvement, +6.02%): Pipelining resolution events into private predictor-row registers cuts live EX-to-metadata-enable paths and preserves consecutive-alias ordering without forwarding by committing against current row state, raising median Fmax from 147.75 to 156.62 MHz with essentially unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles at +559 FFs and +17 LUT4s.\"}\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Bank-local registered training commands for the target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current row-local predictor reaches 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz,\\nwith all three reported critical paths running from ID/EX resolve-PC\\nbits through local tag matching to metadata enables. The median path\\nis 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of routing. This makes training fanout\\nand placement a more evidenced opportunity than greater associativity:\\nhyp-20260905-003-r2s2 lost frequency with essentially unchanged cycles.\\nThe delayed row-snapshot experiment hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached only\\n143.41 MHz, below the current winner, but changed scored cycles from\\n4,215,937 to 4,215,262. That supports a small feedback-latency budget,\\nwithout establishing that pipelining alone will improve this winner.\\nBank-local command registers can isolate the current high-fanout\\nresolve-PC path while retaining its successful scalar metadata and\\nindexed target storage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current row-local predictor reaches 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz,\\nwith all three reported critical paths running from ID/EX resolve-PC\\nbits through local tag matching to metadata enables. The median path\\nis 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of routing. This makes training fanout\\nand placement a more evidenced opportunity than greater associativity:\\nhyp-20260905-003-r2s2 lost frequency with essentially unchanged cycles.\\nThe delayed row-snapshot experiment hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached only\\n143.41 MHz, below the current winner, but changed scored cycles from\\n4,215,937 to 4,215,262. That supports a small feedback-latency budget,\\nwithout establishing that pipelining alone will improve this winner.\\nBank-local command registers can isolate the current high-fanout\\nresolve-PC path while retaining its successful scalar metadata and\\nindexed target storage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one cycle of predictor-training latency using four bank-local\\nregistered command slots inside if_stage. Keep the existing sixteen\\ndirect-mapped entries, PC[5:2] index, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned\\ntarget[31:2] values, and two-bit branch counters. Partition only the\\nmetadata update circuitry into four groups of four consecutive rows:\\nresolve_pc[5:4] selects the bank and resolve_pc[3:2] selects its row.\\nPrediction policy, fetch lookup, instruction pipeline depth, and\\narchitectural recovery latency retain their current definitions.\\n\\nOn edge N, accept the existing resolve_valid event. Each bank updates\\nits pending-valid bit every non-reset edge to resolve_valid AND its\\nbank-index match. The selected bank captures the full 26-bit tag,\\na four-bit one-hot row selection, and legal/branch/jump/taken flags.\\nGive each bank payload its own bank-specific capture enable: these\\nregisters retain distinct histories, allowing local placement and\\nlimiting each registered command tag to four metadata rows. Capture\\nraw command fields, with no table-state read, tag comparison, or\\ncounter calculation on this capture path. Existing resolve_valid\\nqualification remains authoritative for valid EX advancement through\\nmemory holds and divide waits.\\n\\nDuring the following cycle, each pending command compares its tag\\nwith the addressed row's live scalar tag and computes that row's\\nnext state. Commit on edge N+1. Keep the successful constant-index\\nvalid/tag/counter registers and local saturation logic from r3s2.\\nThe committed state from the preceding event is already visible to\\nthe next event's calculation, including consecutive same-row updates\\nand different tags aliasing a row. Thus commands sustain one accepted\\nresolution per cycle and need neither captured metadata snapshots\\nnor maintenance forwarding. Every metadata update uses registered\\nbank command fields rather than live resolve_pc or resolve flags.\\n\\nKeep the separate 16x30 indexed target array and its single synchronous\\nwrite port. Register its write index, target data, and qualified write\\nevent alongside the bank commands, so a target write commits on the\\nsame edge as its matching metadata allocation/update. Its write event\\nis derived only from the accepted legal jump or taken legal branch,\\nindependently of old tags and counters. Retaining this storage form\\navoids converting the target payload into per-row flip-flops. Verify\\nthat synthesis retains the indexed target memory and distinct bank\\ncommand sources, and that live resolve-PC bits no longer feed metadata\\ntag-comparison or counter/valid-enable cones within the same cycle.\\n\\nPreserve the original state-transition policy in resolution order.\\nA legal jump writes tag/target, sets valid, and sets counter to 11.\\nA legal taken branch writes tag/target and valid, allocates counter\\n10 on a miss, or saturating-increments a hit. A legal not-taken branch\\ndecrements only a matching counter, retaining its target and allocating\\nnothing on a miss. A non-control or trapping resolution invalidates\\nonly a matching tag. Unselected rows retain their state. Reset clears\\ntable valid bits and every pending-valid/write-event bit with priority\\nover capture and commit; unused payloads remain masked and unreset.\\n\\nAn accepted command commits exactly once even if the next cycle has\\nan IF stall, a data-memory hold, a divide wait, or a younger redirect.\\nThose events suppress new acceptance only through resolve_valid and\\ncannot hold, discard, or replay an older command. Fetch reads committed\\ntable state only, with pre-edge read semantics and no pending-command\\nbypass. The intentional cost is one extra cycle before training becomes\\nvisible; cold self-jumps can incur a second miss, while warm self-jumps\\nstill retire every cycle. Preserve the exact prediction captured with\\neach accepted instruction, EX comparison with the architectural next\\nPC for every advancing instruction, immediate recovery priority over\\nimem stalls, and resolved next-PC reporting at RVFI retirement.\\n\\nEstimate a four percent fitness improvement from higher median Fmax,\\napproximately 153.66 MHz if bracketed cycles remain comparable. This\\nis an unmeasured estimate with medium confidence. The extra command\\nregisters and routing can offset the shorter training path, training\\ndelay can change prediction accuracy, and hazard/divide-wait or fetch\\nrecovery logic may become the limit. Evaluate the actual product of\\nfull-precision iterations/cycle and three-seed median Fmax against\\n350.46 using the identical scored workload, toolchain, and ready seed;\\nreport cycles, LUT4/FF cost, and critical-path endpoints separately.\\nPrior logs identify the scored workload as 2 KB, so also verify the\\nrequested 6 KB run and its canonical CRCs separately without changing\\neither evaluation contract or mixing their absolute fitness numbers.\\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, stalled cosim, UART, bounds,\\ntiming-marker, and FPGA gates. Keep nret=1, all external ports, decoder\\nlegality, RVFI order/operands/traps, and divider/ALTOPS behavior intact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add four bank-local registered resolution-command slots and a\\nsynchronized registered command for the existing indexed target\\nwrite port. Apply commands one edge after acceptance using live\\nconstant-index metadata and the existing transition policy. Give\\nbank payloads distinct capture enables, refresh pending-valid bits\\nevery cycle, and cancel pending work on reset. Preserve fetch\\nselection, full tag/target widths, recovery, and module ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent public-output predictor reference model\\nwith an explicit one-edge command delay and check predictions\\nbefore and after each edge. Cover every bank and row, back-to-back\\nsame-row counter transitions, alias replacement and invalidation,\\ninterleaved banks, target/metadata commit atomicity, full address\\nbits, saturation, fetch/training collisions, bubbles, and reset\\nwith a pending update. Check that idle/stall/redirect cycles drain\\naccepted work exactly once and that fetch sees committed state\\nwith no pending-update bypass. Adjust only expectations tied to\\nthe specified training visibility latency; retain semantic checks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for pending training committing through imem\\nbackpressure, an older memory hold, divide waits, and a younger\\nredirect, including reset before commit and no repeated acceptance\\nwhile EX is held. Model cold self-loop timing with the extra\\ntraining edge and require unchanged warm one-retirement-per-cycle\\nbehavior. Preserve assertions for exact accepted-prediction\\nrecovery, PC chaining, RVFI order, links, stale non-control and\\nchanging JALR predictions, illegal/misaligned traps, forwarded\\noperands, and absence of wrong-path retirements or stores.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add four bank-local registered resolution-command slots and a\\nsynchronized registered command for the existing indexed target\\nwrite port. Apply commands one edge after acceptance using live\\nconstant-index metadata and the existing transition policy. Give\\nbank payloads distinct capture enables, refresh pending-valid bits\\nevery cycle, and cancel pending work on reset. Preserve fetch\\nselection, full tag/target widths, recovery, and module ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent public-output predictor reference model\\nwith an explicit one-edge command delay and check predictions\\nbefore and after each edge. Cover every bank and row, back-to-back\\nsame-row counter transitions, alias replacement and invalidation,\\ninterleaved banks, target/metadata commit atomicity, full address\\nbits, saturation, fetch/training collisions, bubbles, and reset\\nwith a pending update. Check that idle/stall/redirect cycles drain\\naccepted work exactly once and that fetch sees committed state\\nwith no pending-update bypass. Adjust only expectations tied to\\nthe specified training visibility latency; retain semantic checks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for pending training committing through imem\\nbackpressure, an older memory hold, divide waits, and a younger\\nredirect, including reset before commit and no repeated acceptance\\nwhile EX is held. Model cold self-loop timing with the extra\\ntraining edge and require unchanged warm one-retirement-per-cycle\\nbehavior. Preserve assertions for exact accepted-prediction\\nrecovery, PC chaining, RVFI order, links, stale non-control and\\nchanging JALR predictions, illegal/misaligned traps, forwarded\\noperands, and absence of wrong-path retirements or stores.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 346.34, \"delta_pct\": -1.18, \"vs_baseline\": 22.46, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.99, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6516, \"ff\": 2807, \"seeds\": [145.99, 148.81, 145.41], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the bank-local registered training hypothesis in `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv`, with latency-aware reference and directed tests in `cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py` and `cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`. Correctness gates pass. The measured scored fitness is **346.33671643660585 iter/s**, **1.1765347153438688% below 350.46**. The estimated four-percent gain was not realized.\\n\\nFour command slots use resolve PC[5:4] to select their individual capture enables. Each retains a full 26-bit tag, four-bit one-hot PC[3:2] row selection, and raw legal/branch/jump/taken flags. Every non-reset edge refreshes each pending-valid bit, independently of IF stalls, memory holds, divide waits, flushes, and redirects. Payload registers capture only for their selected bank and otherwise retain their distinct histories. No table read, tag comparison, or counter calculation precedes capture.\\n\\nOn the following edge, the selected constant-index scalar metadata row commits against its live valid/tag/counter state. Consecutive commands therefore observe preceding commits without snapshots or forwarding. The original transition policy is retained: jumps allocate/update to 11; taken branches allocate 10 on a miss or saturating-increment a hit; not-taken branches decrement only a hit and retain its target; non-control or illegal/trapping resolutions invalidate only a matching tag. Unselected rows retain state.\\n\\nThe separate 16x30 indexed target array retains one synchronous write port. An independent registered write event, four-bit index, and 30-bit target commit on the matching metadata edge. Target payload capture is enabled only for an accepted legal jump or taken legal branch. Reset has priority over both capture and commit, clears all table valid bits and pending-valid/write-event bits, and leaves unused payloads unreset. The declaration adds 175 command-state bits; implementation optimization produces a net increase of 172 device FFs.\\n\\nFetch uses committed state only. A fetch accepted on either the capture edge or the following commit edge uses its pre-edge prediction, with no pending-command bypass. Instruction pipeline depth, recovery priority, accepted-prediction capture, EX architectural-next-PC comparison, resolve_valid qualification, RVFI reporting, decoder legality, nret=1, all external ports, divider, and ALTOPS behavior are unchanged. No architectural deviation from the hypothesis was needed.\\n\\nThe independent reference model carries one ordered raw command, applies it one edge after acceptance against current model state, and checks the complete public IF output bundle and fetch address before and after each edge. It does not inspect DUT metadata or command registers. Coverage includes all sixteen rows and four banks, consecutive same-row increments/decrements, both saturation limits, alias replacement and invalidation, interleaved banks, target/metadata atomicity, every tag and stored target bit, PC wraparound, fetch/training collisions, bubbles, idle/stall/flush/redirect draining, and reset with pending and concurrently offered commands.\\n\\nPipeline traces also check those public IF outputs against the same delayed reference before and after every edge. Four new directed tests exercise pending commits during imem backpressure, invalidation from an advancing load during its subsequent older-memory hold, divide waits, a younger redirect, and reset immediately before commit in each bank. Existing held-EX, forwarded-operand, RVFI order/PC-chain, links, trap, changing-JALR, stale-code, wrong-path retirement/store, and real divider checks remain. Only latency-dependent expectations changed: cold self-jumps miss twice before warm one-retirement-per-cycle execution, and alternating full-tag aliases can hit the older resident entry before its pending replacement commits. The alias test still requires full-width destinations and recovery matching the exact accepted prediction.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings. Channel-0 contract lint and `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Complete cocotb suite: PASS, seven pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU builds, four IF tests, and all 37 pipeline tests. The initial pipeline run exposed the old immediate-training alias expectation; that expectation was corrected as described above and the complete suite rerun successfully. No RTL fix was required.\\n- Unchanged `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, using the automatically selected nret=1 wrapper/configuration. Zero counterexamples and zero formal fix attempts. This is the standard ALTOPS gate; formal-deep was not run for this predictor-only change.\\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim`: PASS, selftest RVFI comparison and scored CoreMark CRC validation. Additional selftest cosim with both bus stalls passed all 115 retirements against the unchanged reference/comparator for ready seeds 0xDEADBEEF, 1, and 42.\\n- Unchanged FPGA evaluation: PASS, all three seeds routed successfully. Both workloads separately passed EBREAK completion, bounds (`oob=false`), UART validation, exact CRCs, and positive start/stop timing brackets.\\n\\nSynthesized-netlist inspection found exactly 464 scalar metadata FFs and 104 distinct bank-command tag FFs, with four distinct bank payload enables. All 272 examined command-payload data/enable pins are independent of table metadata. All 976 examined metadata data/control pins have combinational sources restricted to their own row state, their own bank's registered command, and reset: no live resolve-PC or resolve-flag source remains. Each bank tag reaches only its four consecutive metadata rows. The target store remains eight `RAM16SDP4` blocks, all named under `cpu.u_if.target_q`, sharing one write enable. Their write address/data come from the registered target command; their write-enable cones are independent of prior metadata and live resolution inputs.\\n\\nFinal measurements use the prior winner's OSS CAD Suite Yosys 0.64+149, git `2dc69a757-dirty`, OSS Verilator 5.047, unchanged Gowin synthesis and single-retirement FPGA wrapper, device/constraints, and nextpnr seeds 1/2/3. A preliminary synthesis selected Homebrew Yosys through the shell PATH; it was superseded before FPGA evaluation by an explicit OSS-toolchain synthesis. All reported timing and structural evidence uses the latter.\\n\\n| Scored 2 KB metric | Prior winner | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Median Fmax | 147.75 MHz | 145.99 MHz |\\n| Fitness | 350.46 iter/s | 346.33671643660585 iter/s |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 4,215,937 | 4,215,262 |\\n| LUT4 | 6,567 | 6,516 |\\n| FF | 2,635 | 2,807 |\\n\\nThe unchanged scored workload is TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, ITERATIONS=10, -O3, both random bus stalls, and ready seed 0xDEADBEEF. Full-precision iterations/cycle is `10 / 4215262 = 2.372331779139707e-6`; fitness is `145.99 * 1e6 * 10 / 4215262`. The evaluator's rounded IPC field was not used. Cycles improve by 675, LUT4 decreases by 51, and FF increases by 172. Candidate timing markers are start=15,229 and stop=4,230,491; total elapsed cycles are not scored.\\n\\nFinal post-route critical paths:\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Source | Destination | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 145.99 | cpu.u_if.commands[3].tag_q_DFFE_Q_9.Q | cpu.u_if.metadata[15].valid_q_DFFRE_Q.CE | 6.85 | 2.05 | 4.80 |\\n| 2 | 148.81 | cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_160.Q | cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_5.RESET | 6.72 | 2.30 | 4.42 |\\n| 3 | 145.41 | cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_163.Q | cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_161.RESET | 6.88 | 2.15 | 4.72 |\\n\\nThe requested register boundary is present, but the median seed still has a bank-tag-to-metadata-enable path dominated by routing. Compared with the prior median path's 3.05 ns logic plus 3.72 ns routing, logic falls to 2.05 ns while routing rises to 4.80 ns. ID/EX hazard/flush reset paths limit the other two seeds. Placement/routing cost and the extra FFs outweigh the small cycle reduction in this measurement. This implementation therefore does not establish a fitness improvement.\\n\\nThe supplemental 6 KB run was built separately through the unchanged Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options, with ITERATIONS=10 and ready seed 0xDEADBEEF. Candidate brackets are start=53,747 and stop=30,876,537, giving **30,822,790 cycles**. Exact CRCs are seed=0x8a02, list=0xd4b0, matrix=0xbe52, state=0x5e47, final=0x5949. `Correct operation validated.` is present. Its separate throughput at the candidate median frequency is 47.364304139891296 iter/s; it is not compared with the 2 KB fitness of 350.46. Neither evaluation contract was changed. Both build logs explicitly use -O3, although the unmodified port prints a hardcoded -O2 compiler-flags label.\\n\\nAn independently rebuilt original HEAD bench core ran both exact candidate ELFs with the same ready schedule. Baseline brackets reproduce 4,215,937 cycles for 2 KB and 30,823,120 for 6 KB, so the respective candidate reductions are 675 and 330 cycles. UART and final architectural RVFI records agree; the simulation cycle field differs as expected. Both current ELFs also have byte-identical PT_LOAD segments and entry points to the prior winner's corresponding builds; whole-file hashes differ in non-loaded contents.\\n\\nCurrent ELF SHA-256 values: 2 KB `f1b1abdef49294c67824a7faacf2d8f3eaf64b4657cc577bae28e068702f1466`; 6 KB `4bc18805b3a172aec28d012e2b1d2a820e528266f66c285d6a69bc62dee5bdfb`. Concatenated PT_LOAD data SHA-256 values: 2 KB `e8753ffebc32bb14977fc72c1ca0cfeea2b0ae980e344311eacf3765ea16eedb`; 6 KB `f34c3e24f7dcea0bab50c655d56b4b9d47df646d1c9b01a23715153b0af29ee0`.\\n\\nAll generated validation artifacts and unchanged gate copies are under `$TMPDIR/hyp-20260905-003-r4s2-validation`, with sibling `-baseline` and `-6k` directories. Logs/evidence alongside them use prefix `hyp-20260905-003-r4s2-`: `formal.log`, `unit-final.log`, `synth-oss.log`, `build.log`, `cosim.log`, `fpga.log`, `structure.json`, `timing.json`, `metrics.json`, `workloads.json`, `elf-equivalence.json`, `cycle-comparison.json`, and `stalled-cosim.json`. Staged source and contract files were checked byte-for-byte against the worktree. No generated artifact or helper script was added to the worktree. These notes are at the explicitly requested worktree-root location, which `tools/orchestrator.py` permits and reads through `_read_notes`; the only source changes are the three bench files listed above.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T16:50:31.619380Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r4s2 (regression, -1.18%): Bank-local registered predictor tags driving four metadata rows shortened tag-to-valid-enable logic delay by 1.00 ns but added 1.08 ns of routing, lowering median Fmax from 147.75 to 145.99 MHz despite 675 fewer CoreMark cycles.\"}\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Remove wide ID/EX payload registers from bubble-control fanout\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, scores 371.55 iter/s at\\n156.62 MHz median Fmax. Its median-seed timing report identifies a\\n6.39 ns path from predicted_next_pc[0], through prediction recovery\\nand hazard/divide qualification, to an ID/EX operand register RESET.\\nThe final control net alone contributes 1.95 ns of routing. In the\\ncurrent RTL, reset || flush clears the entire id_ex_t payload, and\\nstall_id also includes the load-use condition even though that case\\ninserts a bubble. Recovery therefore controls many data-register\\nreset/enable pins whose values cannot matter for a killed instruction.\\n\\nPrior micro_opt attempts rewrote register-file selection, ALU operation\\nselection, and predictor training/tag comparisons; none isolated ID/EX\\nbubble controls from payload storage. LESSONS.md records that the ALU\\nrewrite exposed this recovery path, while forced predictor comparison\\nboundaries regressed timing. The latest winner has already registered\\npredictor training events. A local rewrite of bubble insertion now\\ntargets a measured control-distribution bottleneck without changing\\npredictor policy, arithmetic, or architectural scheduling.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, scores 371.55 iter/s at\\n156.62 MHz median Fmax. Its median-seed timing report identifies a\\n6.39 ns path from predicted_next_pc[0], through prediction recovery\\nand hazard/divide qualification, to an ID/EX operand register RESET.\\nThe final control net alone contributes 1.95 ns of routing. In the\\ncurrent RTL, reset || flush clears the entire id_ex_t payload, and\\nstall_id also includes the load-use condition even though that case\\ninserts a bubble. Recovery therefore controls many data-register\\nreset/enable pins whose values cannot matter for a killed instruction.\\n\\nPrior micro_opt attempts rewrote register-file selection, ALU operation\\nselection, and predictor training/tag comparisons; none isolated ID/EX\\nbubble controls from payload storage. LESSONS.md records that the ALU\\nrewrite exposed this recovery path, while forced predictor comparison\\nboundaries regressed timing. The latest winner has already registered\\npredictor training events. A local rewrite of bubble insertion now\\ntargets a measured control-distribution bottleneck without changing\\npredictor policy, arithmetic, or architectural scheduling.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split ID/EX sequential updates into a narrow control/valid group and\\na payload group. On a flush, clear the entire ctrl field and valid\\nexactly as the existing zero bundle does. Do not clear, mask, or hold\\npayload registers merely because of flush. Keep their deterministic\\nreset-to-zero behavior, but otherwise capture pc, predicted_next_pc,\\nboth operand values, immediate, register addresses, rd, and instruction\\nwhenever there is no actual backend hold. Each field must have exactly\\none sequential driver; do not mix whole-struct and partial assignments\\nacross separate always_ff blocks.\\n\\nMake stall_id mean only dmem_stall || div_wait. Remove load_use_hazard\\nfrom this ID/EX hold signal while preserving stall_if, flush_if,\\nflush_id, stall_ex_mem, and hold_mem_wb behavior. This is a redundant\\nhold removal: whenever a load-use hazard occurs without a backend\\nhold, the existing flush_id already forces an ID/EX bubble. On that\\nedge the payload may capture the still-unaccepted younger instruction,\\nbut ctrl=0 and valid=0 make it inert, and the held fetch is accepted\\nnormally on the following eligible edge. When a memory or divide wait\\nis active, both control and payload must remain held exactly as before.\\nPreserve reset priority and flush priority for the control/valid group.\\n\\nClearing valid alone is insufficient: forwarding and memory control\\nlogic consult ctrl fields even when valid is zero. Continue clearing\\nall control bits on every flush, particularly reg_write, mem_read,\\nmem_write, is_branch, and is_jump. Retained or newly captured bubble\\npayload must never launch a divide, redirect, train the predictor,\\nforward as a writer, access memory, or retire. Leave EX/MEM full-bundle\\ndivide bubbles and MEM/WB forwarding-data retention unchanged. All\\nlive instructions retain their exact operands, instruction metadata,\\naccepted prediction, trap behavior, and RVFI fields through holds.\\n\\nThis single micro-optimization removes recovery and load-use logic\\nfrom the wide payload registers' direct reset and enable controls.\\nExisting upstream instruction-to-NOP selection may still affect data\\ninputs; it need not be redesigned. Add no stage, state bit, execution\\nshortcut, new prediction behavior, or architectural latency. Preserve\\nnret=1, all external ports, all decoder legality rules, and every hard\\ninvariant and evaluation check.\\n\\nExpect an unmeasured three-percent fitness improvement from higher\\nmedian Fmax, with identical bracketed cycles for the same binary and\\nbus-ready schedule. Confidence is medium: the other winning seeds are\\nlimited by local predictor tag-to-counter-enable paths at 6.36 and\\n6.42 ns, so they may cap the benefit; synthesis may also reconstruct\\nunwanted control dependencies or alter placement adversely. Inspect\\nthe mapped payload reset/enable cones and routed critical paths to\\nestablish whether the intended control isolation survives synthesis.\\n\\nRun the existing unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA gates.\\nCompare accepted bus transactions and valid RVFI retirements, including\\ntheir cycle positions, against the current winner under identical\\ninputs; inactive payload bit patterns need not match. Use full-precision\\niterations/bracketed-cycles times median Fmax and report LUT/FF costs.\\nThe saved 371.55 result is documented as a 2 KB scored run; the separate\\ncanonical 6 KB winner run takes 30,822,790 bracketed cycles. Keep those\\nconfigurations distinct and unchanged, and verify the required 6 KB\\nCRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, memory bounds, markers, and validation UART literal.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Partition ID/EX control/valid and payload updates. Keep reset-to-zero\\nfor every field. Flush clears only ctrl and valid; payload captures\\nwhenever stall is low, independently of flush. Preserve every field\\nduring actual memory/divide holds and give each field one sequential\\nowner. Document that invalid bubble payload is inert because its\\ncontrols are cleared, while live instruction metadata is unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Set stall_id to dmem_stall || div_wait, removing the redundant\\nload-use hold from ID/EX payload enables. Keep the existing load-use\\nfetch stall and flush_id bubble equation, including backend-hold\\npriority, and leave every other output equation unchanged. Update\\ncomments to describe control-only bubble insertion and actual holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed coverage for load-use bubbles and misprediction flushes\\nwith nonzero payload, including wrong-path stores and DIV/REM, and\\nfor overlapping instruction stalls, prolonged data stalls, divide\\nwaits/completion, and reset. Verify exact surviving RVFI operands,\\npredictions, traps, order, cycle timing, and accepted memory effects;\\nkilled payload must produce no retirement, training, divide launch,\\nor memory request. Check complete ID/EX stability during backend\\nholds and correct fresh payload on the next accepted instruction.\\nRetain all existing assertions, including EX/MEM zero bubbles during\\ndivide waits and MEM/WB forwarding retention across memory stalls.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Partition ID/EX control/valid and payload updates. Keep reset-to-zero\\nfor every field. Flush clears only ctrl and valid; payload captures\\nwhenever stall is low, independently of flush. Preserve every field\\nduring actual memory/divide holds and give each field one sequential\\nowner. Document that invalid bubble payload is inert because its\\ncontrols are cleared, while live instruction metadata is unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Set stall_id to dmem_stall || div_wait, removing the redundant\\nload-use hold from ID/EX payload enables. Keep the existing load-use\\nfetch stall and flush_id bubble equation, including backend-hold\\npriority, and leave every other output equation unchanged. Update\\ncomments to describe control-only bubble insertion and actual holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed coverage for load-use bubbles and misprediction flushes\\nwith nonzero payload, including wrong-path stores and DIV/REM, and\\nfor overlapping instruction stalls, prolonged data stalls, divide\\nwaits/completion, and reset. Verify exact surviving RVFI operands,\\npredictions, traps, order, cycle timing, and accepted memory effects;\\nkilled payload must produce no retirement, training, divide launch,\\nor memory request. Check complete ID/EX stability during backend\\nholds and correct fresh payload on the next accepted instruction.\\nRetain all existing assertions, including EX/MEM zero bubbles during\\ndivide waits and MEM/WB forwarding retention across memory stalls.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 388.66, \"delta_pct\": 4.61, \"vs_baseline\": 37.42, \"fmax_mhz\": 163.83, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6618, \"ff\": 3198, \"seeds\": [163.83, 155.33, 168.55], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T17:43:43.123618Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r5s0 (improvement, +4.61%): Restricting ID/EX flushes to `ctrl`/`valid` and payload holds to `dmem_stall || div_wait` removes recovery/load-use fanout from wide payload reset/enable pins, but bubbles must still clear every control bit because forwarding and memory logic are not fully gated by `valid`.\"}\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Separate ID/EX validity from the wide recovery payload\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ID/EX register clears its entire packed bundle on reset OR\\nflush. In addition, IF replaces the entire instruction with a NOP on\\nrecovery, putting redirect into instruction decode, register addressing,\\nand load-hazard inputs. Thus a one-instruction squash controls hundreds\\nof payload bits even though only the instruction's validity must change.\\n\\nThe structural history identifies this as a recurring timing limit.\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 removed indexed predictor maintenance from the\\ncritical paths but exposed hazard/divide control into ID/EX resets.\\nThe current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, reports 156.62 MHz median\\nFmax and 371.55 fitness. Its median seed has a 6.39 ns path from\\npredicted_next_pc[0] through mismatch/hazard control to an ID/EX\\noperand register's RESET pin. The other two seeds remain limited by\\nlocal predictor comparisons. Earlier store buffering saved only 0.75%\\nof cycles, and the divider split saved area without timed-cycle gains.\\nSeparating instruction validity from payload transport directly attacks\\nthe measured recovery fanout while retaining the current schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ID/EX register clears its entire packed bundle on reset OR\\nflush. In addition, IF replaces the entire instruction with a NOP on\\nrecovery, putting redirect into instruction decode, register addressing,\\nand load-hazard inputs. Thus a one-instruction squash controls hundreds\\nof payload bits even though only the instruction's validity must change.\\n\\nThe structural history identifies this as a recurring timing limit.\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 removed indexed predictor maintenance from the\\ncritical paths but exposed hazard/divide control into ID/EX resets.\\nThe current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, reports 156.62 MHz median\\nFmax and 371.55 fitness. Its median seed has a 6.39 ns path from\\npredicted_next_pc[0] through mismatch/hazard control to an ID/EX\\noperand register's RESET pin. The other two seeds remain limited by\\nlocal predictor comparisons. Earlier store buffering saved only 0.75%\\nof cycles, and the divider split saved area without timed-cycle gains.\\nSeparating instruction validity from payload transport directly attacks\\nthe measured recovery fanout while retaining the current schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the IF-to-ID/EX boundary around a separate registered valid\\ntoken. Keep the current pipeline depth, single issue, exact accepted\\nprediction metadata, and one-younger-slot recovery. This experiment\\nchanges squash distribution, with no predictor-policy, arithmetic,\\nmemory-latency, or instruction-scheduling redesign.\\n\\nIn IF, always present raw imem_data as out.instr. Keep out.valid low\\non flush or redirect, and preserve the existing PC update priorities:\\nreset, immediate EX redirect, then accepted predicted advancement.\\nRecovery must still override instruction-bus backpressure. Preserve\\nevery predictor table, pending event, training rule, and commit edge.\\nRemoving the recovery-controlled NOP mux is necessary; splitting only\\nthe ID/EX register would leave a second wide recovery-to-payload path.\\n\\nIn ID, register validity separately from the decoded control and wide\\npayload. The valid register retains the existing reset/flush priority\\nover capture/hold: reset or flush clears it, otherwise !stall captures\\nin.valid, otherwise it holds. Payload registers capture the raw PC,\\nprediction, instruction, immediate, register addresses, operand values,\\nand decoded control whenever !stall, even when flush is asserted.\\nTheir existing reset initialization can remain. Flush must not select\\ntheir reset, data mux, or clock enable. In particular, do not replace\\nthe old wide flush reset with a wide flush-controlled hold enable.\\nKeep the public id_ex_t layout and module ports unchanged.\\n\\nAt the ID/EX output boundary, expose the captured payload unchanged\\nbut present ctrl='0 whenever the REGISTERED valid token is zero.\\nValid instructions see their complete original decoded control.\\nThis compact control mask reproduces the current downstream bubble\\ncontract without zeroing operands, addresses, PCs, or instructions.\\nIt is essential because hazard, forwarding, and memory logic currently\\ninspect control fields independently of retirement validity. Invalid\\nraw stores, loads, jumps, illegal instructions, and DIV/REM encodings\\nmust not request memory, write registers, launch a divide, redirect,\\ntrain the predictor, or retire. Do not mask the entire packed bundle.\\nDecoder legality defaults and all valid-instruction trap rules remain\\nexactly as before.\\n\\nPreserve hazard_unit's raw source-field comparison and stall equations.\\nWith raw IF data, its load-use match must remain independent of\\nredirect, flush, and the combinational IF valid signal; qualifying\\nthat match with recovery-derived validity would reconnect redirect\\nto the wide payload clock enable. A raw match on an unavailable fetch\\nor a killed fetch is harmless: imem backpressure already prevents\\nacceptance, redirect overrides the PC hold, and ID/EX validity clears.\\nA real load-use interlock still creates exactly one bubble. Memory\\nbackpressure and divide waits still hold the active ID/EX payload and\\nvalid token. Preserve EX/MEM drain behavior, accepted divide operands,\\nand MEM/WB's retained forwarding fields when only retirement validity\\nclears during a memory hold; do not gate that retained bypass by its\\ncleared retirement-valid bit.\\n\\nPredict unchanged accepted-fetch, resolution, bus-handshake, and RVFI\\nretirement schedules for identical input streams, with higher Fmax\\nfrom lower recovery fanout. Estimate a three percent fitness gain;\\nit is unmeasured. The new valid-to-control mask can become critical,\\nand the near-equal local predictor paths in the other seeds may cap\\nthe improvement. Inspect synthesized cones and all three routed seeds\\nto confirm that combinational redirect/flush no longer reaches ID/EX\\npayload RESET, CE, or D pins through instruction substitution or\\nhazard qualification. Report the new endpoints and LUT4/FF costs.\\n\\nRun unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA gates. Compare\\noriginal and candidate with the same toolchain and deterministic bus\\nstalls, including cycle-level architectural equivalence checks.\\nKeep nret=1, external ports, ordered RVFI, memory bounds and handshakes,\\nmarker ordering, decoder checks, and real/ALTOPS arithmetic intact.\\nHistorical implementation notes distinguish the scored 2 KB workload\\nbehind 371.55 from the required canonical 6 KB run. Preserve the\\nexisting scored gate and separately validate the 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration\\nrun with both bus stalls, d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, the validation literal,\\nand start/stop bracketing. Compare like workloads using exact\\niterations/cycle times median Fmax; do not change evaluation files.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate registered instruction validity from raw decoded control\\nand wide payload storage. Clear only validity on flush; payload\\ncapture depends on the existing stall signal and reset, never\\nflush. Present a zero ctrl_t for invalid ID/EX tokens using the\\nregistered valid bit, while passing valid-instruction control and\\nall payload fields unchanged. Preserve the packed output layout,\\nvalid priority, reset initialization, and true hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the flush/redirect-controlled instruction-to-NOP mux and\\nalways transport raw imem_data. Preserve invalidation through\\nout.valid, PC priorities, exact prediction metadata, and the\\nentire existing predictor/training implementation. Document that\\ninvalid instruction bits are payload and cannot authorize work.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve the current raw source comparison and stall/flush\\nequations. Update the bubble contract comments for validity-only\\nsquashes, explaining why raw matches during imem stalls or\\nrecovery do not admit work. Keep redirect/flush and IF validity\\nout of the load-match and payload-hold cones; introduce no new\\nsource-use optimization or recovery-dependent stall qualification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Replace the implementation-specific invalid-instruction NOP\\nexpectation with an exact raw-imem-data transport assertion.\\nRetain every validity, PC, prediction, training, reset, and\\nacceptance assertion. Exercise flush and redirect with dangerous\\nraw instruction encodings, concurrent stalls, and pending commits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all architectural and cycle-schedule checks. Add directed\\ntests placing stores, loads, divides, branches, and illegal words\\nin squashed or unavailable fetch slots, including raw source\\nmatches against an advancing load during stale-prediction recovery.\\nCover load-use bubbles, simultaneous bus stalls, held divides,\\nreset during holds, and retained MEM/WB forwarding. Assert no\\ninvalid-token side effects, exactly-once resolution and retirement,\\ncorrect operands/traps/PC chains, unchanged one-slot recovery,\\nand identical architectural traces under matched bus inputs.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate registered instruction validity from raw decoded control\\nand wide payload storage. Clear only validity on flush; payload\\ncapture depends on the existing stall signal and reset, never\\nflush. Present a zero ctrl_t for invalid ID/EX tokens using the\\nregistered valid bit, while passing valid-instruction control and\\nall payload fields unchanged. Preserve the packed output layout,\\nvalid priority, reset initialization, and true hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the flush/redirect-controlled instruction-to-NOP mux and\\nalways transport raw imem_data. Preserve invalidation through\\nout.valid, PC priorities, exact prediction metadata, and the\\nentire existing predictor/training implementation. Document that\\ninvalid instruction bits are payload and cannot authorize work.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve the current raw source comparison and stall/flush\\nequations. Update the bubble contract comments for validity-only\\nsquashes, explaining why raw matches during imem stalls or\\nrecovery do not admit work. Keep redirect/flush and IF validity\\nout of the load-match and payload-hold cones; introduce no new\\nsource-use optimization or recovery-dependent stall qualification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Replace the implementation-specific invalid-instruction NOP\\nexpectation with an exact raw-imem-data transport assertion.\\nRetain every validity, PC, prediction, training, reset, and\\nacceptance assertion. Exercise flush and redirect with dangerous\\nraw instruction encodings, concurrent stalls, and pending commits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all architectural and cycle-schedule checks. Add directed\\ntests placing stores, loads, divides, branches, and illegal words\\nin squashed or unavailable fetch slots, including raw source\\nmatches against an advancing load during stale-prediction recovery.\\nCover load-use bubbles, simultaneous bus stalls, held divides,\\nreset during holds, and retained MEM/WB forwarding. Assert no\\ninvalid-token side effects, exactly-once resolution and retirement,\\ncorrect operands/traps/PC chains, unchanged one-slot recovery,\\nand identical architectural traces under matched bus inputs.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 261.48, \"delta_pct\": -29.62, \"vs_baseline\": -7.55, \"fmax_mhz\": 110.22, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6644, \"ff\": 3166, \"seeds\": [110.22, 103.78, 112.15], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the separate ID/EX valid token and raw IF instruction transport. Functional and schedule checks pass, but this implementation is a measured timing regression: median Fmax is 110.22 MHz versus 156.62 MHz, and scored fitness is 261.478409 versus 371.554603 (-29.625846%). The estimated three percent gain did not materialize. The candidate is left implemented for the orchestrator to evaluate; no evaluation contract was changed.\\n\\n`id_stage.sv` now clocks `reg_q.valid` in its own sequential block: reset or flush clears it, otherwise an unstalled edge captures `in.valid`, otherwise it holds. The other 229 bits retain reset initialization and capture whenever `!stall`, including flush edges. Flush selects neither their reset, data nor enable. The public `id_ex_t` and ports are unchanged. At the output, only the 22-bit `ctrl_t` is zeroed when the registered token is invalid; every other field remains the captured payload.\\n\\n`if_stage.sv` always exports `imem_data`; its validity equation, reset/redirect/accepted-prediction PC priorities, predictor lookup, all metadata and target tables, pending events, training rules and commit edges are unchanged. `hazard_unit.sv` and `core.sv` changed only comments. A comparison with comments and whitespace removed confirmed identical hazard equations and top-level wiring. Decoder, arithmetic, EX/MEM drain, accepted divide operands, retained MEM/WB forwarding and NRET=1 were not changed.\\n\\nThe IF tests replace only the invalid-instruction NOP expectation with exact raw-data transport, retaining all PC/prediction/validity/training assertions. Added directed pending-commit tests cover SW, LW, all four divides/remainders, branches, jumps, illegal words and ECALL under flush, redirect and stalls. The new `test_id_stage.py` checks raw payload capture during flush, hold behavior, reset priority and complete control masking, with directed and deterministic random vectors. Pipeline additions cover dangerous squashed and unavailable slots, raw load-source matches during stale prediction recovery, one load-use bubble, simultaneous bus holds, divide/reset interactions, no invalid-token memory/divide/resolution effects, and original/candidate cycle comparisons. All existing pipeline and decoder checks remain present, including retained MEM/WB forwarding.\\n\\nLocal validation used scratch copies rooted at `$TMPDIR/valid-token-r5s1/`, with separate `original/` and `candidate/` trees. The original RTL is the incoming HEAD, b9a8841, carrying hypothesis hyp-20260905-002-r4s1. Only scratch copies were used for generated formal, cocotb, program and FPGA outputs; the worktree's evaluation files and other cores were not edited. The root notes file is the explicitly requested exception to the RTL/test editing scope.\\n\\n| Gate | Original | Candidate |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Required Verilator lint command | Pass, no warnings | Pass, no warnings |\\n| Complete cocotb suite | 7 pytest runners pass | 8 pytest runners pass |\\n| `bash formal/run_all.sh`, automatic NRET=1 selection | 53 pass, 0 fail | 53 pass, 0 fail |\\n| `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` | Pass, 2 ELFs | Pass, 2 ELFs |\\n| `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv` | 3/3 routes and benchmark pass | 3/3 routes and benchmark pass |\\n| Separate 6 KB, -O3, 10-iteration run with both stalls | Pass | Pass |\\n\\nCandidate local formal status: PASS on the first invocation, zero fix attempts, no counterexample. The unchanged fast gate uses ALTOPS; the complete cocotb suite separately passed its real and ALTOPS ALU builds. The unchanged ISS cosim and benchmark runs use real arithmetic.\\n\\nMatched gates used Verilator 5.046, Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61), nextpnr-himbaechel nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f, and xPack RISC-V GCC 15.2.0. The requested lint command also passed with the workspace's Verilator 5.047. Both variants used identical per-gate toolchains, FPGA wrapper, constraints, route seeds 1/2/3, ELFs and bus PRNG inputs. Reproducing 156.62 MHz, 6,584 LUT4s, 3,194 FFs and 371.554603 fitness on the original confirms the historical comparison.\\n\\nThe new `matched_bus_cycle_equivalence` cocotb test exported the original schedule and compared the candidate against it using `PIPELINE_SCHEDULE_OUTPUT` and `PIPELINE_SCHEDULE_REFERENCE`. Six matched streams cover four deterministic bus-stall phases through loads, stores, divides, remainders, loop exits and start/stop stores, plus stale-load recovery with and without concurrent bus stalls. Comparison includes every fetch PC and prediction, accepted instruction, resolution, predictor commit, active memory request and handshake cycle, divide acceptance, and all recorded RVFI fields including retirement cycle. Both JSON files have SHA-256 `0be8fb87b93a7c103507752580cd1600c74c78f1e4432546ffc7344ba41941db`. Additional full selftest RVFI traces, including cycles, were byte-identical between variants for bus seeds 1, 17, 12345 and 12648430, and each passed the unchanged Python ISS comparison (115 retirements; last cycles 198, 189, 192 and 195 respectively).\\n\\nSynthesis confirms the intended recovery isolation. In an optimized flattened core with both bus-ready inputs unconstrained, combinational fanout analysis stops at sequential cells. Originally redirect reached payload D cones covering 165 bits, EN cones covering all 229 bits, and SRST cones covering all 229 bits. `flush_if` had the same reach; `flush_id` reached all 229 payload SRST inputs. In the candidate, redirect, `flush_if` and `flush_id` reach zero payload D, EN or SRST inputs. They still affect the single valid token. Raw IF instruction bits alias the imem-data bits directly.\\n\\nIn the scored mapped netlist, 212 physical DFFREs implement the surviving/merged ID/EX payload bits in each variant. All 212 candidate RESET pins connect only to `cpu.reset`; all 212 original RESET pins connect to the combined recovery/reset net. Each routed seed was also inspected: all candidate payload RESET pins retain that reset-only connection, and all payload D/CE/RESET drivers match synthesis after verifying and traversing nextpnr's identity LUTs. The three-seed route checks found no changed payload-pin driver. Evidence: `cone-audit.json`, `routed-cone-audit.json`, each variant's `core-opt.json`, and `cores/bench/generated/{synth.json,pnr_seed*/pnr.json}` under the scratch root.\\n\\n| Route seed | Original MHz | Original critical path | Candidate MHz | Candidate critical path |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | --- |\\n| 1 | 157.11 | Predictor row 0 tag[6] Q to counter CE, 6.36 ns | 110.22 | ID/EX PC[3] Q to EX/MEM alu_result[31] D, 9.07 ns |\\n| 2 | 156.62 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[0] Q to ID/EX rs1_val[13] RESET, 6.39 ns | 103.78 | ID/EX PC[0] Q to EX/MEM alu_result[31] D, 9.64 ns |\\n| 3 | 155.67 | Predictor row 12 pending_tag[11] Q to counter CE, 6.42 ns | 112.15 | ID/EX PC[5] Q to EX/MEM alu_result[31] D, 8.92 ns |\\n\\nThe candidate physical endpoints are `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_28.Q`, `_31.Q` and `_26.Q` respectively, all ending at `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_64.D`. The paths traverse the ALU operand selection, arithmetic and result-selection logic. The median candidate path has 5.84 ns logic and 3.23 ns routing. The original median endpoint is `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_82.RESET`, reached from `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_63.Q`. Recovery is removed from the intended payload cones, but the resulting mapping exposes a longer execute-data path in every seed; the limiting paths do not start at the registered valid bit. No arithmetic or scheduling redesign was attempted to hide this outcome.\\n\\n| Routed resource | Original | Candidate | Change |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| LUT4 | 6584 | 6644 | +60 |\\n| FF | 3194 | 3166 | -28 |\\n\\nThe scored workload remains the unchanged 2 KB, -O3, ten-iteration gate with `--bench --istall --dstall` and default bus seed 0xDEADBEEF. Its CRC gate passed unchanged. Both variants take exactly 4,215,262 marker-bracketed cycles. Fitness is computed from exact `10 / 4215262` iterations/cycle, not the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark: 2e-06` display.\\n\\nThe separate required canonical workload was built from unchanged sources/Makefile in `canonical/` with `make -B -f bench/programs/Makefile bench/programs/coremark.elf COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`; ITERATIONS remains 10. Both variants ran that identical ELF with both bus stalls and seed 0xDEADBEEF. Start at 0x10000100 was cycle 53,747; stop at 0x10000104 was cycle 30,876,537, giving exactly 30,822,790 timed cycles in both. EBREAK completed at cycle 30,883,293 with no OOB flag. UART and benchmark metadata match exactly: seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949, ten iterations, and `Correct operation validated.`. The untouched port prints a static `-O2` compiler-flags string; the recorded compiler invocation proves the actual build used `-O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`. Its displayed CoreMark Size=2000 is the per-algorithm share of the 6000-byte total.\\n\\n| Like-for-like workload | Timed cycles, both variants | Exact iterations/cycle | Original fitness | Candidate fitness |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Scored 2 KB | 4215262 | 10 / 4215262 | 371.554603 | 261.478409 |\\n| Required canonical 6 KB | 30822790 | 10 / 30822790 | 50.813051 | 35.759255 |\\n\\nThese fitness values multiply the exact iteration/cycle fraction by the corresponding three-seed median Fmax in Hz. They do not mix the 2 KB and 6 KB cycle counts. The scored ELF SHA-256 is `d74b3e935ec6b7302df7413a48c743209d2beb9d450ceec4d832a1cfd6eb1050`; canonical ELF SHA-256 is `ee05de62d7579bf11d4f8048a15a2e1aa8ce731d86e414e58d20d46c3ce2860b`.\\n\\nAdditional evidence under the scratch root: `timing-comparison.json`, `canonical-comparison.json`, `selftest-stalled-comparison.json`, both `schedules.json` files, and each variant's `formal-gate.log`, `cocotb.log`, `equivalence.log`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-gate.log`, `synth.log`, `canonical.log` and per-seed `nextpnr.log`. The candidate scratch RTL was byte-checked against every final worktree RTL file. `git diff --check` passed. The only departures from the advisory file list are a comment correction in `core.sv`, the focused ID-stage unit test, and this required implementation note.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T17:48:06.642623Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r5s1 (regression, -29.62%): Raw IF transport plus separate ID/EX validity and output `ctrl` masking removed recovery-to-payload fanout but exposed longer PC-to-EX/MEM ALU paths in all three routed seeds, cutting median Fmax from 156.62 to 110.22 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles; eliminating a reset bottleneck can worsen overall timing through remapping.\"}\n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Eight-entry return stack with direct return recognition\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current predictor remembers one last destination per tagged PC. A\\nreturn shared by several callers therefore either predicts the previous\\ncaller's continuation or misses after target-table eviction. The saved\\n6 KB CoreMark ELF has several call sites for crcu16 and crc16, providing\\na concrete opportunity for caller-sensitive return prediction; this is\\nstatic evidence, not a measured return-misprediction rate.\\n\\nPrior predictor experiments favor a small, simple lookup: r2s2's two-way\\ntable lost Fmax without reducing cycles, and r4s2's shared bank commands\\nlost frequency through routing. Retain r4s1's 16-entry target table and\\nprivate registered training events. Add one focused predictor mechanism\\nthat can eliminate return-recovery bubbles, including on target-table\\nmisses, while keeping its top address directly in registers.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current predictor remembers one last destination per tagged PC. A\\nreturn shared by several callers therefore either predicts the previous\\ncaller's continuation or misses after target-table eviction. The saved\\n6 KB CoreMark ELF has several call sites for crcu16 and crc16, providing\\na concrete opportunity for caller-sensitive return prediction; this is\\nstatic evidence, not a measured return-misprediction rate.\\n\\nPrior predictor experiments favor a small, simple lookup: r2s2's two-way\\ntable lost Fmax without reducing cycles, and r4s2's shared bank commands\\nlost frequency through routing. Retain r4s1's 16-entry target table and\\nprivate registered training events. Add one focused predictor mechanism\\nthat can eliminate return-recovery bubbles, including on target-table\\nmisses, while keeping its top address directly in registers.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add an eight-entry return-address stack (RAS) to if_stage. Recognize only\\nthe exact canonical return instructions JALR x0,x1,0 and JALR x0,x5,0\\ndirectly from raw imem_data. When either instruction is fetched and the\\nRAS is nonempty, predict its registered top address. Otherwise use the\\nexisting tagged target prediction and PC+4 fallback unchanged. A RAS\\nprediction does not require a target-table hit. This narrow constant\\ninstruction match introduces no register-file read or target adder in\\nIF. Use raw imem_data, never the flush-dependent out.instr, for the match.\\n\\nKeep all eight full 32-bit return addresses in a shift stack, with entry\\nzero as the directly readable top and a four-bit occupancy count in\\n0..8. Compute return recognition and the existing target-table lookup in\\nparallel, and select among sequential PC, table target, and RAS top with\\nmutually exclusive source enables. Avoid an indexed stack read on the\\nfetch path. The stack is address-history prediction only: no additional\\ninstruction stage, frontend interlock, or architectural stack is added.\\n\\nDerive push/pop hints from the decoded instruction in EX, qualified by\\nthe existing resolve_valid and resolve_legal, including misalignment\\nchecks. Treat x1 and x5 as link registers. A legal JAL pushes PC+4 iff\\nrd is a link register. For a legal JALR: push when only rd is a link\\nregister; pop when only rs1 is a link register; push when both are the\\nsame link register; pop then push when both are different link registers;\\notherwise do neither. These hints update history even for noncanonical\\nJALRs, but only the two exact zero-immediate return encodings above may\\nselect the RAS in IF. All other JALRs retain last-target prediction.\\n\\nPreserve the successful EX-to-predictor register boundary. At resolution\\nedge N capture just a pending action and, for pushes, the full PC+4 link\\nvalue. Commit the action on edge N+1 against the CURRENT stack while\\naccepting the next action. Do not snapshot the stack or feed live EX\\noperands, comparisons, or flags into stack commit logic. Reuse EX's\\nexisting PC+4 value for link data. This supports one action per cycle\\nwithout maintenance forwarding. No speculative fetch-time push or pop,\\ncheckpoint, rollback, or pending-action bypass is required.\\n\\nPush shifts retained addresses down and installs the new link at the\\ntop; occupancy saturates at eight and overflow discards the oldest\\naddress. A nonempty pop shifts addresses up and decrements occupancy;\\nan empty pop is a no-op. Pop-then-push replaces the top at unchanged\\nnonzero occupancy, or installs one entry when empty. Reset clears\\noccupancy and pending validity with priority over capture and commit;\\nunused address payloads need not reset and must remain masked.\\n\\nRefresh pending validity every non-reset edge. An accepted older action\\ncommits once through subsequent fetch stalls, memory holds, divide waits,\\nand younger redirects. Such events may prevent new EX acceptance but\\nmust not replay, hold, cancel, or duplicate an already accepted action.\\nWrong-path calls never reach valid EX resolution. A mispredicted call\\nstill pushes its correct architectural PC+4 when it resolves legally.\\n\\nFetch uses pre-edge committed RAS and target-table state. Neither bus\\nbackpressure nor a load-use/divide hold consumes a prediction or pops\\nthe stack. The added training delay can leave the stack stale for very\\nshort callees or consecutive returns; accept the resulting prediction\\nmisses and recover with the existing exact next-PC comparison. Preserve\\nthe exact prediction captured with each accepted instruction, recovery\\nfor EVERY advancing instruction, redirect priority over imem stalls,\\nand architectural next-PC reporting in RVFI. Incorrect hints, overwritten\\nlink registers, overflow, and underflow must affect performance only.\\n\\nEstimate a two-percent net fitness improvement, not a measured result.\\nThe direct imem-to-next-PC recognition path, stack update fanout, and\\nroughly 300 added state bits may reduce Fmax; caller changes may also\\nbe too rare to repay that cost. Measure the actual product of full-\\nprecision iterations/cycle and three-seed median Fmax, with identical\\nELFs, toolchain, and ready schedules for current RTL and candidate.\\nReport bracketed cycles, return-related recoveries, Fmax, LUT4/FF cost,\\nand critical-path endpoints. Use the unchanged correctness and FPGA\\ngates, including stalled execution, bounds, timing markers, and UART.\\nValidate the requested 6 KB run and canonical d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs. The\\nhistorical log identifies 371.55 as the scored 2 KB result; report that\\ncomparison separately from 6 KB throughput without changing either\\nevaluation contract. Keep nret=1, external IO, decoder legality, RVFI\\norder and traps, memory behavior, and all M-extension logic intact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the eight-entry full-address shift RAS, occupancy count, and\\none-edge pending push/pop/link record. Implement the specified\\nordered updates, overflow/underflow policy, reset cancellation,\\nand unconditional pending-action drain. Select committed RAS top\\nfor the two exact canonical return encodings independently of BTB\\nresidency; otherwise preserve the existing prediction. Retain all\\n16 target rows, tags, counters, indexed target memory, row-private\\nevent registers, transition policy, and target commit timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Export internal RAS push/pop hints derived from legal decoded\\nJAL/JALR rd and rs1 link-register combinations, plus PC+4 link data.\\nUse existing advancing-resolution and nontrapping qualification.\\nPreserve all actual-target computation, accepted-prediction\\ncomparisons, stale-prediction recovery, forwarding, link writes,\\ndivide scheduling, and architectural EX/MEM fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX RAS hints and link value to IF using internal signals.\\nKeep the core port declaration, nret=1 retirement wiring, existing\\ntarget-predictor feedback, and hazard connections unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent public-output reference with raw-instruction\\nreturn recognition, an eight-entry logical stack, and the explicit\\none-edge action delay. Cover all depths, overflow, underflow,\\nsimultaneous pop/push, consecutive actions, full address bits,\\nPC+4 wraparound via supplied link values, pre-edge visibility,\\nreset with pending work, and action drain through stall/flush/\\nredirect cycles. Check canonical returns on BTB hits and misses,\\nempty-stack fallback, and near-match illegal/noncanonical encodings.\\nRetain every existing target-table policy and collision assertion.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Model EX hint classification and delayed RAS commits independently\\nfrom DUT state. Add a callee with the same return PC reached from\\nalternating callers, with distinct link addresses, enough callee\\ninstructions for pending pushes to commit, and deliberate BTB\\neviction before return. Require correct first-use return prediction\\nwith a populated RAS and no return-recovery bubble in those cases.\\nCover nested JAL/JALR calls, x1/x5, recursion beyond depth eight,\\ntail calls, noncanonical JALRs, coroutine pop/push hints, overwritten\\nlink registers, empty returns, and very short/consecutive returns\\nwith documented stale-stack recovery. Exercise wrong-path calls,\\nillegal/misaligned jumps, stalled memory-dependent JALRs, imem\\nstalls, divide waits, and reset between capture and commit. Keep\\nstrict RVFI order, exact accepted-prediction recovery, PC chaining,\\nlink/operand/trap correctness, and no wrong-path retirement/store\\nassertions; revise only predictor-policy-specific expectations.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the eight-entry full-address shift RAS, occupancy count, and\\none-edge pending push/pop/link record. Implement the specified\\nordered updates, overflow/underflow policy, reset cancellation,\\nand unconditional pending-action drain. Select committed RAS top\\nfor the two exact canonical return encodings independently of BTB\\nresidency; otherwise preserve the existing prediction. Retain all\\n16 target rows, tags, counters, indexed target memory, row-private\\nevent registers, transition policy, and target commit timing.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Export internal RAS push/pop hints derived from legal decoded\\nJAL/JALR rd and rs1 link-register combinations, plus PC+4 link data.\\nUse existing advancing-resolution and nontrapping qualification.\\nPreserve all actual-target computation, accepted-prediction\\ncomparisons, stale-prediction recovery, forwarding, link writes,\\ndivide scheduling, and architectural EX/MEM fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX RAS hints and link value to IF using internal signals.\\nKeep the core port declaration, nret=1 retirement wiring, existing\\ntarget-predictor feedback, and hazard connections unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent public-output reference with raw-instruction\\nreturn recognition, an eight-entry logical stack, and the explicit\\none-edge action delay. Cover all depths, overflow, underflow,\\nsimultaneous pop/push, consecutive actions, full address bits,\\nPC+4 wraparound via supplied link values, pre-edge visibility,\\nreset with pending work, and action drain through stall/flush/\\nredirect cycles. Check canonical returns on BTB hits and misses,\\nempty-stack fallback, and near-match illegal/noncanonical encodings.\\nRetain every existing target-table policy and collision assertion.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Model EX hint classification and delayed RAS commits independently\\nfrom DUT state. Add a callee with the same return PC reached from\\nalternating callers, with distinct link addresses, enough callee\\ninstructions for pending pushes to commit, and deliberate BTB\\neviction before return. Require correct first-use return prediction\\nwith a populated RAS and no return-recovery bubble in those cases.\\nCover nested JAL/JALR calls, x1/x5, recursion beyond depth eight,\\ntail calls, noncanonical JALRs, coroutine pop/push hints, overwritten\\nlink registers, empty returns, and very short/consecutive returns\\nwith documented stale-stack recovery. Exercise wrong-path calls,\\nillegal/misaligned jumps, stalled memory-dependent JALRs, imem\\nstalls, divide waits, and reset between capture and commit. Keep\\nstrict RVFI order, exact accepted-prediction recovery, PC chaining,\\nlink/operand/trap correctness, and no wrong-path retirement/store\\nassertions; revise only predictor-policy-specific expectations.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 356.76, \"delta_pct\": -3.98, \"vs_baseline\": 26.14, \"fmax_mhz\": 150.13, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4208138, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6886, \"ff\": 3489, \"seeds\": [143.95, 163.67, 150.13], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the eight-entry return-address stack in `cores/bench`. Correctness checks pass. The measured hypothesis does not improve fitness: the 6 KB throughput product decreases 3.963175%, and the separate scored 2 KB product decreases 3.981512%. The proposed +2% was an estimate, not a measured result.\\n\\nChanges:\\n\\n- `rtl/if_stage.sv`: eight full 32-bit shift-stack entries, directly readable entry-zero top, four-bit saturating occupancy, and a registered valid/push/pop/link event. Resolution edge N captures the event; edge N+1 commits against current history while accepting the next event. Push discards the oldest entry on overflow; empty pop does nothing; pop/push replaces a nonempty top or creates one entry. Reset clears occupancy and pending validity. Payloads remain unreset and masked.\\n- IF recognizes only raw `imem_data` values `0x00008067` and `0x00028067`. Mutually exclusive sequential/table/RAS enables select the next PC. RAS selection is independent of table residency. All 16 table rows, private registered training events, target memory, counters, transition rules, and commit timing remain intact.\\n- `rtl/ex_stage.sv`: decoded x1/x5 JAL/JALR hints are qualified by existing advancing resolution and legality, including target misalignment. The existing PC+4 expression is factored into one shared value for architectural link writes, sequential recovery, and pending RAS link data. Same-link JALRs push; different-link JALRs pop/push. Live EX signals terminate at the RAS event registers.\\n- `rtl/core.sv`: internal hint/link connections only. Core ports, nret=1, retirement order, exact accepted-prediction recovery, architectural RVFI next PC, decoder, hazards, memory, and M-extension logic are preserved.\\n- `test/test_if_stage.py`: independent public-output logical-stack reference with explicit delayed events, every occupancy, overflow/underflow, replacement at every depth, consecutive actions, all address bits, wrapped links, pre-edge visibility, raw decode despite flush, table hit/miss fallback, near encodings, reset cancellation, and once-only draining through holds/redirects. Existing table-policy/collision assertions are retained.\\n- `test/test_pipeline.py`: independent instruction-word hint classification and delayed logical history. Added alternating callers with deliberate return-table eviction, x1/x5 hint matrix, noncanonical and trapping JALRs, nested/tail/coroutine calls, eleven-level recursion, overwritten links, empty/short/consecutive returns, wrong-path calls/stores, memory-dependent held JALRs, instruction stalls, divide drain, reset before commit, and PC+4 wraparound. Existing exact prediction, RVFI order/chaining, operands, links, traps, and accepted-store checks remain enabled.\\n- `test/test_ras_benchmark.py`: optional measurement test adds read-only counters to a generated scratch copy of the existing harness. It counts canonical returns and EX redirects within the timing bracket and requires byte-identical stdout against the unmodified harness, including final retirement, cycles, bounds, timing markers, and UART. Neither the official harness nor synthesized RTL receives instrumentation.\\n\\nThere are no architectural deviations. No speculative stack changes, checkpoints, rollback, indexed top read, pending bypass, interlock, or new instruction stage were introduced. The extra measurement test provides the requested recovery counts without editing evaluation files. Validation artifacts are under `$TMPDIR/ras-r5s2`; formal and unit-test copies were staged there to avoid writes to protected directories.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: pass, no warnings. `git diff --check`: pass.\\n- Formal: **pass, first attempt; 53 passed, 0 failed; no RTL fix attempts or counterexamples**. Ran the unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` in the isolated candidate copy with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`; it selected `wrapper_si.sv` and `checks_si.cfg` from unchanged nret=1 configuration. This was the normal ALTOPS gate; M-extension RTL was not modified.\\n- Final cocotb/pytest suite: **7 runner tests passed, 1 optional benchmark test skipped**. The pipeline runner contains 42 passing cases; the IF runner contains 7 passing cases. Decoder, immediate, register-file, and arithmetic/divider runners also pass. The optional benchmark test was separately enabled and passed for both designs and both ELFs. An initial directed hint-matrix expectation was corrected by adding a setup NOP so the seed push commits before the canonical return fetch; no RTL or existing invariant was relaxed.\\n- Unchanged Python-ISS/selftest cosim: **115 retirements matched** in each of zero-wait and `--istall --dstall` runs, with normal successful completion.\\n- FPGA: unchanged `fpga/scripts/synth.tcl` with `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, followed by unchanged `fpga/scripts/nextpnr_run.sh` for seeds 1, 2, 3. All six placements/routes completed normally. Same Gowin device, constraints, Yosys, nextpnr, and Verilator installation for baseline and candidate. Generated files stayed in scratch directories.\\n\\nMatched benchmark inputs and contracts:\\n\\n- Both designs used the same saved ELF for each size, ITERATIONS=10, the unchanged harness, and identical default-seed `--bench --istall --dstall` schedules (xorshift seed `0xDEADBEEF`, approximately 22% stalls on each bus). Only stop-marker minus start-marker cycles are scored; UART's placeholder time/throughput values are not used.\\n- Compiler: xPack GNU RISC-V Embedded GCC arm64 15.2.0. Fresh scratch builds using unchanged sources/Makefile with `COREMARK_OPT=-O3` and `COREMARK_SIZE=2000` / `6000` reproduce every PT_LOAD record and byte of the respective saved ELF. Whole ELF container hashes differ from the scratch rebuilds, but the loaded program/data are identical. The pre-existing UART banner says `-O2`; it is a fixed string, not the actual build flags, and was not changed.\\n- Saved 2 KB ELF SHA256: `f1b1abdef49294c67824a7faacf2d8f3eaf64b4657cc577bae28e068702f1466`.\\n- Saved 6 KB ELF SHA256: `4bc18805b3a172aec28d012e2b1d2a820e528266f66c285d6a69bc62dee5bdfb`.\\n- Both sizes, both designs: EBREAK reached, no out-of-bounds access, both timing markers present, positive brackets, ITERATIONS=10, and literal `Correct operation validated.` present.\\n- Requested 6 KB validation: `seedcrc=8a02`, `crclist=d4b0`, `crcmatrix=be52`, `crcstate=5e47`, `crcfinal=5949`; reported per-algorithm CoreMark Size=2000, totaling 6000 bytes.\\n- The unchanged evaluator currently scores the 2 KB contract, with `seedcrc=e9f5`, `crclist=e714`, `crcmatrix=1fd7`, `crcstate=8e3a`, `crcfinal=fcaf`. Both outputs pass its existing `validate_coremark_uart`. These constants, ELFs, timing policy, and evaluation files were not changed. The historical 371.55 entry is the 2 KB score, not 6 KB throughput.\\n\\nMeasured FPGA cost and frequency:\\n\\n| Metric | Current r4s1 RTL | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 157.11 | 143.95 |\\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 156.62 | 163.67 |\\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 155.67 | 150.13 |\\n| Three-seed median Fmax, MHz | 156.62 | 150.13 |\\n| LUT4 | 6584 | 6886 |\\n| FF | 3194 | 3489 |\\n\\nAdded cost is 302 LUT4 and 295 FF. Source-level RAS state is 256 address bits + 4 occupancy bits + 35 pending-event bits = 295 bits. Median Fmax decreases 4.143788%.\\n\\nRequested 6 KB throughput, kept separate from the scored 2 KB contract:\\n\\n| Metric | Current | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Start marker cycle | 53,747 | 53,746 |\\n| Stop marker cycle | 30,876,537 | 30,818,569 |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 30,822,790 | 30,764,823 |\\n| Canonical return resolutions in bracket | 104,164 | 104,164 |\\n| Canonical return recoveries in bracket | 56,092 | 0 |\\n| Iterations/cycle | 3.2443526364745047e-7 | 3.2504656373287116e-7 |\\n| Median Fmax \\u00d7 full-precision iterations/cycle, iterations/s | 50.813050992463694 | 48.79924061321594 |\\n\\n6 KB cycle savings are 57,967 cycles (0.188065%). The resulting full-precision throughput change is **-3.963175483295478%**.\\n\\nHistorical/scored 2 KB comparison:\\n\\n| Metric | Current | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Start marker cycle | 15,229 | 15,229 |\\n| Stop marker cycle | 4,230,491 | 4,223,367 |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 4,215,262 | 4,208,138 |\\n| Canonical return resolutions in bracket | 13,735 | 13,735 |\\n| Canonical return recoveries in bracket | 7,802 | 0 |\\n| Iterations/cycle | 2.372331779139707e-6 | 2.3763479239511633e-6 |\\n| Full-precision fitness, iterations/s | 371.55460324886093 | 356.7611138227881 |\\n| Rounded score | 371.55 | 356.76 |\\n\\n2 KB cycle savings are 7,124 cycles (0.169005%); full-precision fitness decreases **3.9815115454684302%**. Products were calculated before rounding iterations/cycle; the historical rounded score is reproduced independently.\\n\\nPost-route critical-path endpoints (cell names exactly as reported; all share prefix `cpu.`):\\n\\n| Design / seed | Launch | Capture | Delay, ns |\\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: |\\n| Current / 1 | `u_if.metadata[0].tag_q_DFFE_Q_19.Q` | `u_if.metadata[0].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE` | 6.36 |\\n| Current / 2 | `u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_63.Q` | `u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_82.RESET` | 6.39 |\\n| Current / 3 | `u_if.metadata[12].pending_tag_q_DFFE_Q_14.Q` | `u_if.metadata[12].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE` | 6.42 |\\n| Candidate / 1 | `u_if.metadata[5].tag_q_DFFE_Q_25.Q` | `u_if.metadata[5].valid_q_DFFRE_Q.CE` | 6.95 |\\n| Candidate / 2 | `u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_176.Q` | `u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_92.D` | 6.11 |\\n| Candidate / 3 | `u_if.metadata[3].tag_q_DFFE_Q_9.Q` | `u_if.metadata[3].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE` | 6.66 |\\n\\nThe candidate median seed is limited by target-table metadata training (1.98 ns logic, 4.68 ns routing), rather than a reported direct-return recognition path. The larger design changes placement/routing, and its seed spread is substantial. The observed return bubble elimination is too small a share of total cycles to repay the median frequency loss. Very short callees and consecutive returns still intentionally see stale committed history; directed tests verify exact architectural recovery. Overflow, underflow, overwritten links, and noncanonical hints remain prediction-only effects. The requested implementation is retained for the orchestrator to evaluate; no acceptance or fitness improvement is claimed.\\n\\nEvidence: `$TMPDIR/ras-r5s2/final-metrics.json`, `return-metrics.json`, `elf-provenance.json`, `selftest-cosim.json`, `return-metrics.log`, `candidate/tests-final.log`, `candidate/formal-gate.log`, and each design's `synth.log`, `coremark-{2000,6000}.jsonl`, and `generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T17:53:06.146480Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r5s2 (regression, -3.98%): An eight-entry shift-stack RAS eliminated measured canonical-return recoveries but saved only 0.17% of scored 2 KB CoreMark cycles, too little to offset a 4.14% median Fmax loss with target-table metadata training still critical; return accuracy gains must be weighed against total-cycle savings and routing cost.\"}\n17:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Replace local saturating-counter arithmetic with thermometer state\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 388.66 fitness at 163.83 MHz after isolating\\nID/EX payload controls. The bench history identifies another specific\\ntiming opportunity: r4s1's seeds 1 and 3 ended at local predictor-counter\\nclock enables, reached from committed or pending tags in 6.36/6.42 ns.\\nRow-private event registers already isolate these updates from live EX.\\nHowever, each row still uses binary increment/decrement, saturation\\nselection, and conditional counter writes after its full-tag comparison.\\n\\nPrior micro_opt attempts at register-file selection and ALU predecode\\ndid not produce accepted gains, and r4s0's preserved equality tree lost\\n14.62 percent by exposing a different path. This hypothesis instead\\nsimplifies the small state-update circuit following the existing local\\ncomparison. Those timing reports describe r4s1, not a verified critical\\npath of r5s0; whether counter updates still limit the winner must be\\nestablished in the matched implementation evaluation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 388.66 fitness at 163.83 MHz after isolating\\nID/EX payload controls. The bench history identifies another specific\\ntiming opportunity: r4s1's seeds 1 and 3 ended at local predictor-counter\\nclock enables, reached from committed or pending tags in 6.36/6.42 ns.\\nRow-private event registers already isolate these updates from live EX.\\nHowever, each row still uses binary increment/decrement, saturation\\nselection, and conditional counter writes after its full-tag comparison.\\n\\nPrior micro_opt attempts at register-file selection and ALU predecode\\ndid not produce accepted gains, and r4s0's preserved equality tree lost\\n14.62 percent by exposing a different path. This hypothesis instead\\nsimplifies the small state-update circuit following the existing local\\ncomparison. Those timing reports describe r4s1, not a verified critical\\npath of r5s0; whether counter updates still limit the winner must be\\nestablished in the matched implementation evaluation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode only each row's two-bit saturating direction counter as a\\nthree-bit thermometer register q[2:0]. Logical counts 0, 1, 2, and 3\\nmap respectively to 000, 001, 011, and 111. Fetch uses q[1] directly\\nfor taken prediction, preserving the current threshold without a\\nbinary conversion or extra lookup logic. A taken hit increments by\\nshifting in one: {q[1:0], 1'b1}. A not-taken hit decrements by shifting\\nin zero: {1'b0, q[2:1]}. These operations saturate intrinsically.\\nA jump sets 111; a taken miss allocates 011. A not-taken miss,\\ninvalidation, and absence of a pending event retain the counter.\\n\\nExpress counter next state as parallel Boolean data terms, followed\\nby an unconditional clocked assignment, rather than arithmetic or\\nnested conditional writes. Specifically, let E be pending_valid_q\\nAND !reset and H be the existing valid-qualified full-tag row_hit.\\nDefine J = E AND command_is_JUMP, A = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND !H,\\nU = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND H, D = E AND command_is_NOT_TAKEN AND H,\\nand K = !(J OR A OR U OR D). Use bitwise equations:\\n  next_q[2] = J | (U & q[1]) | (K & q[2]);\\n  next_q[1] = J | A | (U & q[0]) | (D & q[2]) | (K & q[1]);\\n  next_q[0] = J | A | U | (D & q[1]) | (K & q[0]).\\nDecode the pending command locally and retain a single sequential\\nowner for each counter. Allocation terms must produce known constants\\neven when the old, invalid counter payload is uninitialized. Counter\\npayloads need no reset: reset suppresses updates and the existing\\nvalid reset masks them until a complete legal allocation.\\n\\nThe intended mapped circuit has no saturation detection or binary\\narithmetic and no tag-dependent counter CE, SET, or RESET input.\\nHit qualification and self-retention are represented in the data\\nequations. Inspect the mapped netlist to verify this property: an\\nequivalent rewrite that synthesis folds back into hit-qualified\\ncounter enables does not establish the intended optimization. Do not\\nintroduce device primitives, synthesis-only behavior, or blanket keep\\nattributes to force a result. The source-level cost is just sixteen\\nadditional counter bits; actual mapped LUT/FF cost must be measured.\\n\\nPreserve the sixteen rows, full 26-bit tags, exact tag comparisons,\\nvalid/tag update logic, separate indexed target store, all private\\npending events, and the existing capture/commit edges. Commit against\\ncurrent row state, including consecutive events and different tags\\naliasing one row. Keep matching-only invalidation and not-taken updates,\\ntarget retention, reset cancellation, and once-only pending-event drain\\nthrough stalls and redirects. Fetch still observes committed state\\nwith the same pre-edge semantics and no update bypass. This is a\\ncounter-encoding and logic-mapping micro-optimization, with identical\\nprediction policy, capacity, training latency, and instruction timing.\\n\\nEstimate an unmeasured four-percent fitness improvement through Fmax\\nalone. For identical ELF and ready schedules, require identical public\\nIF predictions, accepted memory transactions, valid RVFI retirements\\nwith their cycle positions, and marker-bracketed cycles. Confidence is\\nmedium: full-tag equality remains, valid-bit update or EX paths may\\nbecome limiting, and added data gating may worsen routing or be\\nremapped into the original enable structure.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed\\nFPGA gates. Compare against the current winner with the same tools,\\nELF, and bus-ready schedules; report critical paths and LUT4/FF cost.\\nCompute fitness from full-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles times\\nmedian Fmax. Historical notes identify 388.66's scored workload as\\n2 KB; keep that comparison separate from the requested canonical 6 KB\\nrun, verifying d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, memory bounds, both timing markers,\\nand the validation UART literal without altering evaluation files.\\nPreserve nret=1, external IO, RVFI order and traps, decoder legality,\\nmemory handshakes, all M-extension/ALTOPS logic, and every invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace each binary counter with the specified three-bit thermometer\\nregister and parallel next-data equations. Feed its middle bit\\ndirectly into the existing indexed fetch direction selection.\\nPreserve row-private event capture, full-tag matching, valid/tag\\nupdates, indexed target memory, and all reset/commit semantics.\\nAudit synthesized counter data/control cones for removal of binary\\nsaturation logic and tag-dependent counter enables; leave all other\\npipeline and predictor mechanisms unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Retain the independent logical integer-counter reference and its\\nexisting before/after-edge assertions. Extend public-output coverage\\nto systematically exercise all four logical counter states against\\njump, taken, not-taken, invalidating, and absent events on matching\\nand colliding tags in every row. Establish states through legal\\ntraining and distinguish same-direction states with subsequent\\nthreshold-crossing probes, without inspecting the encoded DUT bits.\\nCover allocation after reset/invalidation, both saturation limits,\\nconsecutive alias replacements and decrements, full tag/target bits,\\ntarget retention, capture/commit collisions, pending drain during\\nholds and redirects, and reset with an outstanding event. Preserve\\nevery existing assertion and all prediction timing expectations.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace each binary counter with the specified three-bit thermometer\\nregister and parallel next-data equations. Feed its middle bit\\ndirectly into the existing indexed fetch direction selection.\\nPreserve row-private event capture, full-tag matching, valid/tag\\nupdates, indexed target memory, and all reset/commit semantics.\\nAudit synthesized counter data/control cones for removal of binary\\nsaturation logic and tag-dependent counter enables; leave all other\\npipeline and predictor mechanisms unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Retain the independent logical integer-counter reference and its\\nexisting before/after-edge assertions. Extend public-output coverage\\nto systematically exercise all four logical counter states against\\njump, taken, not-taken, invalidating, and absent events on matching\\nand colliding tags in every row. Establish states through legal\\ntraining and distinguish same-direction states with subsequent\\nthreshold-crossing probes, without inspecting the encoded DUT bits.\\nCover allocation after reset/invalidation, both saturation limits,\\nconsecutive alias replacements and decrements, full tag/target bits,\\ntarget retention, capture/commit collisions, pending drain during\\nholds and redirects, and reset with an outstanding event. Preserve\\nevery existing assertion and all prediction timing expectations.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 354.4, \"delta_pct\": -8.81, \"vs_baseline\": 25.31, \"fmax_mhz\": 149.39, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6761, \"ff\": 3218, \"seeds\": [149.39, 144.3, 154.92], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T18:36:46.042631Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 6}\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Separate predictor tag validation from ordered metadata commit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner scores 388.66 iter/s at 163.83 MHz. Its\\npredictor already captures resolution events into private row registers,\\nbut each row still combines a 26-bit tag comparison, command decoding,\\ncounter selection, and metadata write control within one clock period.\\nRegistering the comparison result would divide this remaining maintenance\\npath without adding an instruction stage or extending EX recovery.\\n\\nThe structural history supports this specific timing direction:\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 separated indexed row reads from updates, and\\nhyp-20260905-002-r4s1 raised median Fmax from 147.75 to 156.62 MHz by\\ncapturing events before row-local updates. The latter's implementation\\nhistory identifies local tag-to-counter-enable paths in two routed seeds.\\nThese are historical bottlenecks, not a claim that a current route has\\nbeen reproduced. The winning r5s0 subsequently reduced ID/EX recovery\\nfanout. In contrast, structural r5s1 lost 29.62% fitness after changing\\nraw IF transport and validity masking, and r1s1 store buffering saved\\nonly 0.75% of cycles. Further partitioning maintenance work is therefore\\nthe most promising structural experiment while retaining the winning\\ninstruction pipeline and its bubble-control implementation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner scores 388.66 iter/s at 163.83 MHz. Its\\npredictor already captures resolution events into private row registers,\\nbut each row still combines a 26-bit tag comparison, command decoding,\\ncounter selection, and metadata write control within one clock period.\\nRegistering the comparison result would divide this remaining maintenance\\npath without adding an instruction stage or extending EX recovery.\\n\\nThe structural history supports this specific timing direction:\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 separated indexed row reads from updates, and\\nhyp-20260905-002-r4s1 raised median Fmax from 147.75 to 156.62 MHz by\\ncapturing events before row-local updates. The latter's implementation\\nhistory identifies local tag-to-counter-enable paths in two routed seeds.\\nThese are historical bottlenecks, not a claim that a current route has\\nbeen reproduced. The winning r5s0 subsequently reduced ID/EX recovery\\nfanout. In contrast, structural r5s1 lost 29.62% fitness after changing\\nraw IF transport and validity masking, and r1s1 store buffering saved\\nonly 0.75% of cycles. Further partitioning maintenance work is therefore\\nthe most promising structural experiment while retaining the winning\\ninstruction pipeline and its bubble-control implementation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one register boundary between row-local tag validation and predictor\\nmetadata commit in rtl/if_stage.sv. This is one structural retiming of\\nthe existing training transaction. Retain the sixteen direct-mapped rows,\\nPC[5:2] index, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, two-bit counter\\npolicy, allocation rules, and committed-state-only fetch lookup.\\n\\nUse three edges for an accepted event E. At edge N, the existing private\\nrow capture records E's tag and command. At edge N+1, transfer that event\\ninto a new commit register and capture one hit bit computed against the\\nrow's logical tag/valid state AFTER the older event committing on that\\nsame edge. At edge N+2, apply E using its registered hit, command, and\\ntag, together with the row's then-current two-bit counter. The existing\\ndesign commits at N+1, so this adds exactly one training-visibility cycle.\\nCapture, validate, and commit must overlap at one event per clock,\\nincluding an uninterrupted stream of resolutions to the same row.\\n\\nResolve the validation-stage read-after-write hazard locally. Let C be\\nthe older event currently in this row's commit register and P the event\\nin its existing pending register. Compute two full-width comparisons in\\nparallel: table_tag == P.tag and C.tag == P.tag. If C.valid and C.command\\nis JUMP or TAKEN, P's effective hit is the second comparison because C\\nallocates or refreshes a valid row with C.tag on the validation edge.\\nOtherwise, if C.valid, C.command is INVALIDATE, and C.hit is true, P's\\neffective hit is false because C clears the row. In all other cases use\\ntable_valid && (table_tag == P.tag). Register this effective hit with P.\\nSelection between comparator results is narrow; do not mux a 26-bit\\neffective tag ahead of one equality comparator. All forwarding is private\\nto the row and concerns tag/valid state only. No live resolve input or\\nindexed metadata read may enter the validation or commit datapath.\\n\\nAt commit, use the registered hit without re-comparing tags. JUMP writes\\nvalid=1, the event tag, target, and counter=11. TAKEN writes valid=1,\\nthe event tag and target, and counter=10 on a registered miss or a\\nsaturating increment of the current counter on a registered hit.\\nNOT_TAKEN decrements the current counter with saturation only on a\\nregistered hit and retains tag, valid, and target. INVALIDATE clears\\nvalid only on a registered hit. Preserve the current field-specific\\nupdate policy. Do not snapshot or forward the counter into validation:\\nin-order commits ensure the current counter already includes all older\\nevents. This avoids a new counter-update feedback bypass. After the\\npipeline drains, every table field must equal sequential application of\\nthe accepted resolution stream, even for alternating full-tag aliases.\\n\\nAdd a matching second delay register to the single indexed target-write\\npath, carrying target-valid, index, and target. The target and its metadata\\nmust become visible on the same N+2 edge, including consecutive writes\\nto the same index with different tags or JALR destinations. Do not let a\\nnewer target become visible under an older committed tag. The estimated\\nextra state is 16*(1 valid + 26 tag + 2 command + 1 hit) plus\\n(1 valid + 4 index + 30 target), or 515 source-level bits before mapping.\\n\\nRefresh both event-stage validity bits every clock independently of IF\\nstalls, memory holds, divide waits, and younger redirects. An accepted\\nolder event commits once even if instruction execution subsequently\\nstalls. Keep EX resolve_valid qualification unchanged so held instructions\\ncannot enqueue repeatedly. Reset clears every table-valid and both stages\\nof event/target validity, suppresses commit, and prevents pre-reset work\\nfrom repopulating the table. Invalid pipeline payload is never consulted\\nwithout its corresponding validity qualification.\\n\\nKeep the architectural instruction pipeline unchanged: no fetch bypass\\nfrom pending events, no training backpressure, no delayed EX redirect,\\nand no change to the exact predicted_next_pc captured with an instruction.\\nRecovery still compares that prediction with the resolved architectural\\ndestination and has the existing one-younger-slot penalty. Warm loops\\nmust retain one-instruction-per-cycle throughput; cold or rapidly changing\\nbranches can incur another miss because training becomes visible later.\\nPreserve IF's instruction-to-NOP behavior, the r5s0 split of ID/EX control\\nand payload updates, forwarding retention, load-use and bus interlocks,\\nand the real/ALTOPS multiplier and eight-edge divider schedules.\\n\\nPredict an unmeasured four-percent fitness gain from higher median Fmax\\nwith nearly unchanged CoreMark cycles. The added alias comparator,\\nroughly 515 extra state bits, placement changes, and delayed training\\ncan erase that gain; inspect all three post-route paths to determine\\nwhether tag comparison is actually separated from counter write control.\\nDo not add keep constraints, predictor-policy changes, or frontend\\nredesigns to this experiment. Success is a higher exact\\niterations/bracketed-cycles times three-seed median Fmax than the incoming\\nwinner under the identical workload, toolchain, and evaluation settings.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and FPGA gates.\\nPreserve nret=1 and all top-level ports, strict channel-0 retirement order,\\ndecoder legality and traps, memory bounds, stable stalled requests, and\\nexactly-once MMIO/marker accesses. Existing history distinguishes the\\nscored 2 KB run underlying 388.66 from the required canonical 6 KB run;\\nreport like-for-like results separately without altering the evaluator.\\nAlso validate the unchanged 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration workload with both\\nbus stalls, CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, Correct operation validated., and timing\\nstrictly between start/stop marker handshakes. All hard invariants and\\nprotected files remain intact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a per-row validation-to-commit register containing event-valid,\\nfull tag, command, and effective hit. Forward the older committing\\nevent's tag/valid effects into validation with parallel equalities;\\ncommit against the current counter using only the registered hit.\\nExtend the indexed target-write pipeline by the same one edge.\\nPreserve one-event-per-clock throughput, reset cancellation, exact\\nsequential update semantics, fetch lookup, and immediate recovery.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent sequential policy model to the additional\\ncommit delay and check intermediate committed state on every edge.\\nExercise uninterrupted same-row streams: allocate A then update A;\\nallocate A then allocate B then update A; invalidate a matching tag\\nthen reallocate; invalidate a different tag; jump followed by taken\\nand not-taken branches; saturation and changing targets. Cover all\\ncommand combinations, full-width aliases, different rows, idle drain,\\narbitrary stalls/redirects, and reset with both event stages occupied.\\nAssert atomic tag/target visibility, no fetch bypass, no dropped or\\nduplicated updates, and equality to the policy model after drain.\\nUpdate only intentional training-latency expectations while retaining\\nevery architectural and predictor-policy assertion.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt training observation to the extra maintenance stage and add\\ndirected tight-loop, alias, changing-JALR-target, stale-entry, and\\ntrapping-control tests with random imem/dmem stalls and divides.\\nCheck each redirect against the prediction actually accepted with\\nthat instruction, exactly-once resolution/retirement, ordered RVFI\\noperands and PC chains, wrong-path side-effect suppression, and\\ncontinued commit of older events during holds or younger recovery.\\nRetain the one-slot recovery and warm-loop throughput checks, all\\nmemory/marker/decoder/arithmetic checks, and existing forwarding\\ncoverage. Compare architectural traces rather than requiring the\\nold prediction or cycle schedule after the intentional latency change.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a per-row validation-to-commit register containing event-valid,\\nfull tag, command, and effective hit. Forward the older committing\\nevent's tag/valid effects into validation with parallel equalities;\\ncommit against the current counter using only the registered hit.\\nExtend the indexed target-write pipeline by the same one edge.\\nPreserve one-event-per-clock throughput, reset cancellation, exact\\nsequential update semantics, fetch lookup, and immediate recovery.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent sequential policy model to the additional\\ncommit delay and check intermediate committed state on every edge.\\nExercise uninterrupted same-row streams: allocate A then update A;\\nallocate A then allocate B then update A; invalidate a matching tag\\nthen reallocate; invalidate a different tag; jump followed by taken\\nand not-taken branches; saturation and changing targets. Cover all\\ncommand combinations, full-width aliases, different rows, idle drain,\\narbitrary stalls/redirects, and reset with both event stages occupied.\\nAssert atomic tag/target visibility, no fetch bypass, no dropped or\\nduplicated updates, and equality to the policy model after drain.\\nUpdate only intentional training-latency expectations while retaining\\nevery architectural and predictor-policy assertion.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt training observation to the extra maintenance stage and add\\ndirected tight-loop, alias, changing-JALR-target, stale-entry, and\\ntrapping-control tests with random imem/dmem stalls and divides.\\nCheck each redirect against the prediction actually accepted with\\nthat instruction, exactly-once resolution/retirement, ordered RVFI\\noperands and PC chains, wrong-path side-effect suppression, and\\ncontinued commit of older events during holds or younger recovery.\\nRetain the one-slot recovery and warm-loop throughput checks, all\\nmemory/marker/decoder/arithmetic checks, and existing forwarding\\ncoverage. Compare architectural traces rather than requiring the\\nold prediction or cycle schedule after the intentional latency change.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 230.66, \"delta_pct\": -40.65, \"vs_baseline\": -18.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 97.2, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4214035, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7197, \"ff\": 3685, \"seeds\": [105.33, 97.2, 96.29], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T18:42:57.903603Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6}\n19:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Eight-bit partial tags for the target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Predictor timing has delivered substantially more fitness than the recent\\naccuracy additions. The r2s2 two-way table lost 7.32%, and the r5s2 return\\nstack saved only 0.17% of scored cycles while losing 3.98% fitness. The\\nsuccessful r4s1 event pipeline isolated live EX feedback, but two routed\\nseeds then had local stored-tag/pending-tag paths into counter enables.\\nThat comparison still spans 26 bits in the current r5s0 RTL, whose score\\nis 388.66 at 163.83 MHz. Shortening predictor identity is an untried way\\nto reduce comparator logic, indexed fetch mux width, and register routing\\nwithout increasing capacity, adding another prediction source, or changing\\nthe existing update latency. These historical paths motivate the proposal;\\nthey are not a new measurement of the current winner's critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Predictor timing has delivered substantially more fitness than the recent\\naccuracy additions. The r2s2 two-way table lost 7.32%, and the r5s2 return\\nstack saved only 0.17% of scored cycles while losing 3.98% fitness. The\\nsuccessful r4s1 event pipeline isolated live EX feedback, but two routed\\nseeds then had local stored-tag/pending-tag paths into counter enables.\\nThat comparison still spans 26 bits in the current r5s0 RTL, whose score\\nis 388.66 at 163.83 MHz. Shortening predictor identity is an untried way\\nto reduce comparator logic, indexed fetch mux width, and register routing\\nwithout increasing capacity, adding another prediction source, or changing\\nthe existing update latency. These historical paths motivate the proposal;\\nthey are not a new measurement of the current winner's critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the 26-bit full tags with fixed eight-bit partial tags PC[13:6]\\nin the existing 16-entry direct-mapped target predictor. Keep the index\\nPC[5:2]. Apply this exact partial identity consistently to fetch lookup,\\nrow-local training comparison, stored tags, and private pending-event\\ntags. Use direct bit slices, with no hash, high-address qualifier, or\\ninstruction decode added to the fetch path. This is solely a predictor\\ntag-width experiment. All PCs, stored targets[31:2], accepted predicted\\nnext PCs, and architectural next PCs retain their existing full widths.\\n\\nThe table shrinks from 944 to 656 source-level state bits, and pending\\nevents from 499 to 211 bits: 576 fewer bits in total, before synthesis.\\nEight-bit equality also gives a smaller LUT4 reduction than 26-bit\\nequality. The intended benefit is higher median Fmax with approximately\\nunchanged benchmark cycles, rather than an assumed accuracy improvement.\\nFPGA mapping and routing must confirm both the storage and timing effects.\\n\\nKeep each row's independently enabled pending-event registers and the\\nexisting resolution-edge capture followed by next-edge commit. Commit\\nagainst current local state while accepting the next event, including\\nconsecutive same-row aliases. Preserve the indexed 16x30 target store\\nand its aligned delayed write. Pending validity drains every edge through\\nfetch stalls, backend holds, and redirects; reset cancels pending work\\nand clears table validity. Lookup uses committed state only, with the\\npre-edge prediction selected on a commit edge and no update bypass.\\n\\nRetain all counter rules under the new partial-tag match: jumps allocate\\nstrongly taken; taken conditional misses allocate weakly taken and hits\\nsaturating-increment; not-taken hits saturating-decrement while retaining\\nthe target, and misses do nothing. Non-control or trapping resolutions\\ninvalidate a partial-tag hit and never allocate. Legal target writes and\\ntheir metadata become visible together. Train only on the existing valid,\\nadvancing EX resolution pulse, never repeatedly during memory/divide waits.\\n\\nPCs with identical low 14 bits now intentionally share predictor identity,\\nincluding addresses separated by 16 KiB. Such aliases can cause false\\npredictions, shared hysteresis, or invalidation of another PC's entry;\\nthis is the explicit accuracy tradeoff. Do not restrict executable address\\nranges or assume that high PC bits are zero. Preserve EX's exact full-width\\nactual_next_pc versus accepted predicted_next_pc comparison for EVERY\\nadvancing instruction, including non-controls, illegal instructions,\\ncompleted divides, and JALR target changes. Recovery must still kill the\\nyounger slot before it can retire or issue a data access. Preserve recovery\\npriority over imem stalls, deferred resolution during backend holds, and\\nresolved architectural RVFI next PC. No top-level IO, nret=1, decoder,\\ntrap, forwarding, memory, or M-extension behavior changes are proposed.\\n\\nEstimate a five percent net fitness gain with medium confidence; this is\\nunmeasured. Extra alias recoveries or a different routing bottleneck can\\nerase the clock gain. Compare the current winner and candidate using the\\nsame ELF, ready schedule, toolchain, and three P&R seeds. Report bracketed\\ncycles, recovery counts, median Fmax, LUT4/FF cost, and critical endpoints.\\nCompute fitness with full-precision iterations/cycle. Run unchanged formal,\\nunit, cosim, bounds, marker, and UART gates, including the requested 6 KB\\nrun with d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs and Correct operation validated. The historical\\nimplementation notes identify the logged scores as 2 KB results; keep\\nthat like-for-like score comparison separate from 6 KB throughput without\\nchanging either benchmark or any evaluation-contract file.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Narrow tag_view, each row's tag_q, and pending_tag_q to eight bits.\\nUse PC[13:6] and resolve_pc[13:6] for capture and comparisons. Retain\\nall sixteen rows, full targets, private event capture, commit timing,\\ncounter policy, valid gating, and PC update priority. Update comments\\nand state accounting to describe intentional partial-tag aliases;\\ndo not add speculative interlocks or a secondary predictor.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update the independent public-output policy model to eight-bit tags\\nwhile preserving exact predictions and edge-by-edge commit checks.\\nRetain all rows, counter states, reset, target-bit, and stall coverage.\\nCheck every retained tag bit rejects mismatches; explicitly test that\\ntoggling each omitted PC bit aliases, including consecutive colliding\\nupdates and non-control/trap invalidation of a partial-tag match.\\nCover distinct tags, full-address targets, simultaneous capture/commit,\\nand reset cancellation. Track probe PCs explicitly rather than\\nreconstructing a unique full PC from a now-ambiguous stored tag.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Adjust only predictor-policy expectations and the independent training\\nmodel for partial tags. Add programs at in-bounds PCs such as 0x100\\nand 0x4100 that deliberately collide but require different next PCs.\\nCover false hits on ALU instructions, loads/stores, conditional branches,\\njumps, changing JALRs, illegal/misaligned transfers, and a blocking\\ndivide. Put observable stores on mispredicted paths and require zero\\nwrong-path data accesses or retirements. Exercise load-use interlocks,\\nimem stalls, dmem holds, pending commits, and reset. Preserve exact\\naccepted-prediction recovery, once-only training, strict RVFI order,\\nPC chaining, operand/link values, arithmetic, and all trap assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Narrow tag_view, each row's tag_q, and pending_tag_q to eight bits.\\nUse PC[13:6] and resolve_pc[13:6] for capture and comparisons. Retain\\nall sixteen rows, full targets, private event capture, commit timing,\\ncounter policy, valid gating, and PC update priority. Update comments\\nand state accounting to describe intentional partial-tag aliases;\\ndo not add speculative interlocks or a secondary predictor.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update the independent public-output policy model to eight-bit tags\\nwhile preserving exact predictions and edge-by-edge commit checks.\\nRetain all rows, counter states, reset, target-bit, and stall coverage.\\nCheck every retained tag bit rejects mismatches; explicitly test that\\ntoggling each omitted PC bit aliases, including consecutive colliding\\nupdates and non-control/trap invalidation of a partial-tag match.\\nCover distinct tags, full-address targets, simultaneous capture/commit,\\nand reset cancellation. Track probe PCs explicitly rather than\\nreconstructing a unique full PC from a now-ambiguous stored tag.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Adjust only predictor-policy expectations and the independent training\\nmodel for partial tags. Add programs at in-bounds PCs such as 0x100\\nand 0x4100 that deliberately collide but require different next PCs.\\nCover false hits on ALU instructions, loads/stores, conditional branches,\\njumps, changing JALRs, illegal/misaligned transfers, and a blocking\\ndivide. Put observable stores on mispredicted paths and require zero\\nwrong-path data accesses or retirements. Exercise load-use interlocks,\\nimem stalls, dmem holds, pending commits, and reset. Preserve exact\\naccepted-prediction recovery, once-only training, strict RVFI order,\\nPC chaining, operand/link values, arithmetic, and all trap assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 340.12, \"delta_pct\": -12.49, \"vs_baseline\": 20.26, \"fmax_mhz\": 143.37, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6080, \"ff\": 2595, \"seeds\": [143.37, 142.57, 151.17], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T18:39:27.162560Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r6s2 (regression, -12.49%): Eight-bit `PC[13:6]` predictor tags saved 538 LUT4s and 603 FFs with unchanged 2 KB/6 KB CoreMark cycles and recoveries, but full-width target generation into `pending_target_q.D` became critical in all three seeds, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 143.37 MHz; reducing tag width can worsen timing outside the tag comparator.\"}\n20:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Predecode the multiply-high result class before execute\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner scores 388.66 at 163.83 MHz. Saved baseline RTL in\\n.tmp/thermometer-r6s0/baseline/cores/bench/rtl matches every current bench\\nRTL file byte-for-byte, and its generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log\\nreports reproduce 163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz. In seeds 1 and 3 the\\ncritical path starts at ID/EX alu_op[4] and alu_op[2], respectively,\\nand ends at EX/MEM alu_result[25], taking 6.10 and 5.93 ns. Inspection\\nof the corresponding synth.json LUT truth table identifies the first\\ndecode term as exactly op in {12,13,14}: MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\nThis is current-source timing evidence for result-class decoding,\\nrather than an assumption that predictor metadata still limits Fmax.\\n\\nThe prior r2s0 full nineteen-bit ALU predecode lost 3.92 percent after\\nexposing recovery fanout; r5s0 subsequently isolated that wide payload\\ncontrol. The recent r6s0 counter rewrite, r6s1 additional predictor\\nupdate stage, and r6s2 shortened tags all regressed. This proposal\\ntargets only the measured multiply-high class term with one additional\\ninstruction bit and leaves the rest of ALU operation decoding intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner scores 388.66 at 163.83 MHz. Saved baseline RTL in\\n.tmp/thermometer-r6s0/baseline/cores/bench/rtl matches every current bench\\nRTL file byte-for-byte, and its generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log\\nreports reproduce 163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz. In seeds 1 and 3 the\\ncritical path starts at ID/EX alu_op[4] and alu_op[2], respectively,\\nand ends at EX/MEM alu_result[25], taking 6.10 and 5.93 ns. Inspection\\nof the corresponding synth.json LUT truth table identifies the first\\ndecode term as exactly op in {12,13,14}: MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\\nThis is current-source timing evidence for result-class decoding,\\nrather than an assumption that predictor metadata still limits Fmax.\\n\\nThe prior r2s0 full nineteen-bit ALU predecode lost 3.92 percent after\\nexposing recovery fanout; r5s0 subsequently isolated that wide payload\\ncontrol. The recent r6s0 counter rewrite, r6s1 additional predictor\\nupdate stage, and r6s2 shortened tags all regressed. This proposal\\ntargets only the measured multiply-high class term with one additional\\ninstruction bit and leaves the rest of ALU operation decoding intact.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move the single predicate dec_alu_op == ALU_MULH ||\\ndec_alu_op == ALU_MULHU || dec_alu_op == ALU_MULHSU across the ID/EX\\nregister. Add a mul_high_sel bit to id_ex_t, preferably at its most\\nsignificant end so existing lower field positions remain stable.\\nCapture this predicate in ID with the control group: reset or flush\\nclears it, a backend stall holds it, and ordinary capture updates it\\nfrom dec_alu_op. Keep one sequential owner. Preserve the existing\\nindependent wide-payload update rules and the five-bit ALU encoding.\\nDo not use the auxiliary bit to determine instruction legality.\\n\\nWire the registered bit through EX into an additional internal ALU\\ninput. Factor only the multiply-high contribution out of the main\\nresult case. Keep all current integer, MUL-low, and DIV/REM case arms\\nand default-zero behavior in a non_high_result path; omit the three\\nhigh-product arms there, so that path is zero for their opcodes.\\nSelect the high-product candidate using only op[1:0], whose current\\nmapping is 00=MULH, 01=MULHU, 10=MULHSU, with 11 producing zero.\\nCombine the two disjoint contributions as\\n  out = non_high_result | ({32{mul_high_sel}} & high_result).\\nThe registered class bit directly enables the high-result term;\\ndo not reconstruct its five-bit opcode comparison in EX or qualify\\nit with a second full-op decode. For every reachable instruction the\\nregistered bit and opcode must agree, including bubbles and holds.\\n\\nPreserve the existing signed/unsigned product expressions, widths,\\nand bit slices. In RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, the same suboperation mapping\\nmust select the existing MULH/MULHU add-XOR formulas and MULHSU\\nsubtract-XOR formula with their exact constants. MUL-low and all\\ndivider arithmetic, request/result handshakes, and the shared real\\nand ALTOPS eight-edge schedule remain unchanged. This is one local\\ncontrol-retiming optimization, with no new arithmetic algorithm,\\ninstruction stage, execution latency, or predictor-policy change.\\n\\nExpect an unmeasured four-percent fitness gain through median Fmax\\nalone. The intended mapped benefit is removing the measured encoded\\nclass detector from the high-result selection path. Inspect synthesis\\nand all three routed reports to verify that the registered select is\\nactually used. Added ID decode delay, select routing, synthesis\\nrefactoring, or the seed-2 recovery path becoming limiting can erase\\nthe gain. Do not expand this into whole-ALU one-hot selection, opcode\\nrenumbering, predictor changes, or synthesis keep constraints.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, real and ALTOPS unit suites, formal, stalled\\ncosim, and three-seed FPGA gates. Check exact M-extension results and\\nselect/op alignment through load-use bubbles, redirects, dmem holds,\\nand divide waits. Require identical valid RVFI retirements with their\\ncycle positions, accepted bus transactions, predictions, and bracketed\\ncycles for matched ELF and ready schedules. Preserve nret=1, all\\nexternal ports, decoder/trap rules, strict retirement order, memory\\nbounds, markers, UART validation, and every protected evaluation file.\\nCompute fitness with full-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles times\\nmedian Fmax. Historical notes identify the recorded 388.66 comparison\\nas a 2 KB score; keep that like-for-like comparison separate from the\\nrequested unchanged 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration run with both bus stalls,\\nCRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, and the literal Correct operation validated.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add one mul_high_sel field to id_ex_t only. Preserve all existing\\noperation constants, ctrl_t fields, and downstream bundle meanings.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode membership in MULH/MULHU/MULHSU before ID/EX and register\\nit with control reset/flush/hold priorities. Preserve the r5s0\\nseparation between control updates and wide-payload updates.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the registered multiply-high class bit to the internal\\nALU. Keep forwarded operands, link selection, divide interlocks,\\nfull EX/MEM bubbles, branch resolution, and training unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept mul_high_sel and extract only the three high-multiply\\nresult arms into a suboperation-selected contribution gated by\\nthis bit. OR it with the disjoint remaining result case. Retain\\nexact real products, ALTOPS formulas, and divider behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Drive the new class input consistently at every opcode change,\\nincluding setup and divider input-perturbation tests. Retain all\\nexact real/ALTOPS vectors and handshake assertions. Add alternating\\nhigh-multiply, MUL-low, integer, and divide selections with signed\\nboundary operands to catch stale or incorrectly decoded classes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend reset/flush/stall priority checks to the new control bit,\\ncovering all three high-multiply operations and neighboring MUL\\nand DIV encodings. Assert alignment with the captured operation\\nindependently of payload capture, including flush plus hold.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update id_ex_t unpacking and assert the auxiliary selector agrees\\nwith the held or advancing opcode. Exercise high multiplies with\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, stalled-load dependencies, load-use\\nbubbles, redirects, divide waits, and reset. Preserve exact RVFI\\narithmetic, operands, PC chains, retirement order, bus effects,\\nand all existing prediction and cycle-level assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add one mul_high_sel field to id_ex_t only. Preserve all existing\\noperation constants, ctrl_t fields, and downstream bundle meanings.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode membership in MULH/MULHU/MULHSU before ID/EX and register\\nit with control reset/flush/hold priorities. Preserve the r5s0\\nseparation between control updates and wide-payload updates.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the registered multiply-high class bit to the internal\\nALU. Keep forwarded operands, link selection, divide interlocks,\\nfull EX/MEM bubbles, branch resolution, and training unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept mul_high_sel and extract only the three high-multiply\\nresult arms into a suboperation-selected contribution gated by\\nthis bit. OR it with the disjoint remaining result case. Retain\\nexact real products, ALTOPS formulas, and divider behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Drive the new class input consistently at every opcode change,\\nincluding setup and divider input-perturbation tests. Retain all\\nexact real/ALTOPS vectors and handshake assertions. Add alternating\\nhigh-multiply, MUL-low, integer, and divide selections with signed\\nboundary operands to catch stale or incorrectly decoded classes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend reset/flush/stall priority checks to the new control bit,\\ncovering all three high-multiply operations and neighboring MUL\\nand DIV encodings. Assert alignment with the captured operation\\nindependently of payload capture, including flush plus hold.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update id_ex_t unpacking and assert the auxiliary selector agrees\\nwith the held or advancing opcode. Exercise high multiplies with\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, stalled-load dependencies, load-use\\nbubbles, redirects, divide waits, and reset. Preserve exact RVFI\\narithmetic, operands, PC chains, retirement order, bus effects,\\nand all existing prediction and cycle-level assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 350.46, \"delta_pct\": -9.83, \"vs_baseline\": 23.92, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.73, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6632, \"ff\": 3203, \"seeds\": [149.12, 144.63, 147.73], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the multiply-high predecode hypothesis in the four proposed bench RTL files. `id_ex_t.mul_high_sel` is the new most-significant bit (230); all previous field positions and the five-bit ALU encoding remain unchanged. ID owns its only sequential assignment alongside ctrl/valid: reset or flush clears it, a backend hold preserves it, and normal capture records membership in MULH/MULHU/MULHSU independently of validity. The independent wide-payload update block is unchanged.\\n\\nEX wires that register directly to the internal ALU. The main result case retains integer operations, MUL-low, DIV/REM and default zero. Only the three high-product arms move to a case on op[1:0], with 11 returning zero. The final result is the requested OR of non_high_result and the selector-masked high_result. All signedness, product widths/slices, ALTOPS constants and add/sub formulas remain exact. The divider, eight-edge schedule, forwarding, prediction, decoder/trap rules, RVFI ports, and nret=1 are unchanged.\\n\\nTests now drive the new ALU input at every opcode change, including reset and rejected/perturbed divide requests. Added alternating result classes with signed boundary operands, all reserved ALU default-zero opcodes, high multiplication while the divider is occupied, ID control/hold priority combinations (including flush+hold and invalid captures), and whole-pipeline selector/op assertions before and after every edge. Directed real and ALTOPS pipeline tests cover all high operations, EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, dependent stalled loads, load-use bubbles, redirects with instruction stalls, divide waits and reset of both held live instructions and nonzero bubbles. Existing assertions were retained.\\n\\nLocal verification:\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\\n- `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider cores/bench/test/`: PASS, 10 pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU tests and the additional ALTOPS high-multiply pipeline tests.\\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, no RTL fix attempts. The first invocation stopped before generating checks because the default Python lacked PyYAML. Selecting the installed conda Python on PATH allowed the unchanged `formal/run_all.sh` to auto-detect nret=1 and run wrapper_si/checks_si. The script and framework were copied into bench scratch space to avoid writing into the shared protected formal tree. Full run evidence: `cores/bench/.tmp/mul-high-select/candidate/formal-gate.log`.\\n- Unchanged cosim gate: PASS for selftest and default 2 KB CoreMark. Additional selftest cosim against the unchanged Python ISS passed with both bus stalls for ready seeds 0xDEADBEEF, 1 and 42 (115 retirements each).\\n- Three unchanged FPGA routing gates: PASS. Candidate seeds 1/2/3 = 149.12 / 144.63 / 147.73 MHz; median = 147.73 MHz. Mapped resources: 6,632 LUT4, 3,203 DFF.\\n- Fresh synthesis and all three routes of the saved, byte-identical pre-edit RTL reproduce the historical baseline exactly: 163.83 / 155.33 / 168.55 MHz, median 163.83 MHz, 6,618 LUT4 and 3,198 DFF. The candidate adds 14 mapped LUT4 and 5 mapped DFF despite adding only one source-level instruction bit.\\n\\nSaved the original bench RTL before editing and compared baseline/candidate executions of the exact same ELFs and xorshift ready schedules, with both bus stalls enabled. An observation-only scratch copy of the unchanged C++ harness emitted binary records for every cycle's prediction/readiness/acceptance/redirect/divide-wait state, accepted instruction and data bus transactions, and every field of every valid RVFI retirement including its cycle. Direct block-by-block byte comparisons passed. The candidate also asserted selector/op alignment before and after every simulated edge. The unmodified C++ harness independently reproduced the 6 KB run's final RVFI, UART, and markers exactly.\\n\\n| Workload | Valid retirements | Start / stop cycles | Bracketed cycles | Full-precision iterations/cycle | Candidate fitness |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 2,937,037 | 15,229 / 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 10 / 4,215,262 | 350.4645737323089 |\\n| 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 22,093,920 | 53,747 / 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 10 / 30,822,790 | 47.92882149863785 |\\n\\nFitness uses `(10 / bracketed_cycles) * median_Fmax_MHz * 1,000,000` without rounding iterations/cycle. The historical 388.66 comparison is the 2 KB score: 163.83 MHz gives 388.65911537645826 on this same 2 KB ELF/schedule. The candidate regresses 9.827259964597456 percent. The corresponding 6 KB baseline score at 163.83 MHz is 53.152229243361816 and is kept separate.\\n\\nThe requested 6 KB workload completed in bounds, with seedcrc 8a02, crclist d4b0, crcmatrix be52, crcstate 5e47, crcfinal 5949, 10 iterations and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. This checkout's protected Makefile/evaluator actually default to 2 KB and its CRCs; those gates were run unchanged. The extra 6 KB ELF was built from unchanged copied benchmark sources with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`. UART's fixed compiler-description string still says -O2; the captured compiler commands confirm -O3 for both ELFs. No evaluation source was modified.\\n\\nSynthesis confirms the intended retiming: `cpu.u_ex.u_alu.mul_high_sel_DFFRE_Q` drives only `mul_high_sel_LUT3_I0`, whose inputs are the registered selector, op[1], and op[0], with INIT=0x2a (selector AND suboperation != 11). No five-bit high-class comparison remains at that enable. All three routed netlists retain this DFFRE and LUT3, placed respectively at X9Y16/DFF4 -> X9Y15/LUT3, X11Y12/DFF3 -> X11Y13/LUT1, and X11Y16/DFF1 -> X7Y16/LUT6.\\n\\nThe measured gain did not materialize. All three candidate critical paths instead traverse EX/MEM destination-register forwarding selection, the forwarded JALR target addition, and the predictor's existing pending target register. Seed 1: ex_mem_rd[2] -> pending_target_q[26], 6.71 ns (3.62 logic + 3.09 routing). Seed 2: ex_mem_rd[4] -> pending_target_q[28], 6.92 ns (4.02 + 2.90). Seed 3: ex_mem_rd[0] -> pending_target_q[25], 6.77 ns (3.49 + 3.28). This remains one local control retiming; no predictor or recovery policy change was added to chase the exposed paths.\\n\\nDetailed benchmark markers, ELF/trace SHA-256 hashes, byte counts, synthesis connectivity, routed timing summaries and fitness calculations are in `cores/bench/.tmp/mul-high-select/`. Routed candidate reports are in `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/`. Real-arithmetic formal-deep was not run; real arithmetic is covered by exact ALU vectors, pipeline tests, stalled ISS cosim and baseline-equivalent full CoreMark traces.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T19:21:32.984860Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r7s0 (regression, -9.83%): A single registered `mul_high_sel` removed the measured five-bit EX class detector yet lowered median Fmax from 163.83 to 147.73 MHz with identical stalled CoreMark cycles because EX/MEM `rd` forwarding through the JALR target adder into `pending_target_q` became critical in all three seeds; narrow predecode still requires checking timing outside the ALU.\"}\n21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Move PC-relative address generation across the ID/EX boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 388.66 iter/s at 163.83 MHz. Execute\\nstill computes PC+4 and PC+immediate, and AUIPC selects PC onto the\\nshared ALU operand bus before arithmetic and result selection. These\\ncalculations depend only on instruction metadata already available in\\ndecode, so their register boundary can move without delaying execution\\nor changing branch prediction.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment history favor a different\\npartition from further predictor-maintenance staging. Structural r6s1\\nadded tag-validation staging but fell to 97.20 MHz with almost unchanged\\ncycles. Structural r5s1 exposed PC-to-EX/MEM ALU critical paths in all\\nthree seeds; the r6s2 lesson also reports full-width target generation\\ninto pending_target_q.D as critical. These are observations on related\\ncandidates, not a reproduced timing diagnosis of the current winner.\\nNo recorded bench hypothesis has moved PC-relative arithmetic into ID.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 388.66 iter/s at 163.83 MHz. Execute\\nstill computes PC+4 and PC+immediate, and AUIPC selects PC onto the\\nshared ALU operand bus before arithmetic and result selection. These\\ncalculations depend only on instruction metadata already available in\\ndecode, so their register boundary can move without delaying execution\\nor changing branch prediction.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment history favor a different\\npartition from further predictor-maintenance staging. Structural r6s1\\nadded tag-validation staging but fell to 97.20 MHz with almost unchanged\\ncycles. Structural r5s1 exposed PC-to-EX/MEM ALU critical paths in all\\nthree seeds; the r6s2 lesson also reports full-width target generation\\ninto pending_target_q.D as critical. These are observations on related\\ncandidates, not a reproduced timing diagnosis of the current winner.\\nNo recorded bench hypothesis has moved PC-relative arithmetic into ID.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retime the operand-independent PC arithmetic from EX into the existing\\ndecode-to-execute register. Add exactly two 32-bit ID/EX payload fields:\\npc_plus4 = in.pc + 32'd4 and pc_relative = in.pc + decoded immediate,\\nboth computed modulo 2^32 in id_stage. Retain the original PC, immediate,\\ninstruction, operands, and exact predicted_next_pc. This is one\\nstructural redistribution of work across an existing pipeline boundary;\\nit adds no instruction stage, issue bubble, or training stage.\\n\\nGive both new fields the same update discipline as the winning r5s0\\npayload: deterministic zero on reset, hold only on the existing backend\\nstall, and otherwise capture with the rest of the instruction payload.\\nFlush continues to clear every control bit and valid independently of\\npayload capture. Preserve flush-versus-hold priority and the current IF\\ninstruction-to-NOP behavior. Compute the sums in parallel with register\\nreads and control decode; no register operand or forwarding selection\\nbelongs in either sum. A held instruction must retain its own sums\\neven when the live fetch data, prediction, or immediate changes.\\n\\nIn EX, use registered pc_relative for branch targets, direct JAL\\ntargets, and the AUIPC writeback result. Use registered pc_plus4 for\\nJAL/JALR link values and the default architectural next PC, including\\nnot-taken branches, traps, ordinary instructions, and completed divides\\nrecovering from stale predictions. Preserve full-width target bits\\nuntil the existing alignment checks; never round a misaligned direct\\ntarget into a legal one.\\n\\nRemove the is_auipc ? PC : rs1 selection from the shared ALU A input:\\nordinary ALU and M-extension operations take the existing forwarded\\nrs1, while AUIPC selects its registered pc_relative at the EX result\\nselection beside the jump-link selection. Thus ID/EX PC no longer\\ndrives the common ALU operand arithmetic, and direct-target calculation\\nno longer adds a carry chain before EX recovery or predictor-event\\ncapture. Keep the existing ALU B selection and all ALU operations.\\n\\nJALR remains in EX, using the actual post-forward rs1 plus the held\\nimmediate and clearing bit zero exactly as today. Its base must not\\ncome from an early regfile snapshot. Branch conditions also stay in EX\\nwith existing operand forwarding. Keep misalignment/trap qualification,\\nresult-write suppression, resolved pc_next, and the exact comparison\\nagainst the prediction accepted with that instruction. Predictor row\\norganization, full tags, counters, event timing, and target commit are\\nunchanged. Resolve and retire each advancing instruction exactly once.\\n\\nExpect an unmeasured five-percent fitness improvement from shorter EX\\narithmetic and control paths with identical instruction scheduling.\\nRisks are a new decode-immediate-adder critical path, the extra result\\nselection for AUIPC, roughly 64 extra payload bits before mapping, and\\nunchanged predictor or JALR paths limiting Fmax. This experiment must\\nstand on its own; retain the present frontend, predictor, arithmetic\\nengines, forwarding, load-use interlock, and bus-hold protocol.\\n\\nValidate the changed stage boundary with ID and pipeline cocotb tests,\\nthen run the unchanged lint, formal, stalled cosim, and FPGA gates.\\nRequire unchanged architectural traces and cycle schedules for identical\\ninstruction streams and bus-ready sequences. Inspect all three routed\\ncritical paths to see whether moving the adders improved the actual\\nbottleneck. Compare exact iterations/bracketed cycles times three-seed\\nmedian Fmax against the incoming winner using the identical ELF and\\nevaluation settings; do not use the rounded core.yaml iterations/cycle\\nvalue. Record the working-set configuration, keeping the historical\\nscored workload and any separate canonical 6 KB run distinct. Validate\\nthe required 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration run with both bus stalls, CRCs\\nd4b0/be52/5e47, and the literal Correct operation validated.\\n\\nPreserve nret=1 and the entire external port contract, channel-0 RVFI\\norder and operand values, decoder legality and trap checks, memory\\nbounds and side-effect ordering, and start/stop marker handshakes.\\nReal and ALTOPS arithmetic, including the eight-edge divider schedule,\\nare unchanged. Only bench RTL and its permitted unit tests are targets;\\nno evaluation, wrapper, benchmark, or other-core files change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend id_ex_t with 32-bit pc_plus4 and pc_relative payload fields.\\nKeep original metadata and control fields and the other pipeline\\nbundle layouts intact. Document that these sums belong to the\\ncaptured instruction and are available without EX arithmetic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the two full-width PC sums from in.pc and the existing\\ndecoded immediate, and capture them in the payload sequential block\\nwith reset-only clearing and the existing backend-hold enable.\\nPreserve the separate control/valid flush logic and its priorities.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace PC+4 and PC+immediate consumers with their ID/EX fields.\\nRoute AUIPC's registered sum into the EX/MEM result selection and\\nremove PC selection ahead of the common ALU A operand. Retain\\nforwarded JALR addition, bit-zero clearing, branch comparisons,\\nalignment traps, exact prediction recovery, divider handshakes,\\nand every RVFI operand and next-PC field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend payload assertions for the two sums and retain every existing\\nreset/flush/stall combination. Add independently decoded B/J/U\\nimmediate vectors with positive and negative offsets, carry across\\nbit 16 and bit 31, modulo-32-bit wraparound, and unaligned targets.\\nUse synthetic stage inputs for arbitrary full-width PCs. Check\\natomic capture with instruction metadata and stability through holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update _id_fields and payload-input expectations for the added fields\\nwithout weakening control, forwarding, or prediction assertions.\\nCover AUIPC followed immediately by dependent ALU, load/store, and\\nJALR instructions; direct jumps and taken/not-taken branches; link\\nvalues, wrong-path stores, stale predictions, and misalignment traps.\\nExercise imem/dmem stalls, held instructions, reset, and divide\\nwaits. Keep the predictor reference model and timing expectations\\nunchanged, and assert exact channel-0 order, PC chains, operands,\\nand memory effects under both zero-wait and stalled execution.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend id_ex_t with 32-bit pc_plus4 and pc_relative payload fields.\\nKeep original metadata and control fields and the other pipeline\\nbundle layouts intact. Document that these sums belong to the\\ncaptured instruction and are available without EX arithmetic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the two full-width PC sums from in.pc and the existing\\ndecoded immediate, and capture them in the payload sequential block\\nwith reset-only clearing and the existing backend-hold enable.\\nPreserve the separate control/valid flush logic and its priorities.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace PC+4 and PC+immediate consumers with their ID/EX fields.\\nRoute AUIPC's registered sum into the EX/MEM result selection and\\nremove PC selection ahead of the common ALU A operand. Retain\\nforwarded JALR addition, bit-zero clearing, branch comparisons,\\nalignment traps, exact prediction recovery, divider handshakes,\\nand every RVFI operand and next-PC field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend payload assertions for the two sums and retain every existing\\nreset/flush/stall combination. Add independently decoded B/J/U\\nimmediate vectors with positive and negative offsets, carry across\\nbit 16 and bit 31, modulo-32-bit wraparound, and unaligned targets.\\nUse synthetic stage inputs for arbitrary full-width PCs. Check\\natomic capture with instruction metadata and stability through holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update _id_fields and payload-input expectations for the added fields\\nwithout weakening control, forwarding, or prediction assertions.\\nCover AUIPC followed immediately by dependent ALU, load/store, and\\nJALR instructions; direct jumps and taken/not-taken branches; link\\nvalues, wrong-path stores, stale predictions, and misalignment traps.\\nExercise imem/dmem stalls, held instructions, reset, and divide\\nwaits. Keep the predictor reference model and timing expectations\\nunchanged, and assert exact channel-0 order, PC chains, operands,\\nand memory effects under both zero-wait and stalled execution.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.41, \"delta_pct\": -11.13, \"vs_baseline\": 22.13, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.6, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6554, \"ff\": 3233, \"seeds\": [150.65, 145.6, 145.14], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the PC-relative ID/EX retiming as proposed. All correctness gates passed, but the measured fitness regressed by 11.1273881462%. The candidate remains implemented for the orchestrator to evaluate; no unrelated optimization or predictor change was added.\\n\\nChanged only cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv, id_stage.sv, ex_stage.sv and the permitted test_id_stage.py / test_pipeline.py, plus this requested writeup. The ID/EX bundle has exactly two additional 32-bit fields, pc_plus4 and pc_relative. ID computes both from the incoming PC and decoded immediate, modulo 2^32, alongside decode and register reads. Both fields reset to zero, hold only under the existing backend stall, and otherwise capture with the payload, including on flush. The separate control/valid flush logic, flush-over-hold priority, and IF instruction-to-NOP behavior are unchanged.\\n\\nEX uses the registered relative sum for branches, direct JAL and AUIPC, and the registered PC+4 for jump links and the default architectural next PC. The shared ALU A port now takes forwarded rs1 directly. JALR still adds post-forward rs1 to the held immediate in EX and clears only bit zero. Target alignment checks, traps, write suppression, exact accepted-prediction comparison, forwarding, predictor event/commit timing, load-use interlock, bus protocol, retirement and both arithmetic implementations are unchanged. The external port contract and nret=1 are unchanged. There are no architectural deviations from the proposal.\\n\\nValidation results:\\n\\n- The exact requested Verilator lint command passed without warnings: `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`.\\n- The full bench pytest suite passed all 9 runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU tests. After adding the final stalled stale-prediction cases, the pipeline runner passed again: 39 cocotb tests. The ID runner passed both tests. Existing assertions and the predictor reference model/timing expectations were retained.\\n- ID tests independently decode immediates and exercise 300 B/J/U instruction/PC combinations, with positive/negative offsets, bit-16/bit-31 carries, modulo wraparound, arbitrary unaligned PCs/targets, and two holds per captured instruction while live instruction, prediction, validity and operand inputs change. The original complete reset/flush/stall matrix remains.\\n- Pipeline tests cover immediate AUIPC dependencies through ALU, load/store base, store data, JALR and DIVU forwarding. Directed execution crosses 0x00010000, generates bit-31 and wraparound results, checks links, taken/not-taken branches, negative and positive misaligned direct targets, JALR bit-zero clearing, stale AUIPC/divide/trap predictions, wrong-path stores, simultaneous bus stalls, payload holds, resets and marker writes. Channel-0 order, PC chains, operands and memory effects remain checked.\\n- Local formal status: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, using the unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` with RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl, CORE_NAME=bench and JOBS=8; nret=1 selected wrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg. No counterexample and no RTL fix attempts were needed. An initial setup launch exited before generating checks because the default Python lacked PyYAML; prepending the installed Conda Python to PATH resolved this without changing any gate file.\\n- The unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench` gate passed both ELFs. Separately, the unchanged Python ISS comparison passed all 115 selftest retirements with zero wait states and with both bus stalls. The incoming and candidate selftest records, including retirement cycles, compared exactly equal.\\n- The unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench` gate completed successfully for both the incoming snapshot and candidate, using NRET=1 and BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv. All six routes succeeded. The reproduced incoming result exactly matches the logged winner's seeds, cycles and resources.\\n\\nEvaluation used unchanged protected source copies in the permitted TMPDIR, with isolated output directories. No evaluation, formal, wrapper, benchmark, constraint or other-core source file in the worktree was changed. Existing tooling: Verilator 5.047 devel v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6 (mod); Yosys 0.64+149, 2dc69a757-dirty; nextpnr 0.10-45-g98c18d7f; xPack RISC-V GCC 15.2.0. FPGA device/constraints and seeds 1, 2, 3 are the unchanged gate defaults. Stalled simulation uses --istall --dstall and the harness's default 0xDEADBEEF LFSR seed, with a 50,000,000-cycle ceiling.\\n\\nThe historical scored workload is TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10. The separately required canonical workload is TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10, built by passing the existing Makefile overrides in a scratch source copy. The canonical UART prints `CoreMark Size : 2000` because this is the per-algorithm allocation within the 6000-byte total; its banner identifies the 6k performance run. The protected UART compiler-flags string still says -O2; the actual recorded compiler command uses -O3. Neither string was edited.\\n\\nBoth designs consumed the identical ELF for each comparison. Independently built 2 KB ELFs initially differed only in temporary-object symbol names, with identical loadable segments; the same complete ELF was then supplied to both scored gates. ELF SHA256:\\n\\n- 2 KB: `6c6732c9843736caeac05310d0a699197c9fe6a37f16fbf5e4923c63a154b5f2`\\n- 6 KB: `69953e959fdf71d4837912ff8055518269f93eb0cdaef0e56d34b0ac63caf903`\\n\\nEvery row below is identical for incoming and candidate. Counts are between accepted start/stop marker handshakes, not total elapsed cycles.\\n\\n| Total working set | Bus readiness | Start cycle | Stop cycle | Bracketed cycles | Exact iterations/cycle |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2000 bytes, historical score | zero wait | 11,448 | 3,218,135 | 3,206,687 | 10 / 3,206,687 |\\n| 2000 bytes, historical score | both random stalls | 15,229 | 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 10 / 4,215,262 |\\n| 6000 bytes, separate canonical run | zero wait | 40,153 | 23,391,752 | 23,351,599 | 10 / 23,351,599 |\\n| 6000 bytes, separate canonical run | both random stalls | 53,747 | 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 10 / 30,822,790 |\\n\\nFull emitted RVFI traces were streamed and SHA256-compared between the two implementations for all four rows, including every record's cycle field, operands, memory fields and channel-0 order. All hashes, record counts, final retirements and marker records matched. Each 2 KB trace contained 2,937,037 retirements; each 6 KB trace contained 22,093,920. Each trace had exactly one start and one stop marker retirement with full word write masks. Both implementations completed in bounds. This establishes unchanged observed architectural traces and scheduling for identical instruction streams and ready sequences, beyond merely matching total cycles.\\n\\nThe canonical 6 KB runs, including both bus stalls, produced seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47 and crcfinal=5949, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. The historical 2 KB runs passed the unchanged evaluator's separate e9f5/e714/1fd7/8e3a/fcaf CRC expectations. The evaluator's CRC table was not changed to conflate the workloads.\\n\\n| Metric | Incoming r5s0, reproduced | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Routed Fmax, seed 1 | 163.83 MHz | 150.65 MHz |\\n| Routed Fmax, seed 2 | 155.33 MHz | 145.60 MHz |\\n| Routed Fmax, seed 3 | 168.55 MHz | 145.14 MHz |\\n| Three-seed median | 163.83 MHz | 145.60 MHz |\\n| LUT4 | 6618 | 6554 |\\n| FF | 3198 | 3233 |\\n| Historical scored bracketed cycles | 4,215,262 | 4,215,262 |\\n| Exact calculated historical fitness | 388.659115376458 iter/s | 345.411507042741 iter/s |\\n| Separate 6 KB stalled throughput | 53.152229243362 iter/s | 47.237774387069 iter/s |\\n\\nFitness was calculated as `median_Fmax_MHz * 1,000,000 * 10 / bracketed_cycles`, never from core.yaml or the evaluator's rounded 0.000002 iterations/cycle. Relative fitness change is -11.127388146249%. The two source-level fields add 64 payload bits; after mapping, total FF count rises by 35 and LUT4 count falls by 64.\\n\\nAll three candidate post-route critical paths were inspected and their endpoints mapped through synth.json:\\n\\n| Seed | Source | Endpoint | Logic / routing | Total |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[25], id_ex_w[255] | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[1] D, id_ex_w[51], through prediction recovery and decode | 2.98 / 3.66 ns | 6.64 ns |\\n| 2 | predictor row 0 tag_q[6] | row 0 counter_q[0] CE | 2.79 / 4.08 ns | 6.87 ns |\\n| 3 | predictor row 2 tag_q[12] | row 2 counter_q[0] CE | 2.32 / 4.57 ns | 6.89 ns |\\n\\nThe reproduced incoming critical paths were ctrl.alu_op[4] to EX/MEM alu_result[25].D (seed 1, 2.87 ns logic + 3.24 ns routing), predicted_next_pc[3] to ID/EX valid.RESET (seed 2, 2.77 + 3.67 ns), and ctrl.alu_op[2] to EX/MEM alu_result[25].D (seed 3, 2.78 + 3.15 ns). Thus the source retiming removed the intended EX PC arithmetic, but did not improve the measured overall bottleneck. Recovery/decode and unchanged predictor maintenance dominate the candidate's reported worst paths. The new decode adder and AUIPC result mux are not the reported worst path in these seeds. No claim is made that the historical related-candidate paths diagnosed this winner.\\n\\nThe main concern is the measured timing/fitness regression despite identical scheduling and a small LUT reduction. The proposed five-percent improvement was not observed. Real and ALTOPS arithmetic and the eight-edge divider schedule passed their existing tests and were not modified; formal-deep was not run because no arithmetic implementation changed.\\n\\nScratch evidence directory:\\n\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/pc-retime-r7s1-ob5xe6pv`\\n\\nEvidence includes unit-tests.log, pipeline-tests-final.log, selftest-differential.json, coremark-trace-differential.json, coremark-validation.json, and each design's fpga-gate.log. Candidate formal-gate.log records all 53 passes; candidate cosim-gate.log records the unchanged cosim result. Routed reports and synth.json are under each design's cores/bench/generated/ directory. No helper scripts were added to the worktree.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T19:19:11.800142Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r7s1 (regression, -11.13%): Registering `PC+4`/`PC+imm` in ID/EX and removing AUIPC's ALU-input mux left recovery/decode and predictor `tag_q`-to-`counter_q` enable paths as the routed bottlenecks, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 145.60 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; EX adder removal alone does not guarantee a timing gain.\"}\n22:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Eight-entry target predictor with shorter lookup and less training fanout\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining prediction-accuracy opportunity is small relative to FPGA\\ntiming sensitivity. Saved r6s2 measurements of the current winner show\\n550409 recoveries in 30822790 bracketed cycles for canonical 6 KB CoreMark.\\nThe r5s2 return stack removed canonical-return recoveries but saved only\\n0.188% of those cycles, while frequency loss made fitness worse. The\\ntwo-way predictor, extra metadata-validation stage, thermometer counters,\\nand partial tags also regressed. In particular, partial tags reduced\\nstorage without reducing the number of rows or target-selection inputs,\\nand exposed a slower EX-to-target-capture path.\\n\\nTest a capacity-for-frequency trade instead: halve the direct-mapped\\ntable itself. This reduces the depth of indexed reads and the number of\\nprivate training destinations while retaining exact tags and the proven\\nevent pipeline. The inspected history contains no eight-entry target-table\\nexperiment; the earlier eight-entry structure was a return stack.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining prediction-accuracy opportunity is small relative to FPGA\\ntiming sensitivity. Saved r6s2 measurements of the current winner show\\n550409 recoveries in 30822790 bracketed cycles for canonical 6 KB CoreMark.\\nThe r5s2 return stack removed canonical-return recoveries but saved only\\n0.188% of those cycles, while frequency loss made fitness worse. The\\ntwo-way predictor, extra metadata-validation stage, thermometer counters,\\nand partial tags also regressed. In particular, partial tags reduced\\nstorage without reducing the number of rows or target-selection inputs,\\nand exposed a slower EX-to-target-capture path.\\n\\nTest a capacity-for-frequency trade instead: halve the direct-mapped\\ntable itself. This reduces the depth of indexed reads and the number of\\nprivate training destinations while retaining exact tags and the proven\\nevent pipeline. The inspected history contains no eight-entry target-table\\nexperiment; the earlier eight-entry structure was a return stack.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the current sixteen-entry target predictor with exactly eight\\ndirect-mapped entries. Use PC[4:2] for both fetch and resolution indices\\nand the complete remaining PC[31:5] as the 27-bit tag. Bit 5 moves from\\nthe index into the tag; it is not discarded. Keep full target[31:2]\\nstorage, two-bit saturating counters, and the current fetch equation:\\na valid full-tag hit with counter[1] set selects the stored aligned\\ntarget, otherwise select PC+4. Introduce no hash, associativity, return\\nstack, instruction predecode, additional fetch stage, or update bypass.\\n\\nPreserve the existing private resolution-event registers and their\\ncapture-at-N, commit-at-N+1 schedule, now for eight rows. Each event\\ncommits against its row's current state, so consecutive resolutions\\nwith identical or conflicting tags remain ordered without forwarding.\\nLegal taken branches allocate weakly taken on a miss or increment a hit;\\njumps allocate strongly taken. Both write the resolved aligned target.\\nNot-taken branches decrement matching counters without replacing targets\\nor allocating on a miss. Non-controls and traps invalidate only matching\\nfull tags. Keep the delayed indexed target write atomic with metadata.\\nPending validity refreshes every clock, independent of frontend/backend\\nholds, and reset cancels pending events and invalidates every row.\\n\\nThe intended hardware change is an eight-input rather than sixteen-input\\nindexed read for tags, counters, and targets, with half as many private\\nrow destinations for resolution feedback. Source-level table state falls\\nfrom 944 to 480 bits; event state falls from 499 to 274 bits, a combined\\nreduction of 689 bits before synthesis. The extra tag bit preserves full\\naddress discrimination. Inspect the synthesized mapping and routed paths\\nto establish whether the shorter selection network and lower feedback\\nfanout actually improve timing; storage savings alone are not evidence.\\n\\nThis deliberately changes prediction residency and may increase conflict\\nrecoveries. The current residual-recovery count does not bound the new\\nmisses caused by downsizing. Estimate an unmeasured three-percent net\\nfitness improvement, with low confidence: reduced routing and lookup cost\\nmust outweigh the measured cycle penalty, and the winner already has\\ncompeting EX datapath and recovery critical paths. Do not truncate tags,\\nretime training, or add another predictor mechanism to rescue the result.\\n\\nKeep exact accepted predicted_next_pc capture and EX comparison for every\\nadvancing instruction. Predictions remain advisory; architectural next\\nPC, full addresses, JALR target changes, traps, wrong-path suppression,\\nonce-only training through memory/divide holds, and instruction-stall\\nrecovery priority retain their current semantics. Preserve nret=1,\\nevery external port, decoder legality, M-extension/ALTOPS behavior,\\nall invariants, and all evaluation files.\\n\\nEvaluate the candidate against the current winner using identical ELFs,\\ntools, and ready schedules, the unchanged correctness gates, and median\\nFmax from all three FPGA seeds. Measure bracketed cycles and recoveries\\nby control-flow class, plus LUT4/FF use and critical-path endpoints.\\nCompute fitness as iterations divided by bracketed cycles times median\\nFmax in Hz, retaining full precision. A cycle increase of fraction c\\nrequires a frequency increase greater than c to improve the product.\\nHistorical artifacts identify 388.66 as the scored 2 KB result; report\\nthat comparison separately from canonical 6 KB throughput. For 6 KB,\\nrequire d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, both timing markers, bounded accesses, and\\nthe validation UART literal. Do not change either workload or evaluator.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the fixed eight-entry organization described above. Resize\\nall table views, row generate bounds, and target arrays consistently;\\nuse three-bit fetch/resolve/pending-target indices and 27-bit stored\\nand pending tags. Update row-enable constants and full-tag comparisons.\\nRetain binary counter policy, row-private delayed events, target-write\\nsynchronization, reset cancellation, PC priority, and all public ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update the independent public-output predictor reference and table\\nsweeps to eight rows and the new index/tag partition. Add directed\\nPCs separated by 0x20 that now share an index, toggling each tag bit\\nfrom 5 through 31, and eight-versus-nine active-control working sets.\\nVerify exact expected replacement, hysteresis, matching-only decrement\\nand invalidation, target retention, changing targets, and every target\\nbit. Preserve pre-edge lookup visibility, back-to-back same-row event\\nordering, concurrent capture/commit, stalled draining, and reset tests.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Adjust only the independent predictor model and expectations that\\nexplicitly assume sixteen rows. Add conflicting control-flow PCs\\nseparated by 0x20 with observable wrong-path stores, alternating JALR\\ndestinations, and taken/not-taken transitions. Exercise load-use,\\ninstruction stalls, memory holds, blocking divide, and reset around\\nthose conflicts. Retain all architectural assertions for exact RVFI\\norder and PC chaining, register/link values, legal and trapping\\ninstructions, and absence of wrong-path retirement or memory effects.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the fixed eight-entry organization described above. Resize\\nall table views, row generate bounds, and target arrays consistently;\\nuse three-bit fetch/resolve/pending-target indices and 27-bit stored\\nand pending tags. Update row-enable constants and full-tag comparisons.\\nRetain binary counter policy, row-private delayed events, target-write\\nsynchronization, reset cancellation, PC priority, and all public ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Update the independent public-output predictor reference and table\\nsweeps to eight rows and the new index/tag partition. Add directed\\nPCs separated by 0x20 that now share an index, toggling each tag bit\\nfrom 5 through 31, and eight-versus-nine active-control working sets.\\nVerify exact expected replacement, hysteresis, matching-only decrement\\nand invalidation, target retention, changing targets, and every target\\nbit. Preserve pre-edge lookup visibility, back-to-back same-row event\\nordering, concurrent capture/commit, stalled draining, and reset tests.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Adjust only the independent predictor model and expectations that\\nexplicitly assume sixteen rows. Add conflicting control-flow PCs\\nseparated by 0x20 with observable wrong-path stores, alternating JALR\\ndestinations, and taken/not-taken transitions. Exercise load-use,\\ninstruction stalls, memory holds, blocking divide, and reset around\\nthose conflicts. Retain all architectural assertions for exact RVFI\\norder and PC chaining, register/link values, legal and trapping\\ninstructions, and absence of wrong-path retirement or memory effects.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 355.23, \"delta_pct\": -8.6, \"vs_baseline\": 25.6, \"fmax_mhz\": 150.94, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4249105, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6158, \"ff\": 2732, \"seeds\": [159.13, 150.94, 150.49], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T19:24:26.151973Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r7s2 (regression, -8.60%): Halving the full-tag target predictor to eight `PC[4:2]` rows shortened mapped lookup and halved training fanout, but extra taken-branch/JAL recoveries raised scored 2 KB CoreMark cycles 0.80% while EX/recovery and row-counter paths limited median Fmax to 150.94 MHz (from 163.83 MHz); reducing predictor capacity must improve the routed bottleneck enough to repay added conflicts.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
