{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,270p' cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Target-table behavior independent of instruction decoding and pipeline timing.\"\"\"\nimport random\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.triggers import Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\n\n\nclass _PredictorReference:\n    \"\"\"Architectural table policy; observe only the public IF outputs.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.pc = 0\n        self.rows = [dict(valid=False, tag=0, target=0, counter=0)\n                     for _ in range(16)]\n        self.pending = None\n        self.edges = 0\n\n    def predict(self):\n        row = self.rows[(self.pc >> 2) & 15]\n        if row[\"valid\"] and row[\"tag\"] == self.pc >> 6 and row[\"counter\"] >= 2:\n            return row[\"target\"]\n        return (self.pc + 4) & 0xFFFFFFFF\n\n    def check(self, dut, inputs, phase):\n        payload = int(dut.out.value)\n        killed = inputs[\"flush\"] or inputs[\"redirect\"]\n        expected = (self.pc, self.predict(),\n                    0x13 if killed else inputs[\"imem_data\"], int(not killed))\n        observed = (payload >> 65, (payload >> 33) & 0xFFFFFFFF,\n                    (payload >> 1) & 0xFFFFFFFF, payload & 1)\n        context = f\"edge {self.edges} {phase}: inputs={inputs}\"\n        assert observed == expected, f\"{context}: {observed} != {expected}\"\n        assert int(dut.imem_addr.value) == self.pc, context\n\n    def advance(self, inputs):\n        # Choose the accepted next PC before applying any table update.\n        prediction = self.predict()\n        if inputs[\"reset\"]:\n            self.pc = 0\n            self.pending = None\n            for row in self.rows:\n                row[\"valid\"] = False\n        else:\n            if inputs[\"redirect\"]:\n                self.pc = inputs[\"redirect_target\"]\n            elif not (inputs[\"stall\"] or inputs[\"flush\"]):\n                self.pc = prediction\n\n            # Apply the preceding accepted event to CURRENT committed\n            # state, then enqueue this edge's event without a hit/counter\n            # snapshot. Prediction above always uses pre-commit state.\n            if self.pending is not None:\n                event = self.pending\n                pc = event[\"resolve_pc\"]\n                row = self.rows[(pc >> 2) & 15]\n                hit = row[\"valid\"] and row[\"tag\"] == pc >> 6\n                jump = event[\"resolve_jump\"]\n                branch = event[\"resolve_branch\"]\n                if not event[\"resolve_legal\"] or not (branch or jump):\n                    if hit:\n                        row[\"valid\"] = False\n                elif jump or event[\"resolve_taken\"]:\n                    row[\"counter\"] = (3 if jump else\n                                      min(3, row[\"counter\"] + 1) if hit else 2)\n                    row.update(valid=True, tag=pc >> 6,\n                               target=event[\"resolve_target\"] & 0xFFFFFFFC)\n                elif hit:\n                    row[\"counter\"] = max(0, row[\"counter\"] - 1)\n            self.pending = dict(inputs) if inputs[\"resolve_valid\"] else None\n        self.edges += 1\n\n\nasync def _cycle(dut, model=None, **overrides):\n    values = dict(reset=0, stall=1, flush=0, redirect=0, redirect_target=0,\n                  resolve_valid=0, resolve_pc=0, resolve_target=0,\n                  resolve_branch=0, resolve_jump=0, resolve_taken=0,\n                  resolve_legal=1, imem_data=0x00000013)\n    values.update(overrides)\n    dut.clock.value = 0\n    for name, value in values.items():\n        getattr(dut, name).value = value\n    await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n    # IF/ID layout: pc, predicted_next_pc, instr, valid. Check the actual\n    # exported selection, not a reconstructed value from table internals.\n    before = (int(dut.out.value) >> 33) & 0xFFFFFFFF\n    if model is not None:\n        model.check(dut, values, \"before\")\n        model.advance(values)\n    dut.clock.value = 1\n    await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n    after = (int(dut.out.value) >> 33) & 0xFFFFFFFF\n    if model is not None:\n        model.check(dut, values, \"after\")\n    return before, int(dut.imem_addr.value), after\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def lookup_has_no_update_bypass(dut):\n    await _cycle(dut, reset=1)\n    # Acceptance must not publish metadata or targets, even with a held PC.\n    before, pc, after = await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1,\n                                    resolve_jump=1, resolve_target=0x80000200)\n    assert before == after == 4 and pc == 0\n    # A fetch accepted on the commit edge still takes the old prediction.\n    before, pc, _ = await _cycle(dut, stall=0)\n    assert before == 4 and pc == 4\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, redirect=1, redirect_target=0)\n    assert predicted == 0x80000200\n    await _cycle(dut, reset=1)\n    before, pc, _ = await _cycle(dut, stall=0, resolve_valid=1,\n                                resolve_jump=1, resolve_target=0x80000200)\n    assert before == 4 and pc == 4  # acceptance sees the pre-update miss\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, redirect=1, redirect_target=0)\n    assert predicted == 0x80000200\n    # A bogus feedback payload with valid=0 cannot change the prediction.\n    for insn in (0xFFFFFFFF, 0x0000006F, 0x00000073):\n        before, pc, after = await _cycle(dut, resolve_legal=0, imem_data=insn)\n        assert before == after == 0x80000200 and pc == 0\n    # Updating a hit remains invisible on acceptance, including when the\n    # old prediction advances the PC on that edge.\n    before, pc, _ = await _cycle(dut, stall=0, resolve_valid=1,\n                                resolve_jump=1, resolve_target=0x400)\n    assert before == 0x80000200 and pc == 0x80000200\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, redirect=1, redirect_target=0)\n    assert predicted == 0x400\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1,\n                                   resolve_jump=1, resolve_target=0x800)\n    assert predicted == 0x400\n    before, pc, _ = await _cycle(dut, stall=0)\n    assert before == pc == 0x400  # hit on the commit edge also uses old target\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, redirect=1, redirect_target=0)\n    assert predicted == 0x800\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def branch_counter_allocation_target_retention_and_reset(dut):\n    await _cycle(dut, reset=1)\n    # Taken branch allocates weakly taken; one not-taken crosses the threshold.\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1, resolve_branch=1,\n                                   resolve_taken=1, resolve_target=0x100)\n    assert predicted == 4\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1, resolve_branch=1,\n                                   resolve_target=0x200)\n    assert predicted == 0x100\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut)\n    assert predicted == 4\n    # Saturate at zero, then increment twice. The not-taken target must\n    # never overwrite the retained taken target.\n    for _ in range(3):\n        await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1, resolve_branch=1, resolve_target=0x200)\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1, resolve_branch=1,\n                                   resolve_taken=1, resolve_target=0x100)\n    assert predicted == 4\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1, resolve_branch=1,\n                                   resolve_taken=1, resolve_target=0x100)\n    assert predicted == 4\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut)\n    assert predicted == 0x100\n    # Jumps become strongly taken. A subsequent not-taken keeps prediction\n    # taken and must still use the jump's last resolved target.\n    await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1, resolve_jump=1, resolve_target=0x300)\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1, resolve_branch=1,\n                                   resolve_target=0x200)\n    assert predicted == 0x300\n    _, _, predicted = await _cycle(dut)\n    assert predicted == 0x300\n\n    # Fill every index with nonzero payloads, then invalidate all on reset.\n    for index in range(16):\n        await _cycle(dut, resolve_valid=1, resolve_pc=index * 4,\n                     resolve_jump=1, resolve_target=0x80001000 + index * 4)\n    await _cycle(dut, reset=1, resolve_valid=1, resolve_jump=1, resolve_target=0x100)\n    for index in range(16):\n        _, pc, predicted = await _cycle(dut, redirect=1, redirect_target=index * 4)\n        assert pc == index * 4 and predicted == pc + 4\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def continuous_alias_events_commit_against_current_row(dut):\n    \"\"\"Every edge commits one event and accepts another without snapshots.\"\"\"\n    await _cycle(dut, reset=1)\n    model = _PredictorReference()\n    for index in range(16):\n        pc = 0x34567800 + index * 4\n        alias = pc ^ 0x80000040\n        events = [\n            (pc, dict(resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1)),\n            (pc, dict(resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1)),\n            (pc, dict(resolve_branch=1)),\n            (pc, dict(resolve_branch=1)),\n            (pc, dict(resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1)),\n            (alias, dict(resolve_branch=1)),\n            (alias, dict()),\n            (alias, dict(resolve_jump=1)),\n            (pc, dict(resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1)),\n            (alias, dict(resolve_branch=1)),\n            (pc, dict()),\n            (pc, dict(resolve_branch=1)),\n            (pc, dict(resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1)),\n            (alias, dict(resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1)),\n            (pc, dict(resolve_legal=0, resolve_jump=1)),\n            (alias, dict(resolve_legal=0, resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1)),\n            (alias, dict(resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1)),\n        ]\n        for step, (event_pc, controls) in enumerate(events):\n            await _cycle(dut, model, resolve_valid=1, resolve_pc=event_pc,\n                         resolve_target=(0xC0000080 ^ (step << 18) ^ (index << 2)),\n                         redirect=1, redirect_target=pc if step & 1 else alias,\n                         stall=step & 1, flush=(step % 3 == 0), **controls)\n        # Drain while fetch is stalled, then leave validity low for several\n        # edges: a sticky pending bit would replay the last taken update.\n        for _ in range(4):\n            await _cycle(dut, model)\n        for victim in (pc, alias):\n            await _cycle(dut, model, redirect=1, redirect_target=victim)\n            await _cycle(dut, model, resolve_valid=1, resolve_pc=victim,\n                         resolve_branch=1, resolve_target=0xFFFFFFFC)\n            await _cycle(dut, model)\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def predictor_matches_reference_before_and_after_every_edge(dut):\n    await _cycle(dut, reset=1)\n    model = _PredictorReference()\n\n    async def cycle(**inputs):\n        return await _cycle(dut, model=model, **inputs)\n\n    async def fetch(pc):\n        await cycle(redirect=1, redirect_target=pc)\n        await cycle(imem_data=0x89ABCDEF)\n\n    async def train(pc, **inputs):\n        await cycle(resolve_valid=1, resolve_pc=pc, **inputs)\n\n    # Populate all rows, including distinct high tag and target bits. Later\n    # sweeps expose accidental writes to every unselected row, via fetch.\n    pcs = [0x80001000 + index * 0x01010040 + index * 4 for index in range(16)]\n    aliases = [pc ^ 0x80000000 for pc in pcs]\n    for index, pc in enumerate(pcs):\n        await train(pc, resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1,\n                    resolve_target=0xC0001000 + index * 0x00100004)\n    for pc in pcs + aliases:\n        await fetch(pc)\n\n    for index, (pc, alias) in enumerate(zip(pcs, aliases)):\n        await fetch(pc)\n        # With fetch held, matching branches cross both thresholds and\n        # saturate repeatedly. Not-taken targets must never replace data.\n        for taken in [1] * 5 + [0] * 6 + [1] * 5 + [0, 1, 0, 1]:\n            await train(pc, resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=taken,\n                        resolve_target=(0xA5550000 + index * 4) if taken else 0xDEADBEE0)\n\n        # Same-index misses cannot decrement, invalidate, or allocate over\n        # the resident tag, even with nonsensical invalid control payloads.\n        for inputs in (dict(resolve_branch=1), dict(),\n                       dict(resolve_legal=0, resolve_jump=1, resolve_taken=1)):\n            await train(alias, resolve_target=0xFFFFFFFC, **inputs)\n        for inputs in (dict(resolve_branch=1, resolve_taken=1),\n                       dict(resolve_jump=1), dict(resolve_legal=0)):\n            await cycle(resolve_pc=pc, resolve_target=0x40000000, **inputs)\n        await fetch(alias)\n        await fetch(pc)\n\n        # Same-PC replacement and same-index alias allocation must advance\n        # an accepted fetch using the prediction visible BEFORE the edge.\n        await train(pc, stall=0, resolve_jump=1, resolve_target=0xF0000000 + index * 4)\n        await fetch(pc)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows = [json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor r in rows:\n    print(json.dumps({k:r.get(k) for k in ['id','category','title','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lesson']},ensure_ascii=False))\nfor wanted in ['hyp-20260905-002-r6s1','hyp-20260905-001-r6s0','hyp-20260905-001-r5s0']:\n    for r in rows:\n        if r.get('id') == wanted:\n            h = r.get('hypothesis',{})\n            print('\\\\nDETAIL',wanted)\n            print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n            print('IMPLEMENTATION NOTES\\\\n'+r.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows = [json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor r in rows:\n    print(json.dumps({k:r.get(k) for k in ['id','category','title','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','lesson']},ensure_ascii=False))\nfor wanted in ['hyp-20260905-002-r6s1','hyp-20260905-001-r6s0','hyp-20260905-001-r5s0']:\n    for r in rows:\n        if r.get('id') == wanted:\n            h = r.get('hypothesis',{})\n            print('\\\\nDETAIL',wanted)\n            print(h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n            print('IMPLEMENTATION NOTES\\\\n'+r.get('implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-609cf36\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"lesson\": null}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r1s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Balance register-file reads with shared one-hot address decoding\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 284.34, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.71, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"lesson\": null}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r1s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Single pending-store slot with direct zero-wait completion\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 300.35, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.89, \"cycles\": 4457730, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 (regression, +6.20%): With `mem_wait` blocking younger loads/stores through the pending store's drain cycle, CoreMark cycles fell only 0.75%, so the fitness gain came mainly from higher median Fmax (127.03 to 133.89 MHz).\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r1s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Small tagged target predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 319.45, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.68, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r1s2 (improvement, +12.95%): A 16-entry tagged target predictor removes the combinational IF/ID frontend's one-slot EX redirect bubble on correctly predicted taken branches/jumps, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles 6.14% while median Fmax rose 6.02%, at about 11% LUT/FF growth.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r2s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Register one-hot ALU operation selects before execute\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 306.93, \"fmax_mhz\": 129.4, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r2s0 (regression, -3.92%): Registered one-hot ALU selects with balanced OR selection shifted the timing bottleneck to the `predicted_next_pc` mismatch recovery path feeding ID/EX, lowering median Fmax from 134.68 to 129.40 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles despite 377 fewer LUT4s.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r2s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Split execution into a fast ALU and an eight-cycle divider\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 320.28, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.03, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.26%): An eight-cycle DIV/REM engine cut LUT4 use 44.85% without changing CoreMark cycles because the timed region executes no DIV/REM, but predictor tag lookup and counter-write control remained the critical path, leaving the fitness gain small relative to FPGA seed variation.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r2s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Two-way target prediction at unchanged 16-entry capacity\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 296.07, \"fmax_mhz\": 124.94, \"cycles\": 4220017, \"lesson\": null}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r3s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Localize predictor training to remove indexed feedback muxes\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 203.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 85.77, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"lesson\": null}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r3s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Pipeline resolution feedback with registered predictor-row reads\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 340.22, \"fmax_mhz\": 143.41, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 (regression, +6.23%): Registering raw predictor-row reads before tag comparison/counter writes, with same-index post-update forwarding for back-to-back resolutions, removed the maintenance lookup from all three critical paths and raised median Fmax from 135.03 to 143.41 MHz with essentially unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles, exposing hazard/divide-wait control as the next timing limit.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r3s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Row-local predictor metadata training to remove the indexed feedback path\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 350.46, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.75, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r3s2 (improvement, +9.42%): Constant-index predictor metadata registers with row-local tag comparison and counter saturation remove the shared tag-read-to-counter-WRE feedback path without an extra update stage, raising median Fmax from 135.03 to 147.75 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles at +11.85% LUT4 and +14.97% FF cost, with resolve-PC fanout and local tag matching becoming critical.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r4s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Balance predictor training tag comparisons at LUT reduction boundaries\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 299.22, \"fmax_mhz\": 126.15, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r4s0 (regression, -14.62%): Preserving three LUT levels for resolve-tag equality with `keep` shifted the critical path to operand forwarding through the EX unsigned branch comparator into predictor counter data, cutting median Fmax from 147.75 to 126.15 MHz despite fewer LUTs and unchanged CoreMark cycles; equality depth alone does not bound the training path.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r4s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Pipeline row-addressed resolution events before local predictor updates\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 371.55, \"fmax_mhz\": 156.62, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r4s1 (improvement, +6.02%): Pipelining resolution events into private predictor-row registers cuts live EX-to-metadata-enable paths and preserves consecutive-alias ordering without forwarding by committing against current row state, raising median Fmax from 147.75 to 156.62 MHz with essentially unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles at +559 FFs and +17 LUT4s.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r4s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Bank-local registered training commands for the target predictor\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 346.34, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.99, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r4s2 (regression, -1.18%): Bank-local registered predictor tags driving four metadata rows shortened tag-to-valid-enable logic delay by 1.00 ns but added 1.08 ns of routing, lowering median Fmax from 147.75 to 145.99 MHz despite 675 fewer CoreMark cycles.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r5s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Remove wide ID/EX payload registers from bubble-control fanout\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 388.66, \"fmax_mhz\": 163.83, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r5s0 (improvement, +4.61%): Restricting ID/EX flushes to `ctrl`/`valid` and payload holds to `dmem_stall || div_wait` removes recovery/load-use fanout from wide payload reset/enable pins, but bubbles must still clear every control bit because forwarding and memory logic are not fully gated by `valid`.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r5s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Separate ID/EX validity from the wide recovery payload\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 261.48, \"fmax_mhz\": 110.22, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r5s1 (regression, -29.62%): Raw IF transport plus separate ID/EX validity and output `ctrl` masking removed recovery-to-payload fanout but exposed longer PC-to-EX/MEM ALU paths in all three routed seeds, cutting median Fmax from 156.62 to 110.22 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles; eliminating a reset bottleneck can worsen overall timing through remapping.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r5s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Eight-entry return stack with direct return recognition\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 356.76, \"fmax_mhz\": 150.13, \"cycles\": 4208138, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r5s2 (regression, -3.98%): An eight-entry shift-stack RAS eliminated measured canonical-return recoveries but saved only 0.17% of scored 2 KB CoreMark cycles, too little to offset a 4.14% median Fmax loss with target-table metadata training still critical; return accuracy gains must be weighed against total-cycle savings and routing cost.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r6s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Replace local saturating-counter arithmetic with thermometer state\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 354.4, \"fmax_mhz\": 149.39, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": null}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r6s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Separate predictor tag validation from ordered metadata commit\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 230.66, \"fmax_mhz\": 97.2, \"cycles\": 4214035, \"lesson\": null}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r6s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Eight-bit partial tags for the target predictor\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 340.12, \"fmax_mhz\": 143.37, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r6s2 (regression, -12.49%): Eight-bit `PC[13:6]` predictor tags saved 538 LUT4s and 603 FFs with unchanged 2 KB/6 KB CoreMark cycles and recoveries, but full-width target generation into `pending_target_q.D` became critical in all three seeds, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 143.37 MHz; reducing tag width can worsen timing outside the tag comparator.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r7s0\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"title\": \"Predecode the multiply-high result class before execute\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 350.46, \"fmax_mhz\": 147.73, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r7s0 (regression, -9.83%): A single registered `mul_high_sel` removed the measured five-bit EX class detector yet lowered median Fmax from 163.83 to 147.73 MHz with identical stalled CoreMark cycles because EX/MEM `rd` forwarding through the JALR target adder into `pending_target_q` became critical in all three seeds; narrow predecode still requires checking timing outside the ALU.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r7s1\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"title\": \"Move PC-relative address generation across the ID/EX boundary\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.41, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.6, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r7s1 (regression, -11.13%): Registering `PC+4`/`PC+imm` in ID/EX and removing AUIPC's ALU-input mux left recovery/decode and predictor `tag_q`-to-`counter_q` enable paths as the routed bottlenecks, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 145.60 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; EX adder removal alone does not guarantee a timing gain.\"}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r7s2\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"title\": \"Eight-entry target predictor with shorter lookup and less training fanout\", \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 355.23, \"fmax_mhz\": 150.94, \"cycles\": 4249105, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r7s2 (regression, -8.60%): Halving the full-tag target predictor to eight `PC[4:2]` rows shortened mapped lookup and halved training fanout, but extra taken-branch/JAL recoveries raised scored 2 KB CoreMark cycles 0.80% while EX/recovery and row-counter paths limited median Fmax to 150.94 MHz (from 163.83 MHz); reducing predictor capacity must improve the routed bottleneck enough to repay added conflicts.\"}\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-002-r6s1\nAdd one register boundary between row-local tag validation and predictor\nmetadata commit in rtl/if_stage.sv. This is one structural retiming of\nthe existing training transaction. Retain the sixteen direct-mapped rows,\nPC[5:2] index, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, two-bit counter\npolicy, allocation rules, and committed-state-only fetch lookup.\n\nUse three edges for an accepted event E. At edge N, the existing private\nrow capture records E's tag and command. At edge N+1, transfer that event\ninto a new commit register and capture one hit bit computed against the\nrow's logical tag/valid state AFTER the older event committing on that\nsame edge. At edge N+2, apply E using its registered hit, command, and\ntag, together with the row's then-current two-bit counter. The existing\ndesign commits at N+1, so this adds exactly one training-visibility cycle.\nCapture, validate, and commit must overlap at one event per clock,\nincluding an uninterrupted stream of resolutions to the same row.\n\nResolve the validation-stage read-after-write hazard locally. Let C be\nthe older event currently in this row's commit register and P the event\nin its existing pending register. Compute two full-width comparisons in\nparallel: table_tag == P.tag and C.tag == P.tag. If C.valid and C.command\nis JUMP or TAKEN, P's effective hit is the second comparison because C\nallocates or refreshes a valid row with C.tag on the validation edge.\nOtherwise, if C.valid, C.command is INVALIDATE, and C.hit is true, P's\neffective hit is false because C clears the row. In all other cases use\ntable_valid && (table_tag == P.tag). Register this effective hit with P.\nSelection between comparator results is narrow; do not mux a 26-bit\neffective tag ahead of one equality comparator. All forwarding is private\nto the row and concerns tag/valid state only. No live resolve input or\nindexed metadata read may enter the validation or commit datapath.\n\nAt commit, use the registered hit without re-comparing tags. JUMP writes\nvalid=1, the event tag, target, and counter=11. TAKEN writes valid=1,\nthe event tag and target, and counter=10 on a registered miss or a\nsaturating increment of the current counter on a registered hit.\nNOT_TAKEN decrements the current counter with saturation only on a\nregistered hit and retains tag, valid, and target. INVALIDATE clears\nvalid only on a registered hit. Preserve the current field-specific\nupdate policy. Do not snapshot or forward the counter into validation:\nin-order commits ensure the current counter already includes all older\nevents. This avoids a new counter-update feedback bypass. After the\npipeline drains, every table field must equal sequential application of\nthe accepted resolution stream, even for alternating full-tag aliases.\n\nAdd a matching second delay register to the single indexed target-write\npath, carrying target-valid, index, and target. The target and its metadata\nmust become visible on the same N+2 edge, including consecutive writes\nto the same index with different tags or JALR destinations. Do not let a\nnewer target become visible under an older committed tag. The estimated\nextra state is 16*(1 valid + 26 tag + 2 command + 1 hit) plus\n(1 valid + 4 index + 30 target), or 515 source-level bits before mapping.\n\nRefresh both event-stage validity bits every clock independently of IF\nstalls, memory holds, divide waits, and younger redirects. An accepted\nolder event commits once even if instruction execution subsequently\nstalls. Keep EX resolve_valid qualification unchanged so held instructions\ncannot enqueue repeatedly. Reset clears every table-valid and both stages\nof event/target validity, suppresses commit, and prevents pre-reset work\nfrom repopulating the table. Invalid pipeline payload is never consulted\nwithout its corresponding validity qualification.\n\nKeep the architectural instruction pipeline unchanged: no fetch bypass\nfrom pending events, no training backpressure, no delayed EX redirect,\nand no change to the exact predicted_next_pc captured with an instruction.\nRecovery still compares that prediction with the resolved architectural\ndestination and has the existing one-younger-slot penalty. Warm loops\nmust retain one-instruction-per-cycle throughput; cold or rapidly changing\nbranches can incur another miss because training becomes visible later.\nPreserve IF's instruction-to-NOP behavior, the r5s0 split of ID/EX control\nand payload updates, forwarding retention, load-use and bus interlocks,\nand the real/ALTOPS multiplier and eight-edge divider schedules.\n\nPredict an unmeasured four-percent fitness gain from higher median Fmax\nwith nearly unchanged CoreMark cycles. The added alias comparator,\nroughly 515 extra state bits, placement changes, and delayed training\ncan erase that gain; inspect all three post-route paths to determine\nwhether tag comparison is actually separated from counter write control.\nDo not add keep constraints, predictor-policy changes, or frontend\nredesigns to this experiment. Success is a higher exact\niterations/bracketed-cycles times three-seed median Fmax than the incoming\nwinner under the identical workload, toolchain, and evaluation settings.\n\nRun the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and FPGA gates.\nPreserve nret=1 and all top-level ports, strict channel-0 retirement order,\ndecoder legality and traps, memory bounds, stable stalled requests, and\nexactly-once MMIO/marker accesses. Existing history distinguishes the\nscored 2 KB run underlying 388.66 from the required canonical 6 KB run;\nreport like-for-like results separately without altering the evaluator.\nAlso validate the unchanged 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration workload with both\nbus stalls, CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, Correct operation validated., and timing\nstrictly between start/stop marker handshakes. All hard invariants and\nprotected files remain intact.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION NOTES\n\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-001-r6s0\nRe-encode only each row's two-bit saturating direction counter as a\nthree-bit thermometer register q[2:0]. Logical counts 0, 1, 2, and 3\nmap respectively to 000, 001, 011, and 111. Fetch uses q[1] directly\nfor taken prediction, preserving the current threshold without a\nbinary conversion or extra lookup logic. A taken hit increments by\nshifting in one: {q[1:0], 1'b1}. A not-taken hit decrements by shifting\nin zero: {1'b0, q[2:1]}. These operations saturate intrinsically.\nA jump sets 111; a taken miss allocates 011. A not-taken miss,\ninvalidation, and absence of a pending event retain the counter.\n\nExpress counter next state as parallel Boolean data terms, followed\nby an unconditional clocked assignment, rather than arithmetic or\nnested conditional writes. Specifically, let E be pending_valid_q\nAND !reset and H be the existing valid-qualified full-tag row_hit.\nDefine J = E AND command_is_JUMP, A = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND !H,\nU = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND H, D = E AND command_is_NOT_TAKEN AND H,\nand K = !(J OR A OR U OR D). Use bitwise equations:\n  next_q[2] = J | (U & q[1]) | (K & q[2]);\n  next_q[1] = J | A | (U & q[0]) | (D & q[2]) | (K & q[1]);\n  next_q[0] = J | A | U | (D & q[1]) | (K & q[0]).\nDecode the pending command locally and retain a single sequential\nowner for each counter. Allocation terms must produce known constants\neven when the old, invalid counter payload is uninitialized. Counter\npayloads need no reset: reset suppresses updates and the existing\nvalid reset masks them until a complete legal allocation.\n\nThe intended mapped circuit has no saturation detection or binary\narithmetic and no tag-dependent counter CE, SET, or RESET input.\nHit qualification and self-retention are represented in the data\nequations. Inspect the mapped netlist to verify this property: an\nequivalent rewrite that synthesis folds back into hit-qualified\ncounter enables does not establish the intended optimization. Do not\nintroduce device primitives, synthesis-only behavior, or blanket keep\nattributes to force a result. The source-level cost is just sixteen\nadditional counter bits; actual mapped LUT/FF cost must be measured.\n\nPreserve the sixteen rows, full 26-bit tags, exact tag comparisons,\nvalid/tag update logic, separate indexed target store, all private\npending events, and the existing capture/commit edges. Commit against\ncurrent row state, including consecutive events and different tags\naliasing one row. Keep matching-only invalidation and not-taken updates,\ntarget retention, reset cancellation, and once-only pending-event drain\nthrough stalls and redirects. Fetch still observes committed state\nwith the same pre-edge semantics and no update bypass. This is a\ncounter-encoding and logic-mapping micro-optimization, with identical\nprediction policy, capacity, training latency, and instruction timing.\n\nEstimate an unmeasured four-percent fitness improvement through Fmax\nalone. For identical ELF and ready schedules, require identical public\nIF predictions, accepted memory transactions, valid RVFI retirements\nwith their cycle positions, and marker-bracketed cycles. Confidence is\nmedium: full-tag equality remains, valid-bit update or EX paths may\nbecome limiting, and added data gating may worsen routing or be\nremapped into the original enable structure.\n\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed\nFPGA gates. Compare against the current winner with the same tools,\nELF, and bus-ready schedules; report critical paths and LUT4/FF cost.\nCompute fitness from full-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles times\nmedian Fmax. Historical notes identify 388.66's scored workload as\n2 KB; keep that comparison separate from the requested canonical 6 KB\nrun, verifying d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, memory bounds, both timing markers,\nand the validation UART literal without altering evaluation files.\nPreserve nret=1, external IO, RVFI order and traps, decoder legality,\nmemory handshakes, all M-extension/ALTOPS logic, and every invariant.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION NOTES\n\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-001-r5s0\nSplit ID/EX sequential updates into a narrow control/valid group and\na payload group. On a flush, clear the entire ctrl field and valid\nexactly as the existing zero bundle does. Do not clear, mask, or hold\npayload registers merely because of flush. Keep their deterministic\nreset-to-zero behavior, but otherwise capture pc, predicted_next_pc,\nboth operand values, immediate, register addresses, rd, and instruction\nwhenever there is no actual backend hold. Each field must have exactly\none sequential driver; do not mix whole-struct and partial assignments\nacross separate always_ff blocks.\n\nMake stall_id mean only dmem_stall || div_wait. Remove load_use_hazard\nfrom this ID/EX hold signal while preserving stall_if, flush_if,\nflush_id, stall_ex_mem, and hold_mem_wb behavior. This is a redundant\nhold removal: whenever a load-use hazard occurs without a backend\nhold, the existing flush_id already forces an ID/EX bubble. On that\nedge the payload may capture the still-unaccepted younger instruction,\nbut ctrl=0 and valid=0 make it inert, and the held fetch is accepted\nnormally on the following eligible edge. When a memory or divide wait\nis active, both control and payload must remain held exactly as before.\nPreserve reset priority and flush priority for the control/valid group.\n\nClearing valid alone is insufficient: forwarding and memory control\nlogic consult ctrl fields even when valid is zero. Continue clearing\nall control bits on every flush, particularly reg_write, mem_read,\nmem_write, is_branch, and is_jump. Retained or newly captured bubble\npayload must never launch a divide, redirect, train the predictor,\nforward as a writer, access memory, or retire. Leave EX/MEM full-bundle\ndivide bubbles and MEM/WB forwarding-data retention unchanged. All\nlive instructions retain their exact operands, instruction metadata,\naccepted prediction, trap behavior, and RVFI fields through holds.\n\nThis single micro-optimization removes recovery and load-use logic\nfrom the wide payload registers' direct reset and enable controls.\nExisting upstream instruction-to-NOP selection may still affect data\ninputs; it need not be redesigned. Add no stage, state bit, execution\nshortcut, new prediction behavior, or architectural latency. Preserve\nnret=1, all external ports, all decoder legality rules, and every hard\ninvariant and evaluation check.\n\nExpect an unmeasured three-percent fitness improvement from higher\nmedian Fmax, with identical bracketed cycles for the same binary and\nbus-ready schedule. Confidence is medium: the other winning seeds are\nlimited by local predictor tag-to-counter-enable paths at 6.36 and\n6.42 ns, so they may cap the benefit; synthesis may also reconstruct\nunwanted control dependencies or alter placement adversely. Inspect\nthe mapped payload reset/enable cones and routed critical paths to\nestablish whether the intended control isolation survives synthesis.\n\nRun the existing unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA gates.\nCompare accepted bus transactions and valid RVFI retirements, including\ntheir cycle positions, against the current winner under identical\ninputs; inactive payload bit patterns need not match. Use full-precision\niterations/bracketed-cycles times median Fmax and report LUT/FF costs.\nThe saved 371.55 result is documented as a 2 KB scored run; the separate\ncanonical 6 KB winner run takes 30,822,790 bracketed cycles. Keep those\nconfigurations distinct and unchanged, and verify the required 6 KB\nCRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, memory bounds, markers, and validation UART literal.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION NOTES\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
