{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md schemas/hypothesis.schema.json'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 (regression, +6.20%): With `mem_wait` blocking younger loads/stores through the pending store's drain cycle, CoreMark cycles fell only 0.75%, so the fitness gain came mainly from higher median Fmax (127.03 to 133.89 MHz).\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r1s2 (improvement, +12.95%): A 16-entry tagged target predictor removes the combinational IF/ID frontend's one-slot EX redirect bubble on correctly predicted taken branches/jumps, cutting stalled CoreMark cycles 6.14% while median Fmax rose 6.02%, at about 11% LUT/FF growth.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r2s0 (regression, -3.92%): Registered one-hot ALU selects with balanced OR selection shifted the timing bottleneck to the `predicted_next_pc` mismatch recovery path feeding ID/EX, lowering median Fmax from 134.68 to 129.40 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles despite 377 fewer LUT4s.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.26%): An eight-cycle DIV/REM engine cut LUT4 use 44.85% without changing CoreMark cycles because the timed region executes no DIV/REM, but predictor tag lookup and counter-write control remained the critical path, leaving the fitness gain small relative to FPGA seed variation.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r2s2 (regression, -7.32%): Two-way lookup at fixed 16-entry capacity added tag-match and way-selection logic with essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles, while median Fmax fell from 134.68 to 124.90 MHz, so predictor associativity needs a timing budget even when storage barely grows.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 (regression, +6.23%): Registering raw predictor-row reads before tag comparison/counter writes, with same-index post-update forwarding for back-to-back resolutions, removed the maintenance lookup from all three critical paths and raised median Fmax from 135.03 to 143.41 MHz with essentially unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles, exposing hazard/divide-wait control as the next timing limit.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r3s2 (improvement, +9.42%): Constant-index predictor metadata registers with row-local tag comparison and counter saturation remove the shared tag-read-to-counter-WRE feedback path without an extra update stage, raising median Fmax from 135.03 to 147.75 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles at +11.85% LUT4 and +14.97% FF cost, with resolve-PC fanout and local tag matching becoming critical.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r4s0 (regression, -14.62%): Preserving three LUT levels for resolve-tag equality with `keep` shifted the critical path to operand forwarding through the EX unsigned branch comparator into predictor counter data, cutting median Fmax from 147.75 to 126.15 MHz despite fewer LUTs and unchanged CoreMark cycles; equality depth alone does not bound the training path.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r4s1 (improvement, +6.02%): Pipelining resolution events into private predictor-row registers cuts live EX-to-metadata-enable paths and preserves consecutive-alias ordering without forwarding by committing against current row state, raising median Fmax from 147.75 to 156.62 MHz with essentially unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles at +559 FFs and +17 LUT4s.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r4s2 (regression, -1.18%): Bank-local registered predictor tags driving four metadata rows shortened tag-to-valid-enable logic delay by 1.00 ns but added 1.08 ns of routing, lowering median Fmax from 147.75 to 145.99 MHz despite 675 fewer CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r5s0 (improvement, +4.61%): Restricting ID/EX flushes to `ctrl`/`valid` and payload holds to `dmem_stall || div_wait` removes recovery/load-use fanout from wide payload reset/enable pins, but bubbles must still clear every control bit because forwarding and memory logic are not fully gated by `valid`.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r5s1 (regression, -29.62%): Raw IF transport plus separate ID/EX validity and output `ctrl` masking removed recovery-to-payload fanout but exposed longer PC-to-EX/MEM ALU paths in all three routed seeds, cutting median Fmax from 156.62 to 110.22 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles; eliminating a reset bottleneck can worsen overall timing through remapping.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r5s2 (regression, -3.98%): An eight-entry shift-stack RAS eliminated measured canonical-return recoveries but saved only 0.17% of scored 2 KB CoreMark cycles, too little to offset a 4.14% median Fmax loss with target-table metadata training still critical; return accuracy gains must be weighed against total-cycle savings and routing cost.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r6s2 (regression, -12.49%): Eight-bit `PC[13:6]` predictor tags saved 538 LUT4s and 603 FFs with unchanged 2 KB/6 KB CoreMark cycles and recoveries, but full-width target generation into `pending_target_q.D` became critical in all three seeds, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 143.37 MHz; reducing tag width can worsen timing outside the tag comparator.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r7s0 (regression, -9.83%): A single registered `mul_high_sel` removed the measured five-bit EX class detector yet lowered median Fmax from 163.83 to 147.73 MHz with identical stalled CoreMark cycles because EX/MEM `rd` forwarding through the JALR target adder into `pending_target_q` became critical in all three seeds; narrow predecode still requires checking timing outside the ALU.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r7s1 (regression, -11.13%): Registering `PC+4`/`PC+imm` in ID/EX and removing AUIPC's ALU-input mux left recovery/decode and predictor `tag_q`-to-`counter_q` enable paths as the routed bottlenecks, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 145.60 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; EX adder removal alone does not guarantee a timing gain.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r7s2 (regression, -8.60%): Halving the full-tag target predictor to eight `PC[4:2]` rows shortened mapped lookup and halved training fanout, but extra taken-branch/JAL recoveries raised scored 2 KB CoreMark cycles 0.80% while EX/recovery and row-counter paths limited median Fmax to 150.94 MHz (from 163.83 MHz); reducing predictor capacity must improve the routed bottleneck enough to repay added conflicts.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r8s0 (regression, -11.14%): Selecting completed JALR sums after three independent adders survived synthesis but added 201 LUT4s and 64 carry cells with unchanged CoreMark cycles, while ALU decode/result selection limited all three seeds and median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 145.58 MHz; moving forwarding selection past arithmetic must account for resource cost and remaining EX timing limits.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r8s1 (regression, -6.12%): Retiming four independent dependency matches into ID/EX removed EX destination-address comparisons without changing CoreMark cycles, yet predictor valid/tag-to-counter-enable paths limited all three routed seeds and median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 153.80 MHz; measure the incoming winner's bottleneck before targeting forwarding paths observed in related candidates.\n- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r8s2 (regression, -33.64%): Untagged `PC[5:2]` direction training removed tag-to-counter dependencies but increased alias recoveries, while one-hot-to-binary `pending_command_q` remapping accompanied an EX-to-command capture bottleneck that cut median Fmax from 163.83 to 108.97 MHz; simplifying counter updates requires checking synthesized event encoding and routed capture timing.\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
