/checks/rvfi_reg_check.sv:41:\t\t\t\tif (register_written && register_index == rvfi_rs1_addr[`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX*5 +: 5])\nformal/riscv-formal/checks/rvfi_reg_check.sv:42:\t\t\t\t\tassert(register_shadow == rvfi_rs1_rdata[`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX*`RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN +: `RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN]);\nformal/riscv-formal/checks/rvfi_reg_check.sv:43:\t\t\t\tif (register_written && register_index == rvfi_rs2_addr[`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX*5 +: 5])\nformal/riscv-formal/checks/rvfi_reg_check.sv:44:\t\t\t\t\tassert(register_shadow == rvfi_rs2_rdata[`RISCV_FORMAL_CHANNEL_IDX*`RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN +: `RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN]);\nformal/riscv-formal/insns/insn_addi.v:7:  input  [`RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN   - 1 : 0] rvfi_rs1_rdata,\nformal/riscv-formal/insns/insn_addi.v:8:  input  [`RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN   - 1 : 0] rvfi_rs2_rdata,\nformal/riscv-formal/insns/insn_addi.v:17:  output [                       4 : 0] spec_rs1_addr,\nformal/riscv-formal/insns/insn_addi.v:18:  output [                       4 : 0] spec_rs2_addr,\nformal/riscv-formal/insns/insn_addi.v:31:  wire [4:0] insn_rs1    = rvfi_insn[19:15];\nformal/riscv-formal/insns/insn_addi.v:44:  wire [`RISCV_FORMAL_XLEN-1:0] result = rvfi_rs1_rdata + insn_imm;\nformal/riscv-formal/insns/insn_addi.v:46:  assign spec_rs1_addr = insn_rs1;\nformal/riscv-formal/insns/insn_addi.v:52:  assign spec_rs2_addr = 0;\ntest/cosim/reference.py:109:                wrd=0, wval=0, rs1=0, rs2=0,\ntest/cosim/reference.py:110:                rs1_rdata_pre=0, rs2_rdata_pre=0,\ntest/cosim/reference.py:129:            'rs1': rs1, 'rs1_rdata': rs1_rdata_pre,\ntest/cosim/reference.py:130:            'rs2': rs2, 'rs2_rdata': rs2_rdata_pre,\ntest/cosim/reference.py:143:        rs1 = (instr >> 15) & 0x1F\ntest/cosim/reference.py:144:        rs2 = (instr >> 20) & 0x1F\ntest/cosim/reference.py:156:        # Snapshot rs1/rs2 BEFORE writing rd — RVFI requires reporting the\ntest/cosim/reference.py:158:        rs1_rdata_pre = self.regs[rs1]\ntest/cosim/reference.py:159:        rs2_rdata_pre = self.regs[rs2]\ntest/cosim/reference.py:164:            rs1=rs1, rs2=rs2,\ntest/cosim/reference.py:165:            rs1_rdata_pre=rs1_rdata_pre, rs2_rdata_pre=rs2_rdata_pre,\ntest/cosim/reference.py:169:            a, b = r[rs1], r[rs2]\ntest/cosim/reference.py:199:            a = r[rs1]\ntest/cosim/reference.py:220:            addr = (r[rs1] + imm_i) & MASK\ntest/cosim/reference.py:242:            addr = (r[rs1] + imm_s) & MASK\ntest/cosim/reference.py:247:                self.wb(addr, r[rs2])\ntest/cosim/reference.py:248:                mem_wmask, mem_wdata = 1, r[rs2] & 0xFF\ntest/cosim/reference.py:250:                self.whw(addr, r[rs2])\ntest/cosim/reference.py:251:                mem_wmask, mem_wdata = 3, r[rs2] & 0xFFFF\ntest/cosim/reference.py:253:                self.ww(addr, r[rs2])\ntest/cosim/reference.py:254:                mem_wmask, mem_wdata = 15, r[rs2]\ntest/cosim/reference.py:262:            a, b = r[rs1], r[rs2]\ntest/cosim/reference.py:283:            target = (r[rs1] + imm_i) & ~1 & MASK\n# CLAUDE.md — non-negotiable invariants and don't-touch list\n\nEvery item below was a real bug in the prior Chisel iteration. Each must be\nenforced by a check that fails loudly when violated. **Do not weaken these\nchecks** to make a hypothesis pass.\n\n## Hard invariants\n\n| # | Invariant                                                                                                  | Enforced by                                  |\n|---|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| 1 | Top module `core` exposes an RVFI port set selected by `cores/<target>/core.yaml`'s `nret` field (default 2). **nret=1**: only `io_rvfi_*_0` ports exist; the single retirement always lands on channel 0. **nret=2**: both `io_rvfi_*_0` and `io_rvfi_*_1` exist; channel 0 carries the older of two simultaneous retirements, channel 1 the younger; single-retire cycles MUST place the retirement on channel 0 with `io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0`; all channel-1 ports MUST be driven (no X/Z, tie unused fields to `'0`). | cocotb smoke test, `riscv-formal` wrapper (`wrapper.sv` for nret=2, `wrapper_si.sv` for nret=1) |\n| 2 | `rvfi_trap = 1` iff the retiring instruction is illegal per RV32IM (decoder default = illegal)             | `riscv-formal ill` check                     |\n| 3 | EBREAK is the *only* SYSTEM (opcode `0x73`) instruction the core treats as valid; ECALL / CSR / MRET trap  | dedicated decoder unit tests                 |\n| 4 | `rvfi_order` strictly monotonic +1 per retirement across both channels combined (no gaps, no duplicates). When both channels retire in the same cycle, channel-0 order = N and channel-1 order = N+1.                                       | `riscv-formal unique` check                  |\n| 5 | CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic instruction stream                                           | `riscv-formal liveness` check                |\n| 5b | M-extension arithmetic produces RV32M-correct bit results                                                | cocotb `test_alu.py` + `make formal-deep`    |\n| 6 | All memory accesses bounded to `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` plus UART/markers `[0x10000000, 0x10000200)`     | sim emits `oob:true`, eval treats as failure |\n| 7 | CoreMark CRCs match canonical 6 KB perf run: `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`       | `validate_coremark_uart` in fpga eval        |\n| 8 | CoreMark timing brackets `start_time` / `stop_time` markers — total elapsed cycles do NOT count            | MMIO writes at `0x10000100` / `0x10000104`   |\n| 9 | \"Correct operation validated.\" literal must appear in CoreMark UART output                                 | `validate_coremark_uart`                     |\n|10 | Decoder defaults `isIllegal = 1`; a bit is only cleared inside a validated opcode/funct match              | decoder unit tests with reserved encodings   |\n\n## Don't-touch list (the orchestrator never modifies these)\n\nThese directories are part of the eval contract and are *never* modified by\nhypothesis-implementation agents. The hypothesis is \"what RTL changes would\nimprove fitness\", not \"what eval relaxations would let this RTL pass\".\n\n- `tools/` — orchestrator, worktree manager, eval gates.\n- `schemas/` — hypothesis and eval-result JSON schemas.\n- `formal/wrapper.sv`, `formal/wrapper_si.sv`, `formal/checks.cfg`,\n  `formal/checks_si.cfg`, `formal/run_all.sh` — they define the\n  correctness contract (dual-channel + single-issue variants).\n- `formal/riscv-formal/` — vendored upstream submodule.\n- `bench/programs/` — selftest, crt0, link.ld, CoreMark sources, portme.\n- `fpga/core_bench.sv`, `fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, `fpga/scripts/*`,\n  `fpga/constraints/*` — they define the FPGA fitness contract\n  (dual-channel + single-issue variants).\n- `test/cosim/main.cpp`, `test/cosim/reference.py`, `test/cosim/run_cosim.py`\n  — Verilator harness + Python ISS = the cosim contract.\n- This file (`CLAUDE.md`), `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `README.md`, `setup.sh`,\n  `Makefile`.\n\n## What hypotheses MAY change\n\nEverything under `cores/<TARGET>/rtl/` and the cocotb unit tests under\n`cores/<TARGET>/test/test_*.py`. The agent's `implementation_notes.md`\nlives at `cores/<TARGET>/implementation_notes.md` (gitignored). The\norchestrator may also update `cores/<TARGET>/core.yaml` (current:\nsection only).\n\nThe only top-level invariant on `rtl/core.sv` is that it exposes a port\nnamed `core` whose IO matches the RVFI wrapper's expectations: the\nimem/dmem port set (`io_imemAddr`, `io_imemData`, `io_imemReady`,\n`io_dmemAddr`, `io_dmemRData`, `io_dmemWData`, `io_dmemWEn`,\n`io_dmemREn`, `io_dmemReady`), `clock`, `reset`, and the RVFI port\nset determined by `core.yaml`'s `nret` field — only `io_rvfi_*_0`\nfields for `nret: 1`; both `io_rvfi_*_0` and `io_rvfi_*_1` for\n`nret: 2` (default).\n\nA hypothesis may:\n- Merge stages into a single file or split a module across many.\n- Rename or delete files inside `rtl/`.\n- Add new modules (branch predictors, caches, reservation stations…).\n- Change pipeline depth (add or remove stages).\n- Restructure the pipeline topology end to end.\n- Rewrite any `rtl/` module from scratch.\n\nIt must not:\n- Change `core`'s top-level IO shape relative to the `nret` value declared in `core.yaml` — specifically: the imem/dmem port set, and the RVFI port set (only `_0` fields for `nret: 1`; both `_0` and `_1` for `nret: 2` with channel-0-older convention and the single-retire-on-channel-0 rule).\n- Change `core.yaml`'s `nret` value mid-hypothesis. nret picks the formal/FPGA wrappers used by eval; flipping it is a contract-level change, not a per-hypothesis decision.\n- Weaken any invariant in the table above.\n- Modify any path in the don't-touch list.\n- Edit any other core's directory (cores/<other>/...). Each loop is\n  scoped to a single TARGET.\n\n## Working notes\n\nThis file is the contract. `docs/bootstrap-prompt.md` is the long-form\nplan that produced it.\n\n### RVFI contract: nret=1 vs nret=2\n\nCores declare their retirement width in `cores/<TARGET>/core.yaml`\nunder the top-level `nret` field. Two values are supported; the\ndefault is 2 for backward compat with cores authored before this\nfield existed.\n\n- `nret: 1` — single-issue. Top module exposes only `io_rvfi_*_0`\n  ports (no `_1`). Orchestrator wires `formal/wrapper_si.sv` +\n  `formal/checks_si.cfg` for formal and `fpga/core_bench_si.sv` for\n  FPGA Fmax. No `_1` tie-offs to write; no vacuous-pass machinery\n  involved. This is the recommended shape for any in-order or\n  single-retire pipeline.\n- `nret: 2` — dual-issue (or single-issue cores that opt into the\n  wider contract). Top module exposes both `io_rvfi_*_0` and\n  `io_rvfi_*_1` with the channel-0-older convention.\n  `formal/wrapper.sv` + `formal/checks.cfg` and `fpga/core_bench.sv`\n  apply. Single-issue cores declared as `nret: 2` must still tie off\n  channel 1 (`io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0` plus all other `_1` fields driven\n  to `'0`) — about 21 `assign io_rvfi_*_1 = '0;` lines.\n\nTriple-issue or wider would be a future contract bump (NRET=K), not a\nper-hypothesis decision.\n\nContract-side files keyed off `nret`:\n- nret=1: `formal/wrapper_si.sv`, `formal/checks_si.cfg`,\n  `fpga/core_bench_si.sv`.\n- nret=2: `formal/wrapper.sv`, `formal/checks.cfg`,\n  `fpga/core_bench.sv`.\n- shared: `formal/run_all.sh`, `fpga/scripts/synth.tcl`,\n  `test/cosim/main.cpp` (per-channel drain that gracefully no-ops\n  on unused channels).\n\nVacuous-pass on channel 1 (`nret: 2` only): `riscv-formal`'s\nper-channel BMC checks assume `rvfi_valid_<ch>=1` to test their\nproperty. When a single-issue hypothesis declared as `nret: 2`\nhardwires `io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0`, that assumption becomes\nunsatisfiable and SBY exits `Status: PREUNSAT` (rc=16) — technically\na vacuous pass over the empty set of ch1-valid traces.\n`formal/run_all.sh` counts `*_ch1` PREUNSAT as pass, so such\nhypotheses pass formal naturally and true dual-issue hypotheses\n(where ch1 is sometimes valid) get the regular `DONE (PASS` outcome\non the same checks. Caveat: a hypothesis that *claims* dual-issue\nbut whose channel 1 silently never retires would also vacuous-pass\nthese checks; the FPGA-fitness IPC and the cover statement (which\nfires on either channel retiring) catch the fully-stuck case.\nCores declared `nret: 1` sidestep this machinery entirely —\nchecks_si.cfg generates no `_ch1` tasks at all.\n\n## Caveat: `make formal` runs ALTOPS-mode by default\n\n`formal/checks.cfg` defines `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`, which substitutes\nthe M-extension `MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU` operations\nwith simpler XOR-of-add/sub formulas (riscv-formal docs §7.6). The\nDUT's `alu.sv` mirrors the same substitution under the same `\\`ifdef`.\n\nThis means the per-iteration formal gate proves **M-ext bypassing,\noperand routing, stall/forwarding behavior, and trap discipline** — but\nit does NOT prove that the actual hardware multiplier and divider\nproduce RV32M-correct bit results. M-ext arithmetic correctness is\ncovered separately by:\n\n  1. `test/test_alu.py` (30+ vectors per M-ext op, exact bit results)\n  2. cosim against `test/cosim/reference.py` (each retired MUL/DIV/REM\n     diff'd field-by-field with the Python ISS)\n  3. `make formal-deep` (formal without ALTOPS — slow but the only path\n     that proves real arithmetic via SMT). Run periodically, not in\n     the orchestrator loop.\n\nA hypothesis that touches the multiplier/divider must keep both the\nALTOPS branch and the real branch in `alu.sv` consistent. The cocotb\nsuite is the fast guard against arithmetic regressions.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20260905-001-r10s0','hyp-20260905-001-r5s0','hyp-20260906-001-r12s0','hyp-20260905-001-r6s0'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if x['id'] in ids:\n        print('\\\\n'+x['id'])\n        print(json.dumps({k:x[k] for k in ('title','category','motivation','hypothesis','changes','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','implementation_notes','error') if k in x},indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nids={'hyp-20260905-001-r10s0','hyp-20260905-001-r5s0','hyp-20260906-001-r12s0','hyp-20260905-001-r6s0'}\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if x['id'] in ids:\n        print('\\\\n'+x['id'])\n        print(json.dumps({k:x[k] for k in ('title','category','motivation','hypothesis','changes','outcome','fitness','fmax_mhz','cycles','implementation_notes','error') if k in x},indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nhyp-20260905-001-r5s0\n{\n  \"title\": \"Remove wide ID/EX payload registers from bubble-control fanout\",\n  \"category\": \"micro_opt\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, scores 371.55 iter/s at\\n156.62 MHz median Fmax. Its median-seed timing report identifies a\\n6.39 ns path from predicted_next_pc[0], through prediction recovery\\nand hazard/divide qualification, to an ID/EX operand register RESET.\\nThe final control net alone contributes 1.95 ns of routing. In the\\ncurrent RTL, reset || flush clears the entire id_ex_t payload, and\\nstall_id also includes the load-use condition even though that case\\ninserts a bubble. Recovery therefore controls many data-register\\nreset/enable pins whose values cannot matter for a killed instruction.\\n\\nPrior micro_opt attempts rewrote register-file selection, ALU operation\\nselection, and predictor training/tag comparisons; none isolated ID/EX\\nbubble controls from payload storage. LESSONS.md records that the ALU\\nrewrite exposed this recovery path, while forced predictor comparison\\nboundaries regressed timing. The latest winner has already registered\\npredictor training events. A local rewrite of bubble insertion now\\ntargets a measured control-distribution bottleneck without changing\\npredictor policy, arithmetic, or architectural scheduling.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, scores 371.55 iter/s at\\n156.62 MHz median Fmax. Its median-seed timing report identifies a\\n6.39 ns path from predicted_next_pc[0], through prediction recovery\\nand hazard/divide qualification, to an ID/EX operand register RESET.\\nThe final control net alone contributes 1.95 ns of routing. In the\\ncurrent RTL, reset || flush clears the entire id_ex_t payload, and\\nstall_id also includes the load-use condition even though that case\\ninserts a bubble. Recovery therefore controls many data-register\\nreset/enable pins whose values cannot matter for a killed instruction.\\n\\nPrior micro_opt attempts rewrote register-file selection, ALU operation\\nselection, and predictor training/tag comparisons; none isolated ID/EX\\nbubble controls from payload storage. LESSONS.md records that the ALU\\nrewrite exposed this recovery path, while forced predictor comparison\\nboundaries regressed timing. The latest winner has already registered\\npredictor training events. A local rewrite of bubble insertion now\\ntargets a measured control-distribution bottleneck without changing\\npredictor policy, arithmetic, or architectural scheduling.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Split ID/EX sequential updates into a narrow control/valid group and\\na payload group. On a flush, clear the entire ctrl field and valid\\nexactly as the existing zero bundle does. Do not clear, mask, or hold\\npayload registers merely because of flush. Keep their deterministic\\nreset-to-zero behavior, but otherwise capture pc, predicted_next_pc,\\nboth operand values, immediate, register addresses, rd, and instruction\\nwhenever there is no actual backend hold. Each field must have exactly\\none sequential driver; do not mix whole-struct and partial assignments\\nacross separate always_ff blocks.\\n\\nMake stall_id mean only dmem_stall || div_wait. Remove load_use_hazard\\nfrom this ID/EX hold signal while preserving stall_if, flush_if,\\nflush_id, stall_ex_mem, and hold_mem_wb behavior. This is a redundant\\nhold removal: whenever a load-use hazard occurs without a backend\\nhold, the existing flush_id already forces an ID/EX bubble. On that\\nedge the payload may capture the still-unaccepted younger instruction,\\nbut ctrl=0 and valid=0 make it inert, and the held fetch is accepted\\nnormally on the following eligible edge. When a memory or divide wait\\nis active, both control and payload must remain held exactly as before.\\nPreserve reset priority and flush priority for the control/valid group.\\n\\nClearing valid alone is insufficient: forwarding and memory control\\nlogic consult ctrl fields even when valid is zero. Continue clearing\\nall control bits on every flush, particularly reg_write, mem_read,\\nmem_write, is_branch, and is_jump. Retained or newly captured bubble\\npayload must never launch a divide, redirect, train the predictor,\\nforward as a writer, access memory, or retire. Leave EX/MEM full-bundle\\ndivide bubbles and MEM/WB forwarding-data retention unchanged. All\\nlive instructions retain their exact operands, instruction metadata,\\naccepted prediction, trap behavior, and RVFI fields through holds.\\n\\nThis single micro-optimization removes recovery and load-use logic\\nfrom the wide payload registers' direct reset and enable controls.\\nExisting upstream instruction-to-NOP selection may still affect data\\ninputs; it need not be redesigned. Add no stage, state bit, execution\\nshortcut, new prediction behavior, or architectural latency. Preserve\\nnret=1, all external ports, all decoder legality rules, and every hard\\ninvariant and evaluation check.\\n\\nExpect an unmeasured three-percent fitness improvement from higher\\nmedian Fmax, with identical bracketed cycles for the same binary and\\nbus-ready schedule. Confidence is medium: the other winning seeds are\\nlimited by local predictor tag-to-counter-enable paths at 6.36 and\\n6.42 ns, so they may cap the benefit; synthesis may also reconstruct\\nunwanted control dependencies or alter placement adversely. Inspect\\nthe mapped payload reset/enable cones and routed critical paths to\\nestablish whether the intended control isolation survives synthesis.\\n\\nRun the existing unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA gates.\\nCompare accepted bus transactions and valid RVFI retirements, including\\ntheir cycle positions, against the current winner under identical\\ninputs; inactive payload bit patterns need not match. Use full-precision\\niterations/bracketed-cycles times median Fmax and report LUT/FF costs.\\nThe saved 371.55 result is documented as a 2 KB scored run; the separate\\ncanonical 6 KB winner run takes 30,822,790 bracketed cycles. Keep those\\nconfigurations distinct and unchanged, and verify the required 6 KB\\nCRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, memory bounds, markers, and validation UART literal.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 3,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Partition ID/EX control/valid and payload updates. Keep reset-to-zero\\nfor every field. Flush clears only ctrl and valid; payload captures\\nwhenever stall is low, independently of flush. Preserve every field\\nduring actual memory/divide holds and give each field one sequential\\nowner. Document that invalid bubble payload is inert because its\\ncontrols are cleared, while live instruction metadata is unchanged.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Set stall_id to dmem_stall || div_wait, removing the redundant\\nload-use hold from ID/EX payload enables. Keep the existing load-use\\nfetch stall and flush_id bubble equation, including backend-hold\\npriority, and leave every other output equation unchanged. Update\\ncomments to describe control-only bubble insertion and actual holds.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n        \"description\": \"Add directed coverage for load-use bubbles and misprediction flushes\\nwith nonzero payload, including wrong-path stores and DIV/REM, and\\nfor overlapping instruction stalls, prolonged data stalls, divide\\nwaits/completion, and reset. Verify exact surviving RVFI operands,\\npredictions, traps, order, cycle timing, and accepted memory effects;\\nkilled payload must produce no retirement, training, divide launch,\\nor memory request. Check complete ID/EX stability during backend\\nholds and correct fresh payload on the next accepted instruction.\\nRetain all existing assertions, including EX/MEM zero bubbles during\\ndivide waits and MEM/WB forwarding retention across memory stalls.\\n\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"changes\": [\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Partition ID/EX control/valid and payload updates. Keep reset-to-zero\\nfor every field. Flush clears only ctrl and valid; payload captures\\nwhenever stall is low, independently of flush. Preserve every field\\nduring actual memory/divide holds and give each field one sequential\\nowner. Document that invalid bubble payload is inert because its\\ncontrols are cleared, while live instruction metadata is unchanged.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\n      \"description\": \"Set stall_id to dmem_stall || div_wait, removing the redundant\\nload-use hold from ID/EX payload enables. Keep the existing load-use\\nfetch stall and flush_id bubble equation, including backend-hold\\npriority, and leave every other output equation unchanged. Update\\ncomments to describe control-only bubble insertion and actual holds.\\n\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Add directed coverage for load-use bubbles and misprediction flushes\\nwith nonzero payload, including wrong-path stores and DIV/REM, and\\nfor overlapping instruction stalls, prolonged data stalls, divide\\nwaits/completion, and reset. Verify exact surviving RVFI operands,\\npredictions, traps, order, cycle timing, and accepted memory effects;\\nkilled payload must produce no retirement, training, divide launch,\\nor memory request. Check complete ID/EX stability during backend\\nholds and correct fresh payload on the next accepted instruction.\\nRetain all existing assertions, including EX/MEM zero bubbles during\\ndivide waits and MEM/WB forwarding retention across memory stalls.\\n\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"outcome\": \"improvement\",\n  \"fitness\": 388.66,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 163.83,\n  \"cycles\": 4215262,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"error\": null\n}\n\nhyp-20260905-001-r6s0\n{\n  \"title\": \"Replace local saturating-counter arithmetic with thermometer state\",\n  \"category\": \"micro_opt\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 388.66 fitness at 163.83 MHz after isolating\\nID/EX payload controls. The bench history identifies another specific\\ntiming opportunity: r4s1's seeds 1 and 3 ended at local predictor-counter\\nclock enables, reached from committed or pending tags in 6.36/6.42 ns.\\nRow-private event registers already isolate these updates from live EX.\\nHowever, each row still uses binary increment/decrement, saturation\\nselection, and conditional counter writes after its full-tag comparison.\\n\\nPrior micro_opt attempts at register-file selection and ALU predecode\\ndid not produce accepted gains, and r4s0's preserved equality tree lost\\n14.62 percent by exposing a different path. This hypothesis instead\\nsimplifies the small state-update circuit following the existing local\\ncomparison. Those timing reports describe r4s1, not a verified critical\\npath of r5s0; whether counter updates still limit the winner must be\\nestablished in the matched implementation evaluation.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The current winner reaches 388.66 fitness at 163.83 MHz after isolating\\nID/EX payload controls. The bench history identifies another specific\\ntiming opportunity: r4s1's seeds 1 and 3 ended at local predictor-counter\\nclock enables, reached from committed or pending tags in 6.36/6.42 ns.\\nRow-private event registers already isolate these updates from live EX.\\nHowever, each row still uses binary increment/decrement, saturation\\nselection, and conditional counter writes after its full-tag comparison.\\n\\nPrior micro_opt attempts at register-file selection and ALU predecode\\ndid not produce accepted gains, and r4s0's preserved equality tree lost\\n14.62 percent by exposing a different path. This hypothesis instead\\nsimplifies the small state-update circuit following the existing local\\ncomparison. Those timing reports describe r4s1, not a verified critical\\npath of r5s0; whether counter updates still limit the winner must be\\nestablished in the matched implementation evaluation.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Re-encode only each row's two-bit saturating direction counter as a\\nthree-bit thermometer register q[2:0]. Logical counts 0, 1, 2, and 3\\nmap respectively to 000, 001, 011, and 111. Fetch uses q[1] directly\\nfor taken prediction, preserving the current threshold without a\\nbinary conversion or extra lookup logic. A taken hit increments by\\nshifting in one: {q[1:0], 1'b1}. A not-taken hit decrements by shifting\\nin zero: {1'b0, q[2:1]}. These operations saturate intrinsically.\\nA jump sets 111; a taken miss allocates 011. A not-taken miss,\\ninvalidation, and absence of a pending event retain the counter.\\n\\nExpress counter next state as parallel Boolean data terms, followed\\nby an unconditional clocked assignment, rather than arithmetic or\\nnested conditional writes. Specifically, let E be pending_valid_q\\nAND !reset and H be the existing valid-qualified full-tag row_hit.\\nDefine J = E AND command_is_JUMP, A = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND !H,\\nU = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND H, D = E AND command_is_NOT_TAKEN AND H,\\nand K = !(J OR A OR U OR D). Use bitwise equations:\\n  next_q[2] = J | (U & q[1]) | (K & q[2]);\\n  next_q[1] = J | A | (U & q[0]) | (D & q[2]) | (K & q[1]);\\n  next_q[0] = J | A | U | (D & q[1]) | (K & q[0]).\\nDecode the pending command locally and retain a single sequential\\nowner for each counter. Allocation terms must produce known constants\\neven when the old, invalid counter payload is uninitialized. Counter\\npayloads need no reset: reset suppresses updates and the existing\\nvalid reset masks them until a complete legal allocation.\\n\\nThe intended mapped circuit has no saturation detection or binary\\narithmetic and no tag-dependent counter CE, SET, or RESET input.\\nHit qualification and self-retention are represented in the data\\nequations. Inspect the mapped netlist to verify this property: an\\nequivalent rewrite that synthesis folds back into hit-qualified\\ncounter enables does not establish the intended optimization. Do not\\nintroduce device primitives, synthesis-only behavior, or blanket keep\\nattributes to force a result. The source-level cost is just sixteen\\nadditional counter bits; actual mapped LUT/FF cost must be measured.\\n\\nPreserve the sixteen rows, full 26-bit tags, exact tag comparisons,\\nvalid/tag update logic, separate indexed target store, all private\\npending events, and the existing capture/commit edges. Commit against\\ncurrent row state, including consecutive events and different tags\\naliasing one row. Keep matching-only invalidation and not-taken updates,\\ntarget retention, reset cancellation, and once-only pending-event drain\\nthrough stalls and redirects. Fetch still observes committed state\\nwith the same pre-edge semantics and no update bypass. This is a\\ncounter-encoding and logic-mapping micro-optimization, with identical\\nprediction policy, capacity, training latency, and instruction timing.\\n\\nEstimate an unmeasured four-percent fitness improvement through Fmax\\nalone. For identical ELF and ready schedules, require identical public\\nIF predictions, accepted memory transactions, valid RVFI retirements\\nwith their cycle positions, and marker-bracketed cycles. Confidence is\\nmedium: full-tag equality remains, valid-bit update or EX paths may\\nbecome limiting, and added data gating may worsen routing or be\\nremapped into the original enable structure.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed\\nFPGA gates. Compare against the current winner with the same tools,\\nELF, and bus-ready schedules; report critical paths and LUT4/FF cost.\\nCompute fitness from full-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles times\\nmedian Fmax. Historical notes identify 388.66's scored workload as\\n2 KB; keep that comparison separate from the requested canonical 6 KB\\nrun, verifying d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, memory bounds, both timing markers,\\nand the validation UART literal without altering evaluation files.\\nPreserve nret=1, external IO, RVFI order and traps, decoder legality,\\nmemory handshakes, all M-extension/ALTOPS logic, and every invariant.\\n\",\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"fitness_delta_pct\": 4,\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    \"changes\": [\n      {\n        \"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\n        \"description\": \"Replace each binary counter with the specified three-bit thermometer\\nregister and parallel next-data equations. Feed its middle bit\\ndirectly into the existing indexed fetch direction selection.\\nPreserve row-private event capture, full-tag matching, valid/tag\\nupdates, indexed target memory, and all reset/commit semantics.\\nAudit synthesized counter data/control cones for removal of binary\\nsaturation logic and tag-dependent counter enables; leave all other\\npipeline and predictor mechanisms unchanged.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\",\n        \"description\": \"Retain the independent logical integer-counter reference and its\\nexisting before/after-edge assertions. Extend