{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files --hidden -g AGENTS.md -g '*timing*' -g '*pnr*' -g '*report*' -g '*r5s0*' -g '*r13s*' -g '\"'!formal/riscv-formal/**'\"' -g '\"'!.git/**'\"' -g '\"'!bench/**'\"'\ncat CLAUDE.md\nsed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/test/test_alu.py\nsed -n '1,100p' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\"","aggregated_output":"fpga/scripts/nextpnr_run.sh\ntools/bench/report.py\ntools/bench/test_report.py\n# CLAUDE.md — non-negotiable invariants and don't-touch list\n\nEvery item below was a real bug in the prior Chisel iteration. Each must be\nenforced by a check that fails loudly when violated. **Do not weaken these\nchecks** to make a hypothesis pass.\n\n## Hard invariants\n\n| # | Invariant                                                                                                  | Enforced by                                  |\n|---|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| 1 | Top module `core` exposes an RVFI port set selected by `cores/<target>/core.yaml`'s `nret` field (default 2). **nret=1**: only `io_rvfi_*_0` ports exist; the single retirement always lands on channel 0. **nret=2**: both `io_rvfi_*_0` and `io_rvfi_*_1` exist; channel 0 carries the older of two simultaneous retirements, channel 1 the younger; single-retire cycles MUST place the retirement on channel 0 with `io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0`; all channel-1 ports MUST be driven (no X/Z, tie unused fields to `'0`). | cocotb smoke test, `riscv-formal` wrapper (`wrapper.sv` for nret=2, `wrapper_si.sv` for nret=1) |\n| 2 | `rvfi_trap = 1` iff the retiring instruction is illegal per RV32IM (decoder default = illegal)             | `riscv-formal ill` check                     |\n| 3 | EBREAK is the *only* SYSTEM (opcode `0x73`) instruction the core treats as valid; ECALL / CSR / MRET trap  | dedicated decoder unit tests                 |\n| 4 | `rvfi_order` strictly monotonic +1 per retirement across both channels combined (no gaps, no duplicates). When both channels retire in the same cycle, channel-0 order = N and channel-1 order = N+1.                                       | `riscv-formal unique` check                  |\n| 5 | CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic instruction stream                                           | `riscv-formal liveness` check                |\n| 5b | M-extension arithmetic produces RV32M-correct bit results                                                | cocotb `test_alu.py` + `make formal-deep`    |\n| 6 | All memory accesses bounded to `[0x00000000, 0x00100000)` plus UART/markers `[0x10000000, 0x10000200)`     | sim emits `oob:true`, eval treats as failure |\n| 7 | CoreMark CRCs match canonical 6 KB perf run: `crclist=0xd4b0`, `crcmatrix=0xbe52`, `crcstate=0x5e47`       | `validate_coremark_uart` in fpga eval        |\n| 8 | CoreMark timing brackets `start_time` / `stop_time` markers — total elapsed cycles do NOT count            | MMIO writes at `0x10000100` / `0x10000104`   |\n| 9 | \"Correct operation validated.\" literal must appear in CoreMark UART output                                 | `validate_coremark_uart`                     |\n|10 | Decoder defaults `isIllegal = 1`; a bit is only cleared inside a validated opcode/funct match              | decoder unit tests with reserved encodings   |\n\n## Don't-touch list (the orchestrator never modifies these)\n\nThese directories are part of the eval contract and are *never* modified by\nhypothesis-implementation agents. The hypothesis is \"what RTL changes would\nimprove fitness\", not \"what eval relaxations would let this RTL pass\".\n\n- `tools/` — orchestrator, worktree manager, eval gates.\n- `schemas/` — hypothesis and eval-result JSON schemas.\n- `formal/wrapper.sv`, `formal/wrapper_si.sv`, `formal/checks.cfg`,\n  `formal/checks_si.cfg`, `formal/run_all.sh` — they define the\n  correctness contract (dual-channel + single-issue variants).\n- `formal/riscv-formal/` — vendored upstream submodule.\n- `bench/programs/` — selftest, crt0, link.ld, CoreMark sources, portme.\n- `fpga/core_bench.sv`, `fpga/core_bench_si.sv`, `fpga/scripts/*`,\n  `fpga/constraints/*` — they define the FPGA fitness contract\n  (dual-channel + single-issue variants).\n- `test/cosim/main.cpp`, `test/cosim/reference.py`, `test/cosim/run_cosim.py`\n  — Verilator harness + Python ISS = the cosim contract.\n- This file (`CLAUDE.md`), `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `README.md`, `setup.sh`,\n  `Makefile`.\n\n## What hypotheses MAY change\n\nEverything under `cores/<TARGET>/rtl/` and the cocotb unit tests under\n`cores/<TARGET>/test/test_*.py`. The agent's `implementation_notes.md`\nlives at `cores/<TARGET>/implementation_notes.md` (gitignored). The\norchestrator may also update `cores/<TARGET>/core.yaml` (current:\nsection only).\n\nThe only top-level invariant on `rtl/core.sv` is that it exposes a port\nnamed `core` whose IO matches the RVFI wrapper's expectations: the\nimem/dmem port set (`io_imemAddr`, `io_imemData`, `io_imemReady`,\n`io_dmemAddr`, `io_dmemRData`, `io_dmemWData`, `io_dmemWEn`,\n`io_dmemREn`, `io_dmemReady`), `clock`, `reset`, and the RVFI port\nset determined by `core.yaml`'s `nret` field — only `io_rvfi_*_0`\nfields for `nret: 1`; both `io_rvfi_*_0` and `io_rvfi_*_1` for\n`nret: 2` (default).\n\nA hypothesis may:\n- Merge stages into a single file or split a module across many.\n- Rename or delete files inside `rtl/`.\n- Add new modules (branch predictors, caches, reservation stations…).\n- Change pipeline depth (add or remove stages).\n- Restructure the pipeline topology end to end.\n- Rewrite any `rtl/` module from scratch.\n\nIt must not:\n- Change `core`'s top-level IO shape relative to the `nret` value declared in `core.yaml` — specifically: the imem/dmem port set, and the RVFI port set (only `_0` fields for `nret: 1`; both `_0` and `_1` for `nret: 2` with channel-0-older convention and the single-retire-on-channel-0 rule).\n- Change `core.yaml`'s `nret` value mid-hypothesis. nret picks the formal/FPGA wrappers used by eval; flipping it is a contract-level change, not a per-hypothesis decision.\n- Weaken any invariant in the table above.\n- Modify any path in the don't-touch list.\n- Edit any other core's directory (cores/<other>/...). Each loop is\n  scoped to a single TARGET.\n\n## Working notes\n\nThis file is the contract. `docs/bootstrap-prompt.md` is the long-form\nplan that produced it.\n\n### RVFI contract: nret=1 vs nret=2\n\nCores declare their retirement width in `cores/<TARGET>/core.yaml`\nunder the top-level `nret` field. Two values are supported; the\ndefault is 2 for backward compat with cores authored before this\nfield existed.\n\n- `nret: 1` — single-issue. Top module exposes only `io_rvfi_*_0`\n  ports (no `_1`). Orchestrator wires `formal/wrapper_si.sv` +\n  `formal/checks_si.cfg` for formal and `fpga/core_bench_si.sv` for\n  FPGA Fmax. No `_1` tie-offs to write; no vacuous-pass machinery\n  involved. This is the recommended shape for any in-order or\n  single-retire pipeline.\n- `nret: 2` — dual-issue (or single-issue cores that opt into the\n  wider contract). Top module exposes both `io_rvfi_*_0` and\n  `io_rvfi_*_1` with the channel-0-older convention.\n  `formal/wrapper.sv` + `formal/checks.cfg` and `fpga/core_bench.sv`\n  apply. Single-issue cores declared as `nret: 2` must still tie off\n  channel 1 (`io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0` plus all other `_1` fields driven\n  to `'0`) — about 21 `assign io_rvfi_*_1 = '0;` lines.\n\nTriple-issue or wider would be a future contract bump (NRET=K), not a\nper-hypothesis decision.\n\nContract-side files keyed off `nret`:\n- nret=1: `formal/wrapper_si.sv`, `formal/checks_si.cfg`,\n  `fpga/core_bench_si.sv`.\n- nret=2: `formal/wrapper.sv`, `formal/checks.cfg`,\n  `fpga/core_bench.sv`.\n- shared: `formal/run_all.sh`, `fpga/scripts/synth.tcl`,\n  `test/cosim/main.cpp` (per-channel drain that gracefully no-ops\n  on unused channels).\n\nVacuous-pass on channel 1 (`nret: 2` only): `riscv-formal`'s\nper-channel BMC checks assume `rvfi_valid_<ch>=1` to test their\nproperty. When a single-issue hypothesis declared as `nret: 2`\nhardwires `io_rvfi_valid_1 = 0`, that assumption becomes\nunsatisfiable and SBY exits `Status: PREUNSAT` (rc=16) — technically\na vacuous pass over the empty set of ch1-valid traces.\n`formal/run_all.sh` counts `*_ch1` PREUNSAT as pass, so such\nhypotheses pass formal naturally and true dual-issue hypotheses\n(where ch1 is sometimes valid) get the regular `DONE (PASS` outcome\non the same checks. Caveat: a hypothesis that *claims* dual-issue\nbut whose channel 1 silently never retires would also vacuous-pass\nthese checks; the FPGA-fitness IPC and the cover statement (which\nfires on either channel retiring) catch the fully-stuck case.\nCores declared `nret: 1` sidestep this machinery entirely —\nchecks_si.cfg generates no `_ch1` tasks at all.\n\n## Caveat: `make formal` runs ALTOPS-mode by default\n\n`formal/checks.cfg` defines `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`, which substitutes\nthe M-extension `MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU/DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU` operations\nwith simpler XOR-of-add/sub formulas (riscv-formal docs §7.6). The\nDUT's `alu.sv` mirrors the same substitution under the same `\\`ifdef`.\n\nThis means the per-iteration formal gate proves **M-ext bypassing,\noperand routing, stall/forwarding behavior, and trap discipline** — but\nit does NOT prove that the actual hardware multiplier and divider\nproduce RV32M-correct bit results. M-ext arithmetic correctness is\ncovered separately by:\n\n  1. `test/test_alu.py` (30+ vectors per M-ext op, exact bit results)\n  2. cosim against `test/cosim/reference.py` (each retired MUL/DIV/REM\n     diff'd field-by-field with the Python ISS)\n  3. `make formal-deep` (formal without ALTOPS — slow but the only path\n     that proves real arithmetic via SMT). Run periodically, not in\n     the orchestrator loop.\n\nA hypothesis that touches the multiplier/divider must keep both the\nALTOPS branch and the real branch in `alu.sv` consistent. The cocotb\nsuite is the fast guard against arithmetic regressions.\n\"\"\"Unit tests for rtl/alu.sv.\n\nThe integer/multiply path is combinational. Divide/remainder uses the\nintegrated eight-edge engine, with the same handshake in ALTOPS builds.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.triggers import Timer\nimport os\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport random\nimport tempfile\n\nimport pytest\nfrom cocotb_tools.runner import get_runner\n\nfrom _helpers import (\n    ALU_ADD, ALU_SUB, ALU_AND, ALU_OR, ALU_XOR,\n    ALU_SLT, ALU_SLTU, ALU_SLL, ALU_SRL, ALU_SRA,\n    ALU_LUI,\n    ALU_MUL, ALU_MULH, ALU_MULHU, ALU_MULHSU,\n    ALU_DIV, ALU_DIVU, ALU_REM, ALU_REMU,\n)\n\nMASK32 = 0xFFFFFFFF\nALTOPS = os.environ.get(\"ALU_ALTOPS\", \"0\") == \"1\"\nALT_MASKS = {\n    ALU_MUL: 0x5876063E, ALU_MULH: 0xF6583FB7,\n    ALU_MULHU: 0x949CE5E8, ALU_MULHSU: 0xECFBE137,\n    ALU_DIV: 0x7F8529EC, ALU_DIVU: 0x10E8FD70,\n    ALU_REM: 0x8DA68FA5, ALU_REMU: 0x3138D0E1,\n}\n\n\ndef _expected(op, a, b, real):\n    if ALTOPS and op in ALT_MASKS:\n        value = a + b if op in (ALU_MUL, ALU_MULH, ALU_MULHU) else a - b\n        return (value ^ ALT_MASKS[op]) & MASK32\n    return real & MASK32\n\n\nasync def _edge(dut):\n    await Timer(4, \"ns\")\n    dut.clock.value = 1\n    await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n    dut.clock.value = 0\n    await Timer(1, \"ns\")\n\n\nasync def _setup(dut):\n    dut.clock.value = 0\n    dut.reset.value = 1\n    dut.div_req_valid.value = 0\n    dut.div_result_ready.value = 0\n    dut.op.value = ALU_ADD\n    dut.a.value = 0\n    dut.b.value = 0\n    await _edge(dut)\n    dut.reset.value = 0\n    await Timer(1, \"ns\")\n    assert int(dut.div_req_ready.value) == 1\n    assert int(dut.div_result_valid.value) == 0\n\n\nasync def _check(dut, op, a, b, expected):\n    \"\"\"Drive op/a/b and check the combinational result.\"\"\"\n    dut.op.value = op\n    dut.a.value  = a & MASK32\n    dut.b.value  = b & MASK32\n    await Timer(1, \"ns\")\n    actual = int(dut.out.value) & MASK32\n    expected = _expected(op, a & MASK32, b & MASK32, expected)\n    assert actual == expected, (\n        f\"op={op} a=0x{a & MASK32:08x} b=0x{b & MASK32:08x} \"\n        f\"expected=0x{expected & MASK32:08x} got=0x{actual:08x}\"\n    )\n\n\nasync def _check_div(dut, op, a, b, expected):\n    \"\"\"Count launch as edge one; perturb inputs and offer rejected requests.\"\"\"\n    expected = _expected(op, a & MASK32, b & MASK32, expected)\n    dut.op.value = op\n    dut.a.value = a & MASK32\n    dut.b.value = b & MASK32\n    dut.div_req_valid.value = 1\n    dut.div_result_ready.value = 0\n    await Timer(1, \"ns\")\n    assert int(dut.div_req_ready.value) == 1\n    assert int(dut.div_result_valid.value) == 0\n    for edge in range(1, 9):\n        await _edge(dut)\n        assert int(dut.div_req_ready.value) == 0, \"occupied engine accepted a request\"\n        assert int(dut.div_result_valid.value) == (edge == 8), f\"completion on edge {edge}\"\n        if edge < 8:\n            assert int(dut.out.value) == 0, \"partial/stale quotient published\"\n        else:\n            assert int(dut.out.value) == expected, (\n                f\"op={op} a={a & MASK32:08x} b={b & MASK32:08x}: \"\n                f\"{int(dut.out.value):08x} != {expected:08x}\"\n            )\n        # The op, operands, and a continuously offered request may all change\n        # while occupied. They must not change the saved calculation.\n        dut.a.value = (a ^ (0x12345678 * edge)) & MASK32\n        dut.b.value = (b + 12345 * edge) & MASK32\n        dut.op.value = (ALU_DIV, ALU_DIVU, ALU_REM, ALU_REMU)[edge % 4]\n\n    for _ in range(3):\n        await _edge(dut)\n        assert int(dut.div_result_valid.value) == 1\n        assert int(dut.div_req_ready.value) == 0\n        assert int(dut.out.value) == expected, \"unaccepted result changed\"\n    dut.div_result_ready.value = 1\n    await _edge(dut)\n    dut.div_req_valid.value = 0\n    dut.div_result_ready.value = 0\n    assert int(dut.div_result_valid.value) == 0\n    assert int(dut.div_req_ready.value) == 1\n\n\n\"\"\"End-to-end pipeline tests for rtl/core.sv.\n\nDrives a minimal Python-side imem/dmem against the core's combinational\nfetch/load model and captures RVFI retirements. Covers the §8 phase-2\nacceptance set: forwarding (EX->EX, MEM->EX), branches, JAL, JALR,\nSW+LW roundtrip, load-use stall, predictor recovery/training under bus\nbackpressure, and trap discipline (illegal / ECALL trap, EBREAK legal).\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.triggers import Timer\nimport random\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\n\n\n# ── RV32I instruction encoder helpers ──────────────────────────────────────\ndef _r(funct7, rs2, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((funct7 & 0x7F) << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _i(imm, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((imm & 0xFFF) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _s(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    imm_hi = (imm >> 5) & 0x7F\n    imm_lo = imm & 0x1F\n    return (imm_hi << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (imm_lo << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _b(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    # imm is a 13-bit signed number; bit 0 is implicitly zero.\n    b12  = (imm >> 12) & 1\n    b105 = (imm >> 5)  & 0x3F\n    b41  = (imm >> 1)  & 0xF\n    b11  = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    return (b12 << 31) | (b105 << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (b41 << 8) | (b11 << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _j(imm, rd, opcode):\n    # 21-bit signed imm; bit 0 implicit zero.\n    b20    = (imm >> 20) & 1\n    b101   = (imm >> 1)  & 0x3FF\n    b11    = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0010011)\ndef ADD (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(0, rs2, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0110011)\ndef LW  (rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b010, rd, 0b0000011)\ndef SW  (rs2, rs1, imm): return _s(imm & 0xFFF, rs2, rs1, 0b010, 0b0100011)\ndef BEQ (rs1, rs2, imm): return _b(imm, rs2, rs1, 0b000, 0b1100011)\ndef BNE (rs1, rs2, imm): return _b(imm, rs2, rs1, 0b001, 0b1100011)\ndef LUI (rd, imm):      return (imm & 0xFFFFF000) | (rd << 7) | 0x37\ndef JAL (rd, imm):       return _j(imm, rd, 0b1101111)\ndef JALR(rd, rs1, imm):  return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b1100111)\ndef NOP():               return ADDI(0, 0, 0)        # = 0x00000013\ndef EBREAK():            return 0x00100073\ndef ECALL():             return 0x00000073\ndef MOP(funct3, rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(1, rs2, rs1, funct3, rd, 0x33)\ndef DIV (rd, rs1, rs2): return MOP(4, rd, rs1, rs2)\ndef DIVU(rd, rs1, rs2): return MOP(5, rd, rs1, rs2)\ndef REM (rd, rs1, rs2): return MOP(6, rd, rs1, rs2)\ndef REMU(rd, rs1, rs2): return MOP(7, rd, rs1, rs2)\n\n\ndef _id_fields(bundle):\n    \"\"\"Unpack core_pkg.id_ex_t, including otherwise unobservable payload.\"\"\"\n    fields = {}\n    for name, width in reversed([\n        (\"pc\", 32), (\"predicted_next_pc\", 32), (\"rs1_val\", 32),\n        (\"rs2_val\", 32), (\"imm\", 32), (\"rd\", 5), (\"rs1_addr\", 5),\n        (\"rs2_addr\", 5), (\"ctrl\", 22), (\"instr\", 32), (\"valid\", 1),\n    ]):\n        fields[name] = bundle & ((1 << width) - 1)\n        bundle >>= width\n    assert bundle == 0\n    return fields\n\n\ndef _bundle_ctrl(bundle):\n    # All three registered bundles end with ctrl, instr, valid.\n    return (bundle >> 33) & ((1 << 22) - 1)\n\n\nclass _TrainingReference:\n    \"\"\"Ordered resolution policy with one edge between acceptance and commit.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.rows = {}\n        self.pending = None\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(x) for x in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if x.strip()]\nfor r in rows:\n print(f\\\"{r['id']} | {r.get('category')} | {r.get('outcome')} | MHz={r.get('fmax_mhz')} | cycles={r.get('cycles')} | {r.get('title')}\\\")\nids={'hyp-20260905-001-r5s0','hyp-20260905-001-r7s0','hyp-20260906-003-r13s2'}\nfor r in rows:\n if r['id'] in ids:\n  print('\\\\nDETAIL',r['id'])\n  print('motivation:',r.get('motivation'))\n  h=r.get('hypothesis',{})\n  print('hypothesis:',h.get('hypothesis',h) if isinstance(h,dict) else h)\n  print('notes:',r.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"baseline-bench-609cf36 | micro_opt | improvement | MHz=127.03 | cycles=4491485 | Baseline retest for cores/bench\nhyp-20260905-001-r1s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=127.71 | cycles=4491485 | Balance register-file reads with shared one-hot address decoding\nhyp-20260905-002-r1s1 | structural | regression | MHz=133.89 | cycles=4457730 | Single pending-store slot with direct zero-wait completion\nhyp-20260905-003-r1s2 | predictor | improvement | MHz=134.68 | cycles=4215937 | Small tagged target predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis\nhyp-20260905-001-r2s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=129.4 | cycles=4215937 | Register one-hot ALU operation selects before execute\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1 | structural | improvement | MHz=135.03 | cycles=4215937 | Split execution into a fast ALU and an eight-cycle divider\nhyp-20260905-003-r2s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=124.94 | cycles=4220017 | Two-way target prediction at unchanged 16-entry capacity\nhyp-20260905-001-r3s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=85.77 | cycles=4215937 | Localize predictor training to remove indexed feedback muxes\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 | structural | regression | MHz=143.41 | cycles=4215262 | Pipeline resolution feedback with registered predictor-row reads\nhyp-20260905-003-r3s2 | predictor | improvement | MHz=147.75 | cycles=4215937 | Row-local predictor metadata training to remove the indexed feedback path\nhyp-20260905-001-r4s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=126.15 | cycles=4215937 | Balance predictor training tag comparisons at LUT reduction boundaries\nhyp-20260905-002-r4s1 | structural | improvement | MHz=156.62 | cycles=4215262 | Pipeline row-addressed resolution events before local predictor updates\nhyp-20260905-003-r4s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=145.99 | cycles=4215262 | Bank-local registered training commands for the target predictor\nhyp-20260905-001-r5s0 | micro_opt | improvement | MHz=163.83 | cycles=4215262 | Remove wide ID/EX payload registers from bubble-control fanout\nhyp-20260905-002-r5s1 | structural | regression | MHz=110.22 | cycles=4215262 | Separate ID/EX validity from the wide recovery payload\nhyp-20260905-003-r5s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=150.13 | cycles=4208138 | Eight-entry return stack with direct return recognition\nhyp-20260905-001-r6s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=149.39 | cycles=4215262 | Replace local saturating-counter arithmetic with thermometer state\nhyp-20260905-002-r6s1 | structural | regression | MHz=97.2 | cycles=4214035 | Separate predictor tag validation from ordered metadata commit\nhyp-20260905-003-r6s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=143.37 | cycles=4215262 | Eight-bit partial tags for the target predictor\nhyp-20260905-001-r7s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=147.73 | cycles=4215262 | Predecode the multiply-high result class before execute\nhyp-20260905-002-r7s1 | structural | regression | MHz=145.6 | cycles=4215262 | Move PC-relative address generation across the ID/EX boundary\nhyp-20260905-003-r7s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=150.94 | cycles=4249105 | Eight-entry target predictor with shorter lookup and less training fanout\nhyp-20260905-001-r8s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=145.58 | cycles=4215262 | Select forwarded JALR targets after parallel operand additions\nhyp-20260905-002-r8s1 | structural | regression | MHz=153.8 | cycles=4215262 | Retime operand dependency matching across the ID/EX boundary\nhyp-20260905-003-r8s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=108.97 | cycles=4224967 | Train bimodal direction counters independently of target tags\nhyp-20260905-001-r9s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=124.67 | cycles=4215262 | Fold jump link values into the ALU result selection\nhyp-20260905-002-r9s1 | structural | regression | MHz=134.17 | cycles=4215262 | Execute multiply-high in MEM outside the fast EX result path\nhyp-20260905-003-r9s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=125.47 | cycles=4215262 | Capture raw predictor events before update classification\nhyp-20260905-001-r10s0 | micro_opt | broken | MHz=None | cycles=None | Suppress load-use bubbles for non-source instruction fields\nhyp-20260905-002-r10s1 | structural | regression | MHz=85.53 | cycles=4036238 | Decouple fetch from decode with one fall-through instruction slot\nhyp-20260905-003-r10s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=143.66 | cycles=4214035 | Train the predictor from registered EX/MEM results\nhyp-20260905-001-r11s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=148.06 | cycles=4215262 | Remove divide-bubble reset fanout from EX/MEM payload registers\nhyp-20260905-002-r11s1 | structural | regression | MHz=148.61 | cycles=4250523 | Move the complete multiply family into one shared MEM execution lane\nhyp-20260905-003-r11s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=56.61 | cycles=4215262 | Prevalidate sequential and direct-target predictions in decode\nhyp-20260906-001-r12s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=142.82 | cycles=4215262 | Align M-extension operation codes for shallow ALU class selection\nhyp-20260906-002-r12s1 | structural | regression | MHz=153.99 | cycles=4215262 | Rebuild the integer register file as replicated distributed RAM\nhyp-20260906-003-r12s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=138.33 | cycles=4220552 | XOR-folded PC indexing for the existing tagged target predictor\nhyp-20260906-001-r13s0 | micro_opt | regression | MHz=145.99 | cycles=4215262 | Remove false load-use bubbles with exact late RVFI operand forwarding\nhyp-20260906-002-r13s1 | structural | regression | MHz=150.17 | cycles=4215262 | Register integer and multiply results separately before final selection\nhyp-20260906-003-r13s2 | predictor | regression | MHz=146.65 | cycles=4215262 | Validate sequential predictions from unflushed IF state\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-001-r5s0\nmotivation: The current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, scores 371.55 iter/s at\n156.62 MHz median Fmax. Its median-seed timing report identifies a\n6.39 ns path from predicted_next_pc[0], through prediction recovery\nand hazard/divide qualification, to an ID/EX operand register RESET.\nThe final control net alone contributes 1.95 ns of routing. In the\ncurrent RTL, reset || flush clears the entire id_ex_t payload, and\nstall_id also includes the load-use condition even though that case\ninserts a bubble. Recovery therefore controls many data-register\nreset/enable pins whose values cannot matter for a killed instruction.\n\nPrior micro_opt attempts rewrote register-file selection, ALU operation\nselection, and predictor training/tag comparisons; none isolated ID/EX\nbubble controls from payload storage. LESSONS.md records that the ALU\nrewrite exposed this recovery path, while forced predictor comparison\nboundaries regressed timing. The latest winner has already registered\npredictor training events. A local rewrite of bubble insertion now\ntargets a measured control-distribution bottleneck without changing\npredictor policy, arithmetic, or architectural scheduling.\n\nhypothesis: Split ID/EX sequential updates into a narrow control/valid group and\na payload group. On a flush, clear the entire ctrl field and valid\nexactly as the existing zero bundle does. Do not clear, mask, or hold\npayload registers merely because of flush. Keep their deterministic\nreset-to-zero behavior, but otherwise capture pc, predicted_next_pc,\nboth operand values, immediate, register addresses, rd, and instruction\nwhenever there is no actual backend hold. Each field must have exactly\none sequential driver; do not mix whole-struct and partial assignments\nacross separate always_ff blocks.\n\nMake stall_id mean only dmem_stall || div_wait. Remove load_use_hazard\nfrom this ID/EX hold signal while preserving stall_if, flush_if,\nflush_id, stall_ex_mem, and hold_mem_wb behavior. This is a redundant\nhold removal: whenever a load-use hazard occurs without a backend\nhold, the existing flush_id already forces an ID/EX bubble. On that\nedge the payload may capture the still-unaccepted younger instruction,\nbut ctrl=0 and valid=0 make it inert, and the held fetch is accepted\nnormally on the following eligible edge. When a memory or divide wait\nis active, both control and payload must remain held exactly as before.\nPreserve reset priority and flush priority for the control/valid group.\n\nClearing valid alone is insufficient: forwarding and memory control\nlogic consult ctrl fields even when valid is zero. Continue clearing\nall control bits on every flush, particularly reg_write, mem_read,\nmem_write, is_branch, and is_jump. Retained or newly captured bubble\npayload must never launch a divide, redirect, train the predictor,\nforward as a writer, access memory, or retire. Leave EX/MEM full-bundle\ndivide bubbles and MEM/WB forwarding-data retention unchanged. All\nlive instructions retain their exact operands, instruction metadata,\naccepted prediction, trap behavior, and RVFI fields through holds.\n\nThis single micro-optimization removes recovery and load-use logic\nfrom the wide payload registers' direct reset and enable controls.\nExisting upstream instruction-to-NOP selection may still affect data\ninputs; it need not be redesigned. Add no stage, state bit, execution\nshortcut, new prediction behavior, or architectural latency. Preserve\nnret=1, all external ports, all decoder legality rules, and every hard\ninvariant and evaluation check.\n\nExpect an unmeasured three-percent fitness improvement from higher\nmedian Fmax, with identical bracketed cycles for the same binary and\nbus-ready schedule. Confidence is medium: the other winning seeds are\nlimited by local predictor tag-to-counter-enable paths at 6.36 and\n6.42 ns, so they may cap the benefit; synthesis may also reconstruct\nunwanted control dependencies or alter placement adversely. Inspect\nthe mapped payload reset/enable cones and routed critical paths to\nestablish whether the intended control isolation survives synthesis.\n\nRun the existing unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA gates.\nCompare accepted bus transactions and valid RVFI retirements, including\ntheir cycle positions, against the current winner under identical\ninputs; inactive payload bit patterns need not match. Use full-precision\niterations/bracketed-cycles times median Fmax and report LUT/FF costs.\nThe saved 371.55 result is documented as a 2 KB scored run; the separate\ncanonical 6 KB winner run takes 30,822,790 bracketed cycles. Keep those\nconfigurations distinct and unchanged, and verify the required 6 KB\nCRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, memory bounds, markers, and validation UART literal.\n\nnotes: \n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-001-r7s0\nmotivation: The current winner scores 388.66 at 163.83 MHz. Saved baseline RTL in\n.tmp/thermometer-r6s0/baseline/cores/bench/rtl matches every current bench\nRTL file byte-for-byte, and its generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log\nreports reproduce 163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz. In seeds 1 and 3 the\ncritical path starts at ID/EX alu_op[4] and alu_op[2], respectively,\nand ends at EX/MEM alu_result[25], taking 6.10 and 5.93 ns. Inspection\nof the corresponding synth.json LUT truth table identifies the first\ndecode term as exactly op in {12,13,14}: MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU.\nThis is current-source timing evidence for result-class decoding,\nrather than an assumption that predictor metadata still limits Fmax.\n\nThe prior r2s0 full nineteen-bit ALU predecode lost 3.92 percent after\nexposing recovery fanout; r5s0 subsequently isolated that wide payload\ncontrol. The recent r6s0 counter rewrite, r6s1 additional predictor\nupdate stage, and r6s2 shortened tags all regressed. This proposal\ntargets only the measured multiply-high class term with one additional\ninstruction bit and leaves the rest of ALU operation decoding intact.\n\nhypothesis: Move the single predicate dec_alu_op == ALU_MULH ||\ndec_alu_op == ALU_MULHU || dec_alu_op == ALU_MULHSU across the ID/EX\nregister. Add a mul_high_sel bit to id_ex_t, preferably at its most\nsignificant end so existing lower field positions remain stable.\nCapture this predicate in ID with the control group: reset or flush\nclears it, a backend stall holds it, and ordinary capture updates it\nfrom dec_alu_op. Keep one sequential owner. Preserve the existing\nindependent wide-payload update rules and the five-bit ALU encoding.\nDo not use the auxiliary bit to determine instruction legality.\n\nWire the registered bit through EX into an additional internal ALU\ninput. Factor only the multiply-high contribution out of the main\nresult case. Keep all current integer, MUL-low, and DIV/REM case arms\nand default-zero behavior in a non_high_result path; omit the three\nhigh-product arms there, so that path is zero for their opcodes.\nSelect the high-product candidate using only op[1:0], whose current\nmapping is 00=MULH, 01=MULHU, 10=MULHSU, with 11 producing zero.\nCombine the two disjoint contributions as\n  out = non_high_result | ({32{mul_high_sel}} & high_result).\nThe registered class bit directly enables the high-result term;\ndo not reconstruct its five-bit opcode comparison in EX or qualify\nit with a second full-op decode. For every reachable instruction the\nregistered bit and opcode must agree, including bubbles and holds.\n\nPreserve the existing signed/unsigned product expressions, widths,\nand bit slices. In RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS, the same suboperation mapping\nmust select the existing MULH/MULHU add-XOR formulas and MULHSU\nsubtract-XOR formula with their exact constants. MUL-low and all\ndivider arithmetic, request/result handshakes, and the shared real\nand ALTOPS eight-edge schedule remain unchanged. This is one local\ncontrol-retiming optimization, with no new arithmetic algorithm,\ninstruction stage, execution latency, or predictor-policy change.\n\nExpect an unmeasured four-percent fitness gain through median Fmax\nalone. The intended mapped benefit is removing the measured encoded\nclass detector from the high-result selection path. Inspect synthesis\nand all three routed reports to verify that the registered select is\nactually used. Added ID decode delay, select routing, synthesis\nrefactoring, or the seed-2 recovery path becoming limiting can erase\nthe gain. Do not expand this into whole-ALU one-hot selection, opcode\nrenumbering, predictor changes, or synthesis keep constraints.\n\nRun the unchanged lint, real and ALTOPS unit suites, formal, stalled\ncosim, and three-seed FPGA gates. Check exact M-extension results and\nselect/op alignment through load-use bubbles, redirects, dmem holds,\nand divide waits. Require identical valid RVFI retirements with their\ncycle positions, accepted bus transactions, predictions, and bracketed\ncycles for matched ELF and ready schedules. Preserve nret=1, all\nexternal ports, decoder/trap rules, strict retirement order, memory\nbounds, markers, UART validation, and every protected evaluation file.\nCompute fitness with full-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles times\nmedian Fmax. Historical notes identify the recorded 388.66 comparison\nas a 2 KB score; keep that like-for-like comparison separate from the\nrequested unchanged 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration run with both bus stalls,\nCRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, and the literal Correct operation validated.\n\nnotes: Implemented the multiply-high predecode hypothesis in the four proposed bench RTL files. `id_ex_t.mul_high_sel` is the new most-significant bit (230); all previous field positions and the five-bit ALU encoding remain unchanged. ID owns its only sequential assignment alongside ctrl/valid: reset or flush clears it, a backend hold preserves it, and normal capture records membership in MULH/MULHU/MULHSU independently of validity. The independent wide-payload update block is unchanged.\n\nEX wires that register directly to the internal ALU. The main result case retains integer operations, MUL-low, DIV/REM and default zero. Only the three high-product arms move to a case on op[1:0], with 11 returning zero. The final result is the requested OR of non_high_result and the selector-masked high_result. All signedness, product widths/slices, ALTOPS constants and add/sub formulas remain exact. The divider, eight-edge schedule, forwarding, prediction, decoder/trap rules, RVFI ports, and nret=1 are unchanged.\n\nTests now drive the new ALU input at every opcode change, including reset and rejected/perturbed divide requests. Added alternating result classes with signed boundary operands, all reserved ALU default-zero opcodes, high multiplication while the divider is occupied, ID control/hold priority combinations (including flush+hold and invalid captures), and whole-pipeline selector/op assertions before and after every edge. Directed real and ALTOPS pipeline tests cover all high operations, EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, dependent stalled loads, load-use bubbles, redirects with instruction stalls, divide waits and reset of both held live instructions and nonzero bubbles. Existing assertions were retained.\n\nLocal verification:\n\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\n- `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider cores/bench/test/`: PASS, 10 pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU tests and the additional ALTOPS high-multiply pipeline tests.\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, no RTL fix attempts. The first invocation stopped before generating checks because the default Python lacked PyYAML. Selecting the installed conda Python on PATH allowed the unchanged `formal/run_all.sh` to auto-detect nret=1 and run wrapper_si/checks_si. The script and framework were copied into bench scratch space to avoid writing into the shared protected formal tree. Full run evidence: `cores/bench/.tmp/mul-high-select/candidate/formal-gate.log`.\n- Unchanged cosim gate: PASS for selftest and default 2 KB CoreMark. Additional selftest cosim against the unchanged Python ISS passed with both bus stalls for ready seeds 0xDEADBEEF, 1 and 42 (115 retirements each).\n- Three unchanged FPGA routing gates: PASS. Candidate seeds 1/2/3 = 149.12 / 144.63 / 147.73 MHz; median = 147.73 MHz. Mapped resources: 6,632 LUT4, 3,203 DFF.\n- Fresh synthesis and all three routes of the saved, byte-identical pre-edit RTL reproduce the historical baseline exactly: 163.83 / 155.33 / 168.55 MHz, median 163.83 MHz, 6,618 LUT4 and 3,198 DFF. The candidate adds 14 mapped LUT4 and 5 mapped DFF despite adding only one source-level instruction bit.\n\nSaved the original bench RTL before editing and compared baseline/candidate executions of the exact same ELFs and xorshift ready schedules, with both bus stalls enabled. An observation-only scratch copy of the unchanged C++ harness emitted binary records for every cycle's prediction/readiness/acceptance/redirect/divide-wait state, accepted instruction and data bus transactions, and every field of every valid RVFI retirement including its cycle. Direct block-by-block byte comparisons passed. The candidate also asserted selector/op alignment before and after every simulated edge. The unmodified C++ harness independently reproduced the 6 KB run's final RVFI, UART, and markers exactly.\n\n| Workload | Valid retirements | Start / stop cycles | Bracketed cycles | Full-precision iterations/cycle | Candidate fitness |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 2,937,037 | 15,229 / 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 10 / 4,215,262 | 350.4645737323089 |\n| 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 22,093,920 | 53,747 / 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 10 / 30,822,790 | 47.92882149863785 |\n\nFitness uses `(10 / bracketed_cycles) * median_Fmax_MHz * 1,000,000` without rounding iterations/cycle. The historical 388.66 comparison is the 2 KB score: 163.83 MHz gives 388.65911537645826 on this same 2 KB ELF/schedule. The candidate regresses 9.827259964597456 percent. The corresponding 6 KB baseline score at 163.83 MHz is 53.152229243361816 and is kept separate.\n\nThe requested 6 KB workload completed in bounds, with seedcrc 8a02, crclist d4b0, crcmatrix be52, crcstate 5e47, crcfinal 5949, 10 iterations and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. This checkout's protected Makefile/evaluator actually default to 2 KB and its CRCs; those gates were run unchanged. The extra 6 KB ELF was built from unchanged copied benchmark sources with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`. UART's fixed compiler-description string still says -O2; the captured compiler commands confirm -O3 for both ELFs. No evaluation source was modified.\n\nSynthesis confirms the intended retiming: `cpu.u_ex.u_alu.mul_high_sel_DFFRE_Q` drives only `mul_high_sel_LUT3_I0`, whose inputs are the registered selector, op[1], and op[0], with INIT=0x2a (selector AND suboperation != 11). No five-bit high-class comparison remains at that enable. All three routed netlists retain this DFFRE and LUT3, placed respectively at X9Y16/DFF4 -> X9Y15/LUT3, X11Y12/DFF3 -> X11Y13/LUT1, and X11Y16/DFF1 -> X7Y16/LUT6.\n\nThe measured gain did not materialize. All three candidate critical paths instead traverse EX/MEM destination-register forwarding selection, the forwarded JALR target addition, and the predictor's existing pending target register. Seed 1: ex_mem_rd[2] -> pending_target_q[26], 6.71 ns (3.62 logic + 3.09 routing). Seed 2: ex_mem_rd[4] -> pending_target_q[28], 6.92 ns (4.02 + 2.90). Seed 3: ex_mem_rd[0] -> pending_target_q[25], 6.77 ns (3.49 + 3.28). This remains one local control retiming; no predictor or recovery policy change was added to chase the exposed paths.\n\nDetailed benchmark markers, ELF/trace SHA-256 hashes, byte counts, synthesis connectivity, routed timing summaries and fitness calculations are in `cores/bench/.tmp/mul-high-select/`. Routed candidate reports are in `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/`. Real-arithmetic formal-deep was not run; real arithmetic is covered by exact ALU vectors, pipeline tests, stalled ISS cosim and baseline-equivalent full CoreMark traces.\n\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260906-003-r13s2\nmotivation: Preserve the winner's prediction accuracy and target-table organization\nwhile shortening prediction recovery. The r8s2 and r12s1 implementation\nnotes reproduce an incoming seed-2 critical path from registered\npredicted_next_pc[3] to ID/EX valid.RESET; the other two incoming seeds\nare limited by ALU result selection. Predictor capacity, associativity,\nreturn prediction, partial tags, and XOR indexing have already regressed.\nThese results favor a small change to recovery timing with unchanged\nprediction policy rather than assuming substantial residual cycle savings.\n\nThe related r11s2 experiment regressed 65.45% because prevalidating a\ndirect target in ID put recovery, IF NOP substitution, immediate decoding,\na 32-bit addition, and equality on one register-to-register path. That\nresult does not separately measure sequential-only validation from raw\nfetch state. PC+4 prediction correctness needs neither instruction bits\nnor an immediate, so it can cross the existing ID/EX boundary without\nintroducing that measured dependency.\n\nhypothesis: Add exactly one prediction metadata bit, prediction_matches_seq, to the\ncombinational IF bundle and the existing ID/EX payload. In if_stage,\ncompute seq_pc = pc + 32'd4 with 32-bit wraparound, and define the bit\nas the exact equality predicted_next_pc == seq_pc. Compute it solely\nfrom the local, unflushed PC and committed predictor lookup. Reuse the\nexisting PC+4 expression. An equivalent implementation is true on the\nsequential selection and a full 32-bit target-versus-seq_pc equality\non a selected target-table hit. Selecting a table target equal to PC+4\nmust also produce true; a table-hit or predicted-direction bit alone\nis insufficient.\n\nThis combinational bit must not depend on imem_data, out.instr, decoded\nimmediates, flush, redirect, redirect_target, or live resolution inputs.\nLike out.pc and out.predicted_next_pc, drive it even when the instruction\noutput is replaced by a NOP. In ID, copy the bit without arithmetic or\ncomparison into its single payload register: zero on reset, hold on the\nexisting backend stall, otherwise capture regardless of flush. Preserve\nthe full predicted_next_pc and the independent ctrl/valid flush logic.\nThere is one additional pipeline state bit and no extra pipeline stage.\n\nFactor EX prediction validation by the resolved successor class. For\nordinary instructions, untaken branches, and the existing trap-to-PC+4\npath, prediction_correct is the held prediction_matches_seq bit. For a\nlegal taken branch or JAL, compare the full recorded prediction with\nthe existing EX branch_target = in.pc + in.imm. For a legal JALR,\ncompare it with the existing forwarded jalr_sum after clearing bit zero.\nCompute these target equalities in EX and select their Boolean results;\ndo not precompute any direct-target equality or PC+imm addition in ID.\nKeep redirect = resolve_valid && !prediction_correct. Leave the current\nactual_next_pc computation authoritative for redirect_target and RVFI.\n\nRequire Boolean equivalence to the old expression on every advancing\ninstruction: redirect == resolve_valid &&\n(actual_next_pc != in.predicted_next_pc). Preserve exact full-address\ncomparisons, legal and misaligned target handling, taken transfers to\nPC+4, stale predictions on non-controls and completed divides, and\nchanging JALR destinations. Existing resolve_valid qualification,\nredirect priority over instruction stalls, backend holds, forwarding,\nmemory side effects, retirement order, and nret=1 remain authoritative.\n\nKeep all sixteen predictor rows, PC[5:2] indexing, full tags and targets,\nbinary saturating counters, command encoding, allocation/invalidation\nrules, and capture-at-N/commit-at-N+1 training unchanged. The new bit\ndescribes the same pre-edge prediction captured with the instruction,\neven when training commits on its acceptance edge. It must never be\nrecomputed for a held instruction from newer table contents. No policy,\npredictor capacity, benchmark-specific rule, or evaluation change is\npart of this hypothesis.\n\nThe intended benefit is moving sequential next-PC equality before the\nID/EX boundary and removing the selected actual_next_pc bus from EX's\nmismatch comparator. Expect unchanged predictions, recoveries, and\nbracketed cycles, with an unmeasured three-percent fitness gain from\nhigher median Fmax. Confidence is low: the added IF equality may become\ncritical, separate target comparisons may increase LUT/routing cost,\nand unchanged ALU or predictor-maintenance paths may still set Fmax.\n\nInspect mapped cones to confirm that the new metadata D input has no\ncombinational recovery/NOP/decode dependency, and that EX uses its\nregistered value for sequential recovery. Compare the incoming and\ncandidate designs under identical binaries and ready schedules, retaining\nthe independent full-next-PC mismatch assertion. Run unchanged lint,\ncocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and all three FPGA P&R seeds. Report\nmarker-bracketed cycles, recoveries, median Fmax, LUT4/FF cost, and the\nthree critical paths. Compute fitness using unrounded iterations/cycle.\nValidate the requested 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration workload with both bus\nstalls, canonical CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, bounded accesses, both markers,\nand the literal Correct operation validated. If existing evaluation\nstill reproduces the historical score with a 2 KB image, report that\nseparately from the canonical 6 KB comparison without editing the gates.\n\nnotes: # Sequential prediction validation from unflushed IF state\n\nImplemented the hypothesis on incoming commit `248468e`. Functional behavior is\nunchanged in the comparisons below, but the measured timing benefit did not\nmaterialize: median Fmax and fitness both regress **10.4864798877%**.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Added exactly one `prediction_matches_seq` field to the combinational IF\n  bundle and one bit to the existing ID/EX payload. Its meaning is the full\n  equality of the accepted prediction and modulo-2^32 PC+4.\n- IF reuses one `seq_pc = pc + 32'd4` expression for sequential prediction and\n  the equality. The bit is driven from raw PC and committed lookup state even\n  while the instruction output is replaced by a NOP. It has no instruction,\n  decode, recovery, or live resolution input.\n- ID copies the bit in the existing payload register group: zero on reset,\n  hold on backend stall, otherwise capture, including flush. No ID arithmetic,\n  comparison, new enable, or additional stage was introduced.\n- EX selects Boolean match results. PC+4 successors use the registered bit;\n  legal taken branches/JAL compare the full prediction with `branch_target`;\n  legal JALR compares with the forwarded `jalr_sum` with bit zero cleared.\n  `actual_next_pc`, redirect target, resolve qualification, and RVFI remain\n  authoritative and unchanged.\n- All 16 predictor rows, PC[5:2] indexing, full tags/targets, counters, commands,\n  allocation/invalidation, and capture-at-N/commit-at-N+1 training are unchanged.\n  The external contract remains `nret: 1`.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\n- Cocotb: all nine original pytest runners PASS; the added comparison runner\n  also PASS. The pipeline runner passes all 38 cocotb tests, including the new\n  taken-PC+4 branch/JAL/JALR test under zero wait and both bus stalls.\n- IF tests check the new bit against independent full equality at every public\n  predictor sample, before and after edges. Added selected targets equal to\n  PC+4, single-bit target differences at bits 2 through 31, wraparound, and\n  unclocked changes to instruction, flush, redirect and resolution inputs.\n  Existing pending-commit, alias, counter, stall and reset oracles remain.\n- ID tests seed the opposite bit before exercising both values under every\n  reset/flush/stall combination, independently of decoding and prediction data.\n- Pipeline tests retain the original accepted-prediction mismatch assertion\n  and add a separate full-next-PC assertion on every cycle. Accepted metadata,\n  backend retention and flush capture are checked independently.\n- Incoming/candidate directed comparison: **34 runs, 1,011 cycles, 510\n  retirements, 97 recoveries**, with exact equality of original payloads,\n  controls, cycle timing, predictions, memory activity and RVFI. Only the new\n  metadata field is omitted from cross-design comparison. Programs cover\n  changing load-forwarded JALRs, stale ordinary/divide predictions, traps,\n  load-use, both bus stalls, pending commits, reset and wrong-path stores.\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim`: PASS, selftest plus the evaluator's 2 KB\n  CoreMark CRC run. Additional selftest RVFI comparisons against the unchanged\n  Python ISS pass with both stalls at seeds 1, 42 and 0x13579bdf (115\n  retirements each). Incoming/candidate cycle traces are identical.\n- **Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, using the unchanged\n  `bash formal/run_all.sh`, `wrapper_si.sv`, `checks_si.cfg`, and ALTOPS mode.\n  No RTL fix attempts or counterexamples. An initial launch exited before\n  generating checks because the default Python lacked PyYAML; selecting the\n  installed Miniconda Python resolved the environment issue. Actual M-extension\n  arithmetic remains covered by the unchanged arithmetic tests and cosim;\n  the multiplier/divider RTL was not changed.\n\n## Mapped cones\n\nThe candidate's additional state is one `DFFRE`,\n`cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_96`, Q = ID/EX bit 166. CE is the existing payload\nenable and RESET is the existing reset-only signal.\n\nBackward traversal of its mapped D cone reaches 483 combinational cells and\n514 state outputs, exclusively IF PC state and committed predictor state.\nThis includes the synthesis-generated copies of PC bits used by the target\nLUTRAM read address. Asynchronous LUTRAM read addresses were traversed; clocked\nwrite inputs were treated as state boundaries. No decode, instruction-LFSR,\nEX, pending-training, flush or redirect state appears in the D cone.\n\nThe registered metadata Q reaches the ID/EX valid-reset recovery cone. A\nthree-valued evaluation of mapped LUTs with an advancing ordinary instruction,\nreset low and all unrelated state unknown gives valid.RESET = 1 for metadata\n0 and valid.RESET = 0 for metadata 1. Thus sequential recovery uses the held\nbit without needing any target comparison to resolve. The IF equality itself\nwas not the worst critical path in these seeds.\n\n## Workloads and fitness\n\nBoth designs used the exact same ELF for each workload, `ITERATIONS=10`,\n`COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, and the unchanged harness with `--bench --istall --dstall`\nand its default 0xdeadbeef ready-schedule seed. Fitness below is calculated as\n`median_Fmax_MHz * 1e6 * (10 / bracketed_cycles)`, without rounding iter/cycle.\n\n| Measurement | Incoming | Candidate |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 163.83 | 142.29 |\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 155.33 | 150.99 |\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 168.55 | 146.65 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 163.83 | 146.65 |\n| LUT4 | 6,618 | 6,661 |\n| FF | 3,198 | 3,199 |\n| Requested 6 KB bracketed cycles | 30,822,790 | 30,822,790 |\n| 6 KB recoveries inside markers | 550,409 | 550,409 |\n| 6 KB recoveries, entire run | 552,348 | 552,348 |\n| 6 KB iterations/cycle | 3.2443526364745047e-7 | 3.2443526364745047e-7 |\n| 6 KB fitness, iterations/s | 53.15222924336181 | 47.57843141389861 |\n| Historical evaluator 2 KB bracketed cycles | 4,215,262 | 4,215,262 |\n| 2 KB recoveries inside markers | 80,662 | 80,662 |\n| 2 KB recoveries, entire run | 81,398 | 81,398 |\n| 2 KB iterations/cycle | 2.372331779139707e-6 | 2.372331779139707e-6 |\n| 2 KB fitness, iterations/s | 388.6591153764582 | 347.90245541083806 |\n\nAll six P&R runs completed successfully using unmodified `synth.tcl`,\n`nextpnr_run.sh` and `tools.eval.fpga`, with the single-retirement wrapper.\nThe unchanged historical evaluator reports **388.66 -> 347.90** on its 2 KB\nimage; its displayed `ipc_coremark=2e-6` is rounded and was not used to compute\nthe numbers above. The requested 6 KB comparison is reported separately.\n\nThe 6 KB start/stop cycles are **53,747 / 30,876,537**. Both runs reached EBREAK\nafter 22,093,920 retirements with `oob:false`, both markers, ten iterations,\n`seedcrc=8a02`, `crclist=d4b0`, `crcmatrix=be52`, `crcstate=5e47`,\n`crcfinal=5949`, and the literal **Correct operation validated.**\n\nThe 2 KB start/stop cycles are **15,229 / 4,230,491**. Both runs reached EBREAK\nafter 2,937,037 retirements with `oob:false`, both markers, ten iterations,\n`seedcrc=e9f5`, `crclist=e714`, `crcmatrix=1fd7`, `crcstate=8e3a`,\n`crcfinal=fcaf`, and the validation literal.\n\nRecovery counts were collected by a read-only observer linked to the existing\nVerilator model and including the unchanged C++ harness. It also asserts the\nindependent full-next-PC mismatch on every advancing instruction and checks\nthe candidate's held sequential bit. Cycle/bus/prediction and RVFI hashes\nmatch between designs for each complete run:\n\n| Workload | Cycle hash (64-bit FNV-1a) | RVFI hash (64-bit FNV-1a) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 6 KB | 4ea675b489fcb68e | 343eb8fae9f1c7b4 |\n| 2 KB | 57769743d7c2834d | f842f18640adf3f8 |\n\nThese hashes supplement the exact bounded-program trace comparison; they are\nnot a formal equivalence proof. A separate observer also checks the exact\nrequested memory/MMIO bounds, including the narrower `[0x10000000,\n0x10000200)` write window, without changing the harness or evaluation gates.\nBoth workloads pass this stricter check. Their only MMIO write addresses are\n0x10000000, 0x10000100 and 0x10000104. The 6 KB run exercises 6,753,957\ninstruction-not-ready cycles and 6,756,660 data-not-ready cycles.\n\nThe existing UART compiler-flags string is hardcoded to `-O2 -march=rv32im`;\nthe actual build commands used `-O3`. Likewise, `CoreMark Size` prints the\nper-algorithm allocation (2000 for total 6000, 666 for total 2000), as defined\nin the unchanged `core_main.c`. Neither reporting string was patched.\n\n## Critical paths\n\nThe following are final post-route register-to-register paths. The selected\nactual-next-PC mismatch path seen in incoming seed 2 no longer limits the\ncandidate. ALU selection/routing and predictor maintenance remain limiting.\n\n| Design / seed | Source -> destination | Delay ns | Logic / routing ns |\n|---|---|---:|---:|\n| Incoming 1 | ID/EX alu_op[4] -> EX/MEM alu_result[25].D | 6.10 | 2.87 / 3.24 |\n| Incoming 2 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[3] -> ID/EX valid.RESET | 6.44 | 2.77 / 3.67 |\n| Incoming 3 | ID/EX alu_op[2] -> EX/MEM alu_result[25].D | 5.93 | 2.78 / 3.15 |\n| Candidate 1 | ID/EX alu_op[3] -> EX/MEM alu_result[29].D | 7.03 | 3.52 / 3.51 |\n| Candidate 2 | Predictor row 0 tag[1] -> row 0 counter.CE | 6.62 | 2.15 / 4.47 |\n| Candidate 3 | ID/EX alu_op[3] -> EX/MEM alu_result[15].D | 6.82 | 3.26 / 3.56 |\n\nCandidate endpoints are respectively `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_66.D`,\n`cpu.u_if.metadata[0].counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE`, and\n`cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_80.D`. Both candidate ALU paths originate at\n`cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_195.Q`; the maintenance path originates at\n`cpu.u_if.metadata[0].tag_q_DFFE_Q_24.Q`.\n\n## Deviations, concerns and artifacts\n\nThere is no architectural deviation. An additional `test_prediction_compare.py`\nreplays existing bounded tests for incoming/candidate comparison. Build and\nformal artifacts were isolated under `$TMPDIR/seq-validation-r13s2/` to avoid\nwriting into shared formal, benchmark, tool, or other-core directories. All\ncandidate RTL/tests match the measured scratch copy; contract files were\nchecked byte-for-byte against the worktree.\n\nThe 6 KB ELF SHA-256 is\n`c3b80e911a9292eda01e35bc0034752cb93de46dd68ee8c89cff37bb2bfa7c5c`;\nthe 2 KB ELF SHA-256 is\n`c79d46c4618aeeff26037b744257d1e5c11e0c86acae942bc669e8f110bb7d5a`.\nScratch evidence includes `incoming-directed.json`, `candidate-directed.json`,\n`{incoming,candidate}-{2,6}k-measurement.json`, `mapped-cone.json`,\n`timing-summary.json`, per-seed `nextpnr.log` files, cocotb XML, and the formal\n`candidate/formal/last_run-31875.log` plus per-check PASS statuses.\n\nThe hypothesis preserves cycles and recoveries but adds 43 LUT4s and changes\nmapping/placement enough to lower Fmax. This is a measured regression, not a\nfitness improvement; the implementation is retained for the orchestrator's\nevaluation without altering policy or gates.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
