{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor x in rows:\n    if x.get('category')=='structural':\n        print(json.dumps({k:x.get(k) for k in ('id','title','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','outcome')}, ensure_ascii=False))\n        h=x.get('hypothesis',{})\n        if isinstance(h,dict): h=h.get('hypothesis','')\n        print(str(h)[:1700])\nfor key in ['hyp-20260905-001-r5s0','hyp-20260905-002-r10s1','hyp-20260905-001-r10s0','hyp-20260905-002-r11s1']:\n    for x in rows:\n        if x.get('id')==key:\n            print('\\\\nDETAIL',key)\n            print(x.get('implementation_notes','')[:10000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nrows=[json.loads(s) for s in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines() if s.strip()]\nfor x in rows:\n    if x.get('category')=='structural':\n        print(json.dumps({k:x.get(k) for k in ('id','title','fitness','delta_pct','fmax_mhz','cycles','outcome')}, ensure_ascii=False))\n        h=x.get('hypothesis',{})\n        if isinstance(h,dict): h=h.get('hypothesis','')\n        print(str(h)[:1700])\nfor key in ['hyp-20260905-001-r5s0','hyp-20260905-002-r10s1','hyp-20260905-001-r10s0','hyp-20260905-002-r11s1']:\n    for x in rows:\n        if x.get('id')==key:\n            print('\\\\nDETAIL',key)\n            print(x.get('implementation_notes','')[:10000])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Single pending-store slot with direct zero-wait completion\", \"fitness\": 300.35, \"delta_pct\": 6.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.89, \"cycles\": 4457730, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nDecouple store retirement from bus completion with exactly one pending\nstore slot. Allocate it only for a valid, legal, naturally aligned store\nwhose complete access lies in normal RAM [0x00000000, 0x00100000), when\nthe slot is empty and io_dmemReady=0. Capture the effective byte address,\nexisting replicated write data, and byte mask atomically. Advance that\ninstruction into MEM/WB once, carrying its original PC, instruction,\noperands, and aligned RVFI memory fields. Its eventual bus drain produces\nno additional retirement. The slot holds only the physical write request;\nRVFI metadata continues along the existing pipeline.\n\nWith the slot empty and io_dmemReady=1, loads and stores use the existing\ndirect MEM path without an enqueue cycle. While the slot is valid, give\nits request sole ownership of dmem, hold address/data/byte enables stable\nuntil a ready handshake, and suppress any younger external request.\nDrain independently of frontend stalls and redirects. Permit younger\nnon-memory instructions to advance and retire in order while it drains.\nBlock every younger load or store until the slot is empty, including the\ncycle that drains the old request; omit simultaneous drain/refill,\ncoalescing, and store-to-load forwarding to keep arbitration small.\n\nAll MMIO, UART, start/stop marker accesses, and out-of-range addresses\nremain on the existing unbuffered path. Never use buffering to hide an\nout-of-bounds access from the existing checks. FENCE/FENCE.I, EBREAK,\nand trapping instructions must also wait for an older pending store to\ndrain. Marker writes complete only on their real bus handshake, after\nprior writes, so the timed region cannot exclude undrained work. Invalid,\nill\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Split execution into a fast ALU and an eight-cycle divider\", \"fitness\": 320.28, \"delta_pct\": 0.26, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.03, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"outcome\": \"improvement\"}\nPartition execution into the existing one-cycle integer/multiply datapath\nand one blocking, eight-cycle divide/remainder engine. Only legal valid\nDIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU instructions enter the engine. Retain the current\npipeline depth and forwarding for every other instruction, including all\nfour multiply variants. This changes execution-resource scheduling and\npipeline advancement; it does not add another retirement channel.\n\nImplement unsigned restoring division with four quotient bits computed\nper clock, using four cascaded shift/subtract/select steps and one shared\nremainder/quotient state. Perform the first four steps on the accepting\nlaunch edge and the remaining seven groups on the next seven edges.\nNormalize signed operands on launch and include quotient/remainder sign\ncorrection in the final result capture. Do not add separate normalization\nor correction cycles. Capture the original operands and operation at\nlaunch; changing live forwarding values afterward cannot affect the\noperation. Preserve truncation toward zero, remainder sign matching the\ndividend, quotient all-ones on division by zero, original dividend on\nremainder by zero, and INT_MIN/-1 quotient INT_MIN with remainder zero.\nSpecial cases use the same eight-edge schedule. No real / or % operator\nremains in the synthesized execution datapath.\n\nUse request acceptance and a retained result-valid/result-ready handshake.\nLaunch once when a valid legal divide is in ID/EX, the engine is idle,\nand an older EX/MEM memory instruction is not blocking advancement.\nFreeze the fetch PC and ID/EX while the divide is incomplete. Retain PC,\ninstruction, destination, control, and exact predicted_next_pc in ID/EX;\nlatch \n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline resolution feedback with registered predictor-row reads\", \"fitness\": 340.22, \"delta_pct\": 6.23, \"fmax_mhz\": 143.41, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nSplit resolved-instruction feedback into a row-read stage and an update\nstage inside if_stage. Retain exactly sixteen direct-mapped entries,\nPC[5:2] indexing, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, and the\nexisting two-bit counter policy. The single architectural change is\nadding one register boundary to the training transaction; do not add\nan instruction pipeline stage or change execution-resource scheduling.\n\nOn edge N with resolve_valid asserted, capture the resolution's index,\nPC tag, target, legal/branch/jump/taken flags, and the selected row's\nvalid, tag, and two-bit counter into a maintenance register. Capture\nraw row state here, not an already-computed tag hit or counter update:\nthe indexed read must end at this register. The existing target need\nnot be read for training because neither hit detection nor counter\narithmetic consumes it. Carry a separate maintenance-valid bit.\n\nDuring the following cycle, compare the captured tags and compute the\nrow update solely from the captured event and row state. Commit it on\nedge N+1. A legal jump writes its full target and counter 11. A legal\ntaken branch writes its target, allocates counter 10 on a miss, and\nincrements with saturation on a hit. A legal not-taken branch only\ndecrements a matching counter with saturation, retaining the target\nand allocating nothing on a miss. An illegal, trapping, or non-control\nresolution invalidates only a matching tag. Preserve the existing\nfield-specific write enables and full architectural address widths.\nIn particular, no live indexed tag lookup may feed the update-stage\nwrite enable, or the proposed timing cut would disappear.\n\nSustain one resolution per cycle by committing the ol\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline row-addressed resolution events before local predictor updates\", \"fitness\": 371.55, \"delta_pct\": 6.02, \"fmax_mhz\": 156.62, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"outcome\": \"improvement\"}\nAdd one pipeline boundary to resolution maintenance, using a private\npending-event register at each of the sixteen existing metadata rows.\nThis is one structural retiming of the feedback transaction. Keep the\ninstruction pipeline, immediate EX redirect, predictor algorithm,\ncapacity, fetch lookup, and execution-resource scheduling unchanged.\n\nEach row gets a pending-valid bit, full 26-bit resolving PC tag, and\ntwo-bit command. Encode the four commands as matching-tag invalidation\nfor illegal/trapping/non-control instructions, legal jump, legal taken\nbranch, and legal not-taken branch. Classification uses the existing\nresolve_legal/branch/jump/taken outputs, with illegality taking priority.\nOn edge N, pending_valid[row] becomes resolve_valid &&\n(resolve_pc[5:2] == row). Update that row's pending tag and command only\non this acceptance; otherwise retain its payload. These independently\nenabled registers must remain local to their rows, rather than becoming\none shared registered PC broadcast into sixteen comparisons. Capture\nthe event only; do not read, snapshot, or compare old table metadata\non this edge. At most one row accepts per cycle, without backpressure.\n\nOn edge N+1, each row with its old pending-valid asserted commits using\nits private pending tag/command and its current local valid/tag/counter.\nA jump writes the pending tag, sets valid, and sets the counter to 11.\nA taken branch writes the tag and valid, allocates counter 10 on a miss,\nor increments a hit with saturation. A not-taken branch decrements only\na matching counter with saturation and retains its target. Invalidation\nclears only a matching row's valid bit. Preserve all other fields.\nCommit-time tag compa\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Separate ID/EX validity from the wide recovery payload\", \"fitness\": 261.48, \"delta_pct\": -29.62, \"fmax_mhz\": 110.22, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nRestructure the IF-to-ID/EX boundary around a separate registered valid\ntoken. Keep the current pipeline depth, single issue, exact accepted\nprediction metadata, and one-younger-slot recovery. This experiment\nchanges squash distribution, with no predictor-policy, arithmetic,\nmemory-latency, or instruction-scheduling redesign.\n\nIn IF, always present raw imem_data as out.instr. Keep out.valid low\non flush or redirect, and preserve the existing PC update priorities:\nreset, immediate EX redirect, then accepted predicted advancement.\nRecovery must still override instruction-bus backpressure. Preserve\nevery predictor table, pending event, training rule, and commit edge.\nRemoving the recovery-controlled NOP mux is necessary; splitting only\nthe ID/EX register would leave a second wide recovery-to-payload path.\n\nIn ID, register validity separately from the decoded control and wide\npayload. The valid register retains the existing reset/flush priority\nover capture/hold: reset or flush clears it, otherwise !stall captures\nin.valid, otherwise it holds. Payload registers capture the raw PC,\nprediction, instruction, immediate, register addresses, operand values,\nand decoded control whenever !stall, even when flush is asserted.\nTheir existing reset initialization can remain. Flush must not select\ntheir reset, data mux, or clock enable. In particular, do not replace\nthe old wide flush reset with a wide flush-controlled hold enable.\nKeep the public id_ex_t layout and module ports unchanged.\n\nAt the ID/EX output boundary, expose the captured payload unchanged\nbut present ctrl='0 whenever the REGISTERED valid token is zero.\nValid instructions see their complete original decoded control.\nThis\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Separate predictor tag validation from ordered metadata commit\", \"fitness\": 230.66, \"delta_pct\": -40.65, \"fmax_mhz\": 97.2, \"cycles\": 4214035, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nAdd one register boundary between row-local tag validation and predictor\nmetadata commit in rtl/if_stage.sv. This is one structural retiming of\nthe existing training transaction. Retain the sixteen direct-mapped rows,\nPC[5:2] index, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, two-bit counter\npolicy, allocation rules, and committed-state-only fetch lookup.\n\nUse three edges for an accepted event E. At edge N, the existing private\nrow capture records E's tag and command. At edge N+1, transfer that event\ninto a new commit register and capture one hit bit computed against the\nrow's logical tag/valid state AFTER the older event committing on that\nsame edge. At edge N+2, apply E using its registered hit, command, and\ntag, together with the row's then-current two-bit counter. The existing\ndesign commits at N+1, so this adds exactly one training-visibility cycle.\nCapture, validate, and commit must overlap at one event per clock,\nincluding an uninterrupted stream of resolutions to the same row.\n\nResolve the validation-stage read-after-write hazard locally. Let C be\nthe older event currently in this row's commit register and P the event\nin its existing pending register. Compute two full-width comparisons in\nparallel: table_tag == P.tag and C.tag == P.tag. If C.valid and C.command\nis JUMP or TAKEN, P's effective hit is the second comparison because C\nallocates or refreshes a valid row with C.tag on the validation edge.\nOtherwise, if C.valid, C.command is INVALIDATE, and C.hit is true, P's\neffective hit is false because C clears the row. In all other cases use\ntable_valid && (table_tag == P.tag). Register this effective hit with P.\nSelection between comparator results is narrow; do no\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Move PC-relative address generation across the ID/EX boundary\", \"fitness\": 345.41, \"delta_pct\": -11.13, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.6, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nRetime the operand-independent PC arithmetic from EX into the existing\ndecode-to-execute register. Add exactly two 32-bit ID/EX payload fields:\npc_plus4 = in.pc + 32'd4 and pc_relative = in.pc + decoded immediate,\nboth computed modulo 2^32 in id_stage. Retain the original PC, immediate,\ninstruction, operands, and exact predicted_next_pc. This is one\nstructural redistribution of work across an existing pipeline boundary;\nit adds no instruction stage, issue bubble, or training stage.\n\nGive both new fields the same update discipline as the winning r5s0\npayload: deterministic zero on reset, hold only on the existing backend\nstall, and otherwise capture with the rest of the instruction payload.\nFlush continues to clear every control bit and valid independently of\npayload capture. Preserve flush-versus-hold priority and the current IF\ninstruction-to-NOP behavior. Compute the sums in parallel with register\nreads and control decode; no register operand or forwarding selection\nbelongs in either sum. A held instruction must retain its own sums\neven when the live fetch data, prediction, or immediate changes.\n\nIn EX, use registered pc_relative for branch targets, direct JAL\ntargets, and the AUIPC writeback result. Use registered pc_plus4 for\nJAL/JALR link values and the default architectural next PC, including\nnot-taken branches, traps, ordinary instructions, and completed divides\nrecovering from stale predictions. Preserve full-width target bits\nuntil the existing alignment checks; never round a misaligned direct\ntarget into a legal one.\n\nRemove the is_auipc ? PC : rs1 selection from the shared ALU A input:\nordinary ALU and M-extension operations take the existing forwarded\nrs1, whi\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Retime operand dependency matching across the ID/EX boundary\", \"fitness\": 364.86, \"delta_pct\": -6.12, \"fmax_mhz\": 153.8, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nSplit forwarding into early address matching and late write qualification.\nMove only the four source/destination equality comparisons into decode\nand capture their results with the instruction in ID/EX. Preserve the\nexisting pipeline stages, instruction acceptance schedule, data bypass\nsources, and execute-stage operand muxes.\n\nFor each incoming raw source field s, compute two independent bits from\npre-edge state:\n  match_ex = (s != 0) && (s == current_id_ex.rd)\n  match_wb = (s != 0) && (s == current_ex_mem.rd)\nThe current ID/EX instruction becomes the EX/MEM producer when the\nconsumer is accepted; the current EX/MEM instruction becomes its MEM/WB\nproducer. Use the same IF instruction source fields as today's decode,\nincluding the existing NOP substitution. Compare both fields even for\ninstructions that do not architecturally consume both, preserving the\nexisting operand/RVFI convention and load-use detector behavior.\n\nAdd exactly four match bits to the ID/EX payload. Reset them to zero;\notherwise capture whenever the existing ID/EX payload captures, and hold\nwhenever stall_id holds that payload. Follow the winning r5s0 separation:\nflush clears all controls and valid, without adding flush to these\npayload registers' reset or enable. Matches captured in a bubble are\ninert. A later accepted instruction always receives newly computed bits.\n\nIn the following execute cycle, select EX/MEM when its registered match\nbit and current EX/MEM ctrl.reg_write are both true. Otherwise select\nMEM/WB when its independent registered match bit and current MEM/WB\nctrl.reg_write are true; otherwise use the original ID/EX operand value.\nKeep the existing 00/01/10 selector encoding and EX/MEM-o\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Execute multiply-high in MEM outside the fast EX result path\", \"fitness\": 318.3, \"delta_pct\": -18.1, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.17, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nMove execution of MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU from EX into a dedicated\ncombinational multiply-high lane in MEM. Keep low-half MUL and all\nordinary integer operations in EX. This is one execution-stage\nrepartition: high products become available at MEM/WB instead of\nEX/MEM, without adding an instruction stage or changing retirement\nwidth. Remove high-product arithmetic and result arms from the fast\nALU rather than retaining an unused fast completion path.\n\nAdd one is_mul_high control bit, derived in ID only from a legal\ndecoded MULH/MULHU/MULHSU. Capture it with the existing control bundle;\nreset and every bubble clear it, and backend holds retain it. In EX,\nkeep operand forwarding and capture the existing post-forward rs1_val\nand rs2_val into EX/MEM. These existing fields become the high lane's\narithmetic operands as well as its RVFI operands. The EX ALU may emit\nzero for these three internal operation codes, but that placeholder\nmust never be forwarded, written architecturally, or reported as the\nretiring result. Keep the original instruction, operation, destination,\nprediction, resolved pc_next, and trap metadata paired with operands.\n\nCompute the selected high product only from the registered EX/MEM\noperands and operation. Move the current signed/signed,\nunsigned/unsigned, and signed/unsigned expressions and their exact\nhigh-half slices into the new lane without an arithmetic redesign.\nOn the normal MEM/WB capture edge, select this result into alu_result\nfor is_mul_high; other instructions retain the existing MEM behavior.\nWB, register-file write-first bypass, and RVFI then receive the final\nregistered result through their current paths. Do not forward the\ncombinational ME\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch from decode with one fall-through instruction slot\", \"fitness\": 211.91, \"delta_pct\": -45.48, \"fmax_mhz\": 85.53, \"cycles\": 4036238, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nAdd exactly one instruction holding slot inside if_stage, with a\ncombinational fall-through path when empty. Store only the complete\nif_id_t fetch tuple: PC, instruction, exact predicted_next_pc, and validity.\nRead registers and decode at actual ID acceptance, never at enqueue time.\nKeep the existing ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB stages and single retirement.\n\nSeparate the two acceptance events. Let q_valid denote slot occupancy,\navailable = q_valid || io_imemReady, and issue_fire mean available and\ndecode can accept, with neither reset nor recovery active. Decode can\naccept only when the existing load-use interlock, data-memory hold, and\ndivide wait allow it. Let fetch_fire = io_imemReady && (!q_valid ||\nissue_fire), also suppressed by reset or recovery. Only fetch_fire advances\nthe fetch PC to the raw fetch's prediction. Decode always selects the\nstored tuple when q_valid is set, otherwise the live fetch tuple.\n\nWhen empty, fetch_fire plus issue_fire transfers directly to ID without\noccupying the slot or adding a cycle. When empty and decode is blocked,\nfetch_fire saves the tuple and advances the fetch PC once. When occupied,\nissue_fire consumes the stored instruction even if io_imemReady is zero;\nsimultaneous fetch_fire replaces it with the next raw fetch. An occupied\nslot with no issue_fire retains every field and holds the fetch PC. Support\nconsume/refill on the same edge so ready buses still sustain one issue per\ncycle. Neither an unready response nor an unaccepted repeated response can\noverwrite the slot. Keep availability independent of downstream flushes\nand acceptance so this control has no combinational ready/valid loop.\n\nDrive hazard detection from the selected ol\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Move the complete multiply family into one shared MEM execution lane\", \"fitness\": 349.63, \"delta_pct\": -10.04, \"fmax_mhz\": 148.61, \"cycles\": 4250523, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nExecute MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU together in one combinational\nmultiplier lane in MEM, using the existing EX/MEM registered,\npost-forward rs1_val and rs2_val. EX forwards and captures multiply\noperands; MEM performs multiplication and captures the final 32-bit\nresult into MEM/WB. Ordinary integer operations remain in EX. This is\none structural relocation of the entire multiplication resource, with\nno added instruction stage, retirement channel, or predictor change.\n\nMove all four real multiply result arms and their shared product\nexpressions out of the production EX ALU into rtl/mul_unit.sv. Preserve\nthe existing signed/signed, unsigned/unsigned, and signed/unsigned\narithmetic and low/high slice semantics, including the shared unsigned\nproduct for MUL and MULHU. Keep every expression on the same registered\noperand pair and in the same lane so synthesis can retain the incoming\ndesign's sharing. Do not retain a separate EX MUL-low instance or add\nanother multiplier algorithm. Inspect the mapped design: the intended\nresource result is one shared MULT36X36, rather than r9s1's two. Treat\nsharing as something to verify, not a guaranteed inference from RTL.\n\nAdd a legal is_mul control bit in ID, covering all four multiply\noperations. Reset and bubbles clear it with all other controls, while\nbackend holds retain it. EX still captures the exact instruction,\ndestination, operands, resolved next PC, and trap metadata. Its unused\nmultiply alu_result may be zero; this placeholder must never supply an\narchitectural result or override a correct forwarding source. In MEM,\nselect the lane result into MEM/WB.alu_result for is_mul and retain the\nordinary memory and nonmultiply paths for\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Rebuild the integer register file as replicated distributed RAM\", \"fitness\": 365.32, \"delta_pct\": -6.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 153.99, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"outcome\": \"regression\"}\nImplement the 32x32, two-read/one-write integer register file using two\nidentical data copies, one per asynchronous read port. Organize each copy\nas two 16x32 banks selected by address bit 4, with bits 3:0 addressing a\nbank. Broadcast each accepted nonzero-register write to the corresponding\nbank in both copies. Use synthesizable behavioral memory arrays with\ndistributed-RAM inference attributes; their clocked processes contain\nwrites only, with no data-array reset loop, scrub sequence, or initial\ncontents requirement. This organization is intended to map to roughly\n32 RAM16SDP4 cells before optimization. Do not instantiate vendor-only\nsimulation black boxes or introduce different formal/simulation hardware.\n\nAdd a shared written-since-reset bitmap. A synchronous reset clears the\nbitmap and suppresses physical writes on that edge. A non-reset write to\nx1..x31 writes both copies and sets its bitmap bit on the same edge. Leave\nx0 permanently invalid and unwritable. Reads select zero for x0, then the\nexisting live write-first bypass on a matching nonzero address, then the\nselected bank's data if its bitmap bit is set, otherwise zero. In\nparticular, bypass takes priority over an unset bitmap bit. Preserve the\nexisting combinational bypass even while reset is asserted: reset wins\nat the storage edge, but does not itself gate the live read mux. When\nbypass is removed after a reset edge, every unwritten register reads zero.\nUninitialized or pre-reset RAM contents must never affect a visible read.\n\nKeep reg_file's ports, synchronous write/reset semantics, two independent\nzero-cycle reads, x0 behavior, and same-cycle WB visibility exact. Keep\ninstruction stages, hazards, forwardin\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-001-r5s0\n\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-002-r10s1\n# One fall-through fetch slot\n\nAll correctness and FPGA gates passed. The measured hypothesis regressed fitness: scored 2 KB CoreMark fell from 388.659115376458 to 211.905244438014 iterations/s (-45.48%), despite 4.25% fewer bracketed cycles, because median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 85.53 MHz.\n\nImplemented the hypothesis in bench RTL only. The frontend now holds exactly one `if_id_t` tuple: PC, instruction, the prediction used to advance the fetch PC, and validity. An empty slot bypasses directly into ID. Fetch and issue have independent acceptance events, including capture during load-use/memory/divide waits, issue with an unready instruction bus, and atomic consume/refill. Register operands are still captured by the unchanged ID stage at issue.\n\n`available` is the unflushed OR of slot occupancy and instruction-bus readiness. The hazard unit uses this availability and the oldest selected instruction's raw source fields. EX recovery cancels both transfers, invalidates the slot, substitutes a decoding NOP, and redirects regardless of instruction-bus readiness or occupancy. Only validity is reset/flushed; the 96 payload bits have an ordinary capture/refill enable with no reset or recovery input.\n\nA legal FENCE.I uses the existing advancement-qualified EX recovery mechanism even when its saved prediction equals PC+4. This discards younger work after an older stalled memory operation completes and refetches PC+4. Its existing legal NOP result and single retirement are preserved. Decoder legality and ordinary FENCE are unchanged.\n\nThe sixteen full-tag predictor entries, counter policy, private row event capture, target commit timing, and advancement-only training remain unchanged. ID/EX control-versus-payload reset separation, EX/MEM, MEM/WB forwarding retention, real/ALTOPS arithmetic, the eight-edge divider, public ports, `nret: 1`, and channel-0 retirement wiring are unchanged.\n\n## Implementation choices and scope\n\n- Added two narrow unflushed source-address outputs to IF for hazard detection. Using the recovery-substituted ID instruction for these comparisons would indirectly put recovery into the slot's wide enable through `stall_if`; these outputs remove that path. ID still receives the prescribed NOP substitution.\n- Kept the existing internal `stall`/`stall_if` names, now describing issue blocking. An empty fetch slot can fill while they are asserted.\n- Inert payload can update on empty bypass or cancellation; validity alone determines whether it is retained work. Full occupied holds preserve the entire tuple.\n- Gate sources and the incoming RTL were copied unchanged beneath the supplied TMPDIR. Generated artifacts stayed there. The delivered RTL and tests were checked byte-for-byte against the final gate inputs. Protected gate and benchmark sources were also checked unchanged. This notes file uses the explicitly requested current-directory location.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings.\n- Unchanged pytest/cocotb suite with extended permitted tests: PASS, 9 pytest cases / 148 cocotb tests, including 27 real and 27 ALTOPS ALU tests, 43 pipeline tests, six frontend tests, and the exhaustive hazard truth table.\n- Formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness, on the delivered RTL. Both local verification runs passed. No formal counterexample or RTL fix after a formal failure occurred (zero formal fix attempts).\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench`: PASS, selftest ISS and scored CoreMark validation.\n- Additional unchanged ISS comparison under both bus stalls: PASS, 115 retirements each for seeds `0xDEADBEEF`, `0x16C0FFEE`, and `0xF37C4`.\n- Canonical 6000-byte, `-O3`, ten-iteration CoreMark: PASS for incoming and delivered RTL with both bus stalls. Accepted start/stop markers, `oob=false`, and `Correct operation validated.` were required. CRCs: seed `8a02`, list `d4b0`, matrix `be52`, state `5e47`, final `5949`.\n- Scored 2000-byte, `-O3`, ten-iteration CoreMark: PASS for both implementations. CRCs: `e9f5/e714/1fd7/8e3a/fcaf`.\n- Synthesized slot audit: 97 source bits; all 96 payload bits are plain DFFE registers sharing one enable. No reset, EX PC, or EX saved-prediction input reaches that enable's combinational leaf cone.\n\nThe extended scoreboards account for every accepted fetch as issued, discarded, or still occupying the single slot. They check saved tuple stability on every occupied edge, prediction policy before/after commits, unique EX resolutions, ordered exactly-once RVFI, and physical read/store handshakes. Directed cases cover differing queued/live source fields, a register write after enqueue, divides, reset, full-slot recovery with imem unready, wrong-path stores/divides, ready-bus throughput, and instruction-memory changes at an older store's acceptance before FENCE.I recovery.\n\nThe three deterministic random pipeline runs recorded 55 queued issues with imem unready, 27 captures during load-use, five during memory holds, 26 during divide waits, three recovery discards, and 223 consume/refills. Capture categories can overlap. These are unit-test coverage counts, not CoreMark instrumentation. Ready-bus straight-line code retained consecutive retirements starting on cycle 2.\n\nAn initial test assertion incorrectly required every load-use wait to begin empty. It was corrected to require capture exactly when the slot is empty and retention when full. An initial direct `make fpga` invocation selected the default dual-retirement wrapper; subsequent invocations explicitly selected the unchanged single-retirement wrapper with `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv` and `NRET=1`. A preliminary FPGA run was stopped after the final hazard-source separation; its timing is excluded.\n\n## Workload comparison\n\nBoth implementations ran each identical ELF with `--bench --istall --dstall --seed 3735928559` and a 50,000,000-cycle ceiling. Brackets below use accepted physical MMIO start/stop handshakes, excluding initialization and output. The final hazard-source separation preserved the complete benchmark output and these cycle counts.\n\n| Workload | Incoming start | Incoming stop | Incoming bracket | Candidate start | Candidate stop | Candidate bracket |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Scored 2 KB | 15,229 | 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 14,657 | 4,050,895 | 4,036,238 |\n| Canonical 6 KB | 53,747 | 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 51,485 | 29,763,181 | 29,711,696 |\n\nBoth designs retired 2,937,037 instructions for the scored workload and 22,093,920 for the canonical workload. Cycle savings are 179,024 and 1,111,094 respectively. Fitness must use `10 / exact_bracketed_cycles * median_Fmax_Hz`, not rounded IPC or total elapsed cycles. The historical 388.66 score belongs to the 2 KB workload and is kept separate from canonical 6 KB validation.\n\nELF SHA256:\n\n- 2 KB: `e66a95e5465b8e8bbf101f05d12a1486e669d45f25423c2261be4c0c0061514e`\n- 6 KB: `188f31354709614455809c9a0caf07305ce70db072fd87e20691d255fadad91b`\n\nBoth were built using the existing Makefile's size/optimization overrides. The unchanged UART compiler-flags string says `-O2`; the actual compiler commands use `-O3`. The 6 KB UART size is the per-algorithm allocation, while the compiler command sets `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000` and the canonical CRCs confirm the workload.\n\n## FPGA measurement and concerns\n\nThe unchanged FPGA gate passed all three routed seeds for both designs. The incoming winner was rebuilt and measured live, reproducing the historical 388.66 result. The cycle savings do not compensate for the Fmax loss.\n\n| Metric | Incoming winner | Delivered candidate |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 163.83 | 85.53 |\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 155.33 | 86.41 |\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 168.55 | 79.31 |\n| Median Fmax, Hz | 163,830,000 | 85,530,000 |\n| LUT4 | 6,618 | 6,775 |\n| FF | 3,198 | 3,299 |\n| Scored 2 KB iterations/s | 388.659115376458 | 211.905244438014 |\n| Separate canonical 6 KB iterations/s | 53.152229243362 | 28.786643482082 |\n\nMedian Fmax decreased 47.79%; LUT4 increased 2.37% and FF increased 3.16%. Canonical 6 KB cycles decreased 3.60%, but its separately calculated fitness decreased 45.84%. These calculations use the exact bracket lengths and median frequency in Hz.\n\nAll final critical paths originate in EX instruction opcode/funct3 bits used by FENCE.I detection and return through recovery/NOP substitution to ID. Seeds 1 and 2 terminate at ID/EX `rs1_val[14]` and `rs1_val[13]`; seed 3 terminates at ID/EX `ctrl.is_illegal`. This recovery-to-decode path is the measured timing concern; separating the slot payload enable did not remove the NOP-substitution data path required by the hypothesis.\n\n| Seed | Logic delay, ns | Routing delay, ns | Total, ns |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 5.40 | 6.29 | 11.69 |\n| 2 | 5.40 | 6.17 | 11.57 |\n| 3 | 6.40 | 6.21 | 12.61 |\n\nThe added tuple mux, changed placement/fanout, increased prediction age, and discarded prefetches remain broader concerns. The measurements do not isolate every contribution to the regression. Power was not measured. The four-percent improvement estimate is rejected by this measurement; the implementation is left intact for the orchestrator to evaluate.\n\nTools: pinned Verilator 5.047 devel `v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6`, Yosys 0.64+149 `2dc69a757-dirty`, nextpnr `0.10-45-g98c18d7f`, existing RISC-V GCC, and Conda Python dependencies. FPGA device, constraints, wrappers, and seeds 1/2/3 are unchanged.\n\n## Evidence\n\nArtifacts: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/fetch-slot-r10s1/`.\n\nFinal evidence includes `unit-delivered.log`, `formal-final.log`, `cosim-final.log`, `stalled-iss-final.json`, `fpga-final.log`, `incoming-fpga.log`, `workload-results.json`, `measurement-summary.json`, `slot-netlist-audit.json`, `critical-paths.json`, `critical-path-seed*.txt`, the four `incoming-*` / `final-*` benchmark outputs, `canonical-build.log`, and all three routed reports beneath each design's `cores/bench/generate\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-001-r10s0\n\n\nDETAIL hyp-20260905-002-r11s1\nImplemented the complete MEM multiply lane. Correctness gates pass, but the measured hypothesis regresses fitness: the scored 2 KB result is 349.627563 iterations/s versus the incoming 388.659115 (-10.042618%). The proposed +4% gain was an unmeasured, low-confidence estimate and was not achieved.\n\nChanges made:\n\n- Added `cores/bench/rtl/mul_unit.sv` with all four original real multiply expressions and result slices. MUL/MULHU share the unsigned product; MULH and MULHSU retain the original signed extensions and high-half semantics. All four original ALTOPS formulas moved with them.\n- Removed every product expression and multiply result arm from the production EX ALU. Multiply opcodes now select its zero default.\n- Added `ctrl_t.is_mul`, classified in ID only for valid, legal decoded multiplies. The existing control reset/flush and payload hold separation remains intact.\n- MEM consumes the EX/MEM registered post-forward operand pair and operation, and captures the selected multiply result in MEM/WB.alu_result. WB and channel-0 RVFI use the existing registered-result mux.\n- Generalized the historical `id_ex_mem_read` hazard input to `mem_read | is_mul`, preserving its name, every hazard equation, both raw fetched source-field comparisons, and the nonzero-rd requirement. Masked only multiply EX/MEM forwarding eligibility. Retained MEM/WB forwarding remains independent of retirement-valid.\n- Kept the divider, EX advancement/recovery, predictor, decoder, external ports, nret=1, memory handling, and retirement order unchanged. No live MEM-product bypass, extra stage, multiplier algorithm, or EX MUL-low instance was added.\n\nTests preserve all prior assertions and vectors. The ALU test uses a temporary wrapper to exercise the unchanged integer/divider ALU and the relocated multiply lane together; all 185 boundary/random pairs per M operation and explicit expected results remain covered in real and ALTOPS builds. Integrated retirement also exercises all 740 multiply boundary/random vectors, the nine original explicit multiply expectations, and seven MUL vectors used while the divider is busy. Added coverage includes all four variants, immediate and one-gap consumers, both raw unused-source aliases, consecutive independent multiplication, repeated rd, x0, branches/JALR, store base/data, loads, multiply/divide chains, retained WB forwarding after valid clears, older memory holds, separate and simultaneous bus stalls, exactly-once markers, reset, illegal encodings, wrong-path multiplies, and stale prediction recovery with and without instruction-bus backpressure.\n\nAdded opt-in `test_mul_profile.py` to measure existing CoreMark ELFs without modifying the cosim driver. It generates a temporary C++ subclass that observes settled internal signals and retirement while the unchanged driver supplies instructions, memory, and its original bus-ready sequence. Its observed marker cycles match the uninstrumented binaries for every comparison below. A qualified-name filter and explicit executed-test count prevent an accidentally empty ALTOPS pipeline run; an initial unqualified filter was corrected before the final suite.\n\nLocal validation:\n\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS without warnings. The same lint with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`: PASS without warnings.\n- Final full pytest/cocotb suite: **10 passed, 1 skipped**. The skip is the opt-in CoreMark profiler when no ELFs are supplied; it was separately enabled and passed for both incoming and new RTL. The ALTOPS pipeline runner explicitly requires all eight new multiply tests to execute. Integer, divide, decode, predictor, and other existing tests still run in the normal suite.\n- Local formal: **PASS on the first RTL attempt, 53 passed / 0 failed**, including `liveness_ch0`, instruction checks, reg, causal, unique, ill, and cover. No RTL formal fix attempts or counterexamples. Used unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`, which autodetected nret=1 and selected the unchanged single-issue wrapper/configuration.\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim`: PASS, two ELFs (selftest and scored 2 KB CoreMark).\n- Additional stalled ISS comparison through the unchanged `run_cosim.compare`: selftest passed at bus seeds 0xdeadbeef, 1, and 2 (115 retirements each). A generated arithmetic ELF covering all 185 pairs for each of the eight M operations plus immediate consumers passed at the same three bus seeds (3,701 retirements each).\n- Unchanged synthesis and three-seed FPGA evaluator: PASS placement/routing on all seeds. LUT4=6,628; FF=3,209. The gate uses its existing scored 2 KB workload and CRC contract.\n- Canonical requested 6 KB workload: separately built with the unchanged benchmark Makefile using `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, ITERATIONS=10. Both incoming and new binaries passed at all three bus seeds with both stalls, no OOB accesses, exactly one accepted start and stop, d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Seed CRC=8a02 and final CRC=5949. Full real-arithmetic SMT (`formal-deep`) was not run; real arithmetic was checked by lane tests, integrated retirement, and stalled ISS cosim.\n\nResource and stage inspection:\n\n- Mapped design contains exactly **one MULT36X36**, named `cpu.u_mem.u_mul.mul_uu_MULT36X36_DOUT`; no EX multiplier remains.\n- A reverse traversal of the mapped multiplier input cones reaches only EX/MEM flops: rs1, rs2, and the registered multiply operation/control. Yosys merged the identical EX/MEM rs2_val and write_data flops; their mapped bit vectors were verified identical.\n- A forward traversal from every DSP product output, stopping at the first sequential boundary, reaches exactly the 32 MEM/WB.alu_result D inputs and no other register input. There is no combinational product path back into EX.\n- All three final routed critical paths are predictor maintenance paths, not multiply paths:\n\n| P&R seed | Fmax MHz | Routed path | Delay | Logic / routing |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | 142.07 | predictor row 11 valid_q Q through row-hit/update selection to valid_q CE | 7.04 ns | 3.04 / 4.00 ns |\n| 2 | 148.61 | predictor row 11 tag_q[15] Q through row-hit/update selection to counter_q CE | 6.73 ns | 2.60 / 4.13 ns |\n| 3 | 166.42 | predictor row 11 tag_q[0] Q through row-hit/update selection to counter_q CE | 6.01 ns | 2.62 / 3.39 ns |\n\nThe median is **148.61 MHz**, versus the incoming recorded median **163.83 MHz**. Independent multiplies still retire on consecutive ready-bus clocks; an immediate raw dependency produces exactly one ready-bus bubble in both real and ALTOPS tests. A multiply in MEM advances even with dmemReady held low.\n\nMeasurements use the exact same ELF for incoming/new RTL and identical bus seeds. The incoming RTL snapshot is worktree HEAD `301f10e8a5d955c1788b2bb37fb5a091a343291e`. Incoming cycle measurements reproduce the scored winner's 4,215,262 cycles. The old 163.83 MHz median is the recorded winner value, not a new P&R rerun.\n\n| Working set | Bus seed | Incoming bracket cycles | New bracket cycles | Net added cycles | New multiply interlock bubbles |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 2 KB | 0xdeadbeef | 4,215,262 | 4,250,523 | 35,261 | 27,927 |\n| 2 KB | 1 | 4,214,768 | 4,250,288 | 35,520 | 27,897 |\n| 2 KB | 2 | 4,214,802 | 4,250,870 | 36,068 | 27,771 |\n| 6 KB | 0xdeadbeef | 30,822,790 | 31,278,680 | 455,890 | 358,641 |\n| 6 KB | 1 | 30,824,564 | 31,280,915 | 456,351 | 358,262 |\n| 6 KB | 2 | 30,827,534 | 31,283,974 | 456,440 | 358,703 |\n\nBoth RTL versions and every seed execute **93,960 bracketed MULs with 35,640 immediate raw dependencies in 2 KB**, and **1,269,000 bracketed MULs with 459,000 immediate raw dependencies in 6 KB**. All three high multiply variants have zero bracketed executions. Incoming multiply interlock bubbles are zero. Actual bubble counts differ from net cycle increases because fetch stalls can supply the dependency gap and extra cycles shift the deterministic bus-ready sequence. No operand-use filtering was introduced.\n\nUsing exact `10 / bracketed_cycles * median_Fmax_Hz`, for default bus seed 0xdeadbeef:\n\n| Workload | Incoming iterations/s | New iterations/s | Fitness delta | cycles_new / cycles_old |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Scored 2 KB, -O3, ten iterations | 388.659115 | 349.627563 | -10.042618% | 1.008365079 |\n| Required 6 KB, -O3, ten iterations | 53.152229 | 47.511596 | -10.612224% | 1.014790679 |\n\n`Fmax_new/Fmax_old = 0.907098822`, which fails the success inequality for both workloads. At unchanged incoming Fmax, break-even would require the new core to exceed 165.200451 MHz for 2 KB or 166.253157 MHz for 6 KB. Seed 3 alone does not determine acceptance; the three-seed median regresses.\n\nDefault accepted marker cycles (new): 2 KB start=15,229 / stop=4,265,752; 6 KB start=53,747 / stop=31,332,427. Incoming markers: 2 KB 15,229 / 4,230,491; 6 KB 53,747 / 30,876,537. Only these brackets were used for fitness.\n\nELF SHA256 values:\n\n- 2 KB: `c1d5108f203990204c9d1bf6ddb0664662d1e82ea29b76685395d64947f30c76`\n- 6 KB: `731b5d954ca847110437a2a8fde37bee6290eb63735f58405efd34b24bc3aabf`\n\nThe protected CoreMark port reports a hardcoded `-O2` UART label; the actual captured compiler commands use `-O3` for both ELFs. That source and the evaluator's 2 KB CRC table were left unchanged. The separate 6 KB validation enforces its own required CRCs without changing the scored gate.\n\nAll generated gate copies, logs, ELFs, netlists, routes, and profiles are under `$TMPDIR/mem-mul-r11s1-a_3c0e7f/`; no contract-side workspace sources were edited. Useful artifacts there: `formal-gate.stdout`, `fpga-gate.json`, `cosim-gate.json`, `stalled-selftest.json`, `stalled-m-family.json`, `mapped-multiply-report.json`, `measurement-summary.json`, `coremark-comparison.json`, `profile-new.json`, `profile-incoming.json`, `elf-build.stdout`, `elf6000-build.stdout`, and `cores/bench/ge\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
