{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'structural|r13s1|r14s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Single pending-store slot with direct zero-wait completion\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"In cores/bench, hazard_unit freezes IF, ID, and EX whenever a memory\\ninstruction in EX/MEM encounters io_dmemReady=0. An aligned RAM store\\nalready has its address, data, and byte enables at this point, so retaining\\nthose fields separately can release the pipeline to execute younger\\narithmetic while the write waits for the bus. This directly targets the\\napproximately 22% random data-bus backpressure used for CoreMark fitness.\\n\\nBench has no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the baseline:\\n4,491,485 timed cycles and 127.03 MHz median Fmax, yielding 282.82 iter/s.\\nRead-only reference evidence from cores/v1, hyp-20260428-017-r8s1, supports\\na single pending store with a direct ready path: it passed formal/cosim\\nand improved fitness 5.89% on that different design. Its cycle saving was\\nunder 1%, and placement contributed to the result, so that improvement\\nmust not be assumed for bench. The main risk is losing more Fmax to bus\\narbitration than the saved stall cycles recover.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"In cores/bench, hazard_unit freezes IF, ID, and EX whenever a memory\\ninstruction in EX/MEM encounters io_dmemReady=0. An aligned RAM store\\nalready has its address, data, and byte enables at this point, so retaining\\nthose fields separately can release the pipeline to execute younger\\narithmetic while the write waits for the bus. This directly targets the\\napproximately 22% random data-bus backpressure used for CoreMark fitness.\\n\\nBench has no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the baseline:\\n4,491,485 timed cycles and 127.03 MHz median Fmax, yielding 282.82 iter/s.\\nRead-only reference evidence from cores/v1, hyp-20260428-017-r8s1, supports\\na single pending store with a direct ready path: it passed formal/cosim\\nand improved fitness 5.89% on that different design. Its cycle saving was\\nunder 1%, and placement contributed to the result, so that improvement\\nmust not be assumed for bench. The main risk is losing more Fmax to bus\\narbitration than the saved stall cycles recover.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decouple store retirement from bus completion with exactly one pending\\nstore slot. Allocate it only for a valid, legal, naturally aligned store\\nwhose complete access lies in normal RAM [0x00000000, 0x00100000), when\\nthe slot is empty and io_dmemReady=0. Capture the effective byte address,\\nexisting replicated write data, and byte mask atomically. Advance that\\ninstruction into MEM/WB once, carrying its original PC, instruction,\\noperands, and aligned RVFI memory fields. Its eventual bus drain produces\\nno additional retirement. The slot holds only the physical write request;\\nRVFI metadata continues along the existing pipeline.\\n\\nWith the slot empty and io_dmemReady=1, loads and stores use the existing\\ndirect MEM path without an enqueue cycle. While the slot is valid, give\\nits request sole ownership of dmem, hold address/data/byte enables stable\\nuntil a ready handshake, and suppress any younger external request.\\nDrain independently of frontend stalls and redirects. Permit younger\\nnon-memory instructions to advance and retire in order while it drains.\\nBlock every younger load or store until the slot is empty, including the\\ncycle that drains the old request; omit simultaneous drain/refill,\\ncoalescing, and store-to-load forwarding to keep arbitration small.\\n\\nAll MMIO, UART, start/stop marker accesses, and out-of-range addresses\\nremain on the existing unbuffered path. Never use buffering to hide an\\nout-of-bounds access from the existing checks. FENCE/FENCE.I, EBREAK,\\nand trapping instructions must also wait for an older pending store to\\ndrain. Marker writes complete only on their real bus handshake, after\\nprior writes, so the timed region cannot exclude undrained work. Invalid,\\nillegal, and misaligned stores never enqueue or drive a write. Redirects\\ncannot discard an already accepted older store; reset clears slot validity.\\n\\nReplace the hazard unit's raw dmem-stall predicate with a MEM-generated\\nmem_wait signal describing whether the current EX/MEM instruction can\\nadvance. A pending store alone must not assert mem_wait for arithmetic.\\nOn a real wait, preserve the existing EX/MEM and ID/EX holds and the\\nMEM/WB behavior that clears valid but retains forwarding data. Derive\\nmem_wait from current registered state, instruction classification, and\\nbus readiness, without a combinational dependency on hold_wb. Keep the\\ndirect zero-wait path short and retain the existing EX datapath and\\nload-use interlock. Preserve nret=1, all top-level ports, strict channel-0\\nretirement order, decoder legality, and every existing correctness gate.\\n\\nSuccess requires a measured fitness improvement over 282.82 using the\\nunchanged marker-bracketed CoreMark run and three-seed median Fmax. The\\nestimate is a 2% fitness gain from overlapping store waits with useful\\nexecution while keeping Fmax close to baseline; it is not a measured\\nresult. Verify the pending path with directed and random bus stalls,\\nsince a ready-tied-high formal configuration cannot exercise allocation.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry request register with valid, enqueue, address, data,\\nmask, and ready/drain controls. Retain its payload until one accepted\\nwrite, disallow replacement while occupied, and clear valid on reset.\\nDo not retain a second copy of the RVFI retirement bundle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept dmem readiness and pending-slot state. Classify valid aligned\\nRAM stores for enqueue, retain direct ready completion, and emit\\nexplicit mem_wait plus slot enqueue fields. Enqueue and advance the\\noriginal store into MEM/WB on the same edge. Serialize subsequent\\nmemory operations, fences, EBREAK, and traps behind the slot. Keep\\neach live instruction's RVFI masks/data separate from the draining\\nrequest and retain the forwarding payload when MEM/WB valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the slot, route enqueue and mem_wait controls, and give\\nthe pending request priority over the live MEM dmem outputs only\\nwhile slot-valid is set. Suppress live read/write enables during a\\ndrain. Preserve the core's existing single-channel RVFI and bus ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use mem_wait instead of !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op to drive the\\nexisting backend hold controls. Preserve load-use, instruction-bus,\\nredirect priorities, and MEM/WB forwarding retention, while allowing\\narithmetic to progress when only a buffered store is waiting.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Include store_slot.sv in the pipeline source list. Add a handshake-\\naccurate stalled-bus test mode without removing existing assertions.\\nCheck SW/SH/SB enqueue and drain, stable requests, one physical write\\nand one RVFI retirement per store, and arithmetic progress during a\\nprolonged store wait. Cover subsequent same/different-address loads,\\nconsecutive stores, load-use forwarding across a hold, imem stalls,\\nredirects, fences, EBREAK, reset, illegal/misaligned stores, and UART\\nand timing markers behind a pending store. Assert strict RVFI order,\\ncorrect byte data, and that serialization prevents premature markers.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/store_slot.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry request register with valid, enqueue, address, data,\\nmask, and ready/drain controls. Retain its payload until one accepted\\nwrite, disallow replacement while occupied, and clear valid on reset.\\nDo not retain a second copy of the RVFI retirement bundle.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept dmem readiness and pending-slot state. Classify valid aligned\\nRAM stores for enqueue, retain direct ready completion, and emit\\nexplicit mem_wait plus slot enqueue fields. Enqueue and advance the\\noriginal store into MEM/WB on the same edge. Serialize subsequent\\nmemory operations, fences, EBREAK, and traps behind the slot. Keep\\neach live instruction's RVFI masks/data separate from the draining\\nrequest and retain the forwarding payload when MEM/WB valid is cleared.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate the slot, route enqueue and mem_wait controls, and give\\nthe pending request priority over the live MEM dmem outputs only\\nwhile slot-valid is set. Suppress live read/write enables during a\\ndrain. Preserve the core's existing single-channel RVFI and bus ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use mem_wait instead of !dmem_ready && ex_mem_mem_op to drive the\\nexisting backend hold controls. Preserve load-use, instruction-bus,\\nredirect priorities, and MEM/WB forwarding retention, while allowing\\narithmetic to progress when only a buffered store is waiting.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Include store_slot.sv in the pipeline source list. Add a handshake-\\naccurate stalled-bus test mode without removing existing assertions.\\nCheck SW/SH/SB enqueue and drain, stable requests, one physical write\\nand one RVFI retirement per store, and arithmetic progress during a\\nprolonged store wait. Cover subsequent same/different-address loads,\\nconsecutive stores, load-use forwarding across a hold, imem stalls,\\nredirects, fences, EBREAK, reset, illegal/misaligned stores, and UART\\nand timing markers behind a pending store. Assert strict RVFI order,\\ncorrect byte data, and that serialization prevents premature markers.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 300.35, \"delta_pct\": 6.2, \"vs_baseline\": 6.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.89, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4457730, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10274, \"ff\": 1869, \"seeds\": [133.89, 139.94, 124.38], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T14:03:28.213389Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 (regression, +6.20%): With `mem_wait` blocking younger loads/stores through the pending store's drain cycle, CoreMark cycles fell only 0.75%, so the fitness gain came mainly from higher median Fmax (127.03 to 133.89 MHz).\"}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Split execution into a fast ALU and an eight-cycle divider\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench ALU places combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware behind the\\nsame forwarded operands and result selection as ordinary integer work.\\nSharing a bounded iterative execution resource for these four operations\\ncan reduce arithmetic depth, LUT use, and routing pressure without adding\\nstages to RV32I instructions or multiplication. The current winner scores\\n319.45 iter/s at 134.68 MHz and uses 10645 LUT4s. Divider removal is a\\nplausible timing opportunity, not a confirmed current critical path.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the bench structural attempt hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 show\\nonly 0.75% fewer CoreMark cycles from store buffering; most of that\\ncandidate's fitness gain came from Fmax. The adopted target predictor\\nalready reduces control-transfer bubbles. A dedicated multicycle divide\\nexecution path therefore offers a more substantial new structural bet.\\n\\nRead-only reference hyp-20260428-010-r6s0 in cores/v1 successfully removed\\ncombinational division, reporting 159.92 MHz and 5888 LUT4s on that\\ndifferent core. Its 32-step hardware divider used shorter ALTOPS timing,\\nso its formal result does not establish this proposal's control timing.\\nAn earlier reference attempt failed selftest cosim, making operand capture\\nand exactly-once completion essential. Bench has no recorded divider-split\\nattempt. Low timed-region DIV/REM traffic is an expectation to verify,\\nrather than a reason to omit those instructions or arithmetic checks.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench ALU places combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU hardware behind the\\nsame forwarded operands and result selection as ordinary integer work.\\nSharing a bounded iterative execution resource for these four operations\\ncan reduce arithmetic depth, LUT use, and routing pressure without adding\\nstages to RV32I instructions or multiplication. The current winner scores\\n319.45 iter/s at 134.68 MHz and uses 10645 LUT4s. Divider removal is a\\nplausible timing opportunity, not a confirmed current critical path.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the bench structural attempt hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 show\\nonly 0.75% fewer CoreMark cycles from store buffering; most of that\\ncandidate's fitness gain came from Fmax. The adopted target predictor\\nalready reduces control-transfer bubbles. A dedicated multicycle divide\\nexecution path therefore offers a more substantial new structural bet.\\n\\nRead-only reference hyp-20260428-010-r6s0 in cores/v1 successfully removed\\ncombinational division, reporting 159.92 MHz and 5888 LUT4s on that\\ndifferent core. Its 32-step hardware divider used shorter ALTOPS timing,\\nso its formal result does not establish this proposal's control timing.\\nAn earlier reference attempt failed selftest cosim, making operand capture\\nand exactly-once completion essential. Bench has no recorded divider-split\\nattempt. Low timed-region DIV/REM traffic is an expectation to verify,\\nrather than a reason to omit those instructions or arithmetic checks.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Partition execution into the existing one-cycle integer/multiply datapath\\nand one blocking, eight-cycle divide/remainder engine. Only legal valid\\nDIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU instructions enter the engine. Retain the current\\npipeline depth and forwarding for every other instruction, including all\\nfour multiply variants. This changes execution-resource scheduling and\\npipeline advancement; it does not add another retirement channel.\\n\\nImplement unsigned restoring division with four quotient bits computed\\nper clock, using four cascaded shift/subtract/select steps and one shared\\nremainder/quotient state. Perform the first four steps on the accepting\\nlaunch edge and the remaining seven groups on the next seven edges.\\nNormalize signed operands on launch and include quotient/remainder sign\\ncorrection in the final result capture. Do not add separate normalization\\nor correction cycles. Capture the original operands and operation at\\nlaunch; changing live forwarding values afterward cannot affect the\\noperation. Preserve truncation toward zero, remainder sign matching the\\ndividend, quotient all-ones on division by zero, original dividend on\\nremainder by zero, and INT_MIN/-1 quotient INT_MIN with remainder zero.\\nSpecial cases use the same eight-edge schedule. No real / or % operator\\nremains in the synthesized execution datapath.\\n\\nUse request acceptance and a retained result-valid/result-ready handshake.\\nLaunch once when a valid legal divide is in ID/EX, the engine is idle,\\nand an older EX/MEM memory instruction is not blocking advancement.\\nFreeze the fetch PC and ID/EX while the divide is incomplete. Retain PC,\\ninstruction, destination, control, and exact predicted_next_pc in ID/EX;\\nlatch both post-forwarded source values separately for arithmetic and\\nRVFI, since older WB values can disappear while the divider runs.\\n\\nSeparate the divider wait from the existing data-memory hold. While\\ndividing, let older EX/MEM and MEM/WB instructions drain once, then put\\ncomplete bubbles into EX/MEM, clearing valid and all write/memory control\\nbits. Merely holding the previous EX/MEM instruction or clearing valid\\nalone is insufficient because existing bus/forwarding logic also reads\\nits control fields. A real data-bus stall still holds EX/MEM and retains\\nMEM/WB forwarding payload with valid cleared, exactly as today. Starting\\nor accepting a divide result must never override that older memory hold.\\n\\nOnce the result is valid and EX/MEM can accept it, advance the divide\\nexactly once with its saved operands and original instruction metadata,\\nand release ID/EX to the next instruction. Hold result-valid and data\\nuntil that acceptance; never relaunch the held instruction or publish a\\npartial quotient. Forward the completed result through the existing\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB routes, including immediate dependent arithmetic,\\nloads, stores, branches, JALR, and another divide. No younger instruction\\novertakes the divide or issues a memory request before it completes.\\n\\nQualify EX resolution, predictor training, and mismatch recovery by actual\\ninstruction advancement, including divide completion. A stale prediction\\non a divide still recovers to its architectural PC+4 exactly once. Keep\\nthe current predictor organization and all ordinary branch behavior.\\nReset cancels the pending operation and prevents a stale completion.\\n\\nPreserve all eight existing ALTOPS formulas. For DIV/REM, capture the\\ncorresponding existing formula result from the accepted operands and\\nrelease it using the SAME eight-cycle controller, holds, and completion\\nhandshake as real arithmetic. Do not use a shorter formal-only schedule,\\nbypass the engine in formal, or change formal depths. With the first\\ndigit group on launch and correction on the last group, ready-bus\\nexecution must satisfy the existing ten-cycle successor-retirement\\nwindow, including a divide following redirect recovery; verify this in\\nthe unchanged liveness gate. This timing bound is a design requirement.\\n\\nExpect approximately 12% higher fitness from reduced divider hardware\\nand timing pressure, with medium confidence. Four subtract steps plus\\nlaunch normalization or final correction may still limit Fmax, and any\\ntimed DIV/REM instructions add cycles. Measure both effects using the\\nunchanged bracketed, stalled CoreMark evaluation and median of three\\nFPGA seeds against 319.45; report LUT/FF use and inspect the resulting\\ncritical path. This estimate is not a measured result. Preserve nret=1,\\nevery external port and RVFI field, strict retirement order, decoder and\\ntrap rules, memory ordering and bounds, marker handshakes, canonical\\nCoreMark CRC/UART validation, and every existing correctness check.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fixed eight-cycle divider described above, computing four\\nrestoring quotient bits per edge, including the accepting edge.\\nCapture operands, signedness, result selection, and special cases;\\nregister the corrected result on the eighth edge and retain it until\\nconsumed. Use identical control latency in real and ALTOPS modes.\\nReset clears busy/result validity. Reject new requests while occupied.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational real division/remainder and integrate div_unit\\nalongside the unchanged fast ALU. Add internal-module clock, reset,\\ndivide request/acceptance, and result-valid/result-ready signals so\\nthe existing ALU test suite can exercise the actual integrated engine.\\nKeep op/a/b/out and zero-cycle behavior for ordinary ALU and all MUL\\noperations. Route valid divide results from div_unit; preserve the\\nexact ALTOPS formulas across the two arithmetic paths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Launch the engine once from post-forwarded operands and expose a\\nseparate divide-wait signal. Save both source values while ID/EX\\nretains instruction metadata. Drain older work using full EX/MEM\\nbubbles during divide waits, while respecting real memory holds.\\nPublish the completed divide only on result acceptance, with saved\\nRVFI operands, and qualify prediction resolution/training/recovery\\nby that advancement. Keep ordinary instruction timing unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the divide-wait input to fetch and ID/EX hold control without\\ntreating it as a data-memory stall or a flush. Keep EX/MEM hold and\\nMEM/WB forwarding retention governed by actual older memory waits;\\nex_stage supplies bubbles for divide waits. Preserve existing\\nload-use, instruction-ready, reset, and redirect priorities.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the separate long-operation interlock between EX and the hazard\\nunit. Preserve the complete nret=1 core port declaration and existing\\nregister-file, forwarding, memory, predictor, and RVFI connections.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Include div_unit.sv in this runner's local source list and adapt the\\nexisting divide helper to clock and handshake the integrated ALU.\\nRetain every existing exact arithmetic expectation and all multiply\\ntests; do not replace DIV/REM checks with dummy fast-path values.\\nAdd at least 30 deterministic and boundary vectors per M operation,\\nchecking signed division with an integer truncation-toward-zero\\nreference. Verify eight-edge completion, input changes after launch,\\nheld results, reset cancellation, and consecutive requests. Exercise\\nreal arithmetic and ALTOPS builds with identical control timing;\\nkeep any extra runner configuration within this permitted test file.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add div_unit.sv to the local source list. Cover divide operands from\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB, a stalled load feeding a divide, consecutive\\ndivides, rd=x0, all signed/unsigned corner cases, and immediate result\\nconsumers of each instruction class. Cover a divider after branch\\nrecovery, stale prediction at a divide PC, simultaneous imem/dmem\\nstalls, reset during execution, and stores/markers surrounding it.\\nAssert no duplicate retirement, write, request, or predictor update;\\nexact saved RVFI source values and PC chaining; strict order; and\\nbounded completion with ready buses. Preserve all existing assertions\\nand run the unchanged formal, selftest cosim, and CoreMark gates.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 12, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the fixed eight-cycle divider described above, computing four\\nrestoring quotient bits per edge, including the accepting edge.\\nCapture operands, signedness, result selection, and special cases;\\nregister the corrected result on the eighth edge and retain it until\\nconsumed. Use identical control latency in real and ALTOPS modes.\\nReset clears busy/result validity. Reject new requests while occupied.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational real division/remainder and integrate div_unit\\nalongside the unchanged fast ALU. Add internal-module clock, reset,\\ndivide request/acceptance, and result-valid/result-ready signals so\\nthe existing ALU test suite can exercise the actual integrated engine.\\nKeep op/a/b/out and zero-cycle behavior for ordinary ALU and all MUL\\noperations. Route valid divide results from div_unit; preserve the\\nexact ALTOPS formulas across the two arithmetic paths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Launch the engine once from post-forwarded operands and expose a\\nseparate divide-wait signal. Save both source values while ID/EX\\nretains instruction metadata. Drain older work using full EX/MEM\\nbubbles during divide waits, while respecting real memory holds.\\nPublish the completed divide only on result acceptance, with saved\\nRVFI operands, and qualify prediction resolution/training/recovery\\nby that advancement. Keep ordinary instruction timing unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the divide-wait input to fetch and ID/EX hold control without\\ntreating it as a data-memory stall or a flush. Keep EX/MEM hold and\\nMEM/WB forwarding retention governed by actual older memory waits;\\nex_stage supplies bubbles for divide waits. Preserve existing\\nload-use, instruction-ready, reset, and redirect priorities.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the separate long-operation interlock between EX and the hazard\\nunit. Preserve the complete nret=1 core port declaration and existing\\nregister-file, forwarding, memory, predictor, and RVFI connections.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Include div_unit.sv in this runner's local source list and adapt the\\nexisting divide helper to clock and handshake the integrated ALU.\\nRetain every existing exact arithmetic expectation and all multiply\\ntests; do not replace DIV/REM checks with dummy fast-path values.\\nAdd at least 30 deterministic and boundary vectors per M operation,\\nchecking signed division with an integer truncation-toward-zero\\nreference. Verify eight-edge completion, input changes after launch,\\nheld results, reset cancellation, and consecutive requests. Exercise\\nreal arithmetic and ALTOPS builds with identical control timing;\\nkeep any extra runner configuration within this permitted test file.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add div_unit.sv to the local source list. Cover divide operands from\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB, a stalled load feeding a divide, consecutive\\ndivides, rd=x0, all signed/unsigned corner cases, and immediate result\\nconsumers of each instruction class. Cover a divider after branch\\nrecovery, stale prediction at a divide PC, simultaneous imem/dmem\\nstalls, reset during execution, and stores/markers surrounding it.\\nAssert no duplicate retirement, write, request, or predictor update;\\nexact saved RVFI source values and PC chaining; strict order; and\\nbounded completion with ready buses. Preserve all existing assertions\\nand run the unchanged formal, selftest cosim, and CoreMark gates.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 320.28, \"delta_pct\": 0.26, \"vs_baseline\": 13.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215937, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5871, \"ff\": 2292, \"seeds\": [135.03, 144.68, 126.39], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Eight-edge divider implementation\\n\\nImplemented the proposed divider split in `cores/bench` only. The requested implementation notes are at the worktree root, as specified in instruction 5; all RTL and test changes are confined to the permitted bench files.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- Added `rtl/div_unit.sv`: one restoring remainder/quotient state, four cascaded shift/subtract/select steps per edge, first group on request acceptance, and corrected registered result on edge eight. Signed normalization happens on launch. Divide by zero, signed overflow, and remainder signs use the same schedule as ordinary operands.\\n- Integrated the engine into `alu.sv` with request-ready and retained result-valid/result-ready signals. Ordinary integer operations and all four multiplies remain combinational. All eight original ALTOPS formulas are preserved; DIV/REM capture their formula from accepted operands and use the identical eight-edge controller.\\n- EX saves both post-forwarded source operands for RVFI while ID/EX retains the instruction, PC, exact prediction, destination, and controls. Request readiness prevents relaunch while computing or retaining a result. The result advances once through the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB path.\\n- Added a separate divide interlock to EX, hazard control, and core wiring. Divide waits freeze fetch and ID/EX but drain older instructions once and insert complete EX/MEM bubbles. Actual memory holds retain their original EX/MEM priority and MEM/WB forwarding behavior. Resolution, predictor training, and redirects occur only when EX advances, including divide completion.\\n- Reset cancels computation and retained results. The nret=1 top-level ports, retirement logic, decoder/traps, predictor organization, register file, forwarding unit, and memory stage remain unchanged.\\n- Expanded integrated ALU tests and pipeline tests. No gate, formal depth, arithmetic expectation, or existing assertion was weakened.\\n\\nThe controller produces result-valid after edge eight; EX/MEM accepts on edge nine and MEM/WB retires on edge ten. A divide after a cold redirect retires exactly ten cycles after the redirecting instruction. Back-to-back divides retire nine cycles apart with ready buses.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings. Also PASS with `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` defined.\\n- Yosys elaboration/process/optimization followed by `select -assert-none` for `$div`, `$mod`, `$divfloor`, and `$modfloor`: PASS. No real division/remainder operator remains in the synthesized execution datapath.\\n- Complete cocotb suite: PASS, all seven pytest runner cases. ALU runs in real and ALTOPS builds, each checking 185 boundary/deterministic vectors per M operation plus existing exact tests. Tests cover eight-edge completion, changing live operands/opcodes, rejected requests while occupied, retained results, ready asserted before completion, fast ALU operation during division, reset at every phase, and consecutive requests.\\n- Pipeline tests: PASS. Coverage includes EX/MEM and MEM/WB operands, stalled load dependencies, retained older WB payload, consecutive divides, rd=x0, 34 vector pairs for every DIV/REM variant, immediate and one-gap consumers, all four multiply consumers, loads, store data/address, branches, JALR, redirect recovery, stale predictions, simultaneous bus stalls, memory markers, illegal/flushed instructions, and reset during execution. Assertions check saved RVFI operands, full bubbles, strict order/PC chaining, exactly-once launch/completion/resolution, writes and memory requests, and ready-bus retirement bounds.\\n- Unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh`, selecting bench nret=1: **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, including `liveness_ch0`, `unique_ch0`, and all four DIV/REM instruction checks. First completed formal suite passed; zero RTL fix retries. Formal ran in a scratch copy under `$TMPDIR`, with unchanged wrapper, checks, and depth settings.\\n- Unchanged selftest cosim plus CoreMark CRC gate: PASS, two ELFs tested. Additional selftest cosim with both random bus stalls and seeds 3735928559, 1, and 42: PASS, 115 retirements each, byte-exact against the unchanged ISS.\\n- Full real-arithmetic `formal-deep` was not run. ALTOPS formal validates the real controller latency and routing, while real arithmetic is checked by the simulation vectors and cosim.\\n\\n## Measured FPGA and benchmark results\\n\\nThe unchanged local evaluator reports:\\n\\n| Metric | Prior winner | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Median Fmax | 134.68 MHz | 135.03 MHz |\\n| Fitness | 319.45 iter/s | 320.28 iter/s |\\n| LUT4 | 10,645 | 5,871 |\\n| FF | 2,075 | 2,292 |\\n| Bracketed stalled CoreMark cycles | 4,215,937 | 4,215,937 |\\n\\nAll three FPGA seeds routed successfully: seed 1 = 135.03 MHz, seed 2 = 144.68 MHz, seed 3 = 126.39 MHz. Fitness improved **0.26%**, LUT4 use fell **44.85%**, and FF use rose **10.46%**. The approximately 12% anticipated fitness gain was not realized.\\n\\nThe baseline cycle count was independently reproduced from the original HEAD bench RTL using the same ELF and unchanged stalled harness. A streamed full candidate retirement trace contained 2,937,037 instructions: the measured region executed 93,960 MULs and **zero DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU instructions**. The complete program executed two DIVUs outside the markers. Thus divider latency did not affect the scored cycle count.\\n\\nAll three post-route critical paths run through predictor tag lookup and counter write control. For the median seed, the path starts at `cpu.u_if.tag_q.0.1_RAD_DFFR_Q_3.Q` and ends at `cpu.u_if.counter_q.1.0.WRE`: 7.41 ns total, comprising 3.77 ns logic and 3.63 ns routing. The divider is not on the reported critical path. The small fitness change and substantial seed spread do not establish the predicted timing benefit.\\n\\n## Benchmark contract discrepancy and supplemental 6 KB run\\n\\nThe request describes a 6 KB performance workload with CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47. The checked-in `bench/programs/Makefile` actually defaults to `COREMARK_SIZE=2000`, and the unchanged evaluator requires the 2 KB CRC set e714/1fd7/8e3a (seed e9f5, final fcaf). The 319.45 reference is consistent with this checked-in configuration. No benchmark source, Makefile, evaluator, or CRC check was changed; the primary fitness above uses the unchanged evaluation.\\n\\nA supplemental scratch build used the existing `COREMARK_SIZE=6000` option, `-O3`, and ITERATIONS=10 with both random stalls. It passed EBREAK, memory bounds, marker bracketing, the literal `Correct operation validated.`, and exact CRCs: seed 8a02, list d4b0, matrix be52, state 5e47, final 5949. It measured 30,823,120 bracketed cycles, equivalent to 43.81 iter/s at the measured 135.03 MHz median. This supplemental score is not compared to the differently configured 319.45 baseline.\\n\\n## Evidence and remaining concerns\\n\\nScratch artifacts are under `$TMPDIR/hyp-20260905-002-r2s1-checks`; post-route reports are in its `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`. Logs alongside that scratch directory use the prefix `hyp-20260905-002-r2s1-`, including `formal.log`, `unit-tests.log`, `cosim.log`, `selftest-stalls.log`, `fpga.log`, `baseline-cycles.log`, `div-traffic.log`, and `6k.log`.\\n\\nNo functional deviation from the proposed divider schedule was needed. The material limitations are the unchanged predictor critical path, the small measured fitness gain relative to seed variation, and the workload-dependent eight extra EX hold cycles for each divide/remainder compared with the old combinational ALU. Synthesis emitted only the expected array-to-wire lowering messages for the four combinational digit steps; required lint is clean.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T15:02:37.734111Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r2s1 (improvement, +0.26%): An eight-cycle DIV/REM engine cut LUT4 use 44.85% without changing CoreMark cycles because the timed region executes no DIV/REM, but predictor tag lookup and counter-write control remained the critical path, leaving the fitness gain small relative to FPGA seed variation.\"}\n9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline resolution feedback with registered predictor-row reads\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 320.28 iter/s at 135.03 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1 implementation notes identify the predictor's\\nindexed tag lookup through counter-write control as the critical path\\nin all three FPGA seeds; the median path is 7.41 ns. Removing nearly\\nhalf the LUTs with a separate divider improved fitness only 0.26%,\\nwith unchanged CoreMark cycles. The other prior structural attempt,\\nhyp-20260905-002-r1s1, saved only 0.75% of cycles by buffering stores.\\nThese results favor splitting the measured feedback path over another\\nbackend resource change. LESSONS.md also records the timing regression\\nfrom two-way prediction, despite essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nIn the present if_stage.sv, one cycle contains an indexed table read,\\na full-tag comparison, saturating-counter decisions, and table write\\ncontrol. Registering the selected row before comparison creates a\\nstructural pipeline boundary in this read/modify/write transaction.\\nThe prediction algorithm, capacity, instruction pipeline, and immediate\\nEX recovery can retain their existing organization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 320.28 iter/s at 135.03 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1 implementation notes identify the predictor's\\nindexed tag lookup through counter-write control as the critical path\\nin all three FPGA seeds; the median path is 7.41 ns. Removing nearly\\nhalf the LUTs with a separate divider improved fitness only 0.26%,\\nwith unchanged CoreMark cycles. The other prior structural attempt,\\nhyp-20260905-002-r1s1, saved only 0.75% of cycles by buffering stores.\\nThese results favor splitting the measured feedback path over another\\nbackend resource change. LESSONS.md also records the timing regression\\nfrom two-way prediction, despite essentially unchanged CoreMark cycles.\\n\\nIn the present if_stage.sv, one cycle contains an indexed table read,\\na full-tag comparison, saturating-counter decisions, and table write\\ncontrol. Registering the selected row before comparison creates a\\nstructural pipeline boundary in this read/modify/write transaction.\\nThe prediction algorithm, capacity, instruction pipeline, and immediate\\nEX recovery can retain their existing organization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split resolved-instruction feedback into a row-read stage and an update\\nstage inside if_stage. Retain exactly sixteen direct-mapped entries,\\nPC[5:2] indexing, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, and the\\nexisting two-bit counter policy. The single architectural change is\\nadding one register boundary to the training transaction; do not add\\nan instruction pipeline stage or change execution-resource scheduling.\\n\\nOn edge N with resolve_valid asserted, capture the resolution's index,\\nPC tag, target, legal/branch/jump/taken flags, and the selected row's\\nvalid, tag, and two-bit counter into a maintenance register. Capture\\nraw row state here, not an already-computed tag hit or counter update:\\nthe indexed read must end at this register. The existing target need\\nnot be read for training because neither hit detection nor counter\\narithmetic consumes it. Carry a separate maintenance-valid bit.\\n\\nDuring the following cycle, compare the captured tags and compute the\\nrow update solely from the captured event and row state. Commit it on\\nedge N+1. A legal jump writes its full target and counter 11. A legal\\ntaken branch writes its target, allocates counter 10 on a miss, and\\nincrements with saturation on a hit. A legal not-taken branch only\\ndecrements a matching counter with saturation, retaining the target\\nand allocating nothing on a miss. An illegal, trapping, or non-control\\nresolution invalidates only a matching tag. Preserve the existing\\nfield-specific write enables and full architectural address widths.\\nIn particular, no live indexed tag lookup may feed the update-stage\\nwrite enable, or the proposed timing cut would disappear.\\n\\nSustain one resolution per cycle by committing the older transaction\\nwhile accepting the next. Handle their read-after-write hazard with\\nexplicit maintenance forwarding: when the committing transaction and\\nincoming resolution address the same index, capture the older\\ntransaction's post-update valid/tag/counter as the incoming row\\nsnapshot instead of the pre-edge table values. Forward the complete\\nlogical state of those fields, including invalidation, allocation,\\nsaturation, and unchanged fields. Compare indices for this bypass,\\nnot tags: different PCs aliasing one row require forwarding too.\\nThis bypass is confined to the maintenance pipeline. After draining,\\ntable state must equal applying the accepted resolution stream in\\norder under the original update policy; no event may be lost or\\ncounted twice, including consecutive updates to the same branch.\\n\\nSet maintenance-valid from resolve_valid every non-reset edge, rather\\nthan holding it on a frontend or backend stall. A previously accepted\\nevent commits once even if IF is stalled, a later divide is waiting,\\nor a younger instruction redirects. Acceptance still uses the existing\\nadvancing-EX resolve_valid qualification; held EX instructions cannot\\nenqueue repeatedly. Reset has priority over both capture and commit,\\nclears all table valid bits and maintenance-valid, and cannot permit\\na pre-reset transaction to repopulate the predictor afterward.\\n\\nFetch continues to consult committed table state only. There is no\\npending-update bypass to predicted_next_pc, no training backpressure,\\nand no extra delay on the EX actual-next-PC comparison or redirect.\\nPreserve the exact prediction accepted with each instruction through\\nID/EX and retain resolved architectural pc_next at RVFI retirement.\\nRecovery still overrides imem stalls, and dmem/divide holds keep their\\ncurrent priorities. Training becomes visible one cycle later than\\ntoday; a very short cold loop can therefore mispredict an additional\\ntime. Warm self-jumps must still sustain one retirement per cycle,\\nand each recovery retains its existing single younger-slot bubble.\\n\\nExpect a 6% fitness improvement, with medium confidence, from separating\\nthe table-read mux from tag comparison and update write control. This\\nis an estimate, not a measured result. The maintenance bypass could\\nbecome critical, delayed training can increase cycles, and fetch or\\nEX recovery may set the new timing limit. Evaluate the unchanged\\nmarker-bracketed stalled CoreMark workload and three-seed median Fmax;\\nreport exact iterations/cycles, LUT/FF usage, and post-route critical\\npaths. Compare the fitness product with 320.28 using the same workload\\nconfiguration, identifying the configuration in the results. Preserve\\nnret=1 and every external port, all RVFI order/operand/trap rules,\\ndecoder legality, divider and ALTOPS behavior, memory handshakes and\\nbounds, and the required CoreMark CRC/UART/marker checks. All formal\\nconfigurations, test harnesses, and evaluation gates remain intact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert the registered row-read and resolution-event stage ahead\\nof tag comparison and counter-update generation. Implement one\\ncommit and one capture per cycle with same-index post-update\\nforwarding of valid/tag/counter, field-specific table writes,\\nexact event validity, and reset cancellation. Keep fetch lookup,\\nprediction policy, module ports, PC advancement, and immediate\\nrecovery behavior as specified. Document the two-edge training\\nschedule and keep the maintenance bypass off the fetch path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing behavior checks with a sequential reference\\nmodel and cycle-exact capture/commit assertions. Update observation\\ntiming for the intentional extra training stage while retaining\\nevery existing policy, full-address, reset, and no-fetch-bypass\\nassertion. Test one accepted event per clock, same-PC counter\\nsaturation and threshold crossings, different-tag same-index\\nallocation, invalidation followed by allocation, not-taken misses,\\nno-op updates, different indices, idle drain, and reset with a\\npending update. Check predictions at each edge and final table\\nbehavior after draining a randomized resolution stream.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cold and warm self-jumps, short loops, alternating\\nsame-index control-flow PCs, changing JALR targets, and stale-code\\ninvalidations to exercise the added feedback latency and forwarding.\\nAssert sustained warm one-per-cycle retirement and the unchanged\\nredirect bubble length, with explicit expectations for extra cold\\nmisses. Cover a pending update draining during imem/dmem stalls,\\ndivide waits, redirects, and reset; prove exactly-once training\\nfor held EX instructions. Retain all RVFI order, PC chain, operand,\\ntrap, wrong-path-store, memory, and arithmetic assertions, and run\\nthe unchanged full cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA evaluation gates.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert the registered row-read and resolution-event stage ahead\\nof tag comparison and counter-update generation. Implement one\\ncommit and one capture per cycle with same-index post-update\\nforwarding of valid/tag/counter, field-specific table writes,\\nexact event validity, and reset cancellation. Keep fetch lookup,\\nprediction policy, module ports, PC advancement, and immediate\\nrecovery behavior as specified. Document the two-edge training\\nschedule and keep the maintenance bypass off the fetch path.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing behavior checks with a sequential reference\\nmodel and cycle-exact capture/commit assertions. Update observation\\ntiming for the intentional extra training stage while retaining\\nevery existing policy, full-address, reset, and no-fetch-bypass\\nassertion. Test one accepted event per clock, same-PC counter\\nsaturation and threshold crossings, different-tag same-index\\nallocation, invalidation followed by allocation, not-taken misses,\\nno-op updates, different indices, idle drain, and reset with a\\npending update. Check predictions at each edge and final table\\nbehavior after draining a randomized resolution stream.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed cold and warm self-jumps, short loops, alternating\\nsame-index control-flow PCs, changing JALR targets, and stale-code\\ninvalidations to exercise the added feedback latency and forwarding.\\nAssert sustained warm one-per-cycle retirement and the unchanged\\nredirect bubble length, with explicit expectations for extra cold\\nmisses. Cover a pending update draining during imem/dmem stalls,\\ndivide waits, redirects, and reset; prove exactly-once training\\nfor held EX instructions. Retain all RVFI order, PC chain, operand,\\ntrap, wrong-path-store, memory, and arithmetic assertions, and run\\nthe unchanged full cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA evaluation gates.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 340.22, \"delta_pct\": 6.23, \"vs_baseline\": 20.3, \"fmax_mhz\": 143.41, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5920, \"ff\": 2382, \"seeds\": [143.41, 137.51, 155.74], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Registered predictor maintenance: hyp-20260905-002-r3s1\\n\\nImplemented the proposed training pipeline. All local evaluation gates pass.\\nThe unchanged gate reports **340.22 iter/s**, with **143.41 MHz** three-seed\\nmedian Fmax, **5,920 LUT4s**, and **2,382 FFs**. Using the unrounded fitness\\nproduct, the improvement over the supplied 320.28 is **6.2245848777%**.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- Only `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` changes hardware. At edge N it captures\\n  the resolution index, full 26-bit tag, aligned 30-bit target, four event\\n  flags, and raw selected-row valid/tag/counter. It does not capture a hit\\n  result, a computed counter, or the old target.\\n- Captured tags and counter drive the following cycle's update. Edge N+1\\n  commits it while capturing the next event. Update write enables have no\\n  live indexed tag/counter input. Valid, tag/target, and counter writes\\n  retain the original field-specific policy, including saturated no-ops.\\n- Same-index maintenance forwarding captures the committing transaction's\\n  complete post-update valid/tag/counter, including unchanged fields,\\n  invalidation, and replacement by another full tag. This bypass never\\n  reaches fetch prediction.\\n- Maintenance-valid is assigned from resolve-valid on every non-reset edge.\\n  IF/backend holds and younger redirects do not hold or cancel accepted\\n  training. Reset cancels capture and commit, clears maintenance-valid and\\n  all table valids, and leaves unused payloads unreset.\\n- Capacity, indexing, tag/target widths, prediction algorithm, EX resolution\\n  qualification and immediate recovery, instruction-stage count, execution\\n  scheduling, nret=1, external ports, RVFI, decoder, divider, and ALTOPS are\\n  unchanged. No architectural deviation from the hypothesis.\\n\\n## Tests\\n\\n- `test_if_stage.py`: independent sequential policy model checked before\\n  and after edges, exact committed table fields and captured raw snapshots,\\n  one event per edge, counter saturation/thresholds, target retention,\\n  different-tag same-index replacement, invalidation/allocation, misses,\\n  no-ops, different indices, idle drain, full addresses, no fetch bypass,\\n  and reset cancellation. A deterministic 1,200-cycle random stream includes\\n  aliases, bubbles, stalls, redirects, and resets, followed by a full probe.\\n- `test_pipeline.py`: applies the model to every existing pipeline test and\\n  checks redirects against the prediction actually accepted with the\\n  instruction. Adds short branch loops and pending-update drain during\\n  imem stalls, dmem holds, divide waits, overlapping redirects, and reset.\\n  Existing JALR target changes, stale code/traps, held-EX exactly-once\\n  checks, operand/order/PC-chain checks, wrong-path stores, memory, and\\n  arithmetic assertions remain active.\\n- Intentional latency expectation changes: self-jumps have two cold misses,\\n  each followed by one younger-slot bubble; warm retirement remains one\\n  per cycle. Alternating full-tag aliases can hit an older committed row\\n  before its pending replacement commits, so that test now asserts the\\n  exact delayed-update prediction/redirect sequence instead of all misses.\\n\\n## Local validation\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings/errors.\\n- Full cocotb suite: **7 pytest runners passed**, including **38 pipeline\\n  tests**, **4 IF tests**, and both real-arithmetic and ALTOPS ALU builds.\\n- Local formal status: **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, using the unchanged\\n  `bash formal/run_all.sh`, `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl`, `CORE_NAME=bench`,\\n  `JOBS=6`; automatic nret=1 selection chose `wrapper_si.sv` and\\n  `checks_si.cfg`. The first shell invocation exited before generation\\n  because its Python lacked PyYAML; selecting the installed conda Python\\n  resolved this. First actual proof run passed; no counterexample and no\\n  RTL fix/retry for formal. Formal-deep was not run; arithmetic is unchanged.\\n- Unchanged cosim evaluator: **PASS, 2 ELFs** (selftest full RVFI comparison\\n  and stalled CoreMark CRC/UART validation).\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench`, with `NRET=1` and\\n  `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: PASS, all three seeds routed and benchmark\\n  validation completed. `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Formal, cosim, and FPGA run in an isolated `$TMPDIR` copy; 48 evaluated\\n  RTL/config/harness/source files were byte-compared with the worktree.\\n  No protected harness, source, configuration, or other core was edited.\\n\\n## Workload configuration and exact performance\\n\\nThe checked-in `bench/programs/Makefile` defaults to `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000`,\\n`-O3`, and the evaluator's CRC goldens are for that 2 KB configuration.\\nThis conflicts with the prompt's 6 KB description. The gates were preserved.\\nBoth sizes were measured separately using the unchanged harness,\\n`ITERATIONS=10`, `--bench --istall --dstall`, default stall seed\\n`0xDEADBEEF`, and only the `0x10000100` start / `0x10000104` stop bracket.\\nThe original HEAD RTL was rebuilt in scratch to measure baseline cycles.\\nBaseline Fmax and resource counts below are the supplied winner's values;\\nbaseline FPGA routing was not repeated.\\n\\n| Configuration | Original RTL cycles | New RTL cycles | Original iter/s at 135.03 MHz | New iter/s at 143.41 MHz |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| Unchanged gate: 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 4,215,937 | 4,215,262 | 320.2846721856 | 340.2161004464 |\\n| Supplemental: 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations, both stalls | 30,823,120 | 30,822,790 | 43.8080246257 | 46.5272611597 |\\n\\nThe 2 KB baseline reproduces **320.28** under the same configuration.\\nThe new exact throughput is `10 / 4215262 = 2.372331779139707e-6`\\niterations/cycle; fitness is `143410000 * 10 / 4215262`.\\nIt saves 675 bracketed cycles. The 6 KB result saves 330 cycles and improves\\nthe same-configuration fitness by **6.2071653704%**; comparing its absolute\\nscore directly with 320.28 would mix workloads.\\n\\nThe primary gate validates its unchanged 2 KB goldens: seedcrc=e9f5,\\ncrclist=e714, crcmatrix=1fd7, crcstate=8e3a, crcfinal=fcaf.\\nThe supplemental 6 KB run explicitly checked the requested crclist=d4b0,\\ncrcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, plus seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949.\\nBoth baseline and candidate supplemental runs reached EBREAK, had\\n`oob=false`, reported 10 iterations and the literal\\n`Correct operation validated.`, and contained valid timing markers.\\nFor new 6 KB RTL, start=53,747 and stop=30,876,537. The old stop was\\n30,876,867 with the same start. The UART's compiler-flags string remains\\nthe inherited hardcoded `-O2 -march=rv32im`; the actual build command is\\n`-O3`. The UART size field is per algorithm (666 for 2 KB, 2000 for 6 KB).\\n\\n## Three-seed post-route timing\\n\\nDevice: `GW2A-LV18QN88C8/I7`, family `GW2A-18C`, unchanged Tang Nano 20K\\nconstraints. These are final post-route reports, not placement estimates.\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Critical path ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 1 | 143.41 | 6.97 | 3.95 | 3.02 |\\n| 2 | 137.51 | 7.27 | 2.32 | 4.95 |\\n| 3 | 155.74 | 6.42 | 2.32 | 4.10 |\\n\\n- Seed 1: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_175.Q` (`cpu.id_ex_w[54]`) through\\n  hazard/control logic to `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_63.D`.\\n- Seed 2: `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_161.Q` (`cpu.id_ex_w[11]`) through\\n  hazard/divide-wait control to `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_71.RESET`.\\n- Seed 3: same source and hazard/divide-wait control class as seed 2,\\n  ending at `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_98.RESET`.\\n\\nMedian Fmax increases from 135.03 to 143.41 MHz. The median critical path\\nfalls from the supplied 7.41 ns to 6.97 ns. None of the three final\\ncritical paths crosses the predictor indexed maintenance lookup.\\nAll seeds use 5,920 LUT4s and 2,382 DFFs, versus 5,871 / 2,292 supplied\\nfor the winner: **+49 LUTs, +90 FFs**. The maintenance payload contains\\n94 source-level bits before synthesis optimization.\\n\\n## Concerns and evidence\\n\\nThe intended extra cold self-loop miss remains a performance cost, and\\nalias timing changes can help or hurt particular streams. Observed cycle\\nchanges also include the deterministic stall generator's changed phase.\\nMaintenance forwarding is combinational but was not the reported timing\\nlimit in any seed. Hazard/control routing is the new measured limit;\\nthis change intentionally does not alter it.\\n\\nScratch evidence directory:\\n`$TMPDIR/hyp-r3-validation-3dg8_bd1/`\\n\\nIt contains `formal-gate.log`, `cocotb-gate.log`,\\n`cosim-gate-result.log`, `fpga-gate.log`, `comparison.json`, the raw\\n`baseline-2k.jsonl`, `baseline-6k.jsonl`, `candidate-6k.jsonl`, and\\n`cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` with the complete\\npost-route paths. Implementation notes are at the worktree root as\\nrequested by instruction 5 and consumed by the orchestrator's notes reader.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T15:53:57.352318Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 (regression, +6.23%): Registering raw predictor-row reads before tag comparison/counter writes, with same-index post-update forwarding for back-to-back resolutions, removed the maintenance lookup from all three critical paths and raised median Fmax from 135.03 to 143.41 MHz with essentially unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles, exposing hazard/divide-wait control as the next timing limit.\"}\n12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Pipeline row-addressed resolution events before local predictor updates\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner scores 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-003-r3s2 implementation notes identify all three critical\\npaths as ID/EX resolve-PC registers through local predictor tag matching\\nto metadata enables; the median path is 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of\\nrouting. Row-local metadata removed the indexed feedback mux, but live\\nEX PC bits still feed sixteen full-tag comparisons and tag-write paths.\\n\\nThe structural history favors a targeted timing cut. Store buffering\\nin hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 saved only 0.75% of CoreMark cycles; separating\\ndivision in hyp-20260905-002-r2s1 substantially reduced area but improved\\nfitness only 0.26%. The registered row-read experiment\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached 143.41 MHz with nearly unchanged cycles,\\nlosing to the current winner. It captured selected metadata and needed\\nforwarding between maintenance transactions. The current local rows\\npermit a different cut: register each resolution at its destination row\\nbefore comparison, then update that row directly from its current state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner scores 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz. Its\\nhyp-20260905-003-r3s2 implementation notes identify all three critical\\npaths as ID/EX resolve-PC registers through local predictor tag matching\\nto metadata enables; the median path is 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of\\nrouting. Row-local metadata removed the indexed feedback mux, but live\\nEX PC bits still feed sixteen full-tag comparisons and tag-write paths.\\n\\nThe structural history favors a targeted timing cut. Store buffering\\nin hyp-20260905-002-r1s1 saved only 0.75% of CoreMark cycles; separating\\ndivision in hyp-20260905-002-r2s1 substantially reduced area but improved\\nfitness only 0.26%. The registered row-read experiment\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached 143.41 MHz with nearly unchanged cycles,\\nlosing to the current winner. It captured selected metadata and needed\\nforwarding between maintenance transactions. The current local rows\\npermit a different cut: register each resolution at its destination row\\nbefore comparison, then update that row directly from its current state.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one pipeline boundary to resolution maintenance, using a private\\npending-event register at each of the sixteen existing metadata rows.\\nThis is one structural retiming of the feedback transaction. Keep the\\ninstruction pipeline, immediate EX redirect, predictor algorithm,\\ncapacity, fetch lookup, and execution-resource scheduling unchanged.\\n\\nEach row gets a pending-valid bit, full 26-bit resolving PC tag, and\\ntwo-bit command. Encode the four commands as matching-tag invalidation\\nfor illegal/trapping/non-control instructions, legal jump, legal taken\\nbranch, and legal not-taken branch. Classification uses the existing\\nresolve_legal/branch/jump/taken outputs, with illegality taking priority.\\nOn edge N, pending_valid[row] becomes resolve_valid &&\\n(resolve_pc[5:2] == row). Update that row's pending tag and command only\\non this acceptance; otherwise retain its payload. These independently\\nenabled registers must remain local to their rows, rather than becoming\\none shared registered PC broadcast into sixteen comparisons. Capture\\nthe event only; do not read, snapshot, or compare old table metadata\\non this edge. At most one row accepts per cycle, without backpressure.\\n\\nOn edge N+1, each row with its old pending-valid asserted commits using\\nits private pending tag/command and its current local valid/tag/counter.\\nA jump writes the pending tag, sets valid, and sets the counter to 11.\\nA taken branch writes the tag and valid, allocates counter 10 on a miss,\\nor increments a hit with saturation. A not-taken branch decrements only\\na matching counter with saturation and retains its target. Invalidation\\nclears only a matching row's valid bit. Preserve all other fields.\\nCommit-time tag comparisons, saturation, tag data, and metadata enables\\nmust have no combinational dependency on live EX resolution inputs.\\n\\nCommit the old event while accepting the next, including consecutive\\nevents to the same row with different tags. Do not capture a counter or\\nhit snapshot: the next event sees the preceding event's committed local\\nstate during its own update cycle. Consequently there is no indexed\\nmetadata read and no maintenance forwarding mux. After draining, table\\nstate must equal applying every accepted event in order under the\\nexisting policy, including aliases, invalidations, and saturated updates.\\n\\nDelay the separate indexed target write by the same edge. Capture one\\nshared write-valid bit, four-bit index, and full 30-bit aligned target\\nfor a legal jump or taken branch, then write the existing target array\\non edge N+1. Its delayed write-valid is refreshed every cycle and is\\nindependent of old tags/counters. Tag, valid, counter, and target changes\\nfor an event become visible together; never publish a new tag with an\\nold target. Retain the existing indexed target storage implementation.\\nThis adds approximately 499 source-level event-state bits: 16 times\\n(1+26+2), plus one shared (1+4+30) target-write record.\\n\\nRefresh pending-valid bits every non-reset edge, even during fetch or\\nbackend stalls. An accepted event commits once while a younger divide\\nwaits, a memory request stalls, or a younger instruction redirects.\\nAcceptance remains qualified by the existing advancing-EX resolve_valid;\\nheld instructions cannot retrain repeatedly. Reset overrides capture\\nand commit, clears all pending/table validity, and cancels pending target\\nwrites. Masked payloads need no reset. No added architectural stall is\\npermitted when a maintenance event is pending.\\n\\nFetch consults committed state only, with no pending-event bypass. A\\nprediction accepted on a commit edge uses pre-edge state. Preserve the\\nexact predicted next PC carried with each instruction, the EX comparison\\nagainst actual next PC for every advancing instruction, and resolved\\nRVFI next-PC reporting. Training visibility moves one edge later, which\\ncan add cold-loop misses; warm self-jumps must still retire once per\\ncycle and recovery must still kill only the existing younger slot.\\nPreserve nret=1 and every external port, ordered retirement, operand\\nforwarding, decoder and trap rules, real/ALTOPS arithmetic, memory\\nhandshakes and bounds, and every protected evaluation check.\\n\\nEstimate four percent higher fitness from shorter maintenance paths,\\nwith little change in bracketed cycles. This is unmeasured. Additional\\nevent flops, capture-enable fanout, later training, or the hazard/divide\\ncontrol path exposed in r3s1 could erase the gain. Inspect the synthesized\\ncones and three-seed post-route reports: live resolve-PC paths should\\nterminate at event capture, while tag comparison starts at private row\\nevent registers. Report actual LUT4/FF costs and exact iterations/cycle\\ntimes median Fmax, using the same toolchain and workload for comparison.\\n\\nRun unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA gates. The historical\\nnotes identify the 350.46 score as the existing 2 KB gate and separately\\nreport 30,823,120 cycles for the canonical 6 KB workload. Preserve both\\ncontracts: compare the unchanged scored workload with 350.46, and also\\nrun the required 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration workload with both bus stalls\\nagainst the original RTL. Require canonical d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, the\\nvalidation literal, bounds checks, and start/stop marker bracketing.\\nReport the two configurations separately; do not mix their fitnesses\\nor modify harnesses, benchmark sources, wrappers, or other cores.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert sixteen independently enabled pending-event registers before\\nlocal metadata comparison and update. Implement old-event commit\\nalongside new-event capture, refreshing validity without stall holds.\\nDrive metadata exclusively from private registered events and live\\nlocal row state. Delay the single indexed target write to commit\\natomically with metadata. Preserve all prediction policies, full\\naddress widths, fetch timing, module ports, immediate recovery, and\\nreset cancellation. Introduce no table snapshots or training bypass.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Model accepted resolution events with an explicit one-edge delay and\\ncheck public predictions before and after every edge against an\\nindependent sequential policy model. Retain all existing functional\\nassertions, adjusting only expected training visibility. Cover all\\nrows and tag/target bits, continuous same-row commits and captures,\\nalternating aliases, threshold crossings, saturation, target changes,\\nnot-taken misses, invalidation/allocation, and idle draining. Detect\\nmismatched tag/target publication and fetch bypass. Test reset with\\na pending event, stalled fetch, concurrent recovery, and randomized\\nresolution streams followed by a complete table-behavior probe.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update the training reference model for the intentional delay while\\npreserving every architectural assertion. Check cold and warm short\\nloops, changing JALR targets, stale-code invalidations, and exactly\\none accepted training event per advancing instruction. Exercise\\npending-event commits during imem stalls, dmem holds, divide waits,\\nyounger redirects, and reset. Assert warm one-retirement-per-cycle\\nthroughput, unchanged recovery bubble length, strict RVFI order and\\nPC chaining, correct operands/traps, and no wrong-path memory writes.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Insert sixteen independently enabled pending-event registers before\\nlocal metadata comparison and update. Implement old-event commit\\nalongside new-event capture, refreshing validity without stall holds.\\nDrive metadata exclusively from private registered events and live\\nlocal row state. Delay the single indexed target write to commit\\natomically with metadata. Preserve all prediction policies, full\\naddress widths, fetch timing, module ports, immediate recovery, and\\nreset cancellation. Introduce no table snapshots or training bypass.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Model accepted resolution events with an explicit one-edge delay and\\ncheck public predictions before and after every edge against an\\nindependent sequential policy model. Retain all existing functional\\nassertions, adjusting only expected training visibility. Cover all\\nrows and tag/target bits, continuous same-row commits and captures,\\nalternating aliases, threshold crossings, saturation, target changes,\\nnot-taken misses, invalidation/allocation, and idle draining. Detect\\nmismatched tag/target publication and fetch bypass. Test reset with\\na pending event, stalled fetch, concurrent recovery, and randomized\\nresolution streams followed by a complete table-behavior probe.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update the training reference model for the intentional delay while\\npreserving every architectural assertion. Check cold and warm short\\nloops, changing JALR targets, stale-code invalidations, and exactly\\none accepted training event per advancing instruction. Exercise\\npending-event commits during imem stalls, dmem holds, divide waits,\\nyounger redirects, and reset. Assert warm one-retirement-per-cycle\\nthroughput, unchanged recovery bubble length, strict RVFI order and\\nPC chaining, correct operands/traps, and no wrong-path memory writes.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 371.55, \"delta_pct\": 6.02, \"vs_baseline\": 31.37, \"fmax_mhz\": 156.62, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6584, \"ff\": 3194, \"seeds\": [157.11, 156.62, 155.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the row-addressed resolution-event pipeline in `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv`, with corresponding coverage in `cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py` and `cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`. No other RTL, core configuration, benchmark source, wrapper, harness, or evaluation gate changed. This requested notes file is at the worktree root, as read by the orchestrator and explicitly permitted by its path checks.\\n\\nEach of the sixteen metadata rows now owns a pending-valid bit, independently enabled 26-bit resolving tag, and two-bit command: invalidate=00, jump=01, taken branch=10, not-taken branch=11. Illegality takes priority. Acceptance captures only the event; it neither reads nor snapshots metadata. Each row commits its old event against its current local valid/tag/counter while accepting the next event, including consecutive aliases. The policy remains jump-to-11, taken-miss-to-10, saturating taken-hit increment, matching-only saturating not-taken decrement with retained target, and matching-only invalidation.\\n\\nA shared valid/index/30-bit target record delays the existing indexed target-array write to the same edge as metadata commit. Both pending-valid paths refresh on every non-reset edge, independently of stalls and redirects. Reset cancels capture and commit and clears pending/table validity; masked payloads retain their values. Fetch sees committed state only and uses the pre-edge prediction on a commit edge. There is no training bypass, metadata snapshot, maintenance forwarding, or added architectural interlock. Immediate EX recovery, the exact prediction carried with each instruction, resolved RVFI next PC, nret=1, all external ports, and execution scheduling remain unchanged.\\n\\nNo architectural deviation from the hypothesis was needed. Source event state is exactly `16*(1+26+2) + (1+4+30) = 499` bits. Yosys automatically recodes each two-bit command into four state bits, so mapped event storage is 496 row flops plus 35 shared target flops, or 531 flops. The complete routed core gains 559 FFs after other synthesis/mapping differences.\\n\\nThe IF reference applies accepted events with an explicit one-edge delay and checks the public output bundle and fetch address before and after every checked edge. Coverage includes all rows, every tag and target bit, allocation, threshold crossings, saturation, target replacement/retention, illegal-control priority, not-taken misses, matching/nonmatching invalidations, simultaneous capture/commit, continuous aliases, idle draining, fetch stalls, recovery, flush, and reset with pending work. The existing deterministic 512-cycle random stream is retained, followed by probes of every exercised tag and each row's counter behavior.\\n\\nThe pipeline harness now checks predictions before and after every edge against an independent ordered policy model. It pairs accepted fetches with exactly one advancing-EX resolution, verifies recovery against the exact accepted prediction, and pairs retirements with those resolutions. Existing order, PC-chain, operand, arithmetic, trap, and wrong-path-store assertions remain. New directed tests cover pending commits during instruction stalls, memory holds, divide waits, younger redirects, and reset. Only two existing timing expectations changed: a cold self-jump now misses twice before sustaining one retirement per cycle, and immediate alternating aliases can sometimes hit the still-committed previous entry. Each recovery still kills exactly the existing younger slot and costs one bubble.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS without warnings.\\n- Complete `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, seven pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU builds, four IF tests, and all 35 pipeline tests.\\n- Unchanged `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, using the automatically selected nret=1 wrapper/configuration. No counterexamples and zero RTL fix attempts. The gate was also repeated successfully with the reference toolchain explicitly selected. This is the standard ALTOPS formal suite; real arithmetic RTL was untouched and its cocotb checks passed.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1`: PASS for both original and candidate, covering selftest RVFI comparison and the existing CoreMark CRC gate.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: PASS for both original and candidate; all three seeds routed and the benchmark completed with valid bounds, CRCs, and timing markers.\\n- Synthesized-cone audit: PASS on all 976 metadata data/control input pins. Their combinational sources are exclusively the same row's current metadata, private pending-event registers, and reset. No live EX signal or another row's state reaches commit. All 416 pending-tag bits remain distinct across the sixteen rows; event capture has no table-state dependency. All eight RAM16SDP4 primitives still implement the indexed target store, and all 24 target write-port groups depend only on the delayed target record and reset.\\n\\nFinal results use OSS CAD Suite Yosys 0.64+149 (`2dc69a757-dirty`), Verilator 5.047 (`v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6`), nextpnr 0.10-45 (`g98c18d7f`), and xPack RISC-V GCC 15.2.0. The existing GW2A device, constraints, FPGA wrapper, scripts, and seeds 1/2/3 are unchanged. An initial run selected Homebrew Yosys through shell PATH ordering; all gates were rerun with the reference OSS directory explicitly first. The final original-RTL run reproduces the historical Fmax, area, and scored cycles exactly.\\n\\nThe scored configuration is the unchanged 2 KB, -O3, ITERATIONS=10 workload with `--bench --istall --dstall`, default random seed 0xDEADBEEF:\\n\\n| Metric | Original RTL | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 Fmax | 147.75 MHz | 157.11 MHz |\\n| Seed 2 Fmax | 137.44 MHz | 156.62 MHz |\\n| Seed 3 Fmax | 154.08 MHz | 155.67 MHz |\\n| Median Fmax | 147.75 MHz | 156.62 MHz |\\n| LUT4 | 6,567 | 6,584 |\\n| FF | 2,635 | 3,194 |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 4,215,937 | 4,215,262 |\\n| Start marker cycle | 15,229 | 15,229 |\\n| Stop marker cycle | 4,231,166 | 4,230,491 |\\n| Fitness | 350.45590102508646 iter/s | 371.55460324886093 iter/s |\\n\\nThe exact candidate calculation is `156.62 * 1e6 * 10 / 4215262`, using `10 / 4215262 = 2.372331779139707e-6` iterations/cycle. This is **+6.019119% versus the specified 350.46**, with 675 fewer cycles, 17 additional LUT4s, and 559 additional FFs. The evaluator's rounded IPC field was not used to compute fitness. Both runs passed the unchanged 2 KB CRC gate: seed=e9f5, list=e714, matrix=1fd7, state=8e3a, final=fcaf, ten iterations, and the exact `Correct operation validated.` literal.\\n\\nFinal candidate post-route paths:\\n\\n| Seed | Launch | Endpoint | Total | Logic | Routing |\\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | `cpu.u_if.metadata[0].tag_q_DFFE_Q_19.Q` | same row `counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE` | 6.36 ns | 2.61 ns | 3.76 ns |\\n| 2 | `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_63.Q` | `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_82.RESET` | 6.39 ns | 2.94 ns | 3.44 ns |\\n| 3 | `cpu.u_if.metadata[12].pending_tag_q_DFFE_Q_14.Q` | same row `counter_q_DFFSE_Q_1.CE` | 6.42 ns | 2.13 ns | 4.30 ns |\\n\\nThe old live resolve-PC-to-metadata-enable paths are cut. Seeds 1 and 3 now traverse local committed-tag/private-event comparisons. The median seed exposes the existing prediction-mismatch/hazard/divide-qualified ID/EX flush path: the launching register is `predicted_next_pc[0]`, and the destination is an ID/EX operand register's reset input. Reported component delays are independently rounded by nextpnr. Local comparator routing and the existing recovery-control path remain timing concerns; the extra FF cost is material. Later training can still add cold-loop misses, and one three-seed result does not establish gains for every workload or placement.\\n\\nThe supplemental 6 KB contract was run separately using the unchanged benchmark Makefile with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, ITERATIONS=10, and both bus stalls. Both original and candidate used the exact same ELF and simulator flags:\\n\\n| 6 KB metric | Original RTL | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 30,823,120 | 30,822,790 |\\n| Start marker cycle | 53,747 | 53,747 |\\n| Stop marker cycle | 30,876,867 | 30,876,537 |\\n| Exact iterations/cycle | 10 / 30,823,120 | 10 / 30,822,790 |\\n| Rate at each median Fmax | 47.93479699654026 iter/s | 50.813050992463694 iter/s |\\n\\nBoth 6 KB runs passed EBREAK completion, `oob=false`, positive start/stop bracketing, ten iterations, the validation literal, and exact CRCs seed=8a02, **list=d4b0, matrix=be52, state=5e47**, final=5949. Their UART and final architectural retirement fields match, excluding elapsed cycle. The 330-cycle reduction is reported separately from the 2 KB fitness. CoreMark's existing UART banner statically says -O2; the saved compiler commands actually use -O3. Its displayed sizes 666 and 2000 are per-algorithm sizes; the total build sizes are 2000 and 6000. No benchmark or reporting source was edited.\\n\\nAll scratch sources, unchanged gate copies, builds, and logs are under `$TMPDIR/row-events-j26_tyt7/`. `candidate/` contains the tested RTL/tests and final gate logs at `cores/bench/generated/{formal-gate.log,cocotb-final.log,cosim-gate.log,fpga-gate.log}`. Final routed reports are `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log` in each of `candidate/` and `original/`. The initial diagnostic artifacts are separately retained with `-homebrew` directory suffixes. The original snapshot is Git HEAD `00bf3f9679e60ab7058f8e7f9572388b3ae8a635`.\\n\\nStructured evidence at that scratch root: `metrics.json`, `structure.json`, `workload-comparison.json`, `workload-equivalence.json`, and `input-audit.json`. Raw benchmark output is in each final generated directory as `coremark-2k.jsonl` and `coremark-6k.jsonl`; the supplemental build command is in `supplemental-6k/build.log`. Tested source files and all protected gate copies were byte-compared with this worktree. Both separately linked 2 KB binaries have identical loadable segments; the direct comparison runs use the same candidate ELF for both RTL versions. Its SHA-256 is `0044a04b680dbafccb5ba08117cd7d44e7b1c371a0a70f021da53ff6b528a7d5`; the shared 6 KB ELF SHA-256 is `2f677bbdd235a9bf20cdc7eb4cc58274f1c58f324e7edd763bbfddc1bc8ff314`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T16:54:45.830594Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r4s1 (improvement, +6.02%): Pipelining resolution events into private predictor-row registers cuts live EX-to-metadata-enable paths and preserves consecutive-alias ordering without forwarding by committing against current row state, raising median Fmax from 147.75 to 156.62 MHz with essentially unchanged stalled CoreMark cycles at +559 FFs and +17 LUT4s.\"}\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Bank-local registered training commands for the target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current row-local predictor reaches 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz,\\nwith all three reported critical paths running from ID/EX resolve-PC\\nbits through local tag matching to metadata enables. The median path\\nis 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of routing. This makes training fanout\\nand placement a more evidenced opportunity than greater associativity:\\nhyp-20260905-003-r2s2 lost frequency with essentially unchanged cycles.\\nThe delayed row-snapshot experiment hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached only\\n143.41 MHz, below the current winner, but changed scored cycles from\\n4,215,937 to 4,215,262. That supports a small feedback-latency budget,\\nwithout establishing that pipelining alone will improve this winner.\\nBank-local command registers can isolate the current high-fanout\\nresolve-PC path while retaining its successful scalar metadata and\\nindexed target storage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current row-local predictor reaches 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz,\\nwith all three reported critical paths running from ID/EX resolve-PC\\nbits through local tag matching to metadata enables. The median path\\nis 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of routing. This makes training fanout\\nand placement a more evidenced opportunity than greater associativity:\\nhyp-20260905-003-r2s2 lost frequency with essentially unchanged cycles.\\nThe delayed row-snapshot experiment hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached only\\n143.41 MHz, below the current winner, but changed scored cycles from\\n4,215,937 to 4,215,262. That supports a small feedback-latency budget,\\nwithout establishing that pipelining alone will improve this winner.\\nBank-local command registers can isolate the current high-fanout\\nresolve-PC path while retaining its successful scalar metadata and\\nindexed target storage.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one cycle of predictor-training latency using four bank-local\\nregistered command slots inside if_stage. Keep the existing sixteen\\ndirect-mapped entries, PC[5:2] index, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned\\ntarget[31:2] values, and two-bit branch counters. Partition only the\\nmetadata update circuitry into four groups of four consecutive rows:\\nresolve_pc[5:4] selects the bank and resolve_pc[3:2] selects its row.\\nPrediction policy, fetch lookup, instruction pipeline depth, and\\narchitectural recovery latency retain their current definitions.\\n\\nOn edge N, accept the existing resolve_valid event. Each bank updates\\nits pending-valid bit every non-reset edge to resolve_valid AND its\\nbank-index match. The selected bank captures the full 26-bit tag,\\na four-bit one-hot row selection, and legal/branch/jump/taken flags.\\nGive each bank payload its own bank-specific capture enable: these\\nregisters retain distinct histories, allowing local placement and\\nlimiting each registered command tag to four metadata rows. Capture\\nraw command fields, with no table-state read, tag comparison, or\\ncounter calculation on this capture path. Existing resolve_valid\\nqualification remains authoritative for valid EX advancement through\\nmemory holds and divide waits.\\n\\nDuring the following cycle, each pending command compares its tag\\nwith the addressed row's live scalar tag and computes that row's\\nnext state. Commit on edge N+1. Keep the successful constant-index\\nvalid/tag/counter registers and local saturation logic from r3s2.\\nThe committed state from the preceding event is already visible to\\nthe next event's calculation, including consecutive same-row updates\\nand different tags aliasing a row. Thus commands sustain one accepted\\nresolution per cycle and need neither captured metadata snapshots\\nnor maintenance forwarding. Every metadata update uses registered\\nbank command fields rather than live resolve_pc or resolve flags.\\n\\nKeep the separate 16x30 indexed target array and its single synchronous\\nwrite port. Register its write index, target data, and qualified write\\nevent alongside the bank commands, so a target write commits on the\\nsame edge as its matching metadata allocation/update. Its write event\\nis derived only from the accepted legal jump or taken legal branch,\\nindependently of old tags and counters. Retaining this storage form\\navoids converting the target payload into per-row flip-flops. Verify\\nthat synthesis retains the indexed target memory and distinct bank\\ncommand sources, and that live resolve-PC bits no longer feed metadata\\ntag-comparison or counter/valid-enable cones within the same cycle.\\n\\nPreserve the original state-transition policy in resolution order.\\nA legal jump writes tag/target, sets valid, and sets counter to 11.\\nA legal taken branch writes tag/target and valid, allocates counter\\n10 on a miss, or saturating-increments a hit. A legal not-taken branch\\ndecrements only a matching counter, retaining its target and allocating\\nnothing on a miss. A non-control or trapping resolution invalidates\\nonly a matching tag. Unselected rows retain their state. Reset clears\\ntable valid bits and every pending-valid/write-event bit with priority\\nover capture and commit; unused payloads remain masked and unreset.\\n\\nAn accepted command commits exactly once even if the next cycle has\\nan IF stall, a data-memory hold, a divide wait, or a younger redirect.\\nThose events suppress new acceptance only through resolve_valid and\\ncannot hold, discard, or replay an older command. Fetch reads committed\\ntable state only, with pre-edge read semantics and no pending-command\\nbypass. The intentional cost is one extra cycle before training becomes\\nvisible; cold self-jumps can incur a second miss, while warm self-jumps\\nstill retire every cycle. Preserve the exact prediction captured with\\neach accepted instruction, EX comparison with the architectural next\\nPC for every advancing instruction, immediate recovery priority over\\nimem stalls, and resolved next-PC reporting at RVFI retirement.\\n\\nEstimate a four percent fitness improvement from higher median Fmax,\\napproximately 153.66 MHz if bracketed cycles remain comparable. This\\nis an unmeasured estimate with medium confidence. The extra command\\nregisters and routing can offset the shorter training path, training\\ndelay can change prediction accuracy, and hazard/divide-wait or fetch\\nrecovery logic may become the limit. Evaluate the actual product of\\nfull-precision iterations/cycle and three-seed median Fmax against\\n350.46 using the identical scored workload, toolchain, and ready seed;\\nreport cycles, LUT4/FF cost, and critical-path endpoints separately.\\nPrior logs identify the scored workload as 2 KB, so also verify the\\nrequested 6 KB run and its canonical CRCs separately without changing\\neither evaluation contract or mixing their absolute fitness numbers.\\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, stalled cosim, UART, bounds,\\ntiming-marker, and FPGA gates. Keep nret=1, all external ports, decoder\\nlegality, RVFI order/operands/traps, and divider/ALTOPS behavior intact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add four bank-local registered resolution-command slots and a\\nsynchronized registered command for the existing indexed target\\nwrite port. Apply commands one edge after acceptance using live\\nconstant-index metadata and the existing transition policy. Give\\nbank payloads distinct capture enables, refresh pending-valid bits\\nevery cycle, and cancel pending work on reset. Preserve fetch\\nselection, full tag/target widths, recovery, and module ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent public-output predictor reference model\\nwith an explicit one-edge command delay and check predictions\\nbefore and after each edge. Cover every bank and row, back-to-back\\nsame-row counter transitions, alias replacement and invalidation,\\ninterleaved banks, target/metadata commit atomicity, full address\\nbits, saturation, fetch/training collisions, bubbles, and reset\\nwith a pending update. Check that idle/stall/redirect cycles drain\\naccepted work exactly once and that fetch sees committed state\\nwith no pending-update bypass. Adjust only expectations tied to\\nthe specified training visibility latency; retain semantic checks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for pending training committing through imem\\nbackpressure, an older memory hold, divide waits, and a younger\\nredirect, including reset before commit and no repeated acceptance\\nwhile EX is held. Model cold self-loop timing with the extra\\ntraining edge and require unchanged warm one-retirement-per-cycle\\nbehavior. Preserve assertions for exact accepted-prediction\\nrecovery, PC chaining, RVFI order, links, stale non-control and\\nchanging JALR predictions, illegal/misaligned traps, forwarded\\noperands, and absence of wrong-path retirements or stores.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add four bank-local registered resolution-command slots and a\\nsynchronized registered command for the existing indexed target\\nwrite port. Apply commands one edge after acceptance using live\\nconstant-index metadata and the existing transition policy. Give\\nbank payloads distinct capture enables, refresh pending-valid bits\\nevery cycle, and cancel pending work on reset. Preserve fetch\\nselection, full tag/target widths, recovery, and module ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent public-output predictor reference model\\nwith an explicit one-edge command delay and check predictions\\nbefore and after each edge. Cover every bank and row, back-to-back\\nsame-row counter transitions, alias replacement and invalidation,\\ninterleaved banks, target/metadata commit atomicity, full address\\nbits, saturation, fetch/training collisions, bubbles, and reset\\nwith a pending update. Check that idle/stall/redirect cycles drain\\naccepted work exactly once and that fetch sees committed state\\nwith no pending-update bypass. Adjust only expectations tied to\\nthe specified training visibility latency; retain semantic checks.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks for pending training committing through imem\\nbackpressure, an older memory hold, divide waits, and a younger\\nredirect, including reset before commit and no repeated acceptance\\nwhile EX is held. Model cold self-loop timing with the extra\\ntraining edge and require unchanged warm one-retirement-per-cycle\\nbehavior. Preserve assertions for exact accepted-prediction\\nrecovery, PC chaining, RVFI order, links, stale non-control and\\nchanging JALR predictions, illegal/misaligned traps, forwarded\\noperands, and absence of wrong-path retirements or stores.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 346.34, \"delta_pct\": -1.18, \"vs_baseline\": 22.46, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.99, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6516, \"ff\": 2807, \"seeds\": [145.99, 148.81, 145.41], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the bank-local registered training hypothesis in `cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv`, with latency-aware reference and directed tests in `cores/bench/test/test_if_stage.py` and `cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py`. Correctness gates pass. The measured scored fitness is **346.33671643660585 iter/s**, **1.1765347153438688% below 350.46**. The estimated four-percent gain was not realized.\\n\\nFour command slots use resolve PC[5:4] to select their individual capture enables. Each retains a full 26-bit tag, four-bit one-hot PC[3:2] row selection, and raw legal/branch/jump/taken flags. Every non-reset edge refreshes each pending-valid bit, independently of IF stalls, memory holds, divide waits, flushes, and redirects. Payload registers capture only for their selected bank and otherwise retain their distinct histories. No table read, tag comparison, or counter calculation precedes capture.\\n\\nOn the following edge, the selected constant-index scalar metadata row commits against its live valid/tag/counter state. Consecutive commands therefore observe preceding commits without snapshots or forwarding. The original transition policy is retained: jumps allocate/update to 11; taken branches allocate 10 on a miss or saturating-increment a hit; not-taken branches decrement only a hit and retain its target; non-control or illegal/trapping resolutions invalidate only a matching tag. Unselected rows retain state.\\n\\nThe separate 16x30 indexed target array retains one synchronous write port. An independent registered write event, four-bit index, and 30-bit target commit on the matching metadata edge. Target payload capture is enabled only for an accepted legal jump or taken legal branch. Reset has priority over both capture and commit, clears all table valid bits and pending-valid/write-event bits, and leaves unused payloads unreset. The declaration adds 175 command-state bits; implementation optimization produces a net increase of 172 device FFs.\\n\\nFetch uses committed state only. A fetch accepted on either the capture edge or the following commit edge uses its pre-edge prediction, with no pending-command bypass. Instruction pipeline depth, recovery priority, accepted-prediction capture, EX architectural-next-PC comparison, resolve_valid qualification, RVFI reporting, decoder legality, nret=1, all external ports, divider, and ALTOPS behavior are unchanged. No architectural deviation from the hypothesis was needed.\\n\\nThe independent reference model carries one ordered raw command, applies it one edge after acceptance against current model state, and checks the complete public IF output bundle and fetch address before and after each edge. It does not inspect DUT metadata or command registers. Coverage includes all sixteen rows and four banks, consecutive same-row increments/decrements, both saturation limits, alias replacement and invalidation, interleaved banks, target/metadata atomicity, every tag and stored target bit, PC wraparound, fetch/training collisions, bubbles, idle/stall/flush/redirect draining, and reset with pending and concurrently offered commands.\\n\\nPipeline traces also check those public IF outputs against the same delayed reference before and after every edge. Four new directed tests exercise pending commits during imem backpressure, invalidation from an advancing load during its subsequent older-memory hold, divide waits, a younger redirect, and reset immediately before commit in each bank. Existing held-EX, forwarded-operand, RVFI order/PC-chain, links, trap, changing-JALR, stale-code, wrong-path retirement/store, and real divider checks remain. Only latency-dependent expectations changed: cold self-jumps miss twice before warm one-retirement-per-cycle execution, and alternating full-tag aliases can hit the older resident entry before its pending replacement commits. The alias test still requires full-width destinations and recovery matching the exact accepted prediction.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings. Channel-0 contract lint and `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Complete cocotb suite: PASS, seven pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU builds, four IF tests, and all 37 pipeline tests. The initial pipeline run exposed the old immediate-training alias expectation; that expectation was corrected as described above and the complete suite rerun successfully. No RTL fix was required.\\n- Unchanged `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, using the automatically selected nret=1 wrapper/configuration. Zero counterexamples and zero formal fix attempts. This is the standard ALTOPS gate; formal-deep was not run for this predictor-only change.\\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim`: PASS, selftest RVFI comparison and scored CoreMark CRC validation. Additional selftest cosim with both bus stalls passed all 115 retirements against the unchanged reference/comparator for ready seeds 0xDEADBEEF, 1, and 42.\\n- Unchanged FPGA evaluation: PASS, all three seeds routed successfully. Both workloads separately passed EBREAK completion, bounds (`oob=false`), UART validation, exact CRCs, and positive start/stop timing brackets.\\n\\nSynthesized-netlist inspection found exactly 464 scalar metadata FFs and 104 distinct bank-command tag FFs, with four distinct bank payload enables. All 272 examined command-payload data/enable pins are independent of table metadata. All 976 examined metadata data/control pins have combinational sources restricted to their own row state, their own bank's registered command, and reset: no live resolve-PC or resolve-flag source remains. Each bank tag reaches only its four consecutive metadata rows. The target store remains eight `RAM16SDP4` blocks, all named under `cpu.u_if.target_q`, sharing one write enable. Their write address/data come from the registered target command; their write-enable cones are independent of prior metadata and live resolution inputs.\\n\\nFinal measurements use the prior winner's OSS CAD Suite Yosys 0.64+149, git `2dc69a757-dirty`, OSS Verilator 5.047, unchanged Gowin synthesis and single-retirement FPGA wrapper, device/constraints, and nextpnr seeds 1/2/3. A preliminary synthesis selected Homebrew Yosys through the shell PATH; it was superseded before FPGA evaluation by an explicit OSS-toolchain synthesis. All reported timing and structural evidence uses the latter.\\n\\n| Scored 2 KB metric | Prior winner | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Median Fmax | 147.75 MHz | 145.99 MHz |\\n| Fitness | 350.46 iter/s | 346.33671643660585 iter/s |\\n| Bracketed cycles | 4,215,937 | 4,215,262 |\\n| LUT4 | 6,567 | 6,516 |\\n| FF | 2,635 | 2,807 |\\n\\nThe unchanged scored workload is TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, ITERATIONS=10, -O3, both random bus stalls, and ready seed 0xDEADBEEF. Full-precision iterations/cycle is `10 / 4215262 = 2.372331779139707e-6`; fitness is `145.99 * 1e6 * 10 / 4215262`. The evaluator's rounded IPC field was not used. Cycles improve by 675, LUT4 decreases by 51, and FF increases by 172. Candidate timing markers are start=15,229 and stop=4,230,491; total elapsed cycles are not scored.\\n\\nFinal post-route critical paths:\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Source | Destination | Total ns | Logic ns | Routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 145.99 | cpu.u_if.commands[3].tag_q_DFFE_Q_9.Q | cpu.u_if.metadata[15].valid_q_DFFRE_Q.CE | 6.85 | 2.05 | 4.80 |\\n| 2 | 148.81 | cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_160.Q | cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_5.RESET | 6.72 | 2.30 | 4.42 |\\n| 3 | 145.41 | cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_163.Q | cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_161.RESET | 6.88 | 2.15 | 4.72 |\\n\\nThe requested register boundary is present, but the median seed still has a bank-tag-to-metadata-enable path dominated by routing. Compared with the prior median path's 3.05 ns logic plus 3.72 ns routing, logic falls to 2.05 ns while routing rises to 4.80 ns. ID/EX hazard/flush reset paths limit the other two seeds. Placement/routing cost and the extra FFs outweigh the small cycle reduction in this measurement. This implementation therefore does not establish a fitness improvement.\\n\\nThe supplemental 6 KB run was built separately through the unchanged Makefile's `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3` options, with ITERATIONS=10 and ready seed 0xDEADBEEF. Candidate brackets are start=53,747 and stop=30,876,537, giving **30,822,790 cycles**. Exact CRCs are seed=0x8a02, list=0xd4b0, matrix=0xbe52, state=0x5e47, final=0x5949. `Correct operation validated.` is present. Its separate throughput at the candidate median frequency is 47.364304139891296 iter/s; it is not compared with the 2 KB fitness of 350.46. Neither evaluation contract was changed. Both build logs explicitly use -O3, although the unmodified port prints a hardcoded -O2 compiler-flags label.\\n\\nAn independently rebuilt original HEAD bench core ran both exact candidate ELFs with the same ready schedule. Baseline brackets reproduce 4,215,937 cycles for 2 KB and 30,823,120 for 6 KB, so the respective candidate reductions are 675 and 330 cycles. UART and final architectural RVFI records agree; the simulation cycle field differs as expected. Both current ELFs also have byte-identical PT_LOAD segments and entry points to the prior winner's corresponding builds; whole-file hashes differ in non-loaded contents.\\n\\nCurrent ELF SHA-256 values: 2 KB `f1b1abdef49294c67824a7faacf2d8f3eaf64b4657cc577bae28e068702f1466`; 6 KB `4bc18805b3a172aec28d012e2b1d2a820e528266f66c285d6a69bc62dee5bdfb`. Concatenated PT_LOAD data SHA-256 values: 2 KB `e8753ffebc32bb14977fc72c1ca0cfeea2b0ae980e344311eacf3765ea16eedb`; 6 KB `f34c3e24f7dcea0bab50c655d56b4b9d47df646d1c9b01a23715153b0af29ee0`.\\n\\nAll generated validation artifacts and unchanged gate copies are under `$TMPDIR/hyp-20260905-003-r4s2-validation`, with sibling `-baseline` and `-6k` directories. Logs/evidence alongside them use prefix `hyp-20260905-003-r4s2-`: `formal.log`, `unit-final.log`, `synth-oss.log`, `build.log`, `cosim.log`, `fpga.log`, `structure.json`, `timing.json`, `metrics.json`, `workloads.json`, `elf-equivalence.json`, `cycle-comparison.json`, and `stalled-cosim.json`. Staged source and contract files were checked byte-for-byte against the worktree. No generated artifact or helper script was added to the worktree. These notes are at the explicitly requested worktree-root location, which `tools/orchestrator.py` permits and reads through `_read_notes`; the only source changes are the three bench files listed above.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T16:50:31.619380Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r4s2 (regression, -1.18%): Bank-local registered predictor tags driving four metadata rows shortened tag-to-valid-enable logic delay by 1.00 ns but added 1.08 ns of routing, lowering median Fmax from 147.75 to 145.99 MHz despite 675 fewer CoreMark cycles.\"}\n15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Separate ID/EX validity from the wide recovery payload\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current ID/EX register clears its entire packed bundle on reset OR\\nflush. In addition, IF replaces the entire instruction with a NOP on\\nrecovery, putting redirect into instruction decode, register addressing,\\nand load-hazard inputs. Thus a one-instruction squash controls hundreds\\nof payload bits even though only the instruction's validity must change.\\n\\nThe structural history identifies this as a recurring timing limit.\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 removed indexed predictor maintenance from the\\ncritical paths but exposed hazard/divide control into ID/EX resets.\\nThe current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, reports 156.62 MHz median\\nFmax and 371.55 fitness. Its median seed has a 6.39 ns path from\\npredicted_next_pc[0] through mismatch/hazard control to an ID/EX\\noperand register's RESET pin. The other two seeds remain limited by\\nlocal predictor comparisons. Earlier store buffering saved only 0.75%\\nof cycles, and the divider split saved area without timed-cycle gains.\\nSeparating instruction validity from payload transport directly attacks\\nthe measured recovery fanout while retaining the current schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ID/EX register clears its entire packed bundle on reset OR\\nflush. In addition, IF replaces the entire instruction with a NOP on\\nrecovery, putting redirect into instruction decode, register addressing,\\nand load-hazard inputs. Thus a one-instruction squash controls hundreds\\nof payload bits even though only the instruction's validity must change.\\n\\nThe structural history identifies this as a recurring timing limit.\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 removed indexed predictor maintenance from the\\ncritical paths but exposed hazard/divide control into ID/EX resets.\\nThe current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, reports 156.62 MHz median\\nFmax and 371.55 fitness. Its median seed has a 6.39 ns path from\\npredicted_next_pc[0] through mismatch/hazard control to an ID/EX\\noperand register's RESET pin. The other two seeds remain limited by\\nlocal predictor comparisons. Earlier store buffering saved only 0.75%\\nof cycles, and the divider split saved area without timed-cycle gains.\\nSeparating instruction validity from payload transport directly attacks\\nthe measured recovery fanout while retaining the current schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Restructure the IF-to-ID/EX boundary around a separate registered valid\\ntoken. Keep the current pipeline depth, single issue, exact accepted\\nprediction metadata, and one-younger-slot recovery. This experiment\\nchanges squash distribution, with no predictor-policy, arithmetic,\\nmemory-latency, or instruction-scheduling redesign.\\n\\nIn IF, always present raw imem_data as out.instr. Keep out.valid low\\non flush or redirect, and preserve the existing PC update priorities:\\nreset, immediate EX redirect, then accepted predicted advancement.\\nRecovery must still override instruction-bus backpressure. Preserve\\nevery predictor table, pending event, training rule, and commit edge.\\nRemoving the recovery-controlled NOP mux is necessary; splitting only\\nthe ID/EX register would leave a second wide recovery-to-payload path.\\n\\nIn ID, register validity separately from the decoded control and wide\\npayload. The valid register retains the existing reset/flush priority\\nover capture/hold: reset or flush clears it, otherwise !stall captures\\nin.valid, otherwise it holds. Payload registers capture the raw PC,\\nprediction, instruction, immediate, register addresses, operand values,\\nand decoded control whenever !stall, even when flush is asserted.\\nTheir existing reset initialization can remain. Flush must not select\\ntheir reset, data mux, or clock enable. In particular, do not replace\\nthe old wide flush reset with a wide flush-controlled hold enable.\\nKeep the public id_ex_t layout and module ports unchanged.\\n\\nAt the ID/EX output boundary, expose the captured payload unchanged\\nbut present ctrl='0 whenever the REGISTERED valid token is zero.\\nValid instructions see their complete original decoded control.\\nThis compact control mask reproduces the current downstream bubble\\ncontract without zeroing operands, addresses, PCs, or instructions.\\nIt is essential because hazard, forwarding, and memory logic currently\\ninspect control fields independently of retirement validity. Invalid\\nraw stores, loads, jumps, illegal instructions, and DIV/REM encodings\\nmust not request memory, write registers, launch a divide, redirect,\\ntrain the predictor, or retire. Do not mask the entire packed bundle.\\nDecoder legality defaults and all valid-instruction trap rules remain\\nexactly as before.\\n\\nPreserve hazard_unit's raw source-field comparison and stall equations.\\nWith raw IF data, its load-use match must remain independent of\\nredirect, flush, and the combinational IF valid signal; qualifying\\nthat match with recovery-derived validity would reconnect redirect\\nto the wide payload clock enable. A raw match on an unavailable fetch\\nor a killed fetch is harmless: imem backpressure already prevents\\nacceptance, redirect overrides the PC hold, and ID/EX validity clears.\\nA real load-use interlock still creates exactly one bubble. Memory\\nbackpressure and divide waits still hold the active ID/EX payload and\\nvalid token. Preserve EX/MEM drain behavior, accepted divide operands,\\nand MEM/WB's retained forwarding fields when only retirement validity\\nclears during a memory hold; do not gate that retained bypass by its\\ncleared retirement-valid bit.\\n\\nPredict unchanged accepted-fetch, resolution, bus-handshake, and RVFI\\nretirement schedules for identical input streams, with higher Fmax\\nfrom lower recovery fanout. Estimate a three percent fitness gain;\\nit is unmeasured. The new valid-to-control mask can become critical,\\nand the near-equal local predictor paths in the other seeds may cap\\nthe improvement. Inspect synthesized cones and all three routed seeds\\nto confirm that combinational redirect/flush no longer reaches ID/EX\\npayload RESET, CE, or D pins through instruction substitution or\\nhazard qualification. Report the new endpoints and LUT4/FF costs.\\n\\nRun unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, cosim, and FPGA gates. Compare\\noriginal and candidate with the same toolchain and deterministic bus\\nstalls, including cycle-level architectural equivalence checks.\\nKeep nret=1, external ports, ordered RVFI, memory bounds and handshakes,\\nmarker ordering, decoder checks, and real/ALTOPS arithmetic intact.\\nHistorical implementation notes distinguish the scored 2 KB workload\\nbehind 371.55 from the required canonical 6 KB run. Preserve the\\nexisting scored gate and separately validate the 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration\\nrun with both bus stalls, d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, the validation literal,\\nand start/stop bracketing. Compare like workloads using exact\\niterations/cycle times median Fmax; do not change evaluation files.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate registered instruction validity from raw decoded control\\nand wide payload storage. Clear only validity on flush; payload\\ncapture depends on the existing stall signal and reset, never\\nflush. Present a zero ctrl_t for invalid ID/EX tokens using the\\nregistered valid bit, while passing valid-instruction control and\\nall payload fields unchanged. Preserve the packed output layout,\\nvalid priority, reset initialization, and true hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the flush/redirect-controlled instruction-to-NOP mux and\\nalways transport raw imem_data. Preserve invalidation through\\nout.valid, PC priorities, exact prediction metadata, and the\\nentire existing predictor/training implementation. Document that\\ninvalid instruction bits are payload and cannot authorize work.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve the current raw source comparison and stall/flush\\nequations. Update the bubble contract comments for validity-only\\nsquashes, explaining why raw matches during imem stalls or\\nrecovery do not admit work. Keep redirect/flush and IF validity\\nout of the load-match and payload-hold cones; introduce no new\\nsource-use optimization or recovery-dependent stall qualification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Replace the implementation-specific invalid-instruction NOP\\nexpectation with an exact raw-imem-data transport assertion.\\nRetain every validity, PC, prediction, training, reset, and\\nacceptance assertion. Exercise flush and redirect with dangerous\\nraw instruction encodings, concurrent stalls, and pending commits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all architectural and cycle-schedule checks. Add directed\\ntests placing stores, loads, divides, branches, and illegal words\\nin squashed or unavailable fetch slots, including raw source\\nmatches against an advancing load during stale-prediction recovery.\\nCover load-use bubbles, simultaneous bus stalls, held divides,\\nreset during holds, and retained MEM/WB forwarding. Assert no\\ninvalid-token side effects, exactly-once resolution and retirement,\\ncorrect operands/traps/PC chains, unchanged one-slot recovery,\\nand identical architectural traces under matched bus inputs.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate registered instruction validity from raw decoded control\\nand wide payload storage. Clear only validity on flush; payload\\ncapture depends on the existing stall signal and reset, never\\nflush. Present a zero ctrl_t for invalid ID/EX tokens using the\\nregistered valid bit, while passing valid-instruction control and\\nall payload fields unchanged. Preserve the packed output layout,\\nvalid priority, reset initialization, and true hold behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the flush/redirect-controlled instruction-to-NOP mux and\\nalways transport raw imem_data. Preserve invalidation through\\nout.valid, PC priorities, exact prediction metadata, and the\\nentire existing predictor/training implementation. Document that\\ninvalid instruction bits are payload and cannot authorize work.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Preserve the current raw source comparison and stall/flush\\nequations. Update the bubble contract comments for validity-only\\nsquashes, explaining why raw matches during imem stalls or\\nrecovery do not admit work. Keep redirect/flush and IF validity\\nout of the load-match and payload-hold cones; introduce no new\\nsource-use optimization or recovery-dependent stall qualification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Replace the implementation-specific invalid-instruction NOP\\nexpectation with an exact raw-imem-data transport assertion.\\nRetain every validity, PC, prediction, training, reset, and\\nacceptance assertion. Exercise flush and redirect with dangerous\\nraw instruction encodings, concurrent stalls, and pending commits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain all architectural and cycle-schedule checks. Add directed\\ntests placing stores, loads, divides, branches, and illegal words\\nin squashed or unavailable fetch slots, including raw source\\nmatches against an advancing load during stale-prediction recovery.\\nCover load-use bubbles, simultaneous bus stalls, held divides,\\nreset during holds, and retained MEM/WB forwarding. Assert no\\ninvalid-token side effects, exactly-once resolution and retirement,\\ncorrect operands/traps/PC chains, unchanged one-slot recovery,\\nand identical architectural traces under matched bus inputs.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 261.48, \"delta_pct\": -29.62, \"vs_baseline\": -7.55, \"fmax_mhz\": 110.22, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6644, \"ff\": 3166, \"seeds\": [110.22, 103.78, 112.15], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the separate ID/EX valid token and raw IF instruction transport. Functional and schedule checks pass, but this implementation is a measured timing regression: median Fmax is 110.22 MHz versus 156.62 MHz, and scored fitness is 261.478409 versus 371.554603 (-29.625846%). The estimated three percent gain did not materialize. The candidate is left implemented for the orchestrator to evaluate; no evaluation contract was changed.\\n\\n`id_stage.sv` now clocks `reg_q.valid` in its own sequential block: reset or flush clears it, otherwise an unstalled edge captures `in.valid`, otherwise it holds. The other 229 bits retain reset initialization and capture whenever `!stall`, including flush edges. Flush selects neither their reset, data nor enable. The public `id_ex_t` and ports are unchanged. At the output, only the 22-bit `ctrl_t` is zeroed when the registered token is invalid; every other field remains the captured payload.\\n\\n`if_stage.sv` always exports `imem_data`; its validity equation, reset/redirect/accepted-prediction PC priorities, predictor lookup, all metadata and target tables, pending events, training rules and commit edges are unchanged. `hazard_unit.sv` and `core.sv` changed only comments. A comparison with comments and whitespace removed confirmed identical hazard equations and top-level wiring. Decoder, arithmetic, EX/MEM drain, accepted divide operands, retained MEM/WB forwarding and NRET=1 were not changed.\\n\\nThe IF tests replace only the invalid-instruction NOP expectation with exact raw-data transport, retaining all PC/prediction/validity/training assertions. Added directed pending-commit tests cover SW, LW, all four divides/remainders, branches, jumps, illegal words and ECALL under flush, redirect and stalls. The new `test_id_stage.py` checks raw payload capture during flush, hold behavior, reset priority and complete control masking, with directed and deterministic random vectors. Pipeline additions cover dangerous squashed and unavailable slots, raw load-source matches during stale prediction recovery, one load-use bubble, simultaneous bus holds, divide/reset interactions, no invalid-token memory/divide/resolution effects, and original/candidate cycle comparisons. All existing pipeline and decoder checks remain present, including retained MEM/WB forwarding.\\n\\nLocal validation used scratch copies rooted at `$TMPDIR/valid-token-r5s1/`, with separate `original/` and `candidate/` trees. The original RTL is the incoming HEAD, b9a8841, carrying hypothesis hyp-20260905-002-r4s1. Only scratch copies were used for generated formal, cocotb, program and FPGA outputs; the worktree's evaluation files and other cores were not edited. The root notes file is the explicitly requested exception to the RTL/test editing scope.\\n\\n| Gate | Original | Candidate |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Required Verilator lint command | Pass, no warnings | Pass, no warnings |\\n| Complete cocotb suite | 7 pytest runners pass | 8 pytest runners pass |\\n| `bash formal/run_all.sh`, automatic NRET=1 selection | 53 pass, 0 fail | 53 pass, 0 fail |\\n| `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` | Pass, 2 ELFs | Pass, 2 ELFs |\\n| `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv` | 3/3 routes and benchmark pass | 3/3 routes and benchmark pass |\\n| Separate 6 KB, -O3, 10-iteration run with both stalls | Pass | Pass |\\n\\nCandidate local formal status: PASS on the first invocation, zero fix attempts, no counterexample. The unchanged fast gate uses ALTOPS; the complete cocotb suite separately passed its real and ALTOPS ALU builds. The unchanged ISS cosim and benchmark runs use real arithmetic.\\n\\nMatched gates used Verilator 5.046, Yosys 0.64 (d8dab5b32666564eca8e18f412973853ce006e61), nextpnr-himbaechel nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f, and xPack RISC-V GCC 15.2.0. The requested lint command also passed with the workspace's Verilator 5.047. Both variants used identical per-gate toolchains, FPGA wrapper, constraints, route seeds 1/2/3, ELFs and bus PRNG inputs. Reproducing 156.62 MHz, 6,584 LUT4s, 3,194 FFs and 371.554603 fitness on the original confirms the historical comparison.\\n\\nThe new `matched_bus_cycle_equivalence` cocotb test exported the original schedule and compared the candidate against it using `PIPELINE_SCHEDULE_OUTPUT` and `PIPELINE_SCHEDULE_REFERENCE`. Six matched streams cover four deterministic bus-stall phases through loads, stores, divides, remainders, loop exits and start/stop stores, plus stale-load recovery with and without concurrent bus stalls. Comparison includes every fetch PC and prediction, accepted instruction, resolution, predictor commit, active memory request and handshake cycle, divide acceptance, and all recorded RVFI fields including retirement cycle. Both JSON files have SHA-256 `0be8fb87b93a7c103507752580cd1600c74c78f1e4432546ffc7344ba41941db`. Additional full selftest RVFI traces, including cycles, were byte-identical between variants for bus seeds 1, 17, 12345 and 12648430, and each passed the unchanged Python ISS comparison (115 retirements; last cycles 198, 189, 192 and 195 respectively).\\n\\nSynthesis confirms the intended recovery isolation. In an optimized flattened core with both bus-ready inputs unconstrained, combinational fanout analysis stops at sequential cells. Originally redirect reached payload D cones covering 165 bits, EN cones covering all 229 bits, and SRST cones covering all 229 bits. `flush_if` had the same reach; `flush_id` reached all 229 payload SRST inputs. In the candidate, redirect, `flush_if` and `flush_id` reach zero payload D, EN or SRST inputs. They still affect the single valid token. Raw IF instruction bits alias the imem-data bits directly.\\n\\nIn the scored mapped netlist, 212 physical DFFREs implement the surviving/merged ID/EX payload bits in each variant. All 212 candidate RESET pins connect only to `cpu.reset`; all 212 original RESET pins connect to the combined recovery/reset net. Each routed seed was also inspected: all candidate payload RESET pins retain that reset-only connection, and all payload D/CE/RESET drivers match synthesis after verifying and traversing nextpnr's identity LUTs. The three-seed route checks found no changed payload-pin driver. Evidence: `cone-audit.json`, `routed-cone-audit.json`, each variant's `core-opt.json`, and `cores/bench/generated/{synth.json,pnr_seed*/pnr.json}` under the scratch root.\\n\\n| Route seed | Original MHz | Original critical path | Candidate MHz | Candidate critical path |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | --- |\\n| 1 | 157.11 | Predictor row 0 tag[6] Q to counter CE, 6.36 ns | 110.22 | ID/EX PC[3] Q to EX/MEM alu_result[31] D, 9.07 ns |\\n| 2 | 156.62 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[0] Q to ID/EX rs1_val[13] RESET, 6.39 ns | 103.78 | ID/EX PC[0] Q to EX/MEM alu_result[31] D, 9.64 ns |\\n| 3 | 155.67 | Predictor row 12 pending_tag[11] Q to counter CE, 6.42 ns | 112.15 | ID/EX PC[5] Q to EX/MEM alu_result[31] D, 8.92 ns |\\n\\nThe candidate physical endpoints are `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_28.Q`, `_31.Q` and `_26.Q` respectively, all ending at `cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_64.D`. The paths traverse the ALU operand selection, arithmetic and result-selection logic. The median candidate path has 5.84 ns logic and 3.23 ns routing. The original median endpoint is `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_82.RESET`, reached from `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_63.Q`. Recovery is removed from the intended payload cones, but the resulting mapping exposes a longer execute-data path in every seed; the limiting paths do not start at the registered valid bit. No arithmetic or scheduling redesign was attempted to hide this outcome.\\n\\n| Routed resource | Original | Candidate | Change |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| LUT4 | 6584 | 6644 | +60 |\\n| FF | 3194 | 3166 | -28 |\\n\\nThe scored workload remains the unchanged 2 KB, -O3, ten-iteration gate with `--bench --istall --dstall` and default bus seed 0xDEADBEEF. Its CRC gate passed unchanged. Both variants take exactly 4,215,262 marker-bracketed cycles. Fitness is computed from exact `10 / 4215262` iterations/cycle, not the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark: 2e-06` display.\\n\\nThe separate required canonical workload was built from unchanged sources/Makefile in `canonical/` with `make -B -f bench/programs/Makefile bench/programs/coremark.elf COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`; ITERATIONS remains 10. Both variants ran that identical ELF with both bus stalls and seed 0xDEADBEEF. Start at 0x10000100 was cycle 53,747; stop at 0x10000104 was cycle 30,876,537, giving exactly 30,822,790 timed cycles in both. EBREAK completed at cycle 30,883,293 with no OOB flag. UART and benchmark metadata match exactly: seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949, ten iterations, and `Correct operation validated.`. The untouched port prints a static `-O2` compiler-flags string; the recorded compiler invocation proves the actual build used `-O3 -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`. Its displayed CoreMark Size=2000 is the per-algorithm share of the 6000-byte total.\\n\\n| Like-for-like workload | Timed cycles, both variants | Exact iterations/cycle | Original fitness | Candidate fitness |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Scored 2 KB | 4215262 | 10 / 4215262 | 371.554603 | 261.478409 |\\n| Required canonical 6 KB | 30822790 | 10 / 30822790 | 50.813051 | 35.759255 |\\n\\nThese fitness values multiply the exact iteration/cycle fraction by the corresponding three-seed median Fmax in Hz. They do not mix the 2 KB and 6 KB cycle counts. The scored ELF SHA-256 is `d74b3e935ec6b7302df7413a48c743209d2beb9d450ceec4d832a1cfd6eb1050`; canonical ELF SHA-256 is `ee05de62d7579bf11d4f8048a15a2e1aa8ce731d86e414e58d20d46c3ce2860b`.\\n\\nAdditional evidence under the scratch root: `timing-comparison.json`, `canonical-comparison.json`, `selftest-stalled-comparison.json`, both `schedules.json` files, and each variant's `formal-gate.log`, `cocotb.log`, `equivalence.log`, `cosim-gate.log`, `fpga-gate.log`, `synth.log`, `canonical.log` and per-seed `nextpnr.log`. The candidate scratch RTL was byte-checked against every final worktree RTL file. `git diff --check` passed. The only departures from the advisory file list are a comment correction in `core.sv`, the focused ID-stage unit test, and this required implementation note.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T17:48:06.642623Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r5s1 (regression, -29.62%): Raw IF transport plus separate ID/EX validity and output `ctrl` masking removed recovery-to-payload fanout but exposed longer PC-to-EX/MEM ALU paths in all three routed seeds, cutting median Fmax from 156.62 to 110.22 MHz with unchanged CoreMark cycles; eliminating a reset bottleneck can worsen overall timing through remapping.\"}\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Separate predictor tag validation from ordered metadata commit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner scores 388.66 iter/s at 163.83 MHz. Its\\npredictor already captures resolution events into private row registers,\\nbut each row still combines a 26-bit tag comparison, command decoding,\\ncounter selection, and metadata write control within one clock period.\\nRegistering the comparison result would divide this remaining maintenance\\npath without adding an instruction stage or extending EX recovery.\\n\\nThe structural history supports this specific timing direction:\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 separated indexed row reads from updates, and\\nhyp-20260905-002-r4s1 raised median Fmax from 147.75 to 156.62 MHz by\\ncapturing events before row-local updates. The latter's implementation\\nhistory identifies local tag-to-counter-enable paths in two routed seeds.\\nThese are historical bottlenecks, not a claim that a current route has\\nbeen reproduced. The winning r5s0 subsequently reduced ID/EX recovery\\nfanout. In contrast, structural r5s1 lost 29.62% fitness after changing\\nraw IF transport and validity masking, and r1s1 store buffering saved\\nonly 0.75% of cycles. Further partitioning maintenance work is therefore\\nthe most promising structural experiment while retaining the winning\\ninstruction pipeline and its bubble-control implementation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner scores 388.66 iter/s at 163.83 MHz. Its\\npredictor already captures resolution events into private row registers,\\nbut each row still combines a 26-bit tag comparison, command decoding,\\ncounter selection, and metadata write control within one clock period.\\nRegistering the comparison result would divide this remaining maintenance\\npath without adding an instruction stage or extending EX recovery.\\n\\nThe structural history supports this specific timing direction:\\nhyp-20260905-002-r3s1 separated indexed row reads from updates, and\\nhyp-20260905-002-r4s1 raised median Fmax from 147.75 to 156.62 MHz by\\ncapturing events before row-local updates. The latter's implementation\\nhistory identifies local tag-to-counter-enable paths in two routed seeds.\\nThese are historical bottlenecks, not a claim that a current route has\\nbeen reproduced. The winning r5s0 subsequently reduced ID/EX recovery\\nfanout. In contrast, structural r5s1 lost 29.62% fitness after changing\\nraw IF transport and validity masking, and r1s1 store buffering saved\\nonly 0.75% of cycles. Further partitioning maintenance work is therefore\\nthe most promising structural experiment while retaining the winning\\ninstruction pipeline and its bubble-control implementation.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one register boundary between row-local tag validation and predictor\\nmetadata commit in rtl/if_stage.sv. This is one structural retiming of\\nthe existing training transaction. Retain the sixteen direct-mapped rows,\\nPC[5:2] index, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, two-bit counter\\npolicy, allocation rules, and committed-state-only fetch lookup.\\n\\nUse three edges for an accepted event E. At edge N, the existing private\\nrow capture records E's tag and command. At edge N+1, transfer that event\\ninto a new commit register and capture one hit bit computed against the\\nrow's logical tag/valid state AFTER the older event committing on that\\nsame edge. At edge N+2, apply E using its registered hit, command, and\\ntag, together with the row's then-current two-bit counter. The existing\\ndesign commits at N+1, so this adds exactly one training-visibility cycle.\\nCapture, validate, and commit must overlap at one event per clock,\\nincluding an uninterrupted stream of resolutions to the same row.\\n\\nResolve the validation-stage read-after-write hazard locally. Let C be\\nthe older event currently in this row's commit register and P the event\\nin its existing pending register. Compute two full-width comparisons in\\nparallel: table_tag == P.tag and C.tag == P.tag. If C.valid and C.command\\nis JUMP or TAKEN, P's effective hit is the second comparison because C\\nallocates or refreshes a valid row with C.tag on the validation edge.\\nOtherwise, if C.valid, C.command is INVALIDATE, and C.hit is true, P's\\neffective hit is false because C clears the row. In all other cases use\\ntable_valid && (table_tag == P.tag). Register this effective hit with P.\\nSelection between comparator results is narrow; do not mux a 26-bit\\neffective tag ahead of one equality comparator. All forwarding is private\\nto the row and concerns tag/valid state only. No live resolve input or\\nindexed metadata read may enter the validation or commit datapath.\\n\\nAt commit, use the registered hit without re-comparing tags. JUMP writes\\nvalid=1, the event tag, target, and counter=11. TAKEN writes valid=1,\\nthe event tag and target, and counter=10 on a registered miss or a\\nsaturating increment of the current counter on a registered hit.\\nNOT_TAKEN decrements the current counter with saturation only on a\\nregistered hit and retains tag, valid, and target. INVALIDATE clears\\nvalid only on a registered hit. Preserve the current field-specific\\nupdate policy. Do not snapshot or forward the counter into validation:\\nin-order commits ensure the current counter already includes all older\\nevents. This avoids a new counter-update feedback bypass. After the\\npipeline drains, every table field must equal sequential application of\\nthe accepted resolution stream, even for alternating full-tag aliases.\\n\\nAdd a matching second delay register to the single indexed target-write\\npath, carrying target-valid, index, and target. The target and its metadata\\nmust become visible on the same N+2 edge, including consecutive writes\\nto the same index with different tags or JALR destinations. Do not let a\\nnewer target become visible under an older committed tag. The estimated\\nextra state is 16*(1 valid + 26 tag + 2 command + 1 hit) plus\\n(1 valid + 4 index + 30 target), or 515 source-level bits before mapping.\\n\\nRefresh both event-stage validity bits every clock independently of IF\\nstalls, memory holds, divide waits, and younger redirects. An accepted\\nolder event commits once even if instruction execution subsequently\\nstalls. Keep EX resolve_valid qualification unchanged so held instructions\\ncannot enqueue repeatedly. Reset clears every table-valid and both stages\\nof event/target validity, suppresses commit, and prevents pre-reset work\\nfrom repopulating the table. Invalid pipeline payload is never consulted\\nwithout its corresponding validity qualification.\\n\\nKeep the architectural instruction pipeline unchanged: no fetch bypass\\nfrom pending events, no training backpressure, no delayed EX redirect,\\nand no change to the exact predicted_next_pc captured with an instruction.\\nRecovery still compares that prediction with the resolved architectural\\ndestination and has the existing one-younger-slot penalty. Warm loops\\nmust retain one-instruction-per-cycle throughput; cold or rapidly changing\\nbranches can incur another miss because training becomes visible later.\\nPreserve IF's instruction-to-NOP behavior, the r5s0 split of ID/EX control\\nand payload updates, forwarding retention, load-use and bus interlocks,\\nand the real/ALTOPS multiplier and eight-edge divider schedules.\\n\\nPredict an unmeasured four-percent fitness gain from higher median Fmax\\nwith nearly unchanged CoreMark cycles. The added alias comparator,\\nroughly 515 extra state bits, placement changes, and delayed training\\ncan erase that gain; inspect all three post-route paths to determine\\nwhether tag comparison is actually separated from counter write control.\\nDo not add keep constraints, predictor-policy changes, or frontend\\nredesigns to this experiment. Success is a higher exact\\niterations/bracketed-cycles times three-seed median Fmax than the incoming\\nwinner under the identical workload, toolchain, and evaluation settings.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and FPGA gates.\\nPreserve nret=1 and all top-level ports, strict channel-0 retirement order,\\ndecoder legality and traps, memory bounds, stable stalled requests, and\\nexactly-once MMIO/marker accesses. Existing history distinguishes the\\nscored 2 KB run underlying 388.66 from the required canonical 6 KB run;\\nreport like-for-like results separately without altering the evaluator.\\nAlso validate the unchanged 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration workload with both\\nbus stalls, CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, Correct operation validated., and timing\\nstrictly between start/stop marker handshakes. All hard invariants and\\nprotected files remain intact.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a per-row validation-to-commit register containing event-valid,\\nfull tag, command, and effective hit. Forward the older committing\\nevent's tag/valid effects into validation with parallel equalities;\\ncommit against the current counter using only the registered hit.\\nExtend the indexed target-write pipeline by the same one edge.\\nPreserve one-event-per-clock throughput, reset cancellation, exact\\nsequential update semantics, fetch lookup, and immediate recovery.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent sequential policy model to the additional\\ncommit delay and check intermediate committed state on every edge.\\nExercise uninterrupted same-row streams: allocate A then update A;\\nallocate A then allocate B then update A; invalidate a matching tag\\nthen reallocate; invalidate a different tag; jump followed by taken\\nand not-taken branches; saturation and changing targets. Cover all\\ncommand combinations, full-width aliases, different rows, idle drain,\\narbitrary stalls/redirects, and reset with both event stages occupied.\\nAssert atomic tag/target visibility, no fetch bypass, no dropped or\\nduplicated updates, and equality to the policy model after drain.\\nUpdate only intentional training-latency expectations while retaining\\nevery architectural and predictor-policy assertion.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt training observation to the extra maintenance stage and add\\ndirected tight-loop, alias, changing-JALR-target, stale-entry, and\\ntrapping-control tests with random imem/dmem stalls and divides.\\nCheck each redirect against the prediction actually accepted with\\nthat instruction, exactly-once resolution/retirement, ordered RVFI\\noperands and PC chains, wrong-path side-effect suppression, and\\ncontinued commit of older events during holds or younger recovery.\\nRetain the one-slot recovery and warm-loop throughput checks, all\\nmemory/marker/decoder/arithmetic checks, and existing forwarding\\ncoverage. Compare architectural traces rather than requiring the\\nold prediction or cycle schedule after the intentional latency change.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a per-row validation-to-commit register containing event-valid,\\nfull tag, command, and effective hit. Forward the older committing\\nevent's tag/valid effects into validation with parallel equalities;\\ncommit against the current counter using only the registered hit.\\nExtend the indexed target-write pipeline by the same one edge.\\nPreserve one-event-per-clock throughput, reset cancellation, exact\\nsequential update semantics, fetch lookup, and immediate recovery.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent sequential policy model to the additional\\ncommit delay and check intermediate committed state on every edge.\\nExercise uninterrupted same-row streams: allocate A then update A;\\nallocate A then allocate B then update A; invalidate a matching tag\\nthen reallocate; invalidate a different tag; jump followed by taken\\nand not-taken branches; saturation and changing targets. Cover all\\ncommand combinations, full-width aliases, different rows, idle drain,\\narbitrary stalls/redirects, and reset with both event stages occupied.\\nAssert atomic tag/target visibility, no fetch bypass, no dropped or\\nduplicated updates, and equality to the policy model after drain.\\nUpdate only intentional training-latency expectations while retaining\\nevery architectural and predictor-policy assertion.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt training observation to the extra maintenance stage and add\\ndirected tight-loop, alias, changing-JALR-target, stale-entry, and\\ntrapping-control tests with random imem/dmem stalls and divides.\\nCheck each redirect against the prediction actually accepted with\\nthat instruction, exactly-once resolution/retirement, ordered RVFI\\noperands and PC chains, wrong-path side-effect suppression, and\\ncontinued commit of older events during holds or younger recovery.\\nRetain the one-slot recovery and warm-loop throughput checks, all\\nmemory/marker/decoder/arithmetic checks, and existing forwarding\\ncoverage. Compare architectural traces rather than requiring the\\nold prediction or cycle schedule after the intentional latency change.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 230.66, \"delta_pct\": -40.65, \"vs_baseline\": -18.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 97.2, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4214035, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 7197, \"ff\": 3685, \"seeds\": [105.33, 97.2, 96.29], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T18:42:57.903603Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6}\n21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Move PC-relative address generation across the ID/EX boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 388.66 iter/s at 163.83 MHz. Execute\\nstill computes PC+4 and PC+immediate, and AUIPC selects PC onto the\\nshared ALU operand bus before arithmetic and result selection. These\\ncalculations depend only on instruction metadata already available in\\ndecode, so their register boundary can move without delaying execution\\nor changing branch prediction.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment history favor a different\\npartition from further predictor-maintenance staging. Structural r6s1\\nadded tag-validation staging but fell to 97.20 MHz with almost unchanged\\ncycles. Structural r5s1 exposed PC-to-EX/MEM ALU critical paths in all\\nthree seeds; the r6s2 lesson also reports full-width target generation\\ninto pending_target_q.D as critical. These are observations on related\\ncandidates, not a reproduced timing diagnosis of the current winner.\\nNo recorded bench hypothesis has moved PC-relative arithmetic into ID.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current bench winner reaches 388.66 iter/s at 163.83 MHz. Execute\\nstill computes PC+4 and PC+immediate, and AUIPC selects PC onto the\\nshared ALU operand bus before arithmetic and result selection. These\\ncalculations depend only on instruction metadata already available in\\ndecode, so their register boundary can move without delaying execution\\nor changing branch prediction.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment history favor a different\\npartition from further predictor-maintenance staging. Structural r6s1\\nadded tag-validation staging but fell to 97.20 MHz with almost unchanged\\ncycles. Structural r5s1 exposed PC-to-EX/MEM ALU critical paths in all\\nthree seeds; the r6s2 lesson also reports full-width target generation\\ninto pending_target_q.D as critical. These are observations on related\\ncandidates, not a reproduced timing diagnosis of the current winner.\\nNo recorded bench hypothesis has moved PC-relative arithmetic into ID.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Retime the operand-independent PC arithmetic from EX into the existing\\ndecode-to-execute register. Add exactly two 32-bit ID/EX payload fields:\\npc_plus4 = in.pc + 32'd4 and pc_relative = in.pc + decoded immediate,\\nboth computed modulo 2^32 in id_stage. Retain the original PC, immediate,\\ninstruction, operands, and exact predicted_next_pc. This is one\\nstructural redistribution of work across an existing pipeline boundary;\\nit adds no instruction stage, issue bubble, or training stage.\\n\\nGive both new fields the same update discipline as the winning r5s0\\npayload: deterministic zero on reset, hold only on the existing backend\\nstall, and otherwise capture with the rest of the instruction payload.\\nFlush continues to clear every control bit and valid independently of\\npayload capture. Preserve flush-versus-hold priority and the current IF\\ninstruction-to-NOP behavior. Compute the sums in parallel with register\\nreads and control decode; no register operand or forwarding selection\\nbelongs in either sum. A held instruction must retain its own sums\\neven when the live fetch data, prediction, or immediate changes.\\n\\nIn EX, use registered pc_relative for branch targets, direct JAL\\ntargets, and the AUIPC writeback result. Use registered pc_plus4 for\\nJAL/JALR link values and the default architectural next PC, including\\nnot-taken branches, traps, ordinary instructions, and completed divides\\nrecovering from stale predictions. Preserve full-width target bits\\nuntil the existing alignment checks; never round a misaligned direct\\ntarget into a legal one.\\n\\nRemove the is_auipc ? PC : rs1 selection from the shared ALU A input:\\nordinary ALU and M-extension operations take the existing forwarded\\nrs1, while AUIPC selects its registered pc_relative at the EX result\\nselection beside the jump-link selection. Thus ID/EX PC no longer\\ndrives the common ALU operand arithmetic, and direct-target calculation\\nno longer adds a carry chain before EX recovery or predictor-event\\ncapture. Keep the existing ALU B selection and all ALU operations.\\n\\nJALR remains in EX, using the actual post-forward rs1 plus the held\\nimmediate and clearing bit zero exactly as today. Its base must not\\ncome from an early regfile snapshot. Branch conditions also stay in EX\\nwith existing operand forwarding. Keep misalignment/trap qualification,\\nresult-write suppression, resolved pc_next, and the exact comparison\\nagainst the prediction accepted with that instruction. Predictor row\\norganization, full tags, counters, event timing, and target commit are\\nunchanged. Resolve and retire each advancing instruction exactly once.\\n\\nExpect an unmeasured five-percent fitness improvement from shorter EX\\narithmetic and control paths with identical instruction scheduling.\\nRisks are a new decode-immediate-adder critical path, the extra result\\nselection for AUIPC, roughly 64 extra payload bits before mapping, and\\nunchanged predictor or JALR paths limiting Fmax. This experiment must\\nstand on its own; retain the present frontend, predictor, arithmetic\\nengines, forwarding, load-use interlock, and bus-hold protocol.\\n\\nValidate the changed stage boundary with ID and pipeline cocotb tests,\\nthen run the unchanged lint, formal, stalled cosim, and FPGA gates.\\nRequire unchanged architectural traces and cycle schedules for identical\\ninstruction streams and bus-ready sequences. Inspect all three routed\\ncritical paths to see whether moving the adders improved the actual\\nbottleneck. Compare exact iterations/bracketed cycles times three-seed\\nmedian Fmax against the incoming winner using the identical ELF and\\nevaluation settings; do not use the rounded core.yaml iterations/cycle\\nvalue. Record the working-set configuration, keeping the historical\\nscored workload and any separate canonical 6 KB run distinct. Validate\\nthe required 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration run with both bus stalls, CRCs\\nd4b0/be52/5e47, and the literal Correct operation validated.\\n\\nPreserve nret=1 and the entire external port contract, channel-0 RVFI\\norder and operand values, decoder legality and trap checks, memory\\nbounds and side-effect ordering, and start/stop marker handshakes.\\nReal and ALTOPS arithmetic, including the eight-edge divider schedule,\\nare unchanged. Only bench RTL and its permitted unit tests are targets;\\nno evaluation, wrapper, benchmark, or other-core files change.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend id_ex_t with 32-bit pc_plus4 and pc_relative payload fields.\\nKeep original metadata and control fields and the other pipeline\\nbundle layouts intact. Document that these sums belong to the\\ncaptured instruction and are available without EX arithmetic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the two full-width PC sums from in.pc and the existing\\ndecoded immediate, and capture them in the payload sequential block\\nwith reset-only clearing and the existing backend-hold enable.\\nPreserve the separate control/valid flush logic and its priorities.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace PC+4 and PC+immediate consumers with their ID/EX fields.\\nRoute AUIPC's registered sum into the EX/MEM result selection and\\nremove PC selection ahead of the common ALU A operand. Retain\\nforwarded JALR addition, bit-zero clearing, branch comparisons,\\nalignment traps, exact prediction recovery, divider handshakes,\\nand every RVFI operand and next-PC field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend payload assertions for the two sums and retain every existing\\nreset/flush/stall combination. Add independently decoded B/J/U\\nimmediate vectors with positive and negative offsets, carry across\\nbit 16 and bit 31, modulo-32-bit wraparound, and unaligned targets.\\nUse synthetic stage inputs for arbitrary full-width PCs. Check\\natomic capture with instruction metadata and stability through holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update _id_fields and payload-input expectations for the added fields\\nwithout weakening control, forwarding, or prediction assertions.\\nCover AUIPC followed immediately by dependent ALU, load/store, and\\nJALR instructions; direct jumps and taken/not-taken branches; link\\nvalues, wrong-path stores, stale predictions, and misalignment traps.\\nExercise imem/dmem stalls, held instructions, reset, and divide\\nwaits. Keep the predictor reference model and timing expectations\\nunchanged, and assert exact channel-0 order, PC chains, operands,\\nand memory effects under both zero-wait and stalled execution.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend id_ex_t with 32-bit pc_plus4 and pc_relative payload fields.\\nKeep original metadata and control fields and the other pipeline\\nbundle layouts intact. Document that these sums belong to the\\ncaptured instruction and are available without EX arithmetic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the two full-width PC sums from in.pc and the existing\\ndecoded immediate, and capture them in the payload sequential block\\nwith reset-only clearing and the existing backend-hold enable.\\nPreserve the separate control/valid flush logic and its priorities.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace PC+4 and PC+immediate consumers with their ID/EX fields.\\nRoute AUIPC's registered sum into the EX/MEM result selection and\\nremove PC selection ahead of the common ALU A operand. Retain\\nforwarded JALR addition, bit-zero clearing, branch comparisons,\\nalignment traps, exact prediction recovery, divider handshakes,\\nand every RVFI operand and next-PC field.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend payload assertions for the two sums and retain every existing\\nreset/flush/stall combination. Add independently decoded B/J/U\\nimmediate vectors with positive and negative offsets, carry across\\nbit 16 and bit 31, modulo-32-bit wraparound, and unaligned targets.\\nUse synthetic stage inputs for arbitrary full-width PCs. Check\\natomic capture with instruction metadata and stability through holds.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update _id_fields and payload-input expectations for the added fields\\nwithout weakening control, forwarding, or prediction assertions.\\nCover AUIPC followed immediately by dependent ALU, load/store, and\\nJALR instructions; direct jumps and taken/not-taken branches; link\\nvalues, wrong-path stores, stale predictions, and misalignment traps.\\nExercise imem/dmem stalls, held instructions, reset, and divide\\nwaits. Keep the predictor reference model and timing expectations\\nunchanged, and assert exact channel-0 order, PC chains, operands,\\nand memory effects under both zero-wait and stalled execution.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 345.41, \"delta_pct\": -11.13, \"vs_baseline\": 22.13, \"fmax_mhz\": 145.6, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6554, \"ff\": 3233, \"seeds\": [150.65, 145.6, 145.14], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the PC-relative ID/EX retiming as proposed. All correctness gates passed, but the measured fitness regressed by 11.1273881462%. The candidate remains implemented for the orchestrator to evaluate; no unrelated optimization or predictor change was added.\\n\\nChanged only cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv, id_stage.sv, ex_stage.sv and the permitted test_id_stage.py / test_pipeline.py, plus this requested writeup. The ID/EX bundle has exactly two additional 32-bit fields, pc_plus4 and pc_relative. ID computes both from the incoming PC and decoded immediate, modulo 2^32, alongside decode and register reads. Both fields reset to zero, hold only under the existing backend stall, and otherwise capture with the payload, including on flush. The separate control/valid flush logic, flush-over-hold priority, and IF instruction-to-NOP behavior are unchanged.\\n\\nEX uses the registered relative sum for branches, direct JAL and AUIPC, and the registered PC+4 for jump links and the default architectural next PC. The shared ALU A port now takes forwarded rs1 directly. JALR still adds post-forward rs1 to the held immediate in EX and clears only bit zero. Target alignment checks, traps, write suppression, exact accepted-prediction comparison, forwarding, predictor event/commit timing, load-use interlock, bus protocol, retirement and both arithmetic implementations are unchanged. The external port contract and nret=1 are unchanged. There are no architectural deviations from the proposal.\\n\\nValidation results:\\n\\n- The exact requested Verilator lint command passed without warnings: `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`.\\n- The full bench pytest suite passed all 9 runners, including real and ALTOPS ALU tests. After adding the final stalled stale-prediction cases, the pipeline runner passed again: 39 cocotb tests. The ID runner passed both tests. Existing assertions and the predictor reference model/timing expectations were retained.\\n- ID tests independently decode immediates and exercise 300 B/J/U instruction/PC combinations, with positive/negative offsets, bit-16/bit-31 carries, modulo wraparound, arbitrary unaligned PCs/targets, and two holds per captured instruction while live instruction, prediction, validity and operand inputs change. The original complete reset/flush/stall matrix remains.\\n- Pipeline tests cover immediate AUIPC dependencies through ALU, load/store base, store data, JALR and DIVU forwarding. Directed execution crosses 0x00010000, generates bit-31 and wraparound results, checks links, taken/not-taken branches, negative and positive misaligned direct targets, JALR bit-zero clearing, stale AUIPC/divide/trap predictions, wrong-path stores, simultaneous bus stalls, payload holds, resets and marker writes. Channel-0 order, PC chains, operands and memory effects remain checked.\\n- Local formal status: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, using the unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` with RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl, CORE_NAME=bench and JOBS=8; nret=1 selected wrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg. No counterexample and no RTL fix attempts were needed. An initial setup launch exited before generating checks because the default Python lacked PyYAML; prepending the installed Conda Python to PATH resolved this without changing any gate file.\\n- The unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench` gate passed both ELFs. Separately, the unchanged Python ISS comparison passed all 115 selftest retirements with zero wait states and with both bus stalls. The incoming and candidate selftest records, including retirement cycles, compared exactly equal.\\n- The unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench` gate completed successfully for both the incoming snapshot and candidate, using NRET=1 and BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv. All six routes succeeded. The reproduced incoming result exactly matches the logged winner's seeds, cycles and resources.\\n\\nEvaluation used unchanged protected source copies in the permitted TMPDIR, with isolated output directories. No evaluation, formal, wrapper, benchmark, constraint or other-core source file in the worktree was changed. Existing tooling: Verilator 5.047 devel v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6 (mod); Yosys 0.64+149, 2dc69a757-dirty; nextpnr 0.10-45-g98c18d7f; xPack RISC-V GCC 15.2.0. FPGA device/constraints and seeds 1, 2, 3 are the unchanged gate defaults. Stalled simulation uses --istall --dstall and the harness's default 0xDEADBEEF LFSR seed, with a 50,000,000-cycle ceiling.\\n\\nThe historical scored workload is TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=2000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10. The separately required canonical workload is TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000, -O3, ITERATIONS=10, built by passing the existing Makefile overrides in a scratch source copy. The canonical UART prints `CoreMark Size : 2000` because this is the per-algorithm allocation within the 6000-byte total; its banner identifies the 6k performance run. The protected UART compiler-flags string still says -O2; the actual recorded compiler command uses -O3. Neither string was edited.\\n\\nBoth designs consumed the identical ELF for each comparison. Independently built 2 KB ELFs initially differed only in temporary-object symbol names, with identical loadable segments; the same complete ELF was then supplied to both scored gates. ELF SHA256:\\n\\n- 2 KB: `6c6732c9843736caeac05310d0a699197c9fe6a37f16fbf5e4923c63a154b5f2`\\n- 6 KB: `69953e959fdf71d4837912ff8055518269f93eb0cdaef0e56d34b0ac63caf903`\\n\\nEvery row below is identical for incoming and candidate. Counts are between accepted start/stop marker handshakes, not total elapsed cycles.\\n\\n| Total working set | Bus readiness | Start cycle | Stop cycle | Bracketed cycles | Exact iterations/cycle |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2000 bytes, historical score | zero wait | 11,448 | 3,218,135 | 3,206,687 | 10 / 3,206,687 |\\n| 2000 bytes, historical score | both random stalls | 15,229 | 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 10 / 4,215,262 |\\n| 6000 bytes, separate canonical run | zero wait | 40,153 | 23,391,752 | 23,351,599 | 10 / 23,351,599 |\\n| 6000 bytes, separate canonical run | both random stalls | 53,747 | 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 10 / 30,822,790 |\\n\\nFull emitted RVFI traces were streamed and SHA256-compared between the two implementations for all four rows, including every record's cycle field, operands, memory fields and channel-0 order. All hashes, record counts, final retirements and marker records matched. Each 2 KB trace contained 2,937,037 retirements; each 6 KB trace contained 22,093,920. Each trace had exactly one start and one stop marker retirement with full word write masks. Both implementations completed in bounds. This establishes unchanged observed architectural traces and scheduling for identical instruction streams and ready sequences, beyond merely matching total cycles.\\n\\nThe canonical 6 KB runs, including both bus stalls, produced seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47 and crcfinal=5949, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. The historical 2 KB runs passed the unchanged evaluator's separate e9f5/e714/1fd7/8e3a/fcaf CRC expectations. The evaluator's CRC table was not changed to conflate the workloads.\\n\\n| Metric | Incoming r5s0, reproduced | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Routed Fmax, seed 1 | 163.83 MHz | 150.65 MHz |\\n| Routed Fmax, seed 2 | 155.33 MHz | 145.60 MHz |\\n| Routed Fmax, seed 3 | 168.55 MHz | 145.14 MHz |\\n| Three-seed median | 163.83 MHz | 145.60 MHz |\\n| LUT4 | 6618 | 6554 |\\n| FF | 3198 | 3233 |\\n| Historical scored bracketed cycles | 4,215,262 | 4,215,262 |\\n| Exact calculated historical fitness | 388.659115376458 iter/s | 345.411507042741 iter/s |\\n| Separate 6 KB stalled throughput | 53.152229243362 iter/s | 47.237774387069 iter/s |\\n\\nFitness was calculated as `median_Fmax_MHz * 1,000,000 * 10 / bracketed_cycles`, never from core.yaml or the evaluator's rounded 0.000002 iterations/cycle. Relative fitness change is -11.127388146249%. The two source-level fields add 64 payload bits; after mapping, total FF count rises by 35 and LUT4 count falls by 64.\\n\\nAll three candidate post-route critical paths were inspected and their endpoints mapped through synth.json:\\n\\n| Seed | Source | Endpoint | Logic / routing | Total |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[25], id_ex_w[255] | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[1] D, id_ex_w[51], through prediction recovery and decode | 2.98 / 3.66 ns | 6.64 ns |\\n| 2 | predictor row 0 tag_q[6] | row 0 counter_q[0] CE | 2.79 / 4.08 ns | 6.87 ns |\\n| 3 | predictor row 2 tag_q[12] | row 2 counter_q[0] CE | 2.32 / 4.57 ns | 6.89 ns |\\n\\nThe reproduced incoming critical paths were ctrl.alu_op[4] to EX/MEM alu_result[25].D (seed 1, 2.87 ns logic + 3.24 ns routing), predicted_next_pc[3] to ID/EX valid.RESET (seed 2, 2.77 + 3.67 ns), and ctrl.alu_op[2] to EX/MEM alu_result[25].D (seed 3, 2.78 + 3.15 ns). Thus the source retiming removed the intended EX PC arithmetic, but did not improve the measured overall bottleneck. Recovery/decode and unchanged predictor maintenance dominate the candidate's reported worst paths. The new decode adder and AUIPC result mux are not the reported worst path in these seeds. No claim is made that the historical related-candidate paths diagnosed this winner.\\n\\nThe main concern is the measured timing/fitness regression despite identical scheduling and a small LUT reduction. The proposed five-percent improvement was not observed. Real and ALTOPS arithmetic and the eight-edge divider schedule passed their existing tests and were not modified; formal-deep was not run because no arithmetic implementation changed.\\n\\nScratch evidence directory:\\n\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/pc-retime-r7s1-ob5xe6pv`\\n\\nEvidence includes unit-tests.log, pipeline-tests-final.log, selftest-differential.json, coremark-trace-differential.json, coremark-validation.json, and each design's fpga-gate.log. Candidate formal-gate.log records all 53 passes; candidate cosim-gate.log records the unchanged cosim result. Routed reports and synth.json are under each design's cores/bench/generated/ directory. No helper scripts were added to the worktree.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T19:19:11.800142Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r7s1 (regression, -11.13%): Registering `PC+4`/`PC+imm` in ID/EX and removing AUIPC's ALU-input mux left recovery/decode and predictor `tag_q`-to-`counter_q` enable paths as the routed bottlenecks, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 145.60 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; EX adder removal alone does not guarantee a timing gain.\"}\n24:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Retime operand dependency matching across the ID/EX boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current forwarding path compares two five-bit EX source addresses\\nagainst both downstream destination registers, resolves writer priority,\\nand selects operands before ALU arithmetic, branch comparison, and JALR\\ntarget generation. The r7s0 implementation notes identify EX/MEM rd\\nthrough forwarding and the JALR adder into pending_target_q as the worst\\npath in all three candidate seeds. The r4s0 lesson also exposed forwarding\\nbefore branch comparison. These are related-candidate measurements, not\\nproof that forwarding is the incoming winner's worst path: the reproduced\\nwinner instead has ALU-control and prediction-recovery endpoints.\\n\\nThe structural history includes store decoupling, divider separation,\\npredictor-event pipelines, recovery distribution, and PC-adder retiming,\\nbut no retiming of operand dependency comparisons. Further predictor\\nvalidation stages and PC precomputation regressed substantially. Test\\nmoving dependency detection across an existing instruction boundary:\\nthe incoming instruction's sources and its two preceding destinations\\nare already available before ID/EX capture. Four registered comparison\\nbits can remove address equality from the execute operand path without\\nincreasing instruction latency or registering a wide forwarded value.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current forwarding path compares two five-bit EX source addresses\\nagainst both downstream destination registers, resolves writer priority,\\nand selects operands before ALU arithmetic, branch comparison, and JALR\\ntarget generation. The r7s0 implementation notes identify EX/MEM rd\\nthrough forwarding and the JALR adder into pending_target_q as the worst\\npath in all three candidate seeds. The r4s0 lesson also exposed forwarding\\nbefore branch comparison. These are related-candidate measurements, not\\nproof that forwarding is the incoming winner's worst path: the reproduced\\nwinner instead has ALU-control and prediction-recovery endpoints.\\n\\nThe structural history includes store decoupling, divider separation,\\npredictor-event pipelines, recovery distribution, and PC-adder retiming,\\nbut no retiming of operand dependency comparisons. Further predictor\\nvalidation stages and PC precomputation regressed substantially. Test\\nmoving dependency detection across an existing instruction boundary:\\nthe incoming instruction's sources and its two preceding destinations\\nare already available before ID/EX capture. Four registered comparison\\nbits can remove address equality from the execute operand path without\\nincreasing instruction latency or registering a wide forwarded value.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split forwarding into early address matching and late write qualification.\\nMove only the four source/destination equality comparisons into decode\\nand capture their results with the instruction in ID/EX. Preserve the\\nexisting pipeline stages, instruction acceptance schedule, data bypass\\nsources, and execute-stage operand muxes.\\n\\nFor each incoming raw source field s, compute two independent bits from\\npre-edge state:\\n  match_ex = (s != 0) && (s == current_id_ex.rd)\\n  match_wb = (s != 0) && (s == current_ex_mem.rd)\\nThe current ID/EX instruction becomes the EX/MEM producer when the\\nconsumer is accepted; the current EX/MEM instruction becomes its MEM/WB\\nproducer. Use the same IF instruction source fields as today's decode,\\nincluding the existing NOP substitution. Compare both fields even for\\ninstructions that do not architecturally consume both, preserving the\\nexisting operand/RVFI convention and load-use detector behavior.\\n\\nAdd exactly four match bits to the ID/EX payload. Reset them to zero;\\notherwise capture whenever the existing ID/EX payload captures, and hold\\nwhenever stall_id holds that payload. Follow the winning r5s0 separation:\\nflush clears all controls and valid, without adding flush to these\\npayload registers' reset or enable. Matches captured in a bubble are\\ninert. A later accepted instruction always receives newly computed bits.\\n\\nIn the following execute cycle, select EX/MEM when its registered match\\nbit and current EX/MEM ctrl.reg_write are both true. Otherwise select\\nMEM/WB when its independent registered match bit and current MEM/WB\\nctrl.reg_write are true; otherwise use the original ID/EX operand value.\\nKeep the existing 00/01/10 selector encoding and EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB\\npriority. Do not resolve priority before capture: a matching younger\\nproducer can have its write suppressed by a trap, in which case the\\nmatching older producer must remain available. Qualifying with the\\nactual downstream control registers preserves both execute- and\\nmemory-stage alignment-trap suppression without a new combinational\\ntrap-feedback path into the early comparisons. Source zero never matches.\\n\\nPreserve MEM/WB forwarding independently of its retirement-valid bit.\\nDuring a data-memory hold, ID/EX and EX/MEM retain their identities and\\nMEM/WB retains its data/control even after valid clears. Therefore the\\nheld match bits still refer to the correct retained producers. Keep\\nthe existing load-use bubble: a load result is forwarded from MEM/WB,\\nnever from the EX/MEM address. Leave register-file write-first bypass\\nintact so older WB values are captured normally at decode.\\n\\nA divide is the deliberate exception to producer identity remaining\\nfixed during an ID/EX hold: older stages drain after its launch. Use\\nthese matches only to obtain the existing once-accepted post-forward\\ndivide operands. Retain div_rs1_q/div_rs2_q and the divider's captured\\narithmetic operands through completion; never resample operands after\\nthe producers move or relaunch the held divide. The next instruction\\ncaptures fresh matches on divide completion, including a dependency on\\nthe divide moving into EX/MEM. Preserve the real and ALTOPS eight-edge\\nhandshake, exactly-once advancement, and reset cancellation unchanged.\\n\\nThis is one structural redistribution of dependency detection across\\nID/EX. The intended cut is destination-address equality before execute\\noperand selection; ALU arithmetic, branch/JALR resolution, predictor\\norganization and event timing, hazard equations, memory ordering, and\\nretirement stay as they are. No extra issue bubble, instruction stage,\\nspeculative value capture, or memory bypass is introduced.\\n\\nEstimate an unmeasured four-percent fitness improvement with low\\nconfidence. Identical input streams and ready schedules should produce\\nidentical accepted instructions, architectural traces, and scored cycle\\ncounts, so any benefit must come from higher median Fmax. A new decode\\ncomparison path, retained late write-control fanout, placement changes,\\nor the winner's competing ALU/recovery/predictor paths can erase it.\\nInspect synthesis and all three routed seeds to verify that register\\naddress equality ends at ID/EX and does not remain in the execute mux\\ncone. Do not add unrelated retiming or synthesis constraints to rescue\\nan unfavorable result.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and FPGA gates.\\nCompare the candidate and incoming winner with identical ELFs, tools,\\nbus-ready sequences, and three-seed Fmax evaluation; calculate fitness\\nfrom exact iterations/bracketed cycles times median Fmax in Hz. Existing\\nnotes identify 388.66 as the historical scored 2 KB result; keep that\\ncomparison distinct from the mandatory canonical 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration\\nrun. Require d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, Correct operation validated., bounded\\naccesses, and timing strictly between accepted start/stop marker writes.\\nPreserve nret=1, every external port and RVFI field, strict channel-0\\norder, legality defaults, traps, all arithmetic, and every invariant.\\nOnly bench RTL and its permitted unit tests are implementation targets.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add four ID/EX payload bits, independently recording rs1/rs2 matches\\nagainst the producers that will occupy EX/MEM and MEM/WB. Retain\\nall existing source addresses, operand values, controls, prediction,\\nand RVFI metadata; other pipeline bundles remain unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination addresses as new\\ninternal inputs. Compute the four source-nonzero equality bits and\\ncapture them with the existing payload under its reset/hold rules.\\nKeep late producer write eligibility out of this early comparator\\npath. Preserve decoder legality and the control-versus-payload\\nflush discipline, including simultaneous flush and backend hold.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the live source/destination address inputs and comparisons\\nwith the four registered match inputs. Qualify them using current\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB ctrl.reg_write and produce the existing selectors,\\nchoosing the younger eligible writer first. Retain the older match\\nwhen the younger write is suppressed. Do not gate retained MEM/WB\\nforwarding with retirement valid or wb_stage.w_en.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire pre-edge ID/EX and EX/MEM destination addresses into id_stage,\\nand route its registered matches into forward_unit alongside the\\nexisting downstream write controls. Keep forward_unit's selectors\\nconnected to the existing ex_stage operand muxes. Preserve all\\nother stage wiring, holds, divider operand retention, and public IO.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing reset/flush/stall matrix to drive both producer\\ndestinations and independently predict all four captured matches.\\nCover every five-bit source/destination value, x0, simultaneous\\nmatches, and changes to live sources/destinations during repeated\\nholds. Preserve all current payload and control assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update the ID/EX unpacker for the four fields and add architectural\\ndependency tests with immediate and one-gap producers, both sources,\\nrepeated writes to one rd, x0, and trapping younger writers falling\\nback to older values. Cover ALU/multiply, branch/JALR, store data/base,\\nand stalled load-use consumers. Exercise retained MEM/WB forwarding\\nafter valid clears, imem bubbles, redirects, reset, a divide waiting\\nbehind stalled memory, draining older producers during division,\\nconsecutive divides, and immediate divide-result consumers. Check\\nexact RVFI operands/order/PC chaining, memory effects, marker writes,\\nand unchanged schedules against the incoming design without weakening\\nany existing assertions or altering protected test harnesses.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add four ID/EX payload bits, independently recording rs1/rs2 matches\\nagainst the producers that will occupy EX/MEM and MEM/WB. Retain\\nall existing source addresses, operand values, controls, prediction,\\nand RVFI metadata; other pipeline bundles remain unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Accept the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination addresses as new\\ninternal inputs. Compute the four source-nonzero equality bits and\\ncapture them with the existing payload under its reset/hold rules.\\nKeep late producer write eligibility out of this early comparator\\npath. Preserve decoder legality and the control-versus-payload\\nflush discipline, including simultaneous flush and backend hold.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the live source/destination address inputs and comparisons\\nwith the four registered match inputs. Qualify them using current\\nEX/MEM and MEM/WB ctrl.reg_write and produce the existing selectors,\\nchoosing the younger eligible writer first. Retain the older match\\nwhen the younger write is suppressed. Do not gate retained MEM/WB\\nforwarding with retirement valid or wb_stage.w_en.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire pre-edge ID/EX and EX/MEM destination addresses into id_stage,\\nand route its registered matches into forward_unit alongside the\\nexisting downstream write controls. Keep forward_unit's selectors\\nconnected to the existing ex_stage operand muxes. Preserve all\\nother stage wiring, holds, divider operand retention, and public IO.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing reset/flush/stall matrix to drive both producer\\ndestinations and independently predict all four captured matches.\\nCover every five-bit source/destination value, x0, simultaneous\\nmatches, and changes to live sources/destinations during repeated\\nholds. Preserve all current payload and control assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update the ID/EX unpacker for the four fields and add architectural\\ndependency tests with immediate and one-gap producers, both sources,\\nrepeated writes to one rd, x0, and trapping younger writers falling\\nback to older values. Cover ALU/multiply, branch/JALR, store data/base,\\nand stalled load-use consumers. Exercise retained MEM/WB forwarding\\nafter valid clears, imem bubbles, redirects, reset, a divide waiting\\nbehind stalled memory, draining older producers during division,\\nconsecutive divides, and immediate divide-result consumers. Check\\nexact RVFI operands/order/PC chaining, memory effects, marker writes,\\nand unchanged schedules against the incoming design without weakening\\nany existing assertions or altering protected test harnesses.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 364.86, \"delta_pct\": -6.12, \"vs_baseline\": 29.01, \"fmax_mhz\": 153.8, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6607, \"ff\": 3200, \"seeds\": [148.13, 156.64, 153.8], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Operand dependency matching across ID/EX\\n\\nImplemented the requested structural change. All correctness gates passed. The\\nmeasured result is a regression: median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 153.80 MHz\\n(-6.1222%), with identical benchmark marker cycles. The candidate remains in\\nplace for the orchestrator; no unrelated retiming or constraints were added.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- Added exactly four bits to `id_ex_t`: `rs1_match_ex`, `rs1_match_wb`,\\n  `rs2_match_ex`, and `rs2_match_wb`. Other pipeline bundle declarations are\\n  unchanged.\\n- `id_stage` compares each raw, nonzero IF source field with the pre-edge\\n  ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations. Both age matches are independent; neither\\n  validity, producer write eligibility, priority, nor trap feedback qualifies\\n  these comparisons. Existing IF NOP substitution remains in effect.\\n- Matches reset to zero and capture with the existing ID/EX payload when\\n  `!stall`. Flush still clears only controls/valid. Simultaneous flush and\\n  hold preserves the match payload.\\n- `forward_unit` uses registered matches and the actual EX/MEM and MEM/WB\\n  `ctrl.reg_write` fields. Selectors remain 00/01/10, with EX/MEM priority and\\n  MEM/WB fallback when the younger write is suppressed. MEM/WB retirement\\n  valid and `wb_stage.w_en` do not qualify forwarding.\\n- `core` wires producer destinations into decode and matches into forwarding.\\n  The existing execute operand muxes are unchanged.\\n- Divider launch, captured arithmetic operands, `div_rs1_q`/`div_rs2_q`, result\\n  consumption, eight-edge real/ALTOPS timing, reset cancellation, and pipeline\\n  drain behavior are unchanged. Hazards, register-file write-first bypass,\\n  memory ordering, prediction/recovery, retirement, nret=1, and external ports\\n  are unchanged.\\n\\nThe only change to `ex_stage.sv` is a local UNUSEDSIGNAL annotation documenting\\nthat the four new input bundle bits are consumed by the separate forwarding\\nunit. This resolves Verilator's unused-bits warning without changing logic.\\n\\n## Unit tests\\n\\nExisting assertions were preserved and augmented:\\n\\n- Updated the ID/EX unpacker and payload checks for all four fields.\\n- Extended the reset/flush/stall matrix with independently driven producer\\n  destinations and predicted match values.\\n- Exhausted all 32^3 source/younger/older address triples for both raw source\\n  fields, including x0 and simultaneous matches. Changed live instructions,\\n  data, and destinations during repeated holds and flush+hold; reset also\\n  clears a held payload with all four matches set.\\n- Exhausted late match/write qualification combinations, including removal\\n  and restoration of the younger writer's eligibility.\\n- Added a live-address forwarding oracle at operand consumption and an\\n  architectural register scoreboard checking both raw RVFI operands at every\\n  retirement, including unused source fields.\\n- Added every-register immediate/one-gap dependencies, repeated writes,\\n  ALU/all multiply forms, branch/JALR, store base/data, loads, and EX/MEM trap\\n  suppression. Misaligned JAL/JALR tests explicitly require the independent\\n  older match to remain usable; misaligned loads retain the load-use bubble\\n  and fall back to the captured register-file value.\\n- Added exact accepted marker-write schedules with repeated memory holds and\\n  forwarding from MEM/WB after valid clears. Existing imem-bubble, redirect,\\n  wrong-path, prediction, memory, and reset assertions remain active.\\n- Strengthened divide checks to compare retained RVFI operand snapshots\\n  throughout older-stage draining. Added real and ALTOPS pipeline runs covering\\n  a divide behind stalled memory, consecutive divides, immediate consumers,\\n  and reset during division, all with exactly-once eight-edge handshakes.\\n\\n## Local gates\\n\\nThe incoming bench RTL was copied before any edits. Builds and gate outputs\\nwere isolated under `$TMPDIR/operand-retime-7q7ly7a6/`, with `incoming/` and\\n`candidate/` trees. Protected harnesses were copied without modification;\\nthe shared riscv-formal checkout was not used as a writable build directory.\\n\\n- Required command `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv\\n  +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings.\\n- Candidate `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, all 12 pytest runners, including\\n  real and ALTOPS arithmetic and pipeline runs. The incoming suite passed all\\n  9 runners. The final additional all-matches reset assertion passed in a\\n  subsequent targeted ID-stage run.\\n- `CORE_NAME=bench RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh`:\\n  candidate PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed on the first attempt, zero RTL fix\\n  attempts. Incoming PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed. Single-channel wrapper and\\n  ALTOPS configuration were selected from unchanged `nret: 1`.\\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: PASS on both designs, selftest ISS cosim\\n  and stalled 2 KB CoreMark CRC gate.\\n- Additional unchanged-harness stalled cosim: one common selftest ELF and\\n  one common dependency ELF, with both `--istall --dstall` and seeds 1, 7, 97.\\n  All six comparisons passed the unchanged Python ISS comparison. Complete\\n  JSON retirement streams, including every emitted field and cycle number,\\n  were byte-identical between incoming and candidate. Selftest retired 115\\n  instructions at final cycles 198/194/190; the dependency program retired\\n  419 at cycles 635/611/613.\\n- `NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv make fpga TARGET=bench`: PASS on both\\n  designs, all three routes successful. Direct Makefile invocation initially\\n  omitted NRET and tried compiling the two-channel C++ harness; setting the\\n  existing single-channel environment knobs fixed that invocation without\\n  source changes. Both final evaluations used identical tool versions.\\n\\nTools: Verilator 5.047 devel v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6; Yosys 0.64+149\\n2dc69a757; nextpnr 0.10-45-g98c18d7f; xPack RISC-V GCC 15.2.0; Python 3.13.12.\\n\\n## Timing and fitness\\n\\n| Design | Seed 1 MHz | Seed 2 MHz | Seed 3 MHz | Median Hz | LUT4 | FF |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Incoming winner | 163.83 | 155.33 | 168.55 | 163830000 | 6618 | 3198 |\\n| Candidate | 148.13 | 156.64 | 153.80 | 153800000 | 6607 | 3200 |\\n\\nThe source payload adds exactly four bits. The mapped netlists contain\\nexactly four match DFFRE cells; total mapped FF count changes by two after\\nsynthesis optimization.\\n\\nFitness below uses `10 / exact_bracketed_cycles * median_Fmax_Hz`, without\\nusing the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark` field.\\n\\n| Workload | Accepted start | Accepted stop | Bracketed cycles, both designs | Incoming fitness | Candidate fitness |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Historical scored 2 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 15229 | 4230491 | 4215262 | 388.6591153764582 | 364.8646276316870 |\\n| Mandatory canonical 6 KB, -O3, 10 iterations | 53747 | 30876537 | 30822790 | 53.15222924336181 | 49.898143548977885 |\\n\\nThe incoming default evaluator result reproduces historical **388.66**. That\\nis the 2 KB result and is separate from the canonical 6 KB measurement. Both\\nmeasurements use the same ~22% imem/dmem ready schedule for both designs and\\ncount only the accepted start/stop MMIO bracket, excluding initialization and\\nreporting. The default protected evaluator checks 2 KB CRCs, so it was left\\nunchanged and the mandatory 6 KB image was validated separately.\\n\\nFor each size, both designs were also run against one identical ELF pathname\\nwith `cosim_sim ELF 50000000 --bench --istall --dstall`. Final retirement and\\nbenchmark JSON outputs are byte-identical. The independently built default\\ngate ELFs also have identical load segments; the explicit comparisons use\\nthe same ELF file, avoiding irrelevant assembler temporary-symbol differences.\\n\\nThe canonical image used the unchanged CoreMark/crt0 sources and build flags\\n`-march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 -O3 -static -nostartfiles -specs=nano.specs\\n-specs=nosys.specs -T bench/programs/link.ld -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000`, with the\\nexisting two include directories and unchanged ITERATIONS=10 header. Both\\ncanonical runs completed at EBREAK, reported `oob:false`, had valid increasing\\naccepted marker brackets, and produced:\\n\\n```\\nseedcrc=8a02 crclist=d4b0 crcmatrix=be52 crcstate=5e47 crcfinal=5949\\nCorrect operation validated.\\n```\\n\\nThe protected UART compiler-flags banner still says `-O2`; the actual compiler\\ninvocation was `-O3`. The reported CoreMark Size 2000 is per algorithm, with\\nTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000 and the explicit `6k performance run parameters` banner.\\nNeither protected reporting string was edited.\\n\\nShared ELF SHA-256 values:\\n\\n- 6 KB: `6e691f7224c3099c065da4fb2c756eada2d7b96b58d627357de923329770b2fa`\\n- 2 KB: `166dd0abb3e5bdd5398785d7fd19095e6090a219dc9dc01354c051345a952df6`\\n- Selftest: `85b700313e073e889d389242f8bead8bddc19a07a17a64a705136426e19dbc24`\\n- Dependencies: `fc26cef43a82984047a1f065723e5f2015c431cff22f15b48aeb151ddce0ff1e`\\n\\n## Structural audit and concerns\\n\\nTraversed the mapped and routed combinational cones backwards from the 64\\npost-forward operand capture D pins (`div_rs1_q`/`div_rs2_q`), stopping at\\nsequential outputs. The incoming operand cones contain all ten EX/MEM and\\nMEM/WB destination-address bits. The candidate synthesis and every routed\\nseed contain zero of those bits and all four match-register outputs. Each\\nmatch D cone includes all five bits of its correct pre-edge producer. The\\nfour match DFFREs share the original ID/EX payload destination registers'\\nclock enable and reset. Address equality therefore ends at ID/EX rather than\\nremaining in the execute operand-selection cone.\\n\\nThe incoming routed limits start in ID/EX control and end in EX/MEM data\\n(seeds 1/3) or ID/EX recovery reset (seed 2). Candidate limits are predictor\\nmetadata paths into counter clock enables: row 0 valid -> counter CE in\\nseed 1, row 0 tag -> counter CE in seed 2, and row 15 tag -> counter CE in\\nseed 3. All candidate routed limits are outside the moved dependency\\ncomparisons. The intended structural cut succeeded, but competing paths and\\nplacement erased the proposed benefit. No functional discrepancy was found.\\n\\nEvidence is retained under `$TMPDIR/operand-retime-7q7ly7a6/`:\\n`incoming/` and `candidate/` gate logs, synthesis JSON and all routed\\n`pnr_seed*/{pnr.json,nextpnr.log}`, `netlist-audit.json`,\\n`stalled-cosim-comparison.json`, `canonical-6k-validation.json`, and\\n`timing-fitness-comparison.json`. No other core, protected harness, core.yaml,\\nbuild script, predictor, hazard equation, or arithmetic implementation was\\nchanged. This requested notes file is the sole worktree-root artifact.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T20:06:09.087307Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r8s1 (regression, -6.12%): Retiming four independent dependency matches into ID/EX removed EX destination-address comparisons without changing CoreMark cycles, yet predictor valid/tag-to-counter-enable paths limited all three routed seeds and median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 153.80 MHz; measure the incoming winner's bottleneck before targeting forwarding paths observed in related candidates.\"}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Fold jump link values into the ALU result selection\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner scores 388.66 at 163.83 MHz. The saved RTL under\\n.tmp/thermometer-r6s0/baseline/cores/bench/rtl matches every current\\nbench RTL file byte for byte. Its final nextpnr reports reproduce\\n163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz. Seeds 1 and 3 have critical paths from\\nID/EX alu_op through result selection to EX/MEM alu_result[25], taking\\n6.10 and 5.93 ns. The final mapped LUT on those paths also receives\\nis_jump: ex_stage currently selects PC+4 after the complete ALU result.\\nThis independent selection dimension gates ordinary arithmetic results\\neven though a jump never needs its nominal ADD result for writeback.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt entries were reviewed. Full registered ALU\\npredecode in r2s0 and multiply-high predecode in r7s0 regressed; both\\nretained the final link mux. The r8s0 parallel JALR adders also regressed\\nand left ALU result selection as the routed limit. This proposal removes\\nthe separate link-result selection through mutually exclusive operation\\nencoding, without adding predecode state or duplicating target adders.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current winner scores 388.66 at 163.83 MHz. The saved RTL under\\n.tmp/thermometer-r6s0/baseline/cores/bench/rtl matches every current\\nbench RTL file byte for byte. Its final nextpnr reports reproduce\\n163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz. Seeds 1 and 3 have critical paths from\\nID/EX alu_op through result selection to EX/MEM alu_result[25], taking\\n6.10 and 5.93 ns. The final mapped LUT on those paths also receives\\nis_jump: ex_stage currently selects PC+4 after the complete ALU result.\\nThis independent selection dimension gates ordinary arithmetic results\\neven though a jump never needs its nominal ADD result for writeback.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt entries were reviewed. Full registered ALU\\npredecode in r2s0 and multiply-high predecode in r7s0 regressed; both\\nretained the final link mux. The r8s0 parallel JALR adders also regressed\\nand left ALU result selection as the routed limit. This proposal removes\\nthe separate link-result selection through mutually exclusive operation\\nencoding, without adding predecode state or duplicating target adders.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add one internal five-bit operation, ALU_LINK = 5'd19, leaving all\\nexisting operation values 0 through 18 unchanged. Within the decoder's\\nalready validated JAL and legal JALR arms, select ALU_LINK instead of\\nthe default ALU_ADD. Keep is_jump, is_jalr, alu_src, register-write\\ncontrols, immediate decoding, and every legality condition unchanged.\\nReserved JALR encodings must retain the decoder's illegal defaults.\\n\\nGive the internal alu module a 32-bit link_value input. Add ALU_LINK\\nas a peer arm of its existing result case, returning link_value directly.\\nIn EX, drive link_value from the existing modulo-2^32 in.pc + 4 value\\nand capture alu_result directly into reg_q.alu_result for every\\nadvancing instruction. Remove the independent is_jump ? PC+4 :\\nalu_result selection at this register input. Express PC+4 once for\\nlink_value and the existing sequential-next-PC calculation. Keep the\\ncurrent forwarded alu_a/alu_b selection and all branch/JAL/JALR target\\narithmetic unchanged; ALU_LINK simply ignores a and b for its result.\\n\\nThe intended hardware improvement is one mutually exclusive result\\nselection network that includes the link value, replacing an ALU\\nnetwork followed by a separate jump override. Do not recreate the\\noverride inside alu, such as an outer op == ALU_LINK ternary around\\nthe old result case. There is no new register, bundle field, pipeline\\nboundary, instruction latency, operand forwarding policy, or ALU-op\\nrenumbering. The existing ctrl.alu_op field transports ALU_LINK with\\nexactly the current ID/EX reset, flush, and backend-hold rules.\\n\\nJAL/JALR must still write their own PC+4, including rd equal to rs1\\nand immediate link consumers. JALR target computation must still use\\nthe original post-forward rs1 plus the full immediate and clear bit 0.\\nMisaligned targets must retain their current trap, PC recovery, and\\nwrite-suppression behavior. Keep raw RVFI operands, architectural next\\nPC, accepted predictions, predictor training, nret=1, and external\\nports exact. Preserve all control-bit bubble clearing, EX/MEM divide\\nbubbles, and MEM/WB forwarding retention during data stalls.\\n\\nAll existing integer and multiply expressions, product signedness,\\nALTOPS formulas, and the divider's eight-edge real/ALTOPS controller\\nremain unchanged. ALU_LINK has the same meaning in both builds and\\nmust never request a divide. Internal opcodes 20 through 31 continue\\nto return zero. No instruction is newly legalized by adding this\\ninternal result operation.\\n\\nExpect an unmeasured three-percent fitness gain through median Fmax,\\nwith identical bracketed cycles for the same ELF and ready schedule.\\nConfidence is low because synthesis may absorb the old mux already,\\nmap the new case poorly, or expose the existing recovery, predictor\\nmetadata, or target-generation paths. Inspect synthesis and all three\\nrouted reports for the actual result-selection depth and removal of\\nis_jump from the result-data cone. Report LUT/FF changes; do not treat\\na source-level mux deletion as evidence of a timing gain.\\n\\nRun existing lint, real and ALTOPS unit suites, formal, stalled cosim,\\nand the unchanged three-seed FPGA evaluation. Compare valid RVFI\\nrecords and their cycle positions, accepted bus transactions, and\\nmarker brackets against the incoming winner under identical inputs.\\nUse full-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles times median Fmax_Hz.\\nThe log identifies historical 388.66 as the 2 KB score; keep that\\ncomparison separate from the mandatory unchanged 6 KB, -O3,\\nten-iteration run with both random bus stalls. Require canonical\\n6 KB CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, bounded accesses, accepted start/stop markers,\\nand the literal Correct operation validated. Preserve every hard\\ninvariant and all protected evaluation files.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add ALU_LINK = 5'd19. Preserve all existing operation constants,\\nctrl_t and pipeline bundle layouts, and retirement widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Assign ALU_LINK only in the existing validated JAL and JALR arms.\\nPreserve all other controls and default-illegal decoding, including\\nreserved JALR funct3 and the SYSTEM/EBREAK rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the internal link_value input and an ALU_LINK peer arm in the\\ncommon result case. Keep existing arithmetic and divider behavior\\nexact in both builds. Preserve default-zero results for remaining\\nunsupported internal opcodes and avoid a separate outer link mux.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Supply the instruction's existing PC+4 value to alu.link_value\\nand write alu_result directly to EX/MEM, removing the is_jump\\nresult override. Share the PC+4 expression with actual_next_pc's\\ndefault. Preserve target adders, forwarded source values, traps,\\nredirects, predictor events, and all sequential hold/bubble rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Check ALU_LINK for legal JAL/JALR while retaining their complete\\ncontrol expectations. Keep all illegal-encoding assertions and\\nexplicitly require reserved JALR forms to remain illegal without\\nlink selection. Define the new internal constant locally if needed;\\nno helper or evaluation-contract file needs modification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Initialize and drive link_value in the existing ALU harness. Add\\nexact combinational link passthrough checks, alternating LINK and\\nevery existing result class while varying the unused link input.\\nRetain every real/ALTOPS arithmetic and divider-handshake assertion\\nand cover all remaining unsupported internal opcodes 20 through 31.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add legal jump captures and check ALU_LINK alignment with the held\\ninstruction through reset, flush, backend holds, and invalid input.\\nRetain all existing control-zero bubble and payload-owner checks;\\nupdate only jump operation expectations affected by the encoding.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise JAL/JALR links followed by dependent arithmetic, JALR with\\nrd equal to rs1, x0 links, forwarded and load-dependent targets,\\nand misaligned-target write suppression. Cover imem backpressure,\\nprolonged dmem holds with retained WB forwarding, divide waits,\\nrecovery, and reset. Preserve exact operand, result, PC-chain,\\norder, prediction, cycle, and accepted-memory assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add ALU_LINK = 5'd19. Preserve all existing operation constants,\\nctrl_t and pipeline bundle layouts, and retirement widths.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Assign ALU_LINK only in the existing validated JAL and JALR arms.\\nPreserve all other controls and default-illegal decoding, including\\nreserved JALR funct3 and the SYSTEM/EBREAK rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the internal link_value input and an ALU_LINK peer arm in the\\ncommon result case. Keep existing arithmetic and divider behavior\\nexact in both builds. Preserve default-zero results for remaining\\nunsupported internal opcodes and avoid a separate outer link mux.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Supply the instruction's existing PC+4 value to alu.link_value\\nand write alu_result directly to EX/MEM, removing the is_jump\\nresult override. Share the PC+4 expression with actual_next_pc's\\ndefault. Preserve target adders, forwarded source values, traps,\\nredirects, predictor events, and all sequential hold/bubble rules.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Check ALU_LINK for legal JAL/JALR while retaining their complete\\ncontrol expectations. Keep all illegal-encoding assertions and\\nexplicitly require reserved JALR forms to remain illegal without\\nlink selection. Define the new internal constant locally if needed;\\nno helper or evaluation-contract file needs modification.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Initialize and drive link_value in the existing ALU harness. Add\\nexact combinational link passthrough checks, alternating LINK and\\nevery existing result class while varying the unused link input.\\nRetain every real/ALTOPS arithmetic and divider-handshake assertion\\nand cover all remaining unsupported internal opcodes 20 through 31.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Add legal jump captures and check ALU_LINK alignment with the held\\ninstruction through reset, flush, backend holds, and invalid input.\\nRetain all existing control-zero bubble and payload-owner checks;\\nupdate only jump operation expectations affected by the encoding.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Exercise JAL/JALR links followed by dependent arithmetic, JALR with\\nrd equal to rs1, x0 links, forwarded and load-dependent targets,\\nand misaligned-target write suppression. Cover imem backpressure,\\nprolonged dmem holds with retained WB forwarding, divide waits,\\nrecovery, and reset. Preserve exact operand, result, PC-chain,\\norder, prediction, cycle, and accepted-memory assertions.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 295.76, \"delta_pct\": -23.9, \"vs_baseline\": 4.58, \"fmax_mhz\": 124.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6607, \"ff\": 3202, \"seeds\": [124.67, 111.59, 129.58], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the proposed ALU_LINK fold. Correctness checks pass, but the measured FPGA result is a regression: median Fmax falls from 163.83 MHz to 124.67 MHz (-23.90282610022584%). The implementation is left in place for the orchestrator to evaluate; the predicted three-percent gain was not observed.\\n\\nThe RTL change follows the hypothesis exactly. ALU_LINK is 5'd19, with operations 0 through 18 unchanged. Only the existing legal JAL and funct3=0 JALR decoder arms select it. The ALU has a 32-bit link_value input and a peer ALU_LINK result-case arm shared by real and ALTOPS builds. EX computes a single 32-bit pc_plus_4 for that input and sequential actual_next_pc, and captures alu_result directly into EX/MEM. There is no outer link override in the ALU.\\n\\nNo pipeline state, bundle fields, retirement width, external ports, forwarding rules, target adders, prediction/training rules, trap behavior, or sequential hold/bubble rules were changed. All integer, multiply, signed-product, ALTOPS and divider expressions and the eight-edge divider controller remain unchanged. Unsupported internal operations 20 through 31 still produce zero.\\n\\nTests now check complete legal jump and reserved-JALR decoder controls, exact LINK passthrough while alternating every result class, varying unused link inputs, all unsupported internal opcodes, and divider independence while idle, busy and retaining a result. ID tests check LINK ownership through legal/invalid captures, reset, flush, hold and simultaneous flush/hold. Pipeline additions cover dependent link consumers, rd=rs1 JALR, full signed immediates and bit-zero clearing, x0 links, forwarded/load-dependent targets, eleven-cycle data holds with retained WB forwarding, instruction stalls, divide waits, misaligned write suppression, reset and PC+4 wraparound. Existing assertions remain in place. An optional PIPELINE_TRANSCRIPT output records architectural events for comparison against the incoming RTL.\\n\\nLocal verification completed:\\n\\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings. ALTOPS lint also passes without warnings.\\n- Existing pytest unit suite: 9 runner cases passed, including both real and ALTOPS ALU builds and all 40 pipeline tests.\\n- Local formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, using unchanged formal/run_all.sh, wrapper_si.sv and checks_si.cfg with nret=1. No RTL fix attempts or counterexamples. An initial shell launch exited before generating checks because the default Python lacked PyYAML; selecting the already installed Miniconda Python resolved that environment issue.\\n- Selftest cosim against the unchanged Python ISS: PASS, 115 retirements with zero wait and with both random bus stalls. Baseline and candidate RVFI records, including cycle positions, match byte for byte in each schedule.\\n- All 108 pipeline runs match the incoming winner's architectural transcript byte for byte: 1,881 valid retirements, 5,635 clocked cycles, 2,086 accepted fetches, 97 accepted reads and 83 accepted writes. The transcript includes cycle positions, raw RVFI operands/results, exact accepted predictions, resolution and predictor-commit events, accepted memory data/masks and marker transactions. Deliberately cancelled pre-edge observations in reset tests are excluded from acceptance counts. Both complete pipeline suites passed again after finalizing this transcript bookkeeping.\\n- Full stalled CoreMark RVFI streams match byte for byte under the same ELF and default ready-sequence seed: 22,093,920 records for 6 KB, and 2,937,037 records for 2 KB. Every emitted RVFI field and its cycle position was compared, including inactive memory data fields.\\n\\nThe mandatory 6 KB run used the unchanged benchmark sources and Makefile with COREMARK_SIZE=6000, COREMARK_OPT=-O3 and ITERATIONS=10. Both CPUs consumed the same ELF with --istall --dstall. Start marker acceptance was cycle 53,747 and stop marker acceptance was cycle 30,876,537, giving exactly 30,822,790 bracketed cycles on both designs. Both reached EBREAK with oob=false and bench_bracketed=true, canonical crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, and the literal \\\"Correct operation validated.\\\" Additional observed CRCs were seedcrc=8a02 and crcfinal=5949.\\n\\nAll 22,093,920 six-KB retirement records were additionally checked for bounded instruction and memory addresses. The highest retired/next instruction address was 0x39e8. Memory addresses were within [0, 0x00100000), with MMIO limited to 0x10000000, 0x10000100 and 0x10000104, all within the required MMIO window. The existing C++ harness also completed without its sticky out-of-bounds flag. The protected UART compiler-description string still says -O2; the captured compiler invocation is explicitly -O3. CoreMark prints the per-algorithm size as 2000 for TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000. Neither protected reporting string was edited.\\n\\nUnchanged synthesis and three-seed routing were run on both the incoming winner and candidate, using fpga/scripts/synth.tcl, fpga/scripts/nextpnr_run.sh, the existing constraints and the single-issue wrapper. All six routes completed normally. The fresh baseline synthesis JSON is byte-identical to the saved thermometer-r6s0 baseline, and its final routed frequencies reproduce the historical reports exactly.\\n\\n| Measurement | Incoming winner | ALU_LINK candidate | Change |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 final Fmax, MHz | 163.83 | 124.67 | -39.16 |\\n| Seed 2 final Fmax, MHz | 155.33 | 111.59 | -43.74 |\\n| Seed 3 final Fmax, MHz | 168.55 | 129.58 | -38.97 |\\n| Median final Fmax, MHz | 163.83 | 124.67 | -39.16 |\\n| Mapped LUT4 sites | 6618 | 6607 | -11 |\\n| Mapped flip-flops | 3198 | 3202 | +4 |\\n\\nFitness was calculated from 10 / bracketed_cycles times median Fmax_Hz, without using the evaluator's rounded ipc_coremark field. The exact fractions are authoritative; decimals below are displayed to 15 decimal places.\\n\\n| Workload | Identical bracketed cycles | Incoming winner fitness | Candidate fitness |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Mandatory 6 KB, -O3, ten iterations, both random stalls | 30,822,790 | 53.152229243361811 | 40.447344318927651 |\\n| Separate historical 2 KB, -O3, ten iterations, both random stalls | 4,215,262 | 388.659115376458213 | 295.758602905347283 |\\n\\nThe six-KB fractions are 1,638,300,000 / 30,822,790 and 1,246,700,000 / 30,822,790. The two-KB fractions have the same numerators and denominator 4,215,262. Historical 388.66 is therefore a two-KB score, not the mandatory six-KB result. The unchanged tools.eval.fpga gate passed its existing two-KB CRC validation for both designs and reported 388.66 versus 295.76. A same-ELF check also confirmed identical two-KB marker acceptances at cycles 15,229 and 4,230,491. Independently linked two-KB ELFs differ in non-loadable content; their loadable segments are identical, and explicit cross-design trace/marker comparisons used the same baseline ELF.\\n\\nMapped and routed cone inspection confirms the intended structural change. In the incoming netlist, ID/EX is_jump reaches all 32 EX/MEM alu_result D cones and directly drives I0 of bit 25's final LUT4. It reaches none of those 32 D cones in the candidate synthesis or any of the three final routed netlists. A backward traversal stopping at registers finds maximum alu_op-to-result-D structural depth 11 versus 9 mapped combinational cells; bit 25 is 8 versus 7. These unweighted cell counts are structural observations, not measured delays or evidence of a fitness gain.\\n\\nAll final routed critical reports were inspected:\\n\\n| Design / seed | Critical path | Total delay, ns | Logic / routing, ns |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Incoming / 1 | ID/EX alu_op[4] to EX/MEM alu_result[25] | 6.10 | 2.87 / 3.24 |\\n| Incoming / 2 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[3] through recovery to ID/EX valid reset | 6.44 | 2.77 / 3.67 |\\n| Incoming / 3 | ID/EX alu_op[2] to EX/MEM alu_result[25] | 5.93 | 2.78 / 3.15 |\\n| Candidate / 1 | EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding and JALR target generation to EX/MEM pc_next[28] | 8.02 | 4.57 / 3.45 |\\n| Candidate / 2 | EX/MEM rd[0] through forwarding and JALR target generation to EX/MEM pc_next[29] | 8.96 | 4.54 / 4.43 |\\n| Candidate / 3 | EX/MEM rd[3] through forwarding and JALR target generation to EX/MEM pc_next[25] | 7.72 | 4.06 / 3.66 |\\n\\nTiming columns preserve nextpnr's printed rounding. Incoming seeds 1 and 3 traverse seven mapped combinational cells on their reported result-selection paths. Candidate critical paths instead traverse the forwarding comparison/muxes, the existing JALR carry chain and the next-PC selection. Removing the result override succeeded structurally, but these routed paths determine the worse Fmax. The mapped FF increase is reported despite there being no added RTL register or pipeline boundary. No attempt was made to tune unrelated arithmetic, placement seeds or evaluation files to rescue the result.\\n\\nValidation used isolated scratch copies under $TMPDIR/bench-link-r9s0 so the gates could generate artifacts without touching protected worktree files. RTL changes were limited to the four planned bench files, with the four planned test files extended. This notes file is the explicitly requested worktree-root writeup. There were no architectural deviations. The evaluation-specific accommodation was running the existing two-KB gate unchanged and separately validating the mandatory six-KB configuration via the existing Makefile's command-line knobs; no evaluator, helper, benchmark, wrapper, configuration, other-core or tool file was edited.\\n\\nEvidence directory:\\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/bench-link-r9s0\\n\\nEvidence includes candidate/formal-gate.log, candidate/unit-tests.log, both pipeline-final.log and fpga-evaluation.json files, both cores/bench/generated/synth.json files and pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log reports, both pipeline-transcript.jsonl files, pipeline-comparison.json, selftest-comparison.json, coremark-rvfi-comparison.json, coremark-6kb-comparison.json, coremark-6kb-bounds.json, coremark-2kb-brackets.json, mapped-result-cones.json, routed-result-cones.json, routed-critical-paths.json, fitness-full-precision.json and sixkb/build.log.\\n\\nThe common six-KB ELF SHA-256 is a6902689a03fa4f82ab3a8762b46b189d9881d23cabd3a2459a13ca190820218. The identical six-KB RVFI stream SHA-256 is 8ca365f85a629c0e8a9ff46ef22b637d75d7601508c56df753ed39c80a009866; the identical two-KB stream SHA-256 is 2a593ed8e392590724cd08183d4028967d70829877d58517049bd0e205dcb7d9. The full streams were compared incrementally rather than stored; their counts, sizes, hashes and final records are retained in coremark-rvfi-comparison.json.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T20:49:05.682503Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-001-r9s0 (regression, -23.90%): Folding `PC+4` into an `ALU_LINK` case arm removed `is_jump` from all EX/MEM result cones and reduced mapped selection depth, yet forwarding through the unchanged JALR target adder into `EX/MEM.pc_next` became critical in all three seeds, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 124.67 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; fewer result-selection levels do not guarantee better routed timing.\"}\n27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Execute multiply-high in MEM outside the fast EX result path\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The incoming winner reaches 163.83 MHz and historical fitness 388.66.\\nSaved r6s0-baseline RTL matches the current bench RTL byte-for-byte;\\nits final routed reports reproduce 163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz.\\nSeeds 1 and 3 run from ID/EX ALU control to EX/MEM result data in\\n6.10 and 5.93 ns. The r7s0 netlist investigation identifies the\\nmultiply-high class decoder in this cone. Merely registering that\\nclass regressed 9.83 percent: it retained the combined execution\\ndatapath and exposed forwarding/JALR timing instead.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and structural log entries were reviewed. Divider separation\\nsuccessfully removed expensive arithmetic from ordinary execution;\\nlater predictor, PC-adder, and dependency-comparison retiming failed\\nto improve the winner. No bench experiment has moved high-half\\nmultiplication into the otherwise idle arithmetic capacity of MEM.\\nThe inspected bench/programs/coremark.elf contains twelve MULHU sites,\\nall under core_list_init or ee_printf, and no MULH/MULHSU sites.\\nThis static evidence suggests little direct timed dependency cost,\\nbut does not establish dynamic frequencies for either benchmark size.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The incoming winner reaches 163.83 MHz and historical fitness 388.66.\\nSaved r6s0-baseline RTL matches the current bench RTL byte-for-byte;\\nits final routed reports reproduce 163.83, 155.33, and 168.55 MHz.\\nSeeds 1 and 3 run from ID/EX ALU control to EX/MEM result data in\\n6.10 and 5.93 ns. The r7s0 netlist investigation identifies the\\nmultiply-high class decoder in this cone. Merely registering that\\nclass regressed 9.83 percent: it retained the combined execution\\ndatapath and exposed forwarding/JALR timing instead.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and structural log entries were reviewed. Divider separation\\nsuccessfully removed expensive arithmetic from ordinary execution;\\nlater predictor, PC-adder, and dependency-comparison retiming failed\\nto improve the winner. No bench experiment has moved high-half\\nmultiplication into the otherwise idle arithmetic capacity of MEM.\\nThe inspected bench/programs/coremark.elf contains twelve MULHU sites,\\nall under core_list_init or ee_printf, and no MULH/MULHSU sites.\\nThis static evidence suggests little direct timed dependency cost,\\nbut does not establish dynamic frequencies for either benchmark size.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move execution of MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU from EX into a dedicated\\ncombinational multiply-high lane in MEM. Keep low-half MUL and all\\nordinary integer operations in EX. This is one execution-stage\\nrepartition: high products become available at MEM/WB instead of\\nEX/MEM, without adding an instruction stage or changing retirement\\nwidth. Remove high-product arithmetic and result arms from the fast\\nALU rather than retaining an unused fast completion path.\\n\\nAdd one is_mul_high control bit, derived in ID only from a legal\\ndecoded MULH/MULHU/MULHSU. Capture it with the existing control bundle;\\nreset and every bubble clear it, and backend holds retain it. In EX,\\nkeep operand forwarding and capture the existing post-forward rs1_val\\nand rs2_val into EX/MEM. These existing fields become the high lane's\\narithmetic operands as well as its RVFI operands. The EX ALU may emit\\nzero for these three internal operation codes, but that placeholder\\nmust never be forwarded, written architecturally, or reported as the\\nretiring result. Keep the original instruction, operation, destination,\\nprediction, resolved pc_next, and trap metadata paired with operands.\\n\\nCompute the selected high product only from the registered EX/MEM\\noperands and operation. Move the current signed/signed,\\nunsigned/unsigned, and signed/unsigned expressions and their exact\\nhigh-half slices into the new lane without an arithmetic redesign.\\nOn the normal MEM/WB capture edge, select this result into alu_result\\nfor is_mul_high; other instructions retain the existing MEM behavior.\\nWB, register-file write-first bypass, and RVFI then receive the final\\nregistered result through their current paths. Do not forward the\\ncombinational MEM multiplier output back into EX: that would recreate\\na multiplier-plus-consumer path across the intended stage boundary.\\n\\nGeneralize the existing load-use interlock to an EX producer whose\\nresult is late: ctrl.mem_read OR ctrl.is_mul_high. Retain the current\\nnonzero-rd and raw source-field comparisons. An immediately dependent\\ninstruction holds at fetch and inserts one ID/EX bubble, exactly like\\na load consumer. On its later EX cycle, it uses MEM/WB forwarding.\\nQualify EX/MEM forwarding eligibility with NOT is_mul_high so the\\nplaceholder cannot override an older correct value. Retain the\\nestablished MEM/WB forwarding qualification, including forwarding\\nfrom retained payload after retirement valid clears during a memory\\nhold. Do not introduce operand-use filtering in this experiment.\\n\\nIndependent high multiplies can occupy EX and MEM in consecutive\\ncycles and still retire at one instruction per cycle. A high multiply\\nin MEM is not a memory request and must advance even if dmemReady is\\nlow; an older stalled load/store retains the existing backend hold.\\nPreserve the complete MEM/WB forwarding payload on a real memory\\nhold. Keep divider launch, accepted operand retention, eight-edge\\nreal/ALTOPS schedule, older-stage draining, and result consumption\\nunchanged. A divide consuming a high result receives the same late\\nproducer interlock as any other consumer. Reset, redirects, and invalid\\nslots cannot leave a high-operation control bit attached to another\\ninstruction. EX recovery and predictor training retain their current\\nacceptance rules; high multiply resolves its architectural PC normally.\\n\\nPreserve MUL-low's current ALTOPS formula in the fast ALU. Relocate\\nMULH=(a+b)^0xf6583fb7, MULHU=(a+b)^0x949ce5e8, and\\nMULHSU=(a-b)^0xecfbe137 into the MEM lane under RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS,\\nusing 32-bit modular arithmetic and the same captured operands.\\nReal and ALTOPS builds must use identical result availability and\\ninterlocks. Preserve every existing high-multiply arithmetic vector\\nby moving its coverage to the new lane and checking integrated CPU\\nretirement; removing high operations from the ALU is not permission\\nto remove their correctness coverage or weaken formal checks.\\n\\nPredict an unmeasured 5 percent fitness gain, with low confidence,\\nfrom removing high arithmetic and its selection from fast EX. The\\nmoved lane may become MEM's critical path, separately placed low/high\\nmultipliers may increase DSP/LUT demand, and existing recovery or\\npredictor paths may cap Fmax. Dependent high multiplies add bubbles;\\nmeasure their actual marker-bracketed frequency and cycle cost. Earlier\\nhigh operations can also change the random-ready phase at the start\\nmarker, so unchanged static timed code does not imply identical cycles.\\nInspect mapped and all three routed cones to confirm high arithmetic\\nis fed by EX/MEM registers and cannot reach the fast EX result directly.\\n\\nRun unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled ISS cosim, and FPGA gates.\\nCompare exact iterations/bracketed cycles times three-seed median\\nFmax against the incoming RTL with identical ELFs and ready schedules.\\nHistory identifies 388.66 as the scored 2 KB result; keep that comparison\\nseparate from the required canonical 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration run.\\nRequire d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, Correct operation validated., bounded\\naccesses, and timing strictly between accepted start/stop marker writes.\\nPreserve nret=1, every external port, strict channel-0 retirement order,\\ndecoder defaults and traps, memory ordering, and all hard invariants.\\nOnly bench RTL and its permitted unit tests are implementation targets.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add is_mul_high to ctrl_t so the validated execution class travels\\nwith all existing pipeline metadata. Preserve source operands and\\noperation codes; no extra instruction stage or retirement channel.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive is_mul_high from legal decoded MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and assign\\nit in ctrl_decoded. Preserve the winning split between control\\nflushes and payload holds, clearing this bit with every bubble.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove high-half multiply expressions and result cases from fast\\nexecution, including their ALTOPS cases. Retain exact combinational\\nMUL-low, ordinary operations, and the existing divider interface\\nand eight-edge behavior. High operation codes have an inert zero\\nfast result; their architectural result is supplied by MEM.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_high_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a combinational high-product lane accepting the original\\nfive-bit operation and two 32-bit operands. Preserve all three\\nexisting full-width real expressions, signedness, high-half slices,\\nand ALTOPS formulas; default to zero for other operation codes.\\nInstantiate it only on registered MEM operands in the CPU.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate mul_high_unit on in.rs1_val and in.rs2_val. Select its\\nresult into MEM/WB.alu_result for in.ctrl.is_mul_high on normal\\nadvancement. Preserve all other data, trap, bus, and RVFI behavior,\\nincluding held forwarding payload and valid-only clearing. High\\noperations neither drive dmem nor wait for unrelated dmem readiness.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Send mem_read OR is_mul_high to the generalized late-result hazard\\ninput. Mask EX/MEM reg_write eligibility at the forwarding-unit\\nconnection when is_mul_high is set. Leave MEM/WB eligibility and\\nforwarding data intact; never add a combinational MEM-high bypass.\\nPreserve the existing public interface and other pipeline wiring.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize id_ex_mem_read to id_ex_late_result while retaining\\nexactly the existing dependency equation, nonzero-rd rule,\\nbubble/hold priorities, redirect handling, and memory/divide waits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt the fast-ALU runner to its new internal result contract while\\nretaining all integer, MUL-low, divider, and ALTOPS assertions.\\nTransfer every high-multiply exact-vector and randomized check\\nto test_mul_high.py; continue checking no multiply launches DIV.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_mul_high.py\", \"description\": \"Run the new lane in real and ALTOPS builds with all transferred\\nhigh-product vectors plus randomized operand pairs, signed extrema,\\nzero, all-ones, mixed signs, carry-heavy products, and alternating\\nsuboperations. Compute expected results independently in Python.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Update the exhaustive truth table for the renamed late-result\\ninput without removing any original combinations or assertions.\\nRetain exact one-bubble behavior and backend-hold precedence.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing control/payload matrix with all three high\\noperations and reserved encodings. Check positive classification,\\nreset, flush, repeated holds, and simultaneous flush plus hold.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add the lane to source lists and update control-bundle unpackers.\\nPreserve all current assertions and add real/ALTOPS retirement\\nscoreboards for every high operation. Cover independent throughput,\\nimmediate and one-gap dependencies on both operands, repeated rd,\\nx0, signed boundaries, and high results feeding ALU/MUL, another\\nhigh multiply, DIV/REM, branch/JALR, and load/store base or data.\\nCheck one dependency bubble with ready buses and no EX/MEM high\\nplaceholder forwarding. Exercise memory holds, retained MEM/WB\\nforwarding after valid clears, instruction stalls, redirects,\\nwrong-path high operations, reset, traps, and exactly-once marker\\nstores. Require exact RVFI operands, results, PC chaining, and order.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add is_mul_high to ctrl_t so the validated execution class travels\\nwith all existing pipeline metadata. Preserve source operands and\\noperation codes; no extra instruction stage or retirement channel.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive is_mul_high from legal decoded MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and assign\\nit in ctrl_decoded. Preserve the winning split between control\\nflushes and payload holds, clearing this bit with every bubble.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove high-half multiply expressions and result cases from fast\\nexecution, including their ALTOPS cases. Retain exact combinational\\nMUL-low, ordinary operations, and the existing divider interface\\nand eight-edge behavior. High operation codes have an inert zero\\nfast result; their architectural result is supplied by MEM.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mul_high_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a combinational high-product lane accepting the original\\nfive-bit operation and two 32-bit operands. Preserve all three\\nexisting full-width real expressions, signedness, high-half slices,\\nand ALTOPS formulas; default to zero for other operation codes.\\nInstantiate it only on registered MEM operands in the CPU.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate mul_high_unit on in.rs1_val and in.rs2_val. Select its\\nresult into MEM/WB.alu_result for in.ctrl.is_mul_high on normal\\nadvancement. Preserve all other data, trap, bus, and RVFI behavior,\\nincluding held forwarding payload and valid-only clearing. High\\noperations neither drive dmem nor wait for unrelated dmem readiness.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Send mem_read OR is_mul_high to the generalized late-result hazard\\ninput. Mask EX/MEM reg_write eligibility at the forwarding-unit\\nconnection when is_mul_high is set. Leave MEM/WB eligibility and\\nforwarding data intact; never add a combinational MEM-high bypass.\\nPreserve the existing public interface and other pipeline wiring.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize id_ex_mem_read to id_ex_late_result while retaining\\nexactly the existing dependency equation, nonzero-rd rule,\\nbubble/hold priorities, redirect handling, and memory/divide waits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt the fast-ALU runner to its new internal result contract while\\nretaining all integer, MUL-low, divider, and ALTOPS assertions.\\nTransfer every high-multiply exact-vector and randomized check\\nto test_mul_high.py; continue checking no multiply launches DIV.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_mul_high.py\", \"description\": \"Run the new lane in real and ALTOPS builds with all transferred\\nhigh-product vectors plus randomized operand pairs, signed extrema,\\nzero, all-ones, mixed signs, carry-heavy products, and alternating\\nsuboperations. Compute expected results independently in Python.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Update the exhaustive truth table for the renamed late-result\\ninput without removing any original combinations or assertions.\\nRetain exact one-bubble behavior and backend-hold precedence.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing control/payload matrix with all three high\\noperations and reserved encodings. Check positive classification,\\nreset, flush, repeated holds, and simultaneous flush plus hold.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add the lane to source lists and update control-bundle unpackers.\\nPreserve all current assertions and add real/ALTOPS retirement\\nscoreboards for every high operation. Cover independent throughput,\\nimmediate and one-gap dependencies on both operands, repeated rd,\\nx0, signed boundaries, and high results feeding ALU/MUL, another\\nhigh multiply, DIV/REM, branch/JALR, and load/store base or data.\\nCheck one dependency bubble with ready buses and no EX/MEM high\\nplaceholder forwarding. Exercise memory holds, retained MEM/WB\\nforwarding after valid clears, instruction stalls, redirects,\\nwrong-path high operations, reset, traps, and exactly-once marker\\nstores. Require exact RVFI operands, results, PC chaining, and order.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 318.3, \"delta_pct\": -18.1, \"vs_baseline\": 12.55, \"fmax_mhz\": 134.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6631, \"ff\": 3205, \"seeds\": [137.74, 134.17, 133.99], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Execute multiply-high in MEM\\n\\nCorrectness gates pass, but the measured hypothesis regresses fitness by\\n18.10%: three-seed median Fmax falls from 163.83 to 134.17 MHz with exactly\\nunchanged benchmark cycles. The scored 2 KB fitness falls from 388.66 to\\n318.30. The predicted 5% improvement is not supported.\\n\\nImplemented the requested stage repartition in cores/bench only. MULH,\\nMULHU, and MULHSU now execute in mul_high_unit, instantiated by mem_stage\\non the existing registered EX/MEM post-forward operands and operation.\\nTheir original full-width signed/unsigned expressions and high-half\\nslices are preserved. Their ALTOPS formulas also moved to this lane.\\nMEM/WB captures the final result; there is no combinational high-result\\nbypass to EX. The fast ALU retains MUL-low and emits its default zero for\\nhigh operation codes. The divider and EX operand capture are unchanged.\\n\\nThe new ctrl.is_mul_high bit is classified in ID from a valid, legal\\ndecoded high operation. Existing control reset/flush and backend hold\\nrules carry it with the instruction. The load-use equation is generalized\\nto mem_read OR is_mul_high with the original nonzero-rd and raw source\\ncomparisons. EX/MEM forwarding excludes high operations. MEM/WB forwarding\\nqualification and every retained payload field remain unchanged when a\\nreal memory hold clears retirement valid. nret=1 and all public ports,\\nchannel-0 retirement, predictor acceptance, and trap rules are unchanged.\\n\\n## Coverage and local gates\\n\\n- Strict requested Verilator lint: PASS, no warnings. ALTOPS lint: PASS.\\n- Complete bench pytest/cocotb suite: 12 runners PASS, including real and\\n  ALTOPS ALU, high-lane, and integrated CPU builds. All existing real CPU\\n  tests and assertions remain enabled. All original high arithmetic\\n  vectors, including the identical deterministic random pairs, moved to\\n  the lane and integrated retirement tests. Additional checks cover\\n  alternating signedness, carry-heavy operands, independent throughput,\\n  one-bubble immediate dependencies, one-gap dependencies, both sources,\\n  raw immediate-field hazards, repeated rd, x0, every M consumer, ALU,\\n  branch/JALR, load/store bases and data, retained WB forwarding with\\n  valid=0, memory/instruction stalls, reset, traps, wrong-path high ops,\\n  stale prediction recovery, and exactly-once marker stores.\\n- Local formal: PASS on the first invocation, 53 passed / 0 failed.\\n  Ran unchanged bash formal/run_all.sh with CORE_NAME=bench and\\n  RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl; automatic nret=1 selection used the unchanged\\n  wrapper_si.sv and checks_si.cfg. No formal failures or fix retries.\\n  This is the ALTOPS gate; formal-deep was not run.\\n- Unchanged cosim gate: PASS, three ELFs (selftest, directed high-product\\n  fixture, scored CoreMark). Additionally, unchanged ISS comparison and\\n  Verilator harness passed selftest (115 retirements) and the generated\\n  multiply-high fixture (5,008 retirements) with --istall --dstall and\\n  ready seeds 1, 7, and 42. No out-of-bounds accesses.\\n- Both incoming and candidate RTL synthesized successfully with the\\n  unchanged nret=1 FPGA wrapper and synthesis script. All three routes\\n  completed normally for each variant. The unchanged FPGA evaluator\\n  passed placement and benchmark validation for both; results are below.\\n\\nAll build, formal, simulation, ELF, and FPGA outputs are scratch artifacts\\nunder $TMPDIR/mul-high-r9-vz6qtk7y. The baseline RTL was copied before any\\nedits. Candidate scratch RTL is byte-identical to the delivered RTL.\\nGate scripts, wrappers, reference ISS, benchmark sources, and constraints\\nwere copied unchanged; no contract file or other core was edited.\\n\\n## Benchmark comparison\\n\\nBoth variants use the identical ELF for each size, ten iterations, -O3,\\nand the unchanged --istall --dstall ready schedule. Timing uses accepted\\nstart/stop writes only. Full retirement streams independently confirm\\nexactly one write to each marker, their acceptance cycles, and the\\ndynamic high-operation counts strictly within the bracket.\\n\\n| Workload | Incoming start / stop | Candidate start / stop | Bracketed cycles, both | Timed retirements | Timed high ops / raw dependencies |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Historical scored 2 KB | 15,229 / 4,230,491 | 15,229 / 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 2,922,015 | 0 / 0 |\\n| Required canonical 6 KB | 53,747 / 30,876,537 | 53,747 / 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 22,052,222 | 0 / 0 |\\n\\nWhole-program high operations are 10 MULHU for 2 KB and 11 MULHU for 6 KB;\\nthere are no MULH/MULHSU or immediate raw-source high dependencies in\\neither program. High architectural results, operands, PCs, orders, and\\nretirement cycles match the incoming RTL. Non-memory mem_rdata is allowed\\nto differ with both masks zero. Measured cycle cost is zero for these\\ntwo workloads, including any ready-phase effects; unit tests separately\\nrequire exactly one bubble for an immediate high dependency on ready buses.\\n\\nThe 2 KB gate validated its unchanged e714/1fd7/8e3a CRCs and fcaf final CRC.\\nThe separate 6 KB run validated d4b0/be52/5e47 and 5949 final CRC, seedcrc\\n8a02, ten iterations, and \\\"Correct operation validated.\\\" Both runs\\ncompleted at EBREAK with oob=false and valid timing brackets. The existing\\nUART compiler-flags string says -O2; the actual recorded compiler command\\nuses -O3. Its CoreMark Size reports per-algorithm size (2000 for the 6000\\ntotal working set); the build command and 6 KB CRCs establish the size.\\n\\nELF SHA256:\\n\\n- 2 KB: 4fa22d06fd8c705d5d1c895c223ce464ec9193d3c6e5151f14dc1c39239704ea\\n- 6 KB: 07066d591e1381e2cbfdf6182211bc8ab2f4d593217dc4d8eba129d3505290b7\\n\\n## Structural evidence and concerns\\n\\nMapped netlist traversal confirms every sequential source of the MEM\\nDSP's operand/sign-selection cone is an EX/MEM register bit (70 bits,\\nincluding the operand and control bits). All 32 first sequential captures\\nreachable from its result are MEM/WB data inputs. There is no direct\\ncombinational path from this high multiplier to EX/MEM result capture.\\nThe original single MULT36X36 becomes two MULT36X36 cells: one for EX MUL-low\\nand one for MEM high multiplication. Thus the predicted DSP cost occurs.\\nYosys shares the three high expressions within the new lane; the source\\narithmetic itself was not redesigned.\\n\\nAll three final routed netlists independently pass the same boundary\\ntraversal: 70 EX/MEM source register bits, 32 MEM/WB result capture bits,\\nand no direct path to EX result capture. The high DSP is at\\nX10Y18/MULT36X36 in every candidate route. This is a graph check of actual\\nrouted connectivity, including combinational sign selection and result\\nfanout, rather than an inference from hierarchy names.\\n\\n| Metric | Incoming | Candidate |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Routed Fmax seeds 1 / 2 / 3 (MHz) | 163.83 / 155.33 / 168.55 | 137.74 / 134.17 / 133.99 |\\n| Median Fmax (MHz) | 163.83 | 134.17 |\\n| LUT4, reported by unchanged FPGA gate | 6,618 | 6,631 |\\n| FF | 3,198 | 3,205 |\\n| MULT36X36 | 1 | 2 |\\n| Scored 2 KB fitness | 388.659115 | 318.295755 |\\n| Separate required 6 KB fitness | 53.152229 | 43.529479 |\\n\\nFitness above is median_MHz * 1,000,000 * 10 / exact_bracketed_cycles,\\nnever the evaluator's rounded ipc_coremark field. Both workload scores\\nregress 18.104132%; the 6 KB comparison is separate from the historical\\n388.66 2 KB result.\\n\\nFinal critical cones, inspected in each routed report:\\n\\n- Incoming seed 1: ID/EX ALU control to EX/MEM ALU result, 6.10 ns.\\n- Incoming seed 2: ID/EX operand through recovery to ID/EX control reset,\\n  6.44 ns.\\n- Incoming seed 3: ID/EX ALU control to EX/MEM ALU result, 5.93 ns.\\n- Candidate seed 1: ID/EX alu_op[1] (id_ex_w[51]) through fast ALU result\\n  selection to EX/MEM alu_result[29], 7.26 ns (2.46 logic / 4.80 routing).\\n- Candidate seed 2: EX/MEM rd[2] through forwarding and the JALR adder to\\n  predictor pending_target_q[24], 7.45 ns (3.61 logic / 3.85 routing).\\n- Candidate seed 3: EX/MEM rd[4] through forwarding and the JALR adder to\\n  predictor pending_target_q[13], 7.46 ns (3.13 logic / 4.33 routing).\\n\\nThe MEM high multiplier is absent from all three reported critical cones.\\nRemoving its direct EX completion path did not shorten the remaining\\nALU selection and forwarding/JALR bottlenecks after remapping and routing.\\nThe extra DSP and the measured timing regression are material concerns;\\nthe implementation is retained as the requested experiment, without\\nfolding in unrelated optimizations or relaxing any gate.\\n\\nEvidence in the scratch root includes each variant's fpga-eval.json and\\nper-seed generated/pnr_seedN/{nextpnr.log,pnr.json}, plus candidate\\nformal.log, cocotb.log, cosim-gate.json, stalled-iss.json,\\nmapped-boundary.json, routed-boundaries.json, canonical.jsonl, and both\\nvariants' frequency-2000.json / frequency-6000.json.\\n\\nNo implementation deviation beyond also gating ID high classification\\nwith input validity. Scratch execution protects the read-only/shared\\nformal framework and contract files. The requested root notes file is\\nthe only non-RTL/test deliverable. Local formal remains PASS on the first\\nattempt; there was no RTL correction after starting the validation gates.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T20:54:21.038466Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r9s1 (regression, -18.10%): Moving `MULH/MULHU/MULHSU` from EX to MEM doubled `MULT36X36` use from one to two yet left fast-ALU selection and forwarding/JALR as the routed bottlenecks, lowering median Fmax from 163.83 to 134.17 MHz with unchanged 2 KB/6 KB CoreMark cycles and no timed high multiplies; arithmetic stage isolation can lose multiplier sharing without shortening the limiting paths.\"}\n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Capture raw predictor events before update classification\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current sixteen-entry predictor classifies each live EX resolution\\ninto INVALIDATE/JUMP/TAKEN/NOT_TAKEN before capturing its private row\\nevent. Classification includes target-trap legality and branch outcome;\\nthe same late conditions also qualify the pending target register's\\ncapture enable. This puts prediction maintenance on the execution path\\neven though its writes occur one edge later.\\n\\nThe predictor history supports shortening maintenance paths while\\nretaining prediction behavior: row-local training and registered events\\nimproved fitness in r3s2 and r4s1. Conversely, r8s2 lost 33.64% after\\nuntagged direction training increased recoveries and synthesis changed\\npending commands from one-hot to binary. Its routed bottleneck crossed\\nforwarding and EX classification into command capture. Smaller tables,\\npartial tags, additional tag-validation stages, and thermometer counters\\nhave also regressed. The proposed experiment changes where training\\nclassification occurs, retaining the winner's tagged predictor policy.\\nThe winner itself has competing ALU and recovery limits, so removing\\nthis maintenance path is a hypothesis, not a guaranteed Fmax gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current sixteen-entry predictor classifies each live EX resolution\\ninto INVALIDATE/JUMP/TAKEN/NOT_TAKEN before capturing its private row\\nevent. Classification includes target-trap legality and branch outcome;\\nthe same late conditions also qualify the pending target register's\\ncapture enable. This puts prediction maintenance on the execution path\\neven though its writes occur one edge later.\\n\\nThe predictor history supports shortening maintenance paths while\\nretaining prediction behavior: row-local training and registered events\\nimproved fitness in r3s2 and r4s1. Conversely, r8s2 lost 33.64% after\\nuntagged direction training increased recoveries and synthesis changed\\npending commands from one-hot to binary. Its routed bottleneck crossed\\nforwarding and EX classification into command capture. Smaller tables,\\npartial tags, additional tag-validation stages, and thermometer counters\\nhave also regressed. The proposed experiment changes where training\\nclassification occurs, retaining the winner's tagged predictor policy.\\nThe winner itself has competing ALU and recovery limits, so removing\\nthis maintenance path is a hypothesis, not a guaranteed Fmax gain.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move predictor update classification across the EXISTING event-capture\\nboundary, without adding another stage. In each private row, replace\\npending_command_q with independent registered raw fields: decoded\\nis_branch, decoded is_jump, resolved branch-taken, decoder legality\\nBEFORE EX target-alignment trapping, and target-misaligned. Capture the\\nlast field from resolve_target[1:0] != 0, independently of outcome.\\nContinue capturing the full tag and row validity at the existing edge.\\nEvery payload capture enable must remain resolve_valid && row_index_hit;\\nneither branch outcome nor target legality qualifies that enable.\\n\\nExpose the pre-trap decoder-legality bit from EX through core to IF.\\nKeep EX's architectural trap generation, resolved legality, next-PC\\nselection, and once-only resolve_valid qualification unchanged. The\\noriginal resolved-legality signal may remain available to existing\\nmonitors, but must not feed the new predictor capture cones.\\n\\nAfter capture, derive the original command locally from the registered\\nraw fields. For decoder-produced control classes, effective legality is\\ndecode_legal && !(target_misaligned && (jump || (branch && taken))).\\nAn effectively illegal event or non-control becomes INVALIDATE; a legal\\njump becomes JUMP; otherwise a legal branch becomes TAKEN or NOT_TAKEN.\\nThus an untaken branch with a misaligned encoded destination remains\\nlegal and can decrement a matching counter; a taken misaligned branch\\nor jump invalidates only its matching entry. Derive this combinationally\\nafter the registers rather than storing another encoded command.\\n\\nApply precisely the existing row-local commit rules against current\\ntag/valid/counter state. JUMP sets counter 11; a TAKEN miss allocates 10,\\nand a TAKEN hit saturating-increments. NOT_TAKEN decrements only a tag\\nhit, retaining the target and never allocating. INVALIDATE clears only\\na matching valid entry. Preserve all sixteen PC[5:2] rows, full 26-bit\\ntags, thirty-bit aligned targets, and committed-state-only fetch lookup.\\n\\nMake the single indexed target path capture its raw event on every\\nresolve_valid, rather than only when live target_write is true. Retain\\nits index and target payload, and capture the same raw classification\\nfields alongside them. At the existing commit edge, generate its write\\nenable exclusively from that registered event's validity and locally\\nderived JUMP/TAKEN classification. This global target event and every\\nprivate metadata event must refer to the same resolution age; do not\\ncombine a pending target with live EX fields or an indexed metadata read.\\nMetadata and target still publish together at N+1 for capture at N.\\n\\nCapture and commit must overlap at one event per edge, including\\nconsecutive aliases and changing JALR targets. Pending validity refreshes\\nevery edge independently of fetch stalls, memory holds, divide waits,\\nand redirects, so accepted events drain exactly once. Reset clears all\\ntable/event validity, suppresses writes, and cancels pending work. Raw\\npayloads need no reset because validity masks every commit and lookup.\\n\\nThis experiment targets the late EX classification and target-capture\\nenable cones; it does not change prediction accuracy or visibility.\\nVerify in synthesized and routed netlists that capture D pins receive\\nseparate raw fields and that pending target capture enables no longer\\ndepend on taken/trap classification. Derived command logic belongs only\\nafter the register boundary. Report automatic encoding or enable\\ninference that defeats this separation. Added event bits, routing,\\ntarget-register switching, and competing ALU/recovery or row-counter\\npaths can offset the benefit. Estimate an unmeasured four-percent net\\nfitness gain, with low confidence.\\n\\nRequire identical predictions, redirect decisions, training visibility,\\nretirement and bus-transaction cycle positions, and bracketed CoreMark\\ncycles against the incoming winner for identical ELFs and ready\\nschedules. Preserve the exact accepted predicted_next_pc and full-width\\nEX mismatch recovery for every advancing instruction, including stale\\nnon-controls, traps, and completed divides. Preserve nret=1, all external\\nIO, RVFI order, decoder checks, arithmetic, and every hard invariant.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, unit, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA\\ngates. Evaluate full-precision iterations/bracketed-cycles times median\\nFmax_Hz, recording LUT4/FF cost and all three routed critical paths.\\nHistory identifies 388.66 with the scored 2 KB image; keep that comparison\\nseparate from the required canonical 6 KB measurement without editing\\nthe evaluator. Validate the 6 KB ten-iteration run with both stalls,\\naccepted start/stop markers, bounded accesses, CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, and\\nthe literal Correct operation validated. in UART output.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace each encoded pending command with raw registered event\\nfields and classify commands only after capture. Change the indexed\\ntarget path to capture the raw resolution and qualify writes at\\ncommit using its own registered fields. Preserve original tagged\\ncounter policy, table capacity, capture/commit edges, atomic target\\npublication, alias ordering, reset cancellation, and PC priority.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a raw predictor-feedback decoder-legality bit directly from\\nin.ctrl.is_illegal, before target-alignment trapping. Keep existing\\nresolved target, branch outcome, architectural legality, resolution\\nqualification, recovery, and execution datapaths unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route the raw decoder-legality feedback from EX to IF and adapt\\ninternal predictor wiring. Retain resolved-legality observability\\nfor existing checks without using it for event capture. Preserve\\nthe module's external bus and single-channel RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt stimulus to raw decode legality while retaining the existing\\nindependent predictor-policy oracle and capture/commit expectations.\\nCover illegal decoding, taken versus untaken misaligned branches,\\nmisaligned jumps, every counter transition, and all sixteen rows.\\nAlternate target-writing and non-writing events on consecutive\\nedges, including same-index different-tag aliases, to detect a\\npending target paired with the wrong classification. Check idle\\ndraining, stalls, redirects, full tag/target bits, and reset with\\npending work without weakening existing output assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain the exact accepted-prediction and once-only resolution\\nscoreboard. Extend directed coverage for adjacent legal jumps,\\nuntaken branches, decoded traps, and target-alignment traps around\\nmemory holds, imem stalls, load-use bubbles, and divide completion.\\nCheck unchanged recovery cycles and training visibility, strict\\nRVFI order and PC chaining, link/operand data, and suppression of\\nwrong-path retirement and stores. Compare against the incoming\\ndesign with identical instructions and bus-ready schedules.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace each encoded pending command with raw registered event\\nfields and classify commands only after capture. Change the indexed\\ntarget path to capture the raw resolution and qualify writes at\\ncommit using its own registered fields. Preserve original tagged\\ncounter policy, table capacity, capture/commit edges, atomic target\\npublication, alias ordering, reset cancellation, and PC priority.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a raw predictor-feedback decoder-legality bit directly from\\nin.ctrl.is_illegal, before target-alignment trapping. Keep existing\\nresolved target, branch outcome, architectural legality, resolution\\nqualification, recovery, and execution datapaths unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route the raw decoder-legality feedback from EX to IF and adapt\\ninternal predictor wiring. Retain resolved-legality observability\\nfor existing checks without using it for event capture. Preserve\\nthe module's external bus and single-channel RVFI ports.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt stimulus to raw decode legality while retaining the existing\\nindependent predictor-policy oracle and capture/commit expectations.\\nCover illegal decoding, taken versus untaken misaligned branches,\\nmisaligned jumps, every counter transition, and all sixteen rows.\\nAlternate target-writing and non-writing events on consecutive\\nedges, including same-index different-tag aliases, to detect a\\npending target paired with the wrong classification. Check idle\\ndraining, stalls, redirects, full tag/target bits, and reset with\\npending work without weakening existing output assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Retain the exact accepted-prediction and once-only resolution\\nscoreboard. Extend directed coverage for adjacent legal jumps,\\nuntaken branches, decoded traps, and target-alignment traps around\\nmemory holds, imem stalls, load-use bubbles, and divide completion.\\nCheck unchanged recovery cycles and training visibility, strict\\nRVFI order and PC chaining, link/operand data, and suppression of\\nwrong-path retirement and stores. Compare against the incoming\\ndesign with identical instructions and bus-ready schedules.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 297.66, \"delta_pct\": -23.41, \"vs_baseline\": 5.25, \"fmax_mhz\": 125.47, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6675, \"ff\": 3219, \"seeds\": [125.47, 128.01, 115.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# Capture raw predictor events before update classification\\n\\nImplemented the proposed register boundary change and retained the incoming predictor policy and cycle behavior. All correctness gates pass, and synthesis plus all three routed netlists retain the separate raw capture fields. The measured FPGA result is a regression: median Fmax is 125.47 MHz and the scored 2 KB fitness is 297.656468328659 iterations/s, versus the incoming winner's 163.83 MHz and 388.659115376458 iterations/s, a 23.414515046084% reduction. The proposed low-confidence four-percent gain is not supported by this measurement.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\n- `ex_stage.sv` exposes `resolve_decode_legal = !in.ctrl.is_illegal`. Existing architectural target traps, resolved legality, next PC, full-width prediction mismatch recovery, divide behavior, and once-only `resolve_valid` are unchanged.\\n- `core.sv` routes decoder legality into IF. The original `resolve_legal` remains connected to EX and visible to monitors, but does not feed predictor capture. External buses and the single-channel RVFI interface remain unchanged; `nret` remains 1.\\n- `if_stage.sv` replaces every private pending command register with five independent raw registers: branch, jump, taken, decoder legality, and target misalignment. Misalignment captures `resolve_target[1:0] != 0` without outcome qualification. Tag and all five fields use only `resolve_valid && resolve_index == row` as their payload capture enable.\\n- A combinational `classify_event` function is applied independently to each registered metadata event and to the registered target event. It implements decoder legality with outcome-qualified target trapping after capture. Untaken misaligned branches can decrement a matching row; taken misaligned branches and misaligned jumps invalidate only matching valid rows.\\n- The single target event captures its index, thirty target bits and five raw fields on every `resolve_valid`. Its registered validity and local JUMP/TAKEN classification drive target publication at the same N+1 edge as metadata. No live EX field or indexed metadata read participates in this decision.\\n- All sixteen PC[5:2] rows, full 26-bit tags, aligned 30-bit targets, tagged two-bit saturation policy, target retention on NOT_TAKEN, committed-state-only fetch lookup, overlapping capture/commit, and PC priority remain unchanged. Validity refreshes each edge regardless of holds or recovery. Reset clears table/event validity, cancels pending work, and suppresses publication. Raw payloads have no reset.\\n\\nThe table remains 944 source-level bits. Event state grows from 499 to 552 source-level bits: `16*(1+26+5) + (1+4+30+5)`. There is no extra pipeline stage.\\n\\n## Tests and local gates\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors. `git diff --check`: PASS.\\n- Unchanged `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, all nine runners. Existing decoder, arithmetic, ALTOPS, forwarding, retirement, and pipeline assertions remain intact.\\n- Local formal status: **PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed**, using unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl`, `CORE_NAME=bench`, and `JOBS=6`. Automatic nret=1 selection used `wrapper_si.sv` and `checks_si.cfg`. There was no formal counterexample and no RTL formal-fix attempt. `formal-deep` was not run because arithmetic RTL was unchanged.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: PASS for selftest and scored CoreMark. Additional selftest runs with both stalls, seeds 1, 2, 3 and 0xDEADBEEF, each passed the protected Python ISS comparison for all 115 retirements and matched incoming RVFI output byte-for-byte, including cycle numbers.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: PASS, all three seeds routed successfully and scored CoreMark passed the unchanged evaluator.\\n\\nThe IF oracle continues to check public output before and after every edge. It now derives architectural legality from raw decoder legality and destination/outcome, and adds all-row alternating writes/nonwrites, misaligned taken/untaken branches, misaligned jumps, decoded illegality, colliding tags, target retention, invalid-event draining, and reset cancellation. Existing counter transitions, tag/target bit sweeps, stalls, redirects and visibility checks remain.\\n\\nThe pipeline oracle still trains from EX's independently observed architectural legality. It retains exact accepted-prediction, once-only resolution, strict order, PC chaining, bus and operand checks, and verifies the new decoder-legality output against the ID/EX control bundle even on holds and bubbles. New directed programs put adjacent jumps, untaken misaligned branches, decoded traps and target traps around memory holds, instruction stalls, load-use bubbles and divide completion.\\n\\nOptional trace capture/comparison in `test_pipeline.py` ran the same extended suite on saved incoming RTL and the candidate: **103 runs and 5,694 cycle samples matched exactly**, including predictions, recovery cycles, training visibility, complete pipeline bundles, retirement cycles, and bus signals.\\n\\nAdded `test_predictor_equivalence.py` as an optional ELF comparison utility. It separately compiles the incoming and candidate cores, includes the protected cosim harness verbatim, and compares all 26 external outputs plus prediction, resolution, redirect and hold/flush signals after every evaluation on both clock phases. Both models receive identical harness inputs; a mismatch aborts immediately. This passed for selftest, scored 2 KB CoreMark, and canonical 6 KB CoreMark. The comparison simulator's complete CoreMark stdout exactly matches the ordinary harness stdout for both images. It does not replace or modify any gate.\\n\\n## Tool and artifact isolation\\n\\nProtected gate sources were copied unchanged beneath the supplied TMPDIR so their generated artifacts did not modify protected worktree paths. Final gate RTL and test copies were checked byte-for-byte against the delivered files. No other core, evaluator, benchmark, formal contract, FPGA contract, or core.yaml was edited. This notes file uses the explicitly requested worktree-root location.\\n\\nFinal measurements use the project's pinned Verilator 5.047 devel v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6, Yosys 0.64+149 (2dc69a757-dirty), nextpnr-0.10-45-g98c18d7f, existing RISC-V GCC, and installed Conda Python dependencies. Device, constraints and seeds are unchanged. An initial formal launcher lacked PyYAML, and an initial scratch shell selected Homebrew synthesis tools. These environment issues were corrected; the full final gates were run with the pinned tools. Preliminary Homebrew results are excluded from the reported comparison.\\n\\n## Workloads and full-precision fitness\\n\\nBoth implementations used each identical ELF with `--istall --dstall`, default seed 0xDEADBEEF, and a 50,000,000-cycle ceiling. Both CoreMark builds use ten iterations and actual compiler option `-O3`. The canonical 6000-byte image was built separately with the existing Makefile's size override. It was not substituted into the evaluator, whose 2000-byte CRC expectations remain unchanged.\\n\\n| Workload | Start marker cycle | Stop marker cycle | Bracketed cycles, both designs | Retirements, both designs |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Scored 2 KB | 15,229 | 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 2,937,037 |\\n| Canonical 6 KB | 53,747 | 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 22,093,920 |\\n\\nThe lockstep comparisons covered 4,237,256 active edges for 2 KB and 30,883,294 for 6 KB, including initialization, markers and UART output. Prediction decisions, recovery decisions, retirement positions and bus transactions were identical throughout, rather than merely yielding equal bracket lengths.\\n\\nBoth canonical runs accepted start/stop markers, completed within bounds with `oob=false`, reported ten iterations and `6k performance run parameters for coremark.`, and passed exact CRC checks: seedcrc=8a02, crclist=d4b0, crcmatrix=be52, crcstate=5e47, crcfinal=5949. Both contain the literal `Correct operation validated.`. The 2 KB runs passed e9f5/e714/1fd7/8e3a/fcaf. The protected UART compiler-flags string still says `-O2`; the actual compiler command is `-O3`. The 6 KB UART's `CoreMark Size : 2000` is the per-algorithm allocation.\\n\\nELF SHA256:\\n\\n- Scored 2 KB: `087b5bf9767e1fd817266b1685722cc35dd6ac6a9200160c0e03306e53b36809`\\n- Canonical 6 KB: `13de87c9a2c275cae830760ba696dab32972da5989e7d20942d24caa7a405334`\\n\\n| Metric | Incoming winner | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 163.83 | 125.47 |\\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 155.33 | 128.01 |\\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 168.55 | 115.07 |\\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 163.83 | 125.47 |\\n| LUT4 | 6,618 | 6,675 |\\n| FF | 3,198 | 3,219 |\\n| Scored 2 KB iterations/s | 388.659115376458 | 297.656468328659 |\\n| Separate canonical 6 KB iterations/s | 53.152229243362 | 40.706892529846 |\\n\\nFitness uses `10 / bracketed_cycles * median_Fmax_MHz * 1,000,000`, without using the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark=2e-06`. The historical 388.66 score belongs to the scored 2 KB workload, not the canonical 6 KB measurement. The incoming FPGA column comes from existing three-seed winner artifacts, whose RTL was verified byte-for-byte identical to the incoming design and whose synthesis tool version matches the final candidate run; incoming P&R was not rerun. Incoming execution measurements were rerun live on the same ELFs as the candidate.\\n\\n## Synthesized and routed capture boundary\\n\\nConnection and LUT truth-table checks passed on the synthesized netlist and all three routed netlists, following inserted buffers and actual register pins:\\n\\n- All **85 raw field registers** survive as distinct flip-flops: five per metadata row and five in the target event. There are no encoded pending-command registers.\\n- All **535 payload capture-enable endpoints** were checked: 16 private sets of 31 bits and 39 global target-event bits. Each private enable equals the existing event enable AND its PC index match; every global enable equals the existing event enable. All raw D inputs match their respective fields independently. Decoder legality is the inverse of the ID/EX illegal bit; misalignment has no branch-outcome/decoder-legality qualification.\\n- The eight inferred RAM16SDP4 target banks receive the thirty registered target payload bits and registered index. Their WRE truth tables depend only on registered raw classification, pending validity and reset, and implement legal JUMP/TAKEN publication. No live EX or indexed metadata source appears there.\\n- The incoming command registers were automatically recoded from two source bits into four one-hot bits per row. The candidate retains five independent fields per row instead. This explains why the source-level event increase of 53 bits produces a measured FF increase of 21. No automatic encoding or capture-enable inference defeated the requested separation.\\n\\nAll three final routed critical paths terminate at `cpu.u_if.metadata[0].pending_taken_q_DFFE_Q.D`. They cross forwarding and the unsigned branch comparison before raw outcome capture. The long autogenerated names containing `pending_target_q` on intermediate combinational nets do not imply target payload/classification mixing; the pin-level audit above verifies the actual sources.\\n\\n| Seed | Critical source | Critical endpoint | Logic / routing | Total |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | MEM/WB instr[9] / rd[2], `cpu.u_mem.reg_q_DFFR_Q_98.Q` | Row 0 raw taken D | 3.81 / 4.16 ns | 7.97 ns |\\n| 2 | MEM/WB instr[8] / rd[1], `cpu.u_mem.reg_q_DFFR_Q_99.Q` | Row 0 raw taken D | 3.75 / 4.06 ns | 7.81 ns |\\n| 3 | ID/EX instr[17] / rs1_addr[2], `cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_162.Q` | Row 0 raw taken D | 4.07 / 4.62 ns | 8.69 ns |\\n\\nThe principal concern is the measured raw-outcome capture path and its routing. Moving command classification succeeded structurally but did not remove branch-resolution delay from event capture. Added raw-event fanout, placement changes and target-register switching can offset the intended benefit; power was not measured, and the measurements do not isolate one cause for the timing regression.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n`/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/raw-predictor-r9s2`\\n\\nFinal evidence includes `oss-unit.log`, `oss-formal.log`, `oss-cosim.log`, `oss-fpga.log`, `incoming-pipeline.log`, `pipeline.log`, `winner-traces/`, `workload-results.json`, `measurement-summary.json`, `capture-netlist-audit.json`, all three `critical-path-seed*.txt` reports, the final `gates-oss/cores/bench/generated/` netlists/logs, and `equivalence/` build logs and per-ELF results. The optional equivalence utility and the extended tests are the only additions beyond the suggested RTL/test files.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T20:57:48.923547Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-003-r9s2 (regression, -23.41%): Moving predictor classification after raw-field capture preserved CoreMark cycles and survived synthesis, but forwarding through the unsigned branch comparator into `pending_taken_q.D` limited all three routed seeds, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 125.47 MHz; raw outcome capture still carries branch-resolution delay.\"}\n30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch from decode with one fall-through instruction slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current frontend discards an available instruction whenever load-use,\\ndata-memory backpressure, or a divide prevents decode acceptance. It then\\ndepends on io_imemReady again when execution resumes. Separating fetch\\nacceptance from decode acceptance can overlap these existing waits with\\ninstruction delivery under the approximately 22 percent bus-stall workload.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment history show that recent timing\\nrepartitions have left CoreMark cycles unchanged while reducing Fmax:\\nr8s1 operand-match retiming lost 6.12 percent fitness, and r9s1 moving high\\nmultiplication into MEM lost 18.10 percent. The earlier pending-store\\nexperiment saved only 0.75 percent of cycles. No prior bench hypothesis\\nintroduces an elastic fetch slot. This proposal targets instruction-supply\\noverlap while retaining the winner's execution and predictor organization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current frontend discards an available instruction whenever load-use,\\ndata-memory backpressure, or a divide prevents decode acceptance. It then\\ndepends on io_imemReady again when execution resumes. Separating fetch\\nacceptance from decode acceptance can overlap these existing waits with\\ninstruction delivery under the approximately 22 percent bus-stall workload.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the structural experiment history show that recent timing\\nrepartitions have left CoreMark cycles unchanged while reducing Fmax:\\nr8s1 operand-match retiming lost 6.12 percent fitness, and r9s1 moving high\\nmultiplication into MEM lost 18.10 percent. The earlier pending-store\\nexperiment saved only 0.75 percent of cycles. No prior bench hypothesis\\nintroduces an elastic fetch slot. This proposal targets instruction-supply\\noverlap while retaining the winner's execution and predictor organization.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exactly one instruction holding slot inside if_stage, with a\\ncombinational fall-through path when empty. Store only the complete\\nif_id_t fetch tuple: PC, instruction, exact predicted_next_pc, and validity.\\nRead registers and decode at actual ID acceptance, never at enqueue time.\\nKeep the existing ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB stages and single retirement.\\n\\nSeparate the two acceptance events. Let q_valid denote slot occupancy,\\navailable = q_valid || io_imemReady, and issue_fire mean available and\\ndecode can accept, with neither reset nor recovery active. Decode can\\naccept only when the existing load-use interlock, data-memory hold, and\\ndivide wait allow it. Let fetch_fire = io_imemReady && (!q_valid ||\\nissue_fire), also suppressed by reset or recovery. Only fetch_fire advances\\nthe fetch PC to the raw fetch's prediction. Decode always selects the\\nstored tuple when q_valid is set, otherwise the live fetch tuple.\\n\\nWhen empty, fetch_fire plus issue_fire transfers directly to ID without\\noccupying the slot or adding a cycle. When empty and decode is blocked,\\nfetch_fire saves the tuple and advances the fetch PC once. When occupied,\\nissue_fire consumes the stored instruction even if io_imemReady is zero;\\nsimultaneous fetch_fire replaces it with the next raw fetch. An occupied\\nslot with no issue_fire retains every field and holds the fetch PC. Support\\nconsume/refill on the same edge so ready buses still sustain one issue per\\ncycle. Neither an unready response nor an unaccepted repeated response can\\noverwrite the slot. Keep availability independent of downstream flushes\\nand acceptance so this control has no combinational ready/valid loop.\\n\\nDrive hazard detection from the selected oldest instruction, not the\\ninstruction at the now-ahead bus PC. Replace raw imem readiness in the\\nfrontend bubble/hold equations with instruction availability. A bus stall\\nmust not bubble a valid queued instruction. Retain the existing raw-source\\nload-use comparisons, backend holds, complete control clearing on bubbles,\\nretained MEM/WB forwarding data, and eight-edge real/ALTOPS divider behavior.\\nPreserve the existing NOP substitution for recovery or no available\\ninstruction and the winning ID/EX control-versus-payload reset separation.\\n\\nEX recovery clears slot validity, suppresses both transfers on that edge,\\nkills the younger decode instruction, and redirects the fetch PC even\\nwhen imem is unready or the slot is full. The redirected target uses the\\nempty-slot bypass on its first ready cycle, preserving the existing\\nmisprediction latency. Reset cancels buffered work. Flush only the slot's\\nvalid bit; do not distribute recovery into its wide payload reset/enable.\\nA stored prediction is immutable through all waits and predictor updates;\\nEX compares against the prediction that actually advanced the fetch PC.\\nKeep all sixteen full-tag predictor entries, counter policy, row-local\\nevent capture, target commit timing, and resolve-on-advancement rules.\\nFetching or enqueuing does not train the predictor.\\n\\nInstruction buffering also requires FENCE.I synchronization. On advancement\\nof a legal FENCE.I in EX, use the existing recovery mechanism to discard\\nboth buffered and decoding younger instructions and refetch its resolved\\nPC+4, even if its prediction matched. An older stalled memory operation\\nmust finish before this event, as enforced by the existing backend hold.\\nThis instruction still retires once with its existing legal NOP result\\nand PC+4 RVFI next PC. Preserve decoder legality and ordinary FENCE behavior.\\nNo queued instruction may cause architectural side effects before issue;\\nmemory ordering, physical marker handshakes, and bounds checks remain intact.\\n\\nEstimate an unmeasured four percent fitness improvement, with low\\nconfidence, from fewer instruction-starvation cycles. The added tuple mux,\\nconsume/refill control, increased prediction age, and discarded prefetches\\ncan outweigh the benefit or reduce Fmax. Record queued issues during imem\\nstalls, captures during backend/load-use waits, and recovery discards to\\nestablish whether the intended overlap occurs. Compare exact bracketed\\ncycles and three-seed median Fmax with the incoming winner using identical\\nELFs and ready-generator seeds; do not infer fitness from cycle savings alone.\\n\\nRun unchanged lint, cocotb, formal including liveness, stalled ISS cosim,\\nand FPGA gates. Preserve nret=1, every external port, channel-0-only ordered\\nretirement, all trap and arithmetic checks, and every hard invariant.\\nHistory identifies 388.66 with the scored 2 KB ELF; keep that comparison\\nseparate from the mandatory canonical 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration validation\\nwith d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs and Correct operation validated. Both use accepted\\nstart/stop markers and exact iterations/cycle times median Fmax in Hz.\\nImplementation is confined to bench RTL and permitted bench unit tests.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add one fall-through fetch slot and independent fetch/issue acceptance.\\nExpose unflushed instruction availability, accept imem readiness, and\\nupdate the raw fetch PC only on fetch acceptance or recovery. Preserve\\ncaptured prediction metadata, predictor internals, and output NOP\\nsubstitution. Implement atomic consume/refill, stable full-slot holds,\\nand reset/recovery cancellation without flushing wide payload fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use selected-instruction availability instead of raw imem readiness\\nfor frontend bubbles and issue blocking. Continue comparing the oldest\\nselected instruction's raw source fields for load-use. Preserve all\\nbackend hold, divide drain, forwarding-retention, and flush priorities;\\nissue blocking must allow independent fetch capture into an empty slot.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect io_imemReady directly to the frontend, route its availability\\nto the hazard unit, and keep hazard source addresses tied to the tuple\\nselected for decode. Wire issue blocking and shared recovery without\\nchanging public ports, retirement width, or any backend datapath.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing advancement-qualified recovery condition to a\\nlegal FENCE.I, redirecting to architectural PC+4 so prefetched younger\\ninstructions are discarded after older memory work completes. Keep\\narchitectural next-PC calculation, training acceptance, all arithmetic,\\ntraps, and normal prediction-mismatch recovery unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent frontend reference with one fetched tuple and\\nseparate acceptance events. Preserve all predictor-policy assertions.\\nCover empty bypass, capture while issue is blocked, full retention,\\nconsume with imem unready, simultaneous consume/refill, reset and\\nredirect in every occupancy state, and prediction changes while a\\nstored tuple waits. Verify that recovery retains the original target\\nacceptance latency and that each accepted fetch is consumed or killed once.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Update the exhaustive frontend truth table for instruction availability\\nwhile retaining every backend hold and bubble assertion. Check load-use,\\nredirect, memory holds, and divide waits with an available queued head\\nindependently of live instruction-bus readiness in integration coverage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add architectural retirement and side-effect scoreboards under directed\\nand deterministic random bus stalls. Exercise queued load dependencies,\\nsource fields differing from the live bus instruction, writes occurring\\nbetween enqueue and decode, queued work during divides, full-slot\\nrecovery with imem unready, wrong-path stores/divides, and stable saved\\nprediction metadata. Cover FENCE.I refetch after changed instruction\\ncontents and an older stalled store. Require exactly-once in-order RVFI,\\nmemory and marker handshakes, ready-bus bypass throughput, and demonstrable\\nissue from the slot while imemReady is zero. Preserve existing assertions.\\n\"}]}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add one fall-through fetch slot and independent fetch/issue acceptance.\\nExpose unflushed instruction availability, accept imem readiness, and\\nupdate the raw fetch PC only on fetch acceptance or recovery. Preserve\\ncaptured prediction metadata, predictor internals, and output NOP\\nsubstitution. Implement atomic consume/refill, stable full-slot holds,\\nand reset/recovery cancellation without flushing wide payload fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use selected-instruction availability instead of raw imem readiness\\nfor frontend bubbles and issue blocking. Continue comparing the oldest\\nselected instruction's raw source fields for load-use. Preserve all\\nbackend hold, divide drain, forwarding-retention, and flush priorities;\\nissue blocking must allow independent fetch capture into an empty slot.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect io_imemReady directly to the frontend, route its availability\\nto the hazard unit, and keep hazard source addresses tied to the tuple\\nselected for decode. Wire issue blocking and shared recovery without\\nchanging public ports, retirement width, or any backend datapath.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing advancement-qualified recovery condition to a\\nlegal FENCE.I, redirecting to architectural PC+4 so prefetched younger\\ninstructions are discarded after older memory work completes. Keep\\narchitectural next-PC calculation, training acceptance, all arithmetic,\\ntraps, and normal prediction-mismatch recovery unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_if_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the independent frontend reference with one fetched tuple and\\nseparate acceptance events. Preserve all predictor-policy assertions.\\nCover empty bypass, capture while issue is blocked, full retention,\\nconsume with imem unready, simultaneous consume/refill, reset and\\nredirect in every occupancy state, and prediction changes while a\\nstored tuple waits. Verify that recovery retains the original target\\nacceptance latency and that each accepted fetch is consumed or killed once.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Update the exhaustive frontend truth table for instruction availability\\nwhile retaining every backend hold and bubble assertion. Check load-use,\\nredirect, memory holds, and divide waits with an available queued head\\nindependently of live instruction-bus readiness in integration coverage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add architectural retirement and side-effect scoreboards under directed\\nand deterministic random bus stalls. Exercise queued load dependencies,\\nsource fields differing from the live bus instruction, writes occurring\\nbetween enqueue and decode, queued work during divides, full-slot\\nrecovery with imem unready, wrong-path stores/divides, and stable saved\\nprediction metadata. Cover FENCE.I refetch after changed instruction\\ncontents and an older stalled store. Require exactly-once in-order RVFI,\\nmemory and marker handshakes, ready-bus bypass throughput, and demonstrable\\nissue from the slot while imemReady is zero. Preserve existing assertions.\\n\"}], \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 211.91, \"delta_pct\": -45.48, \"vs_baseline\": -25.07, \"fmax_mhz\": 85.53, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4036238, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6775, \"ff\": 3299, \"seeds\": [85.53, 86.41, 79.31], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"# One fall-through fetch slot\\n\\nAll correctness and FPGA gates passed. The measured hypothesis regressed fitness: scored 2 KB CoreMark fell from 388.659115376458 to 211.905244438014 iterations/s (-45.48%), despite 4.25% fewer bracketed cycles, because median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 85.53 MHz.\\n\\nImplemented the hypothesis in bench RTL only. The frontend now holds exactly one `if_id_t` tuple: PC, instruction, the prediction used to advance the fetch PC, and validity. An empty slot bypasses directly into ID. Fetch and issue have independent acceptance events, including capture during load-use/memory/divide waits, issue with an unready instruction bus, and atomic consume/refill. Register operands are still captured by the unchanged ID stage at issue.\\n\\n`available` is the unflushed OR of slot occupancy and instruction-bus readiness. The hazard unit uses this availability and the oldest selected instruction's raw source fields. EX recovery cancels both transfers, invalidates the slot, substitutes a decoding NOP, and redirects regardless of instruction-bus readiness or occupancy. Only validity is reset/flushed; the 96 payload bits have an ordinary capture/refill enable with no reset or recovery input.\\n\\nA legal FENCE.I uses the existing advancement-qualified EX recovery mechanism even when its saved prediction equals PC+4. This discards younger work after an older stalled memory operation completes and refetches PC+4. Its existing legal NOP result and single retirement are preserved. Decoder legality and ordinary FENCE are unchanged.\\n\\nThe sixteen full-tag predictor entries, counter policy, private row event capture, target commit timing, and advancement-only training remain unchanged. ID/EX control-versus-payload reset separation, EX/MEM, MEM/WB forwarding retention, real/ALTOPS arithmetic, the eight-edge divider, public ports, `nret: 1`, and channel-0 retirement wiring are unchanged.\\n\\n## Implementation choices and scope\\n\\n- Added two narrow unflushed source-address outputs to IF for hazard detection. Using the recovery-substituted ID instruction for these comparisons would indirectly put recovery into the slot's wide enable through `stall_if`; these outputs remove that path. ID still receives the prescribed NOP substitution.\\n- Kept the existing internal `stall`/`stall_if` names, now describing issue blocking. An empty fetch slot can fill while they are asserted.\\n- Inert payload can update on empty bypass or cancellation; validity alone determines whether it is retained work. Full occupied holds preserve the entire tuple.\\n- Gate sources and the incoming RTL were copied unchanged beneath the supplied TMPDIR. Generated artifacts stayed there. The delivered RTL and tests were checked byte-for-byte against the final gate inputs. Protected gate and benchmark sources were also checked unchanged. This notes file uses the explicitly requested current-directory location.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- Required Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings.\\n- Unchanged pytest/cocotb suite with extended permitted tests: PASS, 9 pytest cases / 148 cocotb tests, including 27 real and 27 ALTOPS ALU tests, 43 pipeline tests, six frontend tests, and the exhaustive hazard truth table.\\n- Formal: PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed, including liveness, on the delivered RTL. Both local verification runs passed. No formal counterexample or RTL fix after a formal failure occurred (zero formal fix attempts).\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench`: PASS, selftest ISS and scored CoreMark validation.\\n- Additional unchanged ISS comparison under both bus stalls: PASS, 115 retirements each for seeds `0xDEADBEEF`, `0x16C0FFEE`, and `0xF37C4`.\\n- Canonical 6000-byte, `-O3`, ten-iteration CoreMark: PASS for incoming and delivered RTL with both bus stalls. Accepted start/stop markers, `oob=false`, and `Correct operation validated.` were required. CRCs: seed `8a02`, list `d4b0`, matrix `be52`, state `5e47`, final `5949`.\\n- Scored 2000-byte, `-O3`, ten-iteration CoreMark: PASS for both implementations. CRCs: `e9f5/e714/1fd7/8e3a/fcaf`.\\n- Synthesized slot audit: 97 source bits; all 96 payload bits are plain DFFE registers sharing one enable. No reset, EX PC, or EX saved-prediction input reaches that enable's combinational leaf cone.\\n\\nThe extended scoreboards account for every accepted fetch as issued, discarded, or still occupying the single slot. They check saved tuple stability on every occupied edge, prediction policy before/after commits, unique EX resolutions, ordered exactly-once RVFI, and physical read/store handshakes. Directed cases cover differing queued/live source fields, a register write after enqueue, divides, reset, full-slot recovery with imem unready, wrong-path stores/divides, ready-bus throughput, and instruction-memory changes at an older store's acceptance before FENCE.I recovery.\\n\\nThe three deterministic random pipeline runs recorded 55 queued issues with imem unready, 27 captures during load-use, five during memory holds, 26 during divide waits, three recovery discards, and 223 consume/refills. Capture categories can overlap. These are unit-test coverage counts, not CoreMark instrumentation. Ready-bus straight-line code retained consecutive retirements starting on cycle 2.\\n\\nAn initial test assertion incorrectly required every load-use wait to begin empty. It was corrected to require capture exactly when the slot is empty and retention when full. An initial direct `make fpga` invocation selected the default dual-retirement wrapper; subsequent invocations explicitly selected the unchanged single-retirement wrapper with `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv` and `NRET=1`. A preliminary FPGA run was stopped after the final hazard-source separation; its timing is excluded.\\n\\n## Workload comparison\\n\\nBoth implementations ran each identical ELF with `--bench --istall --dstall --seed 3735928559` and a 50,000,000-cycle ceiling. Brackets below use accepted physical MMIO start/stop handshakes, excluding initialization and output. The final hazard-source separation preserved the complete benchmark output and these cycle counts.\\n\\n| Workload | Incoming start | Incoming stop | Incoming bracket | Candidate start | Candidate stop | Candidate bracket |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Scored 2 KB | 15,229 | 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 14,657 | 4,050,895 | 4,036,238 |\\n| Canonical 6 KB | 53,747 | 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 51,485 | 29,763,181 | 29,711,696 |\\n\\nBoth designs retired 2,937,037 instructions for the scored workload and 22,093,920 for the canonical workload. Cycle savings are 179,024 and 1,111,094 respectively. Fitness must use `10 / exact_bracketed_cycles * median_Fmax_Hz`, not rounded IPC or total elapsed cycles. The historical 388.66 score belongs to the 2 KB workload and is kept separate from canonical 6 KB validation.\\n\\nELF SHA256:\\n\\n- 2 KB: `e66a95e5465b8e8bbf101f05d12a1486e669d45f25423c2261be4c0c0061514e`\\n- 6 KB: `188f31354709614455809c9a0caf07305ce70db072fd87e20691d255fadad91b`\\n\\nBoth were built using the existing Makefile's size/optimization overrides. The unchanged UART compiler-flags string says `-O2`; the actual compiler commands use `-O3`. The 6 KB UART size is the per-algorithm allocation, while the compiler command sets `TOTAL_DATA_SIZE=6000` and the canonical CRCs confirm the workload.\\n\\n## FPGA measurement and concerns\\n\\nThe unchanged FPGA gate passed all three routed seeds for both designs. The incoming winner was rebuilt and measured live, reproducing the historical 388.66 result. The cycle savings do not compensate for the Fmax loss.\\n\\n| Metric | Incoming winner | Delivered candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Seed 1 Fmax, MHz | 163.83 | 85.53 |\\n| Seed 2 Fmax, MHz | 155.33 | 86.41 |\\n| Seed 3 Fmax, MHz | 168.55 | 79.31 |\\n| Median Fmax, Hz | 163,830,000 | 85,530,000 |\\n| LUT4 | 6,618 | 6,775 |\\n| FF | 3,198 | 3,299 |\\n| Scored 2 KB iterations/s | 388.659115376458 | 211.905244438014 |\\n| Separate canonical 6 KB iterations/s | 53.152229243362 | 28.786643482082 |\\n\\nMedian Fmax decreased 47.79%; LUT4 increased 2.37% and FF increased 3.16%. Canonical 6 KB cycles decreased 3.60%, but its separately calculated fitness decreased 45.84%. These calculations use the exact bracket lengths and median frequency in Hz.\\n\\nAll final critical paths originate in EX instruction opcode/funct3 bits used by FENCE.I detection and return through recovery/NOP substitution to ID. Seeds 1 and 2 terminate at ID/EX `rs1_val[14]` and `rs1_val[13]`; seed 3 terminates at ID/EX `ctrl.is_illegal`. This recovery-to-decode path is the measured timing concern; separating the slot payload enable did not remove the NOP-substitution data path required by the hypothesis.\\n\\n| Seed | Logic delay, ns | Routing delay, ns | Total, ns |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 5.40 | 6.29 | 11.69 |\\n| 2 | 5.40 | 6.17 | 11.57 |\\n| 3 | 6.40 | 6.21 | 12.61 |\\n\\nThe added tuple mux, changed placement/fanout, increased prediction age, and discarded prefetches remain broader concerns. The measurements do not isolate every contribution to the regression. Power was not measured. The four-percent improvement estimate is rejected by this measurement; the implementation is left intact for the orchestrator to evaluate.\\n\\nTools: pinned Verilator 5.047 devel `v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6`, Yosys 0.64+149 `2dc69a757-dirty`, nextpnr `0.10-45-g98c18d7f`, existing RISC-V GCC, and Conda Python dependencies. FPGA device, constraints, wrappers, and seeds 1/2/3 are unchanged.\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\nArtifacts: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/fetch-slot-r10s1/`.\\n\\nFinal evidence includes `unit-delivered.log`, `formal-final.log`, `cosim-final.log`, `stalled-iss-final.json`, `fpga-final.log`, `incoming-fpga.log`, `workload-results.json`, `measurement-summary.json`, `slot-netlist-audit.json`, `critical-paths.json`, `critical-path-seed*.txt`, the four `incoming-*` / `final-*` benchmark outputs, `canonical-build.log`, and all three routed reports beneath each design's `cores/bench/generated/`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T21:39:53.470657Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-05 hyp-20260905-002-r10s1 (regression, -45.48%): A fall-through fetch slot cut stalled 2 KB CoreMark cycles 4.25%, but EX `FENCE.I` detection through recovery/NOP substitution into ID/EX became critical in all three seeds as median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 85.53 MHz; isolating slot payload enables does not remove recovery from the decode data path.\"}\n33:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Move the complete multiply family into one shared MEM execution lane\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The incoming winner reaches 163.83 MHz, but its recorded routed limits\\ninclude ID/EX ALU control through result selection into EX/MEM in seeds\\n1 and 3; seed 2 is limited by recovery control. The structural r9s1\\nexperiment moved only MULH/MULHU/MULHSU to MEM and regressed 18.10%:\\nretaining MUL in EX split the original shared multiplier into two\\nMULT36X36 cells, while fast-ALU selection and forwarding/JALR paths\\nremained limiting. It also moved no multiplication executed inside the\\nmeasured CoreMark bracket. This suggests testing a complete multiply\\nstage repartition that preserves sharing and removes every multiply\\nresult from the fast EX selector. Unlike that earlier experiment, this\\nincurs a real dependency cost for timed MUL instructions, so a fitness\\ngain requires a measured clock improvement larger than the cycle cost.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The incoming winner reaches 163.83 MHz, but its recorded routed limits\\ninclude ID/EX ALU control through result selection into EX/MEM in seeds\\n1 and 3; seed 2 is limited by recovery control. The structural r9s1\\nexperiment moved only MULH/MULHU/MULHSU to MEM and regressed 18.10%:\\nretaining MUL in EX split the original shared multiplier into two\\nMULT36X36 cells, while fast-ALU selection and forwarding/JALR paths\\nremained limiting. It also moved no multiplication executed inside the\\nmeasured CoreMark bracket. This suggests testing a complete multiply\\nstage repartition that preserves sharing and removes every multiply\\nresult from the fast EX selector. Unlike that earlier experiment, this\\nincurs a real dependency cost for timed MUL instructions, so a fitness\\ngain requires a measured clock improvement larger than the cycle cost.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Execute MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU together in one combinational\\nmultiplier lane in MEM, using the existing EX/MEM registered,\\npost-forward rs1_val and rs2_val. EX forwards and captures multiply\\noperands; MEM performs multiplication and captures the final 32-bit\\nresult into MEM/WB. Ordinary integer operations remain in EX. This is\\none structural relocation of the entire multiplication resource, with\\nno added instruction stage, retirement channel, or predictor change.\\n\\nMove all four real multiply result arms and their shared product\\nexpressions out of the production EX ALU into rtl/mul_unit.sv. Preserve\\nthe existing signed/signed, unsigned/unsigned, and signed/unsigned\\narithmetic and low/high slice semantics, including the shared unsigned\\nproduct for MUL and MULHU. Keep every expression on the same registered\\noperand pair and in the same lane so synthesis can retain the incoming\\ndesign's sharing. Do not retain a separate EX MUL-low instance or add\\nanother multiplier algorithm. Inspect the mapped design: the intended\\nresource result is one shared MULT36X36, rather than r9s1's two. Treat\\nsharing as something to verify, not a guaranteed inference from RTL.\\n\\nAdd a legal is_mul control bit in ID, covering all four multiply\\noperations. Reset and bubbles clear it with all other controls, while\\nbackend holds retain it. EX still captures the exact instruction,\\ndestination, operands, resolved next PC, and trap metadata. Its unused\\nmultiply alu_result may be zero; this placeholder must never supply an\\narchitectural result or override a correct forwarding source. In MEM,\\nselect the lane result into MEM/WB.alu_result for is_mul and retain the\\nordinary memory and nonmultiply paths for other instructions. WB and\\nRVFI then consume the registered final result through their current mux.\\n\\nGeneralize the existing late-producer interlock from mem_read to\\nmem_read OR is_mul. An immediately dependent instruction holds at fetch\\nand receives one ID/EX bubble on ready buses, then obtains the product\\nfrom MEM/WB forwarding. Retain the current nonzero-rd and BOTH RAW\\nfetched source-field comparisons; do not fold r10s0's failed operand-use\\nfiltering into this experiment. Mask EX/MEM forwarding eligibility for\\nis_mul so the placeholder cannot be selected. Preserve MEM/WB forwarding\\nfrom retained payload even after its retirement-valid bit clears during\\na memory hold. Do not add a combinational MEM-multiplier-to-EX bypass.\\n\\nIndependent multiplies can traverse EX and MEM on consecutive clocks\\nand retire one per clock. A multiply occupying MEM is not a data-bus\\nrequest and must advance when dmemReady is low. Actual older memory\\nrequests retain the existing hold/drain priorities and exactly-once\\nside effects. Keep the divider's eight-edge handshake, accepted operand\\nsnapshots, and retained-result behavior unchanged. A divide dependent\\non a multiply uses the same late-producer interlock before launch.\\nPreserve EX recovery and predictor training timing, including recovery\\nof stale predictions on multiply instructions. Reset and wrong-path\\nbubbles must never leave an active multiply control on inert payload.\\n\\nRelocate the multiply ALTOPS formulas with the operations:\\nMUL=(a+b)^0x5876063e, MULH=(a+b)^0xf6583fb7,\\nMULHU=(a+b)^0x949ce5e8, MULHSU=(a-b)^0xecfbe137, using 32-bit modular\\narithmetic. Real and ALTOPS builds have identical stage availability\\nand dependency interlocks. Preserve every existing multiply test vector\\nand its exact expected value by exercising the new lane and integrated\\nretirement; changing the execution module must not reduce coverage.\\n\\nEstimate an unmeasured four-percent fitness gain with low confidence.\\nRemoving all multiply selection from EX and restoring a single physical\\nmultiply resource may improve placement and fast execution timing, but\\nMEM multiplication, forwarding/JALR, recovery, or predictor maintenance\\nmay become the limit. Immediate MUL dependencies can increase CoreMark\\ncycles enough to defeat any clock gain. Measure bracketed MUL counts,\\nimmediate raw dependencies, and actual added interlock cycles under\\nidentical ELFs and bus-ready seeds. Inspect all three routed paths and\\nconfirm that multiplier operands originate in EX/MEM, its result first\\nreaches MEM/WB, and there is no live product path back into EX.\\n\\nCompare exact iterations/bracketed_cycles times median_Fmax_Hz with the\\nincoming winner; success requires Fmax_new/Fmax_old > cycles_new/cycles_old.\\nHistory associates 388.66 with the scored 2 KB workload, so report that\\nlike-for-like result separately from the required canonical 6 KB run.\\nRun unchanged lint, cocotb, formal including liveness, stalled ISS cosim,\\nand three-seed FPGA gates. Validate the canonical 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration\\nworkload with both bus stalls, d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, the literal Correct\\noperation validated., and accepted start/stop MMIO brackets. Preserve\\nnret=1, all external ports, channel-0 retirement order and RVFI operands,\\ndecoder legality and traps, memory bounds, and every hard invariant.\\nImplementation is confined to cores/bench RTL and permitted unit tests.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the single MEM-stage multiply lane containing all four existing\\nreal multiply expressions/results and corresponding ALTOPS formulas.\\nKeep a common operand pair and shared unsigned low/high product.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove all production EX multiply datapaths and result arms. Retain\\nordinary integer arithmetic and the unchanged blocking divider.\\nMultiply operation codes produce only an inert placeholder here.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add is_mul to ctrl_t so multiply classification travels with each\\ninstruction through the existing stage bundles and bubble controls.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive is_mul from a valid, legal decoded MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and\\ncapture it with controls. Preserve the winning separation between\\ncontrol flushing and payload holds, and preserve raw source fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate mul_unit using registered EX/MEM operands and operation.\\nCapture its selected result in MEM/WB.alu_result for multiplies.\\nPreserve memory qualification, traps, and retained WB forwarding data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route mem_read OR is_mul into the existing late-result hazard input\\nand exclude is_mul from EX/MEM forwarding eligibility. Keep current\\nhazard priorities, MEM/WB forwarding eligibility, and public IO.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Document the generalized late-result producer input while retaining\\nits raw source comparisons and all existing stall/flush equations.\\nApply exactly the existing load-use bubble behavior to multiplies.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve all integer/divide tests and all existing real/ALTOPS\\nmultiply vectors and exact arithmetic assertions. Retarget multiply\\ncoverage to the new lane using permitted self-contained test runners;\\ndo not modify shared conftest or evaluation machinery.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Check legal multiply classification, bundle packing, and reset,\\nflush, bubble, and hold behavior for the new control bit.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve the exhaustive existing truth table and verify the same\\nlate-result behavior applies to load and multiply producer classes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add exact retirement and register-scoreboard checks for every MUL\\nvariant, immediate and one-gap consumers, consecutive independent\\nmultiplies, repeated rd and x0, branches/JALR, store base/data,\\nmultiply/divide chains, and raw unused-source aliases. Exercise\\nretained WB forwarding, independent bus stalls, reset, wrong-path\\nmultiplies, and stale-prediction recovery. Require one ready-bus\\nbubble per immediate multiply dependency, consecutive retirement of\\nindependent multiplies, correct real/ALTOPS operands and results,\\nexactly-once memory/marker accesses, and unchanged existing checks.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add the single MEM-stage multiply lane containing all four existing\\nreal multiply expressions/results and corresponding ALTOPS formulas.\\nKeep a common operand pair and shared unsigned low/high product.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove all production EX multiply datapaths and result arms. Retain\\nordinary integer arithmetic and the unchanged blocking divider.\\nMultiply operation codes produce only an inert placeholder here.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add is_mul to ctrl_t so multiply classification travels with each\\ninstruction through the existing stage bundles and bubble controls.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive is_mul from a valid, legal decoded MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU and\\ncapture it with controls. Preserve the winning separation between\\ncontrol flushing and payload holds, and preserve raw source fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate mul_unit using registered EX/MEM operands and operation.\\nCapture its selected result in MEM/WB.alu_result for multiplies.\\nPreserve memory qualification, traps, and retained WB forwarding data.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Route mem_read OR is_mul into the existing late-result hazard input\\nand exclude is_mul from EX/MEM forwarding eligibility. Keep current\\nhazard priorities, MEM/WB forwarding eligibility, and public IO.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Document the generalized late-result producer input while retaining\\nits raw source comparisons and all existing stall/flush equations.\\nApply exactly the existing load-use bubble behavior to multiplies.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve all integer/divide tests and all existing real/ALTOPS\\nmultiply vectors and exact arithmetic assertions. Retarget multiply\\ncoverage to the new lane using permitted self-contained test runners;\\ndo not modify shared conftest or evaluation machinery.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Check legal multiply classification, bundle packing, and reset,\\nflush, bubble, and hold behavior for the new control bit.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve the exhaustive existing truth table and verify the same\\nlate-result behavior applies to load and multiply producer classes.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add exact retirement and register-scoreboard checks for every MUL\\nvariant, immediate and one-gap consumers, consecutive independent\\nmultiplies, repeated rd and x0, branches/JALR, store base/data,\\nmultiply/divide chains, and raw unused-source aliases. Exercise\\nretained WB forwarding, independent bus stalls, reset, wrong-path\\nmultiplies, and stale-prediction recovery. Require one ready-bus\\nbubble per immediate multiply dependency, consecutive retirement of\\nindependent multiplies, correct real/ALTOPS operands and results,\\nexactly-once memory/marker accesses, and unchanged existing checks.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 349.63, \"delta_pct\": -10.04, \"vs_baseline\": 23.62, \"fmax_mhz\": 148.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4250523, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6628, \"ff\": 3209, \"seeds\": [142.07, 148.61, 166.42], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the complete MEM multiply lane. Correctness gates pass, but the measured hypothesis regresses fitness: the scored 2 KB result is 349.627563 iterations/s versus the incoming 388.659115 (-10.042618%). The proposed +4% gain was an unmeasured, low-confidence estimate and was not achieved.\\n\\nChanges made:\\n\\n- Added `cores/bench/rtl/mul_unit.sv` with all four original real multiply expressions and result slices. MUL/MULHU share the unsigned product; MULH and MULHSU retain the original signed extensions and high-half semantics. All four original ALTOPS formulas moved with them.\\n- Removed every product expression and multiply result arm from the production EX ALU. Multiply opcodes now select its zero default.\\n- Added `ctrl_t.is_mul`, classified in ID only for valid, legal decoded multiplies. The existing control reset/flush and payload hold separation remains intact.\\n- MEM consumes the EX/MEM registered post-forward operand pair and operation, and captures the selected multiply result in MEM/WB.alu_result. WB and channel-0 RVFI use the existing registered-result mux.\\n- Generalized the historical `id_ex_mem_read` hazard input to `mem_read | is_mul`, preserving its name, every hazard equation, both raw fetched source-field comparisons, and the nonzero-rd requirement. Masked only multiply EX/MEM forwarding eligibility. Retained MEM/WB forwarding remains independent of retirement-valid.\\n- Kept the divider, EX advancement/recovery, predictor, decoder, external ports, nret=1, memory handling, and retirement order unchanged. No live MEM-product bypass, extra stage, multiplier algorithm, or EX MUL-low instance was added.\\n\\nTests preserve all prior assertions and vectors. The ALU test uses a temporary wrapper to exercise the unchanged integer/divider ALU and the relocated multiply lane together; all 185 boundary/random pairs per M operation and explicit expected results remain covered in real and ALTOPS builds. Integrated retirement also exercises all 740 multiply boundary/random vectors, the nine original explicit multiply expectations, and seven MUL vectors used while the divider is busy. Added coverage includes all four variants, immediate and one-gap consumers, both raw unused-source aliases, consecutive independent multiplication, repeated rd, x0, branches/JALR, store base/data, loads, multiply/divide chains, retained WB forwarding after valid clears, older memory holds, separate and simultaneous bus stalls, exactly-once markers, reset, illegal encodings, wrong-path multiplies, and stale prediction recovery with and without instruction-bus backpressure.\\n\\nAdded opt-in `test_mul_profile.py` to measure existing CoreMark ELFs without modifying the cosim driver. It generates a temporary C++ subclass that observes settled internal signals and retirement while the unchanged driver supplies instructions, memory, and its original bus-ready sequence. Its observed marker cycles match the uninstrumented binaries for every comparison below. A qualified-name filter and explicit executed-test count prevent an accidentally empty ALTOPS pipeline run; an initial unqualified filter was corrected before the final suite.\\n\\nLocal validation:\\n\\n- Required `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS without warnings. The same lint with `-DRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS`: PASS without warnings.\\n- Final full pytest/cocotb suite: **10 passed, 1 skipped**. The skip is the opt-in CoreMark profiler when no ELFs are supplied; it was separately enabled and passed for both incoming and new RTL. The ALTOPS pipeline runner explicitly requires all eight new multiply tests to execute. Integer, divide, decode, predictor, and other existing tests still run in the normal suite.\\n- Local formal: **PASS on the first RTL attempt, 53 passed / 0 failed**, including `liveness_ch0`, instruction checks, reg, causal, unique, ill, and cover. No RTL formal fix attempts or counterexamples. Used unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh` with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6`, which autodetected nret=1 and selected the unchanged single-issue wrapper/configuration.\\n- Unchanged `tools.eval.cosim`: PASS, two ELFs (selftest and scored 2 KB CoreMark).\\n- Additional stalled ISS comparison through the unchanged `run_cosim.compare`: selftest passed at bus seeds 0xdeadbeef, 1, and 2 (115 retirements each). A generated arithmetic ELF covering all 185 pairs for each of the eight M operations plus immediate consumers passed at the same three bus seeds (3,701 retirements each).\\n- Unchanged synthesis and three-seed FPGA evaluator: PASS placement/routing on all seeds. LUT4=6,628; FF=3,209. The gate uses its existing scored 2 KB workload and CRC contract.\\n- Canonical requested 6 KB workload: separately built with the unchanged benchmark Makefile using `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, ITERATIONS=10. Both incoming and new binaries passed at all three bus seeds with both stalls, no OOB accesses, exactly one accepted start and stop, d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Seed CRC=8a02 and final CRC=5949. Full real-arithmetic SMT (`formal-deep`) was not run; real arithmetic was checked by lane tests, integrated retirement, and stalled ISS cosim.\\n\\nResource and stage inspection:\\n\\n- Mapped design contains exactly **one MULT36X36**, named `cpu.u_mem.u_mul.mul_uu_MULT36X36_DOUT`; no EX multiplier remains.\\n- A reverse traversal of the mapped multiplier input cones reaches only EX/MEM flops: rs1, rs2, and the registered multiply operation/control. Yosys merged the identical EX/MEM rs2_val and write_data flops; their mapped bit vectors were verified identical.\\n- A forward traversal from every DSP product output, stopping at the first sequential boundary, reaches exactly the 32 MEM/WB.alu_result D inputs and no other register input. There is no combinational product path back into EX.\\n- All three final routed critical paths are predictor maintenance paths, not multiply paths:\\n\\n| P&R seed | Fmax MHz | Routed path | Delay | Logic / routing |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 142.07 | predictor row 11 valid_q Q through row-hit/update selection to valid_q CE | 7.04 ns | 3.04 / 4.00 ns |\\n| 2 | 148.61 | predictor row 11 tag_q[15] Q through row-hit/update selection to counter_q CE | 6.73 ns | 2.60 / 4.13 ns |\\n| 3 | 166.42 | predictor row 11 tag_q[0] Q through row-hit/update selection to counter_q CE | 6.01 ns | 2.62 / 3.39 ns |\\n\\nThe median is **148.61 MHz**, versus the incoming recorded median **163.83 MHz**. Independent multiplies still retire on consecutive ready-bus clocks; an immediate raw dependency produces exactly one ready-bus bubble in both real and ALTOPS tests. A multiply in MEM advances even with dmemReady held low.\\n\\nMeasurements use the exact same ELF for incoming/new RTL and identical bus seeds. The incoming RTL snapshot is worktree HEAD `301f10e8a5d955c1788b2bb37fb5a091a343291e`. Incoming cycle measurements reproduce the scored winner's 4,215,262 cycles. The old 163.83 MHz median is the recorded winner value, not a new P&R rerun.\\n\\n| Working set | Bus seed | Incoming bracket cycles | New bracket cycles | Net added cycles | New multiply interlock bubbles |\\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2 KB | 0xdeadbeef | 4,215,262 | 4,250,523 | 35,261 | 27,927 |\\n| 2 KB | 1 | 4,214,768 | 4,250,288 | 35,520 | 27,897 |\\n| 2 KB | 2 | 4,214,802 | 4,250,870 | 36,068 | 27,771 |\\n| 6 KB | 0xdeadbeef | 30,822,790 | 31,278,680 | 455,890 | 358,641 |\\n| 6 KB | 1 | 30,824,564 | 31,280,915 | 456,351 | 358,262 |\\n| 6 KB | 2 | 30,827,534 | 31,283,974 | 456,440 | 358,703 |\\n\\nBoth RTL versions and every seed execute **93,960 bracketed MULs with 35,640 immediate raw dependencies in 2 KB**, and **1,269,000 bracketed MULs with 459,000 immediate raw dependencies in 6 KB**. All three high multiply variants have zero bracketed executions. Incoming multiply interlock bubbles are zero. Actual bubble counts differ from net cycle increases because fetch stalls can supply the dependency gap and extra cycles shift the deterministic bus-ready sequence. No operand-use filtering was introduced.\\n\\nUsing exact `10 / bracketed_cycles * median_Fmax_Hz`, for default bus seed 0xdeadbeef:\\n\\n| Workload | Incoming iterations/s | New iterations/s | Fitness delta | cycles_new / cycles_old |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Scored 2 KB, -O3, ten iterations | 388.659115 | 349.627563 | -10.042618% | 1.008365079 |\\n| Required 6 KB, -O3, ten iterations | 53.152229 | 47.511596 | -10.612224% | 1.014790679 |\\n\\n`Fmax_new/Fmax_old = 0.907098822`, which fails the success inequality for both workloads. At unchanged incoming Fmax, break-even would require the new core to exceed 165.200451 MHz for 2 KB or 166.253157 MHz for 6 KB. Seed 3 alone does not determine acceptance; the three-seed median regresses.\\n\\nDefault accepted marker cycles (new): 2 KB start=15,229 / stop=4,265,752; 6 KB start=53,747 / stop=31,332,427. Incoming markers: 2 KB 15,229 / 4,230,491; 6 KB 53,747 / 30,876,537. Only these brackets were used for fitness.\\n\\nELF SHA256 values:\\n\\n- 2 KB: `c1d5108f203990204c9d1bf6ddb0664662d1e82ea29b76685395d64947f30c76`\\n- 6 KB: `731b5d954ca847110437a2a8fde37bee6290eb63735f58405efd34b24bc3aabf`\\n\\nThe protected CoreMark port reports a hardcoded `-O2` UART label; the actual captured compiler commands use `-O3` for both ELFs. That source and the evaluator's 2 KB CRC table were left unchanged. The separate 6 KB validation enforces its own required CRCs without changing the scored gate.\\n\\nAll generated gate copies, logs, ELFs, netlists, routes, and profiles are under `$TMPDIR/mem-mul-r11s1-a_3c0e7f/`; no contract-side workspace sources were edited. Useful artifacts there: `formal-gate.stdout`, `fpga-gate.json`, `cosim-gate.json`, `stalled-selftest.json`, `stalled-m-family.json`, `mapped-multiply-report.json`, `measurement-summary.json`, `coremark-comparison.json`, `profile-new.json`, `profile-incoming.json`, `elf-build.stdout`, `elf6000-build.stdout`, and `cores/bench/generated/pnr_seed{1,2,3}/nextpnr.log`. Cocotb summaries are `$TMPDIR/mem-mul-tests-final.stdout` and the two `mem-mul-profile-*.stdout` files.\\n\\nConcern: the intended resource sharing and stage repartition are achieved, but placement exposes slower predictor maintenance paths and the real MUL dependency cost further reduces fitness. The RTL is left as the requested structural experiment; no unrelated timing optimization or gate relaxation was applied.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T22:26:58.280251Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260905-002-r11s1 (regression, -10.04%): Moving the complete multiply family to MEM retained one `MULT36X36` but added timed `MUL` dependency stalls, raising 2 KB/6 KB CoreMark cycles 0.84%/1.48%, while predictor maintenance limited all three routed seeds and median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 148.61 MHz; preserving multiplier sharing does not ensure a clock gain sufficient to repay late-result dependencies.\"}\n34:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Prevalidate sequential and direct-target predictions in decode\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Recent predictor changes have repeatedly lost frequency: untagged direction\\ntraining, raw-event capture, and EX/MEM-sourced training regressed by 33.64%,\\n23.41%, and 12.29%. Accuracy additions have offered little cycle headroom;\\nthe return stack saved only 0.17% of the historically scored cycles. The\\nnext predictor experiment should shorten prediction validation itself.\\n\\nThe current RTL matches the saved incoming winner in the r8s2 evidence\\nbyte-for-byte. Its seed-2 critical path starts at ID/EX predicted_next_pc[3]\\nand ends at ID/EX valid RESET, taking 6.44 ns at 155.33 MHz. EX currently\\nselects a full actual_next_pc before comparing it with the prediction.\\nSequential and direct-target equality need no forwarded register operands,\\nso both comparisons can finish before EX. The earlier r7s1 address-retiming\\nexperiment retained the late equality and changed ALU/AUIPC routing; this\\nproposal registers only prediction-match bits and leaves those datapaths\\nintact. Seeds 1 and 3 were ALU-result limited, making confidence low.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Recent predictor changes have repeatedly lost frequency: untagged direction\\ntraining, raw-event capture, and EX/MEM-sourced training regressed by 33.64%,\\n23.41%, and 12.29%. Accuracy additions have offered little cycle headroom;\\nthe return stack saved only 0.17% of the historically scored cycles. The\\nnext predictor experiment should shorten prediction validation itself.\\n\\nThe current RTL matches the saved incoming winner in the r8s2 evidence\\nbyte-for-byte. Its seed-2 critical path starts at ID/EX predicted_next_pc[3]\\nand ends at ID/EX valid RESET, taking 6.44 ns at 155.33 MHz. EX currently\\nselects a full actual_next_pc before comparing it with the prediction.\\nSequential and direct-target equality need no forwarded register operands,\\nso both comparisons can finish before EX. The earlier r7s1 address-retiming\\nexperiment retained the late equality and changed ALU/AUIPC routing; this\\nproposal registers only prediction-match bits and leaves those datapaths\\nintact. Seeds 1 and 3 were ALU-result limited, making confidence low.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Prevalidate the two operand-independent possible successors in ID and\\ncarry their exact equality results with the instruction. Add two one-bit\\nid_ex_t payload fields, prediction_matches_seq and prediction_matches_direct.\\nCompute them in id_stage from the same IF bundle and decoded immediate\\nthat the existing ID/EX payload captures:\\n  prediction_matches_seq = (in.predicted_next_pc == seq_pc);\\n  prediction_matches_direct = (in.predicted_next_pc == direct_pc);\\nHere seq_pc is the 32-bit result of in.pc + 32'd4, and direct_pc is the\\n32-bit result of in.pc + imm. Explicitly truncate both sums modulo 2^32\\nbefore equality. Compare all 32 bits, including target alignment bits.\\nThese are exact destination comparisons, not predicted-direction hints\\nor truncated target signatures. Retain the full predicted_next_pc field.\\n\\nRegister both bits at the existing ID/EX boundary with exactly the winning\\npayload discipline: deterministic zero on reset, retain on the existing\\nbackend stall, otherwise capture even if a flush makes the payload inert.\\nFlush still clears every ctrl bit and valid independently. Give each new\\nfield one sequential owner. Do not add the recovery or load-use signals\\nto these payload fields' reset or enable cones. Never recompute a held\\ninstruction's match bits from live fetch, predictor state, or operands.\\n\\nReplace only EX's final prediction-correctness calculation. Start with\\nprediction_correct = in.prediction_matches_seq. If ctrl_with_trap is legal,\\na jump selects in.prediction_matches_direct for JAL, or the full equality\\nof in.predicted_next_pc and the existing forwarded, bit-zero-cleared\\njump_target for JALR. Otherwise a taken conditional branch selects\\nin.prediction_matches_direct; an untaken branch or non-control retains\\nin.prediction_matches_seq. A decoder or target-alignment trap always uses\\nthe sequential match, following the existing PC+4 trap recovery policy.\\nKeep redirect = resolve_valid && !prediction_correct. Compute the JALR\\nequality against jump_target directly, in parallel with the final Boolean\\nselection, rather than comparing against the selected actual_next_pc.\\n\\nLeave actual_next_pc generation unchanged for redirect_target and EX/MEM\\npc_next, and retain the existing branch comparator, JALR forwarding,\\nalignment checks, link writes, and EX predictor feedback. Retain the\\noriginal resolve_valid qualification by in.valid, !stall, !div_wait, and\\n!reset. This must be Boolean-equivalent to the original full destination\\nmismatch for every advancing instruction. In particular, a taken transfer\\nwhose destination equals PC+4 must not spuriously redirect; a stale target\\non an ordinary instruction or completed divide must still recover; and a\\nchanging JALR target must recover even when both predictions are taken.\\nOnly operand-dependent JALR destination comparison remains in EX.\\n\\nThis is a predictor-validation retiming experiment, with no additional\\npipeline stage, early ID redirect, or change in prediction policy. Keep\\nthe sixteen full-tag target rows, binary counters, private pending events,\\ntraining source and latency, atomic target/tag commit, and reset handling\\nexactly as they are. Preserve accepted-prediction tracking, instruction\\nbus recovery priority, load-use bubbles, memory holds, and divide waits.\\nNo changes to arithmetic selection, AUIPC, forwarding, or the hazard unit\\nare part of this hypothesis. The two ID comparison sums are local wires;\\ndo not add registered PC+4/PC+imm buses or route them into the ALU.\\n\\nThe unmeasured estimate is a 3% fitness improvement through higher median\\nFmax, with identical cycles for identical ELF and ready schedules. Risks\\ninclude the existing BTB lookup plus a comparison becoming an ID critical\\npath, instruction/immediate decode plus addition and equality limiting ID,\\nduplicated adders increasing routing cost, and unchanged ALU, JALR, or\\npredictor-training paths remaining limiting. Inspect synthesized and all\\nthree routed netlists to confirm that the new match bits survive as flops\\nand sequential/direct recovery selects registered one-bit results rather\\nthan retaining the original selected-next-PC equality. Report LUT/FF cost\\nand all three critical paths; source-level shortening is not a result.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA\\ngates. Preserve the pipeline oracle's independent assertion that redirect\\nequals actual_next_pc != the prediction recorded at fetch acceptance;\\ndo not replace its expected value with the new RTL match bits. Compare\\nincoming and candidate prediction decisions, recovery cycles, accepted\\nmemory transactions, and RVFI retirements under identical ready schedules.\\nRequire identical bracketed CoreMark cycles, not merely matching CRCs.\\nCompute fitness using exact iterations/bracketed_cycles times median Fmax.\\nValidate the required canonical 6 KB, ten-iteration, -O3 run with both\\nbus stalls, bounded accesses, accepted start/stop markers, CRCs\\nd4b0/be52/5e47, and the literal Correct operation validated. History assigns\\n388.66 to a scored 2 KB image; report that historical comparison separately\\nfrom like-for-like canonical 6 KB throughput without changing the evaluator.\\nPreserve nret=1, external ports, RVFI ordering and trap checks, decoder\\nlegality, real/ALTOPS arithmetic, and every protected contract file.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add prediction_matches_seq and prediction_matches_direct as one-bit\\nid_ex_t payload fields beside predicted_next_pc. Retain the complete\\nprediction, PC, immediate, and all architectural/RVFI bundle fields.\\nIF/ID, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, and the external interface need no new fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute full-width prediction equality against local 32-bit PC+4 and\\nPC+imm sums and register the two results with the existing payload.\\nUse decoded imm from the same instruction. Reset to zero, retain only\\non the existing stall, and capture on other edges independently of\\nflush. Preserve control/valid flush priority and all existing operand\\nand instruction capture behavior; introduce no early redirect.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the held sequential/direct equality bits according to the\\nexisting legal resolved control-flow outcome. For legal JALR retain\\nexact equality against its post-forward target after bit-zero clearing.\\nQualify the inverted match by unchanged resolve_valid. Keep actual\\nnext-PC generation, target feedback, trap handling, link/ALU results,\\ndivide handshake, and EX/MEM capture unchanged. Remove the original\\nactual_next_pc-versus-predicted_next_pc comparator from redirect logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend payload priority checks for both match bits, including reset,\\nhold, flush, and simultaneous flush plus hold with changing inputs.\\nAdd matching and mismatching predictions, negative B/J immediates,\\n32-bit wraparound, and differences in each target bit. Derive expected\\nB/J immediates and successor values independently from instruction\\nencodings; retain existing control and payload assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update id_ex_t unpacking and payload expectations for the two fields.\\nKeep the predictor-training oracle and exact accepted-destination\\nrecovery assertion unchanged. Add direct branch/JAL targets equal to\\nPC+4, taken and untaken misaligned branches, stale non-controls/traps,\\nand forwarded changing JALR targets. Exercise held match metadata\\nwhile fetch/table contents change during dmem stalls and divide waits,\\nrecovery during imem stalls, and reset/flush boundaries. Assert exact\\nrecovery cycle positions, architectural PC chaining, link and operand\\ndata, strict RVFI order, and absence of wrong-path stores/retirements.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add prediction_matches_seq and prediction_matches_direct as one-bit\\nid_ex_t payload fields beside predicted_next_pc. Retain the complete\\nprediction, PC, immediate, and all architectural/RVFI bundle fields.\\nIF/ID, EX/MEM, MEM/WB, and the external interface need no new fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute full-width prediction equality against local 32-bit PC+4 and\\nPC+imm sums and register the two results with the existing payload.\\nUse decoded imm from the same instruction. Reset to zero, retain only\\non the existing stall, and capture on other edges independently of\\nflush. Preserve control/valid flush priority and all existing operand\\nand instruction capture behavior; introduce no early redirect.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the held sequential/direct equality bits according to the\\nexisting legal resolved control-flow outcome. For legal JALR retain\\nexact equality against its post-forward target after bit-zero clearing.\\nQualify the inverted match by unchanged resolve_valid. Keep actual\\nnext-PC generation, target feedback, trap handling, link/ALU results,\\ndivide handshake, and EX/MEM capture unchanged. Remove the original\\nactual_next_pc-versus-predicted_next_pc comparator from redirect logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_id_stage.py\", \"description\": \"Extend payload priority checks for both match bits, including reset,\\nhold, flush, and simultaneous flush plus hold with changing inputs.\\nAdd matching and mismatching predictions, negative B/J immediates,\\n32-bit wraparound, and differences in each target bit. Derive expected\\nB/J immediates and successor values independently from instruction\\nencodings; retain existing control and payload assertions.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Update id_ex_t unpacking and payload expectations for the two fields.\\nKeep the predictor-training oracle and exact accepted-destination\\nrecovery assertion unchanged. Add direct branch/JAL targets equal to\\nPC+4, taken and untaken misaligned branches, stale non-controls/traps,\\nand forwarded changing JALR targets. Exercise held match metadata\\nwhile fetch/table contents change during dmem stalls and divide waits,\\nrecovery during imem stalls, and reset/flush boundaries. Assert exact\\nrecovery cycle positions, architectural PC chaining, link and operand\\ndata, strict RVFI order, and absence of wrong-path stores/retirements.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 134.3, \"delta_pct\": -65.45, \"vs_baseline\": -52.51, \"fmax_mhz\": 56.61, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6755, \"ff\": 3200, \"seeds\": [56.44, 56.61, 57.12], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the decode prediction-validation retiming exactly as proposed. Correctness gates pass, but the measured hypothesis is a performance regression: median routed Fmax falls from 163.83 MHz to 56.61 MHz (65.445889% lower) with identical benchmark cycles.\\n\\nChanges are limited to three RTL files and three test files, plus these requested notes. `id_ex_t` retains the full prediction and adds two one-bit payload fields. ID computes local 32-bit PC+4 and PC+imm sums and compares all 32 prediction bits. Each match bit has one sequential owner: reset to zero, hold only on the existing backend stall, otherwise capture independently of flush. The existing control/valid flush priority is unchanged. EX selects the registered sequential/direct match or the independent equality against the existing forwarded, bit-zero-cleared JALR jump_target. Trap recovery selects the sequential match. resolve_valid, actual_next_pc, redirect_target, EX/MEM pc_next, arithmetic, forwarding, hazards, predictor policy/state/training, and nret=1 are unchanged. All eleven other RTL files are byte-identical to the incoming snapshot. No registered address buses or pipeline stages were added.\\n\\nTests now decode immediates independently from instruction encodings, unpack both match fields, and check payload capture on every advancing pipeline edge. ID tests cover every reset/flush/hold combination with changing inputs, positive/negative B/J offsets, modulo-2^32 wraparound, exact matches, and a mismatch in each of the 32 target bits. Pipeline coverage includes PC+4 transfers, taken/untaken misaligned branches, stale ordinary/trapping/divide predictions, EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarded changing JALR targets, link/operand values, memory/divide holds, predictor commits during holds, instruction-stalled recovery, reset, strict RVFI order/PC chaining, and wrong-path store/retirement exclusion. The original assertion `redirect == (actual != accepted_prediction)` and predictor-training oracle remain independent of the new match bits.\\n\\nAdded `test_prediction_equivalence.py` to capture/compare normalized incoming and candidate observations under identical cycle-driven ready schedules. The actual comparison passed for 11 scenarios, 365 cycles and 173 retirements, including every fetch prediction/acceptance, resolution/recovery cycle, accepted memory transaction, downstream bundle and complete RVFI record. Only the differently sized internal ID bundle and its derived input-expectation dictionary are excluded. Set PREDICTION_CAPTURE_TRACE to capture an incoming trace and PREDICTION_REFERENCE_TRACE to compare a candidate trace. Normal unit-test execution still exercises the independent pipeline assertions without these optional files.\\n\\nLocal validation completed using isolated scratch copies under `.tmp/prediction-validation/{incoming,candidate}`. Protected scripts, wrappers, benchmark sources, constraints and evaluator were copied unchanged; their generated outputs stay in scratch. No protected worktree files or other cores were changed.\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint command: PASS, no warnings or errors.\\n- Unchanged `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS, all 10 pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS arithmetic, both ID tests and all 39 pipeline tests.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6 bash formal/run_all.sh`: PASS on the first RTL attempt, 53 passed / 0 failed; automatically selected wrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg. No formal counterexample or RTL fix retries. formal-deep was not needed because arithmetic is unchanged.\\n- Unchanged `python -m tools.eval.cosim . bench`: PASS, selftest and stalled 2 KB CoreMark. Additional selftest runs with both stalls and ready seeds 1, 2 and 3 each matched the unchanged Python ISS for all 115 retirements; incoming/candidate full RVFI records, including cycle numbers, were identical.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: PASS for incoming and candidate; all three seeds routed successfully. Explicit NRET/BENCH select the existing single-retirement contract. An initial incoming build omitted NRET and failed to compile channel-1 references; it was rerun with NRET=1, with no source changes.\\n- Two initial new test expectations were corrected: an untaken branch retains the warm target for two counter decrements, and younger instructions can resolve before the harness observes EBREAK retirement. Existing assertions were retained; final complete cocotb gate passes.\\n\\nRouted resource cost is 6,618 -> 6,755 LUT4 (+137) and 3,198 -> 3,200 DFF (+2), identical across all three seeds. Before packing, synthesis reports 6,008 -> 6,111 LUT1-4 cells (+103), 3,150 -> 3,152 FF (+2), and 514 -> 546 ALU carry cells (+32). These are wrapper-inclusive measurements from the unchanged FPGA gate.\\n\\n| Revision | Seed | Fmax MHz | Critical source | Critical endpoint | Delay ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- | ---: | --- |\\n| Incoming | 1 | 163.83 | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[4], cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_193.Q | EX/MEM alu_result[25], cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_70.D | 6.10 | 2.87 / 3.24 |\\n| Incoming | 2 | 155.33 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[3], cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_92.Q | ID/EX valid reset, cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_192.RESET | 6.44 | 2.77 / 3.67 |\\n| Incoming | 3 | 168.55 | ID/EX ctrl.alu_op[2], cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_195.Q | EX/MEM alu_result[25], cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_70.D | 5.93 | 2.78 / 3.15 |\\n| Candidate | 1 | 56.44 | EX/MEM rd[4]/instr[11], cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_96.Q | ID/EX prediction_matches_direct, cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_97.D | 17.72 | 9.78 / 7.94 |\\n| Candidate | 2 | 56.61 | EX/MEM rd[0]/instr[7], cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_100.Q | ID/EX prediction_matches_direct, cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_97.D | 17.67 | 9.82 / 7.84 |\\n| Candidate | 3 | 57.12 | EX/MEM rd[0]/instr[7], cpu.u_ex.reg_q_DFFR_Q_100.Q | ID/EX prediction_matches_direct, cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_97.D | 17.51 | 10.17 / 7.34 |\\n\\nThe optimized pre-mapping netlists confirm that the incoming full predicted-PC comparison consumes actual_next_pc, while the candidate's only EX prediction comparator is a 32-bit equality against jump_target. Actual-next-PC selection still feeds recovery and retirement data. The mapped synthesis netlist AND all three routed netlists retain separate DFFRE cells for sequential/direct matches (`cpu.u_id.reg_q_DFFRE_Q_96` and `_97`). Their clock, reset and enable connections equal those of the retained prediction payload; the valid flop has the separate flush reset. Sequential/direct Q values reach that recovery reset through two/three LUT cells respectively, rather than a selected-next-PC bus equality. Placement of the two match flops is:\\n\\n| Seed | Sequential match | Direct match |\\n| ---: | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | X25Y20/DFF3 | X25Y20/DFF2 |\\n| 2 | X22Y5/DFF4 | X24Y10/DFF0 |\\n| 3 | X14Y3/DFF5 | X17Y26/DFF4 |\\n\\nThe new critical paths reveal the concern that dominates this implementation: EX/MEM destination-register forwarding selection propagates through JALR target/recovery logic, the existing IF instruction-to-NOP flush, immediate decoding, the local ID direct sum, and the direct-match flop's DATA input. Keeping recovery out of the new flop reset/enable cones does not remove this data path. It is sensitized structurally even though a flush makes the captured payload inert. The experiment therefore successfully retimes EX validation but extends recovery-to-decode payload capture enough to lose frequency. No attempt was made to change the specified IF-bundle capture discipline or other datapaths to hide this result.\\n\\nCoreMark measurements use the same ELF and both bus-stall flags for each revision, with accepted MMIO start/stop writes and bounded accesses. Both complete without OOB or max-cycle failure. The complete bench-mode output, including the final RVFI retirement and marker cycle positions, is byte-identical between revisions for each image. Fitness below uses exact `10 / bracketed_cycles * median_Fmax_MHz * 1,000,000`; it does not reuse the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark: 2e-06` display.\\n\\n| Image | Start | Stop | Bracketed cycles, both revisions | Incoming iter/s | Candidate iter/s |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Historical/evaluator 2 KB, 10 iterations, -O3 | 15,229 | 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 388.6591153764582 | 134.29770201709883 |\\n| Required canonical 6 KB, 10 iterations, -O3 | 53,747 | 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 53.15222924336181 | 18.366280275082172 |\\n\\nThe unchanged evaluator actually defaults to and validates the 2 KB image. Its incoming result reproduces the historical 388.66 score; its candidate score is 134.30. This historical comparison is separate from the like-for-like canonical 6 KB throughput. The evaluator was not altered to score the 6 KB image.\\n\\nThe canonical image was compiled from the unchanged Makefile's compiler command with COREMARK_SIZE=6000 and COREMARK_OPT=-O3, writing a separate coremark-6k.elf. ITERATIONS remains 10 from the protected port header. UART reports the 6k performance parameters, seedcrc 8a02, crclist d4b0, crcmatrix be52, crcstate 5e47, crcfinal 5949, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. CoreMark's reported Size=2000 is the per-algorithm share of the 6000-byte total. The port's printed compiler-flags string is hardcoded to -O2; the actual unchanged build command used -O3. Neither reporting string was edited.\\n\\nELF SHA-256: 2 KB `c6f3c1aff9a277dfd36f1936f6c6700ffc2b8c180953706f3c7a287525a374e6`; 6 KB `6a10a144ba1903e664ddd6304f80d8dce4048c261b3745344f5c17fae93ed027`.\\n\\nEvidence is in `.tmp/prediction-validation/results.json`; each revision's `fpga-gate.log`, `coremark-{2,6}k.jsonl`, `prediction-trace.json`, `elaborated.json`, `critical-path-seed{1,2,3}.txt`, and `cores/bench/generated/{synth.json,pnr_seed{1,2,3}/pnr.json}`. Candidate-only evidence includes `formal-gate.log`, `cocotb-final.log`, `cocotb-equivalence.log`, `cosim-gate.log`, `stalled-cosim.json`, and `mapped-match-evidence.json`. The tested candidate snapshot was checked byte-for-byte against final RTL and tests. The implementation is left in place for orchestration; the estimated 3% fitness improvement is disproven by the routed measurements.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T22:21:32.009346Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 11, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260905-003-r11s2 (regression, -65.45%): Prevalidating `PC+4`/`PC+imm` predictions in ID extended EX/MEM forwarding through JALR recovery, IF NOP substitution, immediate decode and a 32-bit add/equality into `prediction_matches_direct.D`, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 56.61 MHz with identical CoreMark cycles; payloads made inert by flush still carry timed recovery dependencies through their data inputs.\"}\n36:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Rebuild the integer register file as replicated distributed RAM\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current register file resets every data word synchronously, tying its\\n31 writable architectural registers to resettable storage and wide read\\nselection. Reorganizing that storage can remove about 992 data flip-flops\\nand replace their reset behavior with 31 useful validity bits, reducing\\nplacement and routing pressure without adding instruction latency.\\n\\nThe structural history includes unsuccessful forwarding retiming\\n(hyp-20260905-002-r8s1), a fall-through fetch slot (r10s1), and relocation\\nof multiplication to MEM (r11s1). The latter raised 6 KB CoreMark cycles\\n1.48% and reduced median Fmax to 148.61 MHz. The register-file experiment\\nhyp-20260905-001-r1s0 changed read selection while retaining the original\\nstorage and achieved only 0.54% fitness improvement. No logged hypothesis\\nhas replaced the register-file storage with replicated distributed RAM.\\n\\nThis is a structural storage and reset reorganization. The installed\\nGowin lutrams.txt describes synchronous-write, asynchronous-read 16x4\\nmemories, and lutrams_map.v maps them to RAM16SDP primitives. The protected\\nsynthesis script enables their inference. The incoming winner's measured\\ncritical paths include ALU result selection and recovery, so a timing\\nimprovement from lower routing pressure is a hypothesis, not evidence\\nthat the register-file read port is currently the bottleneck.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current register file resets every data word synchronously, tying its\\n31 writable architectural registers to resettable storage and wide read\\nselection. Reorganizing that storage can remove about 992 data flip-flops\\nand replace their reset behavior with 31 useful validity bits, reducing\\nplacement and routing pressure without adding instruction latency.\\n\\nThe structural history includes unsuccessful forwarding retiming\\n(hyp-20260905-002-r8s1), a fall-through fetch slot (r10s1), and relocation\\nof multiplication to MEM (r11s1). The latter raised 6 KB CoreMark cycles\\n1.48% and reduced median Fmax to 148.61 MHz. The register-file experiment\\nhyp-20260905-001-r1s0 changed read selection while retaining the original\\nstorage and achieved only 0.54% fitness improvement. No logged hypothesis\\nhas replaced the register-file storage with replicated distributed RAM.\\n\\nThis is a structural storage and reset reorganization. The installed\\nGowin lutrams.txt describes synchronous-write, asynchronous-read 16x4\\nmemories, and lutrams_map.v maps them to RAM16SDP primitives. The protected\\nsynthesis script enables their inference. The incoming winner's measured\\ncritical paths include ALU result selection and recovery, so a timing\\nimprovement from lower routing pressure is a hypothesis, not evidence\\nthat the register-file read port is currently the bottleneck.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement the 32x32, two-read/one-write integer register file using two\\nidentical data copies, one per asynchronous read port. Organize each copy\\nas two 16x32 banks selected by address bit 4, with bits 3:0 addressing a\\nbank. Broadcast each accepted nonzero-register write to the corresponding\\nbank in both copies. Use synthesizable behavioral memory arrays with\\ndistributed-RAM inference attributes; their clocked processes contain\\nwrites only, with no data-array reset loop, scrub sequence, or initial\\ncontents requirement. This organization is intended to map to roughly\\n32 RAM16SDP4 cells before optimization. Do not instantiate vendor-only\\nsimulation black boxes or introduce different formal/simulation hardware.\\n\\nAdd a shared written-since-reset bitmap. A synchronous reset clears the\\nbitmap and suppresses physical writes on that edge. A non-reset write to\\nx1..x31 writes both copies and sets its bitmap bit on the same edge. Leave\\nx0 permanently invalid and unwritable. Reads select zero for x0, then the\\nexisting live write-first bypass on a matching nonzero address, then the\\nselected bank's data if its bitmap bit is set, otherwise zero. In\\nparticular, bypass takes priority over an unset bitmap bit. Preserve the\\nexisting combinational bypass even while reset is asserted: reset wins\\nat the storage edge, but does not itself gate the live read mux. When\\nbypass is removed after a reset edge, every unwritten register reads zero.\\nUninitialized or pre-reset RAM contents must never affect a visible read.\\n\\nKeep reg_file's ports, synchronous write/reset semantics, two independent\\nzero-cycle reads, x0 behavior, and same-cycle WB visibility exact. Keep\\ninstruction stages, hazards, forwarding, arithmetic, predictor, bus\\nacceptance, retirement, and nret=1 unchanged. This hypothesis adds no\\ninstruction bubbles or resource scheduling changes. For identical\\ninstruction images and bus-ready schedules, require identical RVFI\\nrecords and retirement cycles, accepted memory transactions, and timed\\nCoreMark cycles before assessing frequency.\\n\\nInspect the synthesized register-file cone to confirm that RAM16SDP\\nstorage was actually inferred, both read ports retain independent\\naddresses, writes reach both copies, and reset reaches only the bitmap\\nand write qualification. Report RAM, LUT4, and FF counts, including a\\npossible extra bank-select/validity mux. Fewer source-level resets alone\\ndo not demonstrate this storage transformation. Run the unchanged lint,\\ncocotb, formal, stalled cosim, and three-seed FPGA gates. Compare all\\nrouted critical paths against the incoming winner, since RAM placement,\\nreplicated write fanout, validity selection, or unchanged EX/predictor\\ntiming may erase the area benefit or reduce Fmax.\\n\\nThe unmeasured estimate is a 4% fitness gain through median Fmax at equal\\ncycles, with low confidence. Compute throughput from exact iterations\\ndivided by marker-bracketed cycles times median Fmax in Hz, not the\\nrounded ipc_coremark field. Validate the canonical 6 KB, ten-iteration,\\n-O3 run with both bus stalls, bounded accesses, accepted start/stop\\nmarkers, CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, and Correct operation validated. The history\\nassociates 388.66 with a 2 KB evaluator image; keep that historical\\ncomparison separate from like-for-like 6 KB throughput. Preserve every\\nexisting correctness assertion, RVFI/trap rule, and protected eval file.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the resettable data array with two replicated asynchronous-read\\ncopies, each split into two 16x32 distributed-RAM banks. Broadcast\\nnonzero writes to both copies. Implement architectural reset using a\\nshared written-since-reset bitmap, preserving exact read priority:\\nx0, live matching write bypass, valid stored data, zero. Suppress\\nstorage writes during reset without resetting RAM contents. Retain\\nthe existing module interface and all cycle-level behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve existing assertions and add an independent 32-register\\narchitectural scoreboard. Fill every writable register with distinct\\npatterns and check all read-address pairs, including cross-bank pairs\\nand simultaneous same-address reads. Exercise consecutive overwrites,\\nalternating banks, writes observed by either or both read ports, and\\nbypass to a register whose validity bit is still clear. Reset after\\ndirty contents and verify all registers read zero without a scrub;\\ncover reset plus w_en, bypass before/after the reset edge followed by\\nbypass removal, attempted x0 writes, and repeated resets. Sample\\ncombinational reads before edges and stored results after edges with\\nsimulator-safe timing. Check observable data, not internal RAM init.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend integrated register-file coverage with dependent operations\\nspanning x15/x16 and both read ports, same-cycle WB-to-ID visibility,\\nand writes while younger instructions are held by dmem/divide waits.\\nReset after nonzero architectural state, restart from reset PC, and\\nprove that stale RAM data cannot enter EX operands or RVFI. Retain\\nthe existing ISS/prediction/retirement oracles and all prior tests;\\nassert exact operand values, single ordered retirement, and unchanged\\nbus-hold and recovery behavior.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the resettable data array with two replicated asynchronous-read\\ncopies, each split into two 16x32 distributed-RAM banks. Broadcast\\nnonzero writes to both copies. Implement architectural reset using a\\nshared written-since-reset bitmap, preserving exact read priority:\\nx0, live matching write bypass, valid stored data, zero. Suppress\\nstorage writes during reset without resetting RAM contents. Retain\\nthe existing module interface and all cycle-level behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve existing assertions and add an independent 32-register\\narchitectural scoreboard. Fill every writable register with distinct\\npatterns and check all read-address pairs, including cross-bank pairs\\nand simultaneous same-address reads. Exercise consecutive overwrites,\\nalternating banks, writes observed by either or both read ports, and\\nbypass to a register whose validity bit is still clear. Reset after\\ndirty contents and verify all registers read zero without a scrub;\\ncover reset plus w_en, bypass before/after the reset edge followed by\\nbypass removal, attempted x0 writes, and repeated resets. Sample\\ncombinational reads before edges and stored results after edges with\\nsimulator-safe timing. Check observable data, not internal RAM init.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Extend integrated register-file coverage with dependent operations\\nspanning x15/x16 and both read ports, same-cycle WB-to-ID visibility,\\nand writes while younger instructions are held by dmem/divide waits.\\nReset after nonzero architectural state, restart from reset PC, and\\nprove that stale RAM data cannot enter EX operands or RVFI. Retain\\nthe existing ISS/prediction/retirement oracles and all prior tests;\\nassert exact operand values, single ordered retirement, and unchanged\\nbus-hold and recovery behavior.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 365.32, \"delta_pct\": -6.01, \"vs_baseline\": 29.17, \"fmax_mhz\": 153.99, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 4193, \"ff\": 2397, \"seeds\": [153.99, 155.09, 131.2], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the replicated distributed-RAM register file. All required local correctness gates pass, and the storage transformation is confirmed in synthesis and all three routed netlists. The measured result is nevertheless a fitness regression: median Fmax decreases from 163.83 MHz to 153.99 MHz, or 6.0062259659403%, with identical instruction timing and CoreMark cycles. The unmeasured 4% improvement estimate is not supported by this run.\\n\\nOnly `cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv`, `cores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py`, and `cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py` changed, plus these requested notes. Every other RTL file is byte-identical to the incoming HEAD, `49caed5`. The pipeline, arithmetic, hazards, forwarding, predictor, bus interface, retirement rules, and `nret: 1` remain unchanged. No protected evaluator, wrapper, benchmark source, constraint, Makefile, or other core was edited.\\n\\nThe register file now contains four behavioral 16x32 arrays with `ram_style = \\\"distributed\\\"`: low/high banks for each independent asynchronous read port. Both copies receive the same accepted write address, data, clock, and bank enable. Their clocked process performs writes only; there is no RAM reset, initialization, scrub, vendor-only simulation primitive, or formal-specific implementation. A write is physically accepted only when `!reset && w_en && w_addr != 0`.\\n\\nThe shared written-since-reset bitmap has 31 generated sticky bits and a constant-zero x0 bit. The explicit per-register enables ensure exactly 31 validity flip-flops; an initial indexed bitmap assignment synthesized an unnecessary 32nd bit and extra selection logic, so it was replaced before final validation. Reset clears these bits synchronously. Read priority remains x0, matching live write bypass, valid bank data, then zero. In particular, bypass is independent of reset and overrides an unset validity bit; removing bypass after a reset edge immediately exposes zero without another edge.\\n\\nThe original register-file assertions remain. Their clock helpers now wait for simulator evaluation before withdrawing writes or reset. Added tests use an independent flat 32-register architectural scoreboard, all 1,024 read-address pairs, distinct patterns in every writable register, both banks and both ports, same-address reads, consecutive overwrites, disabled and zero-valued writes, randomized accesses, attempted x0 writes, dirty and repeated resets, and reset plus live bypass before/after the edge and after bypass removal. Tests observe public read data and never inspect RAM initialization.\\n\\nThe pipeline harness now checks both live ID register operands against a flat architectural scoreboard on every cycle, including stalls and restart. New integrated programs exercise x15/x16 dependencies, WB-to-ID bypass on either or both ports, WB writes during dmem and divide holds, load-use stalls, recovery, accepted stores, and dirty reset while a WB write is offered. After restart, every writable register is read through both EX operand ports before rewriting; RVFI must report zero operands, ordered retirement, and the original retirement cycles. The unchanged ISS, complete RVFI comparator, predictor oracle, payload/hold assertions, and divider assertions also check these programs.\\n\\nValidation used isolated copies under `$TMPDIR/regfile-r12s1-mxxbsrmg/{incoming,candidate}`. The incoming RTL was extracted from HEAD; the candidate RTL/tests were checked byte-for-byte against the final worktree. Protected scripts and inputs were copied unchanged, and all gate outputs remained in scratch. The explicit single-retirement build settings were `NRET=1` and `BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`.\\n\\n- Exact requested `verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv`: PASS, no warnings/errors.\\n- Unchanged `make test TARGET=bench`: PASS for incoming and final candidate, all 9 pytest runners, including real and ALTOPS arithmetic, all 39 pipeline tests, and all 7 register-file tests. Every prior assertion was retained.\\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=6 bash formal/run_all.sh`: final local status PASS, 53 passed / 0 failed; automatic selection of wrapper_si.sv/checks_si.cfg. Two invocations total. An earlier overlapping invocation lost its staged directory to the protected script's stale-run cleanup and ended with `no_checks_generated`, despite its register-check solver reporting passed. The final invocation completed all checks normally. No RTL counterexample occurred and no formal-driven RTL fix was needed. The final register check took 314 seconds. No further formal retries were run.\\n- Unchanged `python3 -m tools.eval.cosim . bench`: PASS for both revisions, selftest plus the evaluator's stalled 2 KB CoreMark image.\\n- Additional selftest runs with both stalls and seeds 1, 2, 3: PASS against the unchanged ISS, 115 retirements each, with final cycles 198, 189, 187. Incoming/candidate complete RVFI records and cycles are identical.\\n- Optional `REGFILE_CAPTURE_TRACE` / `REGFILE_REFERENCE_TRACE` comparisons: PASS for five integrated scenarios, 373 observed cycles and 240 retirements. Complete traces compare equal, including ID/EX and downstream bundles, fetch prediction/acceptance, recovery, holds, WB, RVFI, retirement cycles, and accepted memory transactions. Normal tests need neither environment variable nor a reference artifact.\\n- Complete canonical 6 KB CoreMark RVFI streams: identical across all 22,093,920 retirements, including every cycle number and every emitted RVFI field. Both streams contain 6,463,492,183 bytes and SHA-256 `8ca365f85a629c0e8a9ff46ef22b637d75d7601508c56df753ed39c80a009866`. Streams were hashed without retaining multi-gigabyte trace files. Both processes exited successfully with bounded accesses; the final EBREAK retires at cycle 30,883,293.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: PASS for incoming and candidate; all three seeds route successfully. Timing improvement is not a correctness-gate condition.\\n\\nSynthesis confirms the intended organization: each of `rs1_low`, `rs1_high`, `rs2_low`, and `rs2_high` becomes eight RAM16SDP4 cells. All 32 register-file RAM cells survive in every routed netlist. The other eight RAM16SDP4 cells belong to the unchanged predictor.\\n\\nMapped connectivity was checked directly, including all 128 combinations of reset, write enable, and write address. Every RAM read-address pin resolves to bits 3:0 of its own read port; every write-address pin resolves to bits 3:0 of w_addr. Corresponding copies have identical DI/WAD/CLK/WRE connections, and each bank contains all 32 write-data bits. x0 validity is constant zero. Reset's combinational fanout terminates only at the 31 bitmap RESET/CE pins and the 32 RAM WRE pins; it does not reach RAM data/address pins or a live read-output mux. There are exactly 31 bitmap Q bits and no data flip-flops in the standalone candidate register file. Synthesized RAM INIT parameters are tool-selected values; correctness requires no initial RAM contents.\\n\\n| Resource / scope | Incoming | Candidate |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Standalone register file RAM16SDP4 | 0 | 32 |\\n| Standalone register file LUT4 cells | 1,453 | 191 |\\n| Standalone register file total LUT1-4 cells | 2,944 | 241 |\\n| Standalone register file MUX2_LUT5 cells | 148 | 6 |\\n| Standalone register file FF cells | 1,001 | 31 |\\n| Full benchmark synthesis RAM16SDP4 | 8 | 40 |\\n| Full benchmark synthesis LUT4 cells | 3,056 | 1,764 |\\n| Full benchmark synthesis total LUT1-4 cells | 6,008 | 3,327 |\\n| Full benchmark synthesis MUX2_LUT5 cells | 544 | 324 |\\n| Full benchmark synthesis FF cells | 3,150 | 2,157 |\\n| Routed LUT4 usage, each seed | 6,618 | 4,193 |\\n| Routed DFF usage, each seed | 3,198 | 2,397 |\\n\\nThe standalone candidate's 241 LUT1-4 cells and six wide mux cells include bank selection, validity selection, write decode, x0 selection, and the retained live bypass; the new selection logic is not free. Standalone and flattened-core optimizations differ, so their counts should not be mixed. In the full benchmark, synthesis removes 1,024 data DFFE cells and adds 31 validity DFFRE cells, a net reduction of 993 FF cells. The incoming mapper retained more data storage than the 992 useful architectural data bits. Routed device accounting differs from primitive FF counts because LUTRAM occupies additional tile resources. The measured routed reductions are 2,425 LUT4 slots and 801 reported DFF slots.\\n\\nAll six final routed critical paths were inspected and saved:\\n\\n| Revision | Seed | Fmax MHz | Source | Endpoint | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | --- | ---: | --- |\\n| Incoming | 1 | 163.83 | ID/EX alu_op[4] | EX/MEM alu_result[25].D | 6.10 | 2.87 / 3.24 |\\n| Incoming | 2 | 155.33 | ID/EX predicted_next_pc[3] | ID/EX valid.RESET | 6.44 | 2.77 / 3.67 |\\n| Incoming | 3 | 168.55 | ID/EX alu_op[2] | EX/MEM alu_result[25].D | 5.93 | 2.78 / 3.15 |\\n| Candidate | 1 | 153.99 | Predictor row 11 tag[10] | Predictor row 11 counter[0].CE | 6.49 | 2.88 / 3.61 |\\n| Candidate | 2 | 155.09 | ID/EX alu_op[3] | EX/MEM alu_result[30].D | 6.45 | 2.72 / 3.73 |\\n| Candidate | 3 | 131.20 | ID/EX alu_op[4] | EX/MEM alu_result[26].D | 7.62 | 2.81 / 4.82 |\\n\\nThe candidate's reported worst paths remain in unchanged predictor maintenance and EX result-selection logic. None of the three reported critical paths traverses register-file RAM or validity selection. Seed 3 has substantially worse EX routing delay. Thus the observed area reduction did not improve the placement/routing of the paths that limit this core; these reports do not identify replicated write fanout or RAM lookup as the new frequency bottleneck. No out-of-scope timing change or seed selection was used to hide the regression.\\n\\nBoth revisions ran the same ELF with `--istall --dstall`, accepted start/stop markers, no out-of-bounds accesses, successful EBREAK completion, and byte-identical bench-mode output. Throughput below is computed as exact iterations divided by marker-bracketed cycles times median Fmax in Hz, never from rounded `ipc_coremark`.\\n\\n| Image, 10 iterations, actual -O3 build | Start cycle | Stop cycle | Bracketed cycles, both revisions | Incoming iter/s | Candidate iter/s |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Historical/evaluator 2 KB | 15,229 | 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 388.6591153764583 | 365.3153706697235 |\\n| Required canonical 6 KB | 53,747 | 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 53.1522292433618 | 49.9597862490709 |\\n\\nThe protected evaluator actually builds and validates the 2 KB image. Its incoming score reproduces the historical 388.66 figure; its candidate score is 365.32. These values are kept separate from the like-for-like 6 KB comparison. The evaluator was not modified.\\n\\nThe canonical image was compiled using the unchanged benchmark Makefile's compiler command with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000`, `COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, and a separate output path `incoming/coremark-6k.elf`; the protected header still specifies ten iterations. UART reports `6k performance run parameters`, seedcrc 8a02, crclist d4b0, crcmatrix be52, crcstate 5e47, crcfinal 5949, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Its displayed Size=2000 is each algorithm's share of the 6000-byte working set. The port's compiler-flags string is hardcoded to -O2; the actual compile command used -O3. Neither display string was changed.\\n\\nELF SHA-256: 2 KB `7ee8291a57dae8c7a41b74356d38c9d73136372704313e5e7f92fc194fcae7c7`; 6 KB `60d2a3461ef6987ed59fd04a0dec48a238cf94d9f6a8d03970136e91e93559ba`.\\n\\nEvidence root: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/regfile-r12s1-mxxbsrmg`. It contains `storage-evidence.json`, `routed-storage-evidence.json`, `timing-results.json`, `coremark-results.json`, `coremark-6k-full-rvfi.json`, and `stalled-selftest.json`. Per-revision evidence includes `register-trace.json`, `regfile-synth.json`, `coremark-{2000,6000}.jsonl`, `critical-path-seed{1,2,3}.txt`, gate logs, and `cores/bench/generated/{synth.json,pnr_seed{1,2,3}/pnr.json}`. Final formal status is in `candidate/formal-final-gate.log`; final full cocotb status is in `candidate/cocotb-final-gate.log`. The implementation is left in place for orchestrator evaluation.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T23:02:20.477585Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 12, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r12s1 (regression, -6.01%): Replacing resettable `reg_file` data with 32 replicated `RAM16SDP4` cells and 31 validity bits saved 2,425 routed LUT4 slots, but unchanged EX result-selection and predictor-maintenance paths remained critical as median Fmax fell from 163.83 to 153.99 MHz at identical CoreMark cycles; storage area savings do not guarantee timing relief on the limiting paths.\"}\n39:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r13s1\", \"title\": \"Register integer and multiply results separately before final selection\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The incoming winner's saved routed reports, reproduced in the r12s1\\nexperiment, put ID/EX alu_op-to-EX/MEM alu_result selection on the\\ncritical path in seeds 1 and 3: 6.10 ns and 5.93 ns. Seed 2 instead\\nlimits on prediction recovery into ID/EX validity at 6.44 ns. These\\nare measured paths of the incoming design, not an assumption that\\nmultiplier arithmetic itself limits frequency.\\n\\nStructural attempts r9s1 and r11s1 moved multiplication into MEM and\\nregressed fitness. The latter preserved one MULT36X36, but added 1.48%\\nto canonical 6 KB CoreMark cycles and reduced median Fmax to 148.61 MHz.\\nThe r12s1 distributed-RAM register file saved area without improving\\nthe limiting EX and predictor paths. A different structural cut is to\\nmove result-family selection across the existing EX/MEM boundary while\\nretaining EX computation and immediate dependent-instruction forwarding.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The incoming winner's saved routed reports, reproduced in the r12s1\\nexperiment, put ID/EX alu_op-to-EX/MEM alu_result selection on the\\ncritical path in seeds 1 and 3: 6.10 ns and 5.93 ns. Seed 2 instead\\nlimits on prediction recovery into ID/EX validity at 6.44 ns. These\\nare measured paths of the incoming design, not an assumption that\\nmultiplier arithmetic itself limits frequency.\\n\\nStructural attempts r9s1 and r11s1 moved multiplication into MEM and\\nregressed fitness. The latter preserved one MULT36X36, but added 1.48%\\nto canonical 6 KB CoreMark cycles and reduced median Fmax to 148.61 MHz.\\nThe r12s1 distributed-RAM register file saved area without improving\\nthe limiting EX and predictor paths. A different structural cut is to\\nmove result-family selection across the existing EX/MEM boundary while\\nretaining EX computation and immediate dependent-instruction forwarding.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the single preselected EX/MEM execution-result register with\\ntwo parallel 32-bit result banks and one registered multiply-family\\nbit. One bank holds the existing non-multiply result, including integer\\nALU work, effective addresses, completed DIV/REM, and JAL/JALR PC+4.\\nThe other holds the selected MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU result. Both banks\\ncapture at the existing EX/MEM edge from the same forwarded operands.\\nThis is a relocation of the final result-family mux across a register\\nboundary; the instruction pipeline gains no stage or scheduling bubble.\\n\\nIn alu.sv, expose separate nonmul_result and multiply_result outputs.\\nThe non-multiply case retains existing integer and divider expressions\\nand defined default-zero behavior, with no multiply contribution. The\\nmultiply candidate contains the complete existing multiply family in\\nEX, using the original product expressions, widths, signedness, and\\nhigh/low slices. Select its suboperation with the current op[1:0]\\nmapping: 11=MUL, 00=MULH, 01=MULHU, 10=MULHSU. This candidate may have\\na defined speculative value on non-multiply instructions; do not put\\na five-bit multiply-family enable back on its wide capture data path.\\nQualify architectural use with the separate family bit, captured from\\na valid, legal decoded multiply. Keep operation encodings unchanged.\\nPreserve alu.out's existing standalone interface and full operation\\nsemantics by selecting from the same candidate outputs. The core uses\\nthe candidates directly, so that compatibility mux must synthesize\\naway from the core's EX/MEM capture cones.\\n\\nKeep the ordinary bank as ex_mem_t.alu_result and add multiply_result\\nand result_is_mul fields. Apply the existing EX/MEM register discipline\\nto every new field: deterministic reset, complete hold on a data-memory\\nstall, complete zero bubble on div_wait, otherwise capture together\\nwith destination, controls, operands, instruction, and PC metadata.\\nKeep JAL/JALR link selection on the ordinary bank and preserve the\\ndivider's eight-edge controller, retained result handshake, and saved\\npost-forward operands. Both arithmetic builds must use this same\\nregister and forwarding organization; ALTOPS retains every original\\noperation-specific formula and constant.\\n\\nSupply both registered banks and result_is_mul to EX forwarding. Keep\\nforward_unit's dependency tests and EX/MEM-over-MEM/WB priority. For\\neach operand, combine source selection and family selection in one\\nparallel mux: original ID/EX operand; MEM/WB write data; EX/MEM ordinary\\nbank when the EX/MEM source wins and result_is_mul is clear; or EX/MEM\\nmultiply bank when that source wins and result_is_mul is set. Use\\nmutually exclusive masks, avoiding a standalone family mux followed\\nserially by the old operand mux. Only registered result banks feed\\nthis bypass; no live multiply output can bypass its EX/MEM register.\\nPreserve x0 handling, the existing raw-source/RVFI convention, and\\nMEM/WB forwarding-data retention during memory holds.\\n\\nIn MEM, use the ordinary bank directly for effective-address, alignment,\\nand memory-port logic. Select the architectural non-load result from\\nthe two registered banks only when capturing MEM/WB. WB and RVFI keep\\ntheir current final registered result path. A MUL immediately followed\\nby an ALU operation, another MUL, a branch, JALR, or a store must use\\nthe product at the same edge as today, with no multiply-use interlock.\\nMaintain the existing load-use interlock, accepted memory transactions,\\nbus hold behavior, predictor policy and training edges, recovery,\\ninstruction legality, trap handling, and channel-0 retirement order.\\nKeep nret=1 and the entire core port set unchanged.\\n\\nThe unmeasured estimate is a 4% fitness improvement through Fmax at\\nunchanged instruction timing, with low confidence. The added bank,\\nwider bypass mux, or exposed predictor/recovery paths may erase the\\nbenefit. Confirm in synthesis that both result banks precede family\\nselection, the bypass mux integrates its selections, and the complete\\nmultiply family still shares one MULT36X36. Inspect all three routed\\npaths, especially bank-select through forwarding/JALR and predictor\\nmetadata updates. Do not infer a timing win from fewer source muxes.\\n\\nRun the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled ISS cosim, and three-seed\\nFPGA gates. For the same ELF and ready schedule, require identical\\nretirement cycles, architectural RVFI records, accepted memory\\ntransactions, and marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles. Compare meaningful\\narchitectural observations rather than inactive new-bank contents.\\nUse exact iterations/bracketed_cycles times median Fmax in Hz. Validate\\nthe requested 6 KB, ten-iteration, -O3 run with both bus stalls, bounded\\naccesses, real start/stop handshakes, d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, and the literal\\nCorrect operation validated. Prior logs associate historical 388.66\\nwith the evaluator's 2 KB image; report that comparison separately if\\nstill applicable, without changing any evaluator or benchmark file.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose independent non-multiply and selected multiply candidate\\noutputs. Keep all multiply arithmetic in EX and select its four\\nvariants using the existing low opcode bits, with family use\\nqualified after registration. Preserve the divider, operation\\nencodings, exact real/ALTOPS arithmetic, and the existing combined\\nout interface for standalone tests, sharing all candidate logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit multiply_result and a one-bit result_is_mul at the\\nmost significant end of ex_mem_t, preserving existing lower field\\npositions. Define alu_result as the ordinary bank; downstream\\narchitectural selection must explicitly consult result_is_mul.\\nLeave ctrl_t, ID/EX, MEM/WB, operation constants, and RVFI unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture the two candidate banks and qualified family bit together\\nat the existing EX/MEM edge, preserving full reset, hold, and\\ndivide-bubble semantics. Replace each three-source operand mux\\nwith four mutually exclusive terms that integrate registered-bank\\nselection with the unchanged forwarding source decision. Keep\\nbranch/JALR resolution, saved divider operands, and link behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire EX/MEM's ordinary and multiply banks and registered family\\nbit to the expanded internal EX forwarding interface. Preserve\\nforward_unit and hazard_unit behavior, module core's complete\\nsingle-retirement IO contract, and all RVFI wiring.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep address and memory qualification sourced from the ordinary\\nbank. Select the appropriate registered execution result into\\nMEM/WB.alu_result using result_is_mul. Preserve load extraction,\\nalignment traps, byte lanes, memory RVFI fields, and retained WB\\nforwarding data during holds; add no execution or interlock stage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing combined-result and divider assertion.\\nExtend real and ALTOPS tests to verify the new selected-family\\ncandidate outputs against independent expected arithmetic for\\nall integer and multiply operations, signed boundary/random\\noperands, and rapid family changes. Preserve default-op and\\ndivider request, latency, result-hold, and reset coverage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve existing ISS, RVFI, predictor, control-zeroing, hold,\\nand divider assertions. Add alternating result-family chains,\\nall four multiplies with immediate consumers on either source,\\nMUL-to-MUL, branch/JALR and store dependencies, competing writers,\\nx0, load-use bubbles, and WB forwarding. Exercise simultaneous\\nbus stalls, divide drain/completion, recovery, and reset of live\\nbanks. Assert exact operand values, ordered single retirement,\\natomic bank/selector holds, and no new ready-bus dependency bubble.\\n\"}]}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose independent non-multiply and selected multiply candidate\\noutputs. Keep all multiply arithmetic in EX and select its four\\nvariants using the existing low opcode bits, with family use\\nqualified after registration. Preserve the divider, operation\\nencodings, exact real/ALTOPS arithmetic, and the existing combined\\nout interface for standalone tests, sharing all candidate logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a 32-bit multiply_result and a one-bit result_is_mul at the\\nmost significant end of ex_mem_t, preserving existing lower field\\npositions. Define alu_result as the ordinary bank; downstream\\narchitectural selection must explicitly consult result_is_mul.\\nLeave ctrl_t, ID/EX, MEM/WB, operation constants, and RVFI unchanged.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture the two candidate banks and qualified family bit together\\nat the existing EX/MEM edge, preserving full reset, hold, and\\ndivide-bubble semantics. Replace each three-source operand mux\\nwith four mutually exclusive terms that integrate registered-bank\\nselection with the unchanged forwarding source decision. Keep\\nbranch/JALR resolution, saved divider operands, and link behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire EX/MEM's ordinary and multiply banks and registered family\\nbit to the expanded internal EX forwarding interface. Preserve\\nforward_unit and hazard_unit behavior, module core's complete\\nsingle-retirement IO contract, and all RVFI wiring.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep address and memory qualification sourced from the ordinary\\nbank. Select the appropriate registered execution result into\\nMEM/WB.alu_result using result_is_mul. Preserve load extraction,\\nalignment traps, byte lanes, memory RVFI fields, and retained WB\\nforwarding data during holds; add no execution or interlock stage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain every existing combined-result and divider assertion.\\nExtend real and ALTOPS tests to verify the new selected-family\\ncandidate outputs against independent expected arithmetic for\\nall integer and multiply operations, signed boundary/random\\noperands, and rapid family changes. Preserve default-op and\\ndivider request, latency, result-hold, and reset coverage.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve existing ISS, RVFI, predictor, control-zeroing, hold,\\nand divider assertions. Add alternating result-family chains,\\nall four multiplies with immediate consumers on either source,\\nMUL-to-MUL, branch/JALR and store dependencies, competing writers,\\nx0, load-use bubbles, and WB forwarding. Exercise simultaneous\\nbus stalls, divide drain/completion, recovery, and reset of live\\nbanks. Assert exact operand values, ordered single retirement,\\natomic bank/selector holds, and no new ready-bus dependency bubble.\\n\"}], \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 356.25, \"delta_pct\": -8.34, \"vs_baseline\": 25.96, \"fmax_mhz\": 150.17, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6667, \"ff\": 3231, \"seeds\": [150.17, 161.6, 142.49], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"Implemented the requested split execution-result banks. Correctness and instruction timing pass locally, but the measured FPGA fitness regresses by 8.3379%. The candidate remains in place for orchestrator evaluation.\\n\\n`alu.sv` now exposes independent `nonmul_result` and `multiply_result` candidates. Integer operations, effective-address sums, and the retained divider result feed the ordinary candidate; multiply operations produce zero there. The multiply candidate selects the unchanged product expressions and slices using only `op[1:0]`, including the original signedness and all four ALTOPS formulas/constants. The combined standalone `out` shares these candidates and preserves default-zero and divider semantics.\\n\\n`ex_mem_t` retains all original lower field positions, with a 32-bit multiply bank and one-bit selector added at its most significant end. EX captures both banks and a valid/legal decoded multiply selector at the existing edge. JAL/JALR PC+4 remains in the ordinary bank. The entire bundle resets to zero, holds on data-memory stalls, and becomes a complete zero bubble during divide wait. The divider controller, result handshake, and saved post-forward operands are unchanged.\\n\\nEach EX operand uses four mutually exclusive masked terms: ID/EX, MEM/WB, EX/MEM ordinary, and EX/MEM multiply. The unchanged forward unit still determines dependencies and younger-writer priority. Both EX/MEM operands originate in registered banks. MEM uses the ordinary bank for every memory address/alignment/byte-lane operation and selects the architectural execution result only into MEM/WB. WB, RVFI, nret=1, top-level ports, hazards, decoder, predictor, and retirement ordering are unchanged.\\n\\nTests retain the original assertions and add candidate arithmetic/default checks, signed boundaries and deterministic random operands, rapid family transitions, all four multiply variants with immediate consumers on either source, multiply chains, competing writers, x0, branch/JALR/store/load consumers, WB forwarding retention, load-use timing, simultaneous bus stalls, divide drain/completion, recovery, and reset of live banks. The extra `test_result_equivalence.py` builds two independent models behind the unchanged `test/cosim/main.cpp`, comparing active memory ports and architectural RVFI at every simulation edge. It is opt-in through `BENCH_INCOMING_RTL` and `BENCH_EQ_ELFS`; it ignores inactive memory data and speculative bank contents.\\n\\nLocal verification:\\n\\n- Exact requested Verilator lint: pass without warnings. An additional lint build with `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` also passes.\\n- Unchanged `make test TARGET=bench`: 10 pytest runners pass, covering both ALU builds, 41 real pipeline cases, the additional ALTOPS pipeline case, and all existing module suites. The optional snapshot comparison is skipped by this default invocation and passes when invoked separately.\\n- Unchanged `bash formal/run_all.sh`, with `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl` and `CORE_NAME=bench`: **53 passed, 0 failed on the first attempt**, using auto-detected nret=1 and the original ALTOPS checks. No formal fixes or retries were needed. Real-arithmetic formal-deep was not run.\\n- Unchanged `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1`: pass, two ELFs. Additional stalled ISS comparisons pass for both revisions at ready-schedule seeds 1, 7, and 19, with 115 ordered retirements each and identical cycles/architectural fields.\\n- Full lockstep comparison passes for selftest, evaluator 2 KB CoreMark, and requested 6 KB CoreMark, with both `--istall` and `--dstall`. The 6 KB comparison checks 22,093,920 retirements, 3,824,882 accepted reads, and 817,301 accepted writes over 30,883,294 elapsed cycles. It verifies exactly one accepted start marker and one accepted stop marker, identical retirement cycles and meaningful RVFI fields, and identical active memory requests throughout holds. No out-of-bounds accesses occur.\\n- Unchanged `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1 BENCH=fpga/core_bench_si.sv`: synthesis and all three routed seeds pass. No seed was dropped.\\n- `git diff --check`: pass. Implementation edits are confined to the five requested bench RTL files and three bench test files, plus these requested notes. Protected evaluator, benchmark, wrapper, and harness sources were not edited.\\n\\nAll gate outputs and build products were staged beneath `$TMPDIR`, including byte-for-byte copies of protected formal/benchmark files where their unchanged commands require writable output directories. There is no architectural deviation from the hypothesis. The additional lockstep test and scratch staging are verification additions. Test development required correcting a shadowed variable in new Python instrumentation and resolving the local Verilator runtime include/time-context setup; neither required an RTL change or weakened assertion.\\n\\nSynthesis inspection confirms distinct ordinary and multiply registers, each 32 bits, plus the selector register, with common clock/hold/reset discipline and zero reset values. The multiply candidate depends only on opcode bits 0 and 1 at the ALU operand boundary. The unused compatibility output and its family mux disappear. The optimized generic netlist retains four parallel masked data terms for each forwarded operand, and the MEM/WB input mux is driven by the two EX/MEM Q banks and their registered selector. The mapped netlist retains all 32 new multiply-bank flip-flops and exactly **one MULT36X36** for the complete multiply family. Routed resource usage is 6,667 LUT4 slots and 3,231 FF slots, versus incoming 6,618 and 3,198: +49 LUT4 and +33 FF.\\n\\nAll three final routed critical paths were inspected. Every candidate path starts in EX/MEM destination dependency decoding, traverses operand forwarding and the JALR target adder, and ends at the predictor's pending target capture. These paths do not identify multiplier arithmetic as the limiting logic.\\n\\n| Seed | Fmax MHz | Source | Endpoint | Total ns | Logic / routing ns |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- | ---: | --- |\\n| 1 | 150.17 | EX/MEM.rd[3] | IF pending_target_q[27].D | 6.66 | 3.69 / 2.97 |\\n| 2 | 161.60 | EX/MEM.rd[0] | IF pending_target_q[26].D | 6.19 | 3.60 / 2.59 |\\n| 3 | 142.49 | EX/MEM.rd[0] | IF pending_target_q[24].D | 7.02 | 3.81 / 3.21 |\\n\\nMedian Fmax is **150.17 MHz**. The incoming saved r12 reproduction reports 163.83 / 155.33 / 168.55 MHz, median **163.83 MHz**. Every incoming RTL file was compared byte-for-byte against that saved evidence before reusing its routed results. The wider forwarding organization exposes a slower dependency-to-JALR/predictor-target path; moving the family mux across EX/MEM did not yield the proposed timing improvement. Predictor row metadata policy and training edges remain unchanged, and no unrelated predictor change was attempted to hide this result.\\n\\nThroughput is calculated as exact `10 / bracketed_cycles * median_Fmax_Hz`, never from the evaluator's rounded `ipc_coremark`.\\n\\n| Image, ten iterations, actual -O3 build | Start cycle | Stop cycle | Bracketed cycles, both revisions | Incoming iter/s | Candidate iter/s |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Historical/evaluator 2 KB | 15,229 | 4,230,491 | 4,215,262 | 388.6591153764582 | 356.2530632734098 |\\n| Requested 6 KB | 53,747 | 30,876,537 | 30,822,790 | 53.1522292433618 | 48.7204435419376 |\\n\\nThe historical 388.66 comparison still applies to the protected evaluator's 2 KB image; its current reported candidate score is 356.25. It is separate from the required 6 KB result. The 6 KB ELF was built from unchanged sources using the unchanged benchmark Makefile with `COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O3`, in a separate scratch tree. Both revisions ran the exact same ELF and ready schedule. The header retains ten iterations. UART reports `6k performance run parameters`, seedcrc `8a02`, crclist `d4b0`, crcmatrix `be52`, crcstate `5e47`, crcfinal `5949`, and the literal `Correct operation validated.`. Its displayed Size=2000 is each algorithm's share of the 6000-byte working set. The protected port prints a hardcoded -O2 compiler-flags string; the saved actual compiler command uses -O3. No reporting string or evaluator expectation was changed.\\n\\nELF SHA-256: 2 KB `5ffe6f0c84b56ce08a2422f4b4e46dc0a2341af1c5f93bbb66e4c031a35fcedb`; 6 KB `7f3124f48d9535a4a9bc7d17ce437c42f9e38f9c643a4d6619ccf9aeb239361a`.\\n\\nEvidence root: `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/r13-result-banks`. It contains `formal.log`, `cocotb-final.log`, `cosim.log`, `stalled-iss.json`, `equivalence.log`, `fpga-result.json`, `synthesis-structure.json`, `generic.json`, `incoming-identity.json`, `coremark-results.json`, and `critical-path-seed{1,2,3}.txt`. The complete synthesis and route outputs are in `verify/cores/bench/generated/`. The successful independent lockstep build and per-ELF observations are in `/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/astra-max-20260905/bench-runs/gpt-6-astra_max-rep1/.tmp/bench-result-equivalence-rko5l06i/`. Incoming routed evidence is in the sibling scratch directory `regfile-r12s1-mxxbsrmg/incoming/`, with its summary in `regfile-r12s1-mxxbsrmg/timing-results.json`.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-05T23:46:20.521015Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-002-r13s1 (regression, -8.34%): Splitting integer/multiply results into parallel EX/MEM banks preserved CoreMark cycles and one `MULT36X36`, but widened forwarding exposed `EX/MEM.rd` through the JALR adder into `pending_target_q` as critical in all three seeds, lowering median Fmax from 163.83 to 150.17 MHz; moving result-family selection past capture must budget its cost in dependent-operand bypass paths.\"}\n41:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-001-r14s0\", \"title\": \"Factor multiply signedness and result selection into a shared ALU candidate\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The winner's saved incoming RTL under .tmp/seq-validation-r13s2/incoming/\\nmatches every current bench RTL file byte-for-byte. Its routed seeds are\\n163.83, 155.33 and 168.55 MHz. Seeds 1 and 3 are limited by alu_op-to-\\nEX/MEM.alu_result selection, beginning with the MULH/MULHU/MULHSU class\\npredicate; seed 2 is limited by prediction recovery. The mapped design\\nalready shares the three source product expressions into one MULT36X36.\\nIts multiplier sign-extension inputs nevertheless contain full-family\\nqualification, and MUL-low and multiply-high enter result selection as\\nseparate contributions. DSP count reduction is therefore not the target.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt log record regressions from full one-hot\\npredecode (r2s0), a registered high-class bit (r7s0), and operation recoding\\n(r12s0). The recent separate result banks (r13s1) also widened forwarding\\nand lost Fmax. A local algebraic factoring of the multiply candidate can\\nsimplify the same selection region while retaining the current decoder,\\npipeline registers, forwarding network and execution schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The winner's saved incoming RTL under .tmp/seq-validation-r13s2/incoming/\\nmatches every current bench RTL file byte-for-byte. Its routed seeds are\\n163.83, 155.33 and 168.55 MHz. Seeds 1 and 3 are limited by alu_op-to-\\nEX/MEM.alu_result selection, beginning with the MULH/MULHU/MULHSU class\\npredicate; seed 2 is limited by prediction recovery. The mapped design\\nalready shares the three source product expressions into one MULT36X36.\\nIts multiplier sign-extension inputs nevertheless contain full-family\\nqualification, and MUL-low and multiply-high enter result selection as\\nseparate contributions. DSP count reduction is therefore not the target.\\n\\nLESSONS.md and the micro_opt log record regressions from full one-hot\\npredecode (r2s0), a registered high-class bit (r7s0), and operation recoding\\n(r12s0). The recent separate result banks (r13s1) also widened forwarding\\nand lost Fmax. A local algebraic factoring of the multiply candidate can\\nsimplify the same selection region while retaining the current decoder,\\npipeline registers, forwarding network and execution schedule.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Rewrite only the real multiply candidate inside rtl/alu.sv as one explicit\\nsigned 33-by-33 product, with signedness and low/high selection determined\\nsolely by op[1:0]. Preserve all existing five-bit operation constants.\\nTheir multiply mapping is 11=MUL, 00=MULH, 01=MULHU and 10=MULHSU for\\nthese two low bits. Define fully specified combinational intermediates:\\n\\n  signed_a = !op[0];\\n  signed_b = !(op[1] || op[0]);\\n  a_ext = {signed_a && a[31], a};\\n  b_ext = {signed_b && b[31], b};\\n  product = $signed(a_ext) * $signed(b_ext);\\n  mul_result = (op[1:0] == 2'b11) ? product[31:0] : product[63:32];\\n\\nDeclare a_ext and b_ext as signed 33-bit vectors and product as a signed\\n66-bit vector, making all expression widths explicit. For MULH both\\noperands represent signed RV32 values; MULHSU sign-extends only a;\\nMULHU and MUL zero-extend both. The low 32 bits implement MUL for every\\ninput, and bits 63:32 implement each required high product. Bits 65:64\\nare intentionally unused. Do not add post-product sign-correction adders.\\n\\nCombine the four real multiply case labels into a single arm returning\\nmul_result. This leaves one fully decoded multiply-family contribution\\nin the existing ALU output case and moves low/high selection into its\\ncandidate using only two opcode bits. Do not qualify signed_a, signed_b\\nor the candidate's slice selection with the full opcode or instruction\\nvalidity. On non-multiply operations the speculative product is defined\\nbut unselected by the existing exact-opcode case; introduce no X values,\\nsynthesis don't-cares or assumptions about which instructions execute.\\n\\nKeep every non-multiply case arm and default-zero behavior unchanged.\\nKeep the four original RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS multiply formulas and constants\\nexactly as they are, with their individual full-opcode cases. Declare\\nthe real product network only in the non-ALTOPS build. All divider logic,\\nrequest/result handshakes, eight-edge latency, combinational multiply\\nlatency, EX jump-link selection and public/internal module ports stay\\nunchanged. This is one combinational datapath/control factoring experiment.\\n\\nThe expected benefit is a simpler multiply selection cone and removal of\\nfull-family decoding from DSP sign-extension inputs. The critical path\\nobserved in the winner is result selection, not DSP arithmetic, so this\\nis a hypothesis about the resulting mapping rather than a measured speedup.\\nEstimate a three-percent fitness gain through Fmax, with low confidence:\\nsynthesis may recreate the selection depth, the two-bit slice selector\\nmay add routing, or recovery, JALR and predictor maintenance may dominate.\\nInspect the mapped sign-input dependencies, shared candidate selection,\\nDSP count and all three routed critical paths. Retain one MULT36X36 and\\nreport LUT4/FF changes; fewer source expressions alone do not establish\\nsuccess. Do not expand the experiment into additional pipeline changes.\\n\\nRequire unchanged lint, cocotb, formal and stalled ISS cosim gates. Real\\narithmetic tests are essential because ALTOPS does not prove multiplication.\\nFor identical ELF and bus-ready schedules, require identical accepted bus\\ntransactions and valid RVFI records including both raw operands and their\\ncycle positions, with unchanged marker-bracketed cycles. Preserve nret=1,\\ndecoder legality, traps, reset/hold behavior and every protected check.\\nMeasure the unchanged three-seed FPGA fitness as full-precision iterations\\ndivided by bracketed cycles times median Fmax_Hz. Keep historical 2 KB\\nscores distinct from the required 6 KB, -O3, ten-iteration run with both\\nbus stalls, CRCs d4b0/be52/5e47, bounded accesses, start/stop markers and\\nthe literal Correct operation validated.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three real product expressions with the explicit shared\\nsigned 33-bit operand formulation and 66-bit product above. Use only\\nop[1:0] for operand extension and low/high candidate selection, then\\none grouped full-opcode multiply case arm. Preserve integer/default\\ncases, exact ALTOPS formulas, divider wiring, interfaces and latency.\\nDocument the current opcode mapping and intentionally unused top bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing real arithmetic oracle with a cross-product of\\nsigned boundaries, zero, one, all-ones, alternating patterns and powers\\nof two, plus deterministic random operand pairs for all four multiply\\noperations. Stress back-to-back signedness and low/high changes with\\nfixed operands. Check all non-M and reserved opcodes sharing each low\\ntwo-bit pattern so the speculative product never leaks into out.\\nRetain every ALTOPS, divide-handshake, latency and special-case check.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed mixed MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU streams with dependent consumers\\nexercising EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, negative/unsigned boundaries,\\nload-use bubbles, instruction/data stalls, divide waits and redirects.\\nCheck exact register results, both raw RVFI operand values, retirement\\norder and unchanged multiply timing. Retain all existing assertions\\nand use the current helpers without modifying protected harnesses.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three real product expressions with the explicit shared\\nsigned 33-bit operand formulation and 66-bit product above. Use only\\nop[1:0] for operand extension and low/high candidate selection, then\\none grouped full-opcode multiply case arm. Preserve integer/default\\ncases, exact ALTOPS formulas, divider wiring, interfaces and latency.\\nDocument the current opcode mapping and intentionally unused top bits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Extend the existing real arithmetic oracle with a cross-product of\\nsigned boundaries, zero, one, all-ones, alternating patterns and powers\\nof two, plus deterministic random operand pairs for all four multiply\\noperations. Stress back-to-back signedness and low/high changes with\\nfixed operands. Check all non-M and reserved opcodes sharing each low\\ntwo-bit pattern so the speculative product never leaks into out.\\nRetain every ALTOPS, divide-handshake, latency and special-case check.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed mixed MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU streams with dependent consumers\\nexercising EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, negative/unsigned boundaries,\\nload-use bubbles, instruction/data stalls, divide waits and redirects.\\nCheck exact register results, both raw RVFI operand values, retirement\\norder and unchanged multiply timing. Retain all existing assertions\\nand use the current helpers without modifying protected harnesses.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 329.11, \"delta_pct\": -15.32, \"vs_baseline\": 16.37, \"fmax_mhz\": 138.73, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4215262, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6611, \"ff\": 3198, \"seeds\": [136.76, 144.32, 138.73], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-06T00:26:40.195348Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 14, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-06 hyp-20260906-001-r14s0 (regression, -15.32%): Factoring multiply signedness and low/high selection onto `op[1:0]` removed full-family decoding from DSP sign inputs and retained one `MULT36X36`, but forwarding through the JALR adder into `pending_target_q` limited all three routed seeds, cutting median Fmax from 163.83 to 138.73 MHz with identical 6 KB CoreMark cycles; simplifying a shared multiply candidate can worsen timing outside its own cone.\"}\n42:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-002-r14s1\", \"title\": \"Resolve control flow in MEM from registered forwarded operands\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"Move control-flow execution across the existing EX/MEM register to\\nseparate operand forwarding from branch comparison, JALR target addition,\\nand recovery. The incoming RTL matches the saved r12 incoming timing\\nsnapshot byte-for-byte: seeds 1 and 3 are limited by ALU selection,\\nwhile seed 2 is limited by predicted-next-PC recovery into ID/EX reset.\\nRelated candidates repeatedly expose forwarding through the JALR adder\\ninto predictor target capture. Removing that whole execution dependency\\nis a more substantial structural experiment than another result mux.\\n\\nRead LESSONS.md and the structural history. r13s1's parallel result banks\\nlost 8.34% with wider forwarding/JALR paths; r8s1's registered dependency\\nmatches lost 6.12% when predictor maintenance became limiting. r10s2\\ndelayed predictor training but retained live EX branch execution and\\nrecovery, losing 12.29%. This proposal instead moves the comparator,\\nfull target adders, and architectural recovery to MEM together.\\n\\nr13s2 records 550,409 recoveries in 30,822,790 canonical 6 KB timed\\ncycles. One extra cycle per old recovery is a first-order 1.79% cycle\\ncost, with a break-even clock near 166.76 MHz versus 163.83 MHz.\\nTraining visibility and overlapping stalls can change that estimate.\\nALU selection and predictor maintenance remain competing limits, so\\na clock improvement is a hypothesis rather than an established result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Move control-flow execution across the existing EX/MEM register to\\nseparate operand forwarding from branch comparison, JALR target addition,\\nand recovery. The incoming RTL matches the saved r12 incoming timing\\nsnapshot byte-for-byte: seeds 1 and 3 are limited by ALU selection,\\nwhile seed 2 is limited by predicted-next-PC recovery into ID/EX reset.\\nRelated candidates repeatedly expose forwarding through the JALR adder\\ninto predictor target capture. Removing that whole execution dependency\\nis a more substantial structural experiment than another result mux.\\n\\nRead LESSONS.md and the structural history. r13s1's parallel result banks\\nlost 8.34% with wider forwarding/JALR paths; r8s1's registered dependency\\nmatches lost 6.12% when predictor maintenance became limiting. r10s2\\ndelayed predictor training but retained live EX branch execution and\\nrecovery, losing 12.29%. This proposal instead moves the comparator,\\nfull target adders, and architectural recovery to MEM together.\\n\\nr13s2 records 550,409 recoveries in 30,822,790 canonical 6 KB timed\\ncycles. One extra cycle per old recovery is a first-order 1.79% cycle\\ncost, with a break-even clock near 166.76 MHz versus 163.83 MHz.\\nTraining visibility and overlapping stalls can change that estimate.\\nALU selection and predictor maintenance remain competing limits, so\\na clock improvement is a hypothesis rather than an established result.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Repartition the existing single-issue pipeline: EX computes ordinary\\ninteger/multiply results and captures forwarded operands; MEM performs\\nall conditional branch comparisons, full-width branch/JAL/JALR target\\ngeneration, architectural next-PC selection, and prediction checking.\\nUse the existing EX/MEM and MEM/WB boundaries. Add no general execution\\nstage or new instruction buffer. Correctly predicted control flow still\\npermits one instruction to advance each cycle. A recovery now squashes\\nthe younger instruction in EX as well as the current combinational\\nIF/ID instruction, costing one additional recovery slot.\\n\\nCarry the exact accepted predicted_next_pc and decoded immediate into\\nEX/MEM alongside its existing PC, instruction, controls, and post-forward\\nrs1_val/rs2_val. Remove the obsolete EX-computed branch_taken,\\nbranch_target, and pc_next fields, replacing their roles explicitly.\\nMEM computes PC+4 and PC+imm from these registers, and computes JALR as\\n(registered_rs1 + immediate) with bit zero cleared. Comparisons use the\\nregistered post-forward operands, with the existing signed/unsigned\\nsemantics. Do not add a live forwarding path into MEM resolution.\\nMEM supplies the resolved pc_next to MEM/WB for RVFI.\\n\\nKeep JAL/JALR's PC+4 link result available in EX/MEM at its current edge\\nfor ordinary EX/MEM forwarding. A trapping jump must never forward a\\nlink that it will not write, even when its predicted PC already equals\\nthe trap's fall-through PC. Therefore retain only a small unconditional\\njump-alignment check in EX: test the low two bits of PC+imm for JAL,\\nand bit one of the low-two-bit rs1+imm sum for JALR after clearing bit\\nzero. Include carry from bit zero. Set the existing trap control and\\nclear reg_write before EX/MEM capture for an illegal-alignment jump.\\nThis must use low-bit arithmetic, not retain a full target adder in EX\\nor introduce MEM trap feedback through the operand forwarding mux.\\nA misaligned conditional branch traps only when taken, resolved in MEM;\\nit has no link write to suppress. Preserve decoder-illegal behavior,\\nmemory-alignment checks, and PC+4 recovery for trapping instructions.\\n\\nDefine resolve_valid from the current EX/MEM instruction's actual MEM\\nadvancement: valid, not reset, and not waiting for its data-bus access.\\nIt must not depend on a younger divide wait, frontend stall, redirect,\\nor hold_wb output feedback. Generate redirect when this event's complete\\narchitectural next PC differs from its captured prediction. Check every\\nvalid instruction, including ordinary ALU work, loads/stores, completed\\ndivides, and traps, so stale predictor entries recover correctly.\\nA held memory instruction resolves and trains exactly once when it\\nadvances; no event is generated from an EX/MEM bubble.\\n\\nRecovery belongs to the older MEM instruction. Let it override younger\\nload-use and divide interlocks, flush all ID/EX controls and validity,\\nand insert a complete zero-control/invalid EX/MEM bubble instead of\\ncapturing the younger EX result. Preserve the resolving MEM instruction's\\nown MEM/WB transfer, accepted memory operation, and eventual retirement.\\nRoute recovery to EX as an explicit squash input and gate divider\\nrequest and result consumption with it. A wrong-path divide must not\\nlaunch while an older MEM instruction redirects; an older data-memory\\nhold already blocks launch. Keep accepted divides' eight-edge engine,\\nsaved operands, result retention, and real/ALTOPS schedule unchanged.\\nThe MEM resolve event must remain independent of div_wait, preventing\\na wrong-path divide from suppressing its own squash. Clear younger\\nmemory controls before they can enter MEM and drive any bus request.\\n\\nPreserve the existing backend hold behavior, including MEM/WB forwarding\\ndata retention after its valid bit clears. Keep load-use detection,\\nEX/MEM-over-MEM/WB forwarding priority, raw RVFI source-value reporting,\\nx0 behavior, register-file writes, and all ALU arithmetic unchanged.\\nIF recovery continues to override imem backpressure. The current\\nID/EX payload/control split remains: recovery clears controls/valid\\nwithout adding recovery to wide payload enables or reset cones.\\n\\nFeed the existing predictor interface from the MEM resolution event.\\nRetain all sixteen rows, full tags, PC[5:2] indexing, counters, target\\nstorage, command encoding, private pending events, and committed-state\\nlookup. Its existing pending capture/commit pipeline is unchanged;\\nresolution reaches it one execution stage later. Do not train squashed\\ninstructions, replay held instructions, or cancel already accepted\\nolder events on recovery. Reset cancels pending events as before.\\nPredictor-policy changes and ALU/result-bank redesigns are outside this\\nsingle control-flow stage-repartition experiment.\\n\\nEstimate a net 4% fitness gain, with low confidence. At the first-order\\n6 KB cycle penalty this requires about 173.43 MHz, roughly 5.86% above\\nthe incoming clock. Measure the actual cycle cost and all three routed\\nseeds; the unchanged ALU selection, predictor maintenance, or a new\\nMEM-to-recovery-to-EX-bubble path may defeat the hypothesis. Inspect\\nmapped cones to confirm full target generation and branch comparison\\nstart at EX/MEM operand registers and no longer traverse EX forwarding.\\n\\nPreserve nret=1, module core's complete port set, strict channel-0\\nretirement order, all trap and progress checks, and every protected\\nfile. Run the unchanged lint, cocotb, formal, stalled ISS cosim, and\\nthree-seed FPGA gates. Update only pipeline-latency expectations in\\nbench tests where this stage move intentionally changes timing; retain\\nindependent architectural, memory, and predictor-state assertions.\\nCompare identical incoming/candidate ELFs with both bus-stall modes,\\nmeasuring iterations/bracketed_cycles times median Fmax in Hz.\\nValidate the canonical 6 KB, ten-iteration, -O3 run, actual start/stop\\nmarker handshakes, memory bounds, d4b0/be52/5e47 CRCs, and the literal\\nCorrect operation validated. History associates 388.66 with the\\nprotected evaluator's 2 KB image; report that comparison separately\\nfrom the requested 6 KB result without modifying evaluation sources.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace EX/MEM's pre-resolved branch/next-PC payload with captured\\nimmediate and exact accepted prediction metadata. Preserve its\\nregistered forwarded operands and normal result. MEM/WB retains\\nthe architectural resolved pc_next and unchanged RVFI fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove full target generation, conditional comparisons, and live\\nresolution/recovery outputs. Capture operands and resolution\\nmetadata for MEM. Retain PC+4 links and a low-bit jump-alignment\\ncheck that suppresses trapping links before forwarding. Add older\\nMEM squash handling that kills the younger EX/MEM transfer and\\nsuppresses divider handshakes, preserving all other arithmetic,\\nforwarding, hold, bubble, reset, and divider behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve branches/jumps and all-instruction prediction mismatches\\nfrom EX/MEM registers, qualified only by actual MEM advancement.\\nExport redirect and the existing predictor-resolution fields.\\nCombine control-flow and existing memory traps into MEM/WB and\\ncapture the architectural next PC. Preserve the resolving\\ninstruction's memory transaction, load extraction, byte masks,\\nRVFI operands, and WB retention during real memory waits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Source redirect and predictor training from MEM instead of EX,\\nwire the older recovery into EX squash, and connect the revised\\ninternal interfaces. Preserve forwarding data sources, the\\nsingle-retirement IO shape, regfile writes, and RVFI order logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Make older MEM recovery flush both younger issue positions and\\ntake priority over a younger load-use or divide wait. Keep actual\\nmemory waits distinct and free of resolve/redirect feedback;\\npreserve ordinary stalls, PC redirect priority, and retained WB\\nforwarding semantics. Do not let div_wait mask a MEM recovery.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve existing load-use and bus-hold coverage. Update the\\nrecovery priority oracle for a MEM-stage redirect, exhaustively\\ncovering simultaneous imem stalls, load-use, and younger divide\\nwaits, with no combinational hold/resolve dependency.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Observe acceptance, EX transfer, MEM resolution, and retirement\\nseparately, preserving independent RVFI and predictor oracles.\\nCheck warm predictions retain throughput and a cold miss squashes\\nboth younger slots. Exercise dependent ALU/load-to-branch/JALR,\\ncorrect and stale non-control predictions, changing targets,\\nsigned comparisons, repeated bus holds, reset, and ordered events.\\nPlace stores, marker writes, traps, and divides in each wrong-path\\nslot and require no request, launch, or retirement from them.\\nTest misaligned JAL/JALR with a fall-through prediction and a\\ndependent successor to prove a suppressed link never forwards,\\nincluding low-bit carry in JALR alignment. Retain real/ALTOPS\\ndivide checks, older memory completion during recovery, and exact\\nsingle-retirement ordering without weakening existing invariants.\\n\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace EX/MEM's pre-resolved branch/next-PC payload with captured\\nimmediate and exact accepted prediction metadata. Preserve its\\nregistered forwarded operands and normal result. MEM/WB retains\\nthe architectural resolved pc_next and unchanged RVFI fields.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove full target generation, conditional comparisons, and live\\nresolution/recovery outputs. Capture operands and resolution\\nmetadata for MEM. Retain PC+4 links and a low-bit jump-alignment\\ncheck that suppresses trapping links before forwarding. Add older\\nMEM squash handling that kills the younger EX/MEM transfer and\\nsuppresses divider handshakes, preserving all other arithmetic,\\nforwarding, hold, bubble, reset, and divider behavior.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Resolve branches/jumps and all-instruction prediction mismatches\\nfrom EX/MEM registers, qualified only by actual MEM advancement.\\nExport redirect and the existing predictor-resolution fields.\\nCombine control-flow and existing memory traps into MEM/WB and\\ncapture the architectural next PC. Preserve the resolving\\ninstruction's memory transaction, load extraction, byte masks,\\nRVFI operands, and WB retention during real memory waits.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Source redirect and predictor training from MEM instead of EX,\\nwire the older recovery into EX squash, and connect the revised\\ninternal interfaces. Preserve forwarding data sources, the\\nsingle-retirement IO shape, regfile writes, and RVFI order logic.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Make older MEM recovery flush both younger issue positions and\\ntake priority over a younger load-use or divide wait. Keep actual\\nmemory waits distinct and free of resolve/redirect feedback;\\npreserve ordinary stalls, PC redirect priority, and retained WB\\nforwarding semantics. Do not let div_wait mask a MEM recovery.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_hazard_unit.py\", \"description\": \"Preserve existing load-use and bus-hold coverage. Update the\\nrecovery priority oracle for a MEM-stage redirect, exhaustively\\ncovering simultaneous imem stalls, load-use, and younger divide\\nwaits, with no combinational hold/resolve dependency.\\n\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Observe acceptance, EX transfer, MEM resolution, and retirement\\nseparately, preserving independent RVFI and predictor oracles.\\nCheck warm predictions retain throughput and a cold miss squashes\\nboth younger slots. Exercise dependent ALU/load-to-branch/JALR,\\ncorrect and stale non-control predictions, changing targets,\\nsigned comparisons, repeated bus holds, reset, and ordered events.\\nPlace stores, marker writes, traps, and divides in each wrong-path\\nslot and require no request, launch, or retirement from them.\\nTest misaligned JAL/JALR with a fall-through prediction and a\\ndependent successor to prove a suppressed link never forwards,\\nincluding low-bit carry in JALR alignment. Retain real/ALTOPS\\ndivide checks, older memory completion during recovery, and exact\\nsingle-retirement ordering without weakening existing invariants.\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: liveness_ch0\\nmmary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:04 (4)\\nSBY  2:18:57 [insn_sub_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY  2:18:57 [insn_sub_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY  2:18:57 [insn_sub_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY  2:19:36 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:01  waiting for solver (1 minute)\\nSBY  2:19:45 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:01:09  Status: passed\\nSBY  2:19:45 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\nSBY  2:19:45 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY  2:19:45 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:01:10 (70)\\nSBY  2:19:45 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:01:06 (66)\\nSBY  2:19:45 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY  2:19:45 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY  2:19:45 [reg_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\n\\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\\nFailed: liveness_ch0\\n\\n--- liveness_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\nSBY  2:18:35 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 8..\\nSBY  2:18:35 [liveness_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\nSBY 