nations for resolution feedback. Source-level table state falls\nfrom 944 to 480 bits; event state falls from 499 to 274 bits, a combined\nreduction of 689 bits before synthesis. The extra tag bit preserves full\naddress discrimination. Inspect the synthesized mapping and routed paths\nto establish whether t\nNOTES \nhyp-20260905-003-r8s2 Train bimodal direction counters independently of target tags regression -33.64 108.97\nConvert the sixteen existing two-bit direction counters into an untagged,\nPC-indexed bimodal direction table while retaining the sixteen-entry,\nfull-tag direct-mapped target table. Both still use PC[5:2]; keep the\ncounters as private scalar registers in their existing rows. This is a\nsingle predictor-policy experiment: direction history belongs to an\nindex and persists across target replacements and invalidations, rather\nthan belonging to the target table's resident full PC.\n\nReset all sixteen counters to 2'b10, independently of table validity.\nAt the existing pending-event commit edge, a legal JUMP sets its row's\ncounter to 2'b11, TAKEN saturating-increments it, and NOT_TAKEN\nsaturating-decrements it. Apply these counter updates regardless of\nrow_hit, tag_q, or valid_q, including not-taken target-table misses and\ntaken allocations. INVALIDATE and absent events leave the counter alone.\nThere is no special weakly-taken initialization on target allocation.\nCounter initialization is necessary because a not-taken event can now\ntrain an index that has never held a valid target.\n\nGive each binary counter one sequential owner separate from target\nmetadata updates. Counter next data and write qualification may depend\nonly on its current two bits, the row's registered pending command and\nvalidity, and reset. Remove all tag-match and target-valid dependencies\nfrom that cone; do not merely move their qualification from a clock\nenable to a data mux. Preserve binary saturation rather than repeating\nthe thermometer experiment. Inspect synthesized and routed logic to\nverify the intended removal of tag-to-counter D/enable paths.\n\nKeep the fetch equation exactly as it is: a valid PC[31:6] tag match\nAND counter[1] selects the stored full target[31:2], otherwise PC+4.\nThus an untagged dir\nNOTES 11270 chars\nhyp-20260905-003-r9s2 Capture raw predictor events before update classification regression -23.41 125.47\nMove predictor update classification across the EXISTING event-capture\nboundary, without adding another stage. In each private row, replace\npending_command_q with independent registered raw fields: decoded\nis_branch, decoded is_jump, resolved branch-taken, decoder legality\nBEFORE EX target-alignment trapping, and target-misaligned. Capture the\nlast field from resolve_target[1:0] != 0, independently of outcome.\nContinue capturing the full tag and row validity at the existing edge.\nEvery payload capture enable must remain resolve_valid && row_index_hit;\nneither branch outcome nor target legality qualifies that enable.\n\nExpose the pre-trap decoder-legality bit from EX through core to IF.\nKeep EX's architectural trap generation, resolved legality, next-PC\nselection, and once-only resolve_valid qualification unchanged. The\noriginal resolved-legality signal may remain available to existing\nmonitors, but must not feed the new predictor capture cones.\n\nAfter capture, derive the original command locally from the registered\nraw fields. For decoder-produced control classes, effective legality is\ndecode_legal && !(target_misaligned && (jump || (branch && taken))).\nAn effectively illegal event or non-control becomes INVALIDATE; a legal\njump becomes JUMP; otherwise a legal branch becomes TAKEN or NOT_TAKEN.\nThus an untaken branch with a misaligned encoded destination remains\nlegal and can decrement a matching counter; a taken misaligned branch\nor jump invalidates only its matching entry. Derive this combinationally\nafter the registers rather than storing another encoded command.\n\nApply precisely the existing row-local commit rules against current\ntag/valid/counter state. JUMP sets counter 11; a TAKEN miss allocates 10,\nand a TAKEN hit saturating-increments. NOT_TAKEN decrements only \nNOTES 12724 chars\nhyp-20260905-003-r10s2 Train the predictor from registered EX/MEM results regression -12.29 143.66\nSource predictor training from the existing EX/MEM register rather than\nlive EX resolution. Introduce one registered training-valid token in\ncore.sv: reset clears it; every other edge assigns it the current\nresolve_valid. Do not clock-enable or hold this token on pipeline stalls.\nThe existing resolve_valid remains the advancing-EX event, qualified by\nin.valid, !stall, !div_wait, and !reset, and continues to qualify immediate\narchitectural recovery. Keep these live resolution signals available to\nthe pipeline checker independently of the delayed training interface.\n\nConnect if_stage's training inputs to that token and the corresponding\nEX/MEM fields: PC from ex_mem_w.pc, target from ex_mem_w.pc_next, branch\nand jump flags from ex_mem_w.ctrl, taken from ex_mem_w.branch_taken, and\nlegal from !ex_mem_w.ctrl.is_illegal. Use the EX/MEM control bundle before\nMEM-stage memory-alignment processing. The registered architectural next\nPC is the exact target for legal jumps and taken branches, including\nJALR's cleared low bit. Its value is irrelevant for NOT_TAKEN and\nINVALIDATE commands, which must never write target storage. Preserve\nEX's existing decoder and target-misalignment trap decisions.\n\nFor an instruction advancing at edge N, EX/MEM captures its results and\nthe token becomes valid at N. The unchanged private row-event registers\nand indexed target-event register accept this registered feedback at\nN+1; metadata and target commit together at N+2. This adds exactly one\ntraining-visibility cycle to the current N+1 commit. At N+1, capture must\nsee the pre-edge EX/MEM payload even if a newer instruction advances on\nthat same edge. Consecutive advancing instructions can generate consecutive\nvalid tokens: sustain one training transaction per clock without a queue,\nbackpressure, ta\nNOTES \nhyp-20260905-003-r11s2 Prevalidate sequential and direct-target predictions in decode regression -65.45 56.61\nPrevalidate the two operand-independent possible successors in ID and\ncarry their exact equality results with the instruction. Add two one-bit\nid_ex_t payload fields, prediction_matches_seq and prediction_matches_direct.\nCompute them in id_stage from the same IF bundle and decoded immediate\nthat the existing ID/EX payload captures:\n  prediction_matches_seq = (in.predicted_next_pc == seq_pc);\n  prediction_matches_direct = (in.predicted_next_pc == direct_pc);\nHere seq_pc is the 32-bit result of in.pc + 32'd4, and direct_pc is the\n32-bit result of in.pc + imm. Explicitly truncate both sums modulo 2^32\nbefore equality. Compare all 32 bits, including target alignment bits.\nThese are exact destination comparisons, not predicted-direction hints\nor truncated target signatures. Retain the full predicted_next_pc field.\n\nRegister both bits at the existing ID/EX boundary with exactly the winning\npayload discipline: deterministic zero on reset, retain on the existing\nbackend stall, otherwise capture even if a flush makes the payload inert.\nFlush still clears every ctrl bit and valid independently. Give each new\nfield one sequential owner. Do not add the recovery or load-use signals\nto these payload fields' reset or enable cones. Never recompute a held\ninstruction's match bits from live fetch, predictor state, or operands.\n\nReplace only EX's final prediction-correctness calculation. Start with\nprediction_correct = in.prediction_matches_seq. If ctrl_with_trap is legal,\na jump selects in.prediction_matches_direct for JAL, or the full equality\nof in.predicted_next_pc and the existing forwarded, bit-zero-cleared\njump_target for JALR. Otherwise a taken conditional branch selects\nin.prediction_matches_direct; an untaken branch or non-control retains\nin.prediction_matches_seq. A decoder or \nNOTES 10136 chars\nhyp-20260906-003-r12s2 XOR-folded PC indexing for the existing tagged target predictor regression -15.67 138.33\nReplace the index function in the existing sixteen-row direct-mapped\ntarget predictor with index(PC) = PC[5:2] XOR PC[9:6]. This is the sole\narchitectural change. Use the identical four-bit function for fetch\nlookup, resolving-event row selection, and the captured target-write\nindex. Retain the complete PC[31:6] tag, thirty-bit aligned target,\ntwo-bit saturating counter, and all sixteen entries.\n\nThe unchanged tag remains sufficient for exact instruction identity:\nfor an aligned PC, PC[9:6] is contained in the tag, and PC[5:2] can be\nrecovered as index XOR tag[3:0]. Consequently two different aligned\nPCs cannot have both the same folded index and the same full tag.\nThis changes placement without introducing partial-tag false hits.\nFor example, 0x100 and 0x140 formerly collided but now select rows\nfour and five. Conversely, 0x100 and 0x144 now collide in row four;\ntheir different full tags must still prevent a false target hit.\n\nPreserve the current private per-row pending-event registers and the\nshared pending-target record. An event accepted at edge N still commits\nat edge N+1 against current row state, with atomic tag/target publication\nand one-event-per-edge throughput. Preserve command classification and\nencoding, jump-to-11, taken-miss-to-10, saturating updates on hits,\nno allocation on not-taken misses, matching-only invalidation, and reset\ncancellation. Pending events must drain independently of fetch stalls,\nbackend holds, and redirects. Fetch must use committed state with the\nexisting pre-edge visibility and no update bypass.\n\nKeep the accepted predicted_next_pc, all EX next-PC comparisons and\nimmediate recovery, the instruction pipeline, module interfaces, and\nnret=1 unchanged. No instruction decode or target arithmetic is added\nto IF. The source-level additio\nNOTES \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
