{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,125p' cores/bench/test/test_id_stage.py\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"ID/EX control priority and payload independence, including flush+hold.\"\"\"\nimport itertools\nimport random\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.triggers import Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\nfrom test_pipeline import ADDI, BEQ, DIV, ECALL, JAL, LUI, LW, REM, SW, _id_fields\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def control_and_payload_priorities(dut):\n    rng = random.Random(0x1DEB0BB1)\n    instructions = [ADDI(9, 3, -17), LW(9, 3, 20), SW(9, 3, 24),\n                    DIV(9, 3, 7), REM(9, 3, 7), BEQ(3, 7, -20),\n                    JAL(9, 124), LUI(9, 0xABCDE000), 0xABCDE497, ECALL()]\n    previous = None\n    # Start with reset, then exercise every reset/flush/stall combination\n    # against nonzero operands, immediates, register addresses and metadata.\n    controls = [(1, 0, 0)] + list(itertools.product((0, 1), repeat=3))\n    for instr in instructions:\n        for reset, flush, stall in controls + [(0, 0, 0)]:\n            pc, prediction, rs1, rs2 = [rng.getrandbits(32) for _ in range(4)]\n            valid = rng.randrange(2)\n            dut.clock.value = 0\n            dut.reset.value = reset\n            dut.flush.value = flush\n            dut.stall.value = stall\n            dut[\"in\"].value = (pc << 65) | (prediction << 33) | (instr << 1) | valid\n            dut.rs1_data.value = rs1\n            dut.rs2_data.value = rs2\n            await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n            decoded = int(dut.ctrl_decoded.value)\n            payload = dict(pc=pc, predicted_next_pc=prediction, rs1_val=rs1,\n                           rs2_val=rs2, imm=int(dut.imm.value), rd=(instr >> 7) & 31,\n                           rs1_addr=(instr >> 15) & 31, rs2_addr=(instr >> 20) & 31,\n                           instr=instr)\n            dut.clock.value = 1\n            await Timer(5, \"ns\")\n            actual = _id_fields(int(dut.out.value))\n            if reset:\n                assert all(value == 0 for value in actual.values())\n            else:\n                for field, value in payload.items():\n                    assert actual[field] == (previous[field] if stall else value), (\n                        f\"{field}: flush={flush} stall={stall} payload mismatch\"\n                    )\n                assert actual[\"ctrl\"] == (0 if flush else previous[\"ctrl\"] if stall else decoded)\n                assert actual[\"valid\"] == (0 if flush else previous[\"valid\"] if stall else valid)\n            previous = actual\n\n\ndef test_id_stage_runner():\n    run_cocotb(toplevel=\"id_stage\",\n               sources=[\"core_pkg.sv\", \"decoder.sv\", \"imm_gen.sv\", \"id_stage.sv\"],\n               test_module=\"test_id_stage\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    e=json.loads(line)\n    if e['id'] in ('hyp-20260905-001-r6s0','hyp-20260905-002-r6s1','hyp-20260905-003-r11s2'):\n        print(e['id'], 'outcome=',e.get('outcome'), 'delta=',e.get('delta_pct'))\n        h=e.get('hypothesis',{})\n        print((h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)[:2800])\n        print('NOTES',e.get('implementation_notes','')[:1800])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    e=json.loads(line)\n    if e['id'] in ('hyp-20260905-001-r6s0','hyp-20260905-002-r6s1','hyp-20260905-003-r11s2'):\n        print(e['id'], 'outcome=',e.get('outcome'), 'delta=',e.get('delta_pct'))\n        h=e.get('hypothesis',{})\n        print((h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h,dict) else h)[:2800])\n        print('NOTES',e.get('implementation_notes','')[:1800])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260905-001-r6s0 outcome= regression delta= -8.81\nRe-encode only each row's two-bit saturating direction counter as a\nthree-bit thermometer register q[2:0]. Logical counts 0, 1, 2, and 3\nmap respectively to 000, 001, 011, and 111. Fetch uses q[1] directly\nfor taken prediction, preserving the current threshold without a\nbinary conversion or extra lookup logic. A taken hit increments by\nshifting in one: {q[1:0], 1'b1}. A not-taken hit decrements by shifting\nin zero: {1'b0, q[2:1]}. These operations saturate intrinsically.\nA jump sets 111; a taken miss allocates 011. A not-taken miss,\ninvalidation, and absence of a pending event retain the counter.\n\nExpress counter next state as parallel Boolean data terms, followed\nby an unconditional clocked assignment, rather than arithmetic or\nnested conditional writes. Specifically, let E be pending_valid_q\nAND !reset and H be the existing valid-qualified full-tag row_hit.\nDefine J = E AND command_is_JUMP, A = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND !H,\nU = E AND command_is_TAKEN AND H, D = E AND command_is_NOT_TAKEN AND H,\nand K = !(J OR A OR U OR D). Use bitwise equations:\n  next_q[2] = J | (U & q[1]) | (K & q[2]);\n  next_q[1] = J | A | (U & q[0]) | (D & q[2]) | (K & q[1]);\n  next_q[0] = J | A | U | (D & q[1]) | (K & q[0]).\nDecode the pending command locally and retain a single sequential\nowner for each counter. Allocation terms must produce known constants\neven when the old, invalid counter payload is uninitialized. Counter\npayloads need no reset: reset suppresses updates and the existing\nvalid reset masks them until a complete legal allocation.\n\nThe intended mapped circuit has no saturation detection or binary\narithmetic and no tag-dependent counter CE, SET, or RESET input.\nHit qualification and self-retention are represented in the data\nequations. Inspect the mapped netlist to verify this property: an\nequivalent rewrite that synthesis folds back into hit-qualified\ncounter enables does not establish the intended optimization. Do not\nintroduce device primitives, synthesis-only behavior, or blanket keep\nattributes to force a result. The source-level cost is just sixteen\nadditional counter bits; actual mapped LUT/FF cost must be measured.\n\nPreserve the sixteen rows, full 26-bit tags, exact tag comparisons,\nvalid/tag update logic, separate indexed target store, all private\npending events, and the existing capture/commit edges. Commit against\ncurrent row state, including consecutive events and different tags\naliasing one row. Keep matching-only invalidation and not-taken updates,\ntarget retention, reset cancellation, and once-only pending-event drain\nthrough stalls and redirects. Fetch still observes committed state\nwith the same pre-edge semantics and no update bypass. This is a\ncounter-encoding and logic-mapping micro-optimization, with identical\nprediction policy, capacity, \nNOTES \nhyp-20260905-002-r6s1 outcome= regression delta= -40.65\nAdd one register boundary between row-local tag validation and predictor\nmetadata commit in rtl/if_stage.sv. This is one structural retiming of\nthe existing training transaction. Retain the sixteen direct-mapped rows,\nPC[5:2] index, full PC[31:6] tags, aligned 30-bit targets, two-bit counter\npolicy, allocation rules, and committed-state-only fetch lookup.\n\nUse three edges for an accepted event E. At edge N, the existing private\nrow capture records E's tag and command. At edge N+1, transfer that event\ninto a new commit register and capture one hit bit computed against the\nrow's logical tag/valid state AFTER the older event committing on that\nsame edge. At edge N+2, apply E using its registered hit, command, and\ntag, together with the row's then-current two-bit counter. The existing\ndesign commits at N+1, so this adds exactly one training-visibility cycle.\nCapture, validate, and commit must overlap at one event per clock,\nincluding an uninterrupted stream of resolutions to the same row.\n\nResolve the validation-stage read-after-write hazard locally. Let C be\nthe older event currently in this row's commit register and P the event\nin its existing pending register. Compute two full-width comparisons in\nparallel: table_tag == P.tag and C.tag == P.tag. If C.valid and C.command\nis JUMP or TAKEN, P's effective hit is the second comparison because C\nallocates or refreshes a valid row with C.tag on the validation edge.\nOtherwise, if C.valid, C.command is INVALIDATE, and C.hit is true, P's\neffective hit is false because C clears the row. In all other cases use\ntable_valid && (table_tag == P.tag). Register this effective hit with P.\nSelection between comparator results is narrow; do not mux a 26-bit\neffective tag ahead of one equality comparator. All forwarding is private\nto the row and concerns tag/valid state only. No live resolve input or\nindexed metadata read may enter the validation or commit datapath.\n\nAt commit, use the registered hit without re-comparing tags. JUMP writes\nvalid=1, the event tag, target, and counter=11. TAKEN writes valid=1,\nthe event tag and target, and counter=10 on a registered miss or a\nsaturating increment of the current counter on a registered hit.\nNOT_TAKEN decrements the current counter with saturation only on a\nregistered hit and retains tag, valid, and target. INVALIDATE clears\nvalid only on a registered hit. Preserve the current field-specific\nupdate policy. Do not snapshot or forward the counter into validation:\nin-order commits ensure the current counter already includes all older\nevents. This avoids a new counter-update feedback bypass. After the\npipeline drains, every table field must equal sequential application of\nthe accepted resolution stream, even for alternating full-tag aliases.\n\nAdd a matching second delay register to\nNOTES \nhyp-20260905-003-r11s2 outcome= regression delta= -65.45\nPrevalidate the two operand-independent possible successors in ID and\ncarry their exact equality results with the instruction. Add two one-bit\nid_ex_t payload fields, prediction_matches_seq and prediction_matches_direct.\nCompute them in id_stage from the same IF bundle and decoded immediate\nthat the existing ID/EX payload captures:\n  prediction_matches_seq = (in.predicted_next_pc == seq_pc);\n  prediction_matches_direct = (in.predicted_next_pc == direct_pc);\nHere seq_pc is the 32-bit result of in.pc + 32'd4, and direct_pc is the\n32-bit result of in.pc + imm. Explicitly truncate both sums modulo 2^32\nbefore equality. Compare all 32 bits, including target alignment bits.\nThese are exact destination comparisons, not predicted-direction hints\nor truncated target signatures. Retain the full predicted_next_pc field.\n\nRegister both bits at the existing ID/EX boundary with exactly the winning\npayload discipline: deterministic zero on reset, retain on the existing\nbackend stall, otherwise capture even if a flush makes the payload inert.\nFlush still clears every ctrl bit and valid independently. Give each new\nfield one sequential owner. Do not add the recovery or load-use signals\nto these payload fields' reset or enable cones. Never recompute a held\ninstruction's match bits from live fetch, predictor state, or operands.\n\nReplace only EX's final prediction-correctness calculation. Start with\nprediction_correct = in.prediction_matches_seq. If ctrl_with_trap is legal,\na jump selects in.prediction_matches_direct for JAL, or the full equality\nof in.predicted_next_pc and the existing forwarded, bit-zero-cleared\njump_target for JALR. Otherwise a taken conditional branch selects\nin.prediction_matches_direct; an untaken branch or non-control retains\nin.prediction_matches_seq. A decoder or target-alignment trap always uses\nthe sequential match, following the existing PC+4 trap recovery policy.\nKeep redirect = resolve_valid && !prediction_correct. Compute the JALR\nequality against jump_target directly, in parallel with the final Boolean\nselection, rather than comparing against the selected actual_next_pc.\n\nLeave actual_next_pc generation unchanged for redirect_target and EX/MEM\npc_next, and retain the existing branch comparator, JALR forwarding,\nalignment checks, link writes, and EX predictor feedback. Retain the\noriginal resolve_valid qualification by in.valid, !stall, !div_wait, and\n!reset. This must be Boolean-equivalent to the original full destination\nmismatch for every advancing instruction. In particular, a taken transfer\nwhose destination equals PC+4 must not spuriously redirect; a stale target\non an ordinary instruction or completed divide must still recover; and a\nchanging JALR target must recover even when both predictions are taken.\nOnly operand-dependent JALR\nNOTES Implemented the decode prediction-validation retiming exactly as proposed. Correctness gates pass, but the measured hypothesis is a performance regression: median routed Fmax falls from 163.83 MHz to 56.61 MHz (65.445889% lower) with identical benchmark cycles.\n\nChanges are limited to three RTL files and three test files, plus these requested notes. `id_ex_t` retains the full prediction and adds two one-bit payload fields. ID computes local 32-bit PC+4 and PC+imm sums and compares all 32 prediction bits. Each match bit has one sequential owner: reset to zero, hold only on the existing backend stall, otherwise capture independently of flush. The existing control/valid flush priority is unchanged. EX selects the registered sequential/direct match or the independent equality against the existing forwarded, bit-zero-cleared JALR jump_target. Trap recovery selects the sequential match. resolve_valid, actual_next_pc, redirect_target, EX/MEM pc_next, arithmetic, forwarding, hazards, predictor policy/state/training, and nret=1 are unchanged. All eleven other RTL files are byte-identical to the incoming snapshot. No registered address buses or pipeline stages were added.\n\nTests now decode immediates independently from instruction encodings, unpack both match fields, and check payload capture on every advancing pipeline edge. ID tests cover every reset/flush/hold combination with changing inputs, positive/negative B/J offsets, modulo-2^32 wraparound, exact matches, and a mismatch in each of the 32 target bits. Pipeline coverage includes PC+4 transfers, taken/untaken misaligned branches, stale ordinary/trapping/divide predictions, EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarded changing JALR targets, link/operand values, memory/divide holds, predictor commits during holds, instruction-stalled recover\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
