{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\":\\\\s*\\\"predictor\\\"|hyp-20260906-003-r14s2|hyp-20260905-001-r5s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-650\"","aggregated_output":"4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Small tagged target predictor with two-bit branch hysteresis\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The bench core always fetches PC+4 and redirects in EX for every taken\\nconditional branch, JAL, and JALR. Its IF/ID bundle is combinational, so\\neach such redirect discards one younger issue slot, not two registered\\nfrontend stages. Predicting recurring taken transfers can remove that\\nbubble. A small PC-indexed target table avoids adding instruction-memory\\ndecode and a branch-immediate adder to the fetch-to-PC path. The target\\nhas no LESSONS.md and its experiment log contains only the 282.82 b\n7:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Two-way target prediction at unchanged 16-entry capacity\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The predictor in hyp-20260905-003-r1s2 cut stalled CoreMark cycles by\\n6.14% and reached 319.45 fitness, establishing that removing the\\ncombinational frontend's one-slot recovery bubbles is valuable. Its\\n16-entry direct-mapped table indexes PC[5:2], so recurring branches and\\njumps separated by multiples of 64 bytes evict each other's targets\\nand two-bit direction history. Such interference can persist even when\\nonly two control-flow PCs are hot. The experiment log contains one\\npredictor attempt a\n10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Row-local predictor metadata training to remove the indexed feedback path\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The existing 16-entry direct-mapped predictor already reduced stalled\\nCoreMark cycles by 6.14%. The two-way experiment hyp-20260905-003-r2s2\\nmeasured 4,220,017 cycles versus 4,215,937 for the direct-mapped winner,\\nwhile lowering median Fmax to 124.94 MHz. More lookup complexity has\\ntherefore not paid for itself in this core. The current winner,\\nhyp-20260905-002-r2s1, reports all three post-route critical paths through\\npredictor tag lookup and counter-write control. Its median pa\n13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Bank-local registered training commands for the target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current row-local predictor reaches 350.46 iter/s at 147.75 MHz,\\nwith all three reported critical paths running from ID/EX resolve-PC\\nbits through local tag matching to metadata enables. The median path\\nis 6.77 ns, including 3.72 ns of routing. This makes training fanout\\nand placement a more evidenced opportunity than greater associativity:\\nhyp-20260905-003-r2s2 lost frequency with essentially unchanged cycles.\\nThe delayed row-snapshot experiment hyp-20260905-002-r3s1 reached only\\n1\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Remove wide ID/EX payload registers from bubble-control fanout\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current winner, hyp-20260905-002-r4s1, scores 371.55 iter/s at\\n156.62 MHz median Fmax. Its median-seed timing report identifies a\\n6.39 ns path from predicted_next_pc[0], through prediction recovery\\nand hazard/divide qualification, to an ID/EX operand register RESET.\\nThe final control net alone contributes 1.95 ns of routing. In the\\ncurrent RTL, reset || flush clears the entire id_ex_t payload, and\\nstall_id also includes the load-use condition even though that case\\ninserts a bubble. Re\n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Eight-entry return stack with direct return recognition\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current predictor remembers one last destination per tagged PC. A\\nreturn shared by several callers therefore either predicts the previous\\ncaller's continuation or misses after target-table eviction. The saved\\n6 KB CoreMark ELF has several call sites for crcu16 and crc16, providing\\na concrete opportunity for caller-sensitive return prediction; this is\\nstatic evidence, not a measured return-misprediction rate.\\n\\nPrior predictor experiments favor a small, simple lookup: r2s2's two-way\\ntable los\n19:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Eight-bit partial tags for the target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Predictor timing has delivered substantially more fitness than the recent\\naccuracy additions. The r2s2 two-way table lost 7.32%, and the r5s2 return\\nstack saved only 0.17% of scored cycles while losing 3.98% fitness. The\\nsuccessful r4s1 event pipeline isolated live EX feedback, but two routed\\nseeds then had local stored-tag/pending-tag paths into counter enables.\\nThat comparison still spans 26 bits in the current r5s0 RTL, whose score\\nis 388.66 at 163.83 MHz. Shortening predictor identity is an untried w\n22:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Eight-entry target predictor with shorter lookup and less training fanout\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining prediction-accuracy opportunity is small relative to FPGA\\ntiming sensitivity. Saved r6s2 measurements of the current winner show\\n550409 recoveries in 30822790 bracketed cycles for canonical 6 KB CoreMark.\\nThe r5s2 return stack removed canonical-return recoveries but saved only\\n0.188% of those cycles, while frequency loss made fitness worse. The\\ntwo-way predictor, extra metadata-validation stage, thermometer counters,\\nand partial tags also regressed. In particular, \n25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Train bimodal direction counters independently of target tags\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current predictor qualifies direction-counter updates and taken-miss\\ninitialization with a 26-bit row-tag comparison. Recent r7s1 and r7s2\\nresults still identify row-counter paths among the routed bottlenecks.\\nReducing capacity, shortening tags, thermometer encoding, and adding a\\ntag-validation pipeline stage all regressed in the inspected history.\\nThe current winner reaches 388.66 fitness at 163.83 MHz, so a useful\\npredictor change must improve the clock/cycle product, not just accurac\n28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Capture raw predictor events before update classification\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current sixteen-entry predictor classifies each live EX resolution\\ninto INVALIDATE/JUMP/TAKEN/NOT_TAKEN before capturing its private row\\nevent. Classification includes target-trap legality and branch outcome;\\nthe same late conditions also qualify the pending target register's\\ncapture enable. This puts prediction maintenance on the execution path\\neven though its writes occur one edge later.\\n\\nThe predictor history supports shortening maintenance paths while\\nretaining prediction behavior: ro\n31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Train the predictor from registered EX/MEM results\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Predictor history favors reducing update-path delay over adding accuracy:\\nthe return stack saved only 0.17% of scored cycles, while associativity,\\nsmaller tables, partial tags, and untagged direction training regressed.\\nThe latest raw-event experiment, hyp-20260905-003-r9s2, still drove sixteen\\nprivate taken-outcome capture registers from the forwarded EX comparison;\\nthat path limited all three seeds to a 125.47 MHz median. Changing the\\nclassification boundary did not isolate the comparator from row \n34:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260905-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Prevalidate sequential and direct-target predictions in decode\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Recent predictor changes have repeatedly lost frequency: untagged direction\\ntraining, raw-event capture, and EX/MEM-sourced training regressed by 33.64%,\\n23.41%, and 12.29%. Accuracy additions have offered little cycle headroom;\\nthe return stack saved only 0.17% of the historically scored cycles. The\\nnext predictor experiment should shorten prediction validation itself.\\n\\nThe current RTL matches the saved incoming winner in the r8s2 evidence\\nbyte-for-byte. Its seed-2 critical path starts \n37:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"XOR-folded PC indexing for the existing tagged target predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current sixteen-entry predictor places every instruction at the same\\noffset within a 64-byte block in the same row, because it indexes only\\nPC[5:2]. Recurring branches and jumps can therefore evict each other's\\ntargets and direction history even when other rows are unused. The log\\nrecords 80,662 recoveries in 4,215,262 bracketed cycles for the winner's\\nhistorical scored workload, and 550,409 in 30,822,790 cycles for its\\nseparate canonical 6 KB run. These totals include direction and \n40:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r13s2\", \"title\": \"Validate sequential predictions from unflushed IF state\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Preserve the winner's prediction accuracy and target-table organization\\nwhile shortening prediction recovery. The r8s2 and r12s1 implementation\\nnotes reproduce an incoming seed-2 critical path from registered\\npredicted_next_pc[3] to ID/EX valid.RESET; the other two incoming seeds\\nare limited by ALU result selection. Predictor capacity, associativity,\\nreturn prediction, partial tags, and XOR indexing have already regressed.\\nThese results favor a small change to recovery timing with unchanged\\npre\n43:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260906-003-r14s2\", \"title\": \"Separate predictor fetch tags from row-local training tags\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The sixteen scalar predictor tags currently serve two different consumers:\\ntheir private maintenance comparators and the shared 16-to-1, 26-bit fetch\\ntag mux. Row-local training and registered events produced the r3s2/r4s1\\ngains, but the fetch mux still connects those otherwise local registers\\nacross the table. Later experiment reports repeatedly expose tag-to-counter\\nenable paths. This is a source-level locality opportunity, not a claim that\\nmaintenance is the current winner's worst path: r1\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
